window.FORTIS_SEARCH = [{"u":"README.html#fortis","p":"Fortis","h":"Fortis","m":"home","x":"Fortis # ! The Fortis emblem: a fortress tower set in a shield Written by AI. Reviewed by people. AI agents now write a lot of the code we ship, and they write it faster than anyone can read it. Fortis is a programming language built for that world. Every decision that matters, who owns a value, what can change it, what can fail and who handles it, what a function is allowed to do, is written in the source, so a person reading a file or a diff can see it at a glance and trust what they approve. Website \u00b7 Tour \u00b7 Guide \u00b7 For agents \u00b7 Release notes"},{"u":"README.html#why-fortis","p":"Fortis","h":"Why Fortis","m":"home","x":"Why Fortis # You can review it without running it in your head. Fortis puts the important facts on the line where they happen. A value that may change is declared so, with let mut or editable , and a method call that changes it needs no further mark. A function that changes something you hand it is marked at the call: normalise(editable readings) . A function that can fail says so in its signature. You don't have to trace the whole program to know what one line does. Failures can't hide. A call that can fail must be handled: passed up, reported, or recovered from, and the compiler checks that it is. Nothing is silently swallowed, so the unhappy path is as easy to review as the happy one. Memory is safe, with no garbage collector. Every value has one owner. Borrowing and moving are checked, and cleanup runs exactly once, at a point you can see in the code. Promises are checked, not trusted. Mark a function pure and the compiler proves it has no effects. Printing, files, the network and the clock all need a capability that is handed in explicitly, so nothing reaches the outside world by accident. One program, three ways to run it, one meaning. The same checked program runs on a reference interpreter, on a fast development VM, and as a native executable compiled through safe Rust ( #![forbid(unsafe_code)] ). All three must agree on every program, and the test suite holds them to it. Made for agents as well as people. The documentation comes in two editions: a guide for people, and a reference written for coding agents, with rules by ID and a fix for every error. Every compiler message is also a structured record, so an agent can read it and correct its own code. The fortis-code skill and the website's llms.txt give agents the whole language in a form they can use. The docs can't drift. Every example in the documentation is checked and run on all three backends whenever the code changes. If the docs say a program prints something, it does."},{"u":"README.html#see-it","p":"Fortis","h":"See it","m":"home","x":"See it # Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error Empty { code : \"demo.empty\" } // The mean of some readings; an empty list is a failure the caller must handle. pure try func mean ( readings : ListView [ Int < 0 .. 1000 > ] ) Int { let n = readings . count ( ) if n <= 0 { fail Empty ( ) } let mut total = 0 for r in readings { total = total + r or fail } return total / n } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut readings : List [ Int < 0 .. 1000 > ] = List . of ( 12 , 30 , 18 ) readings . push ( 40 ) let m = mean ( readings ) catch e { report e recover 0 } console . print ( \"mean of ${readings.count()} readings: ${m}\" ) } output mean of 4 readings: 25 That's a whole program. Reading it, you can tell without running anything: mean can fail ( try ), and fail Empty() is where it does. mean has no side effects ( pure ), and the compiler checks the claim. readings changes, and its declaration says so: let mut . The push needs no other marker. The caller decides what a failure means here: catch reports it, then carries on with 0 . The addition can't silently overflow: total + r is checked, and or fail passes an overflow up. Printing needs the console, which main declares as a parameter and the runtime supplies."},{"u":"README.html#try-it-in-two-minutes","p":"Fortis","h":"Try it in two minutes","m":"home","x":"Try it in two minutes # You need Python 3 and a Rust toolchain with cargo . From a checkout of this repository: sh cargo build --release --manifest-path compiler/Cargo.toml Save the example above as mean.ft , then: To Run check it bin/fortis check mean.ft run it bin/fortis run mean.ft test it bin/fortis test mean.ft build a native executable bin/fortis build mean.ft --out mean format it bin/fortis fmt --write mean.ft Then take the tour ."},{"u":"README.html#where-its-going","p":"Fortis","h":"Where it's going","m":"home","x":"Where it's going # Fortis is released in steps. Each release is a usable piece of the language, with its own documentation. Release What it gives you v0.0.1 Real programs: modules, functions, classes, enums, ownership and failures, checked, run, built, formatted and tested v0.0.2 (now) Code that says only what matters: fewer markers, capabilities as main 's parameters, built-in logging v0.1 Domain modelling: interfaces, generics, refinements and contracts v0.2 Effects you can trust, property tests, packages with dependencies v0.3 Concurrency: async, tasks and structured scopes v0.4 A complete standard library v0.5 and v0.6 Production and scale: observation, runtime kits, an incremental compiler v1.0 Stability: editions and migrations, and performance measured against Rust v0.0.2 is an early release. Its known limitations are listed in full, and the tier-1 platform is Linux x86-64."},{"u":"README.html#learn-more","p":"Fortis","h":"Learn more","m":"home","x":"Learn more # The tour : Fortis from the first program. The guide : the language chapter by chapter, with worked examples and notes for people coming from Rust , TypeScript and Python . The agent reference : the same language, written for coding agents. The Language Reference : the specification, and the source of truth."},{"u":"README.html#for-contributors","p":"Fortis","h":"For contributors","m":"home","x":"For contributors # Directory Contents compiler/ the production toolchain in Rust ( fortisc ): parser, checker, formatter, typed IR, VM and native lowering checker/ the reference checker in Python, which must agree with the production one on every fixture interpreter/ the definitional interpreter, the reference the other backends are compared with library/ the standard package fortis , written in Fortis conformance/ the conformance corpus and its harnesses, including the documentation checks website/ the website, built from this repository docs/releases/ the release notes To work on Fortis itself, start with CONTRIBUTING.md ; agents also follow AGENTS.md. Changes to the language go through the Language Reference first. To report a security problem, see SECURITY.md ."},{"u":"README.html#licence","p":"Fortis","h":"Licence","m":"home","x":"Licence # Fortis is developed by Pickering Technologies Ltd and released under the MIT License ."},{"u":"docs/guide/03-lexical.html#lexical-structure","p":"Lexical structure","h":"Lexical structure","m":"human","x":"Lexical structure # Human mode. Agent mode: Lexical structure, by rule . Source of truth: LR 3 . This chapter is about the text of a program before it means anything: files, lines, names, keywords, literals, strings and comments. Fortis has no semicolons, decides what a name is from its first letter, and has exactly one kind of comment. Each of those choices keeps the page quieter and makes the parser simpler, so that a tool (or a person) never needs to know what a name refers to in order to know how a line is built."},{"u":"docs/guide/03-lexical.html#source-text","p":"Lexical structure","h":"Source text","m":"human","x":"Source text # A source file is UTF-8 text with the extension .ft ( LR 3.1 ). Identifiers are ASCII: a letter or underscore, then letters, digits or underscores. Unicode is welcome everywhere else: in string literals, comments and data. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let word = \"caf\u00e9\" console . print ( \"${word}: ${word.scalarCount()} scalars, ${word.byteCount()} bytes\" ) } output caf\u00e9: 4 scalars, 5 bytes A file that begins with a byte-order mark is rejected ( LR 3.1 ). Source text is also treated as untrusted input with quotas: a file over 16 MiB or 2,000,000 tokens, or with brackets, blocks, prefix operators or string interpolations nested more than 256 deep, is E-LIM-002 and is not parsed at all ( LR 3.1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/03-lexical.html#lines-and-statements","p":"Lexical structure","h":"Lines and statements","m":"human","x":"Lines and statements # A newline ends a statement. There is no semicolon in Fortis, and no rule that inserts one behind your back: a ; anywhere outside a string, a comment or a raw template region (a kit's template text, chapter 16 ) is E-SYN-001 ( LR 3.2 , LR 16 ). The checker's record for it carries a correction, the edit that deletes the semicolon: Fortis // reject: E-SYN-001 func main() { let a = 1; let b = 2 // reject: E-SYN-001 } output example.ft:3:12: error E-SYN-001 [LANG-03-01] (Syntax, violated): ';' is not a token expected_tokens: [] found_token: \";\" correction: delete the semicolon example.ft:3:12-3:13 -> \"\" A statement continues onto the next line in exactly three situations ( LR 3.2 ): the newline is inside a (\u2026) , [\u2026] or {\u2026} that belongs to the same expression or argument list, which is how long argument lists and record literals spread over several lines; the line ends with a token that cannot end an expression: , ( [ = => , an arithmetic, bitwise, comparison or logical operator, ?? , .. , ..< or . ; the next line starts with one of . or catch ?? else ) ] with where implements derive from . So a method chain may put each . at the start of a line, and a failure decision may go on its own line, which is where a reviewer wants it: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 != 0 { return 0 } return Int . div ( n , 2 ) or fail } recovers func main ( console : Console ) { let words : List [ String ] = List . of ( \"alpha\" , \"beta\" , \"gamma\" ) let count = words . reversed ( ) . count ( ) let missing : Int ? = null let n = missing ?? 7 let h = half ( 10 ) or recover - 1 let total = 1 + 2 + 3 console . print ( \"${count} ${n} ${h} ${total}\" ) if total > 5 { console . print ( \"big\" ) } else { console . print ( \"small\" ) } } output 3 7 5 6 big The two lists are not symmetrical, and that is on purpose: a binary operator continues a line only when it is last on the line. A + at the start of a line would make the line before it a complete statement, so it is a parse error rather than a silent change of meaning ( LR 3.2 ): Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 func main ( ) { let a = 1 let b = 2 let total = a + b // reject: E-SYN-000 } The < and > that open and close a refinement such as Int<0..100> are not comparison operators and do not continue a line, so type Score = Int<0..100> ends where it looks like it ends ( LR 3.2 ). A brace either opens a body (after a function head, if , else , a loop, defer , catch e , a declaration and so on) or is part of an expression (a record literal or record type). A body may be empty, span many lines, or sit on one line as { statement } , as { return 0 } does above ( LR 3.2 ). return , fail and give take their operand on the same line; a bare return on its own line returns Void ( LR 3.2 ). A token sequence that is not a sentence of the grammar, and that no more specific syntax code describes, is E-SYN-000 ( LR 3.2 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/03-lexical.html#names-the-first-letter-decides","p":"Lexical structure","h":"Names: the first letter decides","m":"human","x":"Names: the first letter decides # Whether an identifier names a type or a value is decided by its first character, with no symbol table ( LR 3.3 ): First character Names Examples uppercase letter a type, interface, mixin, enum, variant, contract, error kind or observation schema Invoice , List , Draft lowercase letter or _ a value, function, method, field, parameter, module alias or label invoice , total , text This is what lets the parser read List[Int] as a generic type and list[i] as indexing, and Int<0..9> as a refinement where a < b is a comparison, without knowing what any name refers to ( LR 3.3 ). Module path segments and package names are lowercase ( acme.billing ), and constants are ordinary lowercase names declared with const ( LR 3.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/03-lexical.html#keywords","p":"Lexical structure","h":"Keywords","m":"human","x":"Keywords # Keywords are reserved: none of them can name a declaration, a binding or a parameter ( E-SYN-002 , LR 3.4 ). Fortis // reject: E-SYN-002 func main ( ) { let match = 1 // reject: E-SYN-002 } The reserved words include the declaration heads that kits add, such as model , component , context and state , so that no module in any kit can collide with them ( LR 3.4 , LR 16 ). That surprises people when the natural name for a local is state or view ; the checker reports it like any other keyword. There is one relaxation. After a . , and as the name of a method or associated function, a keyword is an ordinary name ( LR 3.4 ). That is how the standard library spells String.from(view) , and your own types may do the same: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Celsius { export degrees : Int // `from` is a keyword, but a method or associated function may use it as its name. export pure func from ( degrees : Int ) Celsius => Celsius ( degrees ) } func main ( console : Console ) { let reading = Celsius . from ( 21 ) console . print ( \"${reading.degrees} degrees\" ) } output 21 degrees Appendix A lists every keyword and the words that look like keywords but are not ( launch , retain , validate and the rest)."},{"u":"docs/guide/03-lexical.html#literals","p":"Lexical structure","h":"Literals","m":"human","x":"Literals # Each literal has a form and a type ( LR 3.5 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let million = 1_000_000 let mask = 0xFF let flags = 0b1010 let mode = 0o17 let ratio = 1.5 let big = 2e3 let single = 3.0f32 let price = 19.99d let ready = true let wait = 250ms let later = 2min let none : Int ? = null console . print ( \"${million} ${mask} ${flags} ${mode}\" ) console . print ( \"${ratio} ${big} ${single} ${price} ${ready}\" ) console . print ( \"${wait} ${later} ${none ?? -1}\" ) } output 1000000 255 10 15 1.5 2000.0 3.0 19.99 true 250ms 2min -1 An integer literal (decimal, 0x , 0b or 0o , with _ between digit groups) takes its type from context, and is an Int when nothing says otherwise. Its value must fit the type it lands in, refinement included, and the checker knows the value exactly, so a literal that does not fit is rejected at compile time ( LR 3.5 ). A float literal needs a . or an exponent; f32 makes a Float32 ( LR 3.5 ). d makes a Decimal , an exact decimal number, which displays with exactly its scale ( LR 3.5 , LR 6.5 ). 250ms , 3s , 2min and 1h are Duration literals ( LR 3.5 ). null is the absent case of an optional type ( LR 3.5 , LR 6.8 ). Fortis // reject: E-REF-001 func main ( ) { let small : Int < 0 .. 10 > = 11 // reject: E-REF-001 } A byte string b\"abc\" is a BytesView of static bytes ( LR 3.5 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let magic = b\"FTS1\" console . print ( \"${magic.byteCount()} bytes\" ) } output 4 bytes"},{"u":"docs/guide/03-lexical.html#strings-and-interpolation","p":"Lexical structure","h":"Strings and interpolation","m":"human","x":"Strings and interpolation # A string literal without interpolation is a StringView from static : a view of text stored in the program, which costs nothing to use ( LR 3.5 ). A literal with ${\u2026} in it builds a new String , which allocates. That String can be passed straight to anything that reads a StringView , but a binding declared as StringView cannot hold it, because there would be nothing for the view to point into once the statement ends ( E-TYP-040 , LR 3.5 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 func main ( ) { let name = \"ada\" let fixed : StringView = \"hello\" let built : String = \"hello, ${name}\" let wrong : StringView = \"hello, ${name}\" // reject: E-TYP-040 } A raw string, \"\"\"\u2026\"\"\" , has no interpolation and no escapes, so ${ inside it is just text ( LR 3.5 ). Each interpolated expression is evaluated exactly once, left to right with the rest of the string, and must have a type that implements Displayable ( LR 3.6 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func note ( console : Console , label : StringView , value : Int ) Int { console . print ( \"evaluating ${label}\" ) return value } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${note(console, \"a\", 1)} then ${note(console, \"b\", 2)}\" ) console . print ( \"\"\"a raw ${string}\"\"\" ) } output evaluating a evaluating b 1 then 2 a raw ${string} A value whose type is not Displayable cannot be interpolated; the record names Displayable as the expected type ( E-TYP-040 , LR 3.5 ). The same applies to the result of a fallible call: an interpolated expression cannot fail or suspend, and an Outcome is not displayable, so write the call on its own line and interpolate the binding ( LR 3.6 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 import fortis . host { Console } class Opaque { export n : Int } try func risky ( n : Int ) Int => n try func main ( console : Console ) { let o = Opaque ( n : 1 ) console . print ( \"${o}\" ) // reject: E-TYP-040 console . print ( \"${risky(2)}\" ) // reject: E-TYP-040 let r = risky ( 2 ) or fail console . print ( \"${r}\" ) } Interpolation never escapes text for HTML or any other context; that is the job of the Web UI kit's templates ( LR 3.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/03-lexical.html#comments","p":"Lexical structure","h":"Comments","m":"human","x":"Comments # There is one comment form: // to the end of the line. There are no block comments and no separate documentation-comment syntax ( LR 3.7 ): Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 / * not a comment in Fortis * / // reject: E-SYN-000 func main ( ) { } A run of // lines directly above a declaration, with no blank line in between, is that declaration's documentation ( LR 3.7 ). fortis view --decls lists each declaration with it: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } // The number of whole minutes in a number of seconds. // Rounds down: 119 seconds is one minute. export pure func minutes ( seconds : Int < 0 .. 86400 > ) Int => seconds / 60 func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${minutes(119)}\" ) } output 1 A fenced fortis block inside documentation is compiled as an example and must start with one of the markers this guide uses ( // accept , // reject: \u2026 , // execute: \u2026 or // illustrative ); a marker directly above a declaration never becomes its documentation ( LR 3.7 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/04-modules.html#modules-and-packages","p":"Modules and packages","h":"Modules and packages","m":"human","x":"Modules and packages # Human mode. Agent mode: Modules and packages, by rule . Source of truth: LR 4 . Preview: dependencies and lock files ( LR 4.1 ) arrive in v0.2. An async entry point ( LR 4.6 , LR 12.3 ) arrives in v0.3. One package, its modules, imports, visibility and the ordinary entry point ( LR 4.1 , 4.2 , 4.3 , 4.4 , 4.5 and 4.6 ) are in v0.0.1. A Fortis program is a set of packages; a package is a set of modules; a module is one file ( LR 1 ). This chapter covers how those three fit together: the manifest that names a package, how a file's path becomes its module's name, how imports find things, who can see what, and where a program starts."},{"u":"docs/guide/04-modules.html#packages-and-their-manifest","p":"Modules and packages","h":"Packages and their manifest","m":"human","x":"Packages and their manifest # A package is the unit of distribution, versioning and visibility: a directory holding a manifest, project.yaml , a lockfile and a src/ directory ( LR 4.1 ). The manifest names the package, its version, its dependencies, which of its modules other packages may import ( exports: ) and, for an application, the module where it starts ( entry: ): YAML # illustrative schema: fortis.project/v1 name: acme.billing version: 1.4.0 exports: [invoices, money] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" Packages are nominal: two versions of one package in the same build are two different packages, and their types never interchange ( LR 4.1 ). A dependency on a standard package ( fortis or fortis.\u2026 ) must name a package of the published compatibility set at the set's version, because the standard packages and kits are tested together as a set ( LR 4.1 ). The lockfile, fortis.lock , written by fortis lock , pins everything a build depends on: the root package, the digests of the compiler, standard library and Rust toolchain, and the identity and content digest of every package the manifests reach. When a package has a lockfile, resolution uses only it and fetches nothing. A dependency the lockfile does not pin, a pin nothing uses, or an unreadable lockfile is E-NAM-020 ; a digest that differs from the pinned one is E-NAM-021 ( LR 4.1 ). A few more checks guard the edges of a build ( LR 4.1 ): Code When LR E-NAM-030 fortis check --target T or fortis build --target T finds an import of a standard module the target's feature set lacks LR 4.1 E-NAM-031 a dependency names a standard package outside the compatibility set, or at another version LR 4.1 E-NAM-032 a native build meets a package that ships a Cargo build script LR 4.1 E-LIM-003 , E-LIM-004 , E-LIM-005 a manifest over 1 MiB or 10,000 lines, a lockfile over 8 MiB or 100,000 lines, or JSON the toolchain reads over 256 MiB or nested more than 128 deep LR 4.1 E-INT-001 , E-INT-002 , E-INT-003 the toolchain itself failed: a checker defect (its record names a minimised reproduction), generated Rust that does not compile, or a native tool that is missing or fails LR 4.1 None of these is a crash: a failure inside the toolchain is always a record ( LR 4.1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/04-modules.html#modules","p":"Modules and packages","h":"Modules","m":"human","x":"Modules # A module is one source file under src/ . Its name is its path relative to src/ , with / replaced by . and the extension dropped: src/orders.ft is orders and src/pricing/tax.ft is pricing.tax . A directory is not itself a module, and a module has no header line declaring its name ( LR 4.2 ). Other packages import it as the package name followed by the module path: guide.shop.pricing.tax . A module contains only declarations and const bindings whose initialisers are compile-time constants ( LR 4.2 ). There is no module initialisation code, so importing a module never runs anything, and the order in which modules load can never change what a program does. A statement at the top level is therefore a syntax error: Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 func main ( ) { } let counter = 0 // reject: E-SYN-000"},{"u":"docs/guide/04-modules.html#a-package-file-by-file","p":"Modules and packages","h":"A package, file by file","m":"human","x":"A package, file by file # Here is a small application package, guide.shop , with three modules, app , orders and pricing.tax , named by their paths under src/ ( LR 4.2 ). Each block is one file: YAML # file: guide.shop/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: guide.shop version: 1.0.0 entry: app exports: [orders] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" Fortis // accept // file: guide.shop/src/pricing/tax.ft // Tax at 20%, in whole pennies, rounded down. export pure func withTax ( pennies : Int < 0 .. 100000 > ) Int => pennies + pennies / 5 Fortis // accept // file: guide.shop/src/orders.ft import guide . shop . pricing . tax { withTax } // An order line. Every field is exported, so other modules may build one. export class Line { export item : String export pennies : Int < 0 .. 100000 > } export pure func total ( lines : List [ Line ] ) Int { let mut sum = 0 for line in lines { sum = Int . saturatingAdd ( sum , withTax ( line . pennies ) ) } return sum } Fortis // accept // file: guide.shop/src/app.ft import fortis . host { Console } import guide . shop . orders { Line , total } import guide . shop . pricing . tax as tax func main ( console : Console ) { let lines = List . of ( Line ( item : \"tea\" , pennies : 250 ) , Line ( item : \"cake\" , pennies : 300 ) ) console . print ( \"total ${total(lines)} pennies\" ) console . print ( \"one cake ${tax.withTax(300)}\" ) } output total 660 pennies one cake 360 Run it with fortis run guide.shop (the package's directory), or build it with fortis build guide.shop ."},{"u":"docs/guide/04-modules.html#imports","p":"Modules and packages","h":"Imports","m":"human","x":"Imports # An import names a module by its full path, and imports appear only at the top of a module, before any declaration ( LR 4.3 ). There are three forms: Form Binds import guide.shop.orders the alias orders ; members are used as orders.total(\u2026) import guide.shop.pricing.tax as tax the alias tax import guide.shop.orders { Line, total } the listed members, directly, and nothing else The path is split into a package and a module by the manifest: the longest prefix that names the current package or one of its declared dependencies is the package, and the rest is the module path ( LR 4.3 ). That is why the modules of guide.shop import each other as guide.shop.orders and not as orders : a bare orders names no package. Inside a package, any module may import any of its siblings ( LR 4.3 ). The standard package is fortis . Its module fortis.prelude is imported into every module automatically and provides the core types ( Int , String , List , Map , Outcome , \u2026) and the built-in interfaces; every other standard module ( fortis.text , fortis.collections , fortis.numeric , fortis.time , fortis.money , fortis.host ) needs an import ( LR 4.3 ). An import after a declaration is a syntax error ( LR 4.3 ): Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 import fortis . host { Host } func main ( ) { } import fortis . text // reject: E-SYN-000 A name imported into a module must not collide with a declaration of that module ( LR 4.3 ). The LR names no code for this yet, so read the record's message (X-152). The LR also says that importing the same name twice is an error, and that an unused import is a warning under the baseline lint level and an error under the strict one ( LR 4.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/04-modules.html#a-packages-root-module","p":"Modules and packages","h":"A package's root module","m":"human","x":"A package's root module # A package may name a root module in its manifest ( root: core ); an import of the bare package name then denotes that module ( LR 4.3 ). Here guide.units is a library whose root module is core , and guide.convert depends on it: YAML # file: guide.units/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: guide.units version: 2.1.0 root: core exports: [core] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" Fortis // accept // file: guide.units/src/core.ft export pure func metres ( feet : Int < 0 .. 10000 > ) Int => feet * 3048 / 10000 test \"ten feet is three metres, rounded down\" { expect metres ( 10 ) == 3 } YAML # file: guide.convert/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: guide.convert version: 1.0.0 entry: main exports: [] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" guide.units: \"2.1.0\" Fortis // accept // file: guide.convert/src/main.ft import fortis . host { Console } import guide . units { metres } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"100 feet is ${metres(100)} metres\" ) } output 100 feet is 30 metres Without a root module, an import of the bare package name is an error, and a set of dependencies whose names could split an import path two ways is rejected as a whole ( E-NAM-010 , LR 4.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/04-modules.html#visibility","p":"Modules and packages","h":"Visibility","m":"human","x":"Visibility # There is one visibility modifier, export ( LR 4.4 ). A declaration without it is private to its module. An exported declaration is visible to every module that imports its module, but whether it is visible outside the package is decided by the manifest: only the modules listed in exports: can be imported by another package, so an export in an unlisted module reaches its siblings only ( LR 4.3 , LR 4.4 ). In guide.shop above, orders is listed and pricing.tax is not, so another package may import orders but not pricing.tax ; the import is refused, with a record naming the modules that are exported. The LR names no code for this refusal yet ( LR 4.3 , X-152). Members follow the same rule. Fields, methods and associated functions of a class are private unless marked export , and using a declaration or member that is not visible is E-NAM-004 , reported where it is used ( LR 4.4 ). A package that another package depends on is a library: it has no entry and no main . Only the root program's entry main creates the capabilities, and a library's functions receive them as parameters ( LR 11.6 ). Here a small library, guide.menu , keeps discountCode private: YAML # file: guide.menu/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: guide.menu version: 1.0.0 exports: [dishes] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" Fortis // accept // file: guide.menu/src/dishes.ft export pure func teaPennies ( ) Int => 250 pure func discountCode ( ) StringView from static => \"SPRING\" test \"tea costs 250 pennies\" { expect teaPennies ( ) == 250 } An application that depends on it cannot reach the private member: YAML # file: guide.till/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: guide.till version: 1.0.0 entry: main exports: [] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" guide.menu: \"1.0.0\" Fortis // reject: E-NAM-004 // file: guide.till/src/main.ft import fortis . host { Console } import guide . menu . dishes func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( dishes . discountCode ( ) ) // reject: E-NAM-004 } An exported signature may name only exported types. An exported function returning a private class would hand callers a value whose type they cannot name, so it is rejected with E-TYP-030 ( LR 4.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-030 class Hidden { export n : Int } export pure func make ( ) Hidden => Hidden ( n : 1 ) // reject: E-TYP-030 func main ( ) { } Two more visibility rules shape how classes are used across modules ( LR 4.4 ). An exported field can be read through any loan and written through an editable one. And a class's generated constructor, Line(item: \u2026, pennies: \u2026) , is available only where every field is visible, which is how a class with private fields forces every other module through its own associated functions ( LR 4.4 , LR 9.2 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/04-modules.html#names-and-resolution","p":"Modules and packages","h":"Names and resolution","m":"human","x":"Names and resolution # Each module has one namespace: its declarations, its imported aliases and its imported members. A name resolves to the innermost enclosing block that declares it, then to the module ( LR 4.5 ). Qualified names take four forms: module.member , Type.associated , Enum.Variant and value.field or value.method(\u2026) . There is no :: . Two rules keep a name's meaning obvious at every use ( LR 4.5 ). A let that would shadow a visible binding is E-NAM-003 (see Statements and expressions ). And a field is always reached through a receiver, self.lines or order.lines ; inside a method, a bare field name is not in scope. A type's identity is its name, its declaring package and version, and its declaring module ( LR 4.1 ). Two modules may each declare a Point , and they are two different types, even with identical fields. Passing one where the other is expected is a type mismatch, E-TYP-040 ( LR 6.1 ), and the record names both modules. Here is a library whose two modules each declare a Point , and a test that uses both correctly: YAML # file: guide.geo/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: guide.geo version: 1.0.0 exports: [flat, screen] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" Fortis // accept // file: guide.geo/src/flat.ft export trivial class Point { export x : Int export y : Int } Fortis // accept // file: guide.geo/src/screen.ft export trivial class Point { export x : Int export y : Int } export pure func column ( p : Point ) Int => p . x Fortis // accept // file: guide.geo/src/both.ft import guide . geo . flat import guide . geo . screen test \"each module's Point is its own type\" { let onScreen = screen . Point ( x : 3 , y : 4 ) let onPaper = flat . Point ( x : 1 , y : 2 ) expect screen . column ( onScreen ) == 3 expect onPaper . x == 1 } A module that passes a flat.Point where a screen.Point is expected is rejected, here in an application that uses the library: YAML # file: guide.geomix/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: guide.geomix version: 1.0.0 entry: app exports: [] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" guide.geo: \"1.0.0\" Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 // file: guide.geomix/src/app.ft import guide . geo . flat import guide . geo . screen func main ( ) { let onPaper = flat . Point ( x : 1 , y : 2 ) discard screen . column ( onPaper ) // reject: E-TYP-040 } This is what lets two versions of one package, or two packages that happen to use the same names, live in one program: each version's functions and types run where they are named ( LR 4.1 , LR 4.4 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/04-modules.html#the-entry-point","p":"Modules and packages","h":"The entry point","m":"human","x":"The entry point # An application names its entry module in the manifest ( entry: app ), and that module declares a function called main with one of four heads ( LR 4.6 ): Head Use it when main func main(\u2026) cannot fail and does not suspend try func main(\u2026) may fail async func main(\u2026) awaits asynchronous work async try func main(\u2026) both main asks for what it needs as parameters, and the runtime supplies each one by its type: List[String] gets the command-line arguments, and each capability type of fortis.host ( Console , FileSystem , Clock and the rest) gets that capability. Names and order are yours, each type appears once, and a main that needs nothing takes no parameters. export is implied on the entry main ( LR 4.6 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console , FileSystem , Path } try func main ( console : Console , files : FileSystem , args : List [ String ] ) { let path = Path . parse ( \"note.txt\" ) or fail files . writeAll ( path , \"${args.count()} arguments\" ) or fail console . print ( files . readText ( path ) or fail ) } output 0 arguments A parameter of any other type is rejected ( E-NAM-005 ), and so is a second parameter of a type already taken ( E-NAM-006 , LR 4.6 ). main owns the capabilities it receives: it lends them to the functions it calls, and it may move one, for example into a Shared that several owners hold ( LR 4.6 , LR 7.8 ). Older code writes export func main(args: List[String]) Void and creates its capabilities with Host.capabilities() in the body; that form is still accepted, and fortis fmt rewrites it to this one. Any of the four may also carry recovers , written like any function whose summary has that effect: a main that reaches a function that recovers a failure writes try recovers func main ( LR 4.6 , LR 11.2 ). Today's checkers refuse recovers on the main of a package, though they accept it in a single file (D-52); until that is fixed, a package's main can report the failures it would recover instead. main gets I/O authority through its capability parameters, and no other function receives capabilities from the runtime or creates them, which is why every function that does I/O through a capability must be handed one; report and the log statements are the io operations that need none ( LR 4.6 , LR 11.6 , LR 10.6 , LR 13 ). A package whose entry module has no main with one of these heads is rejected ( LR 4.6 ); the LR names no code for that yet (X-152). If a try main fails, the runtime writes exactly one report of the failure to standard error and the process exits with status 1 ( LR 4.6 ). Nothing else is needed, and there is no or die : Fortis // accept (exit status 1) import fortis . host { Path , Console , FileSystem } try func main ( console : Console , fileSystem : FileSystem ) { console . print ( \"reading settings\" ) let path = Path . parse ( \"settings.txt\" ) or fail let text = fileSystem . readText ( path ) or fail console . print ( text ) } output reading settings The file does not exist, so readText fails with fs.not_found , or fail passes the failure to main 's caller, and the runtime reports it once: as text on a terminal, and as one JSON record on a pipe ( LR 4.6 ). The report is a record like every other finding. An async main runs on the core cooperative executor as the root task, and the process ends when it completes ( LR 4.6 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Clock , Console } async func greet ( console : Console , clock : Clock ) { await clock . sleep ( 10ms ) console . print ( \"after a short sleep\" ) } async func main ( console : Console , clock : Clock ) { console . print ( \"starting\" ) await greet ( console , clock ) } output starting after a short sleep Cleanup, async and concurrency explains async and await ."},{"u":"docs/guide/05-statements.html#statements-and-expressions","p":"Statements and expressions","h":"Statements and expressions","m":"human","x":"Statements and expressions # Human mode. Agent mode: Statements and expressions, by rule . Source of truth: LR 5 . A Fortis function body is a sequence of statements in blocks. This chapter covers bindings and assignment, conditionals, loops, the four ways to leave a block with a value or a failure, operators and the order in which everything is evaluated. Most of it will look familiar; the differences are deliberate, and each one exists so that a reader can tell from the line itself what it does."},{"u":"docs/guide/05-statements.html#bindings-and-assignment","p":"Statements and expressions","h":"Bindings and assignment","m":"human","x":"Bindings and assignment # let introduces a name; let mut introduces a name that may be reassigned later; = on its own only ever reassigns ( LR 5.1 ). The type comes from the initialiser, or from an annotation when you want a different one. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let greeting = \"hello\" let mut count : Int < 0 .. 10 > = 0 count = 3 console . print ( \"${greeting}, count is ${count}\" ) } output hello, count is 3 There is no let without an initialiser, and assignment is a statement, not an expression, so it never chains ( LR 5.1 ). Assigning to a name that was never declared is E-NAM-001 , and assigning to an immutable binding is E-NAM-002 ; both are reported at the assignment ( LR 5.1 ): Fortis // reject: E-NAM-001, E-NAM-002 func main ( ) { let total = 1 missing = 3 // reject: E-NAM-001 total = 2 // reject: E-NAM-002 } A name may not shadow a binding that is still visible, even in an inner block: E-NAM-003 ( LR 4.5 ). Pick a new name instead; a reader then never has to work out which of two total s a line means. Fortis // reject: E-NAM-003 func main ( ) { let total = 1 { let total = 2 // reject: E-NAM-003 } } An inner { \u2026 } block on its own is a statement. Its purpose is to end loans and drop values early ( LR 5.1 , LR 7.3 ): everything declared inside it is gone at its closing brace."},{"u":"docs/guide/05-statements.html#constants","p":"Statements and expressions","h":"Constants","m":"human","x":"Constants # A module may declare constants with const . A constant's initialiser is a constant expression: literals, other constants, arithmetic over them, enum values, record literals and a few constructors such as List.of with constant arguments ( LR 5.1 ). Constants are built once, before any of your code runs, and are used like any other value. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } const limit = 3 const names = List . of ( \"ada\" , \"brian\" , \"carla\" ) func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"limit ${limit}, ${names.count()} names\" ) } output limit 3, 3 names"},{"u":"docs/guide/05-statements.html#conditionals","p":"Statements and expressions","h":"Conditionals","m":"human","x":"Conditionals # The condition of an if (and of a while ) must be a Bool . Nothing else is accepted: there is no truthiness, so an Int , an optional or a string in a condition is E-TYP-010 ( LR 5.2 ). Fortis // reject: E-TYP-010 func main ( ) { let count = 3 if count { // reject: E-TYP-010 } } if is a statement. It is also an expression when it has an else and every branch ends with give value ; the branches must agree on one type ( LR 5.2 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let count = 0 let label = if count == 0 { give \"none\" } else { give \"some\" } console . print ( label ) } output none A branch can also tell the checker something. Inside if count < 10 { \u2026 } the checker knows the bound, and in the else branch it knows the opposite ( LR 5.2 ). The chapter on proofs explains what the checker does with such facts."},{"u":"docs/guide/05-statements.html#loops","p":"Statements and expressions","h":"Loops","m":"human","x":"Loops # There are three loops: while , for \u2026 in \u2026 and loop ( LR 5.3 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { for i in 1 .. 3 { console . print ( \"inclusive ${i}\" ) } for i in 0 ..< 2 { console . print ( \"exclusive ${i}\" ) } let words = List . of ( \"red\" , \"green\" ) for word in words { console . print ( word ) } } output inclusive 1 inclusive 2 inclusive 3 exclusive 0 exclusive 1 red green a..b includes b and a..<b stops before it. Ranges exist only in for headers and in refinement types, so 1..3 is not a value you can store ( LR 5.6 ). for word in words borrows words for the whole loop and gives each element as a read loan; the list is untouched afterwards. for word in move words would consume the list instead and give each element by value ( LR 5.3 ). The loop variable is immutable: it names the current element, so assigning to it means nothing. loop runs until a break . break and continue apply to the innermost loop, or to a labelled one ( outer: for \u2026 and then break outer ). A label is a lowercase name and a colon in front of the loop, and break carries no value ( LR 5.3 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut n : Int < 0 .. 100 > = 0 loop { if n >= 3 { break } n = n + 1 } console . print ( \"stopped at ${n}\" ) outer : for row in 1 .. 3 { for column in 1 .. 3 { if column == 2 { continue outer } console . print ( \"${row},${column}\" ) } } } output stopped at 3 1,1 2,1 3,1 n = n + 1 is accepted here because the checker can prove it cannot overflow: inside the loop n is below 3, so n + 1 fits Int<0..100> . Integer arithmetic always needs such a proof, or one of the checked operations ( LR 6.4 ); Types shows both."},{"u":"docs/guide/05-statements.html#leaving-a-block-return-fail-give-and-recover","p":"Statements and expressions","h":"Leaving a block: return, fail, give and recover","m":"human","x":"Leaving a block: return , fail , give and recover # Four statements end something with a value ( LR 5.4 ): Statement Ends With return v the enclosing function (or lambda) a success value fail f the enclosing try function a failure give v the enclosing match arm block or if expression branch the branch's value recover v the enclosing catch block a replacement success value return and fail always mean the function you are reading, never a block or a loop, and inside a lambda they mean the lambda ( LR 5.4 ). A function whose return type is not Void must end every path in return , fail or a call that never returns; there is no implicit last value. Falling off the end is E-TYP-110 , reported at the function ( LR 5.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-110 pure func sign ( n : Int ) Int { // reject: E-TYP-110 if n < 0 { return - 1 } } func main ( ) { } give outside an if expression or a match arm, recover outside a catch block, and break or continue outside a loop are E-TYP-120 ( LR 5.4 ). fail outside a try function is E-ERR-001 ( LR 5.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-ERR-001 export error TooLarge { code : \"demo.too_large\" } func check ( n : Int ) { if n > 100 { fail TooLarge ( ) // reject: E-ERR-001 } } func main ( ) { check ( 5 ) } Declaring the function try func check(n: Int) makes the fail legal; callers must then handle the outcome, which is the subject of Outcomes and failures ."},{"u":"docs/guide/05-statements.html#expression-statements-and-discard","p":"Statements and expressions","h":"Expression statements and discard","m":"human","x":"Expression statements and discard # An expression used as a statement must have type Void or Never . A value you compute and then ignore is E-TYP-140 ; if ignoring it is intended, say so with discard ( LR 5.5 ). A function whose result most callers do not need says so once, on its declaration, with @ignorable , and then no caller writes discard ( @ignorable , LR 5.5 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-140 pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * 2 func main ( ) { double ( 4 ) // reject: E-TYP-140 } Fortis // accept pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * 2 func main ( ) { discard double ( 4 ) } discard is not a way out of an obligation: an Outcome , a Task or any other linear value cannot be discarded ( E-OBL-001 , LR 5.5 ). Those must be consumed, as later chapters show. The single exception is a future that was never started and holds nothing linear and no exclusive loan: discarding it is allowed, and the runtime records that it never ran ( LR 5.5 , LR 12.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/05-statements.html#operators","p":"Statements and expressions","h":"Operators","m":"human","x":"Operators # All binary operators are left-associative unless the table says otherwise; higher rows bind tighter ( LR 5.6 ). Level Operators Notes 1 a.b a.b(\u2026) a[i] a(\u2026) a?.b member access, calls, indexing, optional chaining 2 -a !a await a and the markers copy alias editable move unary, right-associative 4 * / % integer operands need proof 5 + - 6 << >> the shift amount must be proved in range 7 < <= > >= comparisons do not chain 8 == != 9, 10, 11 & , ^ , | bitwise and, xor, or 12, 13 && , || short-circuit 14 a ?? b absent-value fallback, right-associative 15 a or fail , a or recover b , a catch e { \u2026 } outcome handling, right-associative 16 a..b a..<b ranges, in for headers and refinements only Three families never mix without parentheses: the outcome operators, ?? , and the Boolean short-circuit operators ( LR 5.6 ). a || b ?? c could mean two different things, so it is rejected ( E-SYN-004 ), and you write the parentheses that say which one you mean: Fortis // reject: E-SYN-004 func main ( ) { let a = false let b : Bool ? = null let c = true let d = a || b ?? c // reject: E-SYN-004 } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let a = false let b : Bool ? = null let c = true let d = a || ( b ?? c ) console . print ( \"${d}\" ) } output true or fail ends an expression, so f() or fail ?? 0 is not a sentence of the grammar at all ( E-SYN-000 ), and await f() or fail means (await f()) or fail ( LR 5.6 ). Because or binds loosest, the idiom is to put the failure decision on its own line: let v = f() or fail , then use v ( LR 1.1 ). There are no user-defined operators and no operator overloading; == and the comparisons work through the built-in interfaces Equatable and Comparable ( LR 6.10 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/05-statements.html#evaluation-order","p":"Statements and expressions","h":"Evaluation order","m":"human","x":"Evaluation order # Operands and arguments are evaluated left to right, exactly once, and the receiver of a method call before its arguments ( LR 5.7 ). && and || evaluate their right operand only when they need it. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func note ( console : Console , label : StringView , value : Int ) Int { console . print ( \"evaluating ${label}\" ) return value } func pair ( a : Int , b : Int ) Int => a func main ( console : Console ) { let first = pair ( note ( console , \"left\" , 1 ) , note ( console , \"right\" , 2 ) ) console . print ( \"got ${first}\" ) } output evaluating left evaluating right got 1 A call runs in four steps ( LR 5.7 ). First every argument is evaluated, and an argument that names a place reserves the loan or move it needs without taking it yet. Then all reservations become active at once. Then the callee runs. Finally the loans taken for the call end when it returns, unless the result carries them. The second step is why some calls that look reasonable are rejected. Here the first argument reserves an exclusive loan of buffer , and the second argument's evaluation reads buffer while that reservation is pending; the read finishes first, but then the move in the second call breaks the reservation ( E-OWN-012 , LR 5.7 ): Fortis // reject: E-OWN-012 class Buffer { export bytes : Int } func update ( target : editable Buffer , size : Int ) { target . bytes = size } func consume ( b : owned Buffer ) Int => b . bytes func main ( ) { let mut buffer = Buffer ( bytes : 0 ) update ( editable buffer , consume ( move buffer ) ) // reject: E-OWN-012 } Evaluate the argument first, on its own line, and pass the result: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Buffer { export bytes : Int } func update ( target : editable Buffer , size : Int ) { target . bytes = size } func size ( b : Buffer ) Int => 7 func main ( console : Console ) { let mut buffer = Buffer ( bytes : 0 ) let n = size ( buffer ) update ( editable buffer , n ) console . print ( \"${buffer.bytes}\" ) } output 7 Temporaries created while evaluating a statement are dropped at the end of that statement, in reverse order of creation ( LR 5.7 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/05-statements.html#calls-named-arguments-and-defaults","p":"Statements and expressions","h":"Calls, named arguments and defaults","m":"human","x":"Calls, named arguments and defaults # Arguments are positional or named ( name: value ). Positional arguments come first, in declaration order; named ones follow in any order, and each parameter is bound once ( LR 5.8 ). When the value is a local of the parameter's own name, the name is written once: Insufficient(sku, held: item.quantity, wanted) means sku: sku and wanted: wanted . A bare name is read that way after a named argument, and anywhere in a class constructor, whose arguments are all named; before the first named argument of any other call it is positional, as before. A local that is moved keeps its label, name: move name ( LR 5.8 ). A parameter may have a default: a pure, non-allocating constant expression, evaluated afresh at every call that leaves the argument out ( LR 5.8 ). That is stricter than a module constant, whose initialiser may build a list with List.of , because a default runs at every such call ( LR 5.1 , LR 5.8 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func page ( items : Int < 0 .. 1000 > , size : Int < 1 .. 100 > = 20 ) Int < 0 .. 1000 > { if items < size { return items } return size } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${page(50)} ${page(50, size: 10)} ${page(size: 5, items: 3)}\" ) } output 20 10 3 A positional argument after a named one, a name that is not a parameter, a parameter bound twice and too many arguments are all E-TYP-130 ( LR 5.8 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-130 pure func area ( width : Int < 0 .. 100 > , height : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int => width * height func main ( ) { discard area ( width : 2 , depth : 3 ) // reject: E-TYP-130 } A parameter with no default that no argument binds is E-TYP-050 , the code that also reports a constructor call missing a field ( LR 5.8 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-050 pure func area ( width : Int < 0 .. 100 > , height : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int => width * height func main ( ) { discard area ( width : 2 ) // reject: E-TYP-050 } Parameter names are part of a function's public signature: renaming an exported parameter breaks its callers ( LR 5.8 ). User functions have no variadic parameters; a function that takes many values takes a ListView[T] , and the caller passes List.of(\u2026) or an existing list ( LR 5.8 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/05-statements.html#results-callers-may-drop-ignorable","p":"Statements and expressions","h":"Results callers may drop: @ignorable","m":"human","x":"Results callers may drop: @ignorable # Some functions return something most callers do not need, such as whether an insert found the item new. Such a function says so once, on its declaration, with @ignorable , and a call of it used as a statement drops the result without discard ( LR 5.5 , LR 11.8 ). Older code writes discard at every such call; it is still accepted, and fortis fmt leaves it, since only the function's author can declare a result ignorable: Fortis // accept @ ignorable func remember ( seen : editable Set [ Int ] , n : Int ) Bool { return seen . add ( n ) } test \"remembering twice keeps one\" { let mut seen : Set [ Int ] = Set . default ( ) remember ( editable seen , 3 ) remember ( editable seen , 3 ) expect seen . count ( ) == 1 } Every other result still needs its discard (see Expression statements and discard ), and a function whose result is linear cannot be @ignorable ( E-TYP-150 , LR 5.5 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-proofs.html#proving-arithmetic-and-indexing","p":"Proving arithmetic and indexing","h":"Proving arithmetic and indexing","m":"human","x":"Proving arithmetic and indexing # Human mode. Agent mode: Proofs, by rule . Source of truth: LR 6.3 , LR 6.4 and LR 6.7 . Preview: predicate refinements ( LR 6.3 ) arrive in v0.1. The interval rules for arithmetic and indexing ( LR 6.4 , 6.7 ) are in v0.0.1. An integer operator in Fortis never overflows, never divides by zero and never shifts out of range, and an index never falls outside its list. The checker guarantees this before the program runs, with a small, fixed set of rules about the range of values each integer can hold. This chapter explains those rules: where the checker learns a range, how conditions narrow it, what makes it forget, and what to write when it cannot prove what you need. There is no solver behind them; what the rules below do not prove, the checker does not prove ( LR 6.3 , LANG-06-02 ). Language 1.0 has exactly these rules. A later edition may let the checker prove more (spike SP-18, register X-146), but it will never make a rejected program's meaning change: it can only accept more programs."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-proofs.html#why-integer-operators-need-a-proof","p":"Proving arithmetic and indexing","h":"Why integer operators need a proof","m":"human","x":"Why integer operators need a proof # The operators + - * / % << >> and unary - on integers are proof-required : the checker accepts one only when it can show that overflow, division by zero, a shift out of range, and MIN / -1 cannot happen. Otherwise the operation is rejected with E-REF-002 , and the record names the operands' ranges and the checked operation to use instead ( LR 6.4 ). Here a and b can be any Int , so their sum can overflow, and the checker says so: Fortis // reject: E-REF-002 pure func total ( a : Int , b : Int ) Int => a + b // reject: E-REF-002 func main ( ) { } output example.ft:2:42: error E-REF-002 [LANG-06-02] (Refinement unproved, unproved): `+` may overflow Int: operands [-9223372036854775808, 9223372036854775807] and [-9223372036854775808, 9223372036854775807] give [-18446744073709551616, 18446744073709551614]; use Int.add, or write the operator as the checked operation: `a + b or fail` (or `or recover`, `catch`) proved_interval: \"[-18446744073709551616, 18446744073709551614]\" required_range: \"[-9223372036854775808, 9223372036854775807]\" widening_point: \"\" The status unproved matters: the checker does not claim the program is wrong, only that it could not prove it right. A record whose status is violated is different: the checker proved the rule broken, as for a literal outside the target's range or a change to a length-refined collection, and the program must change. For an unproved operator there are two ways forward. Tell the checker the ranges you mean, with refined types, and the operator is proved: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func total ( a : Int < 0 .. 1000000 > , b : Int < 0 .. 1000000 > ) Int => a + b func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${total(400000, 250000)}\" ) } output 650000 Or, when the values really can be anything, handle the overflow. An operator the checker cannot prove, written as the left operand of or fail , or recover or catch , is the checked operation: it needs no proof, and it fails with numeric.overflow instead of overflowing ( LR 6.4 ): Fortis // accept (exit status 1) import fortis . host { Console } try func total ( a : Int , b : Int ) Int { let sum = a + b or fail return sum } try func main ( console : Console ) { let small = total ( 1 , 2 ) or fail console . print ( \"${small}\" ) let huge = total ( 9223372036854775807 , 1 ) or fail console . print ( \"${huge}\" ) } output 3 The second call fails, so main ends with one failure record on standard error and exit status 1. Where arithmetic on Int is the left operand of or fail , or recover or catch , every operator in it that the checker cannot prove is checked, and one handler covers the whole expression: value = value * 10 + digit or fail fails at whichever operation overflows first ( LR 6.4 ). The checked operations are also named functions, Int.add , Int.sub , Int.mul , Int.div , Int.rem and Int.shiftLeft , each returning an outcome; older code calls them, Int.add(a, b) or fail , and fortis fmt rewrites such a call into the operator. Without a handler an unproved operator is still E-REF-002 , and so is one inside a call's argument or under a comparison, where the handler would not cover it. An operator the checker proves stays a plain value, and or fail on arithmetic that cannot fail is an error. The wrapping ( Int.wrappingAdd ) and saturating ( Int.saturatingAdd ) families are the other choices, for code that wants two's-complement wrapping or clamping on purpose ( LR 6.4 ). Integer behaviour never depends on the build mode: there is no \"overflow check in debug only\" ( LR 6.4 ). The result of a proved operation has a range too: the base type refined to the computed interval ( LR 6.4 ). That is why total(a, b) above could be returned as Int , and why it could also be returned as Int<0..2000000> ."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-proofs.html#where-the-checker-learns-a-range","p":"Proving arithmetic and indexing","h":"Where the checker learns a range","m":"human","x":"Where the checker learns a range # For every integer place in a function, the checker tracks an interval, a lowest and a highest value ( LR 6.3 ): a literal has exactly its value; a parameter or a field has the range of its declared type: Int<1..12> is [1, 12], plain Int is the whole 64-bit range; a call's result has the range of the callee's declared return type ( LR 6.3 ); the success value of a checked operation , n + 1 or fail (or Int.add(n, 1) or fail ), has the range of the operation on its operands, because the operation fails instead of overflowing ( LR 6.3 , LR 6.4 , X-93); + , - and * combine ranges by interval arithmetic, and / and % give the range of the quotient or remainder ( LR 6.3 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func percent ( part : Int < 0 .. 100 > , whole : Int < 1 .. 100 > ) Int < 0 .. 10000 > => part * 100 / whole try func following ( n : Int < 0 .. > ) Int < 1 .. > { let next = n + 1 or fail // the success value is at least 1 return next } try func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${percent(3, 4)}%\" ) let later = following ( 69 ) or fail console . print ( \"${later}\" ) } output 75% 70 part * 100 is in [0, 10000] and whole is at least 1, so the division is safe and its quotient is in [0, 10000], which fits the return type. n + 1 could overflow when n is the largest Int , so it is checked; when it succeeds its value is at least 1, so next can be returned as Int<1..> with no further check ( LR 6.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-proofs.html#narrowing-what-a-condition-tells-the-checker","p":"Proving arithmetic and indexing","h":"Narrowing: what a condition tells the checker","m":"human","x":"Narrowing: what a condition tells the checker # A comparison in an if , while or match condition narrows the range of the place it compares, inside the branch it guards ( LR 6.3 ). The forms are p < c , p <= c , p > c , p >= c , p == c and p != c , where p is a tracked place and c a literal, a constant or another tracked place (which is narrowed too). && narrows by both comparisons. The else branch learns the opposite of a single comparison. And when a branch ends by leaving ( return , fail , break , continue , or a call that never returns), the rest of the block learns the opposite as well, which is what makes a guard clause work ( LR 6.3 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func clamp ( value : Int , low : Int < 0 .. 100 > , high : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int < 0 .. 100 > { if value < low { return low } if value > high { return high } return value // here value is in [low, high], so within [0, 100] } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${clamp(-5, 0, 10)} ${clamp(7, 0, 10)} ${clamp(99, 0, 10)}\" ) } output 0 7 10 After if value < low { return low } , the checker knows value >= low for the rest of the function, and after the second guard it knows value <= high ( LR 6.3 ). Two forms do not narrow: || , and a function call such as isSmall(value) , whatever the function does ( LR 6.3 ). Write the comparison itself in the condition."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-proofs.html#where-paths-meet-and-loops","p":"Proving arithmetic and indexing","h":"Where paths meet, and loops","m":"human","x":"Where paths meet, and loops # Where two paths meet, the range is the hull of the two: one path with [0, 5] and another with [10, 20] give [0, 20] ( LR 6.3 ). A loop is where most surprises come from. At the top of a loop, every place the loop body assigns is widened to the full range of its declared type, because the checker does not count iterations ( LR 6.3 ). The one exception is the index of a ranged for , which has the range of its range. So a counter keeps a useful range only if its type says so: declare it with a refinement, let mut i: Int<0..1000> = 0 ( LR 6.3 , LR 5.3 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func countdown ( start : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int { let mut n : Int < 0 .. 100 > = start let mut steps : Int < 0 .. 100 > = 0 while n > 0 { n = n - 1 // n > 0 here, so n - 1 is in [0, 99] if steps < 100 { steps = steps + 1 // steps < 100 here, so steps + 1 is in [1, 100] } } return steps } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${countdown(42)}\" ) } output 42 Inside the loop, n > 0 narrows n to [1, 100], so n - 1 fits Int<0..100> . steps is widened to [0, 100] at the top of every iteration, its declared type; the if steps < 100 then narrows it so that steps + 1 fits. Here is what happens without such a condition. total is widened to its declared range at the loop's top, so adding the index can exceed it, and the assignment is rejected with E-REF-001 ( LR 6.3 ): Fortis // reject: E-REF-001 pure func sumTo ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int < 0 .. 500500 > { let mut total : Int < 0 .. 500500 > = 0 for i in 0 .. n { total = total + i // reject: E-REF-001 } return total } func main ( ) { } The repair accumulates into a plain Int with the checked operation, and converts once at the end with validate , which fails if the value does not fit ( LR 6.3 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure try func sumTo ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int < 0 .. 500500 > { let mut total : Int = 0 for i in 0 .. n { total = total + i or fail } return Int < 0 .. 500500 > . validate ( total ) or fail } try func main ( console : Console ) { let s = sumTo ( 1000 ) or fail console . print ( \"${s}\" ) } output 500500"},{"u":"docs/guide/06-proofs.html#what-makes-the-checker-forget","p":"Proving arithmetic and indexing","h":"What makes the checker forget","m":"human","x":"What makes the checker forget # A fact about a place is discarded when something else may have changed it: when the place, a prefix of it or a field of it is passed as an editable argument, is the receiver of an editable self method (written with or without the marker), is loaned exclusively, or is reachable from an editable capture of a closure that is called ( LR 6.3 , LR 7.5 ). The place then has the range of its declared type again. Assigning to the place replaces its facts with the range of the new value. Inside a loop, a place invalidated anywhere in the body is widened at the top, like an assigned place ( LR 6.3 ). Fortis // reject: E-REF-001 class Counter { export n : Int < 0 .. 10 > } func reset ( c : editable Counter ) { c . n = 0 } pure func bump ( c : editable Counter ) { if c . n < 10 { reset ( editable c ) c . n = c . n + 1 // reject: E-REF-001 } } func main ( ) { } c.n < 10 would have made c.n + 1 fit, but reset(editable c) may have changed c.n , so after it c.n is back to its declared [0, 10], and c.n + 1 is [1, 11], which Int<0..10> cannot hold. Test again after the call, or reorder the code so that the test is the last thing before the addition."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-proofs.html#indexing","p":"Proving arithmetic and indexing","h":"Indexing","m":"human","x":"Indexing # Indexing is proof-required too: list[i] is accepted only when i is known to be at least 0 and below the list's length ( LR 6.7 ). The checker knows a length in three situations ( LR 6.3 , LR 6.7 ): an Array[T, N] has length N ; a list whose type is refined by its length, List[T]<length: 0..100> ; after a comparison i < p.count() that still holds, and throughout the body of for i in a..<p.count() when the body neither assigns p nor loans it exclusively ( LR 6.3 , X-93). These length facts give only the upper bound; the lower bound comes from i 's type (a range starting at 0) or a separate i >= 0 test. i <= p.count() is not enough, because i may equal the length ( LR 6.3 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func positives ( xs : List [ Int ] ) Int { let mut found : Int < 0 .. 1000000 > = 0 for i in 0 ..< xs . count ( ) { if xs [ i ] > 0 && found < 1000000 { found = found + 1 } } return found } pure func itemOr ( xs : List [ Int ] , i : Int , fallback : Int ) Int { if i >= 0 && i < xs . count ( ) { return xs [ i ] } return fallback } func main ( console : Console ) { let xs = List . of ( 3 , - 1 , 4 , - 1 , 5 ) console . print ( \"${positives(xs)} positive; xs[2] = ${itemOr(xs, 2, 0)}; xs[9] = ${itemOr(xs, 9, 0)}\" ) } output 3 positive; xs[2] = 4; xs[9] = 0 When no such fact is at hand, the index is rejected with E-REF-002 , and the record suggests the test or the two operations that need no proof ( LR 6.7 ): Fortis // reject: E-REF-002 pure func item ( xs : List [ Int ] , i : Int ) Int => xs [ i ] // reject: E-REF-002 func main ( ) { } xs.get(i) returns the element as an optional ( null outside the list) and xs.at(i) returns an outcome that fails with collection.index ; neither needs a proof ( LR 6.7 ). A collection refined by its length is a property of the value, so such a place cannot be loaned editable or changed, by items.push(1) any more than by editable items.push(1) : that is E-REF-003 ( LR 6.3 , LR 7.5 ). Change an unrefined collection, then validate it into the refined type."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-proofs.html#the-remainder","p":"Proving arithmetic and indexing","h":"The remainder","m":"human","x":"The remainder # For a % d , where d 's range excludes zero and m is the largest absolute value d can have, the result is in [0, m-1] when a is never negative, in [-(m-1), 0] when a is never positive, and in [-(m-1), m-1] otherwise ( LR 6.3 ). That is what makes the last digit of a non-negative number provably a digit: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func lastDigit ( n : Int < 0 .. > ) Int < 0 .. 9 > => n % 10 func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${lastDigit(1234)}\" ) } output 4"},{"u":"docs/guide/06-proofs.html#when-the-value-is-outside-the-targets-range","p":"Proving arithmetic and indexing","h":"When the value is outside the target's range","m":"human","x":"When the value is outside the target's range # Assigning or returning a value whose range lies partly outside the target's refinement is E-REF-001 ( LR 6.3 ), as in sumTo and bump above. The fix is a wider declared type, or an explicit validate , which is the point where a value from the outside world, or from arithmetic the checker cannot follow, enters a refined type ( LR 6.3 , LANG-06-03 ). The rules in this chapter are the whole of what the checker proves. When they cannot reach a bound, the checked operations and validate always can, and they mark the line where a reader should look ( LR 6.3 , LR 6.4 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#types","p":"Types","h":"Types","m":"human","x":"Types # Human mode. Agent mode: Types, by rule . Source of truth: LR 6 . Preview: refinements ( LR 6.3 ), newtypes ( LR 6.9 ) and generics ( LR 6.10 ) arrive in v0.1. Normalisation, case mapping and the collections beyond List , Map and Set ( LR 6.6 , LR 6.7 ) arrive in v0.4. derive Default ( LR 6.10 ) is in v0.0.2. Fortis is nominally typed: two types are the same only when they are the same declaration, whatever their representation ( LR 6.1 ). There is no top type, no Any , no implicit conversion between distinct types and no way to change a type at run time. This chapter walks through the types the core gives you, how you narrow them with refinements, and the few interfaces that the operators and string interpolation rely on."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#nominal-types","p":"Types","h":"Nominal types","m":"human","x":"Nominal types # Every type is one of: a built-in type, a type newtype, a class, an enum, an interface used as a dynamic type, a function type, a tuple or anonymous record, a refinement of a built-in type, a generic instantiation of any of these, or a loan or view type ( LR 6.1 ). \"Nominal\" means the name is what counts. Two newtypes over the same representation never mix: Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 type CustomerId = Int < 1 .. > type OrderId = Int < 1 .. > pure func describe ( customer : CustomerId ) Int => 1 pure func describeOrder ( order : OrderId ) Int { return describe ( order ) // reject: E-TYP-040 } func main ( ) { } OrderId and CustomerId are both positive integers underneath, but a function that takes a customer cannot be handed an order ( LR 6.9 ). The checker reports the mismatch as E-TYP-040 , the code the LR uses for a value of the wrong type ( LR 10.1 ), naming the expected and the actual type."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#the-built-in-types","p":"Types","h":"The built-in types","m":"human","x":"The built-in types # The core's types, all in the implicitly imported prelude ( LR 6.2 , LR 4.3 ): Group Types Unit Void (one value), Never (no value) Boolean Bool Integers Int8 Int16 Int32 Int64 UInt8 UInt16 UInt32 UInt64 ; Int is Int64 , UInt is UInt64 , Byte is UInt8 Floating point Float32 , Float (64-bit) Exact Decimal , BigInt Text String , StringView , Bytes , BytesView Time Duration , Instant , Timestamp Collections List[T] , Array[T, N] , ListView[T] , Map[K, V] , OrderedMap[K, V] , Set[T] , FixedList[T, N] , FixedMap[K, V, N] Compound tuples (A, B) , anonymous records { name: A, other: B } , variant maps VariantMap[E, V] Optional T? Outcome Outcome[T] , Failure , Report Concurrency Future[T] , Task[T] , TaskCompletion[T] , Shared[T] , Weak[T] , Mutex[T] , AtomicInt , AtomicBool Callable fn(Params) Ret , fn mut(Params) Ret , fn once(Params) Ret The N of Array[T, N] and FixedList[T, N] is an integer literal; functions generic over N are not in Language 1.0 ( LR 6.2 ). List.of(a, b, c) takes any number of arguments, a form reserved for the standard library: your own functions take a ListView[T] instead ( LR 6.2 , LR 5.8 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#refinements","p":"Types","h":"Refinements","m":"human","x":"Refinements # A refinement narrows a built-in integer, text, byte or collection type with a predicate in <\u2026> after the type name, and it is allowed only where a type is written ( LR 6.3 ): Form Meaning Int<0..100> 0 to 100 inclusive Int<0..<100> 0 up to but not including 100 Int<1..> , Int<..0> one-sided String<scalars: 1..120> between 1 and 120 Unicode scalars Bytes<length: 0..65536> , List[T]<length: 0..100> element count A refinement is a subtype of its base and of every refinement whose range contains it, with no conversion at all: an Int<0..50> can be used where an Int<0..100> or a plain Int is expected. The other direction never happens on its own ( LR 6.3 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let small : Int < 0 .. 50 > = 20 let wide : Int < 0 .. 100 > = small let any : Int = wide console . print ( \"${small} ${wide} ${any}\" ) } output 20 20 20 To go from a wider type to a narrower one, ask: Int<0..100>.validate(x) , or Percent.validate(x) for a named type, is a pure function that returns an outcome, a success when x is in range and a failure otherwise ( LR 6.3 ). That outcome must be handled like any other ( Outcomes and failures ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } type Percent = Int < 0 .. 100 > try func main ( console : Console ) { let fine = Percent . validate ( 75 ) or fail console . print ( \"accepted ${fine}\" ) let tooBig = Percent . validate ( 140 ) catch e { console . print ( \"rejected with ${e.code}\" ) report e recover Percent ( 0 ) } console . print ( \"fell back to ${tooBig}\" ) } output accepted 75 rejected with refinement.out_of_range fell back to 0 A literal is checked against the type it is given when the program is compiled: 140 does not fit Int<0..100> , so the line is rejected with E-REF-001 ( LR 3.5 , LR 6.3 ). Fortis // reject: E-REF-001 func main ( ) { let score : Int < 0 .. 100 > = 140 // reject: E-REF-001 } Values that are not literals are proved too. The checker follows the possible range of every integer through a function and accepts an assignment, an operator or an index only when it can show the result stays in range; the rules it uses are the subject of Proving arithmetic and indexing , and when they cannot show a bound, validate and the checked operations below are the explicit fallback ( LR 6.3 ). Two refinements are not available. Floats cannot be refined, because NaN and rounding make ranges unreliable ( E-TYP-020 , LR 6.3 ). And a collection refined by its length is a property of a value, so it may not be mutated: change an unrefined collection and validate the result ( E-REF-003 , LR 6.3 ). Fortis // reject: E-TYP-020 type Ratio = Float < 0 .. 1 > // reject: E-TYP-020 func main ( ) { }"},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#integer-arithmetic","p":"Types","h":"Integer arithmetic","m":"human","x":"Integer arithmetic # The integer operators + - * / % << >> are proof-required: each is accepted only when the checker can show it cannot overflow, divide by zero or shift out of range ( LR 6.4 ). When you cannot or do not want to give that proof, handle the failure instead: on Int , an operator the checker cannot prove, written as the left operand of or fail , or recover or catch , is the checked operation, as in big + 1 or recover -1 below ( LR 6.4 ). Int also has three families of named operations ( LR 6.4 ); the sized integer types, Int32 , UInt8 and the rest, have none yet and get theirs in v0.4: Family Example Result Checked Int.add(a, b) , Int.sub , Int.mul , Int.div , Int.rem , Int.shiftLeft ; on Int , the operator under a handler an outcome that fails with numeric.overflow , numeric.divide_by_zero or numeric.shift_range Wrapping Int.wrappingAdd(a, b) , \u2026 always a value; wraps around in two's complement Saturating Int.saturatingAdd(a, b) , \u2026 always a value; clamps at the type's bounds Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } recovers func main ( console : Console ) { let big : Int = 9223372036854775807 let checked : Int = big + 1 or recover - 1 console . print ( \"checked ${checked}\" ) console . print ( \"wrapping ${Int.wrappingAdd(big, 1)}\" ) console . print ( \"saturating ${Int.saturatingAdd(big, 1)}\" ) let q : Int = Int . div ( - 7 , 2 ) or recover 0 let r : Int = Int . rem ( - 7 , 2 ) or recover 0 console . print ( \"-7 / 2 is ${q}, remainder ${r}\" ) } output checked -1 wrapping -9223372036854775808 saturating 9223372036854775807 -7 / 2 is -3, remainder -1 Division truncates toward zero and the remainder takes the sign of the dividend; >> is arithmetic on signed types and logical on unsigned ones; a += b means a = a + b and needs the same proof. None of this depends on the build mode ( LR 6.4 ). main is declared recovers here because or recover discards failures without reporting them; Effects and contracts explains why that has to be written down."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#floating-point-and-decimal","p":"Types","h":"Floating point and Decimal","m":"human","x":"Floating point and Decimal # Float and Float32 follow IEEE 754 strictly: every operator is total (no overflow failure, no proof), the compiler never reorders or fuses operations, and every NaN is canonicalised, so + - * / and Float.sqrt give the same bits on every backend ( LR 6.5 ). A float displays as the shortest text that reads back as the same value, always with a fractional part ( LR 6.5 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${0.1 + 0.2} ${2e3} ${1.5}\" ) let root = Float . sqrt ( - 1.0 ) console . print ( \"${root} is NaN: ${Float.isNaN(root)}\" ) } output 0.30000000000000004 2000.0 1.5 NaN is NaN: true Because NaN is not equal to itself, floats do not implement Equatable , Hashable or Comparable ( LR 6.5 ). == on two floats is still allowed and means IEEE equality, but a float cannot be a map key or a field of a class that derives Equatable ( E-TYP-090 , LR 6.10 ); convert with Float.totalOrder(x) or compare bit patterns with Float.bitsEqual(a, b) when you need those ( LR 6.5 ). Fortis // reject: E-TYP-090 class Reading derive Equatable { // reject: E-TYP-090 export celsius : Float } func main ( ) { } Decimal is exact: a sign, a 96-bit coefficient and a scale of 0 to 28 digits after the point ( LR 6.5 ). It has no NaN and no infinity, and in Language 1.0 it has no operators: arithmetic goes through the checked functions Decimal.add , sub , mul , div and rem , which fail with numeric.overflow or numeric.divide_by_zero ( LR 6.5 ). A Decimal displays with exactly its scale, so 1.50d prints as 1.50 . add and sub give the larger operand scale, mul the sum of the scales and div the largest scale that fits; a result that would need more than 28 fractional digits or more than 96 coefficient bits is rounded half to even, and if its integer part still does not fit, the operation fails. Rounding to a scale of your choosing is explicit: Decimal.round(value, scale, mode) with a RoundingMode ( LR 6.5 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } try func main ( console : Console ) { let price = 1.50d let total = Decimal . add ( price , 2.25d ) or fail let product = Decimal . mul ( 1.5d , 1.25d ) or fail let rounded = Decimal . round ( 2.345d , 2 , HalfEven ) console . print ( \"${price} ${total} ${product} ${rounded}\" ) } output 1.50 3.75 1.875 2.34"},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#text-and-views","p":"Types","h":"Text and views","m":"human","x":"Text and views # String is owned, growable UTF-8 text; StringView is a borrowed, read-only view of text owned by something else; Bytes and BytesView are the byte equivalents ( LR 6.6 ). A string literal is a StringView of text stored in the program, so it can be used wherever a view is expected; where a String is expected, the literal is copied into a new String . That copy is the one implicit conversion in Fortis, and because it allocates it is refused in noalloc code. String.from(view) makes the copy explicit ( LR 6.6 ). A string with ${\u2026} holes is different: it builds a fresh String every time it runs, which a call may borrow for the statement but which a binding declared as StringView cannot keep, because there would be nothing for the view to borrow from ( E-TYP-040 , LR 3.5 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 func main ( ) { let count = 3 let label : StringView = \"count ${count}\" // reject: E-TYP-040 } There is no indexing of text by integer, because UTF-8 scalars have different widths. Instead there are separate operations whose cost you can see ( LR 6.6 ): byteCount() is constant time, scalarCount() walks the text, slice(from, to) takes scalar offsets and returns an outcome (it fails on bad bounds), and scalarAt(i) returns the scalar at an offset as an outcome. text.Builder accumulates text. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } import fortis . text try func main ( console : Console ) { let word : StringView = \"na\u00efve\" console . print ( \"${word.byteCount()} bytes, ${word.scalarCount()} scalars\" ) let start = word . slice ( 0 , 3 ) or fail let third = word . scalarAt ( 2 ) or fail let mut out = text . Builder . create ( ) out . append ( start ) out . append ( \" / \" ) out . appendScalar ( third ) console . print ( out . finish ( ) ) let copied = String . from ( word ) console . print ( \"an owned copy: ${copied}\" ) } output 6 bytes, 5 scalars na\u00ef / \u00ef an owned copy: na\u00efve \u00ef takes two bytes, which is why the word has six bytes but five scalars."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#collections","p":"Types","h":"Collections","m":"human","x":"Collections # List[T] is an owned, growable sequence; Array[T, N] has a fixed size; ListView[T] borrows a contiguous run of elements; Map[K, V] and Set[T] hash their keys, which must be Hashable and Equatable ; OrderedMap needs Comparable keys; FixedList and FixedMap have a capacity fixed in the type and never allocate ( LR 6.7 ). Reading an element by index with list[i] must be proved in bounds ( Proving arithmetic and indexing ). Without a proof, list.get(i) returns an optional and list.at(i) returns an outcome ( LR 6.7 ). Here are the list operations you will use most: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } try func main ( console : Console ) { let mut items = List . of ( 3 , 1 , 2 ) items . push ( 5 ) let last = items . pop ( ) ?? 0 let second = items . get ( 1 ) ?? 0 let third = items . at ( 2 ) or fail console . print ( \"${items.count()} items; popped ${last}, second ${second}, third ${third}\" ) items . set ( 0 , 10 ) or fail let doubled = items . map ( fn ( x : Int ) Int => Int . saturatingMul ( x , 2 ) ) let large = items . filter ( fn ( x : Int ) Bool => x > 1 ) console . print ( \"${doubled.count()} doubled, ${large.count()} above one, ${items.countWhere(fn(x: Int) Bool => x > 1)} counted\" ) for x in items . reversed ( ) { console . print ( \"${x}\" ) } } output 3 items; popped 5, second 1, third 2 3 doubled, 2 above one, 2 counted 2 1 10 map , filter and countWhere take a pure callback; map and filter allocate the new collection they return, while countWhere returns a count. filter , reversed and concat copy elements, so the element type must be copyable ( LR 6.7 ). Loops that do something for each element, such as printing, are for loops ( LR 6.7 ). Map and Set use a hash function fixed for each compiler release and iterate in first-insertion order; replacing a value keeps its key where it was. So their order is deterministic on every backend, and they may be used in pure functions ( LR 6.7 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut ages : Map [ String , Int ] = Map . default ( ) ages . set ( \"brian\" , 41 ) ages . set ( \"ada\" , 36 ) ages . set ( \"brian\" , 42 ) console . print ( \"${ages.count()} people, ada is ${ages.get(\"ada\") ?? 0}\" ) for entry in ages { console . print ( \"${entry.0}: ${entry.1}\" ) } let mut seen : Set [ Int ] = Set . default ( ) let first = seen . add ( 4 ) let again = seen . add ( 4 ) console . print ( \"${first} ${again} ${seen.contains(4)}\" ) } output 2 people, ada is 36 brian: 42 ada: 36 true false true The fixed capacity of FixedList and FixedMap means inserting past it fails with collection.full rather than allocating ( LR 6.7 ). Iterating a Map gives each entry as a (key, value) tuple, read through entry.0 and entry.1 . The LR names Map.iterate() without saying what an entry is, and the library does not declare iterate yet (D-58, X-154), so treat this as the toolchain's current behaviour. A for header binds one name, not a pattern ( LR 5.3 ); to take an entry apart, match it: match entry { (name, age) => \u2026 } ( LR 9.8 ). Today's grammar does not accept a tuple element after a marker, so copy entry.0 is refused although LR 6.9 calls t.0 a field (X-153); copy through the match's bindings instead. A list literal takes its element type from its elements: List.of(\"pear\", \"apple\") is a List[StringView] , because a string literal is a StringView ( LR 3.5 , LR 6.7 ). Annotate the binding when you want owned strings: let fruit: List[String] = List.of(\"pear\", \"apple\") ( LR 6.6 ). A refinement is a subtype of its base where a single value is used ( LR 6.3 ); whether a List[Int<0..9>] may stand for a ListView[Int] the LR does not say, and today's checkers say no (X-154), so convert with map when you need the wider element type."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#optional-values","p":"Types","h":"Optional values","m":"human","x":"Optional values # T? is either null or a present T ( LR 6.8 ). A T is accepted wherever a T? is expected; nothing goes the other way without a test. a ?? b gives a when it is present and otherwise evaluates b ; a?.field is null when a is; and match uses the patterns present(x) and null . Tests on null narrow the type for the code they guard ( LR 6.8 ): inside if n != null { \u2026 } the place n is a plain T ; after if n == null { return \u2026 } it is a T for the rest of the block; and in a != null && e , a is a T inside e . Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Address { export postcode : Int } class Person { export name : String export address : Address ? } pure func describe ( n : Int ? ) String { if n == null { return \"nothing\" } return \"the number ${n}\" } pure func postcodeOf ( p : Person ) Int => p . address ?. postcode ?? 0 func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( describe ( null ) ) console . print ( describe ( 4 ) ) let ada = Person ( name : \"Ada\" , address : Address ( postcode : 10115 ) ) let bo = Person ( name : \"Bo\" , address : null ) console . print ( \"${postcodeOf(ada)} ${postcodeOf(bo)}\" ) let maybe : Int ? = null let label = match maybe { present ( v ) => \"got ${v}\" null => \"got nothing\" } console . print ( label ) } output nothing the number 4 10115 0 got nothing An optional is never an error and never a Boolean: if maybe { \u2026 } is E-TYP-010 ( LR 6.8 ). Absence, falsehood and failure are three different things in Fortis, with three different operators: ?? , || and or ( LR 6.8 , LR 1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#tuples-records-variant-maps-and-newtypes","p":"Types","h":"Tuples, records, variant maps and newtypes","m":"human","x":"Tuples, records, variant maps and newtypes # A tuple (A, B) is read with .0 and .1 ; an anonymous record { name: A, other: B } has named fields; a VariantMap[E, V] maps every variant of an enum E to a value, written { Found: 200, NotFound: 404 } , and the checker insists on exactly one entry per variant ( LR 6.9 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } enum Lookup { Found NotFound } func main ( console : Console ) { let pair = ( 3 , \"three\" ) let person = { name : \"Ada\" , age : 36 } let statuses : VariantMap [ Lookup , Int ] = { Found : 200 , NotFound : 404 } console . print ( \"${pair.0} is ${pair.1}; ${person.name} is ${person.age}\" ) } output 3 is three; Ada is 36 Tuples, records and variant maps are local conveniences: they are structural and cannot appear in an exported signature, where a named class says more ( E-TYP-030 , LR 6.9 ). The two exceptions are aspect parameters and the signatures of the fortis package itself, such as join , which returns a tuple ( LR 6.9 ). A type may be put in parentheses for grouping, which is how an optional function is written: (fn(Int) Bool)? ( LR 6.9 ). Fortis // reject: E-TYP-030 export pure func bounds ( ) ( Int , Int ) => ( 1 , 2 ) // reject: E-TYP-030 func main ( ) { } type Name = Representation declares a newtype, a new nominal type with a private representation ( LR 6.9 ). It is the only meaning type has: there are no transparent aliases in Language 1.0. A newtype value is made only by Name.validate(raw) or by a literal the checker proves in range, written Name(42) . A newtype over a trivial representation implements Equatable , Hashable , Comparable and Displayable exactly when its representation does, displaying the underlying value ( LR 6.9 ). So a newtype over Int can be interpolated and used as a map key with no further declaration, while one over Float displays but cannot be a map key, because Float is not Hashable ( LR 6.5 ). The representation is private ( LR 6.9 ): there is no accessor that turns a LineNo back into an Int , and arithmetic on a newtype is not defined. Keep the raw value where you compute, and make the newtype with validate (or a proved literal) where the value crosses into the part of the program that should not mix it up with other numbers ( LR 6.9 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#generics","p":"Types","h":"Generics","m":"human","x":"Generics # Functions, classes, enums and interfaces take type parameters in square brackets, constrained with where after the return type ( LR 6.10 ). A generic body is checked once, against its bounds, so a call at any type that satisfies them cannot fail to compile. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func occurrences [ T ] ( items : List [ T ] , wanted : T ) Int where T implements Equatable { let mut n = 0 for item in items { if item == wanted { n = Int . saturatingAdd ( n , 1 ) } } return n } pure func larger [ T ] ( a : T , b : T ) T where T implements Comparable , T implements Copyable { if a > b { return copy a } return copy b } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${occurrences(List.of(1, 2, 1), 1)} ${occurrences(List.of(\"a\", \"b\"), \"c\")}\" ) let pear : String = \"pear\" let apple : String = \"apple\" console . print ( \"${larger(3, 8)} ${larger(pear, apple)}\" ) } output 2 0 8 pear Type arguments are never written at a call: they are inferred from the arguments, or from the type the call's result is expected to have, such as an annotated let , a return or a parameter ( LR 6.10 ). When neither fixes them, the call is E-TYP-160 , and the repair is to annotate the binding: Fortis // reject: E-TYP-160 func main ( ) { let names = List . default ( ) // reject: E-TYP-160 } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut names : List [ String ] = List . default ( ) names . push ( \"ada\" ) console . print ( \"${names.count()}\" ) } output 1 A generic function is compiled once per type it is used at. Higher-kinded types, associated types and overloading are not in Language 1.0 ( LR 6.10 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#the-built-in-interfaces-and-derive","p":"Types","h":"The built-in interfaces and derive","m":"human","x":"The built-in interfaces and derive # The operators and interpolation rest on a small set of interfaces in the prelude ( LR 6.10 ): Interface What it gives Notes Equatable == and != Hashable use as a Map key or Set element requires Equatable Comparable < <= > >= requires Equatable Displayable ${value} in a string Iterable , Iterator , Consumable , Consumer for loops LR 5.3 Copyable copy structural: no declaration needed Shareable crossing to another thread structural, LR 12.7 Abandonable being live when a task is cancelled LR 12.4 Default Type.default() Encodable , Decodable , Codec the format-free value tree Codec is both Authority marks capabilities LR 11.6 From[S] Type.from(source) and source.to() There is no operator overloading: Money + Money is spelled Money.add(a, b) , and the comparison operators dispatch only to these interfaces ( LR 6.10 ). derive on a class or enum generates Equatable , Hashable , Comparable , Displayable , Encodable , Decodable or Codec field by field, and is accepted only when every field implements the interface ( E-TYP-090 otherwise, LR 6.10 ). A derived Comparable orders by fields, and variants, in declaration order. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Point derive Equatable , Displayable { export x : Int export y : Int } enum Size derive Equatable , Comparable , Displayable { Small Medium Large } func main ( console : Console ) { let p = Point ( x : 1 , y : 2 ) console . print ( \"${p}, equal to itself: ${p == Point(x: 1, y: 2)}\" ) console . print ( \"${Size.Medium}; small before large: ${Size.Small < Large}\" ) } output Point(x: 1, y: 2), equal to itself: true Medium; small before large: true derive Default builds the value from each field's declared initialiser, and from the field type's own default only for fields without one ( LR 6.10 ). It gives the class Name.default() , with no arguments, so a class whose fields all start empty needs no hand-written constructor, and the header says so; a field whose type has no default, such as a class that does not derive it or Int<1..10> , makes the derivation an error ( E-TYP-090 ). derive From[S] copies every field of the target from the field of the same name in S , which must exist and be copyable; then Summary.from(customer) builds the value, and customer.to() does the same wherever the target type is already known ( LR 6.10 ): Fortis // accept class Customer { export name : String export age : Int export email : String } class Summary derive From [ Customer ] { export name : String export age : Int } test \"a summary keeps the fields it names\" { let ada = Customer ( name : \"Ada\" , age : 36 , email : \"ada@example.org\" ) let summary : Summary = ada . to ( ) expect summary . name == \"Ada\" expect summary . age == 36 }"},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#encoding-values-the-canonical-codec","p":"Types","h":"Encoding values: the canonical codec","m":"human","x":"Encoding values: the canonical codec # A type that derives Codec (so Encodable and Decodable ) gets two canonical encodings with no format package at all ( LR 6.10 ): toText() and fromText(input) read and write JSON with keys in declaration order and no extra whitespace, and toBytes() and fromBytes(input) a deterministic binary form, identical on every backend. Decoding follows the expected type and runs every refinement and constructor check. It fails with codec.invalid for malformed input, a value that does not fit its type, a missing field that has neither an initialiser nor an optional type, or trailing data, and with codec.unknown_field for a text key the type does not declare; a value outside a refinement fails with that refinement's own failure, exactly as validate would ( LR 6.10 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Customer derive Codec , Equatable { export name : String export age : Int < 0 .. 150 > } try func main ( console : Console ) { let ada = Customer ( name : \"Ada\" , age : 36 ) let json = ada . toText ( ) console . print ( json ) let back = Customer . fromText ( json ) or fail console . print ( \"round trip equal: ${back == ada}\" ) let fromBinary = Customer . fromBytes ( ada . toBytes ( ) ) or fail console . print ( \"binary: ${fromBinary.name}\" ) let broken = Customer . fromText ( \"{\\\"name\\\": \\\"Bo\\\"}\" ) catch e { console . print ( \"decoding failed: ${e.code}\" ) report e recover Customer ( name : \"nobody\" , age : 0 ) } console . print ( broken . name ) } output {\"name\":\"Ada\",\"age\":36} round trip equal: true binary: Ada decoding failed: codec.invalid nobody Deriving these interfaces needs every field to be exported and encodable in turn; secrets, capabilities, loans, closures, futures and tasks implement none of them, so they can never end up in an encoded value by accident ( LR 6.10 ). ${value} is a human rendering and is never the encoded form."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#trivial-and-copyable-types","p":"Types","h":"Trivial and copyable types","m":"human","x":"Trivial and copyable types # A trivial type is duplicated by plain assignment, so it never needs an ownership marker ( LR 6.11 ). The set is closed: Void , Never , Bool , the integers, floats, Decimal , Scalar , Duration , Instant , Timestamp ; newtypes over those; enums whose payloads are all trivial; optionals, tuples and records of trivial types; and a class you declare trivial , which is rejected if any field is not trivial ( E-OWN-030 , LR 6.11 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } trivial class Pixel derive Equatable { export x : Int export y : Int } func main ( console : Console ) { let p = Pixel ( x : 1 , y : 2 ) let q = p console . print ( \"${q == p}\" ) } output true Fortis // reject: E-OWN-030 trivial class Tag { // reject: E-OWN-030 export name : String } func main ( ) { } Everything else is ownership-bearing and needs copy , alias or move when an existing value is assigned, returned or handed over; a class is ownership-bearing unless declared trivial , so adding a field never changes what an assignment elsewhere means ( LR 6.11 ). Ownership covers those markers. A copyable type supports copy : trivial types, String , Bytes , BigInt , collections of copyable elements, and classes, enums, tuples and records whose fields are all copyable ( LR 6.11 ). Shared handles, mutexes, atomics, loans, resources, futures, tasks, outcomes, failures and closures are not, and copy of one is E-OWN-002 ( LR 7.5 ). A copy is field by field, cannot fail, and allocates when the value owns heap memory, so it is refused inside noalloc code ( LR 6.11 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#linear-types","p":"Types","h":"Linear types","m":"human","x":"Linear types # A linear value must be used up exactly once on every path ( LR 6.12 ). The linear types are Outcome[T] , Failure , Future[T] , Task[T] , the zero-sized witness Must , closures declared fn once that capture one, and anything that can contain one: an optional, class, enum, tuple, record or collection over a linear type. Dropping one at the end of a block, overwriting it or discard ing it is E-OBL-001 ( LR 6.12 ). The one exception is a future that was never polled: discard of it is allowed when its captured environment holds no linear value and no exclusive loan ( LR 12.3 , Cleanup, async and concurrency ): Fortis // reject: E-OBL-001 func main ( ) { let token = Must . create ( ) // reject: E-OBL-001 } Fortis // accept func main ( ) { let token = Must . create ( ) Must . discharge ( move token ) } Must exists so that a library can make a handle linear: a transaction that holds a private Must field cannot be forgotten, only committed or rolled back ( LR 6.12 ). Most linear values you meet are outcomes, and Outcomes and failures shows every way to consume them. A type parameter is not linear unless it is declared T: linear . That matters only where a generic function holds a T by value; a function that only borrows its T can be used with linear types freely, because the obligation stays with the caller ( LR 6.12 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/06-types.html#looking-up-a-text-key","p":"Types","h":"Looking up a text key","m":"human","x":"Looking up a text key # Map.get only reads its key, so a Map[String, V] takes the key as a StringView : a view of some text, a literal or a String all look the entry up, and nothing is copied to make a key that is thrown away ( LR 6.7 , X-345). set still takes an owned String , because the map keeps that one: Fortis // accept pure func countOf ( counts : Map [ String , Int ] , word : StringView ) Int { return counts . get ( word ) ?? 0 } test \"a view finds the entry\" { let mut counts : Map [ String , Int ] = Map . default ( ) counts . set ( \"cat\" , 2 ) let word : StringView = \"cat\" expect countOf ( counts , word ) == 2 expect countOf ( counts , \"dog\" ) == 0 }"},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#ownership","p":"Ownership","h":"Ownership","m":"human","x":"Ownership # Human mode. Agent mode: Ownership, by rule . Source of truth: LR 7 . Every value in Fortis has exactly one owner, and every other use of it is either a loan or a transfer that the line says out loud. Assigning an existing ownership-bearing value, or passing it where the callee takes ownership, writes copy , alias or move ; handing a value to a function that changes it writes editable , and a value that changes is declared let mut ( LR 1 , LR 7.5 ). Passing a value to an ordinary parameter needs no marker, because it is a read loan for the call, and a trivial value, such as an integer, never needs one ( LR 7.5 , LR 6.11 ). There are no hidden clones and no hidden reference counts, so a reader can answer \"who owns this, and can this call change it?\" from the line and the declarations it names. The rules are close to Rust's borrow checking, made lexical and written into the source; this chapter introduces them one at a time."},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#the-idea-in-one-paragraph","p":"Ownership","h":"The idea in one paragraph","m":"human","x":"The idea in one paragraph # The checker follows, for every binding, its type, whether it is mutable, whether it still owns its value or has had it moved out, and which loans of it are active. Every expression is checked against that environment and records the loans it creates, the effects it has and the obligations it leaves behind; written formally, \u0393 \u22a2 e : \u03c4 \u25b7 L, E, O ( LR 7.1 ). It is a finite dataflow analysis over the function's control flow, with no solver, so its answers are predictable: each rejection names a place, a conflicting loan or move, and both positions."},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#places-and-owners","p":"Ownership","h":"Places and owners","m":"human","x":"Places and owners # A place is a path to storage: a binding ( invoice ), a field path from a binding or from self ( invoice.lines , self.status ), or an Array element at a literal index ( grid[3] ). Nothing else is a place: a call result, a ?? b , a literal or list[i] with a variable i are values ( LR 7.2 ). The ownership markers copy , alias , move and editable apply only to places. A let binding owns the value it was initialised with until the value is moved out, and drops it at the end of its block if it still owns it. A parameter is a binding that can never be reassigned: an owned parameter owns its argument, a plain or editable one holds a loan ( LR 7.2 ). There are no partial moves: you cannot move one field out of a value and keep the rest ( E-OWN-040 , LR 7.2 ). Move the whole value, or take it apart with a consuming pattern ( Classes ). Fortis // reject: E-OWN-040 class Order { export id : Int export lines : List [ String ] } func keep ( lines : owned List [ String ] ) Int => lines . count ( ) func main ( ) { let order = Order ( id : 1 , lines : List . of ( \"tea\" ) ) discard keep ( move order . lines ) // reject: E-OWN-040 }"},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#moves","p":"Ownership","h":"Moves","m":"human","x":"Moves # move p transfers ownership out of p . Afterwards p is uninitialised on that path: reading it, loaning it, moving it again or dropping it is E-OWN-020 , use after move ( LR 7.4 ). Where two paths join, a place counts as initialised only if it is initialised on both. A let mut binding can be given a new value after a move. Fortis // reject: E-OWN-020 func consume ( items : owned List [ Int ] ) Int => items . count ( ) func main ( ) { let items = List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) discard consume ( move items ) discard items . count ( ) // reject: E-OWN-020 } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func consume ( items : owned List [ Int ] ) Int => items . count ( ) func main ( console : Console ) { let mut items = List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) let n = consume ( move items ) items = List . of ( 4 ) console . print ( \"consumed ${n}, now ${items.count()}\" ) } output consumed 3, now 1"},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#markers-and-where-they-are-needed","p":"Ownership","h":"Markers and where they are needed","m":"human","x":"Markers and where they are needed # The four markers say how a place is used; they are not operations on values ( LR 7.5 ): Marker Meaning Allowed when copy p an independent duplicate the type is copyable; refused in noalloc code if it allocates alias p a read loan always editable p an exclusive loan p is rooted in a let mut , an editable parameter or an exclusive loan move p transfer ownership p owns its value and nothing borrows it Where a marker is needed depends on the position. In short ( LR 7.5 ): Position An existing ownership-bearing place needs let x = p , x = p , a field initialiser name: p , return p copy , alias or move return x of a local let or let mut binding nothing: nothing can use x afterwards, so the return moves it argument to a plain parameter, receiver of a self method nothing: an implicit read loan for the call argument or receiver that the result borrows from alias p (or editable p ) receiver of an editable self method, a call of a fn mut closure nothing: p.method(\u2026) , bump() , where editable p is allowed receiver of an owned self method move p.method(\u2026) argument to an editable parameter editable p argument to an owned parameter move p or copy p a capture in a closure's capture list a marker ( LR 8.3 ) operands of == , < and friends, interpolation, match p , for x in p nothing: an implicit read loan A fresh value (a literal, a constructor call, a function result, arithmetic) never needs a marker, and neither does a trivial value such as an Int ( LR 7.5 , LR 6.11 ). A receiver that a method changes needs none either: the call readings.push(40) is accepted only where readings may change (a let mut binding, an editable parameter or capture, an exclusive loan, or the method's own editable self ), so its declaration already says so, and the call takes the same exclusive loan editable readings.push(40) would ( LR 7.5 ). Older code writes the marker; it is still accepted, and fortis fmt drops it. Forgetting a marker where one is required is E-OWN-001 ( LR 7.5 ): Fortis // reject: E-OWN-001 class User { export name : String } func main ( ) { let user = User ( name : \"Ada\" ) let other = user // reject: E-OWN-001 } The checker cannot guess which of the three you meant, and each means something different, so it asks: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class User { export name : String } func main ( console : Console ) { let user = User ( name : \"Ada\" ) let duplicate = copy user let reader = alias user console . print ( \"${duplicate.name} ${reader.name}\" ) let taken = move duplicate console . print ( taken . name ) } output Ada Ada Ada copy of a value whose type is not copyable, such as a Shared handle, is E-OWN-002 ( LR 7.5 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#loans-are-lexical","p":"Ownership","h":"Loans are lexical","m":"human","x":"Loans are lexical # A loan is a reference to a place, either read-only ( alias ) or exclusive ( editable ). Loans last for a lexical region, never \"until last use\" ( LR 7.3 ): a loan held by a binding ( let r = alias p , let r = editable p ) lasts until the end of the block that declares r ; a loan taken for a call's argument or receiver ends when the call returns, unless the result carries it; a loan taken by a comparison, interpolation or capture ends with the statement, and one taken by a for header lasts for the whole loop; a loan taken for an argument of an async call is held by the future the call returns; a loan returned with from is held by whatever binding receives it. To end a loan early, close a block around it ( LR 7.3 ). The aliasing invariant. At any point an owner has either one active exclusive loan or any number of read loans, never both. An exclusive loan of any field freezes the whole owner, while read loans of different fields coexist. While a loan is active the owner may not be moved, dropped or reassigned, and under a read loan not mutated. A conflicting loan is E-OWN-010 ; using an owner while it is loaned is E-OWN-011 ( LR 7.3 ). Fortis // reject: E-OWN-010 class Invoice { export customer : Int export lines : List [ String ] export func issue ( editable self ) { self . customer = 0 } } func main ( ) { let mut invoice = Invoice ( customer : 7 , lines : List . of ( \"tea\" ) ) let customer = alias invoice . customer let lines = alias invoice . lines invoice . issue ( ) // reject: E-OWN-010 } The two read loans of different fields are fine together; the exclusive loan invoice.issue() takes is not, while they are alive. Putting the reads in their own block ends them before the call: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Invoice { export customer : Int export lines : List [ String ] export func issue ( editable self ) { self . customer = 0 } } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut invoice = Invoice ( customer : 7 , lines : List . of ( \"tea\" ) ) { let lines = alias invoice . lines console . print ( \"${lines.count()} line\" ) } invoice . issue ( ) console . print ( \"customer now ${invoice.customer}\" ) } output 1 line customer now 0 Loans through loans. A binding that holds a loan (a plain or editable parameter, or let r = alias p ) is a path to the owner, and every loan is recorded against the owner ( LR 7.3 ). Reading through an exclusive parent creates a read child that freezes the parent; taking an exclusive child suspends the parent entirely until the child ends. A loan-holding binding can never be reassigned. Fortis // reject: E-OWN-011 class Cell { export value : Int } func inspect ( c : Cell ) Int => c . value func use ( c : editable Cell ) { discard inspect ( c ) // a read child of the exclusive parent, ended when inspect returns let r = editable c // an exclusive child held by r: c is suspended until the block ends r . value = 1 discard c . value // reject: E-OWN-011 } func main ( ) { }"},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#parameters-receivers-and-returns","p":"Ownership","h":"Parameters, receivers and returns","m":"human","x":"Parameters, receivers and returns # A parameter's mode says what the callee may do, and the call site repeats it ( LR 7.6 ): Parameter Caller writes Callee holds x: T (ownership-bearing) the place, unmarked a read loan for the call x: editable T editable p an exclusive loan for the call x: owned T move p , copy p or a fresh value ownership x: T (trivial) the value a copy editable gives permission to change the referent, not to rebind the parameter: parameters are never reassignable ( LR 7.6 ). Methods declare their receiver the same way, self , editable self or owned self : invoice.render() reads, invoice.issue(now) changes invoice , which must therefore be declared where it may change, and move invoice.archive() consumes it. A reviewer sees at every call whether the receiver is consumed, and at its declaration whether it can change ( LR 7.5 , LR 7.6 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Counter { export value : Int < 0 .. 1000 > export func bump ( editable self ) { if self . value < 1000 { self . value = self . value + 1 } } export func finish ( owned self ) Int => self . value } func show ( c : Counter ) Int => c . value func main ( console : Console ) { let mut counter = Counter ( value : 0 ) counter . bump ( ) counter . bump ( ) console . print ( \"shown ${show(counter)}\" ) console . print ( \"finished at ${move counter.finish()}\" ) } output shown 2 finished at 2 Returning a loan. A function that returns a loan names where it comes from with from , which names exactly one plain or editable parameter (or self ) ( LR 7.6 ). At the call, the argument the result borrows from is marked with alias (or editable for an exclusive parameter), because that loan now outlives the statement: it is held by the binding that receives the result. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class User { export name : String } pure func displayName ( user : User ) StringView from user { return alias user . name } func main ( console : Console ) { let user = User ( name : \"Ada Lovelace\" ) let name = displayName ( alias user ) console . print ( \"${name} has ${name.scalarCount()} scalars\" ) } output Ada Lovelace has 12 scalars The returned loan has the mode the signature declares: StringView from user is a read loan, and editable Cell from c is an exclusive one, allowed only when c is itself editable , which suspends the caller's c while the result lives ( LR 7.6 ). A loan of a local or of a temporary cannot be returned, since it would outlive its owner ( E-OWN-051 ), and a result may name only one origin ( E-OWN-050 ): return an owned value or split the function ( LR 7.6 ). For the same reason a function whose result is declared from a parameter cannot return a value of its own: return xs.get(0) ?? move d , return move d , a fresh value, a tuple or record built around one ( return (xs.get(0) ?? move d, 1) ) or a match or if one of whose arms gives one would leave the caller holding a loan of a value that ended with the call ( E-OWN-051 ). What produces a from result must lend: a place read through a loan, alias p , a call whose result lends, or a ?? , if or match all of whose results do. Bind the loan and return an owned value from a function returning Token , let v = xs.get(0) then return copy v ?? move d , or lend on every path. Fortis // reject: E-OWN-051 class User { export name : String export pure func guest ( ) User => User ( name : \"guest\" ) } pure func displayName ( user : User ) StringView from user { return alias user . name } func main ( ) { let name = displayName ( User . guest ( ) ) // reject: E-OWN-051 } Fortis // reject: E-OWN-050 class User { export name : String } pure func longer ( a : User , b : User ) StringView from a { if a . name . scalarCount ( ) > b . name . scalarCount ( ) { return alias a . name } return alias b . name // reject: E-OWN-050 } func main ( ) { } To hand back a local you own, return it: return local moves it, since nothing can use it afterwards ( LR 7.5 ). Older code writes retur"},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#parameters-receivers-and-returns","p":"Ownership","h":"Parameters, receivers and returns","m":"human","x":"n it: return local moves it, since nothing can use it afterwards ( LR 7.5 ). Older code writes return move local ; it is still accepted, and fortis fmt drops the move . To hand back a field of a value you only borrow, return copy obj.field or use a from signature; a fresh expression needs no marker ( LR 7.6 ). A loan stays a loan. xs.get(0) lends the list's element, so returning it as an owned Item? , binding it to an owned type, storing it in a field or passing it owned needs copy : bind the loan and copy what it lends, let first = xs.get(0) then return copy first , or match it, present(w) => copy w . Without the copy the checker reports E-OWN-001 ; for a value that cannot be copied, such as a Shared , it reports E-OWN-002 , and the value stays lent or is taken out of its owner ( LR 7.6 ). A ?? over a loan is a loan too: a read parameter name is returned as return copy name ?? \"guest\" . The binding that holds such a loan keeps the list lent until its block ends, like any other loan ( LR 7.3 ). After let first = xs.get(0) , the list cannot change or move in that block: xs.pop() is E-OWN-010 and move xs is E-OWN-011 . Close a block around the loan, or copy what it lends, before changing the list. An element of a trivial type, such as an Int in a List[Int] , is copied out and holds nothing."},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#views-cursors-and-no-stored-loans","p":"Ownership","h":"Views, cursors and no stored loans","m":"human","x":"Views, cursors and no stored loans # StringView , BytesView , ListView[T] and the cursors of the standard collections are loan-carrying types : values that store a read loan of their origin ( LR 7.7 ). A loan lives in exactly three places: a local binding, a parameter, or a result that names its origin with from . It is never stored in a field. A class may not declare a lifetime parameter, and no field may have a loan type or a loan-carrying type other than one from static ; both are E-OWN-053 ( LR 7.7 , X-137). Fortis // reject: E-OWN-053 class Line { // reject: E-OWN-053 export number : Int export text : StringView } func main ( ) { } Store the text itself instead, own it with move or copy , hold it through an explicit Shared , or pass it to each call that needs it ( LR 7.7 ). A parser that would keep a view of its input keeps offsets into it. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Line { export number : Int export text : String } func main ( console : Console ) { let line = Line ( number : 1 , text : \"first\" ) console . print ( \"${line.number}: ${line.text}\" ) } output 1: first Leaving stored loans out keeps every loan's lifetime visible in one function, and adding them later would break no program, whereas removing them after a release would ( LR 7.7 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#shared-ownership","p":"Ownership","h":"Shared ownership","m":"human","x":"Shared ownership # When a value really must have several owners, Shared[T] is a reference-counted owner, and the cost is written where it happens ( LR 7.8 ): Operation Gives Meaning Shared.create(move value) Shared[T] takes ownership; one strong handle s.retain() Shared[T] another strong handle; the only way to duplicate one, since copy is refused s.downgrade() Weak[T] a handle that does not keep the value alive w.upgrade() Shared[T]? a strong handle while one still exists, null afterwards w.retain() Weak[T] another weak handle Reading through a Shared[T] is a read loan of the value ( s.field , s.method() ); a Shared gives read access only, so mutation needs a Mutex or an atomic ( LR 7.8 , LR 12.7 ). retain and downgrade have no effects, so a pure function may create and pass handles; upgrade is nondet , because its answer depends on whether another task has dropped the last strong handle ( LR 7.8 ). Cycles of Shared handles leak and the checker does not detect them; a back-reference uses Weak . Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Settings { export name : String } func main ( console : Console ) { let first = Shared . create ( Settings ( name : \"production\" ) ) let second = first . retain ( ) let weak = second . downgrade ( ) console . print ( \"${first.name} and ${second.name}\" ) match weak . upgrade ( ) { present ( again ) => console . print ( \"still alive: ${again.name}\" ) null => console . print ( \"gone\" ) } } output production and production still alive: production Fortis // reject: E-OWN-002 class Settings { export name : String } func main ( ) { let first = Shared . create ( Settings ( name : \"production\" ) ) let second = copy first // reject: E-OWN-002 } A Shared of a capability carries the capability, which is how several values hold one console: see the example under destruction below and LR 11.6 ."},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#destruction","p":"Ownership","h":"Destruction","m":"human","x":"Destruction # A value is destroyed at exactly one of these events ( LR 7.9 ): Event Order block exit the block's loans end, then its defer s run last-first, then its locals are dropped in reverse order of initialisation assignment to a let mut owner the new value is evaluated, then the old value is dropped, then the store temporaries at the end of the statement, in reverse order of creation break , continue , return , fail each block left, innermost first, in block-exit order a failed constructor function the locals it initialised, in reverse order a class value its own cleanup method if it declares one, then its fields in declaration order A linear value is never destroyed implicitly: the obligation rules make that unreachable ( LR 7.9 ). The order is the same on every backend ( LR 7.9 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Resource { export id : Int export console : Shared [ Console ] cleanup ( editable self ) { self . console . print ( \"release ${self.id}\" ) } } func work ( console : Shared [ Console ] ) { let first = Resource ( id : 1 , console : console . retain ( ) ) let second = Resource ( id : 2 , console : console . retain ( ) ) console . print ( \"working\" ) } func main ( out : Console ) { let console = Shared . create ( move out ) work ( console ) console . print ( \"done\" ) } output working release 2 release 1 done second was initialised last, so it is dropped first. main takes the console out of the capabilities with a consuming pattern and puts it behind a Shared , because a field may not hold a loan of it ( LR 7.7 , LR 11.6 ). Only an owner destroys. A binding that holds a loan, such as let t = xs.get(0) , let a = alias first or a parameter that is not owned , ends without destroying anything, so a class's cleanup runs once, when its owner is destroyed. A value moved to a new owner is cleaned up there, once ( LR 7.9 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/07-ownership.html#the-rust-expressible-subset","p":"Ownership","h":"The Rust-expressible subset","m":"human","x":"The Rust-expressible subset # The ownership rules were chosen so that every accepted program has a lowering to safe Rust, listed shape by shape in LR Appendix C ( LR 7.10 ). A situation the checker can describe but that has no such shape, for example a loan stored inside a Shared , is rejected with E-OWN-090 and a pointer to the nearest supported pattern; it is never lowered with unsafe ( LR 7.10 , LR C.2 ). Fortis // reject: E-OWN-090 class User { export name : String } pure func displayName ( user : User ) StringView from user => alias user . name func run ( user : User ) Int { let seen = displayName ( alias user ) let shared = Shared . create ( move seen ) // reject: E-OWN-090 return shared . scalarCount ( ) } func main ( ) { } Widening the subset is a language change with a recorded decision, never an implementation shortcut, and the checker never inserts a clone, a reference count or hidden sharing to rescue a rejected program ( LR 7.10 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/08-functions.html#functions-and-closures","p":"Functions and closures","h":"Functions and closures","m":"human","x":"Functions and closures # Human mode. Agent mode: Functions and closures, by rule . Source of truth: LR 8 . Functions are where most Fortis code lives, and their heads carry more information than in most languages: what the function may do (its modifiers), what it does with each argument (the parameter modes of Ownership ) and, for a lambda, exactly which outside values it can see and how. This chapter covers named functions, lambdas and their three kinds, capture lists and function types."},{"u":"docs/guide/08-functions.html#named-functions","p":"Functions and closures","h":"Named functions","m":"human","x":"Named functions # A function head lists, in this order: the aspects applied to it (one per line above the head), export , any named contracts, then the modifiers pure , noalloc , async , try and recovers , then func , the name, type parameters, parameters, the success type and an optional from or where clause ( LR 8.1 ): Fortis // illustrative [@aspect(args) \u2026] [export] [Contract \u2026] [pure] [noalloc] [async] [try] [recovers] func name[TypeParams](params) ReturnType [from \u2026] [where \u2026] { body } The modifiers always appear in that order ( LR 8.1 ). Every parameter has a type, and the success type is written unless it is Void : func greet(console: Console) { \u2026 } returns nothing ( LR 8.1 ). Older code writes Void there too; it is still accepted, and fortis fmt drops it. A function type keeps its result, fn(Int) Void , and so does a lambda with a block body ( LR 8.2 , LR 8.4 ). What each modifier means: try : the function may fail; a caller receives an Outcome it must deal with ( LR 8.1 , LR 10.1 ). async : the function may suspend; a caller receives a Future ( LR 8.1 , LR 12.3 ). recovers : the function discharges a failure without reporting it ( LR 8.1 , LR 10.4 ). pure and noalloc : claims the checker verifies ( LR 8.1 , LR 11.2 ). try , async and recovers change what a caller gets, so a function must write them whenever its body has the effect. pure and noalloc are promises a function may choose to make, and callers can rely on them only when they are written ( LR 11.3 ). Effects and contracts covers all five in depth. A body is a block, or => expression for a function whose body is one expression ( LR 8.1 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func square ( n : Int < - 1000 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * n pure func describe ( n : Int < - 1000 .. 1000 > ) String { let s = square ( n ) return \"${n} squared is ${s}\" } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( describe ( 12 ) ) } output 12 squared is 144 A named function is not a value you can store, but you can pass its name wherever a callable is expected: the compiler makes a non-capturing fn of the matching type for you ( LR 8.1 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func increment ( n : Int ) Int => Int . saturatingAdd ( n , 1 ) pure func applyTwice ( f : pure fn ( Int ) Int , x : Int ) Int => f ( f ( x ) ) func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${applyTwice(increment, 5)}\" ) } output 7"},{"u":"docs/guide/08-functions.html#lambdas","p":"Functions and closures","h":"Lambdas","m":"human","x":"Lambdas # A lambda is written fn , an optional capture list in brackets, the parameters, the return type and a body ( LR 8.2 ). It takes the same modifiers as a named function, in the same order, and must write async , try and recovers when its body has those effects; pure and noalloc may be written and are otherwise inferred; the formatter prints no inferred one back, and keeps one you wrote, because the function type the lambda is passed to already says what it must be ( LR 8.2 ). A block body writes its return type; an expression body may leave it out, fn(t) => t.isOpen() , and it is then the result type of the function type the lambda is passed to, or the body's own type where that is a type parameter ( LR 8.2 ). Parameter types may be left out only when the lambda is passed directly to a parameter whose function type supplies them ( LR 8.2 ). Lambdas are not generic. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let numbers = List . of ( 3 , 8 , 1 , 6 ) let evens = numbers . filter ( fn ( n : Int ) Bool => n % 2 == 0 ) let doubled = numbers . map ( fn ( n ) Int => Int . saturatingMul ( n , 2 ) ) console . print ( \"${evens.count()} even, first doubled ${doubled.first() ?? 0}\" ) } output 2 even, first doubled 6 The second lambda leaves out its parameter's type, because map 's parameter already says what it receives."},{"u":"docs/guide/08-functions.html#three-kinds-of-closure","p":"Functions and closures","h":"Three kinds of closure","m":"human","x":"Three kinds of closure # Every lambda is one of three kinds, and the kind is always written, because it tells the reader how often the closure may run and whether it changes anything ( LR 8.2 ): Kind It may Called A call looks like fn read its captures any number of times f(x) fn mut also change what it captured any number of times; the binding must be let mut f(x) fn once also move a capture out exactly once; the call consumes it move f(x) Calling a fn mut takes an exclusive loan of the closure, and its let mut binding already says that it changes, so the call is written bump() ; calling a fn once consumes it, so it is written move finish() ( LR 7.5 , LR 8.2 ). Older code writes editable bump() ; it is still accepted, and fortis fmt rewrites it. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut total = 0 { let mut add = fn mut [ editable total ] ( n : Int ) Void { total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , n ) } add ( 4 ) add ( 6 ) } console . print ( \"total ${total}\" ) let name = String . from ( \"ada\" ) let takeName = fn once [ move name ] ( ) String { return move name } let taken = move takeName ( ) console . print ( \"took ${taken}\" ) } output total 10 took ada The inner block around add matters: add holds an exclusive loan of total for as long as add exists ( LR 8.3 ), so total can be read again only after the block that declares add has ended. A lambda whose body needs a stronger kind than it declares is rejected with E-FUN-010 , which names the capture and the use ( LR 8.2 ). Here a fn writes to its capture: Fortis // reject: E-FUN-010 func main ( ) { let mut count = 0 let bump = fn [ editable count ] ( ) Void { count = Int . saturatingAdd ( count , 1 ) // reject: E-FUN-010 } } Declaring fn mut fixes it. A move capture alone does not make a closure fn once ; what matters is whether the body moves the captured value out ( LR 8.2 ). Declaring a stronger kind than the body needs is allowed ( LR 8.2 ). The binding that holds a fn mut must be let mut , because each call changes it. Through a let mut binding the call needs no editable , since the declaration already says it ( LR 7.5 ); through a let binding it is an ownership error: Fortis // reject: E-OWN-001 func main ( ) { let mut total = 0 let add = fn mut [ editable total ] ( n : Int ) Void { total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , n ) } add ( 4 ) // reject: E-OWN-001 } And a fn once really runs once: after move finish() the closure is gone, and a second call is a use after move ( LR 7.4 , LR 8.2 ): Fortis // reject: E-OWN-020 func main ( ) { let name = String . from ( \"ada\" ) let finish = fn once [ move name ] ( ) String { return move name } let first = move finish ( ) let second = move finish ( ) // reject: E-OWN-020 }"},{"u":"docs/guide/08-functions.html#capture-lists","p":"Functions and closures","h":"Capture lists","m":"human","x":"Capture lists # A lambda sees only the outside names it lists, each with a marker that says how: copy x , alias x , editable x or move x . A trivial value such as an Int may be listed bare ( [threshold] ), which copies it ( LR 8.3 ). The list is written only when it is not empty: a lambda with no captures has no brackets ( LR 8.2 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let threshold = 4 let prefix = String . from ( \"item\" ) let numbers = List . of ( 1 , 5 , 2 , 8 ) let above = numbers . countWhere ( fn [ threshold ] ( n : Int ) Bool => n > threshold ) let labels = numbers . map ( fn [ alias prefix ] ( n : Int ) String => \"${prefix} ${n}\" ) console . print ( \"${above} above ${threshold}; ${labels.first() ?? \"none\"}\" ) } output 2 above 4; item 1 A name that is not in the list is not visible in the body: E-FUN-011 ( LR 8.3 ). Module-level constants, functions and types are never captures and need no entry. Fortis // reject: E-FUN-011 func main ( ) { let limit = 3 let numbers = List . of ( 1 , 5 , 2 , 8 ) let over = numbers . countWhere ( fn ( n : Int ) Bool => n > limit ) // reject: E-FUN-011 } Captures are evaluated, left to right, when the lambda expression is evaluated, not when it is called ( LR 8.3 ). Capturing a field path such as alias self.lines captures that path and takes the matching loan of its owner for as long as the closure lives ( LR 8.3 ). The loan rules of Ownership apply to captures as they do everywhere: two captures of one owner where one of them is editable conflict ( E-OWN-010 , LR 8.3 ). Fortis // reject: E-OWN-010 func main ( ) { let mut count = 0 let mut both = fn mut [ editable count , alias count ] ( ) Void { // reject: E-OWN-010 count = 1 } }"},{"u":"docs/guide/08-functions.html#closures-that-hold-loans","p":"Functions and closures","h":"Closures that hold loans","m":"human","x":"Closures that hold loans # A closure that captures by alias or editable holds loans, and it cannot outlive them ( LR 8.3 ). Passing such a closure to an ordinary callback parameter, as above, needs nothing special: the callee only borrows the closure for the call. Returning one, or passing it to an owned fn parameter, is different: the type must name the region the closure lives in, with from ( LR 8.3 , LR 8.4 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Limit { export value : Int } func above ( limit : Limit ) fn ( Int ) Bool from limit { return fn [ alias limit ] ( n : Int ) Bool => n > limit . value } func countWhere ( items : List [ Int ] , keep : fn ( Int ) Bool ) Int { let mut n = 0 for item in items { if keep ( item ) { n = Int . saturatingAdd ( n , 1 ) } } return n } func main ( console : Console ) { let limit = Limit ( value : 3 ) let check = above ( alias limit ) console . print ( \"${countWhere(List.of(1, 5, 2, 8), check)} above ${limit.value}\" ) } output 2 above 3 The caller writes alias limit , because the result keeps a loan of limit past the call ( LR 7.6 ). Without from limit , the closure's loan would have nowhere to live, and the checker rejects the function ( LR 8.3 ). A closure is never stored in a field together with a loan, because fields never store loans ( LR 7.7 , LR 8.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/08-functions.html#closures-and-linear-values","p":"Functions and closures","h":"Closures and linear values","m":"human","x":"Closures and linear values # A closure that captures a linear value by move (an Outcome , a Future , a Task ) owns an obligation, so it must run exactly once: it must be fn once , and a fn or fn mut that captures one is E-OBL-006 ( LR 8.3 ). Here the closure is also never called, so the obligation it owns is dropped as well, which is E-OBL-001 on the same line ( LR 6.12 ): Fortis // reject: E-OBL-006, E-OBL-001 export error Oops { code : \"demo.oops\" } pure try func risky ( ) Int { fail Oops ( ) } recovers func run ( ) Int { let pending = risky ( ) let mut later = fn mut [ move pending ] ( ) Int { // reject: E-OBL-006, E-OBL-001 return 0 } return 0 } func main ( ) { } The repair is fn once , called exactly once with move later() , with a body that consumes what it captured ( LR 8.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/08-functions.html#function-types","p":"Functions and closures","h":"Function types","m":"human","x":"Function types # A function type names the kind, the parameter types with their modes, the return type and the modifiers ( LR 8.4 ): Fortis // illustrative fn ( Int ) Bool pure fn ( StringView ) Int fn mut ( editable Buffer ) Void try fn once ( owned Job ) Void async fn ( Request ) Response A callable fits a function type when its kind is no more demanding ( fn fits where fn mut or fn once is asked for), its parameter modes match exactly, and its modifiers are at least as strong: a pure callable fits a plain fn , and a callable that cannot fail fits a try fn ( LR 8.4 ). The reverse is never allowed: function types are nominal in their modifiers, so an ordinary callback cannot be passed where a pure fn is required ( LR 8.4 ). Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 import fortis . host { Console } pure func applyPure ( f : pure fn ( Int ) Int , x : Int ) Int => f ( x ) func noisy ( console : Console , n : Int ) Int { console . print ( \"called\" ) return n } func main ( console : Console ) { discard applyPure ( fn [ alias console ] ( n : Int ) Int => noisy ( console , n ) , 1 ) // reject: E-TYP-040 } A lambda literal whose body has no effect that pure excludes is pure without saying so, which is why the lambdas passed to filter and map above needed no modifier ( LR 8.2 , LR 11.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/08-functions.html#closures-in-collections-dynamic-fn","p":"Functions and closures","h":"Closures in collections: dynamic fn","m":"human","x":"Closures in collections: dynamic fn # Each lambda has its own concrete type, which is what lets the backend compile it without boxing ( LR 8.4 ). A list of different closures therefore needs one common type: dynamic fn(\u2026) \u2026 , a boxed, owned closure that allocates when it is created ( LR 8.4 ). Nothing is ever boxed unless you write dynamic ( LR 8.4 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func applyAll ( steps : List [ dynamic fn ( Int ) Int ] , start : Int ) Int { let mut value = start for step in steps { value = step ( value ) } return value } func main ( console : Console ) { let bonus = 10 let double : dynamic fn ( Int ) Int = fn ( n : Int ) Int => Int . saturatingMul ( n , 2 ) let addBonus : dynamic fn ( Int ) Int = fn [ bonus ] ( n : Int ) Int => Int . saturatingAdd ( n , bonus ) let steps = List . of ( move double , move addBonus ) console . print ( \"${applyAll(steps, 5)}\" ) } output 20"},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#classes-interfaces-mixins-and-enums","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","m":"human","x":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums # Human mode. Agent mode: Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums, by rule . Source of truth: LR 9 . Preview: interfaces and mixins ( LR 9.5 , 9.6 ) arrive in v0.1. Classes, cleanup, enums and match ( LR 9.1 , 9.2 , 9.3 , 9.4 , 9.7 and 9.8 ) are in v0.0.1. Fortis has no inheritance. A class is a named record with methods; an interface is a set of method signatures a type can promise to implement; a mixin contributes ready-made methods to classes that ask for it; an enum is a closed set of variants, each with its own payload. match takes any of them apart. Each of these is nominal and visible in the source: a reader never has to search a hierarchy to find which code runs ( LR 9.1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#classes","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Classes","m":"human","x":"Classes # A class declares fields and methods. Fields and methods are private to the module unless marked export , and a field may have a constant default ( LR 9.1 , LR 4.4 ): Fortis // illustrative [export] [trivial] class Name[TypeParams] [derive I1, I2] [implements I3, I4] [with M1, M2] { [export] field: Type [export] field: Type = constantDefault [export] \u2026 func method([editable | owned] self, params) Ret { \u2026 } [export] \u2026 func associated(params) Ret { \u2026 } cleanup(editable self) { \u2026 } } There is no inheritance, no virtual dispatch between classes and no lookup of members at run time ( LR 9.1 ). Fields, methods, associated functions and constants of a class share one namespace, so a name may be declared only once in it ( E-TYP-070 , LR 9.1 ). That includes a field and a method with the same name: Fortis // reject: E-TYP-070 class Account { export owner : String balance : Int export pure func balance ( self ) Int => self . balance // reject: E-TYP-070 } func main ( ) { }"},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#construction","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Construction","m":"human","x":"Construction # Every class has a generated constructor , Name(field: value, \u2026) , which takes every field as a named argument; fields with defaults may be left out ( LR 9.2 ). The constructor takes ownership of what it is given, so a field argument that is an existing ownership-bearing place needs copy or move , exactly as in any other owned position ( LR 9.2 , LR 7.5 ). There is never a half-built object: either every field has a value or there is no object. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Point derive Equatable { export x : Int export y : Int export label : String = \"point\" } func main ( console : Console ) { let name = String . from ( \"home\" ) let a = Point ( x : 1 , y : 2 ) let b = Point ( x : 1 , y : 2 , label : copy name ) console . print ( \"${a.label} ${b.label} ${name}\" ) console . print ( \"${a == Point(x: 1, y: 2)}\" ) } output point home home true The generated constructor is callable only where every field is visible ( LR 9.2 ). A class with a private field can therefore be built only inside its module, which is how a class protects its invariants: other modules must go through the functions it exports. Here the accounts module of a library keeps balance private, and a test in a second module, checks , opens an account through Account.open : YAML # file: demo.accounts/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: demo.accounts version: 1.0.0 exports: [accounts] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" Fortis // accept // file: demo.accounts/src/accounts.ft export class Account { export owner : String balance : Int export pure func open ( owner : owned String ) Account => Account ( owner : move owner , balance : 0 ) export pure func current ( self ) Int => self . balance } Fortis // accept // file: demo.accounts/src/checks.ft import demo . accounts . accounts { Account } test \"a new account is empty\" { let a = Account . open ( \"ada\" ) expect a . current ( ) == 0 } Calling the generated constructor from any other module is E-NAM-004 ( LR 4.4 , LR 9.2 ), here from an application that depends on the library: YAML # file: demo.misuse/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: demo.misuse version: 1.0.0 entry: app exports: [] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" demo.accounts: \"1.0.0\" Fortis // reject: E-NAM-004 // file: demo.misuse/src/app.ft import demo . accounts . accounts { Account } func main ( ) { let a = Account ( owner : \"ada\" , balance : 100 ) // reject: E-NAM-004 }"},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#associated-functions","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Associated functions","m":"human","x":"Associated functions # A function declared inside a class without a self parameter is an associated function , called as Name.function(\u2026) ( LR 9.2 ). Constructors that must validate or may fail are associated functions; by convention they are named create , of or from , since Fortis has no new ( LR 1.1 ). Because associated functions are the only way to reach private fields, they are where a class enforces its invariants against every caller ( LR 9.2 ). Fortis // accept (exit status 1) import fortis . host { Console } export error EmptyName { code : \"people.empty_name\" } class Person { export name : String years : Int < 0 .. 150 > export pure try func create ( name : owned String , age : Int < 0 .. 150 > ) Person { if name . scalarCount ( ) == 0 { fail EmptyName ( ) } return Person ( name : move name , years : age ) } export pure func age ( self ) Int < 0 .. 150 > => self . years } try func main ( console : Console ) { let ada = Person . create ( String . from ( \"Ada\" ) , 36 ) or fail console . print ( \"${ada.name} is ${ada.age()}\" ) let nobody = Person . create ( String . from ( \"\" ) , 0 ) or fail console . print ( \"not reached\" ) } output Ada is 36 The second call fails, or fail passes the failure on, and main ends with exit status 1 and one failure record on standard error ( LR 4.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#spreading-one-value-into-another","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Spreading one value into another","m":"human","x":"Spreading one value into another # A constructor call may start with a spread , ...copy p or ...move p , which fills every field of the new value from the field of the same name and type in p ( LR 9.2 ). The remaining fields are given explicitly after it, and defaults fill the rest. This is how a value is projected into a narrower or wider type without naming each field. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Customer { export name : String export email : String export notes : String } class Summary { export name : String export email : String export invoices : Int } func main ( console : Console ) { let customer = Customer ( name : \"Ada\" , email : \"ada@example.org\" , notes : \"prefers email\" ) let summary = Summary ( ... copy customer , invoices : 3 ) console . print ( \"${summary.name} <${summary.email}>: ${summary.invoices} invoices\" ) console . print ( \"still have ${customer.notes}\" ) } output Ada <ada@example.org>: 3 invoices still have prefers email ...copy p copies the fields it takes and leaves p alone; ...move p consumes the whole of p , dropping the fields the target does not have ( LR 9.2 ). A field given both by the spread and explicitly is rejected, and a field with no source, no explicit value and no default is E-TYP-050 ( LR 9.2 ). Dropping a linear field this way is never allowed: ...move p from a value holding an Outcome the target has no room for is E-OBL-007 ( LR 9.2 ). A spread reads the source's fields, so they must all be visible where it is written, and ...move p is not permitted when p 's class declares a cleanup ( LR 9.2 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#methods-and-receivers","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Methods and receivers","m":"human","x":"Methods and receivers # A method is a function declared in a class whose first parameter is self ( LR 9.3 ). The receiver says what the method may do with the value. A call that consumes it says so with move ; a call that changes it is written plainly and is accepted only on a place that may change (a let mut binding, an editable parameter or capture, an exclusive loan, or the method's own editable self ), so the place's declaration already says it ( LR 9.3 , LR 7.5 , LR 7.6 ): Receiver The method Call site self reads the value value.method(args) editable self may change it place.method(args) , on a place that may change owned self consumes it move place.method(args) Older code writes editable place.method(args) ; it is still accepted, and fortis fmt drops the marker. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Counter { export name : String count : Int < 0 .. 1000 > export pure func start ( name : owned String ) Counter => Counter ( name : move name , count : 0 ) export pure func value ( self ) Int < 0 .. 1000 > => self . count export pure func bump ( editable self ) { if self . count < 1000 { self . count = self . count + 1 } } export func finish ( owned self ) String => \"${self.name}: ${self.count}\" } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut hits = Counter . start ( String . from ( \"hits\" ) ) hits . bump ( ) hits . bump ( ) console . print ( \"value ${hits.value()}\" ) let summary = move hits . finish ( ) console . print ( summary ) } output value 2 hits: 2 Inside a method, fields are always reached through self ( LR 4.5 ). A pure method may write through editable self , because that write is its declared footprint, but it may not perform I/O or write anything else ( LR 9.3 , LR 11.3 ). A class may also declare associated constants, const name = \u2026 , read as Name.name ( LR 9.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#cleanup","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Cleanup","m":"human","x":"Cleanup # A class may declare cleanup(editable self) , which runs when a value of the class is destroyed, before its fields are dropped ( LR 9.4 ). The fields are then dropped in declaration order ( LR 7.9 ). Cleanup can only release: it cannot be try or async , and a finalisation that can fail (a flush, a commit) is an ordinary method that the caller invokes explicitly ( LR 9.4 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Connection { export id : Int export console : Shared [ Console ] cleanup ( editable self ) { self . console . print ( \"closing ${self.id}\" ) } } class Pool { export first : Connection export second : Connection } func open ( console : Shared [ Console ] ) { let pool = Pool ( first : Connection ( id : 1 , console : console . retain ( ) ) , second : Connection ( id : 2 , console : console . retain ( ) ) ) console . print ( \"pool ready\" ) } func main ( c : Console ) { let console = Shared . create ( move c ) open ( console . retain ( ) ) console . print ( \"done\" ) } output pool ready closing 1 closing 2 done The connections hold the console through Shared , because a field never stores a loan ( LR 7.7 , LR 7.8 ). cleanup cannot be called directly; it runs only at destruction ( E-TYP-040 , LR 9.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 class Cell { export value : Int cleanup ( editable self ) { } } func main ( ) { let mut c = Cell ( value : 1 ) c . cleanup ( ) // reject: E-TYP-040 }"},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#interfaces","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Interfaces","m":"human","x":"Interfaces # An interface declares method signatures (with their modifiers and receiver modes), associated functions and associated constants, and no bodies ( LR 9.5 ). A type implements it only by saying so, with implements , with derive , or with an implement Interface for Type { \u2026 } block; having methods with the right names is not enough ( LR 9.5 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } interface Shape { pure func area ( self ) Int pure func name ( self ) StringView from static } class Square implements Shape { export side : Int < 0 .. 1000 > export pure func area ( self ) Int => self . side * self . side export pure func name ( self ) StringView from static => \"square\" } class Rect implements Shape { export width : Int < 0 .. 1000 > export height : Int < 0 .. 1000 > export pure func area ( self ) Int => self . width * self . height export pure func name ( self ) StringView from static => \"rectangle\" } pure func larger [ T ] ( a : T , b : T ) Int where T implements Shape { if a . area ( ) >= b . area ( ) { return a . area ( ) } return b . area ( ) } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${larger(Square(side: 3), Square(side: 4))}\" ) console . print ( \"${Rect(width: 2, height: 5).name()}\" ) } output 16 rectangle A generic function like larger is checked once against its bound and compiled once per type it is used with, so the call costs nothing at run time ( LR 6.10 ). An implementation must provide every member with a compatible signature, and it may not weaken what the interface promises: it cannot drop pure , add try or async , or narrow a parameter's refinement ( LR 9.5 ). Every such mismatch, and a missing member, is E-TYP-080 ( LR 9.5 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-080 interface Priced { pure func price ( self ) Int } class Pear implements Priced { // reject: E-TYP-080 export func price ( self ) Int => 4 } func main ( ) { } The implement block is the form used for implementations declared apart from the type, such as generic ones ( LR 9.5 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } interface Describe { pure func describe ( self ) String } class Temperature { export celsius : Int } implement Describe for Temperature { pure func describe ( self ) String => \"${self.celsius} C\" } pure func show [ T ] ( x : T ) String where T implements Describe => x . describe ( ) func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( show ( Temperature ( celsius : 21 ) ) ) } output 21 C"},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#requirements-bundles-and-markers","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Requirements, bundles and markers","m":"human","x":"Requirements, bundles and markers # An interface may require others: a type implementing Hashable must also implement Equatable ( LR 9.5 ). An interface with requirements and no members of its own is a bundle , satisfied by every type that implements everything it requires, without being named: Codec is Encodable plus Decodable ( LR 9.5 ). An interface with neither members nor requirements is a marker , such as Authority , and is only ever implemented by explicit declaration ( LR 9.5 ). A cycle of requirements is E-TYP-060 ( LR 9.5 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-060 interface Left requires Right { // reject: E-TYP-060 } interface Right requires Left { // reject: E-TYP-060 } func main ( ) { } Deriving an interface and also writing the method the derivation would generate is E-MIX-001 ( LR 9.5 ). There are no default method bodies in interfaces: shared behaviour on top of an interface is a mixin that requires it ( LR 9.5 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#coherence","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Coherence","m":"human","x":"Coherence # A package may implement an interface for a type only if it defines the interface or the type, and one type has at most one implementation of an interface. Both violations are E-TYP-100 ( LR 9.5 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-100 interface Named { pure func name ( self ) String } class Fig implements Named { export pure func name ( self ) String => \"fig\" } implement Named for Fig { // reject: E-TYP-100 pure func name ( self ) String => \"fig\" } func main ( ) { }"},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#dynamic-dispatch","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Dynamic dispatch","m":"human","x":"Dynamic dispatch # When one place must hold values of different types that share an interface, write dynamic Interface ( LR 9.5 ). The value is boxed when it is created, which allocates, and calls through it are dispatched at run time. It is always written with an ownership form: dynamic Shape (owned), alias dynamic Shape (a loan) or Shared[dynamic Shape] ( LR 9.5 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } interface Shape { pure func area ( self ) Int pure func name ( self ) StringView from static } class Square implements Shape { export side : Int < 0 .. 1000 > export pure func area ( self ) Int => self . side * self . side export pure func name ( self ) StringView from static => \"square\" } class Rect implements Shape { export width : Int < 0 .. 1000 > export height : Int < 0 .. 1000 > export pure func area ( self ) Int => self . width * self . height export pure func name ( self ) StringView from static => \"rectangle\" } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut shapes : List [ dynamic Shape ] = List . default ( ) shapes . push ( Square ( side : 2 ) ) shapes . push ( Rect ( width : 2 , height : 5 ) ) let mut total = 0 for shape in shapes { console . print ( \"${shape.name()}: ${shape.area()}\" ) total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , shape . area ( ) ) } console . print ( \"total ${total}\" ) } output square: 4 rectangle: 10 total 14 Only some interfaces can be used this way: an interface is dynamic-eligible when it has no generic methods and no method that takes or returns Self by value ( LR 9.5 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#mixins","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Mixins","m":"human","x":"Mixins # A mixin contributes method bodies to the classes that apply it with with . It declares what it needs as requires method signatures, never fields, and it cannot add fields, constructors or a cleanup ( LR 9.6 ). A method the class declares itself wins over a mixin method of the same name ( LR 9.6 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } mixin Loud { requires pure func name ( self ) String export pure func shout ( self ) String => \"${self.name()}!\" export pure func whisper ( self ) String => \"(${self.name()})\" } class Dog with Loud { export pure func name ( self ) String => \"woof\" export pure func whisper ( self ) String => \"...\" } func main ( console : Console ) { let dog = Dog ( ) console . print ( \"${dog.shout()} ${dog.whisper()}\" ) } output woof! ... A mixin body is checked at each class that applies it and sees only the class's exported members, so a mixin never reads a private field ( LR 9.6 ). Two applied mixins that provide the same method, with no method of that name in the class to settle it, are E-MIX-001 ( LR 9.6 ): Fortis // reject: E-MIX-001 mixin English { export pure func hello ( self ) StringView from static => \"hello\" } mixin French { export pure func hello ( self ) StringView from static => \"bonjour\" } class Greeter with English , French { // reject: E-MIX-001 export id : Int } func main ( ) { }"},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#enums","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Enums","m":"human","x":"Enums # An enum is a closed set of variants, each optionally carrying named payload fields ( LR 9.7 ). A variant is constructed as Enum.Variant or Enum.Variant(field: value) , and the Enum. prefix may be left out wherever the expected type is already that enum: in a match arm pattern, a return , an argument, a field initialiser, an annotated let , or the right side of a comparison such as severity == Error ( LR 9.7 ). If the bare name is also a class, an error or another type in scope, it is ambiguous, and the checker asks for Severity.Error ( E-TYP-040 ), so a name never quietly means something else. Enums may have methods and implement interfaces, and an enum whose payloads are all trivial is itself trivial ( LR 9.7 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } enum Coin derive Equatable { Penny Pound Other ( pence : Int < 0 .. 10000 > ) export pure func pence ( self ) Int < 0 .. 10000 > { return match self { Penny => 1 Pound => 100 Other ( pence : p ) => p } } } pure func larger ( a : Coin , b : Coin ) Coin { if a . pence ( ) >= b . pence ( ) { return a } return b } pure func rounded ( c : Coin ) Coin { if c . pence ( ) >= 100 { return Pound } return Penny } func main ( console : Console ) { let c = larger ( Pound , Other ( pence : 250 ) ) console . print ( \"${c.pence()}\" ) console . print ( \"${rounded(c) == Pound}\" ) } output 250 true return Pound in rounded needs no prefix, because the function returns a Coin . Enums are closed: a source enum is never silently extended, and every match over one must handle every variant ( LR 9.7 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#patterns-and-match","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Patterns and match","m":"human","x":"Patterns and match # match evaluates its subject once, tries the arms from top to bottom and runs the first that matches; there is no fall-through ( LR 9.8 ). As an expression, every arm gives a value of one type, with give at the end of a block arm ( LR 9.8 ). Pattern Matches name anything, and binds it _ anything a literal an equal integer, string or Boolean Variant , Variant(field: name) , Variant(name) an enum variant, binding its payload present(name) , null an optional success(name) , failure(name) an outcome ( Outcomes and failures ) is ErrorName(\u2026) a failure of a declared kind Name(field: p, \u2026) a class, consuming it ( match move only) (p1, p2) , { field: p } a tuple, an anonymous record otherwise the rest, for open types only These are the patterns of LR 9.8 ; patterns nest, and an arm may add a guard with when ( LR 9.8 ). A guarded arm does not count toward exhaustiveness. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } enum Light { Red Amber Green ( seconds : Int < 0 .. 600 > ) } pure func show ( light : Light ) String { return match light { Red => \"stop\" Amber => \"wait\" Green ( seconds : s ) when s > 60 => \"go (long)\" Green ( seconds : s ) => \"go for ${s}s\" } } pure func size ( n : Int ) StringView from static { return match n { 0 => \"none\" 1 => \"one\" otherwise => \"many\" } } pure func orZero ( n : Int ? ) Int { return match n { present ( v ) => v null => 0 } } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${show(Green(seconds: 90))}, ${show(Green(seconds: 20))}, ${show(Red)}\" ) console . print ( \"${size(0)} ${size(7)} ${orZero(null)} ${orZero(5)}\" ) } output go (long), go for 20s, stop none many 0 5"},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#exhaustiveness","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Exhaustiveness","m":"human","x":"Exhaustiveness # A match over a closed type, an enum or an optional, must name every case, and each missing variant is reported by name ( E-MAT-001 , LR 9.8 ). The record points at the match : Fortis // reject: E-MAT-001 enum Light { Red Amber Green } pure func code ( l : Light ) Int { return match l { // reject: E-MAT-001 Red => 1 Amber => 2 } } func main ( ) { } A catch-all ( _ , a bare name or otherwise ) as the last arm over a closed enum is E-MAT-002 : if it were allowed, adding a variant later would silently fall into it, instead of surfacing every match that must now handle it ( LR 9.8 ). otherwise is for open types: integers, strings and Failure ( LR 9.8 ). Fortis // reject: E-MAT-002 enum Light { Red Amber Green } pure func code ( l : Light ) Int { return match l { Red => 1 _ => 2 // reject: E-MAT-002 } } func main ( ) { } An arm may list alternatives, Red | Amber => true , when they share a result: the arm matches when any alternative does. The alternatives bind no names, are never otherwise , and the arm takes no when guard ( E-MAT-003 ); every variant still has to be named somewhere, so a new variant is still reported ( LR 9.8 ). Range patterns and x @ pattern bindings are not in Fortis 1.0."},{"u":"docs/guide/09-classes.html#ownership-in-patterns","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","h":"Ownership in patterns","m":"human","x":"Ownership in patterns # match p borrows p for the match, and the bindings in its arms are read loans. match move p consumes p , and the bindings own their values ( LR 9.8 ). Taking a class apart with a class pattern consumes it, so it is written with match move or let Pattern = move p ; every field is named, and _ drops a field that is not linear ( LR 9.8 ). A class pattern needs every field of the class to be visible where it is written, and a class that declares a cleanup cannot be taken apart this way ( LR 9.8 ). This is the one way to take a linear field out of a class, since there are no partial moves ( LR 9.8 , LR 7.2 ). A let pattern has no other case to fall back on, so it must match every value: a variant, a literal, null or present(\u2026) anywhere inside it is E-MAT-004 , and match is the form that handles the other cases ( LR 5.1 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Parcel { export label : String export grams : Int } pure func heavy ( p : owned Parcel ) Bool { return match move p { Parcel ( label : _ , grams : g ) => g > 1000 } } func main ( console : Console ) { let parcel = Parcel ( label : \"books\" , grams : 2500 ) let Parcel ( label : label , grams : grams ) = move parcel console . print ( \"${label}: ${grams} g\" ) console . print ( \"${heavy(Parcel(label: \"feather\", grams: 1))}\" ) } output books: 2500 g false"},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#outcomes-and-failures","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"Outcomes and failures","m":"human","x":"Outcomes and failures # Human mode. Agent mode: Outcomes and failures, by rule . Source of truth: LR 10 . Preview: linear containers ( LR 10.7 ) arrive in v0.1. The rest of this chapter ( LR 10 ) is in v0.0.1. Fortis has no exceptions. A function that can fail says so with try , and its caller receives an Outcome : a value that holds either the success value or a Failure . An outcome is linear : it must be dealt with exactly once, and the checker rejects a program that drops one ( LR 1 , LR 10.1 ). This chapter shows the ways to deal with an outcome, how failures are declared, matched and reported, and the few conditions, called defects, that are not failures at all. An absent value ( T? ), a Bool and an outcome are three different things with three different operators ( ?? , || , or ), and none converts into another ( LR 1 , LR 6.8 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#fallible-functions","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"Fallible functions","m":"human","x":"Fallible functions # try func f(\u2026) T returns Outcome[T] . Inside it, return v succeeds with v and fail f fails with the failure f ( LR 10.1 ). A call to it needs no prefix, but its result is an Outcome[T] , not a T : the success value cannot be reached without consuming the outcome ( LR 10.1 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error NotEven { code : \"demo.not_even\" payload { value : Int } message : \"${value} is odd\" } pure try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 != 0 { fail NotEven ( value : n ) } return n / 2 } try func main ( console : Console ) { let h = half ( 10 ) or fail console . print ( \"half of 10 is ${h}\" ) } output half of 10 is 5 Using the outcome as if it were the value is a type error (here E-TYP-040 , LR 10.1 ), and leaving it unconsumed is E-OBL-001 ( LR 10.1 ): Fortis // reject: E-OBL-001, E-TYP-040 export error NotEven { code : \"demo.not_even\" } pure try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 != 0 { fail NotEven ( ) } return n / 2 } pure func double ( n : Int ) Int => Int . saturatingMul ( n , 2 ) func main ( ) { half ( 10 ) // reject: E-OBL-001 let d = double ( half ( 10 ) ) // reject: E-TYP-040 }"},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#consuming-an-outcome","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"Consuming an outcome","m":"human","x":"Consuming an outcome # These are all the ways to consume an outcome ( LR 10.2 ): Operation What happens to the failure Where e or fail passed on to the caller in a try function e or recover v replaced by v , which is evaluated only on failure anywhere; adds recovers e catch name { \u2026 } handled in a block that ends in recover , return or fail anywhere match e { success(x) => \u2026, failure(f) => \u2026 } handled by the failure arm, which consumes f anywhere report f written to the diagnostic plane where io is allowed move to an owned Outcome[T] or owned Failure parameter, or into a linear container or field transferred anywhere fail f passed on in a try function There is no bare or value : every discard of a failure is spelled recover , so a reader can find them all. There is no ? , no try at the call site and no unwrap ( LR 10.2 ). Because or binds loosest, the idiom is one decision per line: let v = f() or fail , then use v ( LR 1.1 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error NotEven { code : \"demo.not_even\" payload { value : Int } } pure try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 != 0 { fail NotEven ( value : n ) } return n / 2 } pure recovers func halfOrZero ( n : Int ) Int => half ( n ) or recover 0 recovers func describe ( n : Int ) String { return match half ( n ) { success ( v ) => \"half of ${n} is ${v}\" failure ( f ) => { report f give \"${n} has no half\" } } } recovers func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${halfOrZero(7)} ${halfOrZero(8)}\" ) console . print ( describe ( 6 ) ) console . print ( describe ( 3 ) ) } output 0 4 half of 6 is 3 3 has no half describe(3) reports its failure, so a failure record also goes to standard error; the output above is standard output only."},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#recovers-discarding-a-failure-is-visible","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"recovers: discarding a failure is visible","m":"human","x":"recovers : discarding a failure is visible # A failure discharged without being reported, by or recover , a catch ending in recover , or a match arm that drops it, adds the recovers effect ( LR 10.2 , LR 10.4 ). A function with that effect must say recovers in its head, and because a call imports its callee's effects, so must the functions that call it; a signature therefore shows who swallowed a failure ( LR 8.1 , LR 11.2 , LR 11.3 ). Leaving the modifier off is E-EFF-001 ( LR 11.1 ): Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 export error NotEven { code : \"demo.not_even\" } pure try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 != 0 { fail NotEven ( ) } return n / 2 } pure func halfOrZero ( n : Int ) Int => half ( n ) or recover 0 // reject: E-EFF-001 func main ( ) { }"},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#declaring-failures","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"Declaring failures","m":"human","x":"Declaring failures # Failure is the one failure type. It carries a stable code , a typed payload, an origin (module, function and line) and an optional chain of causes; it is linear and cannot be copied ( LR 10.3 ). Each kind of failure is declared with error ( LR 10.3 ): Fortis // illustrative export error Name { code: \"package.area.kind\" payload { field: Type, \u2026 } message: \"template naming ${field}\" } The declaration gives a constructor, Name(field: \u2026) , that produces a Failure , a pattern is Name(\u2026) for matching, and a display template ( LR 10.3 ). The message is a template, not an interpolated string: its ${\u2026} may name only payload fields, and it is rendered when the failure is displayed ( LR 10.3 ). Codes are unique within a package and stable across versions, and payload fields are trivial values or length-bounded strings, so a failure is bounded and never carries a secret ( LR 10.3 ). A failure raised with fail in a pure function reads no clock and allocates no identifier, so it is as deterministic as the function ( LR 10.3 ). What a program can read from a failure is its code ( e.code , a StringView ) and, through an is Name(field: x) pattern, its payload ( LR 10.3 , LR 10.5 ). The message template is for display: LR 10.3 says it is rendered when the failure is displayed, but Failure does not implement Displayable in Language 1.0's library, so it cannot be interpolated, and the records the toolchain writes carry the code and payload without rendering the template yet (X-154, D-57). Build a message from the payload when a program needs one. One more difference from the LR today: the checkers accept fail Name(\u2026) , but refuse a constructor call where a Failure value is expected ( Outcome.failure(Name(\u2026)) , a binding annotated Failure ), which LR 10.3 allows (D-52). A test or an adapter that needs to build an outcome by hand uses Outcome.success(v) and Outcome.failure(move f) ( LR 10.1 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" payload { key : Int } } pure func fake ( key : Int ) Outcome [ Int ] { if key > 3 { return Outcome . failure ( Missing ( key : key ) ) } return Outcome . success ( key ) } recovers func main ( console : Console ) { let a = fake ( 2 ) or recover - 1 let b = fake ( 9 ) or recover - 1 console . print ( \"${a} ${b}\" ) } output 2 -1"},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#catch-and-recover","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"catch and recover","m":"human","x":"catch and recover # e catch name { \u2026 } names the failure and runs the block only when e failed ( LR 10.4 ). Inside the block, return and fail leave the enclosing function, and recover v ends the block with v as the value of the whole expression. A catch covers only its left operand ( LR 10.4 ). When the failing call returns Void , the replacement is Void 's one value, written recover Void ( LR 6.2 , LR 10.4 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error NotANumber { code : \"parse.not_a_number\" payload { position : Int } } pure try func parseDigits ( text : StringView ) Int { let n = text . scalarCount ( ) let mut value = 0 let mut i = 0 while i < n { let s = text . scalarAt ( i ) or fail let d = Scalar . digitValue ( s ) if d == null { fail NotANumber ( position : i ) } value = value * 10 or fail value = value + d or fail i = Int . add ( i , 1 ) or fail } return value } func parseOrZero ( text : StringView ) Int { let v = parseDigits ( text ) catch e { report e recover 0 } return v } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${parseOrZero(\"2026\")} ${parseOrZero(\"20x6\")}\" ) } output 2026 0 If the block consumes the failure before recover , as report e does here, the recover discards nothing and adds no recovers effect; if it does not, recover consumes the failure and the function gets recovers ( LR 10.4 ). Every path through the block must end in recover , return , fail or a call that never returns ( E-OBL-002 , LR 10.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-OBL-002, E-OBL-001 export error NotEven { code : \"demo.not_even\" } pure try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 != 0 { fail NotEven ( ) } return n / 2 } recovers func careful ( n : Int ) Int { let v = half ( n ) catch e { // reject: E-OBL-002, E-OBL-001 if n > 100 { recover 0 } } return v } func main ( ) { } The path that falls off the end of the block also leaves e unconsumed, which is E-OBL-001 on the same line ( LR 10.1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#matching-on-failures","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"Matching on failures","m":"human","x":"Matching on failures # match move f over a Failure uses is ErrorName(\u2026) patterns, and because Failure is an open type it ends with otherwise(name) , which receives any other failure and must consume it ( LR 10.5 ). The library's own failures ( numeric.overflow , collection.index , text.index , \u2026) have no declared error a program can name in an is pattern yet (D-58), so test their code instead: if e.code == \"numeric.overflow\" { \u2026 } inside a catch e block, or after match f (a read loan) and before the failure is consumed ( LR 10.3 , LR 10.5 ). Payload fields are bound by copy. An is arm without as discharges the failure when it matches, which adds recovers ; an arm that binds the whole failure with as name must consume name ( LR 10.5 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error NotANumber { code : \"parse.not_a_number\" payload { position : Int } } export error Empty { code : \"parse.empty\" } export error TooLong { code : \"parse.too_long\" payload { length : Int } } pure try func parseDigits ( text : StringView ) Int { let n = text . scalarCount ( ) if n == 0 { fail Empty ( ) } if n > 9 { fail TooLong ( length : n ) } let mut value = 0 let mut i = 0 while i < n { let s = text . scalarAt ( i ) or fail let d = Scalar . digitValue ( s ) if d == null { fail NotANumber ( position : i ) } value = value * 10 or fail value = value + d or fail i = Int . add ( i , 1 ) or fail } return value } recovers func explain ( text : StringView ) String { return match parseDigits ( text ) { success ( v ) => \"value ${v}\" failure ( f ) => match move f { is NotANumber ( position : p ) => \"not a digit at ${p}\" is Empty => \"empty input\" is TooLong as long => { report long give \"too long\" } otherwise ( other ) => { report other give \"unexpected\" } } } } recovers func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( explain ( \"42\" ) ) console . print ( explain ( \"4x2\" ) ) console . print ( explain ( \"\" ) ) console . print ( explain ( \"12345678901\" ) ) } output value 42 not a digit at 1 empty input too long A match f without move is for inspection only: it copies payloads, no arm may consume f , and f must still be consumed afterwards ( LR 10.5 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#reports-annotation-and-aggregation","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"Reports, annotation and aggregation","m":"human","x":"Reports, annotation and aggregation # report f is a statement that consumes a failure and writes it to the current task's failure reporter; with report f as receipt it also binds a Report , a small receipt that cannot be turned back into a Failure , for use in a public response ( LR 10.6 ). Once reported, the failure is gone, so it cannot also be propagated ( LR 10.6 ), and a second use is a use after move ( E-OWN-020 , LR 7.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-OWN-020 export error NotEven { code : \"demo.not_even\" } pure try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 != 0 { fail NotEven ( ) } return n / 2 } try func strict ( n : Int ) Int { let v = half ( n ) catch e { report e fail e // reject: E-OWN-020 } return v } func main ( ) { } report has the io effect, but it is the one io operation that needs no capability: it writes only to the diagnostic plane, which cannot feed anything back into the program ( LR 10.6 , LR 11.7 ). A pure function therefore cannot report; it propagates or recovers ( LR 10.6 ). Two library functions help with context and with many failures ( LR 10.6 ): annotate(move f, note) returns a failure with the same code and payload, the original as its cause and the note added. It is the only way to add context, and it keeps exactly one obligation alive. Failures.aggregate(move list) consumes a List[Failure] and gives one failure whose causes are the inputs: the rule for parallel work and retries. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" payload { key : Int } } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( key : key ) } return key * 10 or fail } pure try func lookupBoth ( a : Int , b : Int ) Int { let x = lookup ( a ) catch e { fail annotate ( move e , \"first key\" ) } let y = lookup ( b ) or fail return x + y or fail } func failures ( keys : List [ Int ] ) Failure { let mut errors : List [ Failure ] = List . default ( ) for k in keys { match lookup ( k ) { success ( v ) => { } failure ( f ) => { errors . push ( move f ) } } } return Failures . aggregate ( move errors ) } func main ( console : Console ) { let total = lookupBoth ( 9 , 1 ) catch e { console . print ( \"failed with ${e.code}\" ) report e recover 0 } console . print ( \"total ${total}\" ) let all = failures ( List . of ( 1 , 7 , 9 ) ) report all console . print ( \"reported\" ) } output failed with store.missing total 0 reported The annotated failure still has the code store.missing ; its record lists the note. Metrics, span status and debug logging take a read loan of a failure and consume nothing ( LR 10.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#a-failing-main","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"A failing main","m":"human","x":"A failing main # When a try main fails, the runtime prints exactly one report of the failure to standard error and the process exits with status 1 ( LR 4.6 ). There is no or die : propagating to main is how a program stops on a failure. Fortis // accept (exit status 1) import fortis . host { Console } export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" payload { key : Int } } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( key : key ) } return key } try func main ( console : Console ) { let a = lookup ( 2 ) or fail console . print ( \"found ${a}\" ) let b = lookup ( 8 ) or fail console . print ( \"found ${b}\" ) } output found 2"},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#failures-as-data-one-report-each","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"Failures as data: one report each","m":"human","x":"Failures as data: one report each # A parser or a validator often has to try every input, keep going past the bad ones, and report each problem once. Recovering each failure would add recovers to every caller up to main ( LR 11.3 ). The alternative keeps every failure: a pure function collects them, annotated with where they came from, into a List[Failure] it is lent editable , and the caller reports each one ( LR 10.6 , LR 10.7 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error NotADigit { code : \"parse.not_a_digit\" payload { position : Int } } pure try func digit ( text : StringView ) Int < 0 .. 9 > { let s = text . scalarAt ( 0 ) or fail let d = Scalar . digitValue ( s ) if d == null { fail NotADigit ( position : 0 ) } return d } // Every input is tried; a failure is kept, annotated with where it came from, for one report each. pure func digits ( inputs : List [ String ] , sink : editable List [ Failure ] ) List [ Int ] { let mut found : List [ Int ] = List . default ( ) for text in inputs { match digit ( text ) { success ( d ) => { found . push ( d ) } failure ( f ) => { sink . push ( annotate ( move f , \"input ${text}\" ) ) } } } return found } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut problems : List [ Failure ] = List . default ( ) let inputs : List [ String ] = List . of ( \"4\" , \"x\" , \"7\" , \"\" ) let found = digits ( inputs , editable problems ) console . print ( \"${found.count()} digits, ${problems.count()} problems\" ) move problems . consumeEach ( fn mut ( f : owned Failure ) Void { report f } ) } output 2 digits, 2 problems digits stays pure : pushing onto a list it was lent is a write through its own editable parameter, which is its footprint, not mutate ( LR 11.3 ). No failure is discarded, so nothing is recovers ; annotate keeps each failure's code and payload and adds the note ( LR 10.6 ). The two reports go to standard error, one record each, with the code ( parse.not_a_digit , and text.index for the empty input) and the note ( LR 10.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#linear-containers","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"Linear containers","m":"human","x":"Linear containers # Preview: linear containers ( LR 10.7 ) arrive in v0.1. The example below is checked and runs today; the rules it shows belong to v0.1. A collection of outcomes, such as List[Outcome[T]] , is linear too: every element must be consumed ( LR 10.7 ). The library says in each signature what happens to linear elements: push(move item) transfers one in, pop() transfers one out as a T? that must itself be consumed, and consumeEach(owned self, fn mut(owned T) Void) consumes the collection, calling the callback once per element, in order, with no early exit ( LR 10.7 ). Outcome.collect(move list) turns a list of outcomes into an outcome of a list, consuming every input ( LR 10.7 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" payload { key : Int } } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( key : key ) } return key } try func main ( console : Console ) { let mut results : List [ Outcome [ Int ] ] = List . default ( ) results . push ( lookup ( 1 ) ) results . push ( lookup ( 8 ) ) results . push ( lookup ( 3 ) ) let mut good = 0 let mut bad = 0 move results . consumeEach ( fn mut [ editable good , editable bad ] ( r : owned Outcome [ Int ] ) Void { match move r { success ( v ) => { good = Int . saturatingAdd ( good , 1 ) } failure ( f ) => { report f bad = Int . saturatingAdd ( bad , 1 ) } } } ) console . print ( \"${good} good, ${bad} bad\" ) let all = Outcome . collect ( List . of ( lookup ( 1 ) , lookup ( 2 ) , lookup ( 3 ) ) ) or fail console . print ( \"collected ${all.count()}\" ) } output 2 good, 1 bad collected 3 Clearing or dropping a collection of linear elements is rejected, while count() and isEmpty() only borrow it ( LR 10.7 ). A generic function may hold values of a type parameter in linear positions only if it declares T: linear , and must then consume them ( LR 10.7 , LR 6.12 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/10-outcomes.html#defects","p":"Outcomes and failures","h":"Defects","m":"human","x":"Defects # A defect is a condition that cannot be represented as an outcome, because the program's assumptions are already broken. The list is closed ( LR 10.8 ): Defect Cause Native code Development VM contract violation in trusted code a trusted adapter breaks its declared contract the process aborts the task ends with a TaskDefect stack exhaustion unbounded recursion abort task terminated out of memory the allocator fails abort task terminated runtime invariant a bug in the VM or the runtime abort task terminated Ordinary Fortis code cannot cause a defect: there is no unwrap , no unchecked index and no assertion that aborts ( LR 10.8 ). Expected conditions, such as a bound, a parse error, I/O or arithmetic overflow, are always outcomes. A defect is never a Failure , is never caught by catch , and produces no value; only the supervisor of a task that ended with one sees it, as TaskCompletion.Defect ( LR 10.8 , LR 12.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#effects-and-contracts","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Effects and contracts","m":"human","x":"Effects and contracts # Human mode. Agent mode: Effects and contracts, by rule . Source of truth: LR 11 . Preview: contracts ( LR 11.5 ) arrive in v0.1. Effects, claims, noalloc and capabilities ( LR 11.1 , 11.2 , 11.3 , 11.4 and 11.6 ) arrive in v0.2. The observation plane and aspects ( LR 11.7 , 11.8 ) arrive in v0.5, except the diagnostic plane that report and the log statements write to ( LR 11.7 , LR 13 ), which is in v0.0.2. main 's capability parameters ( LR 4.6 , 11.6 ) and the core aspect @ignorable ( LR 5.5 , 11.8 ) are in v0.0.2. A reader of a Fortis function wants to know what it may do besides compute its result: allocate, touch the outside world, change something it was not handed, read the clock, wait, fail, or quietly swallow a failure. The checker works this out for every function, from its body and everything it calls, and compares the answer with what the function's head claims. This chapter covers that machinery: the eight effects, the modifiers that state claims about them, contracts that name a set of claims, capabilities that carry the authority to do I/O, and aspects, which add declared behaviour around a function."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#the-eight-effects","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"The eight effects","m":"human","x":"The eight effects # Every function has an effect summary : a subset of a closed set of eight effects, computed from its body and its callees ( LR 11.1 ). No package or configuration can add a ninth. Effect Present when the function may\u2026 alloc allocate heap memory, including inside callees, failures, cleanup and the closures it creates io perform input or output through a capability, including report mutate write to storage that is neither fresh in this call nor reachable from one of its own editable parameters nondet read a non-deterministic source: a clock, randomness, the environment, an address suspend reach an await block block the calling thread, such as waiting for a lock synchronously fail return a failure recovers discharge a failure that was not reported When the summary contains something the function's modifiers do not admit, the function is rejected with E-EFF-001 , which names the effect and the statement that introduces it ( LR 11.1 ). Here a function claims to be pure and prints: Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 import fortis . host { Console } pure func greet ( console : Console ) { // reject: E-EFF-001 (a pure function takes no capability) console . print ( \"hello\" ) // reject: E-EFF-001 } func main ( console : Console ) { greet ( console ) }"},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#fresh-storage-is-not-mutation","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Fresh storage is not mutation","m":"human","x":"Fresh storage is not mutation # mutate means writing to something the caller can see. Writing to storage the function created itself, and has not yet stored anywhere reachable from a parameter, a capture or self , is not mutate ( LR 11.1 ). So building a list locally and returning it is pure: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func squares ( n : Int < 0 .. 10 > ) List [ Int ] { let mut out : List [ Int ] = List . default ( ) for i in 1 .. n { out . push ( i * i ) } return out } func main ( console : Console ) { for s in squares ( 4 ) { console . print ( \"${s}\" ) } } output 1 4 9 16"},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#modifiers-are-claims","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Modifiers are claims","m":"human","x":"Modifiers are claims # The function head carries five words that speak about effects ( LR 11.2 ): Modifier Claim the checker verifies pure none of io , mutate , nondet , suspend , block noalloc no alloc async suspend is permitted try fail is permitted recovers recovers is permitted pure and noalloc are independent: a pure function may allocate, and a noalloc function may do I/O ( LR 11.2 ). The two kinds of modifier are also treated differently. async , try and recovers change a function's type, so a function must write them whenever its summary has the effect ( LR 11.3 ). pure and noalloc are guarantees a function may offer: leaving one off never breaks a caller, it only withholds a promise ( LR 11.3 ). A function that silently swallows a failure must say so. half(n) or recover 0 discharges the failure without reporting it, which is the recovers effect, so the function needs the recovers modifier ( LR 11.2 ): Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 export error Odd { code : \"demo.odd\" } try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 == 1 { fail Odd ( ) } return n / 2 } func halfOrZero ( n : Int ) Int => half ( n ) or recover 0 // reject: E-EFF-001 recovers func main ( ) { discard halfOrZero ( 3 ) } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error Odd { code : \"demo.odd\" } try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 == 1 { fail Odd ( ) } return n / 2 } recovers func halfOrZero ( n : Int ) Int => half ( n ) or recover 0 recovers func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${halfOrZero(8)} ${halfOrZero(3)}\" ) } output 4 0 recovers is contagious in the same way as the other effects: main calls halfOrZero , so it inherits the effect and writes the modifier too. A reviewer reading a signature diff can therefore see every function that swallows a failure somewhere below it ( LR 8.1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#how-summaries-are-computed","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"How summaries are computed","m":"human","x":"How summaries are computed # A function's summary is built from its statements and closed over recursion ( LR 11.3 ). A call imports the callee's summary, with two adjustments: fail is imported only if the call's failure leaves the function. or fail keeps it; or recover , a catch that ends in recover , and a match that consumes the failure remove it and add recovers where the failure was not reported ( LR 11.3 ). mutate is imported through the callee's write footprint , described next."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#write-footprints","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Write footprints","m":"human","x":"Write footprints # A method with editable self , or a function with an editable parameter, writes through a loan its caller handed it. Inside the function that write is not mutate ; it is recorded in the function's write footprint ( LR 11.3 ). At each call the footprint is matched against the actual argument: if the caller's place is fresh, nothing is imported; if it is reachable from the caller's own editable parameter, it joins the caller's footprint; anything else, such as a mutable capture, makes the caller mutate ( LR 11.3 ). This is what lets a pure function build an object with mutating methods: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Cell { export value : Int pure func set ( editable self , value : Int ) { self . value = value } } pure func build ( ) Cell { let mut cell = Cell ( value : 0 ) cell . set ( 1 ) // cell is fresh here, so the footprint imports nothing return cell } func main ( console : Console ) { let cell = build ( ) console . print ( \"${cell.value}\" ) } output 1 The same call through a closure that captured the cell editable writes storage the closure's caller can see, so it is mutate , and a pure function cannot do it ( LR 11.3 ): Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 class Cell { export value : Int pure func set ( editable self , value : Int ) { self . value = value } } pure func viaClosure ( ) Int { let mut cell = Cell ( value : 0 ) let mut poke = fn mut [ editable cell ] ( ) Void { cell . set ( 2 ) } poke ( ) // reject: E-EFF-001 return 0 } func main ( ) { discard viaClosure ( ) }"},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#callbacks-and-lambdas","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Callbacks and lambdas","m":"human","x":"Callbacks and lambdas # A callback parameter contributes the effects its function type permits, not those of whatever argument is passed. A lambda literal whose inferred summary has none of the effects pure excludes is itself pure , so it can be passed where a pure fn is required without writing the modifier ( LR 11.3 ). countWhere takes a pure fn ( LR 6.7 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func countAbove ( items : List [ Int ] , threshold : Int ) Int { return items . countWhere ( fn [ threshold ] ( n : Int ) Bool => n > threshold ) } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${countAbove(List.of(3, 8, 1, 9), 5)}\" ) } output 2"},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#what-crosses-a-module-boundary","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"What crosses a module boundary","m":"human","x":"What crosses a module boundary # Inside a module, callers see each function's inferred summary. Across a module boundary only the written claims count: a caller in another module may rely on pure or noalloc only when the callee writes it ( LR 11.3 ). Removing a written pure or noalloc from an exported function, or adding async , try or recovers , is a change to its public API; fortis check --api-baseline OLD reports it as the warning E-EFF-040 at the declaration ( LR 11.3 ). A few functions in the fortis package have no Fortis body: they are declared trusted and the backend supplies their implementation. A trusted claim is reported as trusted evidence, never as proved , and trusted is rejected outside the fortis package ( LR 11.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#noalloc","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"noalloc","m":"human","x":"noalloc # noalloc claims that a function never allocates ( LR 11.4 ). The checker decides it by matching what the function lowers to against a whitelist of allocation-free patterns ( LR C.3 ), not by inspecting optimised code: integer arithmetic, comparisons, field access and reading a list element are allocation-free, while building a list or a string is not. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure noalloc func clamp ( v : Int , lo : Int , hi : Int ) Int { if v < lo { return lo } if v > hi { return hi } return v } pure noalloc func firstOrZero ( xs : List [ Int ] ) Int => xs . get ( 0 ) ?? 0 func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${clamp(150, 0, 100)} ${firstOrZero(List.of(7, 8))}\" ) } output 100 7 Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 noalloc func pair ( a : Int , b : Int ) List [ Int ] => List . of ( a , b ) // reject: E-EFF-001 func main ( ) { discard pair ( 1 , 2 ) } noalloc is a property under a target plan and a telemetry plan: a library is checked with its own plan, and the build checks the claim again against the application's. If the application's telemetry policy would attach an allocating record to a noalloc function, the build is rejected at the policy ( E-EFF-030 ), rather than the instrumentation being dropped quietly ( LR 11.4 ). For an async function, noalloc covers creating the future, every poll, completion and destruction ( LR 11.4 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#contracts","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Contracts","m":"human","x":"Contracts # A contract names a set of claims so that it can be applied by name ( LR 11.5 ). It may contain only modifiers, forbidden effects ( forbids recovers ), required authorities ( requires capability Clock ) and excluded authorities ( excludes capability Network ). Because a contract is a set of modifiers, it is written where modifiers are written, before the single-word modifiers, and never with @ ( LR 11.5 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export contract FastCalculation { pure noalloc } export FastCalculation pure noalloc func square ( value : Int < - 1000000 .. 1000000 > ) Int => value * value func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${square(-12)}\" ) } output 144 The formatter keeps the contract's name and prints the modifiers it expands to after it, so a reader of formatted code sees both ( LR 11.5 ). A contract only subtracts: applying one never changes what a function does; it makes the checker reject a body that does something the contract rules out ( LR 11.5 ). Here NoSilentRecovery forbids the recovers effect, so the function must handle the failure some other way: Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 export error Odd { code : \"demo.odd\" } try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 == 1 { fail Odd ( ) } return n / 2 } contract NoSilentRecovery { forbids recovers } NoSilentRecovery recovers func quiet ( n : Int ) Int => half ( n ) or recover 0 // reject: E-EFF-001 recovers func main ( ) { discard quiet ( 4 ) } An excludes capability clause is transitive: the function may not take the excluded authority, and may not call, directly or through any callee, a function that requires it ( LR 11.5 ). Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 import fortis . host { Network } contract Offline { excludes capability Network } func lookup ( net : Network , name : StringView ) Int => name . scalarCount ( ) Offline func viaNetwork ( net : Network ) Int => lookup ( net , \"example\" ) // reject: E-EFF-001 func main ( network : Network ) { discard viaNetwork ( network ) } Contracts compose by conjunction, and contradictory ones are rejected; a pure function may not take a capability parameter at all, so pure together with requires capability is a contradiction ( LR 11.5 ). Project configuration never attaches or selects a contract: a contract applies to a function only by appearing in its head ( LR 11.5 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#capabilities","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Capabilities","m":"human","x":"Capabilities # I/O needs authority, and authority is a value. A function with the io effect must take an authority value as a parameter: a nominal, non-copyable value implementing the marker interface Authority ( LR 11.6 ). The process-wide authorities are the capabilities FileSystem , Network , Clock , Random , Environment , Process , Supervisor , Console , SecretAccess and RawSql . The runtime creates them once and hands them to the entry main , which declares the ones it needs as parameters: func main(console: Console, files: FileSystem) ( LR 4.6 , LR 11.6 ). No other function receives them from the runtime, and none can create them. Older code creates them in main with Host.capabilities() , which fortis fmt rewrites into parameters; anywhere else that call is rejected ( LR 11.6 ): Fortis // reject: E-EFF-010 import fortis . host { Host } func sneak ( ) { let caps = Host . capabilities ( ) // reject: E-EFF-010 } func main ( ) { sneak ( ) } From main the authorities are passed down explicitly, so a function's signature names every authority it uses. A function that reaches I/O without taking an Authority value, for example by reaching into the Capabilities record Host.capabilities() returns, is rejected with the same code, at the statement that performs the I/O ( LR 11.6 ): Fortis // reject: E-EFF-010 import fortis . host { Capabilities , Host } func write ( caps : Capabilities , text : StringView ) { caps . console . print ( text ) // reject: E-EFF-010 } func main ( ) { let caps = Host . capabilities ( ) write ( caps , \"hello\" ) } Pass the Console itself; the parameter is the visible statement that this function writes to the console: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func say ( console : Console , text : StringView ) { console . print ( text ) } func main ( console : Console ) { say ( console , \"hello\" ) } output hello Capabilities are attenuated by their own methods, never widened: fs.subtree(path) gives a FileSystem confined below path ( LR 11.6 ). The static rule is only that the authority's type is in the signature; which directory or host a value reaches is decided by the value and enforced by the runtime adapter ( LR 11.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#sharing-one-capability-between-owners","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Sharing one capability between owners","m":"human","x":"Sharing one capability between owners # A capability is not copyable, and no field stores a loan ( LR 7.7 ). When several values each need the console, hold it through an explicit Shared[Console] : a Shared[A] of an authority carries A 's authority, so a parameter of that type satisfies the rule exactly as an A parameter does ( LR 11.6 ). Each retain() is visible where a new owner takes a handle ( LR 7.8 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func announce ( console : Shared [ Console ] , who : StringView ) { console . print ( \"hello from ${who}\" ) } func main ( out : Console ) { let console = Shared . create ( move out ) announce ( console . retain ( ) , \"one\" ) announce ( console , \"two\" ) } output hello from one hello from two Per-activation authorities such as RequestContext , Db or TaskContext are minted by a kit's adapter from a capability for one unit of work and passed to it as a parameter, so every authority still traces back to main ( LR 11.6 ). Reading a clock is nondet and requires a Clock ; a pure function receives the time as an ordinary parameter instead ( LR 11.6 ). Capabilities, effects and target feature sets are three different things: a feature set ( core , alloc , host , async , web , browser ) decides which standard modules exist on a compilation target, an effect is a fact about a function, and a capability is a run-time value ( LR 11.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#the-observation-plane","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"The observation plane","m":"human","x":"The observation plane # Observation is the one place where a function may \"do\" something without its summary changing. The records the compiler generates for capture , record and completion events go to a diagnostic plane that can never feed a value back into the program, so instrumenting a pure function leaves it pure ( LR 11.7 ). Optimisation must preserve a program's results and application effects always, and the records an observation schema declares required as well ( LR 11.7 ). Observation schemas and the two statements are the subject of Observation . report sits on the same plane. It is io , so a pure function cannot report, but it is the one I/O operation that needs no capability value ( LR 10.6 , LR 11.6 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error Odd { code : \"demo.odd\" } try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 == 1 { fail Odd ( ) } return n / 2 } func halfOrReport ( n : Int ) Int { return half ( n ) catch e { report e recover 0 } } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${halfOrReport(10)} ${halfOrReport(3)}\" ) } output 5 0 halfOrReport does not need recovers : the failure was reported before recover , so nothing was swallowed ( LR 10.4 ). The report itself is a structured record on standard error, not part of the program's output."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#aspects","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Aspects","m":"human","x":"Aspects # Where a contract subtracts, an aspect adds: code that runs before a function's body, code that runs after it succeeds, a handler for failures the body leaves unhandled, and metadata a library can read ( LR 11.8 ). Aspects are how kits attach routes, authorisation and limits to ordinary functions, and how a team writes its own conventions. They are ordinary declarations, typed, checked and lowered like any other code ( LR 11.8 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#declaring-an-aspect","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Declaring an aspect","m":"human","x":"Declaring an aspect # aspect name(params) { parts } declares one, optionally with a type parameter R after the name. The name is lowercase, R is bound at each application to the target function's success type, and the parts come from a closed set ( LR 11.8 ): Part Meaning needs name: Type the target must have exactly one parameter of this type; the hooks use it by this name needs Contract the target must carry this contract before { \u2026 } runs before the body after(result: R) { \u2026 } runs after a successful body, with a read loan of the result on failure(f: Failure) R { \u2026 } receives any failure left unhandled, and must return an R or fail metadata marks the arguments as data a library reads through the toolchain There is no around hook and no access to the body: an aspect can never change what a line inside the function means, and cannot run the body twice ( LR 11.8 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#applying-aspects-and-in-which-order-they-run","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Applying aspects, and in which order they run","m":"human","x":"Applying aspects, and in which order they run # @name(args) goes on the lines directly above a function head ( LR 11.8 ). Several aspects elaborate, before any ownership or effect checking, into one wrapper around the body: the before hooks run in application order (first line first), the after hooks in reverse, and the on failure handlers innermost first, so the first line is the outermost wrapper ( LR 11.8 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error TooBig { code : \"demo.too_big\" payload { value : Int } } export aspect logged ( label : StringView ) { needs console : Console before { console . print ( \"before ${label}\" ) } after ( result : Int ) { console . print ( \"after ${label}: ${result}\" ) } } export aspect capped ( limit : Int ) { needs console : Console on failure ( f : Failure ) Int { console . print ( \"capped handles ${f.code}\" ) report f return limit } } @ logged ( \"outer\" ) @ logged ( \"inner\" ) func square ( console : Console , n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int { console . print ( \"body\" ) return n * n } @ capped ( limit : 100 ) try func checked ( console : Console , n : Int ) Int { if n > 100 { fail TooBig ( value : n ) } return n } try func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"square: ${square(console, 7)}\" ) let small = checked ( console , 5 ) or fail let big = checked ( console , 500 ) or fail console . print ( \"${small} ${big}\" ) } output before outer before inner body after inner: 49 after outer: 49 square: 49 capped handles demo.too_big 5 100 The handler reports the failure once and completes the function with limit , so for an observer the call succeeded ( LR 11.8 ). The hooks' effects join the function's summary, and the elaborated function is checked as a whole: a hook that allocates under noalloc , reports under pure , or calls Host.capabilities() is rejected by the ordinary rules ( LR 11.8 ). Nothing of an aspect remains at run time. An application that names an unknown aspect, passes arguments that do not match its declaration, or leaves a needs unmet or ambiguous is E-TYP-150 ( LR 11.8 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-150 import fortis . host { Console } export aspect logged ( label : StringView ) { needs console : Console before { console . print ( \"before ${label}\" ) } } @ logged ( \"count\" ) // reject: E-TYP-150 func count ( n : Int ) Int => n func main ( ) { discard count ( 3 ) } An after hook may fail only when the result type is not linear, because a failing after would leave a linear result with no consumer: E-OBL-008 ( LR 11.8 ). Fortis // reject: E-OBL-008 export error Stop { code : \"demo.stop\" } export aspect strict [ R ] ( ) { after ( result : R ) { fail Stop ( ) // reject: E-OBL-008 } } @ strict ( ) try func produce ( n : Int ) Outcome [ Int ] { return Outcome . success ( n ) } func main ( ) { }"},{"u":"docs/guide/11-effects.html#specialisations-and-the-core-aspect","p":"Effects and contracts","h":"Specialisations and the core aspect","m":"human","x":"Specialisations and the core aspect # aspect name(params, ...rest) = other(fixed: value, ...rest) declares one aspect as another with some arguments fixed; ...rest forwards every remaining named argument ( LR 11.8 ). The core declares exactly one aspect, observe(schema) , which attaches an observation schema to a function; every other aspect is a library ( LR 11.8 , LR 13 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export aspect traced ( label : StringView , depth : Int = 1 ) { needs console : Console before { console . print ( \"enter ${label} at depth ${depth}\" ) } } export aspect topLevel ( label : StringView , ... rest ) = traced ( label : label , depth : 0 , ... rest ) @ topLevel ( \"work\" ) func work ( console : Console , n : Int ) Int => n func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${work(console, 3)}\" ) } output enter work at depth 0 3"},{"u":"docs/guide/12-async.html#cleanup-async-and-concurrency","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","h":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","m":"human","x":"Cleanup, async and concurrency # Human mode. Agent mode: Cleanup, async and concurrency, by rule . Source of truth: LR 12 . Preview: async, cancellation, scopes and sharing ( LR 12.3 , 12.4 , 12.5 , 12.6 and 12.7 ) arrive in v0.3. defer and destruction order ( LR 12.1 , 12.2 ) are in v0.0.1. This chapter is about time: what runs when a block ends, in what order values are destroyed, and how a program does several things at once. Fortis keeps all three visible. Cleanup is a defer block you can read, async work is a Future you must await or launch, concurrent tasks live inside a scope block that cannot end while they run, and data shared between threads is either immutable or behind a Mutex ."},{"u":"docs/guide/12-async.html#defer","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","h":"defer","m":"human","x":"defer # defer { \u2026 } registers a block that runs when the enclosing block exits, by any route: falling off the end, return , fail , break , continue , or cancellation ( LR 12.1 ). Deferred blocks run in reverse order of registration, before the block's local values are dropped. There is only the block form. The classic use is closing what you opened, right next to where you opened it: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { FileSystem , Path , Console } try func copyFile ( fs : FileSystem , source : Path , target : Path ) { let mut input = fs . open ( source ) or fail defer { input . close ( ) } let mut output = fs . create ( target ) or fail defer { output . close ( ) } let data = input . readAll ( ) or fail output . writeAll ( data ) or fail } try func main ( fileSystem : FileSystem , console : Console ) { let source = Path . parse ( \"source.txt\" ) or fail let target = Path . parse ( \"copy.txt\" ) or fail fileSystem . writeAll ( source , \"copied by Fortis\" ) or fail copyFile ( fileSystem , source , target ) or fail let text = fileSystem . readText ( target ) or fail console . print ( text ) } output copied by Fortis Whichever or fail leaves copyFile , every file opened before it is closed, and nothing else is. A defer is checked as if its body were written at every exit of its block ( LR 12.1 ): after the loans held by the block's own bindings have ended and before its locals are dropped. That is why the places it names must still be there at every exit. Moving one of them away between the defer and an exit is E-DEF-002 ( LR 12.1 ): Fortis // reject: E-DEF-002 export class Handle { export open : Bool export func close ( editable self ) { self . open = false } } func keep ( h : owned Handle ) { } func main ( ) { let mut input = Handle ( open : true ) defer { input . close ( ) } keep ( move input ) // reject: E-DEF-002 } A deferred block cannot change how the function ends: return , fail , give , break , continue and await are not allowed inside it ( E-DEF-001 , LR 12.1 ). There is no outcome for it to propagate to, so a failure produced inside it is consumed there, with or recover , catch \u2026 recover or report ; or fail is the same E-DEF-001 ( LR 12.1 ): Fortis // reject: E-DEF-001 export error FlushFailed { code : \"demo.flush\" } try func flush ( ) { fail FlushFailed ( ) } try func work ( n : Int ) Int { defer { flush ( ) or fail // reject: E-DEF-001 } return n } try func main ( ) { discard work ( 1 ) or fail } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error FlushFailed { code : \"demo.flush\" } try func flush ( ) { fail FlushFailed ( ) } func work ( console : Console ) { defer { flush ( ) catch e { report e recover Void } } console . print ( \"work\" ) } func main ( console : Console ) { work ( console ) console . print ( \"done\" ) } output work done The failure is reported, labelled as raised during cleanup, on standard error ( LR 12.1 ). Recovering a cleanup failure without reporting it is allowed too, and adds recovers to the function ( LR 12.1 ). Because the block's own loans have ended by the time a deferred block runs, it may not use a binding of its block that holds a loan: E-OWN-052 ( LR 12.1 ). Fortis // reject: E-OWN-052 export class Buffer { export used : Int export func peek ( self ) alias Buffer from self => alias self } func run ( items : Buffer ) Int { let seen = items . peek ( ) defer { discard seen . used } // reject: E-OWN-052 return seen . used } func main ( ) { discard run ( Buffer ( used : 1 ) ) } defer can be this flexible because it lowers to explicit cleanup code at each exit, not to a closure or a destructor, so no loan has to be held across the block ( LR 12.1 ). defer await is not in Language 1.0; asynchronous cleanup belongs to the owning scope or to explicit await s before the block ends ( LR 12.1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/12-async.html#destruction-order","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","h":"Destruction order","m":"human","x":"Destruction order # At a block exit, the order is always the same ( LR 12.2 , LR 7.9 ): the block's loans end; its deferred blocks run, last registered first; its owned locals are dropped, last initialised first; and a class value that is dropped runs its own cleanup method before its fields are dropped. The order does not depend on how the block was left. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export class Noisy { export name : String export console : Shared [ Console ] cleanup ( editable self ) { self . console . print ( \"drop ${self.name}\" ) } } func steps ( console : Shared [ Console ] , stop : Bool ) { let first = Noisy ( name : \"first\" , console : console . retain ( ) ) defer { console . print ( \"defer one\" ) } let second = Noisy ( name : \"second\" , console : console . retain ( ) ) defer { console . print ( \"defer two\" ) } if stop { console . print ( \"early return\" ) return } console . print ( \"end of body\" ) } func main ( out : Console ) { let console = Shared . create ( move out ) steps ( console . retain ( ) , false ) steps ( console . retain ( ) , true ) } output end of body defer two defer one drop second drop first early return defer two defer one drop second drop first The order is the same on every backend and under every kind of exit, including cancellation at an await ( LR 12.2 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/12-async.html#async-functions-and-futures","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","h":"Async functions and futures","m":"human","x":"Async functions and futures # Calling an async func does not run it. The call evaluates the arguments and produces a Future[T] (or Future[Outcome[T]] for an async try function); the body starts when the future is awaited or launched on a scope ( LR 12.3 ). await suspends the current task until the future completes and gives its value. An async program starts from async func main or async try func main , which runs as the root task on the core cooperative executor ( LR 4.6 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } async func work ( console : Console , n : Int ) Int { console . print ( \"work ${n} runs\" ) return n } async func main ( console : Console ) { let pending = work ( console , 1 ) console . print ( \"future built\" ) let value = await pending console . print ( \"got ${value}\" ) } output future built work 1 runs got 1 An async call looks like a call; the await (or the launch ) is the visible point where the work happens ( LR 1.1 ). await may appear only directly in an async function's body, not in a lambda that is not itself async and not in a defer ( LR 12.3 ). A Future is linear: it must be awaited, launched on a scope, or discarded ( LR 12.3 ). Dropping one at the end of a block is E-OBL-001 , like dropping any linear value ( LR 6.12 ): Fortis // reject: E-OBL-001 async func work ( n : Int ) Int => n async func run ( n : Int ) Int { let pending = work ( n ) // reject: E-OBL-001 return n } async func main ( ) { discard await run ( 1 ) } discard of a future that was never polled is the one exception to the rule that a linear value cannot be discarded: it is allowed when the future's captured environment holds no linear value and no exclusive loan, and it writes a not_started record to the task's reporter ( LR 12.3 , LR 5.5 ). A future holds the loans of its arguments, exactly like a value returned from them, so it cannot outlive the places it borrows ( LR 7.3 , LR 12.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/12-async.html#cancellation","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","h":"Cancellation","m":"human","x":"Cancellation # Cancellation is a signal delivered to a task, observed only at a cancellation point : an await on the cooperative executor, and an await or an explicit checkpoint() on the parallel and blocking pools ( LR 12.4 ). It is not a failure: nothing can catch, recover or match it. A task that observes it unwinds to its boundary, running deferred blocks on the way, and completes as cancelled. Synchronous code always runs to completion. Unwinding runs no user code except defer blocks and abandon methods, so a linear value that is still alive at an await would have nobody to consume it if the task were cancelled there. The rule is therefore that no linear value may be live across a cancellation point unless its type implements Abandonable ( E-OBL-005 , LR 12.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-OBL-005 export error Negative { code : \"demo.negative\" } try func parse ( n : Int ) Int { if n < 0 { fail Negative ( ) } return n } async func pause ( ) { } async try func run ( n : Int ) Int { let pending = parse ( n ) await pause ( ) // reject: E-OBL-005 return pending or fail } async try func main ( ) { discard await run ( 3 ) or fail } Consume the outcome before the await ( let value = parse(n) or fail ), pass it into the awaited call, or restructure ( LR 12.4 ). interface Abandonable { func abandon(owned self) } is the escape hatch: its abandon is synchronous, cannot fail and cannot suspend, and the unwind calls it on every live Abandonable local in reverse initialisation order, after the deferred blocks ( LR 12.4 ). Unwinding means leaving by cancellation or by fail , so an Abandonable local is also the one linear value that may be live at an or fail ; a normal exit must still consume it explicitly. This is exactly what a transaction wants: failure rolls back, success must commit. Fortis // accept (exit status 1) import fortis . host { Console } export error Odd { code : \"demo.odd\" } export class Tx implements Abandonable { witness : Must export console : Shared [ Console ] export func begin ( console : Shared [ Console ] ) Tx { console . print ( \"begin\" ) return Tx ( witness : Must . create ( ) , console : console . retain ( ) ) } export func commit ( owned self ) { self . console . print ( \"commit\" ) let Tx ( witness : w , console : c ) = move self Must . discharge ( move w ) } func abandon ( owned self ) { self . console . print ( \"abandon\" ) let Tx ( witness : w , console : c ) = move self Must . discharge ( move w ) } } try func step ( console : Shared [ Console ] , n : Int ) Int { console . print ( \"step ${n}\" ) if n % 2 == 1 { fail Odd ( ) } return n } try func work ( console : Shared [ Console ] , n : Int ) Int { let tx = Tx . begin ( console ) let v = step ( console , n ) or fail move tx . commit ( ) return v } try func main ( out : Console ) { let console = Shared . create ( move out ) discard work ( console , 2 ) or fail discard work ( console , 1 ) or fail } output begin step 2 commit begin step 1 abandon The Must field makes Tx linear ( LR 6.12 ): the only ways to get rid of one are commit and the unwind's abandon . The second call fails, the failure leaves main , and the program ends with status 1 and one report of the failure on standard error ( LR 4.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/12-async.html#structured-scopes-and-launch","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","h":"Structured scopes and launch","m":"human","x":"Structured scopes and launch # Concurrency in Fortis lives inside a block. scope name { \u2026 } is a block that owns tasks, and name.launch(f) consumes a future and starts it as a Task[T] ( LR 12.5 ). A task is linear: it is awaited inside the scope, or handed to a Supervisor with name.detach(move task, supervisor) . A scope never lets a task outlive it. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } async func measure ( text : StringView ) Int => text . scalarCount ( ) async func measureBoth ( a : StringView , b : StringView ) ( Int , Int ) { scope tasks { let first = tasks . launch ( measure ( a ) ) let second = tasks . launch ( measure ( b ) ) return ( await first , await second ) } } async func main ( console : Console ) { let left = \"cooperative\" let right = \"tasks\" let pair = await measureBoth ( left , right ) console . print ( \"${pair.0} and ${pair.1}\" ) } output 11 and 5 The futures borrow a and b . That is allowed for cooperative launch because the scope joins its tasks before any binding declared in or before it is dropped ( LR 12.5 ). When the scope's block exits by fail , return , break or cancellation while tasks are still running, it cancels them and waits for each to finish, as part of ending the block's loans and before its deferred blocks run ( LR 12.5 ). So await first or fail leaving the block cancels second : Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error Bad { code : \"demo.bad\" } async try func quick ( console : Console ) Int { console . print ( \"quick\" ) fail Bad ( ) } async func late ( console : Console ) Int { console . print ( \"late ran\" ) return 1 } async try func run ( console : Console ) Int { scope tasks { let first = tasks . launch ( quick ( console ) ) let second = tasks . launch ( late ( console ) ) let x = await first or fail return Int . wrappingAdd ( x , await second ) } } async recovers func main ( console : Console ) { let r = await run ( console ) or recover - 1 console . print ( \"recovered ${r}\" ) } output quick recovered -1 late was cancelled before it ever ran, so its line never appears. A task cannot leave its scope, by return , give or assignment to an outer binding: E-ASY-001 ( LR 12.3 ): Fortis // reject: E-ASY-001 async func work ( n : Int ) Int => n async func run ( n : Int ) Task [ Int ] { scope tasks { let t = tasks . launch ( work ( n ) ) return move t // reject: E-ASY-001 } } async func main ( ) { }"},{"u":"docs/guide/12-async.html#three-ways-to-launch","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","h":"Three ways to launch","m":"human","x":"Three ways to launch # Form Runs on Requirements ( LR 12.5 ) s.launch(f) the same cooperative executor and thread the future may borrow any binding declared in or before the scope s.launchParallel(f) the CPU worker pool the future's environment is Shareable and holds no loans s.launchBlocking(work) the bounded blocking pool work is a synchronous fn once() T whose environment is Shareable ; the only place block is allowed inside async code Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } async func sum ( items : owned List [ Int ] ) Int { let mut total = 0 for n in items { total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , n ) } return total } async func pools ( ) ( Int , Int ) { let numbers = List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ) let words : List [ String ] = List . of ( \"a\" , \"b\" , \"c\" ) scope tasks { let parallel = tasks . launchParallel ( sum ( copy numbers ) ) let blocking = tasks . launchBlocking ( fn once [ move words ] ( ) Int => words . count ( ) ) return ( await parallel , await blocking ) } } async func main ( console : Console ) { let counts = await pools ( ) console . print ( \"sum ${counts.0}, words ${counts.1}\" ) } output sum 10, words 3 The values cross to the pools by copy and move . A loan, or a value that is not Shareable , cannot cross: E-ASY-003 ( LR 12.7 ). Without the annotation, List.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\") would be a list of StringView s, views of static text, and views are never Shareable : Fortis // reject: E-ASY-003 async func pools ( ) Int { let words = List . of ( \"a\" , \"b\" , \"c\" ) scope tasks { let blocking = tasks . launchBlocking ( fn once [ move words ] ( ) Int => words . count ( ) ) // reject: E-ASY-003 return await blocking } } async func main ( ) { discard await pools ( ) } The v1 executor runs every cooperative task of one scope tree on one thread, which is why launch never needs Shareable ( LR 12.5 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/12-async.html#detaching-a-task","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","h":"Detaching a task","m":"human","x":"Detaching a task # detach transfers a task's join obligation to the Supervisor capability and removes it from the scope ( LR 12.5 ). The scope then no longer waits for it, and a supervisor observes how it ends through a TaskCompletion ( LR 12.6 ). The core does not say when a detached task runs relative to the end of the program, and the completion of an async main ends the process ( LR 4.6 ), so the example below shows only the hand-over: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console , Supervisor } async func work ( ) Int { return 1 } async func run ( console : Console , supervisor : Supervisor ) { scope tasks { let t = tasks . launch ( work ( ) ) tasks . detach ( move t , supervisor ) console . print ( \"detached one task\" ) } } async func main ( console : Console , supervisor : Supervisor ) { await run ( console , supervisor ) console . print ( \"done\" ) } output detached one task done"},{"u":"docs/guide/12-async.html#join-without-a-scope","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","h":"join without a scope","m":"human","x":"join without a scope # join(a, b) consumes two futures, polls both inside the current task and returns (A, B) once both have completed ( LR 12.5 ). It accepts futures that hold loans and never returns early: a failure produced by one future is simply part of the tuple, so the caller consumes both outcomes. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error Bad { code : \"demo.bad\" } async try func left ( console : Console ) Int { console . print ( \"left\" ) fail Bad ( ) } async func right ( console : Console ) Int { console . print ( \"right\" ) return 2 } async recovers func run ( console : Console ) { let pair = await join ( left ( console ) , right ( console ) ) let ( a , b ) = move pair let text = match move a { success ( v ) => \"left ${v}\" failure ( f ) => match move f { is Bad => \"left failed\" otherwise ( other ) => { report other give \"left failed differently\" } } } console . print ( \"${text}, right ${b}\" ) } async recovers func main ( console : Console ) { await run ( console ) } output left right left failed, right 2"},{"u":"docs/guide/12-async.html#task-completion","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","h":"Task completion","m":"human","x":"Task completion # A child task cannot complete as cancelled or defective without the same event reaching its parent: cancelling a scope cancels every task in it, and a defect in any task terminates the whole scope tree up to its supervisor ( LR 12.5 ). So await task simply gives the body's result. Only the supervisor of a detached task sees how it ended, as a TaskCompletion[T] with three variants ( LR 12.6 ): Variant Meaning Completed(value: T) the body's result, which may itself be an Outcome Cancelled(reason: Cancellation) the task was cancelled Defect(info: TaskDefect) the task was terminated by a defect ( LR 10.8 ) Cancelled and Defect are never seen as a Failure . TaskCompletion[T] is linear when T is, and a supervisor consumes it with match ( LR 12.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/12-async.html#sharing-data-between-tasks","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency","h":"Sharing data between tasks","m":"human","x":"Sharing data between tasks # A value may cross launchParallel or launchBlocking only if its type is Shareable , a structural property ( LR 12.7 ). These types are Shareable : trivial types, String , Bytes and BigInt ; List , Array , Map , OrderedMap , Set , FixedList and FixedMap of shareable elements; classes, enums, tuples and records whose fields are all shareable; Shared[T] , Weak[T] and Mutex[T] where T is shareable, and AtomicInt and AtomicBool ; fn and fn once closures whose captures are all copy or move of shareable values; capability values. Loans and views, MutexGuard , fn mut closures, Future , Task and resources that declare themselves thread-bound are not ( LR 12.7 ). Shared data is immutable unless it sits behind a Mutex[T] or is an atomic ( LR 7.8 , LR 12.7 ). In async code await m.lock() suspends until the lock is free; in synchronous code, including launchBlocking bodies, m.lockBlocking() blocks the thread, which is the block effect ( LR 12.7 ). Both return an outcome holding a MutexGuard[T] , an exclusive loan that unlocks when it is destroyed. A guard cannot be held across an await : E-ASY-002 ( LR 12.3 ). Fortis // reject: E-ASY-002 async func pause ( ) { } async try func run ( m : Mutex [ Int ] ) Int { let g = await m . lock ( ) or fail await pause ( ) // reject: E-ASY-002 return 0 } async try func main ( ) { let m = Mutex . create ( 1 ) discard await run ( m ) or fail } End the guard's block before suspending: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } async func pause ( ) { } async try func run ( m : Mutex [ Int ] ) Int { { let g = await m . lock ( ) or fail } await pause ( ) return 0 } async try func main ( console : Console ) { let m = Mutex . create ( 1 ) let r = await run ( m ) or fail console . print ( \"released and resumed: ${r}\" ) } output released and resumed: 0 AtomicInt and AtomicBool give counters and flags that tasks can share without a Mutex ( LR 12.7 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let counter = AtomicInt . create ( 5 ) let previous = counter . fetchAdd ( 2 ) console . print ( \"${previous} then ${counter.load()}\" ) let flag = AtomicBool . create ( true ) flag . store ( false ) console . print ( \"${flag.load()}\" ) } output 5 then 7 false Because an exclusive loan is never shared and shared data is immutable unless it is behind a Mutex or an atomic, Fortis programs are free of data races by construction, on both the VM and native code. What the language does not promise is that two backends interleave tasks with the same timing ( LR 12.7 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/13-observation.html#observation","p":"Observation","h":"Observation","m":"human","x":"Observation # Human mode. Agent mode: Observation, by rule . Source of truth: LR 13 . Preview: observation schemas, capture and record ( LR 13 ) arrive in v0.5. Log statements, log.info , log.warn and log.error ( LR 13 ), are in v0.0.2. Logs and metrics are usually an afterthought: a line printed here, a counter bumped there, and nobody can say which facts every request is guaranteed to record. Fortis makes observation part of the language. A function declares, with a schema, which facts its activation records; the body assigns them with capture ; and the checker proves that every required fact is assigned on every way out of the function ( LR 13 ). The core defines only three things: the observation schema declaration and the two statements capture and record . Where the records go, how they are redacted, sampled and exported is the Telemetry kit's job, not the language's ( LR 13 , LR 15 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/13-observation.html#declaring-a-schema","p":"Observation","h":"Declaring a schema","m":"human","x":"Declaring a schema # A schema is a declaration with typed fields. Each field says when it is required, and the schema may carry a version (1 when omitted) and an encoding bound ( LR 13 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } observation Checkout { version : 1 items : Int required : all total : Int required : success coupon : String < scalars : 0 .. 16 > required : none encoding : bounded ( 256 ) } @ observe ( Checkout ) pure func checkout ( prices : List [ Int < 0 .. 1000 > ] , coupon : String ) Int { capture items = prices . count ( ) capture coupon = coupon let mut total = 0 for price in prices { total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , price ) } capture total = total return total } func main ( console : Console ) { let prices = List . of ( 120 , 45 , 300 ) console . print ( \"total ${checkout(prices, \"SPRING\")}\" ) } output total 465 required takes one of four values ( LR 13 ): required: The field must be assigned all on every path, before every exit success before every successful exit failure before every failing exit none never; it is optional Every field type must be Encodable , checked recursively like any derivation ( LR 13 , LR 6.10 ). Secrets, authorities, loans, resources, closures, futures and tasks are not Encodable , so none of them can ever appear in a captured value ( LR 13 ). A String field must declare a bound in the schema, here String<scalars: 0..16> ; a longer value is truncated or omitted as the schema says, and the frame records which happened ( LR 13 ). The schema is attached to the function with the core aspect @observe(Checkout) on the line above its head ( LR 13 , LR 11.8 ). Notice that checkout is pure : capturing does not change a function's effects, because the records go to a diagnostic plane that can never feed a value back into the program ( LR 11.7 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/13-observation.html#capture-a-snapshot-into-the-frame","p":"Observation","h":"capture: a snapshot into the frame","m":"human","x":"capture : a snapshot into the frame # capture field = expression copies the value of the expression into the named field of the observation frame of the current activation, the record the function produces when it finishes ( LR 13 ). The copy is taken at the statement, so changing the place afterwards does not change the frame: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } observation Counter { start : Int required : all finish : Int required : all } @ observe ( Counter ) pure func countUp ( start : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int { let mut n : Int < 0 .. 200 > = start capture start = n n = n + 50 capture finish = n return n } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"counted to ${countUp(7)}\" ) } output counted to 57 The frame for that call holds start 7 and finish 57: the first capture copied 7 before n changed. The expression of a capture must be pure, infallible and non-suspending, and of the field's declared type; it takes a read loan for the statement only ( LR 13 ). A fallible call or an await is written on its own line first, and the binding is captured."},{"u":"docs/guide/13-observation.html#what-you-see-when-it-runs","p":"Observation","h":"What you see when it runs","m":"human","x":"What you see when it runs # The frame is not printed by your program; it is a record for the task reporter. By default the reporter writes each frame's schema and outcome to standard error and never a captured field value: values leave the process only through the Telemetry kit's pipeline, after redaction ( LR 15 ). To see the values while developing, run with the development plan FORTIS_TELEMETRY=permit-all , which writes every field unchanged to the record stream on standard error ( LR 15 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/13-observation.html#every-required-field-on-every-exit","p":"Observation","h":"Every required field, on every exit","m":"human","x":"Every required field, on every exit # The checker treats the frame as a set of variables that must be definitely assigned, over every path of the function, including the paths through fail , return , await and cancellation ( LR 13 ). A required field that is left unassigned on an exit it applies to is E-OBS-001 , reported at that exit ( LR 13 ): Fortis // reject: E-OBS-001 export error Rejected { code : \"shop.rejected\" } observation Payment { amount : Int required : all reason : Int required : failure } @ observe ( Payment ) try func pay ( amount : Int < 0 .. 10000 > ) Int { capture amount = amount if amount == 0 { fail Rejected ( ) // reject: E-OBS-001 } return amount } recovers func main ( ) { let paid = pay ( 10 ) or recover 0 } reason is required on failure, and the fail leaves without it. Capturing it before the fail fixes the function: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error Rejected { code : \"shop.rejected\" } observation Payment { amount : Int required : all reason : Int required : failure } @ observe ( Payment ) try func pay ( amount : Int < 0 .. 10000 > ) Int { capture amount = amount if amount == 0 { capture reason = 1 fail Rejected ( ) } return amount } recovers func main ( console : Console ) { let paid = pay ( 10 ) or recover 0 let refused = pay ( 0 ) or recover - 1 console . print ( \"paid ${paid}, refused ${refused}\" ) } output paid 10, refused -1 An await is a cancellation point, so it is an exit too: a required: all field must be captured before the first await that could cancel the activation ( LR 13 , LR 12.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-OBS-001 import fortis . host { Clock } observation Wait { tier : Int required : all } @ observe ( Wait ) async func slow ( clock : Clock , tier : Int ) Int { await clock . sleep ( 5ms ) // reject: E-OBS-001 capture tier = tier return tier } async func main ( clock : Clock ) { discard await slow ( clock , 2 ) }"},{"u":"docs/guide/13-observation.html#one-capture-per-field-and-none-in-loops","p":"Observation","h":"One capture per field, and none in loops","m":"human","x":"One capture per field, and none in loops # A field is captured at most once on any path: a second capture of the same field is E-OBS-010 ( LR 13 ). A capture inside a loop body is E-OBS-011 , because the loop might run it many times ( LR 13 ): Fortis // reject: E-OBS-011 observation Batch { last : Int required : none } @ observe ( Batch ) pure func sum ( items : List [ Int < 0 .. 100 > ] ) Int { let mut total = 0 for item in items { capture last = item // reject: E-OBS-011 total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , item ) } return total } func main ( ) { discard sum ( List . of ( 1 , 2 ) ) } Repeated measurements are events, and events are what record is for."},{"u":"docs/guide/13-observation.html#record-events","p":"Observation","h":"record: events","m":"human","x":"record : events # record Name(field: value, \u2026) emits an event of the named schema at once, every time it runs, so it belongs inside loops ( LR 13 ). Its arguments have the same purity requirement as a capture . Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } observation Batch { count : Int required : all } observation Item { value : Int required : all } @ observe ( Batch ) pure func sum ( items : List [ Int < 0 .. 100 > ] ) Int { capture count = items . count ( ) let mut total = 0 for item in items { record Item ( value : item ) total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , item ) } return total } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"sum ${sum(List.of(4, 5, 6))}\" ) } output sum 15 A capture or record whose expression has an effect, or that names a field the attached schema does not declare, is E-OBS-012 ; so is a capture in a function with no schema attached, since there is no field it could name ( LR 13 ): Fortis // reject: E-OBS-012 observation Batch { count : Int required : all } @ observe ( Batch ) pure func size ( items : List [ Int ] ) Int { capture count = items . count ( ) capture colour = 3 // reject: E-OBS-012 return items . count ( ) } func main ( ) { discard size ( List . of ( 1 ) ) }"},{"u":"docs/guide/13-observation.html#logs","p":"Observation","h":"Logs","m":"human","x":"Logs # log.info , log.warn and log.error write a log record: its level, its message and the function that wrote it ( LR 13 , X-345). Logging is the platform's concern, not the program's, so a log needs no capability and no setup: the record goes to standard error, as text on a terminal and as one JSON line on a pipe, where the platform's log collection picks it up. It never mixes into standard output, which stays the program's own product: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func total ( prices : List [ Int ] ) Int { log . info ( \"totalling ${prices.count()} prices\" ) let mut sum = 0 for p in prices { sum = Int . wrappingAdd ( sum , p ) } return sum } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"total ${total(List.of(3, 4, 5))}\" ) } output total 12 A log statement has the io effect, like report , so a pure function cannot log. A failure is recorded with report e ; a log is for what is not a failure, such as a fallback taken, progress or a summary. log is not a keyword: a program that already names something log keeps it, and there log.info(...) is an ordinary call."},{"u":"docs/guide/13-observation.html#what-the-compiler-adds","p":"Observation","h":"What the compiler adds","m":"human","x":"What the compiler adds # Source never writes an activation's start, end, duration or outcome class: the compiler generates them, so they cannot be forged ( LR 13 ). An activation that is cancelled before it starts produces a frame marked not_started with no captured fields ( LR 13 ). Two backends do not yet do all of this, and both gaps are recorded as defects: no backend supplies the duration yet (D-28), and native code writes no not_started frame (D-27). Optimisation must keep every record that a schema declares required, so the trace you get from an optimised build is the trace the source promises; instrumentation that is not required may be merged or elided ( LR 11.7 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/14-tests.html#tests","p":"Tests","h":"Tests","m":"human","x":"Tests # Human mode. Agent mode: Test declarations, by rule . Source of truth: LR 14 . Preview: property tests and shrinking ( LR 14 ) arrive in v0.2. Example tests and cases tables are in v0.0.1. Tests in Fortis are declarations, part of the core grammar, written in the same module as the code they test. Every package can be tested without any kit, and the same runner handles example tests, tables, properties and fuzzing ( LR 14 ). Test declarations are compiled only in test builds, so they cost a production binary nothing, and they may import modules meant only for tests ( LR 14 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/14-tests.html#a-first-test","p":"Tests","h":"A first test","m":"human","x":"A first test # A test has a name and a body. The body behaves like the body of a try function, and a failure that escapes it fails the test; a call that can fail needs no or fail there, because its failure fails the test anyway ( or fail is implied , LR 14 ). expect condition checks a Bool , records what it compared, and fails the test when the condition is false ( LR 14 ). Fortis // accept export error Negative { code : \"demo.negative\" } pure try func root ( n : Int < - 10000 .. 10000 > ) Int { if n < 0 { fail Negative ( ) } let mut r : Int < 0 .. 101 > = 0 while r < 100 && ( r + 1 ) * ( r + 1 ) <= n { r = r + 1 } return r } test \"square roots round down\" { expect root ( 16 ) == 4 expect root ( 17 ) == 4 let zero = root ( 0 ) expect zero == 0 } expect takes a Bool and nothing else, the same rule as an if condition ( LR 14 , LR 5.2 ): Fortis // reject: E-TYP-010 pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * 2 test \"doubling\" { expect double ( 2 ) // reject: E-TYP-010 } The record an expect writes carries the actual and the expected value, so a failing test says what it saw instead of only \"assertion failed\" ( LR 14 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/14-tests.html#expecting-a-failure","p":"Tests","h":"Expecting a failure","m":"human","x":"Expecting a failure # To test that something fails, consume its failure inside the test with catch and inspect it there. Expecting a failure consumes its obligation, exactly as handling it in ordinary code would ( LR 14 , LR 10.4 ): Fortis // accept export error Empty { code : \"cart.empty\" } pure try func average ( total : Int < 0 .. 100000 > , count : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int { if count == 0 { fail Empty ( ) } return total / count } test \"an empty cart has no average\" { let result = average ( 10 , 0 ) catch e { expect e . code == \"cart.empty\" recover - 1 } expect result == - 1 } test \"a full cart averages\" { expect average ( 10 , 4 ) == 2 } To check a failure's payload as well as its code, match the failure inside the catch block through a read loan ( match e , not match move e ): that is inspection only, so it consumes nothing, and the block still ends in recover ( LR 10.5 , LR 10.4 ): Fortis // accept export error NotEven { code : \"demo.not_even\" payload { value : Int } } pure try func half ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int { if n % 2 == 1 { fail NotEven ( value : n ) } return n / 2 } test \"an odd number is refused with its value\" { let r = half ( 7 ) catch e { expect e . code == \"demo.not_even\" match e { is NotEven ( value : v ) => { expect v == 7 } otherwise ( other ) => { expect false } } recover - 1 } expect r == - 1 }"},{"u":"docs/guide/14-tests.html#tables","p":"Tests","h":"Tables","m":"human","x":"Tables # When one check runs over many inputs, write the inputs as a table. cases { \u2026 } holds named rows, and as row binds each row explicitly to the name you choose; nothing else enters scope ( LR 14 ). Fortis // accept pure func grade ( score : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) StringView from static { if score >= 70 { return \"distinction\" } if score >= 40 { return \"pass\" } return \"fail\" } test \"grade boundaries\" cases { \"zero\" : { score : 0 , expected : \"fail\" } \"just below pass\" : { score : 39 , expected : \"fail\" } \"pass\" : { score : 40 , expected : \"pass\" } \"distinction\" : { score : 70 , expected : \"distinction\" } \"top\" : { score : 100 , expected : \"distinction\" } } as row { expect grade ( row . score ) == row . expected } Each row is its own case with its own result, so one wrong row does not hide the others. The type of row is inferred from how the body uses its fields, and then every row is checked against it at compile time ( LR 14 ). Because row.score is passed to an Int<0..100> parameter, a row with score: 150 is checked exactly as the literal 150 would be there, and rejected as out of range ( E-REF-001 , LR 14 , LR 3.5 ): Fortis // reject: E-REF-001 pure func percent ( part : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int => part test \"percent\" cases { \"low\" : { part : 0 , expected : 0 } \"too high\" : { part : 150 , expected : 150 } // reject: E-REF-001 } as row { expect percent ( row . part ) == row . expected } The binding is required: a table without as name is not a sentence of the grammar ( E-SYN-000 , LR 14 , LR 3.2 ), because a test whose body used score with no visible declaration would be one more thing a reader has to guess. Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * 2 test \"doubling\" cases { \"one\" : { n : 1 , expected : 2 } } { // reject: E-SYN-000 expect double ( n ) == expected }"},{"u":"docs/guide/14-tests.html#properties","p":"Tests","h":"Properties","m":"human","x":"Properties # A property states something that must hold for every input of a type. The runner draws the inputs from the parameter types, refinements included, so Int<0..1000> only ever produces values in that range ( LR 14 ): Fortis // accept pure func clampTo ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > , high : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int { if n > high { return high } return n } property \"clamping never exceeds the bound\" forall ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > , high : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) { expect clampTo ( n , high ) <= high } property \"reversing twice is the identity\" forall ( items : List [ Int < 0 .. 100 > ] ) { expect items . reversed ( ) . reversed ( ) == items } When a property fails, the runner shrinks the input to a minimal one that still fails the same way, inside the valid domain, and reports it with the seed and the command that replays it ( LR 14 , X-131)."},{"u":"docs/guide/14-tests.html#fuzzing","p":"Tests","h":"Fuzzing","m":"human","x":"Fuzzing # fortisc test --fuzz turns every property into a coverage-guided fuzz target (the interpreter's fortis test does not take --fuzz yet, D-59): it mutates inputs from a corpus, keeps any input that reaches a new branch, and saves the corpus beside the package so the next run starts from it ( LR 14 , X-131). There is no separate fuzz declaration; the keyword fuzz stays reserved (X-131)."},{"u":"docs/guide/14-tests.html#running-tests","p":"Tests","h":"Running tests","m":"human","x":"Running tests # Each backend has a command ( LR 14 names the runner): Command Runs the tests on Notes fortis test FILE or fortis test DIR the definitional interpreter the reference behaviour fortisc test FILE or fortisc test DIR the development VM the full runner: --test NAME , --case NAME , --seed S , --fuzz , --replay FILE , --cases N fortisc native FILE --tests native code builds the tests into an executable and runs it While you iterate, run the tests on the VM: the interpreter is the reference behaviour, and it is much slower (about a hundred times on a large package), so fortisc test gives the fast loop and fortis test the final word. Every positive example in this guide with tests is run on all three; the few that a backend still fails are listed against their recorded defects (D-53, D-54, D-55), and the guide shows what the language requires. Selecting, replaying and fuzzing work in fortisc test : a failing property's record names the case and its seed, and fortisc test FILE --test NAME --seed S reruns exactly that case. The record writes the command as fortis test \u2026 , but the interpreter's fortis test does not yet take these options and does not shrink (D-59), so run them with fortisc test ."},{"u":"docs/guide/14-tests.html#or-fail-is-implied","p":"Tests","h":"or fail is implied","m":"human","x":"or fail is implied # A test can only react to a failure it did not expect by failing, so in test and property bodies or fail is implied: a call of a fallible function has its success type, and its failure fails the test with its record ( LR 14 ). So expect root(16) == 4 checks the root, and fails the test if root fails. A call that or , catch or match consumes keeps its outcome, so expecting a failure is as explicit as ever, and outside tests nothing changes. Integer arithmetic works the same way: an operation the checker cannot prove safe fails the test if it overflows, so expect a * b == c needs no handler ( LR 6.4 ). Older tests write expect (root(16) or fail) == 4 ; the explicit form is still accepted, and fortis fmt rewrites it. Fortis // accept export error Negative { code : \"demo.negative\" } pure try func root ( n : Int < - 10000 .. 10000 > ) Int { if n < 0 { fail Negative ( ) } let mut r : Int < 0 .. 101 > = 0 while r < 100 && ( r + 1 ) * ( r + 1 ) <= n { r = r + 1 } return r } test \"square roots, with or fail implied\" { expect root ( 16 ) == 4 let zero = root ( 0 ) expect zero == 0 }"},{"u":"docs/guide/15-runtime.html#the-core-runtime","p":"The core runtime","h":"The core runtime","m":"human","x":"The core runtime # Human mode. Agent mode: Core runtime inventory, by rule . Source of truth: LR 15 . Preview: the core runtime inventory ( LR 15 ) arrives in v0.4. Fortis is not only a compiler. A conforming implementation ships a small runtime with it, so that a program that imports no kit at all can still start, run tasks, read files, print, fail cleanly and report what happened. Kits add adapters and policy on top of these pieces; they never replace them ( LR 15 ). This chapter walks through the pieces: the executor, the task reporter, the authorities, the entry point, the standard modules and secrets."},{"u":"docs/guide/15-runtime.html#the-executor","p":"The core runtime","h":"The executor","m":"human","x":"The executor # There is one cooperative executor per process. It runs the root task, which is main when main is async , and every scope the program opens; beside it the core provides a parallel pool and a bounded blocking pool, and the signalling for cancellation and deadlines ( LR 15 , LR 12.5 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Clock , Console } async func pause ( clock : Clock , console : Console ) { console . print ( \"sleeping\" ) await clock . sleep ( 10ms ) console . print ( \"awake\" ) } async func main ( clock : Clock , console : Console ) { await pause ( clock , console ) console . print ( \"main done\" ) } output sleeping awake main done An async entry runs on the core cooperative executor as the root task, and its completion ends the process ( LR 4.6 ). A minimal synchronous program starts no executor it did not ask for. Cleanup, async and concurrency covers scopes, launches and cancellation."},{"u":"docs/guide/15-runtime.html#the-task-reporter","p":"The core runtime","h":"The task reporter","m":"human","x":"The task reporter # Some output is not the program's: a failure that report writes, a failure raised during cleanup, a not_started record for work cancelled before it began, and the frames that capture fills. All of it goes to the task reporter , the sink of the diagnostic plane, which can never feed anything back into the program ( LR 15 , LR 11.7 ). By default the reporter writes reports, and each frame's schema identity, outcome class and duration, to standard error. It never writes a captured field value: field values leave the process only through the Telemetry kit's pipeline, after redaction, and without that kit they are kept in memory for the debugger and then discarded ( LR 15 ). While developing you can choose the plan permit-all by setting FORTIS_TELEMETRY=permit-all , which puts every field on the record stream unchanged; it is an explicit choice, like any plan ( LR 15 ). Every record is written as text on a terminal and as one JSON line otherwise, so a program run inside an agent session is as easy to query as one run under an exporter ( LR 4.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/15-runtime.html#authorities-and-capabilities","p":"The core runtime","h":"Authorities and capabilities","m":"human","x":"Authorities and capabilities # Input and output need authority, and authority is a value. The core defines the marker interface Authority and ten capabilities , which are process-wide authorities: FileSystem , Network , Clock , Random , Environment , Process , Supervisor , Console , SecretAccess and RawSql ( LR 15 , LR 11.6 ). The runtime creates them once, at start, and supplies each one the entry main declares as a parameter ( LR 4.6 , LR 11.6 ). From there they are passed, as ordinary parameters, to whatever needs them. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { FileSystem , Path , Console , Network , Process , Clock , Random } try func saveAndLoad ( fs : FileSystem ) String { let path = Path . parse ( \"notes.txt\" ) or fail fs . writeAll ( path , \"first line\" ) or fail return fs . readText ( path ) or fail } try func main ( fileSystem : FileSystem , console : Console , network : Network , process : Process , clock : Clock , random : Random ) { let text = saveAndLoad ( fileSystem ) or fail console . print ( \"read back: ${text}\" ) let address = network . resolve ( \"localhost\" ) or fail console . print ( \"localhost is ${address}\" ) let status = process . run ( \"true\" , List . of ( ) ) or fail console . print ( \"true exits ${status}\" ) let frozen = clock . frozen ( Timestamp . epoch ( ) ) console . print ( \"a frozen clock reads ${frozen.now()}\" ) let seeded = random . seeded ( 42 ) console . print ( \"seeded draw: ${seeded.below(100)}\" ) } output read back: first line localhost is 127.0.0.1 true exits 0 a frozen clock reads 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z seeded draw: 13 saveAndLoad can touch the file system because, and only because, it takes a FileSystem . A reader can see from its signature everything it may reach; a function whose body reaches I/O through a capability without taking an authority is rejected ( E-EFF-010 , LR 11.6 ), and so is a call to Host.capabilities() , the way older code creates the capabilities in main , anywhere but main . report and the log statements are the exceptions: they need the io effect but no capability, because they write only to the diagnostic plane ( LR 11.6 , LR 10.6 , LR 13 ). Fortis // reject: E-EFF-010 import fortis . host { Host } func sneak ( ) { let caps = Host . capabilities ( ) // reject: E-EFF-010 } func main ( ) { sneak ( ) } The capability types and their core operations are part of the core runtime inventory ( LR 15 ): Capability Core operations FileSystem readText , writeAll , readAll , open , create , subtree , and the File that open and create return Network resolve Clock now , frozen , sleep , elapsedSince Random below(bound: Int<1..>) , seeded Environment get Process run Console print RawSql execute Supervisor , SecretAccess no operations of their own: they authorise scope.detach ( LR 12.5 ) and reveal fortis.host also defines Path , built from text by Path.parse , which fails on invalid text ( LR 15 ). Kits add the remaining operations, attenuation helpers and the adapters behind them ( LR 15 ). Per-activation authorities are the other kind: RequestContext , Identity , Db , Tx , TaskContext and Mail , each minted by its kit's adapter from a capability, for one unit of work, and handed to it as a parameter. The core states only the rule; the kits define the types ( LR 15 , LR 11.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/15-runtime.html#the-development-adapters","p":"The core runtime","h":"The development adapters","m":"human","x":"The development adapters # The reference toolchain's adapters are built for development and tests, and each one's contract is written above it in fortis.host : the file system is rooted in a fresh empty directory for each run, or in the directory FORTIS_DEV_ROOT names (a path relative to where the command runs), on every backend, so a program's input files live in a directory of their own, given with FORTIS_DEV_ROOT=data fortis run app ; the network is offline except that localhost resolves, Random is a seeded generator ( FORTIS_DEV_SEED ), Process only simulates true and false , and RawSql has no database. These are the development adapters' behaviour, not rules of the language. The language's part is the pair of core operations Clock.frozen and Random.seeded ( LR 15 ); with them, the example above prints the same thing on every run."},{"u":"docs/guide/15-runtime.html#the-entry-point","p":"The core runtime","h":"The entry point","m":"human","x":"The entry point # main comes in four forms: plain, try , async and async try ( LR 4.6 , LR 15 ). If a try main fails, the runtime writes exactly one report of the failure to standard error and exits with status 1 ( LR 4.6 ). Here the development file system starts empty, so the file is not there: Fortis // accept (exit status 1) import fortis . host { Path , Console , FileSystem } try func main ( console : Console , fileSystem : FileSystem ) { console . print ( \"reading config.txt\" ) let path = Path . parse ( \"config.txt\" ) or fail let text = fileSystem . readText ( path ) or fail console . print ( text ) } output reading config.txt The one report on standard error names the failure's code, fs.not_found , its payload (the path) and the function it came from. There is no or die : a program that wants to stop on a failure propagates it to main ( LR 4.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/15-runtime.html#the-standard-modules","p":"The core runtime","h":"The standard modules","m":"human","x":"The standard modules # The core package fortis provides these modules; everything else under fortis.\u2026 belongs to a kit ( LR 15 , LR 4.3 ). The core library lists every operation they export, with its contract. The text and collection operations are few: there is no line splitting, trimming, searching or number parsing yet, and a program writes those itself (D-58): Module Provides fortis.prelude the core types, the optional and outcome operators, the built-in interfaces; imported into every module fortis.text Builder , Scalar , grapheme counts and Text.words ; LR 6.6 also places normalisation and case mapping here, which the library does not have yet (D-58) fortis.collections sorted , for a list of Int ( LR 6.7 ) fortis.numeric abs , min and max ; LR 6.5 places transcendental functions in fortis.numeric.math , which does not exist yet (D-58) fortis.time Duration and Timestamp operations fortis.money Money : a Decimal amount with a run-time Currency ; arithmetic fails on a currency mismatch fortis.host the capabilities, Path , Secret[T] and reveal , and Host.capabilities() , which older code calls in main fortis.time gives Duration.days , hours , minutes , seconds and milliseconds , the fallible Duration.add , sub and multiply , comparison operators on Duration , and Timestamp.epoch , add , after and before . A Duration displays in the largest unit that divides it exactly ( LR 15 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } try func main ( console : Console ) { let half = Duration . milliseconds ( 500 ) let total = Duration . add ( half , Duration . seconds ( 1 ) ) or fail console . print ( \"${Duration.minutes(90)} ${total} ${half}\" ) let later = Timestamp . add ( Timestamp . epoch ( ) , Duration . days ( 1 ) ) or fail console . print ( \"${Timestamp.after(later, Timestamp.epoch())}\" ) } output 90min 1500ms 500ms true"},{"u":"docs/guide/15-runtime.html#secrets","p":"The core runtime","h":"Secrets","m":"human","x":"Secrets # Environment.get does not return a String : it returns a Secret[String]? , because environment variables are where credentials live ( LR 15 ). A Secret[T] has no codec, cannot be captured into an observation frame, and displays as a per-process keyed fingerprint, never its value. Reading the value is reveal(secret, cap) , which needs the SecretAccess capability, so every place a secret is opened is visible in a signature ( LR 15 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { reveal , Environment , SecretAccess , Console } func main ( environment : Environment , secretAccess : SecretAccess , console : Console ) { let home = environment . get ( \"HOME\" ) if home != null { let value = reveal ( home , secretAccess ) console . print ( \"HOME is set: ${value.byteCount() > 0}\" ) } } output HOME is set: true Declassifying, sanitising and authentication adapters belong to the Security kit ( LR 15 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/16-kits.html#kits-and-declaration-heads","p":"Kits and declaration heads","h":"Kits and declaration heads","m":"human","x":"Kits and declaration heads # Human mode. Agent mode: Reserved declaration heads for kits, by rule . Source of truth: LR 16 . Preview: declaration heads and kits ( LR 16 ) arrive in v0.5. The core language has no HTTP server, no database layer, no web components and no dependency injection. Those come in kits : named, versioned bundles of packages that ship with Fortis, such as the HTTP kit, the Data kit and the Web UI kit. A kit is a library. It extends Fortis only through ordinary packages, aspects, declaration heads, embedding bindings and verifiers; it never changes what a core program means. This chapter is about the two ways a kit's syntax reaches your module, aspects and declaration heads, and about what the core reserves for them ( LR 16 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/16-kits.html#aspects-need-no-special-syntax","p":"Kits and declaration heads","h":"Aspects need no special syntax","m":"human","x":"Aspects need no special syntax # Most of what a kit adds is function-shaped: a route, an authorisation check, a request limit, a schema for a response. Kits express those as aspects, declared in ordinary modules and applied with @name(args) ( LR 16 , LR 11.8 ). An aspect is an ordinary declaration, imported by name like any other, so it needs no grammar of its own; your own packages can declare aspects in exactly the same way: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } aspect logged ( label : StringView ) { needs console : Console before { console . print ( \"enter ${label}\" ) } } @ logged ( \"work\" ) func work ( console : Console , n : Int ) Int => n func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"result ${work(console, 3)}\" ) } output enter work result 3 Effects and contracts explains the parts of an aspect and how it is elaborated around the function it is applied to."},{"u":"docs/guide/16-kits.html#declaration-heads","p":"Kits and declaration heads","h":"Declaration heads","m":"human","x":"Declaration heads # Some things a kit declares are not functions: an application with its lifecycle, a data model, a component with state and actions, a dependency-injection context. For those a kit uses a declaration head : a fixed keyword that introduces a declaration whose body is validated by a versioned schema and elaborated , before type checking, into ordinary core declarations ( LR 16 ). The core reserves every head word in advance, so that no identifier in any module can ever collide with a head in any kit ( LR 16 ): Head Kit Elaborates to application Runtime host a generated main , capability acquisition and lifecycle ordering explore Developer tooling an exploration declaration context (with provide inside its body) and the inject class member Dependency injection constructor parameters and a generated scope struct model Data a nominal data class, schema, codecs, query metadata view and component (with state , action , view and resource inside its body) Web UI a render function; a class with an explicit state struct, action functions and a render function observation the core itself ( Observation ); consumed by Telemetry a frame type and schema The table is LR 16 's. observation is the one head the core defines and uses itself, which is why you have already met it. The elaboration contract is what keeps kits honest: a head produces classes, functions, enums, contracts and observation schemas that obey every rule of the Language Reference, and nothing else ( LR 16 ). Because elaboration happens before ownership and effect checking, a kit's declarations have no ownership or effect rules of their own; the generated code is checked like code you wrote by hand ( LR 16 ). And packages cannot add heads, tokens or grammar rules ( LR 16 ): the set of heads is fixed by the core."},{"u":"docs/guide/16-kits.html#head-words-are-reserved","p":"Kits and declaration heads","h":"Head words are reserved","m":"human","x":"Head words are reserved # Every head word, the prefixes server , client and hybrid , and the words used inside head bodies ( inject , provide , state , action , resource ) are reserved words, even in a module that imports no kit ( LR 16 , LR A ). Using one as the name of a binding, a parameter, a field or a function is E-SYN-002 , as for any keyword ( LR 3.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-SYN-002 export class Page { export view : Int // reject: E-SYN-002 } func main ( ) { } The one relaxation is the same as for every keyword: after a . , and as the name of a method or an associated function, a keyword is an ordinary name ( LR 3.4 ). That is how a library can offer page.view() or Page.model() : Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export class Page { export title : String export func view ( self ) String => \"<h1>${self.title}</h1>\" export func model ( ) Page => Page ( title : \"home\" ) } func main ( console : Console ) { let page = Page . model ( ) console . print ( page . view ( ) ) } output <h1>home</h1> Head words are never imported: importing a kit's package makes its heads available, while its aspects are imported by name like any other declaration ( LR 16 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/16-kits.html#raw-template-regions","p":"Kits and declaration heads","h":"Raw template regions","m":"human","x":"Raw template regions # A head body is a balanced sequence of ordinary Fortis tokens, with one exception: a raw template region ( LR 16 ). A statement made of view (optionally after export and one of server , client or hybrid ), an optional name and parameter group, and a { , opens a region; the lexer takes the text from that { to the } that balances it, counting every brace including those of ${\u2026} interpolations, and hands the whole text to the Web UI kit unlexed, as one token ( LR 16 ). The region is recognised by its lexical shape alone, with no knowledge of the declaration around it, and the kit validates where it may appear. Template syntax is therefore the Web UI kit's to define, and it never adds a token to the core ( LR 16 ). The embedded-language blocks being designed in SP-23 generalise this: a template would become an html block with typed holes, one mechanism for every embedded language ( Embedded languages )."},{"u":"docs/guide/16-kits.html#the-four-extension-slots","p":"Kits and declaration heads","h":"The four extension slots","m":"human","x":"The four extension slots # A kit's syntax can enter a module in exactly four places, and nowhere else ( LR 16 ): a head block, optionally prefixed by server , client or hybrid ; an [export] inject [lazy] name: Type member inside a class body, where an exported slot becomes an exported field, so a consumer declared in one module can be constructed by a context in another (X-138); aspects, declared by the kit and applied with @name(args) ; they need no slot of their own, being ordinary declarations; the cases table syntax of test declarations ( Tests ). A reader who knows these four slots knows every place where a kit can change how a module reads."},{"u":"docs/guide/README.html#the-fortis-guide","p":"The Fortis guide","h":"The Fortis guide","m":"human","x":"The Fortis guide # Human mode. Agent mode: the Fortis reference for agents . Source of truth: the Language Reference . This guide teaches Fortis Language 1.0, the core language, from the beginning. It is written for people: each chapter explains what a construct is for, shows it working, and shows what the checker says when it is misused. Its companion, the agent mode , states the same rules densely, by rule ID, with the records the tools print and the corrections that fix them, for AI agents writing Fortis. Neither mode replaces the Language Reference (\"the LR\"), which is the source of truth. Every rule in this guide cites the LR section that states it, written LR 7.3 ; if the guide and the LR ever disagree, the LR wins and the guide has a defect ( LR 0 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/README.html#chapters","p":"The Fortis guide","h":"Chapters","m":"human","x":"Chapters # Start with the tour, then read in order; each chapter follows one section of the LR. Chapter LR A tour of Fortis : what the language is for, the tools, and a whole program 1, 2 Lexical structure 3 Modules and packages 4 Statements and expressions 5 Types 6 Proving arithmetic and indexing 6.3, 6.4, 6.7 Ownership 7 Functions and closures 8 Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums 9 Outcomes and failures 10 Effects and contracts 11 Cleanup, async and concurrency 12 Observation 13 Tests 14 The core runtime 15 Kits and declaration heads 16 Embedded languages : what 1.0 has, and what a later edition plans 3, 16 Keywords , closed decisions , lowering to Rust , rule index A, B, C, D Further reading: Worked examples : larger programs, each explained line by line. Notes for readers coming from Rust , TypeScript and Python . The glossary : kit, package, head, binding, verifier and the other terms. Known limitations of v0.0.2 : what this release refuses or does not yet do, and the workaround for each. The core library : every operation of the standard modules, with its contract, generated from the library's declarations."},{"u":"docs/guide/README.html#how-the-examples-are-checked","p":"The Fortis guide","h":"How the examples are checked","m":"human","x":"How the examples are checked # Every example in this guide is a program that the real toolchain checks and runs, so the guide cannot drift from the language. An example's first line says what is expected of it, in the same notation as the LR ( LR 0 ): // accept : both checkers accept it, and it runs. A program with main runs on all three backends (the definitional interpreter, the development VM and native code) and prints exactly the output shown below it. // accept (exit status 1) marks a program that ends by failing, on purpose. A module without main has tests, and they pass on all three backends. // reject: E-XXX-NNN (a real code, such as E-OWN-011 , LR 7.3 ): the checkers reject it with exactly that code, on the line that carries the same marker, and report nothing else. // illustrative : a shape with placeholders such as \u2026 , not a program. The check is python3 -I conformance/tools/docset_check.py ; where a backend still has a recorded defect for an example, the check names the defect, and the example stays as the language says it should behave."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-a-keywords.html#appendix-a-keywords","p":"Appendix A: Keywords","h":"Appendix A: Keywords","m":"human","x":"Appendix A: Keywords # Human mode. Agent mode: Keywords, by rule . Source of truth: LR A . Fortis reserves its keywords: a keyword can never be the name of a variable, a parameter, a field, a type or any other declaration ( LR 3.4 ). There is one relaxation, and it is what lets the standard library read naturally: a keyword may be the name of a method or an associated function, and after a . it is an ordinary member name ( LR A ). This appendix lists the words, says where each is defined, and shows both sides of that rule."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-a-keywords.html#the-reserved-words","p":"Appendix A: Keywords","h":"The reserved words","m":"human","x":"The reserved words # The words are grouped by what they do; each group is defined in the LR section named beside it ( LR A ). Group Words Defined in Declarations func fn let mut const type alias class trivial enum error interface mixin implement implements with derive requires forbids excludes contract aspect needs before after on metadata lifetime from where export import as LR 4.3 , LR 4.4 , LR 5.1 , LR 6.9 , LR 6.10 , LR 7.6 , LR 7.7 , LR 8.1 , LR 8.2 , LR 9 , LR 10.3 , LR 11.5 , LR 11.8 Modifiers pure noalloc async try recovers editable owned dynamic linear trusted once LR 6.12 , LR 7.6 , LR 8.1 , LR 8.2 , LR 9.5 , LR 11.2 , LR 11.3 Ownership copy move LR 7.5 Control if else while for in loop break continue return fail give recover match when otherwise is or catch discard defer await scope LR 5.2 , LR 5.3 , LR 5.4 , LR 5.5 , LR 9.8 , LR 10.2 , LR 10.4 , LR 10.5 , LR 12.1 , LR 12.3 , LR 12.5 Values true false null present success failure self Self LR 3.5 , LR 6.8 , LR 9.3 , LR 9.5 , LR 10.1 Observation capture record report observation required version encoding LR 10.6 , LR 13 Testing test cases property forall fuzz bench expect LR 14 Kit heads (reserved by the core, defined by kits) outer heads application context model component view explore ; prefixes server client hybrid ; words inside head bodies inject provide state action view resource LR 16 Some words are reserved although your own code rarely or never writes them. lifetime is reserved, but stored loans are not in Language 1.0, so no user type declares a lifetime parameter ( LR 7.7 ). trusted is accepted only inside the fortis package ( LR 11.3 ). The kit heads are reserved by the core so that no module, in any kit, can use one as a name, whether or not it imports the kit that defines the head ( LR 16 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-a-keywords.html#keywords-cannot-be-names","p":"Appendix A: Keywords","h":"Keywords cannot be names","m":"human","x":"Keywords cannot be names # Using a keyword where a name is declared is E-SYN-002 : a variable, a parameter, a field and a type all count ( LR 3.4 ). The checker stops at the first such word, so each example below has exactly one. Fortis // reject: E-SYN-002 func main ( ) { let match = 3 // reject: E-SYN-002 } A field is a declaration position too, so a field cannot be called from , even though a method can ( LR 3.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-SYN-002 class Span { export from : Int // reject: E-SYN-002 export until : Int } func main ( ) { } The usual repair is a more specific name: start for from , pattern for match ."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-a-keywords.html#keywords-as-member-names","p":"Appendix A: Keywords","h":"Keywords as member names","m":"human","x":"Keywords as member names # The one relaxation is member position: a method or an associated function may be named with a keyword, and the word after a . is always a member name, never a keyword ( LR 3.4 , LR A ). The lexer never reclassifies a keyword; the grammar simply accepts a keyword where a member name is expected. This is why the standard library can spell String.from(view) , and why your own class can have a from constructor or a where method. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Query { export limit : Int export pure func from ( limit : Int ) Query => Query ( limit ) export pure func where ( self , n : Int ) Int => n } func main ( console : Console ) { let q = Query . from ( 5 ) let text = String . from ( \"view\" ) console . print ( \"${q.where(3)} ${q.limit} ${text}\" ) } output 3 5 view"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-a-keywords.html#contextual-words","p":"Appendix A: Keywords","h":"Contextual words","m":"human","x":"Contextual words # A handful of words have a fixed meaning in one place but are not reserved: launch , launchParallel , launchBlocking and detach (methods on a scope), retain , validate , checkpoint , join , annotate , cleanup , capability and lazy ( LR A ). They are ordinary names everywhere else, so a variable may be called capability or lazy : Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let capability = \"read\" let lazy = true console . print ( \"${capability} ${lazy}\" ) } output read true Each of these words is a method of a standard type ( launch , retain , validate ), a function of the prelude ( checkpoint , join , annotate ), the name of the special method cleanup ( LR 9.4 ), or a word that only means something inside one clause ( capability in a contract, lazy in an inject member; LR 11.5 , LR 16 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-b-decisions.html#appendix-b-closed-decisions","p":"Appendix B: Closed decisions","h":"Appendix B: Closed decisions","m":"human","x":"Appendix B: Closed decisions # Human mode. Agent mode: Closed decisions, by topic . Source of truth: LR B . Some questions come up whenever someone meets Fortis: why no semicolons, why give and not yield , why there is no ? operator. The LR keeps a table of these questions with their answers, the closed decisions ( LR B ). They are closed in the sense that Language 1.0 will not reopen them; each is stated normatively in the section cited beside it, and this appendix explains the reasoning, which comes back again and again to one principle: a piece of notation exists only if it changes what a reader may conclude about ownership, mutation, failure, effects or timing ( LR 1.1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-b-decisions.html#notation-and-syntax","p":"Appendix B: Closed decisions","h":"Notation and syntax","m":"human","x":"Notation and syntax # Decision Why LR let and let mut declare; = only assigns A reader can tell a new name from a change to an existing one without looking anything up. LR 5.1 No semicolons: a ; is a parse error Newlines end statements, with listed continuation tokens; a second way to end a statement would carry no decision. LR 3.2 .ft files, // the only comment form, documentation is the // run directly above a declaration One comment form, so there is never a question of which kind documents what. LR 3.1 , LR 3.7 Unsuffixed integer literals take their type from context (default Int ) and must fit it An out-of-range literal is a compile-time error, not a runtime surprise. LR 3.5 A float literal needs . or an exponent ( f32 suffix for Float32 ); a Decimal literal ends in d The literal's type is visible on the page: 10 is an integer, 10.0 a float, 10d a decimal. LR 3.5 A string literal is a StringView from static , converted to String (allocating) only where a String is expected The one implicit conversion in the language, and the checker counts its allocation. LR 3.5 , LR 6.6 give ends a block that produces a value ( yield was replaced) A distinct word for \"this block's value\", only where a block needs one; single-expression match arms need none. LR 5.4 , LR 9.8 The type of fail is Never fail can stand where any value is expected, such as a match arm. LR 5.4 catch e { \u2026 } names its failure explicitly No implicit binding enters scope. LR 10.4 Named arguments after positionals, in any order, each once; parameter names are public API A call can say what each argument is, and a rename shows up as an API change. LR 5.8 Defaults are pure constant expressions, re-evaluated per call; no user variadics (pass a ListView[T] ) A default never hides an effect or shared state; List.of(\u2026) is the one variadic form. LR 5.8 , LR 6.2 Constructors are named create , of or from ; there is no new Construction is an ordinary associated function, named for what it does. LR 1.1 , LR 9.2 No transparent type aliases: type is always nominal A reader never has to ask whether two type names are the same type. LR 6.9 No operator overloading ( Money.add(a, b) , not a + b ) An operator always means the same thing; behaviour is named. LR 6.10 Range patterns, generic lambdas, const generics over N , associated types and explicit type arguments at calls are not in v1 Each was left out of Language 1.0; the LR states the workaround (annotate the binding; take a generic parameter). LR 6.2 , LR 6.10 , LR 8.2 , LR 9.5 , LR 9.8 The standard package is fortis ( fortis.prelude , fortis.text , \u2026) One name for the standard library. LR 4.3 The decision about semicolons is checked like any rule. A stray ; is E-SYN-001 ( LR 3.2 ), and the checker's record carries a correction that deletes it, as the syntax class allows: Fortis // reject: E-SYN-001 func main() { let a = 1; // reject: E-SYN-001 }"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-b-decisions.html#ownership-and-loans","p":"Appendix B: Closed decisions","h":"Ownership and loans","m":"human","x":"Ownership and loans # Decision Why LR move p , copy p , editable p at the call site for existing places; fresh values need no marker The call shows what happens to each argument. LR 7.5 move p.m() for owned self methods; p.m() for editable self methods and fn mut calls, on a place declared to change The transfer is shown at the call; that p changes is shown once, by its let mut or editable declaration, and the call takes the same exclusive loan editable p.m() would (older code writes it). LR 7.5 , LR 7.6 return x of a local moves it, without move Nothing can use a returned local afterwards, so the marker says nothing the line does not. LR 7.5 alias p or editable p for an argument the result borrows from A loan that outlives the statement is visible where it is taken. LR 7.6 Loans are lexical; no non-lexical lifetimes A loan ends at the end of its block, which a reader can see; to end one early, close a block. LR 7.3 No stored loans: no lifetime parameters on user types, E-OWN-053 A reader never follows a region through a value's type; adding stored loans later breaks nothing. LR 7.7 Stored and returned loans both say from ; within is removed One word for \"this loan comes from there\". LR 7.6 , LR 7.7 Two-phase borrows: reserve at argument evaluation, activate at call entry Ordinary calls such as sum = Money.add(sum, x) or fail are legal without special cases. LR 5.7 A loan from two origins is rejected Every returned loan has one origin a reader can name. LR 7.6 No partial moves A value is either owned or moved, never half of each. LR 7.2"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-b-decisions.html#functions-closures-and-failures","p":"Appendix B: Closed decisions","h":"Functions, closures and failures","m":"human","x":"Functions, closures and failures # Decision Why LR Non- Void functions end every path in return or fail ; Void functions may fall off the end There is no implicit last value to miss. LR 5.4 Closure kinds fn , fn mut , fn once are always written How a closure may be called is part of its type and visible at its definition. LR 8.2 Lambdas take the same modifiers as functions; capture brackets only when non-empty One vocabulary for both; an empty fn[] would carry no information. LR 8.2 recovers is a modifier, written like try A function that swallows a failure says so in its signature. LR 8.1 , LR 10.4 or die is removed; use try main A failing main prints one failure report and exits with status 1. LR 4.6 report is a statement; report e as receipt binds the receipt Reporting consumes the failure, so it cannot also be propagated. LR 10.6 nostate is removed; teams that want to forbid let mut can turn on the opt-in lint no-local-mutation , which is not part of the language Writing to storage that is fresh in the activation is not the mutate effect, so a nostate claim would add nothing a caller could rely on. LR 11.1 , LR 11.2 pure , noalloc , async , try , recovers are the signature vocabulary; a full effects(\u2026) clause was rejected as noise Tools show the inferred alloc , block and nondet ; the signature keeps the claims a caller relies on. LR 11.2 , LR B or die is gone because try main already does what it did. When main fails, the runtime prints exactly one report of the failure to standard error and the process exits with status 1 ( LR 4.6 ): Fortis // accept (exit status 1) import fortis . host { Console } export error Empty { code : \"demo.empty\" message : \"nothing to do\" } try func first ( items : ListView [ Int ] ) Int { if items . count ( ) == 0 { fail Empty ( ) } return items . get ( 0 ) ?? 0 } try func main ( console : Console ) { let items : List [ Int ] = List . default ( ) console . print ( \"starting\" ) let n = first ( items ) or fail console . print ( \"first is ${n}\" ) } output starting The second line never prints: first fails, or fail hands the failure to main 's caller, and the report goes to standard error rather than standard output."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-b-decisions.html#contracts-aspects-interfaces-and-serialisation","p":"Appendix B: Closed decisions","h":"Contracts, aspects, interfaces and serialisation","m":"human","x":"Contracts, aspects, interfaces and serialisation # Decision Why LR Contracts are applied by name in the modifier position ( export FastCalculation func \u2026 ), never with @ , and take no arguments A contract only subtracts, so it sits with the other claims. LR 11.5 @name(args) applies an aspect, and is the only use of @ ; aspects have needs , before , after , on failure , metadata ; on failure may re-fail; specialisation with ...rest An aspect adds behaviour around a function and can never change a line inside it. LR 11.8 No around hook and no re-running the body; failure handlers run innermost first A retry is a library function taking a fn once , not an aspect. LR 11.8 @observe(Schema) attaches an observation schema; it is a core aspect Observation uses the same mechanism as every other aspect. LR 11.8 , LR 13 Interfaces are nominal; requires for sub-interfaces; bundles are satisfied automatically; no method bodies Having methods with the right names is not an implementation; default behaviour is a mixin. LR 9.5 derive Codec (= Encodable + Decodable ); canonical toBytes / toText ; formats are packages with \u2026Serialised mixins; hand-written encode and derive cannot coexist One description of a value's shape serves every format. LR 6.10 Projection by constructor spread ...copy p / ...move p ; derive From[S] ; to() on every type Narrowing one type into another is written once, with its ownership visible. LR 6.10 , LR 9.2 Constants include record and variant-map literals, declaration names and the Type.of(\u2026) constructors Tables and route lists can be constants. LR 5.1 VariantMap[E, V] is written { Variant: value } ; an optional callable is written (fn(\u2026) R)? One entry per variant, checked for completeness; the parentheses say which part is optional. LR 6.9"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-b-decisions.html#the-reading-release-v002","p":"Appendix B: Closed decisions","h":"The reading release (v0.0.2)","m":"human","x":"The reading release (v0.0.2) # v0.0.2 applies one principle: mark only what the reader cannot already see ( LR 1.1 ). Every older form is still accepted, and fortis fmt rewrites each one that means the same; logging, @ignorable and derive Default are the author's choice, so they are left alone. Decision Why LR The entry main declares the capabilities it needs as parameters, supplied by type; export is implied on it, and args is declared only when used Authority stays explicit, with no ambient authority, but it sits in the signature, where a reader looks. Only the entry main receives capabilities. LR 4.6 , LR 11.6 log.info , log.warn and log.error are statements that need no capability A log observes; it has no authority over the world, and its sinks are the platform's. console.print stays for the program's own output. LR 13 , LR 11.6 A Void return type may be left out of a head A head with no result type returns nothing; writing Void adds nothing. A function type keeps its result. LR 8.1 In test and property bodies or fail is implied A test can react to a failure it did not expect only by failing. LR 14 Checked arithmetic is written with operators under a handler: value * 10 + d or fail One handler covers the expression, and the arithmetic reads as arithmetic; an operator the checker proves stays a plain value. LR 6.4 An enum variant may be written bare where the expected type is known; a bare name that also names something in scope must be qualified The expected type already says which enum; an ambiguous name is never guessed. LR 9.7 A | B => v in match Arms with the same result are one arm; the alternatives bind no names and take no guard. LR 9.8 A labelled argument puns: wanted for wanted: wanted The name was written twice. LR 5.8 , LR 9.2 Map[String, V].get takes a StringView key A lookup allocates no key it then throws away. LR 6.7 @ignorable on a declaration, instead of discard at every caller The author decides once that the result may be dropped. LR 5.5 , LR 11.8 derive Default gives Name.default() A class whose fields all have defaults needs no hand-written constructor. LR 6.10 , LR 9.2 The formatter does not print a lambda's inferred pure and noalloc ; an expression-bodied lambda may leave out its result type The function type it is passed to already says both. LR 8.2"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-b-decisions.html#numbers-collections-and-concurrency","p":"Appendix B: Closed decisions","h":"Numbers, collections and concurrency","m":"human","x":"Numbers, collections and concurrency # Decision Why LR Float and Float32 have IEEE == but no Equatable , Hashable or Comparable NaN breaks those interfaces' laws. LR 6.5 Map and Set iterate in a deterministic order per build, so they may be used in pure code Iteration order never varies between runs or backends. LR 6.7 Length-refined collections are immutable; mutate an unrefined one and validate it A length refinement is a property of a value. LR 6.3 One cooperative thread per scope tree; the parallel and blocking pools need Shareable Cooperative tasks can borrow; only data that may cross threads goes to a pool. LR 12.5 Blocking in async code is forbidden; launchBlocking takes a synchronous fn once The cooperative executor has one thread, so a blocked call would stall every task. LR 11.1 , LR 12.5 Atomics are AtomicInt and AtomicBool , with seqcst and acquire / release only A small set of orderings a reader can reason about. LR 12.7 defer has only a block form, and defer await is not in v1 A deferred block lowers to cleanup code at each exit and cannot suspend. LR 12.1 Entry point: main in the manifest's entry module The program's start is named in one place. LR 4.6 Negative examples are marked // reject: E-XXX-NNN Every example says what the checker must do with it. LR 0 Test rows are bound with cases { \u2026 } as row { \u2026 } The only name the table introduces is the one written after as . LR 14"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-c-lowering.html#appendix-c-lowering-to-rust","p":"Appendix C: Lowering to Rust","h":"Appendix C: Lowering to Rust","m":"human","x":"Appendix C: Lowering to Rust # Human mode. Agent mode: Lowering reference, by shape . Source of truth: LR C . Fortis has two execution paths that share one semantics: the development VM runs the checked intermediate representation directly, and the production backend turns it into safe Rust, a crate marked #![forbid(unsafe_code)] , which rustc then compiles ( LR 1 , LR 7.10 ). This appendix explains how Fortis constructs map onto Rust, which checks Rust's own compiler repeats and which only the Fortis frontend can make, and what the language rejects because no safe lowering exists. You do not need any of it to write Fortis; it explains why some ownership rules are shaped the way they are."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-c-lowering.html#every-construct-has-a-shape","p":"Appendix C: Lowering to Rust","h":"Every construct has a shape","m":"human","x":"Every construct has a shape # The ownership rules are chosen so that every program the checker accepts has a documented lowering to safe Rust ( LR 7.10 ). The table in LR C.1 lists one shape per construct. A few rows show the pattern ( LR C.1 ): Fortis Rust a type newtype a struct with one private field a class a struct and an impl block a trivial type a Copy struct or enum a read loan, an exclusive loan &T , &mut T copy p a call of a generated Clone implementation a from return fn f<'a>(x: &'a T) -> &'a U Shared[T] , Weak[T] Arc<T> , Weak<T> an interface, dynamic I a trait with generics; Box<dyn I> , &dyn I or Arc<dyn I> a lambda a closure with a concrete environment ( Fn , FnMut or FnOnce ) a try func a function returning Result<T, Failure> T? Option<T> defer its body inlined at every exit path, not a Drop cleanup an impl Drop that calls the method an async func , a scope an async fn state machine; a join set polled by the parent future Two consequences are worth knowing. First, the checker never inserts a clone, a reference count or hidden sharing to make a rejected program acceptable ( LANG-07-03 , LR 7.10 ): an ownership-bearing value is duplicated where the source asks for it, by a copy (lowered to a generated Clone , LR C.1 ) or by an operation whose defined meaning includes copying, such as filter ( LR 6.7 ), while trivial values copy implicitly ( LR 6.11 ) and retain makes another handle to shared data ( LR 7.8 ). Second, a defer block is plain code at each exit, which is why a deferred block can use the places it names freely between its registration and the exit ( LR 12.1 , LR C.1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-c-lowering.html#who-checks-what","p":"Appendix C: Lowering to Rust","h":"Who checks what","m":"human","x":"Who checks what # Each row of LR C.1 names which checker is authoritative ( LR C.1 ). Rust-checked properties are ones rustc verifies again on the generated crate: loan validity, lifetimes, use after move, Send and Sync , trait bounds and exhaustiveness. If rustc rejects generated code that the Fortis frontend accepted, and the construct is in this Rust-checked fragment, that is a compiler defect ( LR 7.10 , LR C.1 ). Frontend-checked properties have no Rust type: linear obligations (an Outcome must be consumed), the closed effect set, noalloc , closure kinds against the captures the body needs, observation definite assignment. That rustc accepts the generated code says nothing about them; the IR validator and the restricted Rust subset are the controls ( LR C.1 ). For loans the frontend is the stricter of the two: a Fortis loan ends at the end of its block, not at its last use ( LR 7.3 , LR C.1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-c-lowering.html#shapes-with-no-lowering","p":"Appendix C: Lowering to Rust","h":"Shapes with no lowering","m":"human","x":"Shapes with no lowering # A handful of ownership situations can be described but have no safe lowering. They are rejected before lowering, each with the alternative the LR names ( LR C.2 ). Where an earlier rule already excludes a shape, that rule's more specific code is used; E-OWN-090 is for shapes only the lowering can identify ( LR C.2 ). Shape Code Write instead LR a loan returned from two parameters E-SYN-000 (a loan type names one origin) return an owned value, or split the function LR C.2 , LR 7.6 a class that stores a loan E-OWN-053 own the value, copy it, hold a Shared , or pass it per call LR C.2 , LR 7.7 a self-referential class E-OWN-053 indices, or Shared with Weak LR C.2 , LR 7.7 a loan stored in Shared or Mutex E-OWN-090 own the value LR C.2 a fn mut closure escaping into a Shared E-OWN-090 a Mutex around the state and a fn closure LR C.2 a future holding a loan launched on a parallel pool E-ASY-003 pass owned data LR C.2 , LR 12.7 a partial move E-OWN-040 move the whole value, or restructure LR C.2 , LR 7.2 No shape is ever lowered with unsafe or by erasing a lifetime ( LR 7.10 , LR C.2 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-c-lowering.html#a-partial-move","p":"Appendix C: Lowering to Rust","h":"A partial move","m":"human","x":"A partial move # Moving one field out of a value that still owns the others would leave a half-owned value. It is rejected with E-OWN-040 ( LR 7.2 , LR C.2 ): Fortis // reject: E-OWN-040 class Order { export id : Int export lines : List [ Int ] } func main ( ) { let order = Order ( id : 7 , lines : List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) ) let lines = move order . lines // reject: E-OWN-040 } Consuming the whole value with a destructuring let takes every field at once, so nothing is left half-owned ( LR 9.8 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Order { export id : Int export lines : List [ Int ] } func main ( console : Console ) { let order = Order ( id : 7 , lines : List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) ) let Order ( id : id , lines : lines ) = move order console . print ( \"order ${id} has ${lines.count()} lines\" ) } output order 7 has 3 lines"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-c-lowering.html#a-stored-loan","p":"Appendix C: Lowering to Rust","h":"A stored loan","m":"human","x":"A stored loan # A field may not hold a loan or a view: stored loans are not in Language 1.0, so a class with a StringView field is E-OWN-053 ( LR 7.7 , LR C.2 ). The one exception is a loan from static , which borrows from no owner, such as a field of type StringView from static holding a string literal ( LR 7.7 ): Fortis // reject: E-OWN-053 class Line { // reject: E-OWN-053 export number : Int export text : StringView } func main ( ) { } The class owns its text instead ( LR 7.7 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Line { export number : Int export text : String } func main ( console : Console ) { let line = Line ( number : 1 , text : \"first line\" ) console . print ( \"${line.number}: ${line.text}\" ) } output 1: first line"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-c-lowering.html#a-fn-mut-closure-inside-a-shared","p":"Appendix C: Lowering to Rust","h":"A fn mut closure inside a Shared","m":"human","x":"A fn mut closure inside a Shared # A fn mut closure holds an exclusive loan or mutable state, and a Shared hands out only read access to what it holds, so the combination has no lowering: E-OWN-090 ( LR 7.8 , LR C.2 ): Fortis // reject: E-OWN-090 func main ( ) { let mut count = 0 let bump = fn mut [ editable count ] ( ) Void { count = Int . saturatingAdd ( count , 1 ) } let shared = Shared . create ( move bump ) // reject: E-OWN-090 } The LR's alternative puts the state behind something designed for shared mutation and uses a plain fn closure ( LR C.2 ). Here the state is an AtomicInt held in a Shared , and the closure owns its own handle ( LR 7.8 , LR 12.7 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let counter = Shared . create ( AtomicInt . create ( 0 ) ) let handle = counter . retain ( ) let bump = fn [ move handle ] ( ) Void { discard handle . fetchAdd ( 1 ) } bump ( ) bump ( ) console . print ( \"count ${counter.load()}\" ) } output count 2"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-c-lowering.html#a-loan-sent-to-a-parallel-pool","p":"Appendix C: Lowering to Rust","h":"A loan sent to a parallel pool","m":"human","x":"A loan sent to a parallel pool # A task on the parallel pool runs on a worker thread, so the future it runs must hold no loans and everything it carries must be Shareable ; a future that borrows is E-ASY-003 ( LR 12.5 , LR 12.7 , LR C.2 ). Fortis // reject: E-ASY-003 import fortis . host { Console } async func total ( items : ListView [ Int ] ) Int { let mut sum = 0 for x in items { sum = Int . saturatingAdd ( sum , x ) } return sum } async func main ( console : Console ) { let numbers = List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) scope tasks { let t = tasks . launchParallel ( total ( numbers ) ) // reject: E-ASY-003 let sum = await t console . print ( \"${sum}\" ) } } Pass the task owned data instead ( LR 12.5 , LR C.2 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } async func total ( items : owned List [ Int ] ) Int { let mut sum = 0 for x in items { sum = Int . saturatingAdd ( sum , x ) } return sum } async func main ( console : Console ) { let numbers = List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) scope tasks { let t = tasks . launchParallel ( total ( move numbers ) ) let sum = await t console . print ( \"${sum}\" ) } } output 6"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-c-lowering.html#what-counts-as-allocation","p":"Appendix C: Lowering to Rust","h":"What counts as allocation","m":"human","x":"What counts as allocation # noalloc is decided by matching the lowering against a fixed list of allocation-free patterns, not by looking at what the optimiser produced ( LR 11.4 , LR C.3 ). The allocation-free patterns are arithmetic and comparison; field and array access; building trivial and inline values ( Array , FixedList , FixedMap , a bounded Failure ) and any class or record from values that are trivial, moved, freshly built, returned by a call or retained; read and exclusive loans; calls to functions whose summary has no alloc ; match ; defer ; and capture into a frame of bounded size ( LR C.3 ). Everything else allocates: growing a List , String or Map , Shared.create , creating a dynamic value, copy of a heap-owning value, boxed futures from dynamic async calls, extended failure payloads, and launches on the pools. In Language 1.0 a cooperative launch allocates too, because the runtime host declares no inline task storage; join(a, b) does not ( LR C.3 ). So arithmetic on proved integers is noalloc , and pushing onto a list is not ( LR 11.1 , LR C.3 ): Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 pure noalloc func grow ( items : editable List [ Int ] ) { items . push ( 1 ) // reject: E-EFF-001 } func main ( ) { } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure noalloc func square ( n : Int < - 1000 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * n func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${square(12)}\" ) } output 144"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-d-rules.html#appendix-d-rule-index","p":"Appendix D: Rule index","h":"Appendix D: Rule index","m":"human","x":"Appendix D: Rule index # Human mode. Agent mode: Rule index . Source of truth: the rule tables at the end of each LR section, and LR D . Every normative rule of the core has an ID of the form LANG-<section>-<nn> , and each LR section ends with a table of its rules and the evidence that tests them ( LR 0 ). This appendix lists every rule in one place, says in a line what it means, and points to the chapter that explains it. The one-line summaries are paraphrases; the rule text in the LR is the rule."},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-d-rules.html#lexical-structure-and-modules","p":"Appendix D: Rule index","h":"Lexical structure and modules","m":"human","x":"Lexical structure and modules # Rule In short LR Chapter LANG-03-01 A ; outside strings, comments and raw template regions is a parse error. LR 3.2 Lexical structure LANG-03-02 A newline ends a statement except after the listed trailing tokens, before the listed leading tokens, or inside brackets. LR 3.2 Lexical structure LANG-03-03 An identifier's first character decides its category: uppercase for types, lowercase for values. LR 3.3 Lexical structure LANG-03-04 Keywords are reserved in every declaration and binding position, but may name a method or follow a . . LR 3.4 Keywords LANG-03-05 A literal's type follows the literal table, and an out-of-range literal is a compile-time error. LR 3.5 Lexical structure LANG-03-06 An interpolated expression is evaluated once, in order, and cannot fail or suspend. LR 3.6 Lexical structure LANG-04-01 A module's identity is its file path, and a module holds only declarations and constants. LR 4.2 Modules and packages LANG-04-02 Importing a module runs no code. LR 4.2 Modules and packages LANG-04-03 Only modules a package lists in exports: can be imported from other packages. LR 4.3 Modules and packages LANG-04-04 Two versions of a package, and two modules, never share a nominal type. LR 4.1 Modules and packages LANG-04-05 A let may not shadow a visible binding. LR 4.5 Modules and packages LANG-04-06 The program starts at the main of the manifest's entry module, exported or not, in one of four forms; the runtime supplies each of its parameters, List[String] or a capability type, by type. LR 4.6 Modules and packages"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-d-rules.html#statements-and-types","p":"Appendix D: Rule index","h":"Statements and types","m":"human","x":"Statements and types # Rule In short LR Chapter LANG-05-01 let declares, and its pattern matches every value; = only assigns to a visible mutable target. LR 5.1 Statements and expressions LANG-05-02 Leaving a block ends its loans, runs its defer s last-first, then drops its locals in reverse order. LR 5.1 Statements and expressions LANG-05-03 if and while conditions are Bool . LR 5.2 Statements and expressions LANG-05-04 A ranged for gives an immutable index refined to the range. LR 5.3 Statements and expressions LANG-05-05 A non- Void function ends every path in return , fail or a call that never returns. LR 5.4 Statements and expressions LANG-05-06 Operators follow the precedence table, and the outcome, ?? and Boolean families do not mix without parentheses. LR 5.6 Statements and expressions LANG-05-07 Evaluation is left to right, once, in the four steps of a call, identically on every backend. LR 5.7 Statements and expressions LANG-05-08 A linear value cannot be discarded (except a never-polled future holding nothing linear and no exclusive loan); any other unused non- Void value needs discard . LR 5.5 , LR 12.3 Statements and expressions LANG-05-09 Named arguments follow positional ones, bind each parameter once, and are part of the exported signature; a pun name binds the parameter of its name. LR 5.8 Statements and expressions LANG-06-01 A nominal type keeps its identity through calls, storage, serialisation and FFI. LR 6.1 , LR 6.9 Types LANG-06-02 A refinement is a subtype of any wider refinement, and the interval domain is exactly as listed, with no extra inference. LR 6.3 Types , Proving arithmetic and indexing LANG-06-03 An untrusted value passes validate before it can hold a refined or nominal type. LR 6.3 Types LANG-06-04 Integer, Decimal and the listed float operations give bit-identical results on the VM and native code; a checked operator fails as its checked function does, at the same operation. LR 6.4 , LR 6.5 Types LANG-06-05 Float operators are total, strict IEEE 754; float refinements are rejected. LR 6.5 Types LANG-06-06 Map and Set iterate in an order fixed per build. LR 6.7 Types LANG-06-07 The trivial and copyable sets are exactly the listed ones. LR 6.11 Types LANG-06-08 An optional, a Bool and an Outcome never convert into one another. LR 6.8 Types LANG-06-09 A linear value is consumed exactly once. LR 6.12 Types LANG-06-10 An interface is implemented for a type only in the package that defines one of them, and never twice. LR 6.10 , LR 9.5 Types LANG-06-11 A call whose type arguments nothing fixes is rejected. LR 6.10 Types"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-d-rules.html#ownership-functions-and-classes","p":"Appendix D: Rule index","h":"Ownership, functions and classes","m":"human","x":"Ownership, functions and classes # Rule In short LR Chapter LANG-07-01 Assigning, returning or capturing an existing ownership-bearing place says copy , alias or move ; owned and editable arguments say so too. LR 7.5 Ownership LANG-07-02 Parameters are never reassignable. LR 7.6 Ownership LANG-07-03 The checker never inserts a clone, a reference count or hidden sharing to repair a program. LR 7.10 Ownership , Lowering to Rust LANG-07-04 Use after move, conflicting loans, use of a loaned owner and escaping loans are rejected, through closures, defer , await and scope too. LR 7.3 , LR 7.4 Ownership LANG-07-05 Loans are lexical, and an owner never has an exclusive loan alongside any other loan. LR 7.3 Ownership LANG-07-06 A returned loan names one origin; a lifetime parameter, or a field of a loan or loan-carrying type other than a loan from static , is rejected. LR 7.6 , LR 7.7 Ownership LANG-07-07 Values are destroyed in the order of the destruction table, on every backend. LR 7.9 Ownership LANG-07-08 Sharing is explicit ( retain ), and copy of a Shared is rejected. LR 7.8 Ownership LANG-07-09 An ownership shape with no safe lowering is rejected, never lowered unsafely. LR 7.10 Lowering to Rust LANG-08-01 Every function states its parameter types and success type (a lambda's may be inferred as LR 8.2 allows), with modifiers in the fixed order. LR 8.1 Functions and closures LANG-08-02 A lambda lists its captures and declares its kind; a body needing a stronger kind is rejected. LR 8.2 , LR 8.3 Functions and closures LANG-08-03 A lambda body cannot use a name that is not in its capture list. LR 8.3 Functions and closures LANG-08-04 Callables are assignable as LR 8.4 says, and closures are boxed only when dynamic fn is written. LR 8.4 Functions and closures LANG-09-01 There is no inheritance, runtime member lookup or reflection-based construction. LR 9.1 Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums LANG-09-02 The generated constructor is callable only where every field is visible. LR 9.2 Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums LANG-09-03 An implementation never strengthens a precondition or weakens a guarantee, and coherence holds. LR 9.5 Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums LANG-09-04 A mixin requires methods only, adds no state, and conflicting mixins are rejected. LR 9.6 Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums LANG-09-05 A match on a closed type is exhaustive without otherwise , naming each missing variant; or-pattern alternatives each count, bind nothing, and take no guard. LR 9.8 Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums LANG-09-06 Pattern bindings own or borrow as LR 9.8 says. LR 9.8 Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-d-rules.html#failures-effects-concurrency-observation-and-tests","p":"Appendix D: Rule index","h":"Failures, effects, concurrency, observation and tests","m":"human","x":"Failures, effects, concurrency, observation and tests # Rule In short LR Chapter LANG-10-01 An outcome stays wrapped until one of the consuming operations takes it. LR 10.1 , LR 10.2 Outcomes and failures LANG-10-02 Obligations survive storage in collections, fields and closures, and dropping one is rejected. LR 10.7 Outcomes and failures LANG-10-03 A reported failure is gone, and annotate keeps exactly one obligation. LR 10.6 Outcomes and failures LANG-10-04 noalloc try functions use the bounded failure representation. LR 10.3 Outcomes and failures LANG-10-05 Recovering a failure that was not reported adds the recovers effect. LR 10.4 Outcomes and failures LANG-10-06 Defects follow the closed taxonomy and can never be caught. LR 10.8 Outcomes and failures LANG-11-01 There are exactly eight effects, computed transitively to a fixed point. LR 11.1 , LR 11.3 Effects and contracts LANG-11-02 Writes to fresh storage and through a function's own editable parameters are not mutate ; writes through captures, guards and atomics are. LR 11.1 , LR 11.3 Effects and contracts LANG-11-03 noalloc is checked against the allocation whitelist for the actual target and telemetry plan. LR 11.4 Effects and contracts , Lowering to Rust LANG-11-04 A trusted summary is labelled trusted , never proved . LR 11.3 Effects and contracts LANG-11-05 async , try and recovers are always written; across modules only written pure / noalloc count; weakening an exported function's modifiers is a reported API change. LR 11.3 Effects and contracts LANG-11-06 A contract is a conjunction of the LR 11.5 elements, and configuration adds no predicate. LR 11.5 Effects and contracts LANG-11-07 An io effect other than report and a log statement needs an Authority value in the signature; only the entry main receives the capabilities, as parameters (or creates them once with Host.capabilities() ). LR 11.6 Effects and contracts LANG-11-08 Contracts sit in the modifier position without arguments; @ applies aspects only. LR 11.5 , LR 11.8 Effects and contracts LANG-11-09 Aspects have only the listed parts and elaborate before checking into a function that passes every ordinary check. LR 11.8 Effects and contracts LANG-12-01 A defer body is checked at every exit, cannot change the outcome, and its places are initialised at every exit. LR 12.1 Cleanup, async and concurrency LANG-12-02 Failures inside defer are consumed there and never replace the primary outcome. LR 12.1 Cleanup, async and concurrency LANG-12-03 Future and Task are linear, a pending future is never destroyed, and cancellation is observed only at cancellation points. LR 12.3 , LR 12.4 Cleanup, async and concurrency LANG-12-04 A scope joins or cancels every task it launched before its block exits. LR 12.5 Cleanup, async and concurrency LANG-12-05 Only Shareable environments cross the parallel and blocking pools, and loans cross only a cooperative launch . LR 12.5 , LR 12.7 Cleanup, async and concurrency LANG-12-06 A MutexGuard is never live across await . LR 12.3 , LR 12.7 Cleanup, async and concurrency LANG-13-01 capture snapshots a pure, infallible expression at the statement. LR 13 Observation LANG-13-02 Required frame fields are assigned exactly once on every applicable exit; captures in loops and second captures are rejected. LR 13 Observation LANG-13-03 A frame is bounded by its schema, and truncation or omission is recorded. LR 13 Observation LANG-13-04 log.info , log.warn and log.error write one log record to the diagnostic plane, never standard output, need no authority and have the io effect. LR 13 Observation LANG-14-01 Table rows are bound explicitly with as . LR 14 Tests LANG-14-02 expect takes a Bool and records typed evidence. LR 14 Tests LANG-14-03 In a test or property body a failing call that nothing else consumes, and unproved integer arithmetic, have an implied or fail . LR 14 , LR 6.4 Tests"},{"u":"docs/guide/appendix-d-rules.html#old-requirement-ids","p":"Appendix D: Rule index","h":"Old requirement IDs","m":"human","x":"Old requirement IDs # Before FSPEC-0.2 the requirements had IDs such as OWN-001 . LR D maps each to its rule today; where a requirement moved to the toolchain's own requirements or to the Platform Specification it names a family there rather than a rule ( LR D ). Old IDs Now LR SYN-001 LANG-05-05 , LANG-08-01 LR D , LR 5.4 , LR 8.1 , LR 5 , LR 8 SYN-002 LANG-03-03 (the case rule replaced symbol-category disambiguation) LR D , LR 3.3 , LR 3 SYN-003; SYN-005, SYN-006 the toolchain's requirements (formatter; diagnostics) LR D SYN-004 LANG-05-07 LR D , LR 5.7 , LR 5 TYP-001, TYP-002, TYP-003, TYP-004, TYP-006 LANG-06-01 , LANG-06-03 , LANG-06-04 , LANG-06-02 , LANG-06-08 LR D , LR 6.1 , LR 6.3 , LR 6.4 , LR 6.8 , LR 6 TYP-005 the toolchain's requirements (library metadata) LR D OWN-001 \u2026 OWN-006 LANG-07-01 , LANG-07-02 , LANG-07-03 , LANG-07-04 , LANG-07-06 , LANG-07-08 LR D , LR 7.5 , LR 7 FUN-001, FUN-002 LANG-08-02 LR D , LR 8.2 , LR 8 OO-001 LANG-09-01 , LANG-09-04 LR D , LR 9.1 , LR 9 GEN-001 LANG-06-10 , and the toolchain's requirements (bounded solving) LR D , LR 6.10 , LR 6 MOD-001 LANG-04-02 LR D , LR 4.2 , LR 4 DOC-001 the toolchain's requirements: every documentation example runs as a test LR D ERR-001 \u2026 ERR-006 LANG-10-01 , LANG-10-02 , LANG-10-03 (ERR-003 and ERR-004), LANG-10-04 , LANG-09-05 LR D , LR 10.2 , LR 9.8 , LR 9 , LR 10 ERR-007 the Telemetry and Runtime host kits (disposition policy) LR D EFF-001, EFF-002, EFF-004, EFF-005, EFF-006 LANG-11-01 , LANG-11-02 , LANG-11-03 , LANG-11-04 , LANG-11-06 LR D , LR 11.1 , LR 11 EFF-003 removed with nostate LR D , LR 11.2 RES-001, RES-002 LANG-05-02 and LANG-07-07 ; LANG-12-02 LR D , LR 5.1 , LR 7.9 , LR 12.1 , LR 5 , LR 7 , LR 12 ASY-001, ASY-003; ASY-002; ASY-004 LANG-12-03 ; LANG-12-05 ; LANG-12-04 LR D , LR 12.3 , LR 12 ASY-005 the Runtime host kit LR D OBS-001, OBS-002, OBS-006 LANG-13-01 , LANG-13-02 , LANG-13-03 LR D , LR 13 OBS-003, OBS-004, OBS-005 the Telemetry kit LR D"},{"u":"docs/guide/embedded-languages.html#embedded-languages","p":"Embedded languages","h":"Embedded languages","m":"human","x":"Embedded languages # Human mode. Agent mode: Embedded languages . Source of truth: LR 16 . Preview: declaration heads and kits ( LR 16 ) arrive in v0.5. Many programs hold text in another language: a SQL query, an HTML template, a regular expression, a JSON document. This page says what Language 1.0 offers for that, and what it does not."},{"u":"docs/guide/embedded-languages.html#what-language-10-has","p":"Embedded languages","h":"What Language 1.0 has","m":"human","x":"What Language 1.0 has # The only embedded text the core language defines is the raw template region of the Web UI kit ( LR 16 ). The region is opened lexically, with no knowledge of the declaration around it: a statement whose tokens are view , optionally preceded by export and by one of server , client or hybrid , followed by nothing, a name, or a name and a parenthesised group, and then { . The lexer takes the text from that { to the } that balances it, counting every brace including those of ${\u2026} , and hands it to the Web UI kit as one token, unlexed ( LR 16 ). Template syntax is therefore the kit's to define, and a template never adds a token to the core. Recognising a region and permitting it are two steps. The lexer recognises one wherever such a statement occurs; the kit then validates where it may appear, like the rest of a head body: inside a component body, or as a standalone view head of its own ( LR 16 ). Where the view is a declaration, the grammar requires its group to be a parameter list; inside a head body the kit validates it. Kits and declaration heads explains heads and the four extension slots. Everywhere else, text in another language is an ordinary Fortis string, a StringView or String ( LR 3.5 , LR 3.6 ). Interpolation in a string is plain text interpolation: it never escapes for HTML, SQL or any other context ( LR 3.6 ), so a program that builds a query or a page from strings must escape or bind its values itself, through a library that does so."},{"u":"docs/guide/embedded-languages.html#what-a-later-edition-plans","p":"Embedded languages","h":"What a later edition plans","m":"human","x":"What a later edition plans # Fenced blocks of another language, written inside a module with a language tag and typed holes, checked by an embedding binding and lowered to a typed value, are not part of Language 1.0 ; they move to a later edition (X-145). Until then, none of that syntax exists, and a program that tries it is a syntax error ( LR 3.2 ). The word embed is set aside for that edition (X-145)."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#notes-for-readers-coming-from-python","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"Notes for readers coming from Python","m":"human","x":"Notes for readers coming from Python # Human mode. Source of truth: the LR; each note cites the section that states the rule. Preview: newtypes and mixins ( LR 6.9 , 9.6 ) arrive in v0.1 and property tests ( LR 14 ) in v0.2; the examples under \"Exceptions are outcomes\", \"Classes without inheritance\" and \"Tests are part of the language\" that use them are checked and run today. The rest of these notes is in v0.0.2. Python checks a program while it runs; Fortis checks it before anything runs. Every binding has a type the checker knows, every function states the types it takes and returns, and a program the checker rejects never starts ( LR 1 , LR 8.1 ). In exchange, the questions a Python reviewer has to answer by reading carefully (can this call change my list, can it raise, does it do I/O) are answered on the page. This page goes through the differences you will meet first."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#braces-newlines-and-declared-types","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"Braces, newlines and declared types","m":"human","x":"Braces, newlines and declared types # Blocks are delimited by braces, not indentation, and a newline ends a statement unless the line obviously continues (it ends in an operator or a comma, or the next line starts with . , or , catch , ?? and a few others); there are no semicolons ( LR 3.2 ). Every parameter has a type and every function states its return type, unless it returns nothing ( LR 8.1 ). Local types are inferred. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } pure func average ( scores : ListView [ Int < 0 .. 100 > ] ) Int { let n = scores . count ( ) if n <= 0 { return 0 } let mut total = 0 for s in scores { total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , s ) } return total / n } func main ( console : Console ) { let scores : List [ Int < 0 .. 100 > ] = List . of ( 72 , 91 , 64 ) console . print ( \"average ${average(scores)}\" ) } output average 75 let declares a name and let mut one that may be reassigned; plain = only reassigns, so a typo can never create a new variable ( LR 5.1 ). The early return is not only for the empty case. Integer division must be proved safe, and after if n <= 0 { return 0 } the checker knows that n is at least 1 for the rest of the function, so total / n cannot divide by zero ( LR 6.3 , LR 6.4 ). Without the test, the division would be rejected and you would handle its failure instead, total / n or fail , which fails with numeric.divide_by_zero ( LR 6.4 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#nothing-is-truthy","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"Nothing is truthy","m":"human","x":"Nothing is truthy # if items: in Python tests for a non-empty list. A Fortis condition must be a Bool , so a list, a number, a string or an optional in a condition is E-TYP-010 ( LR 5.2 ). Say what you are testing. Fortis // reject: E-TYP-010 pure func describe ( items : List [ Int ] ) StringView from static { if items { // reject: E-TYP-010 return \"some\" } return \"none\" } Fortis // accept pure func describe ( items : List [ Int ] ) StringView from static { if ! items . isEmpty ( ) { return \"some\" } return \"none\" } test \"describe\" { expect describe ( List . of ( 1 ) ) == \"some\" expect describe ( List . default ( ) ) == \"none\" }"},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#none-is-an-optional-type","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"None is an optional type","m":"human","x":"None is an optional type # A value that may be absent has type T? , and null is its absent case ( LR 6.8 ). Python's d.get(key, default) is map.get(key) ?? default ; x.y if x is not None else None is x?.y . After if value != null , the checker knows value is present inside the branch ( LR 6.8 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut stock : Map [ String , Int ] = Map . default ( ) stock . set ( \"tea\" , 4 ) let tea = stock . get ( \"tea\" ) ?? 0 let coffee = stock . get ( \"coffee\" ) ?? 0 console . print ( \"tea ${tea}, coffee ${coffee}\" ) } output tea 4, coffee 0 Absence, failure and False are three different things in Fortis, with three different types and operators, and none of them converts into another ( LR 1 , LR 6.8 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#exceptions-are-outcomes","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"Exceptions are outcomes","m":"human","x":"Exceptions are outcomes # There are no exceptions. A function that can fail is declared try , and its caller must deal with the outcome: pass it on with or fail , replace it with or recover v , or handle it with catch e { \u2026 } ( LR 10.1 , LR 10.2 ). Forgetting to is a compile error, not a crash in production ( LR 10.1 ). Converting untrusted input into a constrained type is the everyday case. Where Python would call int(text) and check a range, Fortis declares the range in the type and converts with validate , a pure fallible conversion ( LR 6.3 , LR 6.9 ) that, by its contract in fortis.prelude , fails with refinement.out_of_range when the value does not fit: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } type Percent = Int < 0 .. 100 > func main ( console : Console ) { for raw in List . of ( 42 , 250 ) { let p = Percent . validate ( raw ) catch e { console . print ( \"${raw} rejected: ${e.code}\" ) report e recover Percent ( 0 ) } console . print ( \"stored ${p}\" ) } } output stored 42 250 rejected: refinement.out_of_range stored 0 report e writes the failure once to standard error as a structured record and consumes it; a failure can be reported or propagated, never both ( LR 10.6 ). A catch covers only the call to its left, never a whole block ( LR 10.4 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#assignment-does-not-share-a-list","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"Assignment does not share a list","m":"human","x":"Assignment does not share a list # In Python, b = a makes b another name for the same list, and appending through one shows through the other. In Fortis each value has one owner, and taking a second hold on it is written down: copy a for an independent duplicate, alias a for a read-only loan, move a to hand it over ( LR 7.5 ). A bare let b = a on a list is rejected ( E-OWN-001 , LR 7.5 ): Fortis // reject: E-OWN-001 func main ( ) { let a = List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) let b = a // reject: E-OWN-001 } Changing a value through a call is also visible. A function that may modify its argument declares the parameter editable , and the caller writes editable too; a plain parameter can only be read ( LR 7.6 ). Python's surprise (a function that quietly appends to the list you passed) cannot happen without the word editable at the call: Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func addDefaults ( items : editable List [ String ] ) { items . push ( \"salt\" ) } func count ( items : List [ String ] ) Int => items . count ( ) func main ( console : Console ) { let mut shopping : List [ String ] = List . of ( \"bread\" ) addDefaults ( editable shopping ) console . print ( \"${count(shopping)} items\" ) } output 2 items"},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#integers-have-a-size","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"Integers have a size","m":"human","x":"Integers have a size # Python's integers grow without bound. Int is 64 bits, and arithmetic that might overflow is either proved safe by the checker or says what happens: a * b or fail fails with numeric.overflow , Int.wrappingMul wraps, Int.saturatingMul clamps ( LR 6.4 ). BigInt is the unbounded integer, and it allocates ( LR 6.2 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let big = 4611686018427387904 let doubled = big * 2 catch e { console . print ( \"overflow: ${e.code}\" ) report e recover 0 } console . print ( \"saturating: ${Int.saturatingMul(big, 2)}\" ) } output overflow: numeric.overflow saturating: 9223372036854775807"},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#classes-without-inheritance","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"Classes without inheritance","m":"human","x":"Classes without inheritance # A class has fields and methods, and no superclass ( LR 9.1 ). What inheritance does in Python is split three ways: an interface declares methods a type promises to have, a mixin contributes methods to the classes that apply it with with , and a class implements an interface only by saying so ( LR 9.5 , LR 9.6 ). A mixin may require methods but never adds fields, and its methods see only the class's exported members, so it never reads private state ( LR 9.6 ). Fortis // accept mixin Greeting { requires pure func name ( self ) String export pure func greet ( self ) String => \"hello, ${self.name()}\" } class Person with Greeting { export first : String export pure func name ( self ) String => copy self . first } test \"a mixin method uses a required method\" { let p = Person ( first : \"Ada\" ) expect p . greet ( ) == \"hello, Ada\" } Fields and methods are private to their module unless marked export ( LR 4.4 ). There is no __init__ : every class has a generated constructor taking each field by name, and a class that must validate provides an associated function, conventionally create , of or from ( LR 9.2 , LR 1.1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#comprehensions-are-map-and-filter","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"Comprehensions are map and filter","m":"human","x":"Comprehensions are map and filter # [x * 2 for x in xs if x > 0] becomes calls to filter and map with lambdas ( LR 6.7 ). Their callbacks must be pure ; a loop that does I/O is a for loop ( LR 6.7 ). A lambda lists the variables it captures, with a marker for each, and a trivial value such as an Int may be listed bare ( LR 8.3 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let readings : List [ Int < - 100 .. 100 > ] = List . of ( - 5 , 12 , 0 , 40 ) let floor = 0 let positive = readings . filter ( fn [ floor ] ( r : Int < - 100 .. 100 > ) Bool => r > floor ) let doubled = positive . map ( fn ( r : Int < - 100 .. 100 > ) Int => r * 2 ) console . print ( \"${doubled}\" ) } output [24, 80]"},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#print-needs-a-capability","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"print needs a capability","m":"human","x":"print needs a capability # There is no global print and no global open . Input and output go through capability values: main declares the ones it needs as parameters, the runtime supplies them, and main passes them to the functions that need them ( LR 4.6 , LR 11.6 ). A function that does I/O therefore names, in its signature, what it may touch. Diagnostics are different: log.info , log.warn and log.error , the counterpart of Python's logging , write a log record to standard error from any function, with no capability, and report e records a failure the same way ( LR 13 , LR 10.6 ). Creating capabilities anywhere but main , as Host.capabilities() does in older code, is E-EFF-010 , and so is doing I/O in a function that takes no capability, so this attempt is reported twice ( LR 11.6 ): Fortis // reject: E-EFF-010 import fortis . host { Host } func say ( message : StringView ) { let caps = Host . capabilities ( ) // reject: E-EFF-010 caps . console . print ( message ) // reject: E-EFF-010 } func main ( ) { say ( \"starting\" ) } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func say ( console : Console , message : StringView ) { console . print ( message ) } func main ( console : Console ) { say ( console , \"starting\" ) } output starting"},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#modules-run-nothing-when-imported","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"Modules run nothing when imported","m":"human","x":"Modules run nothing when imported # A Python module runs its top-level code on import. A Fortis module contains only declarations and constants, so importing it executes nothing, and there is no if __name__ == \"__main__\" : a program's entry point is the main of the entry module its manifest names ( LR 4.2 , LR 4.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-python.html#tests-are-part-of-the-language","p":"Notes for readers coming from Python","h":"Tests are part of the language","m":"human","x":"Tests are part of the language # test declarations live next to the code and need no framework; cases { \u2026 } as row is a table of named rows, like pytest.mark.parametrize , and property \u2026 forall draws inputs from the parameter types ( LR 14 ). expect condition records the actual and expected values and fails the test when the condition is false ( LR 14 ). Fortis // accept pure func isAdult ( age : Int < 0 .. 150 > ) Bool => age >= 18 test \"adult boundary\" cases { \"below\" : { age : 17 , expected : false } \"equal\" : { age : 18 , expected : true } } as row { expect isAdult ( row . age ) == row . expected } property \"adults are at least 18\" forall ( age : Int < 0 .. 150 > ) { expect ! isAdult ( age ) || age >= 18 } fortis test file.ft runs them ( LR 14 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#notes-for-readers-coming-from-rust","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","m":"human","x":"Notes for readers coming from Rust # Human mode. Source of truth: the LR; each note cites the section that states the rule. Preview: interfaces, mixins and generics ( LR 6.10 , 9.5 , 9.6 ) arrive in v0.1, effects and claims as a whole ( LR 11.1 to 11.4, 11.6) are completed in v0.2, and async with scopes ( LR 12.3 , 12.5 , 12.7 ) arrives in v0.3; the examples under \"Traits are interfaces\" and \"Async is structured\" are checked and run today. The rest of these notes is in v0.0.2. Fortis will feel familiar to a Rust programmer: values have one owner, moves are real, shared mutation is not allowed, failures are values, and the native backend emits safe Rust ( LR 1 ). The differences come from one design choice: everything a reader needs to know about a line is written on the line, and nothing is inferred from somewhere the reader cannot see ( LR 1 , LR 1.1 ). This page goes through what carries over and what does not."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#borrows-are-written-at-the-call","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"Borrows are written at the call","m":"human","x":"Borrows are written at the call # In Rust the borrow is written at the call ( &x , &mut x ) and a move is silent. In Fortis a read loan is the silent case, and every other use of an ownership-bearing place carries a marker: editable for an exclusive loan, move to transfer ownership, copy for an independent duplicate ( LR 7.5 , LR 7.6 ). Rust Fortis parameter Fortis call fn f(a: &Account) func f(a: Account) f(account) fn f(a: &mut Account) func f(a: editable Account) f(editable account) fn f(a: Account) func f(a: owned Account) f(move account) or f(copy account) account.clone() copy account Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Account { export owner : String export balance : Int } // A plain parameter is a read loan for the call, like `&Account`. func describe ( a : Account ) String => \"${a.owner}: ${a.balance}\" // An `editable` parameter is an exclusive loan, like `&mut Account`. func deposit ( a : editable Account , amount : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) { a . balance = Int . saturatingAdd ( a . balance , amount ) } // An `owned` parameter takes ownership, like passing `Account` by value. func close ( a : owned Account ) Int => a . balance func main ( console : Console ) { let mut account = Account ( owner : \"ada\" , balance : 10 ) console . print ( describe ( account ) ) deposit ( editable account , 5 ) let backup = copy account console . print ( \"closed with ${close(move account)}\" ) console . print ( describe ( backup ) ) } output ada: 10 closed with 15 ada: 15 The marker is also required where Rust would move silently: let other = account on an ownership-bearing value is E-OWN-001 , because a reader could not tell a move from a copy ( LR 7.5 ). A method with owned self is called as move account.close() . A method with editable self is called as account.deposit(5) , like Rust's &mut self auto-borrow, and only on a place that may change: a let mut binding (Rust's let mut ), an editable parameter or an exclusive loan ( LR 7.5 , LR 7.6 ). Fortis // reject: E-OWN-001 class Account { export owner : String } func main ( ) { let account = Account ( owner : \"ada\" ) let other = account // reject: E-OWN-001 } copy is Rust's Clone without the escape hatches: it is compiler-generated, field by field, cannot fail and never shares mutable state, and it is rejected for types that are not copyable, such as Shared and Mutex ( LR 6.11 , LR 7.5 ). The small set of trivial types (integers, Bool , Decimal , Duration , enums of trivial payloads, trivial class es) are copied implicitly, like Rust's Copy types ( LR 6.11 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#loans-are-lexical","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"Loans are lexical","m":"human","x":"Loans are lexical # Rust's non-lexical lifetimes end a borrow at its last use. Fortis loans end at the end of the block that holds them, and there is no shortening ( LR 7.3 ). Code that Rust accepts can therefore be rejected: Fortis // reject: E-OWN-011 class Counter { export value : Int } test \"a loan lasts until its block ends\" { let mut counter = Counter ( value : 0 ) let r = editable counter r . value = 1 expect counter . value == 1 // reject: E-OWN-011 } The fix is the one Fortis always uses to end a loan early: close a block ( LR 7.3 ). Fortis // accept class Counter { export value : Int } test \"an inner block ends the loan\" { let mut counter = Counter ( value : 0 ) { let r = editable counter r . value = 1 } expect counter . value == 1 } The cost is an occasional extra pair of braces; the benefit is that a loan's extent is visible on the page rather than computed by the checker. Calls get the same treatment Rust's two-phase borrows give them: arguments reserve their loans first and activate them together ( LR 5.7 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#no-lifetime-parameters-and-no-stored-loans","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"No lifetime parameters, and no stored loans","m":"human","x":"No lifetime parameters, and no stored loans # A function that returns a loan names the parameter it borrows from with from , and the call site keeps the loan visibly with alias ( LR 7.6 ). That replaces Rust's lifetime parameters in signatures; a function borrowing from two parameters is not expressible and is rejected, so it returns an owned value instead ( LR 7.6 ). Fortis // accept class User { export name : String } pure func nameOf ( user : User ) StringView from user { return alias user . name } test \"a returned loan names its origin\" { let user = User ( name : \"ada\" ) let name = nameOf ( alias user ) expect name == \"ada\" } There is no Fortis equivalent of struct Line<'a> { text: &'a str } . In Language 1.0 a field never holds a loan of an owner, and a class cannot declare a lifetime parameter ( LR 7.7 ); the one loan a field may hold is a loan from static , such as a string literal, which borrows from no owner ( LR 7.7 ): Fortis // reject: E-OWN-053 export class Line { // reject: E-OWN-053 export number : Int export text : StringView } A value that must live as long as something else is owned, copied, held through an explicit Shared , or passed to each call that needs it; a parser that would keep a view of its input keeps offsets into it instead ( LR 7.7 ). The standard views and cursors ( StringView , ListView , ListCursor ) are compiler-known types that carry a loan, usable in locals, parameters and from returns ( LR 7.7 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#result-is-outcome-and-it-cannot-be-ignored","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"Result is Outcome, and it cannot be ignored","m":"human","x":"Result is Outcome , and it cannot be ignored # A try func f() T returns an Outcome[T] ( LR 10.1 ). Where Rust warns about an unused Result , Fortis rejects it: an outcome is a linear value and must be consumed exactly once ( E-OBL-001 , LR 10.1 , LR 6.12 ). The consuming operations replace Rust's operators and combinators ( LR 10.2 ): Rust Fortis f()? f() or fail f().unwrap_or(v) f() or recover v match f() { Ok(x) => \u2026, Err(e) => \u2026 } match f() { success(x) => \u2026, failure(e) => \u2026 } or f() catch e { \u2026 } f().unwrap() none: there is no unwrap Fortis // accept export error Odd { code : \"demo.odd\" payload { value : Int } } pure try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 != 0 { fail Odd ( value : n ) } return n / 2 } pure try func quarter ( n : Int ) Int { let h = half ( n ) or fail return half ( h ) or fail } test \"outcomes\" { expect quarter ( 12 ) == 3 let fallback = quarter ( 6 ) catch e { expect e . code == \"demo.odd\" recover 0 } expect fallback == 0 } Fortis // reject: E-OBL-001 export error Odd { code : \"demo.odd\" } pure try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 != 0 { fail Odd ( ) } return n / 2 } func main ( ) { half ( 3 ) // reject: E-OBL-001 } There are no panics in ordinary code. What Rust handles with a panic (an index out of bounds, unwrap on None ) is either proved impossible by the checker or written as an outcome; the only process-ending events are the closed set of defects, such as stack exhaustion, which no catch can intercept ( LR 10.8 ). A function that silently discards a failure must say so with the recovers modifier, so a signature shows who swallowed one ( LR 8.1 , LR 10.4 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#integer-arithmetic-is-proved-not-wrapped-or-panicked","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"Integer arithmetic is proved, not wrapped or panicked","m":"human","x":"Integer arithmetic is proved, not wrapped or panicked # In Rust, a + b on i64 panics on overflow in debug builds and wraps in release builds. In Fortis it is accepted only when the checker can show it cannot overflow; otherwise it is rejected with E-REF-002 , and you say what happens on overflow: handle it, a + b or fail (Rust's checked_add(b)? ), or use a named wrapping or saturating operation ( LR 6.4 ). Integer behaviour never depends on the build mode ( LR 6.4 ). Fortis // reject: E-REF-002 pure func sum ( a : Int , b : Int ) Int => a + b // reject: E-REF-002 Fortis // accept pure func sum ( a : Int < 0 .. 1000 > , b : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int < 0 .. 2000 > => a + b pure try func checkedSum ( a : Int , b : Int ) Int => a + b or fail test \"arithmetic\" { expect sum ( 1000 , 1000 ) == 2000 expect checkedSum ( 1 , 2 ) == 3 expect Int . wrappingAdd ( 9223372036854775807 , 1 ) == - 9223372036854775807 - 1 } The refinement Int<0..1000> is part of the type; the checker tracks intervals through each function ( LR 6.3 ). The proofs chapter describes what it can establish on its own."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#traits-are-interfaces","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"Traits are interfaces","m":"human","x":"Traits are interfaces # An interface is a trait without default method bodies and without associated types; a type implements one only by saying so ( implements , derive or an implement \u2026 for block), never by having methods of the right names ( LR 9.5 ). Default behaviour on top of an interface is a mixin ( LR 9.6 ). Generic functions take [T] and bound it with where , and are monomorphised, as in Rust ( LR 6.10 ). Fortis // accept interface Shape { pure func area ( self ) Int } class Square implements Shape { export side : Int < 0 .. 1000 > export pure func area ( self ) Int => self . side * self . side } pure func largest [ T ] ( shapes : ListView [ T ] ) Int where T implements Shape { let mut best = 0 for s in shapes { if s . area ( ) > best { best = s . area ( ) } } return best } test \"largest area\" { expect largest ( List . of ( Square ( side : 3 ) , Square ( side : 5 ) ) ) == 25 } There is no operator overloading: == and the comparisons dispatch to the built-in Equatable and Comparable interfaces, and a sum of two Money values is Money.add(a, b) ( LR 6.10 ). Coherence works like Rust's orphan rule: an implementation lives in the package that defines the interface or the type ( LR 9.5 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#closures-declare-their-kind-and-their-captures","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"Closures declare their kind and their captures","m":"human","x":"Closures declare their kind and their captures # Rust infers Fn , FnMut and FnOnce and infers what a closure captures. Fortis writes both: the kind is fn , fn mut or fn once , and the capture list names every captured variable with its marker ( LR 8.2 , LR 8.3 ). A move capture is Rust's move closure for one variable; a closure that moves a captured value out is fn once and is called as move f() ( LR 8.2 ). Fortis // accept test \"closure kinds\" { let limit = 3 let small = fn [ limit ] ( n : Int ) Bool => n < limit let mut seen = 0 { let mut count = fn mut [ editable seen ] ( n : Int ) Void { seen = Int . saturatingAdd ( seen , n ) } count ( 4 ) count ( 5 ) } let message = String . from ( \"done\" ) let finish = fn once [ move message ] ( ) String => move message expect small ( 2 ) expect seen == 9 expect move finish ( ) == \"done\" } A closure is never boxed behind your back: each lambda has its own concrete type, and a heterogeneous collection of closures needs the explicit dynamic fn type, which allocates ( LR 8.4 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#shared-ownership-is-explicit","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"Shared ownership is explicit","m":"human","x":"Shared ownership is explicit # Rc / Arc is Shared[T] and Weak is Weak[T] . The only way to get a second handle is s.retain() , so every reference-count increment is visible; copy of a Shared is rejected ( E-OWN-002 , LR 7.5 , LR 7.8 ). A Shared[T] gives read access only: shared mutation goes through Mutex[T] or an atomic ( LR 7.8 , LR 12.7 ). Fortis // reject: E-OWN-002 class Config { export name : String } func main ( ) { let config = Shared . create ( Config ( name : \"prod\" ) ) let second = copy config // reject: E-OWN-002 } Fortis // accept class Config { export name : String } test \"retain is the visible clone of a handle\" { let config = Shared . create ( Config ( name : \"prod\" ) ) let second = config . retain ( ) expect second . name == \"prod\" }"},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#effects-are-part-of-the-signature","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"Effects are part of the signature","m":"human","x":"Effects are part of the signature # Rust tracks some effects through the type system ( async , Result ) and none of the others. Fortis has a closed set of eight effects, computed for every function from its body and its callees, and four modifiers that state facts about them: pure (no I/O, no outside mutation, no nondeterminism, no suspension, no blocking), noalloc , async and try ( LR 11.1 , LR 11.2 ). async , try and recovers must be written whenever the function has the effect; pure and noalloc are promises the checker verifies ( LR 11.3 ). There is no ambient I/O either: there is no println! reaching standard output from anywhere, only methods on capability values that main obtains once and passes down ( LR 11.6 ). A pure function that prints is rejected with E-EFF-001 , reported at the statement that introduces the effect ( LR 11.1 ): Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 import fortis . host { Console } pure func greet ( console : Console ) { // reject: E-EFF-001 (a pure function takes no capability) console . print ( \"hello\" ) // reject: E-EFF-001 } func main ( ) { }"},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#async-is-structured","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"Async is structured","m":"human","x":"Async is structured # Like Rust's, a Fortis future does nothing until it is awaited ( LR 12.3 ). Unlike Rust, there is no detached spawn by default: tasks are launched on a scope , which joins or cancels every one before its block ends ( LR 12.5 ). Because the scope always joins first, a cooperative task may borrow locals declared before the scope, with no 'static bound and no Arc ( LR 12.5 ). Pool launches ( launchParallel , launchBlocking ) require Shareable data and no loans, the equivalent of Send + 'static ( LR 12.5 , LR 12.7 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } async func total ( numbers : List [ Int ] ) Int { let mut sum = 0 for n in numbers { sum = Int . saturatingAdd ( sum , n ) } return sum } async func main ( console : Console ) { let odds = List . of ( 1 , 3 , 5 ) let evens = List . of ( 2 , 4 ) scope tasks { let a = tasks . launch ( total ( odds ) ) let b = tasks . launch ( total ( evens ) ) let x = await a let y = await b console . print ( \"${x} and ${y}\" ) } } output 9 and 6 A cancelled task unwinds at its await , running deferred blocks; cancellation is not a failure and cannot be caught ( LR 12.4 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-rust.html#what-the-native-backend-produces","p":"Notes for readers coming from Rust","h":"What the native backend produces","m":"human","x":"What the native backend produces # The production backend lowers checked Fortis to a Rust crate under #![forbid(unsafe_code)] , and rustc re-checks the loans and types it can express ( LR 7.10 , LR C.1 ). Ownership situations with no safe Rust shape are rejected before lowering rather than lowered with unsafe ( LR 7.10 , LR C.2 ). The lowering appendix lists each construct's Rust shape."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-typescript.html#notes-for-readers-coming-from-typescript","p":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript","h":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript","m":"human","x":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript # Human mode. Source of truth: the LR; each note cites the section that states the rule. Preview: newtypes ( LR 6.9 ) arrive in v0.1 and async with join ( LR 12.3 , 12.5 ) in v0.3; the examples under \"Types are nominal\" and \"Promises are futures, and concurrency is scoped\" are checked today. The rest of these notes is in v0.0.2. TypeScript adds static types to a dynamic language and leaves the runtime alone. Fortis is statically checked and compiled all the way down: the checker's view is the program's behaviour, there is no any , and a type can never be wrong at run time ( LR 1 , LR 6.1 ). Much of the syntax will look familiar (braces, let , ${\u2026} interpolation, async / await , ?. and ?? ); the meaning underneath is stricter. This page goes through the differences you will meet first."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-typescript.html#types-are-nominal","p":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript","h":"Types are nominal","m":"human","x":"Types are nominal # TypeScript compares object types by shape: any value with the right fields fits. Fortis compares by name. Two classes with identical fields are different types, and type always declares a new nominal type rather than an alias ( LR 6.1 , LR 6.9 ). That is what lets an id type protect itself: Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 type UserId = Int < 1 .. > type OrderId = Int < 1 .. > pure func describe ( id : UserId ) String => \"user ${id}\" func main ( ) { let order = OrderId ( 7 ) discard describe ( order ) // reject: E-TYP-040 } There is no top type ( any , unknown ), no implicit conversion between distinct types and no runtime type inspection ( LR 6.1 , LR 1 ). Anonymous records ( { name: A, other: B } ) and tuples exist for local convenience but may not appear in an exported signature ( LR 6.9 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-typescript.html#null-is-one-thing-and-nothing-is-truthy","p":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript","h":"null is one thing, and nothing is truthy","m":"human","x":"null is one thing, and nothing is truthy # There is no undefined , and null exists only as the absent case of an optional type T? ( LR 6.8 ). ?? and ?. work as you expect, and a null test narrows the type inside the branch, as TypeScript's control-flow analysis does ( LR 6.8 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class User { export name : String export nickname : String ? } pure func greeting ( user : User ) String { if user . nickname != null { return \"hi ${user.nickname}\" } return \"hello ${user.name}\" } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( greeting ( User ( name : \"Ada\" , nickname : null ) ) ) console . print ( greeting ( User ( name : \"Brian\" , nickname : \"Bri\" ) ) ) let maybe : Int ? = null console . print ( \"${maybe ?? 0}\" ) } output hello Ada hi Bri 0 What TypeScript allows and Fortis does not is using a value as a condition. A condition must be a Bool ; an Int , a string or an optional is E-TYP-010 ( LR 5.2 ). Write the comparison you mean. Fortis // reject: E-TYP-010 pure func label ( count : Int ) StringView from static { if count { // reject: E-TYP-010 return \"some\" } return \"none\" } T? , Bool and a fallible outcome are three different types with three different operators ( ?? , || , or ), and none converts into another ( LR 1 , LR 6.8 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-typescript.html#exceptions-become-outcomes","p":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript","h":"Exceptions become outcomes","m":"human","x":"Exceptions become outcomes # Fortis has no exceptions and no throw . A function that may fail is declared try ; calling it gives an outcome, which the caller must handle, and the compiler rejects code that ignores one ( LR 10.1 , LR 10.2 ). The handling forms are written at the call: or fail passes the failure on (like letting an exception propagate), or recover v replaces it, and catch e { \u2026 } handles it in a block ( LR 10.2 , LR 10.4 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error NotFound { code : \"users.not_found\" payload { id : Int } message : \"no user ${id}\" } pure try func lookup ( id : Int ) String { if id == 1 { return \"ada\" } fail NotFound ( id : id ) } func main ( console : Console ) { let first = lookup ( 1 ) catch e { report e recover \"nobody\" } let second = lookup ( 2 ) catch e { console . print ( \"lookup failed: ${e.code}\" ) report e recover \"nobody\" } console . print ( \"${first}, ${second}\" ) } output lookup failed: users.not_found ada, nobody The difference from try { \u2026 } catch (e) { \u2026 } is that the handler covers exactly one operation, its left operand, so you can always see which call a handler is for ( LR 10.4 ). Failures carry a stable code declared by an error declaration, so callers match on is NotFound , not on message text ( LR 10.3 , LR 10.5 ). report e writes the failure once to the diagnostic plane and consumes it ( LR 10.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-typescript.html#assigning-an-object-does-not-share-it","p":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript","h":"Assigning an object does not share it","m":"human","x":"Assigning an object does not share it # In TypeScript const b = a makes b refer to the same object as a , and a change through one is visible through the other. In Fortis every value has one owner, and copying, borrowing or moving it is written down: copy a , alias a or move a ( LR 7.5 ). A bare let b = a on an object is rejected ( E-OWN-001 ), so the question never arises silently. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } class Cart { export items : List [ String ] } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut cart = Cart ( items : List . of ( \"tea\" ) ) let snapshot = copy cart cart . items . push ( \"milk\" ) console . print ( \"cart has ${cart.items.count()}, snapshot has ${snapshot.items.count()}\" ) } output cart has 2, snapshot has 1 let mut cart is the other half of the rule: a value that may change says so where it is declared, and only there may cart.items.push(\u2026) change it ( LR 7.5 ). A function that may change its argument declares the parameter editable , and the caller writes f(editable cart) ; a function that receives a plain parameter can read it but not change it ( LR 7.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-typescript.html#numbers-are-not-all-one-type","p":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript","h":"Numbers are not all one type","m":"human","x":"Numbers are not all one type # TypeScript's number is a 64-bit float. Fortis has 64-bit integers ( Int ), sized integers, Float (IEEE binary64), exact Decimal for money, and BigInt ( LR 6.2 ). Integer arithmetic must be proved not to overflow, or use the checked operations, which fail instead ( LR 6.4 ). Decimal has no operators in Language 1.0; its arithmetic is the checked Decimal.add , Decimal.mul and so on ( LR 6.5 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } recovers func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${0.1 + 0.2}\" ) let price = Decimal . add ( 0.1d , 0.2d ) or recover 0d console . print ( \"${price}\" ) console . print ( \"${7 / 2}\" ) } output 0.30000000000000004 0.3 3 The first line is ordinary float arithmetic, exactly as in TypeScript; Decimal gives the answer a ledger needs, and integer division truncates toward zero ( LR 6.4 , LR 6.5 ). There is no + on strings: operators are arithmetic only, and text is built with interpolation or a text.Builder ( LR 1.1 , LR 6.6 ). main is declared recovers because or recover 0d discards a possible failure without reporting it. The checker requires the modifier on any function that does that, so a reader of the signature knows the function can swallow a failure ( LR 10.2 , LR 11.2 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-typescript.html#unions-are-enums-and-match-must-be-exhaustive","p":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript","h":"Unions are enums, and match must be exhaustive","m":"human","x":"Unions are enums, and match must be exhaustive # A discriminated union ( { kind: \"circle\"; radius: number } | \u2026 ) is a Fortis enum with payloads, and a switch with a never check is a match , which must cover every variant without a default ( LR 9.7 , LR 9.8 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } enum Shape { Circle ( radius : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Rect ( width : Int < 0 .. 1000 > , height : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) } pure func area ( s : Shape ) Int => match s { Circle ( radius : r ) => 3 * r * r Rect ( width : w , height : h ) => w * h } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${area(Circle(radius: 2))} ${area(Rect(width: 3, height: 4))}\" ) } output 12 12 Leaving a variant out is an error naming it ( E-MAT-001 ), and a catch-all is not allowed as the last arm of a match over an enum, so adding a variant finds every place that must handle it ( LR 9.8 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-typescript.html#arrow-functions-list-their-captures","p":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript","h":"Arrow functions list their captures","m":"human","x":"Arrow functions list their captures # A TypeScript arrow function captures whatever it mentions. A Fortis lambda lists what it captures and how, and names its kind: fn reads, fn mut may change what it captured, fn once runs once ( LR 8.2 , LR 8.3 ). A lambda with no captures has no brackets. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { let prices : List [ Int < 0 .. 1000 > ] = List . of ( 120 , 80 , 45 , 300 ) let limit = 100 let cheap = prices . filter ( fn [ limit ] ( p : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Bool => p < limit ) let doubled = prices . map ( fn ( p : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => p * 2 ) console . print ( \"${cheap} ${doubled}\" ) } output [80, 45] [240, 160, 90, 600] map , filter and countWhere take pure callbacks; a lambda whose body has no effects qualifies without saying so, and anything that does I/O belongs in a for loop ( LR 6.7 , LR 11.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-typescript.html#promises-are-futures-and-concurrency-is-scoped","p":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript","h":"Promises are futures, and concurrency is scoped","m":"human","x":"Promises are futures, and concurrency is scoped # async and await read the same, with two differences. A Fortis future does not start when it is created: the body runs when it is awaited or launched ( LR 12.3 ). And there is no fire-and-forget: concurrent work is launched on a scope , which waits for or cancels every task before it ends, so Promise.all becomes a scope or join ( LR 12.5 ). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } async func square ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * n async func main ( console : Console ) { let both = await join ( square ( 3 ) , square ( 4 ) ) console . print ( \"${both.0} ${both.1}\" ) } output 9 16 A future must be awaited or launched, or deliberately discarded if it never started and holds nothing linear and no exclusive loan; dropping one by accident is an error, like any unconsumed outcome ( LR 12.3 , LR 6.12 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/from-typescript.html#modules-run-nothing-when-imported","p":"Notes for readers coming from TypeScript","h":"Modules run nothing when imported","m":"human","x":"Modules run nothing when imported # A TypeScript module can run code when it is first imported. A Fortis module contains only declarations and constants, so importing one never executes anything ( LR 4.2 ). Side effects begin in main , and I/O needs a capability value: main declares the ones it needs as parameters, func main(console: Console) , and passes them to whatever needs them; there is no global console ( LR 4.6 , LR 11.6 ). Logging is the exception: log.info(...) writes a log record to standard error from any function, with no capability ( LR 13 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/glossary.html#glossary","p":"Glossary","h":"Glossary","m":"human","x":"Glossary # Human mode. Agent mode: Glossary, by source . The words this guide uses with a precise meaning, each with the section that defines it. Where a word has two meanings, both are given. The Language Reference keeps its own short glossary of the terms that name the parts of Fortis and its distribution ( LR E ); this one also defines the words used inside the language."},{"u":"docs/guide/glossary.html#the-pieces-of-the-system","p":"Glossary","h":"The pieces of the system","m":"human","x":"The pieces of the system # Package : the unit of distribution, versioning and visibility: a directory with project.yaml , a lockfile and src/ . Two versions of one package in one build are two different packages ( LR 4.1 ). Module : one source file under src/ ; its path below src/ , with / written as . and without .ft , is its name. A module holds only declarations and constants, so importing one never runs code ( LR 4.2 ). Kit : a named, versioned bundle of packages that ships with the distribution, such as the HTTP kit, the Data kit or the Web UI kit. A kit extends Fortis only through libraries, aspects, declaration heads, embedding bindings and verifiers, and never changes what a core program means. Compatibility set : the published set of standard packages, fortis and the kits, at the versions tested together; a dependency on a standard package must name one of them at the set's version ( LR 4.1 ). Toolchain component : a tool that ships with Fortis and works on programs rather than inside them: the daemon, the language server, the SDK generators. Toolchain components are not kits. Backend : an implementation that runs checked programs. There are three, which must agree: the definitional interpreter (the reference, run by fortis run ), the development VM ( fortisc vm ) and native code (safe Rust, built by fortis build ) ( LR 1 ). Build mode : how a program is built: dev (on the VM), native-dev or release . Optimisation, debug symbols and profile-guided optimisation are settings of a mode. Lint level : how strictly lints are reported: under the baseline level an unused import is a warning, under the strict level an error ( LR 4.3 ). Target feature set : which standard modules exist for a compilation target ( core , alloc , host , async , web , browser ); distinct from effects and from capabilities ( LR 11.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/glossary.html#declarations-and-syntax","p":"Glossary","h":"Declarations and syntax","m":"human","x":"Declarations and syntax # Declaration head (or head ): a fixed keyword that introduces a declaration whose body is validated by a versioned schema and elaborated into ordinary core declarations before type checking ( LR 16 ). The core reserves every head and defines observation itself ( LR 13 ); explore belongs to developer tooling, a toolchain component ( LR E ); the kits define application , context , model , component and view ( LR 16 ). Elaboration : rewriting a head, or a function with aspects applied, into ordinary core declarations before ownership and effect checking, so that the result is checked like any other code ( LR 16 , LR 11.8 ). Extension slot : one of the four places a kit's syntax can enter a module: a head block, an inject member, an aspect application, a cases table ( LR 16 ). Raw template region : the text of a view { \u2026 } in a head body, handed unlexed to the Web UI kit ( LR 16 ). Aspect : declared behaviour applied around a function with @name(args) : before and after hooks, an on failure mapping and metadata. An aspect adds; a contract subtracts ( LR 11.8 ). Contract : a named conjunction of modifiers, forbidden effects and required or excluded authorities, written in the modifier position. Applying one never changes behaviour; it only makes the checker stricter ( LR 11.5 ). Mixin : a set of methods a class applies with with ; it may require methods but never adds state ( LR 9.6 ). Newtype : a nominal type declared with type Name = Representation ; it never interchanges with its representation or with another newtype over it ( LR 6.9 ). Refinement : a predicate on a built-in type, written in <\u2026> : Int<0..100> , String<scalars: 1..120> ( LR 6.3 ). Binding (of a name): a name introduced by let , let mut , a parameter, a pattern or a capture list ( LR 5.1 , LR 7.2 , LR 9.8 ). Binding (of an embedded language): a versioned package that gives a language fenced blocks in Fortis, through the embedding protocol: its meaning is fixed by the compiler and its checking is a verifier (being designed, Embedded languages ). Verifier : a package that can only make the check stricter: it reads the checked program and returns records, never code; it is pure, deterministic, bounded and holds no capability."},{"u":"docs/guide/glossary.html#values-and-ownership","p":"Glossary","h":"Values and ownership","m":"human","x":"Values and ownership # Place : a path that denotes storage: a binding, a field path rooted in a binding or self , or an Array element with a literal index ( LR 7.2 ). Owner : the binding that owns a value until the value is moved out of it ( LR 7.2 ). Loan : a read-only ( alias ) or exclusive ( editable ) reference to a place; loans are lexical ( LR 7.3 ). Marker : one of copy , alias , move , editable written before a place, saying how the place is used ( LR 7.5 ). Loan-carrying value : a value that stores a loan: the standard views ( StringView , BytesView , ListView[T] ) and cursors ( LR 7.7 ). Trivial : a type whose values are duplicated implicitly and never need a marker, such as the integers, Bool and Duration ( LR 6.11 ). Copyable : a type that supports copy , an independent field-wise duplicate ( LR 6.11 ). Linear : a type whose values must be consumed exactly once on every path, such as Outcome[T] , Failure , Future[T] and Task[T] ( LR 6.12 ). Shareable : a type whose values may cross to a parallel or blocking pool ( LR 12.7 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/glossary.html#failures-and-effects","p":"Glossary","h":"Failures and effects","m":"human","x":"Failures and effects # Outcome : the result of a try function, Outcome[T] : a success value or a failure, linear until consumed ( LR 10.1 ). Failure : the single failure type, with a stable code, a typed payload, an origin and a cause chain ( LR 10.3 ). Obligation : the duty to consume a linear value; the checker tracks each one until it is discharged ( LR 7.1 , LR 10.1 ). Defect : a condition that cannot be an outcome because the program's assumptions are broken, such as stack exhaustion; never caught ( LR 10.8 ). Effect : one of the eight facts a function's body may have: alloc , io , mutate , nondet , suspend , block , fail , recovers ( LR 11.1 ). Effect summary : the subset of the eight effects a function has, computed from its body and its callees ( LR 11.1 , LR 11.3 ). Write footprint : the editable parameters a function writes through; writes through them are not mutate ( LR 11.3 ). Fresh storage : storage created in the current activation and not yet stored anywhere reachable from a parameter, capture or self ; mutating it is not mutate ( LR 11.1 ). Authority : a value that grants input or output, implementing the marker interface Authority ( LR 11.6 ). Capability : a process-wide authority ( FileSystem , Network , Clock , Random , Environment , Process , Supervisor , Console , SecretAccess , RawSql ), created once by the runtime and supplied to the entry main , which declares the ones it needs as parameters ( LR 4.6 , LR 11.6 ). Per-activation authority : an authority minted by a kit's adapter from a capability for one unit of work, such as RequestContext or Tx ( LR 11.6 ). Trusted : a standard-library intrinsic with no Fortis body, whose claims are reported as trusted, never as proved ( LR 11.3 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/glossary.html#concurrency-and-observation","p":"Glossary","h":"Concurrency and observation","m":"human","x":"Concurrency and observation # Scope : a block that owns tasks, scope name { \u2026 } , and joins or cancels them all before it exits ( LR 12.5 ). Task : a launched future, Task[T] , linear until awaited or detached ( LR 12.5 ). Cancellation point : an await (and, on the pools, checkpoint() ): the only place a task observes cancellation ( LR 12.4 ). Observation schema : an observation declaration naming the typed facts an activation records ( LR 13 ). Observation frame : the record of one activation's captured fields, assigned by capture ( LR 13 ). Event : a record emitted by record , once per execution ( LR 13 ). Task reporter : the diagnostic-plane sink for reports, cleanup reports and frames ( LR 15 ). Diagnostic plane : where reports and observation records go; nothing on it can feed back into the program ( LR 11.7 ). Record : as a tool's output, a schema-valid object (a finding, a test result, a runtime failure) printed as text on a terminal and as one JSON line otherwise."},{"u":"docs/guide/known-limitations.html#known-limitations-of-v002","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","m":"human","x":"Known limitations of v0.0.2 # Human mode. Agent mode: Known limitations, by defect . The release note is v0.0.2 . v0.0.2 is a subset of the language (the ladder in the delivery plan). These are the open defects inside that subset that do not block the release: a valid program the checkers reject, a missing operation, two tools that disagree, or a checked program one backend refuses with a clear error and a non-zero exit while another runs it (X-343). A program the checkers accept and then run wrongly is listed only when v0.0.1 had the same defect: v0.0.2 is no worse, and those are in a section of their own (X-416). The last section lists what v0.0.2 leaves for later by design, as its release note says. v0.0.2 is a subset of the language (the ladder in the delivery plan). These are the open defects inside that subset that do not block the release: a valid program the checkers reject, a missing operation, two tools that disagree, or a checked program one backend refuses with a clear error and a non-zero exit while another runs it (X-343). A program the checkers accept and every backend then runs wrongly is not in this list. The last section lists what v0.0.2 leaves for later, as its release note says: by design, or as a gap recorded with its defect. This page, like its agent-mode twin, is generated from the defect register and the release's own list; edit those, not the page."},{"u":"docs/guide/known-limitations.html#valid-programs-the-checkers-reject-and-library-gaps","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"Valid programs the checkers reject, and library gaps","m":"human","x":"Valid programs the checkers reject, and library gaps # Defect What you meet Workaround D-51.5 An unused import is accepted with no warning. Delete the import. Nothing else changes. D-52.1 An error constructor is typed as its own kind, so a use that the language accepts where a failure is expected is rejected. Match the constructor's own type, or construct the failure where its own name is expected. D-52.4 Returning a call that passes a loan-holding closure is rejected even when the result is a plain integer. None recorded. D-52.6 A class you declare with the same name as a prelude type, such as Task, is checked as the prelude's linear type. Name the class something the prelude does not declare. D-54.6 For a parameter named with a keyword, fortisc vm reports a generic syntax error and fortisc check reports the keyword-specific one. Rename the parameter. Both tools refuse the program. D-58.1 Map has no iterate operation. A for loop over a map yields key and value tuples, which the language reference does not state. Write for over the map when a key and a value together are enough. D-58.7 Map has no remove and no contains. None recorded. Build a new map without the key, or scan it. D-58.9 Every text accessor can fail, so a comparator written as a pure function of two texts must recover. Recover inside the comparator, or do not mark it pure. D-251 A variant whose name is also a class's is rejected by both checkers, so the program does not reach the VM. List.of(Red) with the list's element type written out runs on the interpreter, the VM and native. Write the variant with its enum prefix, or do not declare a class of the same name. The list form already runs when the expected type is written. D-252 Both checkers reject an optional-chained method call such as lights.get(0)?.code(). The call does not reach the VM. Match the optional and call the method on the present value. D-285 A package whose manifest the loader refuses (conformance/packages/bad.policy) makes fortis check, run and test exit 2 with a message on standard error, and fortisc check exit 1 with a names record on project.yaml. Both tools refuse the package. Read fortisc's record; it names the manifest. D-343 A property runs 25 generated cases with fortis test and 100 with fortisc test, from different generators, so a property that fails on few inputs can pass on one and fail on the other. Run properties with fortisc test as well, and turn a failure it finds into an example test. D-344 The executor trace of a native build has no drop event for an owned optional local, for a temporary a ?? fallback made, or for the bindings of a match arm that owns its scrutinee; the interpreter and the VM trace each, and all three trace a fallback a ?? moved into its result. Cleanup runs once on all three, and the output and the log records agree. Compare traces on the interpreter and the VM; the native trace leaves those drops out. D-363 A tuple that holds a loan, such as (xs.get(0), 1), cannot yet be reassigned to a let mut binding that holds one, or returned from a function declared to return one: both checkers reject it as a loan where an owned value is required, although the Language Reference allows both. The same with an optional, t = xs.get(1), is accepted. Keep the loan in a binding of its own (let t = xs.get(0)) and the other values beside it, and reassign or return those separately. D-365 A defer that changes a list, such as defer { let v = xs.pop() }, is rejected while a binding of the same block holds a loan from that list inside an optional (let t = xs.get(0)), although a deferred block runs after its block's own loans have ended. Put the loan in an inner block that ends before the defer's block does, or make the change after the block. D-400 A function that binds a loan of a type whose cleanup does I/O (let t = alias b.t, or a ?? whose fallback fails) is charged that cleanup's effects, so a function that destroys nothing may be refused as not pure or asked to declare io. De"},{"u":"docs/guide/known-limitations.html#valid-programs-the-checkers-reject-and-library-gaps","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"Valid programs the checkers reject, and library gaps","m":"human","x":"s effects, so a function that destroys nothing may be refused as not pure or asked to declare io. Declare the effect, or keep such loans out of functions declared pure. D-401 A let mut cursor cannot be reassigned (the checkers report a move of a lent owner), although a cursor is owned by its binding ( LR 7.7 ). Make a new cursor in a binding of its own. D-402 After match move o of a binding that holds a loan (let o = xs.get(0)), the loan o held is not released, so the owner stays frozen for the rest of the block (xs.push is refused as a change under a live loan). New with this release's loan holders. Put the match and the binding in an inner block, so the loan ends with it."},{"u":"docs/guide/known-limitations.html#one-backend-refuses-the-program","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"One backend refuses the program","m":"human","x":"One backend refuses the program # Defect What you meet Workaround D-54.10 A catch that returns inside a for is refused: fortisc test and fortis build exit 1, reporting that the typed IR uses a moved binding. The interpreter's test passes. A move in a returning branch, the other half of this entry, builds and its VM test passes. Run that test with fortis test. Keep the catch out of the loop, or bind the failure's value before the loop continues. D-55.6 A narrowed optional is not unwrapped in the native build. copy of a String? after != null, and a field read through a narrowed shared optional, each make fortis build exit 1 (rustc E0308, or no such field). The interpreter and the VM print the value. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-55.8 A dynamic fn in a list or a field does not build natively (rustc E0308 for the list, E0277 for the field). The interpreter and the VM run the programs. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-55.16 A local optional narrowed by != null and moved into a non-optional field is not unwrapped. fortis build exits 1 (rustc E0308). The interpreter and the VM print 7. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-156 A function-type parameter that owns its argument, fn(owned T), is lowered as a borrowed parameter. fortis build exits 1 (rustc E0631 when a named function is passed, E0308 when a lambda is). The interpreter and the VM run the call. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-185 A function whose result is an exclusive loan of its parameter, editable Cell from c, returning editable c (the Language Reference's own example in 7.6), does not build natively, nor does any returned exclusive loan: fortis build exits 1 (rustc E0308), because the returned loan is lowered as a read reference. The interpreter and the VM run it. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. Or change the value through the parameter inside the function and return nothing. D-157.1 A closure stored in a class field does not build natively (rustc E0277, the field's display). The interpreter and the VM run the program. Assigning a second lambda to one closure binding is a separate item and is rejected by both checkers. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. A dynamic fn is the heterogeneous form, and a field of that type is D-55.8, which also refuses natively. D-244 A type named like an item the generated Rust uses does not build natively: a class Vec, From or String, or a type alias String, in any program, Option beside an optional, and Box beside a recursive type (rustc E0107, E0404 and others). The interpreter and the VM run the program. Result, Rc, HashMap and the variant names Some, None, Ok and Err build. Rename the type, or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-245 Arithmetic on literals at a sized integer type, such as let x: Int32 = -2147483647 - 1 or let y: Int32 = 2 + 3, does not build natively: the literals are emitted as 64-bit integers (rustc E0308). The interpreter and the VM print the value. A single literal builds. Write the folded literal (-2147483648, 5), or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-270 A function declared to return one non-dynamic fn type may return two different lambdas. The interpreter and the VM run both (3 and 4). fortis build exits 1 (rustc E0308). Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. Return one lambda, or declare the result dynamic fn when the two bodies really differ. D-342 A test's expect that compares an optional with a literal, such as expect first(names) == \"a\", does not build natively (rustc E0614). fortis test and fortisc test pass the test. Bind the present value first (let got = first(names) ?? \"none\", then expect got == \"a\"), or run the test with fortis test or fortisc test. D-360 A record literal that holds a loan, such as { item: xs.get(0), n: 1 }, does not build natively (rustc E0106), nor does a tuple with a loan passed to a read parameter of tuple type (E0308). The interpreter and the VM run them, and each cleanup runs once. A tuple holding a list's loan, such as (xs.get(0), 1), o"},{"u":"docs/guide/known-limitations.html#one-backend-refuses-the-program","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"One backend refuses the program","m":"human","x":"he VM run them, and each cleanup runs once. A tuple holding a list's loan, such as (xs.get(0), 1), or an alias loan of a local, such as (alias own, make(\"p\")), builds. A tuple bound first around a value of the function's own and returned as a from result (let t = (empty.get(0) ?? move d, 1), then return t from alias (Token, Int) from empty, which the Language Reference allows and the interpreter and the VM run correctly) does not build either (E0308), so a bearing aggregate from result has no spelling that builds natively yet. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. Or keep the loan in its own binding and pass it on its own. D-361 A binding that holds a loan on one path and a value of its own on another does not build natively (rustc E0308): an if that gives xs.get(0) on one branch and move d on the other, or assigning a new class value, which the binding then owns, to a let mut binding that held a loan. The interpreter and the VM run it, and the binding destroys only a value it owns. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. Or give the binding one kind of value: copy the loan, or keep the owned value in a binding of its own. D-353 A function whose result is an optional loan of text, such as (alias String from xs)? returning xs.get(0), does not build natively (rustc E0308). The interpreter and the VM run it. The same result of a class builds, except through a generic from function (D-387). Return an owned copy (let t = xs.get(0), then return copy t from a function returning String?), or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-387 A generic function whose result is an optional loan of its type parameter, such as func first[T](xs: List[T]) (alias T from xs)? => xs.get(0), does not build natively when it is instantiated at a class (rustc E0277). The interpreter and the VM run it. Write the function for the class (func first(xs: List[Token]) (alias Token from xs)?), which builds, or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-390 A byte string returned as the whole BytesView from xs result (=> b\"abc\"), or as a BytesView from static one, does not build natively (rustc E0515), nor does an if or match that gives a string literal on one branch and a view of a String through a loan on another (give \"empty\" beside give alias t.label, as StringView from t; rustc E0308), nor the outcome-expression spellings of that shape: or recover \"missing\" as a (alias StringView from xs)? result, or present(\"none\") in an if branch of one (rustc E0308). The interpreter and the VM run them. A string literal as the whole StringView from xs result builds. Return the literal from a function of its own, or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-415 An anonymous record returned by return r past a deferred block that reads it (let r = { n: 5, m: 8 }, defer { console.print(\"bye ${r.n}\") }, return r) does not build natively (rustc E0382), whatever its field types: the record is moved by the return, where a trivial class, a tuple or an enum is copied. Both checkers accept it and the interpreter and the VM run it. Use a class or a tuple for the value, or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-382 A marker written on a call of a module function, such as let t = alias first(alias xs), passes both checkers and runs on the VM and natively, but the interpreter stops with a dynamic failure (first is not declared). The marker belongs on the argument alone. Write let t = first(alias xs): the call lends what its result declares, and no marker is needed on the call. D-396 first() on a list whose element is a class, such as xs.first() on List[Token], does not build natively (rustc E0277, the element is not Clone). The interpreter and the VM run it. Use xs.get(0), which builds, or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-397 I/O through a capability held in a local's field (let d = make(console, ...); d.console.print(...)) passes both checkers and runs on the interpreter, but the VM's IR validation refuses it (the receiver is not derived from a parameter through places) and native co"},{"u":"docs/guide/known-limitations.html#one-backend-refuses-the-program","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"One backend refuses the program","m":"human","x":"IR validation refuses it (the receiver is not derived from a parameter through places) and native code reports that refusal as an internal compiler defect. Print through the capability parameter itself, or a binding made from it directly. D-399 Array.filled with its size taken from the expected type, such as let arr: Array[Int, 3] = Array.filled(7), passes both checkers, and every backend then refuses it. Write the size on the call: Array[Int, 3].filled(7). D-354 An owned optional local of a class with cleanup, written without a marker on the left of a ?? that moves its fallback (let p: Token? = none ?? move d), does not build natively (rustc E0382). The interpreter and the VM run it. Write move none ?? move d, which builds and runs alike on every backend."},{"u":"docs/guide/known-limitations.html#defects-carried-from-v001","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"Defects carried from v0.0.1","m":"human","x":"Defects carried from v0.0.1 # The checkers accept each of these programs although the Language Reference rejects it, so a checked program can still go wrong. v0.0.1 had every one of them; v0.0.2 is no worse, and each is fixed in the release its record names. Avoid the shape, as the second column says. Defect What you meet What to do D-352 Inside a generic function, a loan can be returned or stored as if it were owned: func id[T](x: T) T { return x } and func first[T](xs: List[T]) T? { return xs.get(0) } are accepted, and the caller then holds a second reference to the argument or the list's element. Write the function for the concrete type, where the checkers require copy or reject the loan. D-355 A loan given out of an if or match branch (give xs.get(0)), or lent by a match's scrutinee that is a temporary, such as match xs.get(0), ends with its statement, so the list can be changed while the loan is still in use. Bind the loan with let first (let t = xs.get(0)) and branch or match on t: a let binding holds the loan to the end of its block. D-356 A field read through an optional loan with ?., such as xs.get(0)?.inner, is typed as owned, so a binding of it can change the list's own element on the interpreter and the VM. Treat a value read with ?. through a loan as read-only: do not change it, or a binding made from it. To change a value of your own, build a new one. D-357 An owned optional binding written without a marker on the left of a ?? whose result is owned, as in let p: Token? = some ?? move d, is accepted, so p and some both own the value. Write move some ?? move d, or copy some ?? move d. D-362 A loan held inside an optional by a let mut binding (let mut t = xs.get(0)) is treated as ended after a while or for loop whose body ends it (t = null), although a loop that runs no times, a break or a continue skips that, so the list can be changed while the loan lives. End the loan before the loop (t = null), or keep the loan in a block that ends before the list changes. D-364 A defer that reads a binding of its block holding a loan inside an optional (let t = xs.get(0), then defer { ... t ... }) is accepted, although a deferred block may not use a binding of its block that holds a loan. Read what the deferred block needs before it, into a value of its own (copy), and use that in the defer. D-369 A binding of a ?? whose fallback it owns, such as let t = empty.get(0) ?? move d, is typed as a loan of the list, so it can be stored in a binding of an outer block (keep = present(t)) and read there after the fallback's cleanup has run. Keep such a binding in its own block, or move the fallback into a binding of its own first (let f = move d) and use that. D-370 On the interpreter, an unmarked copy of such a binding, or of a tuple element holding such a value (let a = t, let a = pair.0), becomes a second owner, so the fallback's cleanup runs when the copy ends, before the binding does. Write copy t (or alias t) to say which you mean. D-375 A ?? whose fallback is the statement's own value, lent to a read parameter (show(console, empty.get(0) ?? move d)), is never destroyed on the interpreter or the VM, so its cleanup does not run; native code destroys it when the statement ends. Bind the fallback first (let v = empty.get(0) ?? move d) and pass v, so the binding's end runs its cleanup. D-376 On the interpreter, assigning to a field of a record literal (rec.side = make(...), or a loan over a field that held a value of the record's own) does not run the old value's cleanup, and a loan assigned over such a field is cleaned up with the record while its owner still holds it. The VM runs each cleanup once, at its owner's end. Use a class for a value whose fields are reassigned, or run the program on the VM. D-379 A fn once closure may return a loan from a move capture (fn once[move t]() Token from t { return move t }), which the consuming call destroys: both checkers accept it, the interpreter and the VM keep the value until the receiving bi"},{"u":"docs/guide/known-limitations.html#defects-carried-from-v001","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"Defects carried from v0.0.1","m":"human","x":" destroys: both checkers accept it, the interpreter and the VM keep the value until the receiving binding ends or read it destroyed, and native code refuses the build. Return the captured value itself from a closure returning the owned type, or let the caller keep the binding and lend it to the closure instead of moving it in. D-388 A tuple built around a value of the function's own (let t = (empty.get(0) ?? move d, 1)), bound first and returned inside present as a from result, passes both checkers; the interpreter destroys the value at the call and the caller then reads it destroyed, and the VM never runs its cleanup. Return the owned value from a function returning the owned type, or keep the fallback in a binding of its own (let f = move d) that outlives the result. D-389 Inside a returned match arm, an if giving a loan on one branch and the function's own value on the other (let v = if flag { give x } else { give move d }; give v) passes both checkers, and both running backends destroy the moved value at the call, so later reads see it destroyed. Give every branch a loan, or return the owned value from a function returning the owned type. D-392 A loan taken from a temporary receiver, such as let t = fresh().get(0) (also .first(), ?? make(...), or a cursor of a temporary), passes both checkers; the temporary is destroyed at the statement's end and t then reads the destroyed element on the interpreter and the VM; native refuses the build. Bind the temporary first (let xs = fresh()) and take the loan from the binding. D-393 A pure function may print through a dropped value's cleanup: the io of a cleanup is not attributed to the function that drops the value, so both checkers accept it and every backend prints. Do not drop a value whose cleanup does I/O inside a function declared pure; give the value to the caller. D-394 A lent match payload (match xs.get(0) { present(x) => ... }) is destroyed at the arm's end on the interpreter, and on the VM for a plain optional, rather than at its owner's end, so a read of the element after the match sees it destroyed; native is right. Read what you need inside the arm, or bind the loan first (let t = xs.get(0)) and match on t. D-395 A result declared from one parameter (from xs) that lends another (ys) is rejected for a direct return but not through give, a match expression or an expression body, so the loan is recorded against the wrong owner and ys can change while the result lives. Declare the result from the parameter it lends, and return it directly. D-413 On the VM, an owned value recovered by a catch expression whose result the statement discards (discard (g() catch e { report e; recover make(...) })) is never destroyed, so its cleanup does not run; the interpreter and native code run it. The or recover form, and a catch whose value a let binds, run it on the VM too. Bind the catch's value (let x = g() catch e { ... }), so the binding's end runs the cleanup, or write or recover. D-414 An expect outside a test body, in an ordinary function or in a deferred block, is accepted by both checkers, and a failing one has no defined outcome: the interpreter stops with a Python traceback and writes no record, the VM reports a dynamic check failure, and native code does not build the program. Write expect in test bodies only. In a function, test the condition with if, and fail or return."},{"u":"docs/guide/known-limitations.html#what-v002-leaves-for-later","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"What v0.0.2 leaves for later","m":"human","x":"What v0.0.2 leaves for later # Defect What you meet What to do The older forms are accepted alongside the new ones, so a reader may meet both until the code is migrated. Run fortis fmt --write on the code. It rewrites each older form where the program means the same. D-404 fortis fmt never rewrites Int.shiftLeft(a, b) or fail to a << b or fail, although the two are the same operation; Int.add, sub, mul, div and rem are rewritten. Write the operator form by hand, or leave the named call. The two mean the same. fortis fmt does not rewrite a logging call, add @ignorable or derive Default, or rewrite a main that reads its Capabilities record other than field by field (a capability inside an interpolation, or the record moved whole). Make those changes by hand. Each is the author's choice. D-406 fortis fmt keeps a pure or noalloc written on a lambda, although the function type the lambda is passed to already states it; it prints no inferred one. Remove the modifier by hand if you want the shorter form. It changes nothing the checker decides. Checked operators cover Int only. The sized integer types (Int32, UInt8 and the rest) have no checked, wrapping or saturating operations yet; they are planned for v0.4. Give the operands a refined type, or test them first, so that the operation is proved. Or compute in Int. fortis fmt prints a module's inferred facts back only when the module checks on its own, so a module of a package that needs the rest of its package to check is not given them. Nothing changes in what the program means; the checker infers the facts either way. Write a fact by hand where you want it shown."},{"u":"docs/guide/tour.html#a-tour-of-fortis","p":"A tour of Fortis","h":"A tour of Fortis","m":"human","x":"A tour of Fortis # Human mode. Agent mode: Overview, by rule . Source of truth: LR 1 and LR 2 . Preview: the whole program at the end declares a newtype ( LR 6.9 ), an interface and a mixin ( LR 9.5 , 9.6 ), which arrive in v0.1; it is checked and runs today. Everything else this tour shows is in v0.0.2. This chapter says what Fortis is for, gets the tools running, and then walks through one whole program, a small to-do list, pointing to the chapter that explains each piece. You do not need to understand every line on first reading; the point is to see how the pieces fit before the later chapters take them one at a time."},{"u":"docs/guide/tour.html#what-fortis-is-for","p":"A tour of Fortis","h":"What Fortis is for","m":"human","x":"What Fortis is for # Fortis is a general-purpose, statically checked, compiled language designed so that software written mostly by AI agents can be read, reviewed and trusted by people ( LR 1 ). Its reader is someone looking at one file, or one diff, who needs to answer a handful of questions without running anything: who owns this value, and can this call change it? can this call fail, and who handles the failure? what effects does this function have: does it allocate, do I/O, read the clock? when is this observation recorded? Each of those questions has a visible answer in the source ( LR 1 ). The answer for a local decision is always on the page: whether a value is copied, lent or moved, whether a failure is propagated or recovered, which effects a function claims. Facts that live elsewhere, such as what an imported function ultimately does, come from the imported function's signature, and the language guarantees that a signature can never claim less than its body does ( LR 1 ). Fortis is library-first. A program is a set of packages, a package is a set of modules, and a module is one file ( LR 1 ). HTTP servers, databases, web interfaces and telemetry are ordinary libraries, grouped into kits , that an application imports; nothing in the core language depends on them ( LR 1 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/tour.html#six-design-choices","p":"A tour of Fortis","h":"Six design choices","m":"human","x":"Six design choices # Everything else in the language follows from a few decisions ( LR 1 ). Explicit ownership. When you assign or pass an existing value that owns resources, you write copy , alias or move ; lending a value for writing is a visible editable , and a value that changes is declared let mut . Nothing is cloned or reference-counted behind your back. See Ownership . Three distinct answers. An optional value ( T? ), a Bool and a fallible outcome ( Outcome[T] ) are three different types with three different operators ( ?? , || , or ). Nothing is \"truthy\". See Types . Linear failures. The result of a call that can fail must be dealt with exactly once: propagated with or fail , replaced with or recover , handled with catch or match , or reported with report . Ignoring it does not compile. See Outcomes and failures . A closed set of effects. pure , noalloc , async and try are checked facts about a function, computed from a fixed set of eight effects; a named contract is a combination of them and nothing else. See Effects and contracts . Lexical loans and synchronous cleanup. A borrow lasts until the end of the block that holds it; defer runs when a block exits; asynchronous code has one place where it can be suspended or cancelled ( await ) and structured scope s that never leak a task. See Cleanup, async and concurrency . Two backends, one meaning. A development VM and a production backend that emits safe Rust run the same checked intermediate representation ( LR 1 ). The reference toolchain adds a third implementation, the definitional interpreter, which every example in this guide also runs on. What Fortis leaves out is as deliberate: there is no runtime reflection, no inheritance, no macros, no exceptions and no implicit conversions ( LR 1 ). A reader can work out every binding's type, ownership and effects from the file and the signatures it imports."},{"u":"docs/guide/tour.html#notation-that-earns-its-place","p":"A tour of Fortis","h":"Notation that earns its place","m":"human","x":"Notation that earns its place # Fortis puts more words on the page than most languages, so every symbol has to justify itself. The rule is that a symbol or keyword exists only if it changes what a reader may conclude about ownership, mutation, failure, effects or timing ( LR 1.1 ). @ only ever applies an aspect; ? only ever means \"optional\", never \"propagate the error\"; = only assigns, and == compares; => is an expression body and nothing else ( LR 1.1 ). Keywords that might look like ceremony each carry a decision you would otherwise have to infer from somewhere you cannot see ( LR 1.1 ): You read It tells you let / let mut a new name / a name that will change copy , alias , move , editable how this place is used: duplicated, lent, given away, lent for writing owned , editable on a parameter what the callee may do with the argument try , pure , noalloc , async , recovers what the function may do or fail , or recover , catch what happens to a failure give this block produces a value export visible outside the module Silence has exactly one meaning in each position ( LR 1.1 ). An argument with no marker is a read-only loan that ends when the call returns, or, for an async call, when the future the call produces completes ( LR 7.3 ); a for loop over a place borrows it read-only for the loop; an assignment or return with no marker is a fresh value or a trivially copyable one. So a line with no markers neither changes nor transfers anything, and keeps no loan beyond the call or the future it is in. Two costs are accepted on purpose ( LR 1.1 ). Because or binds loosest, (f() or fail).amount needs its parentheses, and the idiom is to put the failure decision on its own line: let v = f() or fail . And an async call looks like any call: it builds a future, and the await that runs it is the visible point. Finally, the formatter prints every inferred fact back in canonical form, so a formatted file shows the closure kinds, loan modes and modifiers the checker used ( LR 1.1 ). You will see that in the program below."},{"u":"docs/guide/tour.html#getting-started","p":"A tour of Fortis","h":"Getting started","m":"human","x":"Getting started # The reference toolchain lives in this repository. Build the production compiler once (it needs a Rust toolchain, and no network once the sources are present): sh cd compiler && cargo build --release Then use the fortis command from the repository root ( bin/fortis , or fortis once it is on your PATH ): Command What it does fortis check FILE.ft or fortis check DIR every static check: syntax, names, types, ownership, obligations, effects, contracts fortis run FILE.ft [ARGS\u2026] check, then run main on the definitional interpreter, passing ARGS to it fortis test FILE.ft check, then run the module's tests fortis build FILE.ft --out APP check, then compile to a native executable through safe Rust fortis view PATH read Fortis source as the compiler sees it ( --decls , --symbol NAME , --query Q ) fortis fmt --check PATH / --write PATH the canonical layout A directory with a project.yaml is a package, and every command accepts it in place of a file. The production compiler fortisc , which fortis build , view and fmt run for you, also runs programs on the development VM: fortisc vm FILE.ft runs main there (it prints the program's output lines joined by | , in the form the conformance tools compare), and fortisc test FILE.ft runs the tests there. Every finding and every runtime report is a record: text when the output is a terminal, one JSON object per line when it is a pipe, and --format json or --format text chooses explicitly."},{"u":"docs/guide/tour.html#hello-world","p":"A tour of Fortis","h":"Hello, world","m":"human","x":"Hello, world # Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console , args : List [ String ] ) { console . print ( \"hello, world\" ) console . print ( \"${args.count()} arguments\" ) } output hello, world 0 arguments Save it as hello.ft and run fortis run hello.ft , or fortis build hello.ft --out hello and then ./hello . Words after the file are main 's arguments: fortis run hello.ft one two prints 2 arguments . A program's files are read through the FileSystem capability, from a fresh empty directory for each run unless FORTIS_DEV_ROOT names one (relative to where you run the command); keep a program's input files in a directory of their own and run it as FORTIS_DEV_ROOT=data fortis run app ( The core runtime ). While you iterate on tests, fortisc test on the development VM is much faster than the interpreter's fortis test ( Tests ). Three things already show the language's character: main is an ordinary function in the entry module ( export is implied there). Its parameters say what it needs, and the runtime supplies each one by its type: here the Console and the command-line arguments ( LR 4.6 ). A main that needs neither is func main() { \u2026 } . Printing is input/output, and input/output needs authority: the Console is the capability that writes to standard output, and only main receives it from the runtime, once ( LR 11.6 ). A function that wants to print must be given a Console ; it cannot reach for one. \"${args.count()} arguments\" is string interpolation; the interpolated value must be displayable ( LR 3.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/tour.html#a-whole-program","p":"A tour of Fortis","h":"A whole program","m":"human","x":"A whole program # Here is a complete module: a tiny to-do list with an identifier type, a state enum, a declared failure, an interface, a mixin, a class, a closure, a deferred block, a fallible constructor and a test. It is the guide's counterpart of the invoicing module in LR 2 , written to run on its own. Preview: TodoId is a newtype ( LR 6.9 ), and Describable and Labelled are an interface and a mixin ( LR 9.5 , 9.6 ); they arrive in v0.1. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } // A nominal identifier: an Int that is never confused with any other Int. export type TodoId = Int < 1 .. > // A closed set of states, two of them with payloads. export enum Status derive Equatable { Open Done ( by : String ) Dropped ( reason : String ) } // A failure kind with a stable code and a typed payload. export error EmptyTitle { code : \"todo.empty_title\" payload { id : TodoId } message : \"to-do ${id} has an empty title\" } // Anything that can describe itself in one line. export interface Describable { pure func describe ( self ) String } // A mixin adds methods on top of methods the class provides. export mixin Labelled { requires pure func title ( self ) String export pure func label ( self ) String => \"[${self.title()}]\" } export class Todo implements Describable with Labelled { export id : TodoId name : String status : Status export pure try func create ( id : TodoId , name : owned String ) Todo { if name . byteCount ( ) == 0 { fail EmptyTitle ( id : id ) } return Todo ( id , name : move name , status : Open ) } export pure func title ( self ) String => copy self . name export pure func finish ( editable self , by : StringView ) { self . status = Done ( by : String . from ( by ) ) } export pure func isOpen ( self ) Bool => self . status == Open export pure func describe ( self ) String { let phase = match self . status { Open => \"open\" Done ( by : who ) => \"done by ${who}\" Dropped ( reason : why ) => \"dropped: ${why}\" } return \"${self.id} ${self.label()} ${phase}\" } } export pure func countOpen ( todos : List [ Todo ] ) Int { let open = pure noalloc fn ( t : Todo ) Bool => t . isOpen ( ) return todos . countWhere ( open ) } func show ( console : Console , todos : List [ Todo ] ) { defer { console . print ( \"${countOpen(todos)} open\" ) } for t in todos { console . print ( t . describe ( ) ) } } try func main ( console : Console ) { let mut first = Todo . create ( id : TodoId ( 1 ) , name : \"write the guide\" ) or fail let second = Todo . create ( id : TodoId ( 2 ) , name : \"check the examples\" ) or fail first . finish ( \"ada\" ) let todos = List . of ( move first , move second ) show ( console , todos ) let fallback = Todo . create ( id : TodoId ( 3 ) , name : \"\" ) catch e { report e recover Todo . create ( id : TodoId ( 3 ) , name : \"untitled\" ) or fail } console . print ( fallback . describe ( ) ) } test \"a new to-do is open\" { let t = Todo . create ( id : TodoId ( 9 ) , name : \"x\" ) expect t . isOpen ( ) } output 1 [write the guide] done by ada 2 [check the examples] open 1 open 3 [untitled] open The report e line also writes one failure record, with the code todo.empty_title and the payload id: 3 , to standard error; it is not part of the program's output ( LR 10.6 ). Now the pieces, top to bottom. Imports. import fortis.host { Console } binds a name from the standard module fortis.host . The prelude ( Int , String , List , Bool , \u2026) needs no import ( LR 4.3 ). See Modules and packages . A newtype. type TodoId = Int<1..> declares a new nominal type whose values are integers of at least 1. A TodoId never mixes with any other Int -based type, and the only ways to make one are a literal the checker can prove in range, written TodoId(1) , or the fallible TodoId.validate(n) ( LR 6.9 ). See Types . An enum with payloads. Status has three variants, two of which carry data. derive Equatable generates == for it ( LR 6.10 ). A match over a Status must handle every variant, so adding a fourth variant later makes the checker point at eve"},{"u":"docs/guide/tour.html#a-whole-program","p":"A tour of Fortis","h":"A whole program","m":"human","x":" a Status must handle every variant, so adding a fourth variant later makes the checker point at every match that needs updating ( LR 9.7 , LR 9.8 ). See Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums . A declared failure. error EmptyTitle defines a failure kind with a stable code, a typed payload and a message template; fail EmptyTitle(id: id) creates one ( LR 10.3 ). See Outcomes and failures . An interface and a mixin. Describable declares a method a class promises to provide. Labelled provides a method, label , on top of a method it requires the class to have, title ; a mixin adds no fields and sees only the class's exported members, so it never reads a private field ( LR 9.5 , LR 9.6 ). A class. Todo has an exported id and two private fields. Its methods show the three receiver forms you will meet everywhere: self borrows read-only, editable self borrows for writing, and owned self takes the value. first.finish(\"ada\") changes first , and its declaration, let mut first , already says that it will, so the call needs no marker ( LR 7.5 , LR 9.3 ). Older code writes editable first.finish(\"ada\") ; fortis fmt rewrites it. The generated constructor Todo(id: \u2026, name: \u2026, status: \u2026) is usable only where every field is visible, which is why other modules must go through Todo.create ( LR 9.2 ). In Todo(id, name: move name, status: Open) , the bare id stands for id: id ( LR 9.2 ), and Open needs no Status. in front, because the field's type says which enum it is ( LR 9.7 ). A fallible constructor. pure try func create(\u2026) Todo may fail; its callers receive an outcome that they must consume. name: owned String says the function takes ownership of the string, so it can store it with move name ( LR 7.6 ). copy self.name in title duplicates a string that self only lends ( LR 7.6 ). A closure. pure noalloc fn(t: Todo) Bool => t.isOpen() is a lambda with no captures. You may write it as fn(t: Todo) Bool => t.isOpen() , or fn(t) => t.isOpen() , where countWhere 's parameter type gives the parameter and the result; the checker infers pure noalloc , and countWhere accepts it because it is pure ( LR 8.2 , LR 6.7 ). The formatter does not print the inferred claims back, because the parameter type already states them. See Functions and closures . Effects and authority. countOpen is pure : it does no I/O and changes nothing outside itself. show prints, so it takes a Console parameter; that is how a reader knows, from the signature alone, that it does I/O ( LR 11.6 ). main has the Console because it declares it as a parameter ( LR 4.6 ). See Effects and contracts . A deferred block. The defer in show runs when the function's body exits, after the loop, by whatever route it leaves ( LR 12.1 ). See Cleanup, async and concurrency . Failures, handled three ways. In main , Todo.create(\u2026) or fail propagates a failure to main 's caller, which for a try main means one report and exit status 1 ( LR 4.6 ). The catch e { \u2026 } block handles the empty title: it reports the failure and recovers with a replacement value ( LR 10.4 ). Nothing can be ignored: deleting the or fail would leave an outcome unconsumed, which does not compile ( LR 10.1 ). Ownership at the call site. List.of(move first, move second) moves both values into the list; after that, first and second cannot be used ( LR 7.4 ). show(console, todos) passes both arguments with no marker, which means read-only loans that end when show returns ( LR 7.5 ). A test. test \"\u2026\" { \u2026 } is a declaration like any other. In its body a failing call fails the test, so Todo.create(\u2026) needs no or fail there, and expect records a check ( LR 14 ). fortis test runs it. See Tests ."},{"u":"docs/guide/tour.html#where-to-go-next","p":"A tour of Fortis","h":"Where to go next","m":"human","x":"Where to go next # Read the chapters in order, starting with Lexical structure ; each follows one section of the LR. Some everyday words are keywords and cannot name a variable, a parameter or a field: before , after , from , once , owned , error , record , test , cases , view , state , model and action among them, all listed in Keywords ( LR 3.4 ). The core library lists every operation of the standard modules, generated from their declarations. If you already know Rust, TypeScript or Python, the notes for readers coming from Rust , TypeScript and Python map what you know onto Fortis. The agent mode states the same rules densely, by rule ID."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#worked-examples","p":"Worked examples","h":"Worked examples","m":"human","x":"Worked examples # Human mode. Each program is shown whole, with its output, and then explained piece by piece. Source of truth: the LR; the tour has the worked module of LR 2 . Preview: the ledger's Sku is a newtype ( LR 6.9 ), which arrives in v0.1, and the last program is concurrent ( LR 12.3 , 12.5 ), which arrives in v0.3; both are checked and run today. The word tally and the expression evaluator are in v0.0.1. The chapters each show one construct at a time. Real programs use several at once, and the interesting part is how they fit together: who owns what, where a failure goes, which line is allowed to change which value. These four programs are the size of a first real task. Every one of them is checked and run by the toolchain, like every example in this guide ( LR 0 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#a-word-tally","p":"Worked examples","h":"A word tally","m":"human","x":"A word tally # The first program counts the words of a text, keeps them in the order it first saw them, and reports a few things about the counts. It uses a class with private state, a Map , loops, and closures of two kinds. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } import fortis . text { Text , Builder } // One word and the number of times it occurred. class Entry { export word : String export count : Int } // Counts words, remembering the order in which each was first seen. class Tally { counts : Map [ String , Int ] order : List [ String ] export pure func empty ( ) Tally => Tally ( counts : Map . default ( ) , order : List . default ( ) ) export func add ( editable self , word : StringView ) { let seen = self . counts . get ( word ) ?? 0 if seen == 0 { self . order . push ( String . from ( word ) ) } self . counts . set ( String . from ( word ) , Int . saturatingAdd ( seen , 1 ) ) } export pure func entries ( self ) List [ Entry ] { let mut out : List [ Entry ] = List . default ( ) for word in self . order { let n = self . counts . get ( word ) ?? 0 out . push ( Entry ( word : copy word , count : n ) ) } return out } } // The entry with the highest count; the first one seen wins a tie. pure func mostFrequent ( entries : ListView [ Entry ] ) Entry ? { let mut best : Entry ? = null let mut bestCount = 0 for entry in entries { if entry . count > bestCount { best = Entry ( word : copy entry . word , count : entry . count ) bestCount = entry . count } } return best } func main ( console : Console ) { let mut tally = Tally . empty ( ) for word in Text . words ( \"the cat and the hat and the bat\" ) { tally . add ( word ) } let entries = tally . entries ( ) for entry in entries { console . print ( \"${entry.word}: ${entry.count}\" ) } let threshold = 2 let repeated = entries . countWhere ( fn [ threshold ] ( e : Entry ) Bool => e . count >= threshold ) console . print ( \"${repeated} words occur at least ${threshold} times\" ) let mut summary = Builder . create ( ) { let mut describe = fn mut [ editable summary ] ( e : Entry ) Void { summary . append ( \"[${e.word} ${e.count}]\" ) } for entry in entries { describe ( entry ) } } console . print ( summary . finish ( ) ) match mostFrequent ( entries ) { present ( top ) => console . print ( \"most frequent: ${top.word}\" ) null => console . print ( \"no words\" ) } } output the: 3 cat: 1 and: 2 hat: 1 bat: 1 2 words occur at least 2 times [the 3][cat 1][and 2][hat 1][bat 1] most frequent: the"},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#the-tallys-state-is-private","p":"Worked examples","h":"The tally's state is private","m":"human","x":"The tally's state is private # Tally 's two fields carry no export , so they are private to the module ( LR 4.4 ). Inside this module the generated constructor Tally(counts: \u2026, order: \u2026) is usable, because every field is visible here; another module could not call it and would have to go through Tally.empty() , an associated function: a function in the class with no self ( LR 9.2 ). That is how a class keeps an invariant, here that order lists each key of counts exactly once. Map.default() and List.default() are empty collections; their element types come from the field types they initialise ( LR 6.10 ). Rather than iterating the map, the class keeps the order of first sight in a list of its own, so the order is part of its state and is visible in its declaration."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#changing-the-tally-is-visible-where-it-is-declared","p":"Worked examples","h":"Changing the tally is visible where it is declared","m":"human","x":"Changing the tally is visible where it is declared # add declares editable self : it needs an exclusive loan of the tally. The tally is a let mut binding, which is what allows tally.add(word) to change it, so a reader of main learns from one declaration that tally changes ( LR 7.5 , LR 9.3 ). entries takes plain self , a read loan, so tally.entries() cannot change anything. word is a StringView , a borrowed piece of text. The map's keys are owned String s, so add makes each one with String.from(word) , which is where the allocation happens and is written down ( LR 6.6 ). The two calls to String.from each produce a fresh value, and a fresh value is passed to an owned parameter such as push 's without any marker ( LR 7.5 ). A lookup needs no owned key: a Map with String keys takes its get key as a StringView , so self.counts.get(word) looks the word up as it is, allocating nothing ( LR 6.7 ). Older code writes get(String.from(word)) or get(copy word) ; fortis fmt drops the copy. get returns an optional read loan of the stored count, null when the key is absent, and ?? 0 supplies the fallback ( LR 6.7 , LR 6.8 ). The count is incremented with Int.saturatingAdd , one of the named integer operations, because a plain + on an unbounded Int could overflow and the checker would refuse it ( LR 6.4 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#building-a-list-and-returning-it","p":"Worked examples","h":"Building a list and returning it","m":"human","x":"Building a list and returning it # entries is pure , although it pushes onto a list: the list is created in the same call, so writing to it changes nothing anyone else can see ( LR 11.1 ). It returns the list with return out , which moves the local to the caller, since nothing can use it afterwards ( LR 7.5 ). Each Entry gets its own copy of the word, written copy word : duplicating a String allocates, so it is never implicit ( LR 6.11 , LR 7.5 ). mostFrequent takes a ListView[Entry] , a borrowed view of a list, and main passes entries , the list itself ( LR 6.7 ). It returns Entry? : an entry, or null for an empty list ( LR 6.8 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#two-closures-two-kinds","p":"Worked examples","h":"Two closures, two kinds","m":"human","x":"Two closures, two kinds # countWhere takes a callback. fn[threshold](e: Entry) Bool => \u2026 is a fn , the kind that only reads; its capture list names threshold , which, being an Int , may be listed bare and is copied in ( LR 8.2 , LR 8.3 ). The lambda does nothing but compare, so its inferred summary is pure and it satisfies countWhere 's pure fn parameter without saying so ( LR 6.7 , LR 11.3 ). describe appends to summary , so it captures it with editable summary and is a fn mut ; its binding is let mut , which is what lets each call, describe(entry) , change it ( LR 7.5 , LR 8.2 ). While describe exists it holds an exclusive loan of summary , so the closure lives in an inner block: at the block's closing brace the closure is dropped, the loan ends, and summary.finish() may read the builder again ( LR 7.3 , LR 8.3 ). Without the block the call to finish would be rejected as a use of a loaned owner."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#matching-on-an-optional","p":"Worked examples","h":"Matching on an optional","m":"human","x":"Matching on an optional # match mostFrequent(entries) covers both cases of the optional with the patterns present(top) and null ( LR 6.8 , LR 9.8 ). An if would also work; the match makes both outcomes visible side by side."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#an-inventory-ledger-as-a-package","p":"Worked examples","h":"An inventory ledger, as a package","m":"human","x":"An inventory ledger, as a package # The second program is a package: a library module with the ledger and its tests, and an application module that uses it. It shows nominal ids, declared errors, and the ways a failure can be handled: propagated, recovered after a report, and left to end the program. Preview: Sku is a newtype ( LR 6.9 ), which arrives in v0.1. YAML # file: guide.ledger/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: guide.ledger version: 1.0.0 entry: app exports: [stock] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" Fortis // accept // file: guide.ledger/src/stock.ft // A stock-keeping unit. A nominal type: no Int, and no other id, is ever a Sku by accident. export type Sku = Int < 1 .. 999999 > export error UnknownSku { code : \"ledger.unknown_sku\" payload { sku : Sku } message : \"no item has SKU ${sku}\" } export error Insufficient { code : \"ledger.insufficient\" payload { sku : Sku , held : Int , wanted : Int } message : \"SKU ${sku} holds ${held}, but ${wanted} were wanted\" } export class Item { export sku : Sku export name : String export quantity : Int < 0 .. 10000 > } export class Ledger { items : List [ Item ] export pure func empty ( ) Ledger => Ledger ( items : List . default ( ) ) export func add ( editable self , item : owned Item ) { self . items . push ( move item ) } // Where the item with this SKU is held. pure try func indexOf ( self , sku : Sku ) Int { let mut i = 0 for item in self . items { if item . sku == sku { return i } i = i + 1 or fail } fail UnknownSku ( sku : sku ) } export pure try func quantityOf ( self , sku : Sku ) Int < 0 .. 10000 > { let at = self . indexOf ( sku ) or fail let item = self . items . at ( at ) or fail return item . quantity } // Takes `wanted` units and returns what is left. export try func take ( editable self , sku : Sku , wanted : Int < 0 .. 10000 > ) Int < 0 .. 10000 > { let at = self . indexOf ( sku ) or fail let item = self . items . at ( at ) or fail if wanted > item . quantity { fail Insufficient ( sku : sku , held : item . quantity , wanted ) } let left = Int < 0 .. 10000 > . validate ( item . quantity - wanted ) or fail self . items . set ( at , Item ( sku , name : copy item . name , quantity : left ) ) or fail return left } } test \"taking stock leaves the rest\" { let mut ledger = Ledger . empty ( ) ledger . add ( Item ( sku : Sku ( 7 ) , name : \"washer\" , quantity : 10 ) ) expect ledger . take ( Sku ( 7 ) , 4 ) == 6 expect ledger . quantityOf ( Sku ( 7 ) ) == 6 } test \"taking too much fails and changes nothing\" { let mut ledger = Ledger . empty ( ) ledger . add ( Item ( sku : Sku ( 7 ) , name : \"washer\" , quantity : 3 ) ) let left = ledger . take ( Sku ( 7 ) , 4 ) catch e { expect e . code == \"ledger.insufficient\" recover 0 } expect left == 0 expect ledger . quantityOf ( Sku ( 7 ) ) == 3 } Fortis // accept (exit status 1) // file: guide.ledger/src/app.ft import fortis . host { Console } import guide . ledger . stock { Ledger , Item , Sku } try func main ( console : Console ) { let mut ledger = Ledger . empty ( ) ledger . add ( Item ( sku : Sku ( 101 ) , name : \"bolt\" , quantity : 40 ) ) ledger . add ( Item ( sku : Sku ( 202 ) , name : \"nut\" , quantity : 5 ) ) let bolts = ledger . take ( Sku ( 101 ) , 15 ) or fail console . print ( \"bolts left: ${bolts}\" ) // An expected failure, handled where it happens: reported once, then replaced. let nuts = ledger . take ( Sku ( 202 ) , 9 ) catch e { console . print ( \"could not take 9 nuts: ${e.code}\" ) report e recover ledger . quantityOf ( Sku ( 202 ) ) or fail } console . print ( \"nuts left: ${nuts}\" ) // An unexpected failure propagates: main fails, and the runtime reports it once. let washers = ledger . take ( Sku ( 303 ) , 1 ) or fail console . print ( \"never printed: ${washers}\" ) } output bolts left: 25 could not take 9 nuts: ledger.insufficient nuts left: 5 The program ends with exit status 1 on purpose: the last take names a SKU the ledger does not hold."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#an-inventory-ledger-as-a-package","p":"Worked examples","h":"An inventory ledger, as a package","m":"human","x":"the ledger does not hold."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#the-package","p":"Worked examples","h":"The package","m":"human","x":"The package # A package is a directory with project.yaml and src/ . The manifest names the package, its version, its dependencies (here only the standard package fortis ), the modules other packages may import ( exports ) and, for an application, its entry module ( LR 4.1 ). Each file under src/ is a module named after its path, so src/stock.ft is the module stock , imported from the sibling module as guide.ledger.stock ( LR 4.2 , LR 4.3 ). fortis run guide.ledger checks the package and runs main in the entry module; fortis test guide.ledger runs its tests."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#ids-that-cannot-be-confused","p":"Worked examples","h":"Ids that cannot be confused","m":"human","x":"Ids that cannot be confused # type Sku = Int<1..999999> declares a new nominal type over a refined Int ( LR 6.9 ). A Sku is not an Int : a quantity can never be passed where a SKU is expected, or the other way round. Sku(101) is a literal the checker proves to be in range; a value from outside the program (a parsed line, a request) would go through Sku.validate(raw) , which fails when the value is out of range ( LR 6.3 , LR 6.9 ). Two Sku s compare with == because a newtype over a trivial type is Equatable when its representation is ( LR 6.9 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#declared-failures","p":"Worked examples","h":"Declared failures","m":"human","x":"Declared failures # error UnknownSku { \u2026 } declares a failure kind: a stable code , a typed payload , and a message template that may name only payload fields ( LR 10.3 ). fail UnknownSku(sku: sku) inside a try function ends it with that failure ( LR 10.1 ). indexOf is pure try : it reads only the ledger and may fail. Its counter uses i = i + 1 or fail : the checker cannot prove that i + 1 fits, so under or fail the + is the checked addition, which fails rather than wrapping ( LR 6.4 ). items.at(at) is the checked way to read a list element, an outcome that fails past the end ( LR 6.7 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#the-checkers-view-of-a-subtraction","p":"Worked examples","h":"The checker's view of a subtraction","m":"human","x":"The checker's view of a subtraction # take first refuses to take more than is held. After that test the checker knows that wanted lies in 0..10000 and so does item.quantity , but it does not record that one is at most the other: its facts are intervals of single places ( LR 6.3 ). The difference could, as far as it knows, be negative, so the result is validated into Int<0..10000> , which cannot fail here but says exactly what is being assumed ( LR 6.3 ). The chapter on proving arithmetic and indexing covers what the checker can prove on its own. items.set(\u2026) replaces an element and returns an Outcome[Void] , failing past the end, so it too ends in or fail ( LR 6.7 ). The replacement item copies the name with copy item.name : item is a read loan of the old element, and the new one needs its own String ( LR 7.5 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#three-ways-to-handle-a-failure","p":"Worked examples","h":"Three ways to handle a failure","m":"human","x":"Three ways to handle a failure # In main , every call to a try function produces an outcome that must be consumed exactly once ( LR 10.1 ): ledger.take(Sku(101), 15) or fail propagates any failure out of main ( LR 10.2 ). take changes the ledger, which main declares let mut . The second take is expected to fail. Its catch e { \u2026 } block runs only on failure; it prints the code, writes the failure to the diagnostic plane with report e , which consumes it, and ends with recover \u2026 , the value the expression has instead ( LR 10.4 , LR 10.6 ). Because the failure was reported before being replaced, main needs no recovers modifier ( LR 10.4 ). Inside a catch block, or fail still means the enclosing function, so the fallback read can itself propagate ( LR 10.4 ). The last take fails with ledger.unknown_sku , and or fail passes it out of main . When a try main fails, the runtime writes exactly one report of the failure to standard error and the process exits with status 1 ( LR 4.6 ). The tests in stock.ft are part of the module and are compiled only in test builds ( LR 14 ). In a test body a failing call fails the test, so expect ledger.take(Sku(7), 4) == 6 needs no or fail , and expecting a failure inside catch consumes it ( LR 14 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#an-expression-evaluator","p":"Worked examples","h":"An expression evaluator","m":"human","x":"An expression evaluator # The third program evaluates arithmetic expressions held as a tree. It shows an enum with payloads, exhaustive match , recursion, and arithmetic that fails instead of overflowing. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error DivideByZero { code : \"calc.divide_by_zero\" } // An arithmetic expression: a tree whose nodes own their children. enum Expr { Number ( value : Int ) Add ( left : Expr , right : Expr ) Multiply ( left : Expr , right : Expr ) Divide ( left : Expr , right : Expr ) Negate ( operand : Expr ) } // Evaluates an expression; overflow and division by zero are failures, never wrong answers. pure try func eval ( e : Expr ) Int { return match e { Number ( value : v ) => v Add ( left : l , right : r ) => { let a = eval ( l ) or fail let b = eval ( r ) or fail give a + b or fail } Multiply ( left : l , right : r ) => { let a = eval ( l ) or fail let b = eval ( r ) or fail give a * b or fail } Divide ( left : l , right : r ) => { let a = eval ( l ) or fail let b = eval ( r ) or fail if b == 0 { fail DivideByZero ( ) } give a / b or fail } Negate ( operand : o ) => { let a = eval ( o ) or fail give 0 - a or fail } } } // Renders an expression with full parentheses. pure func show ( e : Expr ) String { return match e { Number ( value : v ) => \"${v}\" Add ( left : l , right : r ) => \"(${show(l)} + ${show(r)})\" Multiply ( left : l , right : r ) => \"(${show(l)} * ${show(r)})\" Divide ( left : l , right : r ) => \"(${show(l)} / ${show(r)})\" Negate ( operand : o ) => \"-${show(o)}\" } } pure func number ( v : Int ) Expr => Number ( value : v ) func main ( console : Console ) { let examples = List . of ( Expr . Add ( left : number ( 2 ) , right : Multiply ( left : number ( 3 ) , right : number ( 4 ) ) ) , Negate ( operand : Divide ( left : number ( 10 ) , right : number ( 3 ) ) ) , Divide ( left : number ( 1 ) , right : Add ( left : number ( 2 ) , right : Negate ( operand : number ( 2 ) ) ) ) , Multiply ( left : number ( 4611686018427387904 ) , right : number ( 2 ) ) , ) for e in examples { match eval ( e ) { success ( v ) => console . print ( \"${show(e)} = ${v}\" ) failure ( f ) => { console . print ( \"${show(e)} fails: ${f.code}\" ) report f } } } } output (2 + (3 * 4)) = 14 -(10 / 3) = -3 (1 / (2 + -2)) fails: calc.divide_by_zero (4611686018427387904 * 2) fails: numeric.overflow"},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#an-enum-is-a-closed-set-of-shapes","p":"Worked examples","h":"An enum is a closed set of shapes","m":"human","x":"An enum is a closed set of shapes # Expr has five variants, each with named payload fields ( LR 9.7 ). Inside a match over an Expr the variant names need no Expr. prefix ( LR 9.7 ). The enum is closed: a match must cover every variant, and if a sixth were added, every match that does not handle it would be reported, each missing variant by name ( LR 9.8 ). Neither eval nor show has a catch-all arm, and none is allowed as the last arm of a match over a closed enum: that is what makes adding a variant safe ( LR 9.8 ). match e takes a read loan of the expression, and the payload bindings l , r and o are read loans of the children ( LR 9.8 ). Nothing is copied while walking the tree."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#arms-that-compute","p":"Worked examples","h":"Arms that compute","m":"human","x":"Arms that compute # A single-expression arm is just Pattern => value . An arm that needs statements is a block and ends with give value ( LR 9.8 , LR 5.4 ). Inside those blocks or fail and fail leave eval itself, not the arm: they always mean the enclosing function ( LR 5.4 ). Each operation is checked: a + b or fail , a * b or fail , a / b or fail and 0 - a or fail cannot be proved safe on any Int , so under or fail each is the checked operation, failing with numeric.overflow or numeric.divide_by_zero ( LR 6.4 ). The evaluator also checks for a zero divisor itself, so that a division by zero reports the calculator's own code. Integer division truncates toward zero, so 10 / 3 is 3 ( LR 6.4 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#matching-on-an-outcome","p":"Worked examples","h":"Matching on an outcome","m":"human","x":"Matching on an outcome # main handles each result with match and the outcome patterns success(v) and failure(f) ( LR 9.8 , LR 10.2 ). The failure arm owns the failure and must consume it, here with report f ( LR 10.2 ). Because every failure is reported rather than silently replaced, main needs no recovers ( LR 10.4 , LR 11.2 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#concurrent-work-with-a-scope","p":"Worked examples","h":"Concurrent work with a scope","m":"human","x":"Concurrent work with a scope # The last program runs work concurrently on the core executor. Each batch of readings is summed by its own task; the tasks belong to a scope , which joins them before it ends. Preview: async functions, scope and join ( LR 12.3 , 12.5 ) arrive in v0.3. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error Empty { code : \"stats.empty\" } // The sum of a batch of readings; overflow is a failure. async try func total ( readings : owned List [ Int ] ) Int { if readings . isEmpty ( ) { fail Empty ( ) } let mut sum = 0 for r in readings { sum = sum + r or fail } return sum } // The largest reading of a batch, or null for an empty one. async func peak ( readings : owned List [ Int ] ) Int ? { let mut best : Int ? = null for r in readings { let current = best ?? r if r >= current { best = r } } return best } // Sums both batches concurrently in one scope; the scope joins all its tasks before it ends. async try func sumAll ( console : Console , a : owned List [ Int ] , b : owned List [ Int ] ) Int { scope tasks { let first = tasks . launch ( total ( move a ) ) let second = tasks . launch ( total ( move b ) ) let x = await first or fail let y = await second or fail console . print ( \"batch totals ${x} and ${y}\" ) return x + y or fail } } async try func main ( console : Console ) { defer { console . print ( \"finished\" ) } let all = await sumAll ( console , List . of ( 3 , 5 , 8 ) , List . of ( 13 , 21 ) ) or fail console . print ( \"grand total ${all}\" ) let peaks = await join ( peak ( List . of ( 4 , 9 , 2 ) ) , peak ( List . default ( ) ) ) console . print ( \"peaks ${peaks.0 ?? -1} and ${peaks.1 ?? -1}\" ) let empty = await sumAll ( console , List . of ( 1 ) , List . default ( ) ) catch f { console . print ( \"could not sum: ${f.code}\" ) report f recover 0 } console . print ( \"after the failure: ${empty}\" ) } output batch totals 16 and 34 grand total 50 peaks 9 and -1 could not sum: stats.empty after the failure: 0 finished"},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#calling-an-async-function-builds-a-future","p":"Worked examples","h":"Calling an async function builds a future","m":"human","x":"Calling an async function builds a future # total and peak are async . Calling one does not run it: total(move a) evaluates its arguments and returns a Future , and the body starts when the future is awaited or launched ( LR 12.3 ). A future is linear: it must be awaited, launched, or discarded, and discarding is allowed only for a future never started whose environment holds no linear value and no exclusive loan ( LR 12.3 ). The batches are passed as owned lists, written move a at the call, so each task owns its data ( LR 7.6 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#the-scope-owns-its-tasks","p":"Worked examples","h":"The scope owns its tasks","m":"human","x":"The scope owns its tasks # scope tasks { \u2026 } is a block that owns tasks. tasks.launch(future) starts a task and returns a Task , which must be awaited inside the scope ( LR 12.5 ). await first or fail waits for the first task and propagates its failure. If that happens, the scope cancels second and waits for it before the block ends, so a scope never leaves a task running behind it ( LR 12.5 ). That is what the third call to sumAll exercises: the second batch is empty, its task fails with stats.empty , the failure leaves sumAll , and main reports it and recovers. console is a plain Console parameter, a read loan of the capability. Cooperative launch may use loans of anything declared before the scope, because the scope joins its tasks before those bindings are dropped ( LR 12.5 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#two-futures-without-a-scope","p":"Worked examples","h":"Two futures without a scope","m":"human","x":"Two futures without a scope # join(a, b) consumes two futures, runs both inside the current task, and returns both results as a tuple once both have finished ( LR 12.5 ). Here the results are optional Int s, read as peaks.0 and peaks.1 ( LR 6.9 )."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#deterministic-output","p":"Worked examples","h":"Deterministic output","m":"human","x":"Deterministic output # async try func main runs on the core cooperative executor as the root task ( LR 4.6 ). Every cooperative task of a scope tree runs on one thread ( LR 12.5 ), and in this program only sumAll and main print, after their awaits, so the output does not depend on how the tasks interleave. Nothing about the timing of interleavings is promised to be the same on every backend ( LR 12.7 ); a program whose output must be stable keeps its printing out of concurrently running tasks, as this one does."},{"u":"docs/guide/worked-examples.html#cleanup-at-every-exit","p":"Worked examples","h":"Cleanup at every exit","m":"human","x":"Cleanup at every exit # defer { \u2026 } registers a block that runs when the enclosing block exits by any route: falling off the end, a return , or a failure leaving through or fail ( LR 12.1 ). Here it prints finished last. A deferred block may not itself return , fail or await ; it can only clean up ( LR 12.1 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/01-overview.html#overview-design-invariants-and-notation-lr-1-lr-2","p":"Overview: design invariants and notation (LR 1, LR 2)","h":"Overview: design invariants and notation (LR 1, LR 2)","m":"agent","x":"Overview: design invariants and notation (LR 1, LR 2) # Agent mode. Human mode: A tour of Fortis . Source of truth: LR 1 and LR 2 . LR 1 and LR 2 state no numbered rules; they fix the invariants every later rule serves. Write code so that each of these is visible on the line."},{"u":"docs/agent/01-overview.html#invariants","p":"Overview: design invariants and notation (LR 1, LR 2)","h":"Invariants","m":"agent","x":"Invariants # Invariant Consequence when writing code LR Every local decision is visible: ownership operations, mutation permission, failure disposition, effect claims, observation points. Never rely on inference for these; write the marker, modifier or handler. A marker the declaration already shows is left out: an editable self call on a let mut binding, return local ( LR 7.5 ). LR 1 Imported signatures can never be weaker than their bodies. Read the callee's signature ( fortis view --symbol ) instead of its body. LR 1 Library-first: program = packages, package = modules, module = one file; HTTP, data, web UI, telemetry, jobs are kits (libraries). The core imports nothing from kits. LR 1 Explicit ownership: assigning or passing an existing owned value states copy , alias or move ; mutable access is editable . No hidden clones or reference counts. See ownership . LR 1 Three distinct answers: T? , Bool , Outcome[T] with ?? , || , or . Nothing is truthy. Compare explicitly ( x != null , n != 0 ). LR 1 Linear failures: an outcome is consumed exactly once ( or fail , or recover , catch , match , report ); dropping one is a compile error. Every call to a try function is followed by a disposition; in a test or property body or fail is implied ( LR 14 ). LR 1 Closed effect set: pure , noalloc , async , try are checked claims over eight effects; contracts are conjunctions of them. See effects . LR 1 Lexical loans; synchronous defer ; one cancellation point ( await ); structured scope . End a loan early by closing a block. LR 1 Two backends, one semantics: development VM and safe-Rust native code run the same checked IR. Never depend on backend behaviour. LR 1 Absent by design: scripting semantics, runtime reflection, inheritance, macros, exceptions, implicit conversions. Do not emulate them. LR 1"},{"u":"docs/agent/01-overview.html#notation","p":"Overview: design invariants and notation (LR 1, LR 2)","h":"Notation","m":"agent","x":"Notation # Every non-alphabetic symbol and what it answers ( LR 1.1 ): Notation Meaning Never { } block, record literal, record type ( ) call, parameter list, tuple, grouping Name[ ] generic arguments after an uppercase name place[ ] index after a lowercase place slices fn[ ] a lambda's capture list, only when non-empty fn[] + - * / % << >> & | ^ arithmetic and bitwise; proof-required on integers overloading == != < <= > >= Equatable , Comparable ; IEEE comparison on floats chained comparisons && || ! Boolean logic truthiness += and friends x = x op e < > after a type refinement (type position only) : has type, named argument, record field, label ternary, slicing = assignment, let initialiser equality => expression body of a function, lambda or match arm map literals . member access, module paths ranges .. ..< integer ranges in for headers and refinements ? ?. optional type suffix, optional chaining error propagation ?? absent-value fallback @name(args) apply an aspect, on the lines above a function head contracts ${ } string interpolation // comment; documentation directly above a declaration /// , /* */ , list separator ... spread a place into a constructor; forward remaining aspect arguments Keywords that carry a decision ( LR 1.1 ): let (new name), mut (changes), copy / alias / move / editable (how a place is used), owned / editable on a parameter, try / pure / noalloc / async / recovers and named contracts in the modifier position, or fail / or recover / catch , give , export . Silence ( LR 1.1 ): an unmarked argument, or the receiver of a self method, is a read loan ending when the call returns (or with the future of an async call); an unmarked for source is a read loan for the loop; an unmarked assignment is a fresh or trivial value. The unmarked receiver of an editable self method, or a fn mut being called, is the exclusive loan its root's declaration shows ( let mut , editable ), and return local moves the local ( LR 7.5 ). Removed, never write ( LR 1.1 ): semicolons; /// , /* */ ; yield ; or die ; nostate ; self: editable Self (write editable self ); alias editable (write editable ); alias type (a type is always nominal); within ; give in a single-expression arm; fn[] ; :: ; operator overloading; implicit it ; postfix ? for errors; new (name constructors create , of , from ); return in an expression-bodied function. Two deliberate costs ( LR 1.1 ): (f() or fail).amount needs parentheses, so write let v = f() or fail on its own line; an async call builds a Future , and await or launch is the visible point where it runs. Formatted source shows every inferred fact (closure kind, loan modes, a contract's modifiers), except a lambda's inferred pure / noalloc , which the function type it is passed to states ( LR 1.1 , LR 8.2 ). fortis fmt --write also rewrites older forms into the forms these pages teach where the meaning is unchanged; run it before review."},{"u":"docs/agent/01-overview.html#correction-patterns-notation-from-other-languages","p":"Overview: design invariants and notation (LR 1, LR 2)","h":"Correction patterns: notation from other languages","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns: notation from other languages # All of these are E-SYN-000 ( LR 3.2 ), a sentence outside the grammar of LR 1.1 's notation."},{"u":"docs/agent/01-overview.html#postfix--for-errors-lr-11","p":"Overview: design invariants and notation (LR 1, LR 2)","h":"Postfix ? for errors (LR 1.1)","m":"agent","x":"Postfix ? for errors (LR 1.1) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 try func parse ( n : Int ) Int => n try func twice ( n : Int ) Int { let v = parse ( n ) ? // reject: E-SYN-000 return v } test \"twice\" { expect twice ( 2 ) == 2 } Fortis // accept try func parse ( n : Int ) Int => n try func twice ( n : Int ) Int { let v = parse ( n ) or fail return v } test \"twice\" { expect twice ( 2 ) == 2 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/01-overview.html#empty-capture-list-lr-11","p":"Overview: design invariants and notation (LR 1, LR 2)","h":"Empty capture list (LR 1.1)","m":"agent","x":"Empty capture list (LR 1.1) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 pure func countPositive ( items : List [ Int ] ) Int { let positive = fn [ ] ( n : Int ) Bool => n > 0 // reject: E-SYN-000 return items . countWhere ( positive ) } test \"countPositive\" { expect countPositive ( List . of ( 1 , - 2 , 3 ) ) == 2 } Fortis // accept pure func countPositive ( items : List [ Int ] ) Int { let positive = pure noalloc fn ( n : Int ) Bool => n > 0 return items . countWhere ( positive ) } test \"countPositive\" { expect countPositive ( List . of ( 1 , - 2 , 3 ) ) == 2 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/01-overview.html#return-in-an-expression-body-lr-11","p":"Overview: design invariants and notation (LR 1, LR 2)","h":"return in an expression body (LR 1.1)","m":"agent","x":"return in an expression body (LR 1.1) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int => return n * 2 // reject: E-SYN-000 test \"double\" { expect double ( 2 ) == 4 } Fortis // accept pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int => n * 2 test \"double\" { expect double ( 2 ) == 4 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/01-overview.html#-paths-and-new-lr-11","p":"Overview: design invariants and notation (LR 1, LR 2)","h":":: paths and new (LR 1.1)","m":"agent","x":":: paths and new (LR 1.1) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 class Point { export x : Int } pure func origin ( ) Point => Point : : new ( 0 ) // reject: E-SYN-000 test \"origin\" { expect origin ( ) . x == 0 } Fortis // accept class Point { export x : Int export pure func of ( x : Int ) Point => Point ( x ) } pure func origin ( ) Point => Point . of ( 0 ) test \"origin\" { expect origin ( ) . x == 0 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/01-overview.html#the-worked-module-lr-2","p":"Overview: design invariants and notation (LR 1, LR 2)","h":"The worked module (LR 2)","m":"agent","x":"The worked module (LR 2) # LR 2 is one module that uses every construct; the tour has a self-contained counterpart that runs. Its constructs, by section: Construct in LR 2 Example LR imports: aliased module, braced members import fortis.text , import fortis.money { Money, Currency } LR 4.3 nominal refined newtype export type CustomerId = Int<1..> LR 6.9 , LR 6.3 class with exported and private fields, generated constructor export class Line derive Equatable { \u2026 } LR 9.1 , LR 9.2 refined field type quantity: Int<1..10000> LR 6.3 method modes total(self) , issue(editable self, \u2026) LR 9.3 , LR 7.6 enum with payloads Issued(at: Timestamp) LR 9.7 declared failure with payload and template export error CurrencyMismatch { \u2026 } LR 10.3 interface, mixin with requires Renderable , Summarised LR 9.5 , LR 9.6 fallible associated constructor pure try func create(\u2026) Invoice with fail LR 9.2 , LR 10.1 or fail propagation, return of a local (a move) let lineTotal = line.total() or fail , return sum LR 10.2 , LR 7.5 editable self call on a let mut binding, unmarked out.appendLine(\u2026) LR 7.5 exhaustive match with payload bindings Paid(at: paidAt, reference: ref) => \u2026 LR 9.8 closure with explicit kind and capture pure fn[alias threshold](line: Line) Bool { \u2026 } LR 8.2 , LR 8.3 defer , catch with report and recover , recovers withAudit LR 12.1 , LR 10.4 tests, table test with as row test \"quantity boundary\" cases { \u2026 } as row { \u2026 } LR 14 entry point in another package, capabilities as parameters try func main(console: Console) LR 4.6"},{"u":"docs/agent/03-lexical.html#lexical-structure-lr-3","p":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","h":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","m":"agent","x":"Lexical structure (LR 3) # Agent mode. Human mode: Lexical structure . Source of truth: LR 3 ."},{"u":"docs/agent/03-lexical.html#rules","p":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-03-01 A ; outside strings, comments and raw template regions is a parse error ( E-SYN-001 ). There is no semicolon token and no automatic insertion. LR 3.2 LANG-03-02 A newline ends a statement unless (a) it is inside (\u2026) , [\u2026] or {\u2026} of the same expression or argument list, (b) the line ends with a trailing continuation token, or (c) the next line starts with a leading continuation token. Refinement < > are not continuation tokens. LR 3.2 LANG-03-03 Uppercase first letter: type, interface, mixin, enum, variant, contract, error kind, observation schema. Lowercase or _ : value, function, method, field, parameter, module alias, label. Decided lexically, no symbol table. LR 3.3 LANG-03-04 Every keyword of LR Appendix A is reserved in every declaration and binding position ( E-SYN-002 ), including the kit heads; a keyword is a valid name after . and as a method or associated-function name. LR 3.4 LANG-03-05 Literal typing follows the LR 3.5 table; an out-of-range literal is rejected at compile time ( E-REF-001 ). LR 3.5 LANG-03-06 Each interpolated expression is evaluated once, left to right, must be Displayable , and must not be fallible or suspending. LR 3.6"},{"u":"docs/agent/03-lexical.html#token-facts","p":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","h":"Token facts","m":"agent","x":"Token facts # Fact LR Source: UTF-8, extension .ft ; identifiers ASCII [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]* ; Unicode only in strings, comments and data; a byte-order mark is rejected. LR 3.1 Quotas: a file over 16 MiB or 2,000,000 tokens, or nesting of brackets, blocks, prefix operators or interpolations deeper than 256, is E-LIM-002 and is not parsed. LR 3.1 Trailing continuation tokens: , ( [ = => + - * / % << >> & | ^ && || ?? == != < <= > >= .. ..< . LR 3.2 Leading continuation tokens: . or catch ?? else ) ] with where implements derive from LR 3.2 Body brace (statement block or declaration body) vs expression brace (record literal or type): decided by the token before { ; expression brace after = , ( , , , : , [ , ?? , == , != , a compound assignment, return , give , fail , recover . A body may be one line: { statement } . LR 3.2 return , fail , give take their operand on the same line; a bare return on its own line returns Void . LR 3.2 Parsing decisions P1 (one NL per run of blank/comment lines), P2 (longest chains), P3 (alternatives in grammar order) are normative for any parser. LR 3.2 Integer literal: decimal, 0x , 0b , 0o , _ separators; type from context, default Int . Float: needs . or exponent; f32 suffix gives Float32 . Decimal: d suffix. Duration: ms s min h . LR 3.5 \"text\" is StringView from static ; \"a ${x}\" is a fresh String that a binding annotated StringView cannot hold ( E-TYP-040 ); \"\"\"raw\"\"\" is StringView from static , no escapes, no interpolation; b\"abc\" is BytesView from static ; null is the absent case of T? . LR 3.5 Interpolation takes a read loan of the places it names; it never escapes for HTML or any other context. LR 3.6 Comments: only // . Documentation is the // run directly above a declaration with no blank line between. A fenced fortis block in documentation needs a first-line marker ( // accept , // reject: CODE , // execute: R , // illustrative ). LR 3.7"},{"u":"docs/agent/03-lexical.html#diagnostics","p":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-SYN-001 ; in code delete it (the record carries the edit) LR 3.2 E-SYN-000 not a sentence of the grammar: a line starting with a binary operator, /* */ , a byte-order mark, a top-level statement move the operator to the end of the previous line; use // ; remove the mark LR 3.2 E-SYN-002 keyword used as a declaration, binding or parameter name ( match , state , view , model , \u2026) rename; keywords are valid only after . and as method or associated-function names LR 3.4 E-REF-001 literal outside its target type or refinement change the literal or widen the declared type LR 3.5 E-TYP-040 interpolated expression not Displayable (including an Outcome from a fallible call); interpolated literal bound to a StringView bind the result first ( let v = f() or fail , then ${v} ); annotate String LR 3.5 E-LIM-002 source over the size, token or nesting quota split the file or reduce nesting LR 3.1"},{"u":"docs/agent/03-lexical.html#correction-patterns","p":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/03-lexical.html#e-syn-001-semicolon-lr-32","p":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","h":"E-SYN-001: semicolon (LR 3.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-SYN-001: semicolon (LR 3.2) # The record carries a correction, an edit that deletes the semicolon; apply it as given. Fortis // reject: E-SYN-001 pure func pair() Int { let a = 1; let b = 2 // reject: E-SYN-001 return a + b } test \"pair\" { expect pair() == 3 } output example.ft:3:12: error E-SYN-001 [LANG-03-01] (Syntax, violated): ';' is not a token expected_tokens: [] found_token: \";\" correction: delete the semicolon example.ft:3:12-3:13 -> \"\" Fortis // accept pure func pair ( ) Int { let a = 1 let b = 2 return a + b } test \"pair\" { expect pair ( ) == 3 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/03-lexical.html#e-syn-000-binary-operator-at-the-start-of-a-line-lr-32","p":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","h":"E-SYN-000: binary operator at the start of a line (LR 3.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-SYN-000: binary operator at the start of a line (LR 3.2) # Binary operators continue a line only when they are last on it. Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 pure func total ( a : Int < 0 .. 100 > , b : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int { let sum = a + b // reject: E-SYN-000 return sum } test \"total\" { expect total ( 1 , 2 ) == 3 } Fortis // accept pure func total ( a : Int < 0 .. 100 > , b : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int { let sum = a + b return sum } test \"total\" { expect total ( 1 , 2 ) == 3 } Leading . , or , catch , ?? and else do continue a line: Fortis // accept pure try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 != 0 { return 0 } return Int . div ( n , 2 ) or fail } pure recovers func pipeline ( fallback : Int ? ) Int { let n = fallback ?? 7 let h = half ( n ) or recover - 1 return h } test \"pipeline\" { expect pipeline ( null ) == 0 expect pipeline ( 10 ) == 5 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/03-lexical.html#e-syn-002-keyword-as-a-name-lr-34","p":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","h":"E-SYN-002: keyword as a name (LR 3.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-SYN-002: keyword as a name (LR 3.4) # Kit head words ( state , view , model , component , context , action , resource , inject , provide , \u2026) are reserved like every other keyword. Fortis // reject: E-SYN-002 pure func size ( state : Int ) Int => state // reject: E-SYN-002 test \"size\" { expect size ( 1 ) == 1 } records {\"class\": \"Syntax\", \"code\": \"E-SYN-002\", \"column\": 16, \"fields\": {\"expected_tokens\": [\"lowerIdent\"], \"found_token\": \"state\"}, \"file\": \"example.ft\", \"line\": 2, \"message\": \"keyword 'state' used as an identifier\", \"rule\": \"LANG-03-04\", \"severity\": \"error\", \"status\": \"violated\"} Fortis // accept pure func size ( current : Int ) Int => current test \"size\" { expect size ( 1 ) == 1 } A keyword is allowed as a method or associated-function name and after . : Fortis // accept class Celsius { export degrees : Int export pure func from ( degrees : Int ) Celsius => Celsius ( degrees ) } test \"from\" { expect Celsius . from ( 21 ) . degrees == 21 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/03-lexical.html#e-ref-001-literal-out-of-range-lr-35","p":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","h":"E-REF-001: literal out of range (LR 3.5)","m":"agent","x":"E-REF-001: literal out of range (LR 3.5) # Fortis // reject: E-REF-001 pure func percent ( ) Int < 0 .. 100 > { let p : Int < 0 .. 100 > = 101 // reject: E-REF-001 return 100 } test \"percent\" { expect percent ( ) == 101 } Fortis // accept pure func percent ( ) Int < 0 .. 100 > { let p : Int < 0 .. 100 > = 100 return p } test \"percent\" { expect percent ( ) == 100 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/03-lexical.html#e-typ-040-interpolation-lr-35-lr-36","p":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","h":"E-TYP-040: interpolation (LR 3.5, LR 3.6)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-040: interpolation (LR 3.5, LR 3.6) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 try func risky ( n : Int ) Int => n try func label ( n : Int ) String { return \"value ${risky(n)}\" // reject: E-TYP-040 } test \"label\" { expect label ( 2 ) == \"value 2\" } Fortis // accept try func risky ( n : Int ) Int => n try func label ( n : Int ) String { let v = risky ( n ) or fail return \"value ${v}\" } test \"label\" { expect label ( 2 ) == \"value 2\" } Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 pure func greeting ( name : StringView ) Int { let text : StringView = \"hello, ${name}\" // reject: E-TYP-040 return text . byteCount ( ) } test \"greeting\" { expect greeting ( \"ada\" ) == 10 } Fortis // accept pure func greeting ( name : StringView ) Int { let text : String = \"hello, ${name}\" return text . byteCount ( ) } test \"greeting\" { expect greeting ( \"ada\" ) == 10 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/03-lexical.html#facts-used-when-writing-code","p":"Lexical structure (LR 3)","h":"Facts used when writing code","m":"agent","x":"Facts used when writing code # Order: interpolated expressions run once each, left to right with the string ( LR 3.6 ). A string literal is free ( StringView from static ); converting it to String where a String is expected allocates and is the one implicit conversion ( LR 6.6 ). A byte string literal b\"\u2026\" is a BytesView from static ; read bytes with get(index) Byte? and byteCount() ( LR 3.5 , LR 6.6 ). Documentation comments are // runs directly above a declaration; fortis view --decls lists them ( LR 3.7 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#modules-and-packages-lr-4","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","m":"agent","x":"Modules and packages (LR 4) # Agent mode. Human mode: Modules and packages . Source of truth: LR 4 . Preview: dependencies and lock files ( LR 4.1 ) arrive in v0.2. An async entry point ( LR 4.6 , LR 12.3 ) arrives in v0.3. One package, its modules, imports, visibility and the ordinary entry point ( LR 4.1 , 4.2 , 4.3 , 4.4 , 4.5 and 4.6 ) are in v0.0.1."},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#rules","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-04-01 A module is one file under src/ ; its path is its identity ( src/a/b.ft is a.b , imported from another package as PKG.a.b ). A module holds only declarations and const bindings with compile-time constant initialisers. LR 4.2 LANG-04-02 Importing a module executes no code (there is no module initialisation). LR 4.2 LANG-04-03 Across packages, only modules listed in the exporting package's exports: may be imported. LR 4.3 LANG-04-04 Two versions of one package are two packages; a type's identity is its name, package, version and declaring module, so same-named types of two modules or versions never interchange. LR 4.1 LANG-04-05 A let may not shadow a visible binding ( E-NAM-003 ). LR 4.5 LANG-04-06 The entry point is the main of the manifest's entry module, exported or not, with one of the four heads of LR 4.6 , each optionally recovers (X-155; refused in packages today, D-52); each parameter is List[String] or a capability type, each type once ( E-NAM-005 , E-NAM-006 ), supplied by the runtime by its type (X-345). LR 4.6"},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#package-and-import-facts","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"Package and import facts","m":"agent","x":"Package and import facts # Fact LR Package = directory with project.yaml , lockfile fortis.lock , src/ . Manifest keys: schema: fortis.project/v1 , name , version , exports: [modules] , dependencies: {name: \"version\"} , optional entry: module (application), optional root: module . LR 4.1 A standard dependency ( fortis , fortis.\u2026 ) names a package of the compatibility set ( library/fortis/compatibility.yaml ) at the set's version, else E-NAM-031 . LR 4.1 Lockfile: when present, resolution uses only it and fetches nothing. Unpinned dependency, unused pin or unreadable lockfile: E-NAM-020 . Digest mismatch (package, compiler, standard library, Rust toolchain): E-NAM-021 . fortis check verifies package, compiler and standard-library pins; a native build also verifies the Rust toolchain. LR 4.1 --target T : an import of a standard module the target's feature set lacks is E-NAM-030 , before any native step. A package shipping a Cargo build script is refused by a native build: E-NAM-032 . LR 4.1 Limits: manifest over 1 MiB or 10,000 lines E-LIM-003 ; lockfile over 8 MiB or 100,000 lines E-LIM-004 ; toolchain JSON over 256 MiB or nested over 128 E-LIM-005 . Toolchain failures are records, never crashes: E-INT-001 (checker defect, with a minimised reproduction ), E-INT-002 (generated Rust does not compile), E-INT-003 (a native tool missing or failing). LR 4.1 Import forms: import p.m binds alias m ; import p.m as x binds x ; import p.m { A, b } binds exactly the listed members. Imports only at the top of a module, before any declaration. LR 4.3 Path split: the longest prefix naming the current package or a declared dependency is the package; the rest is the module path. Sibling modules therefore import each other by the full path ( import guide.shop.orders ). No matching prefix: an error naming the undeclared package. Ambiguous splits across the dependency set: E-NAM-010 . LR 4.3 root: m in the manifest makes import p { \u2026 } mean module m of p ; without it an empty module path is an error. LR 4.3 fortis.prelude is imported implicitly (core types, optional operators, built-in interfaces); fortis.text , fortis.collections , fortis.numeric , fortis.time , fortis.money , fortis.host need imports. Every kit package is named fortis.<kit> . LR 4.3 Importing one name twice, or a name that collides with a local declaration, is an error. Unused import: warning at the baseline lint level, error at the strict one. LR 4.3 export is the only visibility modifier. Unexported: private to the module. Exported: visible to importers; outside the package only if the module is in exports: . Class fields, methods, associated functions: private unless export . Enum variants, interface members and mixin-required methods share their declaration's visibility. LR 4.4 Generated constructor C(field: \u2026) is callable only where every field is visible. An exported field is readable through any loan and writable through an editable one. LR 4.4 Names resolve to the innermost enclosing block, then the module namespace. Qualified forms: module.member , Type.associated , Enum.Variant , value.field , value.method(\u2026) . No :: . A bare field name is not in scope inside a method: use self.field . LR 4.5 func main(console: Console, args: List[String]) with modifiers try , async or async try ; the result is Void , left out; export is implied. Each parameter is supplied by its type: List[String] gets the arguments, a capability type imported from fortis.host gets that capability; declare only what the body uses ( func main() takes nothing). main owns its capabilities and may move one ( Shared.create(move console) ). Older code writes export func main(args: List[String]) Void and calls Host.capabilities() once in the body; that stays accepted in a main with no capability parameter, and fortis fmt rewrites it. A failing try main prints one failure record to standard error and exits 1. An async main is the root task on the core cooperative executor. LR 4.6"},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#package-and-import-facts","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"Package and import facts","m":"agent","x":"n async main is the root task on the core cooperative executor. LR 4.6"},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#diagnostics","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-NAM-003 let shadows a visible binding rename LR 4.5 E-NAM-004 use of a declaration or member not visible at the use site export it (and list its module in exports: for other packages), or use an exported API LR 4.4 E-TYP-030 exported signature (function, method, field, constant) names a non-exported type export the type, or keep the function private LR 4.4 E-NAM-010 dependency set with names that make an import's package/module split ambiguous rename or drop a dependency LR 4.3 E-NAM-020 , E-NAM-021 lockfile does not pin the manifests' dependencies, or a digest differs fortis lock after reviewing the change LR 4.1 E-NAM-030 import of a standard module the --target feature set lacks remove the import or choose a target that has it LR 4.1 E-NAM-031 standard dependency outside the compatibility set or at another version use the set's version LR 4.1 E-NAM-032 package ships a Cargo build script (native build) remove the build script LR 4.1 E-LIM-003 , E-LIM-004 , E-LIM-005 manifest, lockfile or toolchain JSON over its limit reduce it LR 4.1 E-INT-001 , E-INT-002 , E-INT-003 toolchain defect, generated Rust rejected, native tool missing report it with the record's reproduction; not a source error LR 4.1 LR 4.3 and 4.6 state some rules without naming a code: an import of an undeclared package or of a module the exporting package does not list, an import that collides with a local declaration, and a missing or wrongly typed main are rejected, but which code reports each is not yet settled (X-152). Read the record's message and fields.candidates rather than relying on its code."},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#correction-patterns","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#a-package-lr-42-lr-43","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"A package (LR 4.2, LR 4.3)","m":"agent","x":"A package (LR 4.2, LR 4.3) # YAML # file: agent.shop/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: agent.shop version: 1.0.0 entry: main exports: [prices] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" Fortis // accept // file: agent.shop/src/prices.ft export pure func gross ( net : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => net + net / 5 pure func margin ( ) Int => 3 test \"gross adds a fifth\" { expect gross ( 100 ) == 120 } Fortis // accept // file: agent.shop/src/main.ft import fortis . host { Console } import agent . shop . prices func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${prices.gross(100)}\" ) } output 120"},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#e-nam-004-private-member-of-another-module-lr-44","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"E-NAM-004: private member of another module (LR 4.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-NAM-004: private member of another module (LR 4.4) # YAML # file: agent.till/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: agent.till version: 1.0.0 exports: [prices] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" Fortis // accept // file: agent.till/src/prices.ft export pure func gross ( net : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => net + net / 5 pure func margin ( ) Int => 3 test \"gross adds a fifth\" { expect gross ( 100 ) == 120 } agent.till is a library. A package that another package depends on is a library: it has no entry and no main . Only the root program's entry main creates the capabilities, and a library's functions receive them as parameters ( LR 11.6 ). YAML # file: agent.tillmix/project.yaml schema: fortis.project/v1 name: agent.tillmix version: 1.0.0 entry: main exports: [] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" agent.till: \"1.0.0\" Fortis // reject: E-NAM-004 // file: agent.tillmix/src/main.ft import fortis . host { Console } import agent . till . prices func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"${prices.gross(100)}\" ) discard prices . margin ( ) // reject: E-NAM-004 } Correction: write export pure func margin() Int => 3 in prices , or stop using it."},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#e-typ-030-exported-signature-names-a-private-type-lr-44","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"E-TYP-030: exported signature names a private type (LR 4.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-030: exported signature names a private type (LR 4.4) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-030 class Hidden { export n : Int } export pure func make ( ) Hidden => Hidden ( n : 1 ) // reject: E-TYP-030 test \"make\" { expect make ( ) . n == 1 } Fortis // accept export class Shown { export n : Int } export pure func make ( ) Shown => Shown ( n : 1 ) test \"make\" { expect make ( ) . n == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#import-placement-and-collisions-lr-43","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"Import placement and collisions (LR 4.3)","m":"agent","x":"Import placement and collisions (LR 4.3) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 pure func one ( ) Int => 1 import fortis . text // reject: E-SYN-000 test \"one\" { expect one ( ) == 1 } An imported name may not collide with a declaration of the module ( import fortis.money { Money } beside a local class Money is rejected); the LR names no code for it yet (X-152). Rename the local declaration: Fortis // accept import fortis . money { Money , Currency } class Price { export pennies : Int } test \"money\" { expect Price ( pennies : 1 ) . pennies == 1 expect Money . zero ( GBP ) . currency == GBP }"},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#entry-point-lr-46","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"Entry point (LR 4.6)","m":"agent","x":"Entry point (LR 4.6) # main declares the capabilities it needs as parameters; the runtime supplies each by its type (X-345). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console , args : List [ String ] ) { console . print ( \"${args.count()} arguments\" ) } output 0 arguments A failing try main exits with status 1 and one failure record on standard error; standard output keeps what was printed before the failure. Fortis // accept (exit status 1) import fortis . host { Path , Console , FileSystem } try func main ( console : Console , fileSystem : FileSystem ) { console . print ( \"start\" ) let text = fileSystem . readText ( Path . parse ( \"missing.txt\" ) or fail ) or fail console . print ( text ) } output start"},{"u":"docs/agent/04-modules.html#facts-used-when-writing-code","p":"Modules and packages (LR 4)","h":"Facts used when writing code","m":"agent","x":"Facts used when writing code # No module initialisation: a module-level value is a const with a constant initialiser ( LR 4.2 , LR 5.1 ). Within a package, imports may be cyclic for type declarations ( LR 4.2 ). Renaming an exported declaration or parameter, or moving a module out of exports: , breaks other packages ( LR 4.4 , LR 5.8 ). Reach the process's authority only through main 's capability parameters, and pass capabilities down as parameters ( LR 4.6 , LR 11.6 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#statements-and-expressions-lr-5","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","m":"agent","x":"Statements and expressions (LR 5) # Agent mode. Human mode: Statements and expressions . Source of truth: LR 5 ."},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#rules","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-05-01 let x = e / let mut x = e declare (initialiser required); let Pattern = move p destructures a class, tuple or record, and a pattern that can fail to match (a variant, a literal, null , present(\u2026) , at any depth) is E-MAT-004 : use match ; x = e assigns only to a visible let mut binding, or a field path rooted in a let mut binding, an editable parameter or an editable loan. Assignment is a statement and does not chain. LR 5.1 LANG-05-02 Every block exit (fall-through, return , fail , break , continue , give ) ends the block's loans, runs its defer s LIFO, then drops its owned locals in reverse initialisation order. LR 5.1 LANG-05-03 if and while conditions are Bool ; nothing else converts. LR 5.2 LANG-05-04 for i in a..<b (or a..b ) gives an immutable index refined to the range; the index is never assignable. LR 5.3 LANG-05-05 A non- Void function ends every path in return , fail or a Never call; no implicit value. LR 5.4 LANG-05-06 Precedence and associativity follow the LR 5.6 table; outcome operators ( or fail , or recover , catch ), ?? and && / || do not mix without parentheses. LR 5.6 LANG-05-07 Operands and arguments evaluate left to right, once; receiver before arguments; calls run evaluate, activate, invoke, release. LR 5.7 LANG-05-08 A non- Void expression statement needs discard , except a call of a function declared @ignorable ; a linear value ( Outcome , Future , Task , \u2026) cannot be discarded, except a never-polled future whose environment holds no linear value and no exclusive loan (it emits a not_started record). LR 5.5 , 12.3 LANG-05-09 Positional arguments first, then named in any order; each parameter bound once; parameter names are exported API. A pun wanted is wanted: wanted : after a named argument, and anywhere in a class constructor; before the first named argument a bare name is positional; an owned ownership-bearing local is written name: move name , never punned. LR 5.8 , X-345 Related, stated in LR 4.5 : shadowing a visible binding is E-NAM-003 ."},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#diagnostics","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-NAM-001 assignment to an undeclared name declare it with let mut , or fix the name LR 5.1 E-NAM-002 assignment to an immutable binding or a parameter declare it let mut ; a parameter is never reassignable, so copy it into a let mut local LR 5.1 E-NAM-003 let shadows a visible binding choose a new name LR 4.5 E-TYP-010 non- Bool condition ( if n , if maybe ) compare explicitly: n != 0 , maybe != null LR 5.2 E-TYP-110 a path reaches the end of a non- Void function, or a block arm or if -expression branch does not end in give add return / fail on that path, or give in the branch LR 5.4 E-TYP-120 give outside a match arm or if expression, recover outside catch , break / continue outside a loop move the statement into the construct it ends LR 5.4 E-ERR-001 fail outside a try function declare the function try , or handle the condition without failing LR 5.4 E-TYP-140 unused non- Void expression statement (a call of an @ignorable function excepted) discard e , or bind and use the value; declare the function @ignorable when most callers drop its result LR 5.5 E-OBL-001 discard of a linear value (a never-polled future holding nothing linear and no exclusive loan excepted) consume it: or fail , or recover v , catch , match , await , launch LR 5.5 , 12.3 E-SYN-004 outcome operators, ?? and && / || mixed without parentheses parenthesise the intended grouping LR 5.6 E-SYN-000 not a sentence of the grammar (for example f() or fail ?? 0 ) split the expression; let v = f() or fail on its own line LR 5.6 E-OWN-012 a later argument moves, assigns or exclusively loans a place an earlier argument reserved evaluate the conflicting argument into a binding first LR 5.7 E-TYP-130 positional after named, unknown parameter name, parameter bound twice, too many arguments fix the argument list LR 5.8 E-TYP-050 a parameter without a default is not bound pass it LR 5.8"},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#correction-patterns","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#e-nam-002-assignment-to-an-immutable-binding-lr-51","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"E-NAM-002: assignment to an immutable binding (LR 5.1)","m":"agent","x":"E-NAM-002: assignment to an immutable binding (LR 5.1) # Fortis // reject: E-NAM-002 pure func next ( n : Int < 0 .. 9 > ) Int { let x = n x = n + 1 // reject: E-NAM-002 return x } test \"next\" { expect next ( 1 ) == 2 } Fortis // accept pure func next ( n : Int < 0 .. 9 > ) Int { let mut x : Int < 0 .. 10 > = n x = n + 1 return x } test \"next\" { expect next ( 1 ) == 2 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#e-typ-010-non-bool-condition-lr-52","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"E-TYP-010: non-Bool condition (LR 5.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-010: non- Bool condition (LR 5.2) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-010 pure func describe ( n : Int ) StringView from static { if n { // reject: E-TYP-010 return \"non-zero\" } return \"zero\" } test \"describe\" { expect describe ( 0 ) == \"zero\" } Fortis // accept pure func describe ( n : Int ) StringView from static { if n != 0 { return \"non-zero\" } return \"zero\" } test \"describe\" { expect describe ( 0 ) == \"zero\" expect describe ( 3 ) == \"non-zero\" }"},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#e-typ-110-a-path-without-return-lr-54","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"E-TYP-110: a path without return (LR 5.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-110: a path without return (LR 5.4) # The record is reported at the function head. Fortis // reject: E-TYP-110 pure func sign ( n : Int ) Int { // reject: E-TYP-110 if n < 0 { return - 1 } } test \"sign\" { expect sign ( - 4 ) == - 1 } Fortis // accept pure func sign ( n : Int ) Int { if n < 0 { return - 1 } if n == 0 { return 0 } return 1 } test \"sign\" { expect sign ( - 4 ) == - 1 expect sign ( 0 ) == 0 expect sign ( 9 ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#e-typ-120-give-outside-an-if-expression-or-arm-lr-54","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"E-TYP-120: give outside an if expression or arm (LR 5.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-120: give outside an if expression or arm (LR 5.4) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-120 pure func pick ( flag : Bool ) Int { if flag { give 1 // reject: E-TYP-120 } return 0 } test \"pick\" { expect pick ( false ) == 0 } Fortis // accept pure func pick ( flag : Bool ) Int { return if flag { give 1 } else { give 0 } } test \"pick\" { expect pick ( true ) == 1 expect pick ( false ) == 0 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#e-typ-140-unused-value-lr-55","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"E-TYP-140: unused value (LR 5.5)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-140: unused value (LR 5.5) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-140 pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * 2 test \"double\" { double ( 4 ) // reject: E-TYP-140 expect double ( 2 ) == 4 } Fortis // accept pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * 2 test \"double\" { discard double ( 4 ) expect double ( 2 ) == 4 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#e-syn-004-mixed-operator-families-lr-56","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"E-SYN-004: mixed operator families (LR 5.6)","m":"agent","x":"E-SYN-004: mixed operator families (LR 5.6) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-004 pure func choose ( a : Bool , b : Bool ? , c : Bool ) Bool { return a || b ?? c // reject: E-SYN-004 } test \"choose\" { expect choose ( false , null , true ) } Fortis // accept pure func choose ( a : Bool , b : Bool ? , c : Bool ) Bool { return a || ( b ?? c ) } test \"choose\" { expect choose ( false , null , true ) expect ! choose ( false , false , true ) }"},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#e-own-012-a-reservation-broken-by-a-later-argument-lr-57","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"E-OWN-012: a reservation broken by a later argument (LR 5.7)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-012: a reservation broken by a later argument (LR 5.7) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-012 class Buffer { export bytes : Int } func update ( target : editable Buffer , size : Int ) { target . bytes = size } func consume ( b : owned Buffer ) Int => b . bytes test \"update\" { let mut buffer = Buffer ( bytes : 0 ) update ( editable buffer , consume ( move buffer ) ) // reject: E-OWN-012 expect buffer . bytes == 0 } Fortis // accept class Buffer { export bytes : Int } func update ( target : editable Buffer , size : Int ) { target . bytes = size } func size ( b : Buffer ) Int => 7 test \"update\" { let mut buffer = Buffer ( bytes : 0 ) let n = size ( buffer ) update ( editable buffer , n ) expect buffer . bytes == 7 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#e-typ-130-and-e-typ-050-argument-binding-lr-58","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"E-TYP-130 and E-TYP-050: argument binding (LR 5.8)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-130 and E-TYP-050: argument binding (LR 5.8) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-130 pure func area ( width : Int < 0 .. 100 > , height : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int => width * height test \"area\" { expect area ( width : 2 , depth : 3 ) == 6 // reject: E-TYP-130 } Fortis // reject: E-TYP-050 pure func area ( width : Int < 0 .. 100 > , height : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int => width * height test \"area\" { expect area ( width : 2 ) == 6 // reject: E-TYP-050 } Fortis // accept pure func area ( width : Int < 0 .. 100 > , height : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int => width * height test \"area\" { expect area ( 2 , height : 3 ) == 6 expect area ( height : 3 , width : 2 ) == 6 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#ignorable-instead-of-discard-lr-55","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"@ignorable instead of discard (LR 5.5)","m":"agent","x":"@ignorable instead of discard (LR 5.5) # A function whose result most callers do not need is declared @ignorable once, and its call statements drop the result without discard ( LR 5.5 , X-345). A linear result can never be ignorable ( E-TYP-150 ). Fortis // accept @ ignorable func remember ( seen : editable Set [ Int ] , n : Int ) Bool { return seen . add ( n ) } test \"remember\" { let mut seen : Set [ Int ] = Set . default ( ) remember ( editable seen , 4 ) expect seen . count ( ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/05-statements.html#facts-used-when-writing-code","p":"Statements and expressions (LR 5)","h":"Facts used when writing code","m":"agent","x":"Facts used when writing code # Literal booleans, if expressions and match give values; if without else is a statement only ( LR 5.2 ). Ranges a..b (inclusive) and a..<b (exclusive) exist only in for headers and refinement types ( LR 5.6 ). for x in p takes a read loan of p for the loop and binds read loans of the elements; for x in move p consumes p and binds owned elements ( LR 5.3 ). Labels: outer: for \u2026 , then break outer / continue outer ; break carries no value ( LR 5.3 ). return / fail inside a lambda refer to the lambda; give / recover never cross a function or lambda boundary ( LR 5.4 ). await f() or fail is (await f()) or fail ; copy await f() is a parse error: markers take places ( LR 5.6 ). Defaults are pure, non-allocating constant expressions re-evaluated at each call ( LR 5.8 ). No user variadics: take ListView[T] and pass List.of(\u2026) ( LR 5.8 ). Temporaries drop at the end of the full statement in reverse creation order ( LR 5.7 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/06-proofs.html#proofs-for-arithmetic-and-indexing-lr-63-64-67","p":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","h":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","m":"agent","x":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7) # Agent mode. Human mode: Proving arithmetic and indexing . Source of truth: LR 6.3 , LR 6.4 and LR 6.7 . Preview: predicate refinements ( LR 6.3 ) arrive in v0.1. The interval rules for arithmetic and indexing ( LR 6.4 , 6.7 ) are in v0.0.1. Language 1.0's interval domain is exactly the rules below; nothing else is inferred ( LANG-06-02 , LR 6.3 ). SP-18's wider rules are a later edition (X-146)."},{"u":"docs/agent/06-proofs.html#rules","p":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-06-02 Refinement subtyping is structural; the interval domain is exactly the rules of LR 6.3 , with no further inference. LR 6.3 LANG-06-03 An untrusted value reaches a refined or nominal type only through validate (or a literal the checker proves). LR 6.3 , 6.9 LANG-05-04 for i in a..<b / a..b gives the index the range's interval; the index is immutable. LR 5.3 , 6.3"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-proofs.html#the-domain-lr-63","p":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","h":"The domain (LR 6.3)","m":"agent","x":"The domain (LR 6.3) # Tracked: every integer-typed place of a function, as an interval within its base type. Sources: literal = exact; parameter or field = declared type; call result = declared return type, except the success value of a checked operation (a checked operator, or its named function Int.add , Int.sub , Int.mul , Int.div , Int.rem , Int.shiftLeft ), which is the operation on its operands clipped to the type (X-93). + - * : interval arithmetic. / and % : divisor interval must exclude 0; result is the quotient or remainder interval. << : amount within [0, width-1]. % with m = largest |d| : a \u2265 0 gives [0, m-1]; a \u2264 0 gives [-(m-1), 0]; otherwise [-(m-1), m-1]. Narrowing: conditions p < c , p <= c , p > c , p >= c , p == c , p != c ( c a literal, a const or a tracked place, narrowed too) in if , while , match ; && narrows by both; else gets the negation of a single comparison; a true branch ending in return , fail , break , continue or a Never call gives the negation to the rest of the block. || and calls never narrow. Join: hull of the incoming intervals. Loop header: every place assigned or invalidated in the body is widened to its declared type; a ranged for index keeps the range's interval. Refine loop counters ( let mut i: Int<0..1000> = 0 ). Invalidation: an exclusive loan of the place, a prefix or a field (an editable argument, an editable self call on it, marked or not, an editable binding), or an editable capture of a closure that is called; the place returns to its declared type. Assignment replaces the facts with the assigned interval. Length facts: i < p.count() true branch gives i below p 's length; in for i in a..<p.count() the index is below the length throughout a body that neither assigns nor exclusively loans p (X-93); i <= p.count() does not permit indexing; i >= p.count() / i > p.count() give the bound in their else . Upper bound only: the lower bound comes from i 's type or i >= 0 . Length-refined collections ( List[T]<length: \u2026> ) are immutable: no editable loan, no mutation."},{"u":"docs/agent/06-proofs.html#operators-and-indexing-lr-64-67","p":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","h":"Operators and indexing (LR 6.4, 6.7)","m":"agent","x":"Operators and indexing (LR 6.4, 6.7) # Proof-required: integer + - * / % << >> and unary - ; the result type is the base refined to the result interval. No dependence on the build mode. Checked operators, the alternative needing no proof (X-345): Int arithmetic as the left operand of or fail / or recover / catch is a checked region; each unproved + - * / % << or unary - in it is the checked operation, failing numeric.overflow , numeric.divide_by_zero or numeric.shift_range , the first failure ending the region: value = value * 10 + d or fail . The region stops at calls, indexes, fields, comparisons and other operators: f(a + b) or fail , a + b == c or fail and let s = a + b stay E-REF-002 ; write f(a + b or fail) , (a + b or fail) == c , let s = a + b or fail . A fully proved region cannot fail: or fail on it is E-TYP-040 . Only Int has a checked family; >> has none. Named alternatives: checked Int.add \u2026 ( Outcome[Int] , the same failures; older code writes Int.add(a, b) or fail , and fortis fmt writes the operator where the two are equivalent); wrapping Int.wrappingAdd \u2026; saturating Int.saturatingAdd \u2026. list[i] needs i \u2265 0 and below the length (known for Array[T, N] , length-refined lists, and the length facts above). Alternatives: get(i) \u2192 T? , at(i) \u2192 Outcome[T] failing collection.index ."},{"u":"docs/agent/06-proofs.html#diagnostics","p":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-REF-002 an integer operator not proved safe (overflow, zero divisor, shift amount, MIN / -1 ) outside a checked region; an index not proved in bounds refine the operands' types or test them first; else the checked operator, a + b or fail ; for an index, if i >= 0 && i < xs.count() , or get / at LR 6.4 , 6.7 E-REF-001 a value whose interval is not within the target's refinement (assignment, return, argument, literal) widen the target type, narrow first (a guard), or T.validate(x) or fail LR 6.3 , 3.5 E-REF-003 a length-refined collection loaned editable or mutated mutate an unrefined collection, then T.validate(move c) LR 6.3 The records carry required_range , proved_interval and widening_point . Read the status field before correcting ( records ): violated means the checker proved the rule broken (a literal outside the target's range, such as 20 returned as an Int<0..10> , or a mutation of a length-refined collection), so the program is wrong and the value or the type must change; unproved means it could not prove the bound, which is not a claim that the program is wrong, and the correction is a proof (a refinement or a guard) or the checked alternative."},{"u":"docs/agent/06-proofs.html#correction-patterns","p":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-proofs.html#e-ref-002-an-unproved-operator-lr-64","p":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","h":"E-REF-002: an unproved operator (LR 6.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-REF-002: an unproved operator (LR 6.4) # Fortis // reject: E-REF-002 pure func total ( a : Int , b : Int ) Int => a + b // reject: E-REF-002 test \"total\" { expect total ( 1 , 2 ) == 3 } Refined operands (when the domain really is bounded): Fortis // accept pure func total ( a : Int < 0 .. 1000000 > , b : Int < 0 .. 1000000 > ) Int => a + b test \"total\" { expect total ( 1 , 2 ) == 3 } The checked operation (when it is not): Fortis // accept try func total ( a : Int , b : Int ) Int { let sum = a + b or fail return sum } test \"total\" { expect total ( 1 , 2 ) == 3 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-proofs.html#e-ref-001-a-loop-carried-refined-accumulator-lr-63","p":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","h":"E-REF-001: a loop-carried refined accumulator (LR 6.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-REF-001: a loop-carried refined accumulator (LR 6.3) # Fortis // reject: E-REF-001 pure func sumTo ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int < 0 .. 500500 > { let mut total : Int < 0 .. 500500 > = 0 for i in 0 .. n { total = total + i // reject: E-REF-001 } return total } test \"sum\" { expect sumTo ( 3 ) == 6 } Fortis // accept pure try func sumTo ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int < 0 .. 500500 > { let mut total : Int = 0 for i in 0 .. n { total = total + i or fail } return Int < 0 .. 500500 > . validate ( total ) or fail } test \"sum\" { expect sumTo ( 3 ) == 6 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-proofs.html#e-ref-001-facts-lost-to-an-editable-call-lr-63","p":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","h":"E-REF-001: facts lost to an editable call (LR 6.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-REF-001: facts lost to an editable call (LR 6.3) # Fortis // reject: E-REF-001 class Counter { export n : Int < 0 .. 10 > } func reset ( c : editable Counter ) { c . n = 0 } pure func bump ( c : editable Counter ) { if c . n < 10 { reset ( editable c ) c . n = c . n + 1 // reject: E-REF-001 } } test \"bump\" { expect true } Test after the call that invalidates: Fortis // accept class Counter { export n : Int < 0 .. 10 > } func reset ( c : editable Counter ) { c . n = 0 } pure func bump ( c : editable Counter ) { reset ( editable c ) if c . n < 10 { c . n = c . n + 1 } } test \"bump\" { let mut c = Counter ( n : 7 ) bump ( editable c ) expect c . n == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-proofs.html#e-ref-002-an-unproved-index-lr-67","p":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","h":"E-REF-002: an unproved index (LR 6.7)","m":"agent","x":"E-REF-002: an unproved index (LR 6.7) # Fortis // reject: E-REF-002 pure func item ( xs : List [ Int ] , i : Int ) Int => xs [ i ] // reject: E-REF-002 test \"item\" { expect item ( List . of ( 5 ) , 0 ) == 5 } Fortis // accept pure func item ( xs : List [ Int ] , i : Int ) Int ? { if i >= 0 && i < xs . count ( ) { return xs [ i ] } return null } test \"item\" { let xs = List . of ( 5 , 6 ) expect item ( xs , 1 ) == 6 expect item ( xs , 2 ) == null }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-proofs.html#guard-clauses-narrow-the-rest-of-the-block-lr-63","p":"Proofs for arithmetic and indexing (LR 6.3, 6.4, 6.7)","h":"Guard clauses narrow the rest of the block (LR 6.3)","m":"agent","x":"Guard clauses narrow the rest of the block (LR 6.3) # Fortis // accept pure func clamp ( value : Int , low : Int < 0 .. 100 > , high : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int < 0 .. 100 > { if value < low { return low } if value > high { return high } return value } test \"clamp\" { expect clamp ( - 5 , 0 , 10 ) == 0 expect clamp ( 7 , 0 , 10 ) == 7 expect clamp ( 99 , 0 , 10 ) == 10 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#types-lr-6","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"Types (LR 6)","m":"agent","x":"Types (LR 6) # Agent mode. Human mode: Types . Source of truth: LR 6 . Proof rules for integer operators and indexing ( LR 6.3 , 6.4 , 6.7 ) are in Proofs , the complete reference for Language 1.0's interval domain; SP-18's further rules are a later edition (X-146). Preview: refinements ( LR 6.3 ), newtypes ( LR 6.9 ) and generics ( LR 6.10 ) arrive in v0.1. Normalisation, case mapping and the collections beyond List , Map and Set ( LR 6.6 , LR 6.7 ) arrive in v0.4. derive Default ( LR 6.10 ) is in v0.0.2."},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#rules","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-06-01 Nominal identity survives calls, storage, serialisation and FFI: distinct declarations never interchange, whatever their representation. LR 6.1 , 6.9 LANG-06-02 A refinement is a structural subtype of its base and of every refinement containing it; the reverse is never implicit; the interval domain is exactly LR 6.3 's, with no further inference. LR 6.3 LANG-06-03 Untrusted values pass validate before holding a refined or nominal type. LR 6.3 , 6.9 LANG-06-04 Integer, Decimal and the listed float operations agree bit-exactly across VM and native. LR 6.4 , 6.5 LANG-06-05 Float operators are total, strict IEEE 754; float refinements are rejected. LR 6.3 , 6.5 LANG-06-06 Map / Set iteration is deterministic per build (first-insertion order). LR 6.7 LANG-06-07 The trivial and copyable sets are exactly those of LR 6.11 . LR 6.11 LANG-06-08 Optional, Bool and Outcome never convert into one another. LR 6.8 LANG-06-09 Linear values are consumed exactly once on every path. LR 6.12 LANG-06-10 Coherence: an implementation of an interface for a type lives in the package defining one of them; overlaps are rejected. LR 6.10 , 9.5 LANG-06-11 A call whose type arguments are fixed neither by its arguments nor by its expected type is rejected. LR 6.10"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#diagnostics","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-TYP-040 an Outcome[T] passed where T is expected consume the outcome first ( let v = f() or fail ) LR 10.1 E-TYP-040 an interpolated string bound to a StringView binding annotate the binding String , or drop the annotation LR 3.5 E-REF-001 a literal outside its target refinement widen the declared type, or validate LR 3.5 , 6.3 E-TYP-020 a float refinement ( Float<0..1> ) refine an integer representation (for example a count of thousandths), or check the bound in an associated constructor function LR 6.3 E-REF-003 a length-refined collection loaned editable or mutated mutate an unrefined collection, then T.validate(move c) LR 6.3 E-TYP-030 a tuple, record or variant map in an exported signature (aspect parameters and the fortis package's own signatures excepted), or a non-exported type in one declare and export a named class LR 6.9 , 4.4 E-TYP-090 derive I where a field does not implement I ; a derivation rule not met; the codec pair on a non- Encodable / Decodable type implement I for the field type, change the field type, or write the implementation by hand LR 6.10 E-TYP-160 type arguments not fixed ( let names = List.default() ) annotate the receiving binding: let names: List[String] = List.default() LR 6.10 E-OWN-030 trivial class with a non-trivial field (reported at the class) drop trivial , or make every field trivial LR 6.11 E-OWN-002 copy of a non-copyable type ( Shared , Weak , Mutex , loans, futures, closures\u2026) retain() for Shared / Weak ; otherwise move or loan LR 6.11 , 7.5 E-OBL-001 a linear value dropped, overwritten or discarded (except discard of a never-polled future whose environment holds no linear value and no exclusive loan, LR 12.3 ) consume it ( Must.discharge(move m) ; outcomes: or fail , or recover , catch , match ) LR 6.12 , 12.3 E-TYP-010 an optional used as a condition x != null LR 6.8 E-MIX-001 deriving an interface the class also implements by hand keep one of the two LR 6.10"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#built-in-types-lr-62","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"Built-in types (LR 6.2)","m":"agent","x":"Built-in types (LR 6.2) # Void Never Bool ; Int8 Int16 Int32 Int64 UInt8 UInt16 UInt32 UInt64 ( Int = Int64 , UInt = UInt64 , Byte = UInt8 ); Float32 Float ; Decimal BigInt ; String StringView Bytes BytesView ; Duration Instant Timestamp ; List[T] Array[T, N] ListView[T] Map[K, V] OrderedMap[K, V] Set[T] FixedList[T, N] FixedMap[K, V, N] ; tuples, anonymous records, VariantMap[E, V] ; T? ; Outcome[T] Failure Report ; Future[T] Task[T] TaskCompletion[T] Shared[T] Weak[T] Mutex[T] AtomicInt AtomicBool ; fn / fn mut / fn once types. N is an integer literal; no generics over N . List.of(...items: T) is the only variadic form (standard library only) ( LR 6.2 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#facts-used-when-writing-code","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"Facts used when writing code","m":"agent","x":"Facts used when writing code # Refinement forms: Int<0..100> , Int<0..<100> , Int<1..> , Int<..0> , String<scalars: 1..120> , Bytes<length: 0..65536> , List[T]<length: 0..100> ; type position only ( LR 6.3 ). T.validate(x) and Int<0..100>.validate(x) are pure, return Outcome[T] , fail (by the prelude contract) refinement.out_of_range (code observed from the toolchain; LR 6.3 names only the outcome). Integer arithmetic ( LR 6.4 ): an operator the checker cannot prove safe is written as the left operand of an outcome operator, a * b + c or fail , and is the checked operation, failing numeric.overflow , numeric.divide_by_zero , numeric.shift_range (X-345). The named families, on Int only (the sized integer types have none yet; v0.4): checked Int.add/sub/mul/div/rem/shiftLeft \u2192 Outcome (the same operations; fortis fmt writes the operator where the two are equivalent); wrapping\u2026 and saturating\u2026 are total. Division truncates toward zero; % has the dividend's sign; >> arithmetic on signed, logical on unsigned. The success value of a checked intrinsic has the interval of the operation on its operands ( LR 6.3 , X-93), so Int.div(-7, 2) or recover 0 is E-REF-001 (0 is outside [-3, -3] ); annotate the binding Int . Floats ( LR 6.5 ): no Equatable / Hashable / Comparable ; == is IEEE; keys via Float.totalOrder(x) or Float.bitsEqual(a, b) ; display is shortest round-trip with a fractional part ( 2000.0 ), NaN , Infinity . Decimal ( LR 6.5 ): no operators; Decimal.add/sub/mul/div/rem \u2192 Outcome[Decimal] ; scale rules: add/sub larger scale, mul sum of scales, div largest that fits, half-even when over 28 digits; Decimal.round(v, scale, mode) with RoundingMode.HalfEven|HalfAwayFromZero|TowardZero|AwayFromZero ; displays with exactly its scale. Text ( LR 6.6 ): literal is StringView from static ; implicit literal-to- String conversion allocates (refused in noalloc ); String.from(view) ; no integer indexing; byteCount() O(1), scalarCount() O(n), slice(from, to) \u2192 Outcome[StringView from view] , scalarAt(i) \u2192 Outcome[Scalar] ; text.Builder create/append/appendLine/appendScalar/finish . Collections ( LR 6.7 ): list[i] needs a proof ( Proofs ); otherwise get(i) \u2192 (alias T from self)? , at(i) \u2192 Outcome[T] ; set(i, v) \u2192 Outcome[Void] ; push(item) \u2192 Void ; pop() \u2192 T? ; map / filter / countWhere take pure fn ; filter / reversed / concat need T: Copyable . Map.set/get/count , Set.add (\u2192 Bool , true when new) /contains/count ; iteration in first-insertion order, replacement keeps the position. get on a Map[String, V] takes a StringView key, so counts.get(word) takes a view or a String with no copy or String.from ; set takes an owned key. Optionals ( LR 6.8 ): T \u2192 T? implicit; ?? lazy; a?.f ; match with present(x) / null ; narrowing after x != null , after if x == null { return \u2026 } , and in x != null && e . Tuples t.0 ; records { name: a } ; VariantMap[E, V] literal { Found: 200, NotFound: 404 } with one entry per variant; (fn(Int) Bool)? is an optional function ( LR 6.9 ). Newtypes ( LR 6.9 ): type Name = Repr is always nominal with a private representation; values only from Name.validate(raw) or a proved literal Name(42) ; over a trivial representation each of Equatable, Hashable, Comparable and Displayable holds exactly when it holds for the representation (a newtype over Float is not Hashable, LR 6.5 ). Generics ( LR 6.10 ): [T] , where T implements I after the return type; type arguments are always inferred, never written at a call. Derivable ( LR 6.10 ): Equatable , Hashable , Comparable (declaration order), Displayable , Encodable , Decodable , Codec ; Default (field initialisers first; a class gets Name.default() , no arguments); From[S] (fields copied by name). x.to() where the target type is known, else T.from(x) . Canonical codec ( LR 6.10 ): toText() / fromText(input) (JSON, declaration order), toBytes() / fromBytes(input) ; failures codec.invalid , codec.unknown_field , or the refinement's own failure. Encodable derivation needs exported, en"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#facts-used-when-writing-code","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"Facts used when writing code","m":"agent","x":"lid , codec.unknown_field , or the refinement's own failure. Encodable derivation needs exported, encodable fields; secrets, authorities, loans, closures, futures and tasks are never encodable. Trivial ( LR 6.11 ): primitives, Decimal , Scalar , time types, newtypes over trivial, enums/optionals/tuples/ records of trivial, trivial class . Copyable: trivial, String , Bytes , BigInt , collections and aggregates of copyable. Not copyable: Shared , Weak , Mutex , atomics, loans, views, resources, Future , Task , Outcome , Failure , closures. Linear ( LR 6.12 ): Outcome[T] , Failure , Future[T] , Task[T] , Must , fn once capturing one, and any type that can contain one. Type parameters are non-linear unless T: linear ."},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#correction-patterns","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#e-typ-040-a-raw-value-where-a-newtype-is-expected-lr-69-lr-101","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"E-TYP-040: a raw value where a newtype is expected (LR 6.9, LR 10.1)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-040: a raw value where a newtype is expected (LR 6.9, LR 10.1) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 type CustomerId = Int < 1 .. > pure func lookup ( id : CustomerId ) Int => 1 pure func fromInput ( raw : Int ) Int { return lookup ( raw ) // reject: E-TYP-040 } test \"lookup\" { expect fromInput ( 5 ) == 1 } Fortis // accept type CustomerId = Int < 1 .. > pure func lookup ( id : CustomerId ) Int => 1 pure try func fromInput ( raw : Int ) Int { let id = CustomerId . validate ( raw ) or fail return lookup ( id ) } test \"lookup\" { let found = fromInput ( 5 ) expect found == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#e-ref-001-a-literal-outside-its-refinement-lr-35-lr-63","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"E-REF-001: a literal outside its refinement (LR 3.5, LR 6.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-REF-001: a literal outside its refinement (LR 3.5, LR 6.3) # Fortis // reject: E-REF-001 pure func limit ( ) Int < 0 .. 100 > { let score : Int < 0 .. 100 > = 140 // reject: E-REF-001 return 100 } test \"limit\" { expect limit ( ) == 100 } Fortis // accept pure func limit ( ) Int < 0 .. 100 > { let score : Int < 0 .. 100 > = 100 return score } test \"limit\" { expect limit ( ) == 100 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#e-ref-003-mutating-a-length-refined-collection-lr-63","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"E-REF-003: mutating a length-refined collection (LR 6.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-REF-003: mutating a length-refined collection (LR 6.3) # Fortis // reject: E-REF-003 pure func grow ( start : List [ Int ] < length : 0 .. 3 > ) Int { let mut items = copy start items . push ( 1 ) // reject: E-REF-003 return items . count ( ) } test \"grow\" { expect true } Fortis // accept pure try func three ( ) List [ Int ] < length : 0 .. 3 > { let mut items : List [ Int ] = List . default ( ) items . push ( 1 ) return List [ Int ] < length : 0 .. 3 > . validate ( move items ) or fail } test \"three\" { let items = three ( ) expect items . count ( ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#e-typ-160-type-arguments-not-fixed-lr-610","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"E-TYP-160: type arguments not fixed (LR 6.10)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-160: type arguments not fixed (LR 6.10) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-160 pure func names ( ) Int { let found = List . default ( ) // reject: E-TYP-160 return found . count ( ) } test \"names\" { expect names ( ) == 0 } Fortis // accept pure func names ( ) Int { let found : List [ String ] = List . default ( ) return found . count ( ) } test \"names\" { expect names ( ) == 0 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#e-typ-030-a-structural-type-in-an-exported-signature-lr-69","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"E-TYP-030: a structural type in an exported signature (LR 6.9)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-030: a structural type in an exported signature (LR 6.9) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-030 export pure func bounds ( ) ( Int , Int ) => ( 1 , 2 ) // reject: E-TYP-030 test \"bounds\" { expect bounds ( ) . 0 == 1 } Fortis // accept export class Bounds { export low : Int export high : Int } export pure func bounds ( ) Bounds => Bounds ( low : 1 , high : 2 ) test \"bounds\" { expect bounds ( ) . low == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#e-typ-090-deriving-an-interface-a-field-lacks-lr-610","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"E-TYP-090: deriving an interface a field lacks (LR 6.10)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-090: deriving an interface a field lacks (LR 6.10) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-090 class Reading derive Equatable { // reject: E-TYP-090 export celsius : Float } test \"reading\" { expect Reading ( celsius : 1.5 ) . celsius > 1.0 } Fortis // accept class Reading derive Equatable { export celsius : Decimal } test \"reading\" { expect Reading ( celsius : 1.5d ) == Reading ( celsius : 1.5d ) }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#e-typ-020-a-float-refinement-lr-63","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"E-TYP-020: a float refinement (LR 6.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-020: a float refinement (LR 6.3) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-020 type Ratio = Float < 0 .. 1 > // reject: E-TYP-020 test \"ratio\" { expect true } Fortis // accept type BasisPoints = Int < 0 .. 10000 > test \"ratio\" { let half = BasisPoints . validate ( 5000 ) expect half == BasisPoints ( 5000 ) }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#e-own-030-a-trivial-class-with-a-non-trivial-field-lr-611","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"E-OWN-030: a trivial class with a non-trivial field (LR 6.11)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-030: a trivial class with a non-trivial field (LR 6.11) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-030 trivial class Tag { // reject: E-OWN-030 export name : String } test \"tag\" { expect Tag ( name : \"a\" ) . name == \"a\" } Fortis // accept class Tag { export name : String } test \"tag\" { expect Tag ( name : \"a\" ) . name == \"a\" }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#e-obl-001-a-linear-value-dropped-lr-612","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"E-OBL-001: a linear value dropped (LR 6.12)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBL-001: a linear value dropped (LR 6.12) # Fortis // reject: E-OBL-001 pure func witness ( ) Int { let token = Must . create ( ) // reject: E-OBL-001 return 1 } test \"witness\" { expect witness ( ) == 1 } Fortis // accept pure func witness ( ) Int { let token = Must . create ( ) Must . discharge ( move token ) return 1 } test \"witness\" { expect witness ( ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/06-types.html#e-typ-040-an-interpolated-string-held-as-a-view-lr-35-lr-66","p":"Types (LR 6)","h":"E-TYP-040: an interpolated string held as a view (LR 3.5, LR 6.6)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-040: an interpolated string held as a view (LR 3.5, LR 6.6) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 pure func label ( n : Int ) Int { let text : StringView = \"count ${n}\" // reject: E-TYP-040 return text . scalarCount ( ) } test \"label\" { expect label ( 3 ) == 7 } Fortis // accept pure func label ( n : Int ) Int { let text : String = \"count ${n}\" return text . scalarCount ( ) } test \"label\" { expect label ( 3 ) == 7 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#ownership-lr-7","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"Ownership (LR 7)","m":"agent","x":"Ownership (LR 7) # Agent mode. Human mode: Ownership . Source of truth: LR 7 ."},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#rules","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-07-01 Assigning, returning or capturing an existing ownership-bearing place carries copy , alias or move , except that return local moves a local let binding; owned arguments carry move / copy , editable arguments carry editable ; an unmarked editable self receiver or fn mut call is checked exactly as the marked one, and is rejected where the marker could not be written. LR 7.5 LANG-07-02 Parameter bindings are never reassignable. LR 7.6 LANG-07-03 The checker never inserts clones, reference counts or hidden sharing to repair a rejected program. LR 7.10 LANG-07-04 Use after move, conflicting loans, owner use while loaned and escaping loans are rejected statically, including through closures, defer , await and scope . LR 7.3 , 7.4 LANG-07-05 Loans are lexical; the aliasing invariant (one exclusive loan, or any number of read loans, never both) holds at every point. LR 7.3 LANG-07-06 A returned loan names one origin: a function needing loans from two parameters in its result is E-OWN-050 (a loan type written with two origins is not a sentence of the grammar, E-SYN-000 , LR C.2 ), and a loan of a local or temporary returned is E-OWN-051 ; lifetime parameters and loan-carrying fields (other than a loan from static ) are E-OWN-053 (X-137). LR 7.6 , 7.7 , C.2 LANG-07-07 Destruction follows LR 7.9 on every backend. LR 7.9 LANG-07-08 Shared ownership is explicit ( retain ); copy of Shared is rejected. LR 7.8 LANG-07-09 A shape with no safe-Rust lowering is rejected with E-OWN-090 , never lowered with unsafe or erasure. LR 7.10"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#diagnostics","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-OWN-001 an existing ownership-bearing place assigned, returned (other than a local) or initialised into a field without a marker; a loan of a copyable value ( xs.get(0) , a binding holding one) where an owned value is expected; an editable self call or fn mut call, marked or not, on a root that cannot change (a let binding, a read parameter, capture or loan, the self of a read method) copy p (independent duplicate), alias p (read loan), or move p (transfer); for a loan, let v = xs.get(0) then copy v ; for a receiver, declare the binding let mut or the parameter editable LR 7.5 , LR 7.6 E-OWN-002 copy of a non-copyable place ( Shared , Weak , \u2026); a loan of a value that is not Copyable where an owned value is expected s.retain() for Shared / Weak ; otherwise move or a loan LR 7.5 , LR 7.6 E-OWN-010 a loan conflicting with an active loan (exclusive while read loans live, two exclusive) end the earlier loan: put its binding in an inner block, or loan once LR 7.3 E-OWN-011 the owner (or a suspended parent loan) used while loaned use the owner after the loan's block ends, or go through the loan LR 7.3 E-OWN-012 a later argument breaks an earlier argument's reservation evaluate the later argument into a binding first LR 5.7 E-OWN-020 a place used after move copy before the move, re-initialise a let mut , or restructure LR 7.4 E-OWN-040 a partial move ( move x.field while x still owns the rest) move the whole value, or consume it with a class pattern let C(field: f, other: _) = move x LR 7.2 E-OWN-050 a return keeps a loan of a parameter other than the declared from origin return an owned value, or split the function LR 7.6 E-OWN-051 a loan of a local or a temporary returned or kept beyond it; a value of the function's own (a move , a copy, a fresh value, a tuple or record literal, a constructor, an arm or branch giving one) where the result is declared from a parameter bind the owner first ( let u = make() then f(alias u) ), or return an owned value LR 7.6 E-OWN-052 a deferred block uses a binding of its block that holds a loan use the owner, or move the use out of the defer LR 12.1 E-OWN-053 a lifetime parameter, or a field of a loan or loan-carrying type (other than from static ) own the value ( String , not StringView ), hold a Shared , or pass it per call LR 7.7 E-OWN-090 an ownership shape with no lowering (a loan stored in Shared / Mutex , a fn mut escaping into Shared ) own the value; Mutex around state with an fn closure LR 7.10 , C.2"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#facts-used-when-writing-code","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"Facts used when writing code","m":"agent","x":"Facts used when writing code # Places: a binding, a field path from a binding or self , an Array element at a literal index. Call results, a ?? b , literals and list[i] with a variable index are values, not places ( LR 7.2 ). Markers by position ( LR 7.5 ): let x = p , x = p , name: p field initialiser and return p need a marker, except return local , which moves a local let / let mut binding; plain parameter arguments and self receivers are implicit read loans; owned self receivers move p.m() ; editable parameters editable p ; owned parameters move p / copy p ; an argument the result borrows from needs alias p (or editable p for an editable parameter); fn once calls move f() ; comparison and interpolation operands, match p and for x in p are implicit read loans; match move p / for x in move p consume. Fresh and trivial values never need a marker. What the declaration shows ( LR 7.5 ): an editable self receiver and a fn mut call take an exclusive loan with no marker, readings.push(40) , bump() , when the root is a let mut binding, an editable parameter, an editable or move capture, a binding holding an exclusive loan, or the method's own editable self ( self.order.push(w) ). The unmarked call is checked as the marked one (the same loans, E-OWN-010 , E-OWN-020 , and E-REF-003 on a length-refined collection, LR 6.3 ), on a trivial receiver too. On any other root it is E-OWN-001 , marked or not. Arguments keep their markers: normalise(editable readings) . Older code writes editable readings.push(40) , editable bump() and return move out ; they stay accepted and fortis fmt removes the marker. Loan lifetimes ( LR 7.3 ): binding-held loans last to the end of the declaring block; call-argument loans end at return unless the result carries them; comparison/interpolation/ capture loans end with the statement; for -header loans last for the loop; async -call argument loans are held by the future; from results are held by the receiving binding, a loan inside an optional, tuple or record result too ( let t = xs.get(0) : until t 's block ends, xs.pop() is E-OWN-010 and move xs is E-OWN-011 ; a List[Int] element holds nothing); no non-lexical shortening. Child loans ( LR 7.3 ): a read child of an exclusive parent freezes it; an exclusive child suspends it; a parent cannot be moved, reassigned or dropped while a child lives; a loan-holding binding (a parameter, alias p , editable p , a from result that is a loan) is never reassignable, but a let mut holding a loan inside an optional, tuple or record is ( t = xs.get(1) , t = null : the new value's loans replace the old); at a join a loan is live if live on any path. Receivers ( LR 7.6 ): self read loan, editable self exclusive loan, owned self consumes. from names exactly one plain or editable parameter or self , never owned . The kept loan has the declared mode ( StringView from user read; editable Cell from c exclusive, needs c: editable ). Returns ( LR 7.6 ): return local (a move); return move param for an owned parameter; return copy obj.field or a from signature for a borrowed field; fresh expressions unmarked. Loan-carrying types ( LR 7.7 ): StringView , BytesView , ListView[T] , standard cursors. Usable as a local's type, a parameter type, or a from return type; owned by their holder; may not cross launchParallel or launchBlocking . Shared[T] ( LR 7.8 ): Shared.create(move v) , s.retain() , s.downgrade() \u2192 Weak[T] , w.upgrade() \u2192 Shared[T]? ( nondet ), w.retain() . Reads through s.field / s.method() are read loans; mutation needs Mutex[T] or an atomic; cycles leak; neither Shared nor Weak is linear. A Shared[A] of an authority carries A ( LR 11.6 ). Destruction order ( LR 7.9 ): block exit = loans end, defer s last-first, locals in reverse initialisation; assignment = evaluate new, drop old, store; temporaries at statement end in reverse creation; a class value = its cleanup , then fields in declaration order. Only an owner destroys: a loan's end ( xs.get(0) , alias p , a from result, "},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#facts-used-when-writing-code","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"Facts used when writing code","m":"agent","x":"s in declaration order. Only an owner destroys: a loan's end ( xs.get(0) , alias p , a from result, a parameter that is not owned , a borrowed pattern or loop binding) runs no cleanup , which runs once per value, at its owner's destruction."},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#correction-patterns","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-001-missing-marker-lr-75","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-001: missing marker (LR 7.5)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-001: missing marker (LR 7.5) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-001 class User { export name : String } pure func rename ( user : User ) User { let other = user // reject: E-OWN-001 return other } test \"rename\" { expect rename ( User ( name : \"a\" ) ) . name == \"a\" } Fortis // accept class User { export name : String } pure func rename ( user : User ) User { let other = copy user return other } test \"rename\" { expect rename ( User ( name : \"a\" ) ) . name == \"a\" }"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-001-an-editable-self-call-on-a-binding-that-cannot-change-lr-75","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-001: an editable self call on a binding that cannot change (LR 7.5)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-001: an editable self call on a binding that cannot change (LR 7.5) # The receiver of an editable self method needs no marker on a let mut binding; on a let binding the call is rejected, marked or not. Fortis // reject: E-OWN-001 func readings ( ) List [ Int ] { let out : List [ Int ] = List . default ( ) out . push ( 40 ) // reject: E-OWN-001 return out } test \"readings\" { expect readings ( ) . count ( ) == 1 } Fortis // accept func readings ( ) List [ Int ] { let mut out : List [ Int ] = List . default ( ) out . push ( 40 ) return out } test \"readings\" { expect readings ( ) . count ( ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-001-a-loan-where-an-owned-value-is-expected-lr-76","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-001: a loan where an owned value is expected (LR 7.6)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-001: a loan where an owned value is expected (LR 7.6) # xs.get(0) lends the list's element. A loan stays a loan, so returning it as an owned T? , binding it to an owned type, or passing it owned needs copy , which copies the value the loan lends. Fortis // reject: E-OWN-001 func first ( names : List [ String ] ) String ? { return names . get ( 0 ) // reject: E-OWN-001 } test \"first\" { let got = first ( List . of ( \"a\" ) ) ?? \"none\" expect got == \"a\" } Fortis // accept func first ( names : List [ String ] ) String ? { let name = names . get ( 0 ) return copy name } test \"first\" { let got = first ( List . of ( \"a\" ) ) ?? \"none\" expect got == \"a\" } A value that is not Copyable (a Shared , a class holding a capability) has no owned copy: the same return is E-OWN-002 . Keep the loan, or take the value out of its owner."},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-020-use-after-move-lr-74","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-020: use after move (LR 7.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-020: use after move (LR 7.4) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-020 func consume ( items : owned List [ Int ] ) Int => items . count ( ) test \"consume\" { let items = List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) expect consume ( move items ) == 3 expect items . count ( ) == 3 // reject: E-OWN-020 } Fortis // accept func consume ( items : owned List [ Int ] ) Int => items . count ( ) test \"consume\" { let items = List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) expect consume ( copy items ) == 3 expect items . count ( ) == 3 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-010-conflicting-loans-lr-73","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-010: conflicting loans (LR 7.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-010: conflicting loans (LR 7.3) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-010 class Invoice { export customer : Int export lines : List [ String ] export func issue ( editable self ) { self . customer = 0 } } test \"issue\" { let mut invoice = Invoice ( customer : 7 , lines : List . of ( \"tea\" ) ) let lines = alias invoice . lines invoice . issue ( ) // reject: E-OWN-010 expect invoice . customer == 0 } Fortis // accept class Invoice { export customer : Int export lines : List [ String ] export func issue ( editable self ) { self . customer = 0 } } test \"issue\" { let mut invoice = Invoice ( customer : 7 , lines : List . of ( \"tea\" ) ) { let lines = alias invoice . lines expect lines . count ( ) == 1 } invoice . issue ( ) expect invoice . customer == 0 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-011-owner-used-while-loaned-lr-73","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-011: owner used while loaned (LR 7.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-011: owner used while loaned (LR 7.3) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-011 class Cell { export value : Int } func set ( c : editable Cell ) Int { let r = editable c r . value = 1 return c . value // reject: E-OWN-011 } test \"set\" { let mut cell = Cell ( value : 0 ) expect set ( editable cell ) == 1 } Fortis // accept class Cell { export value : Int } func set ( c : editable Cell ) Int { { let r = editable c r . value = 1 } return c . value } test \"set\" { let mut cell = Cell ( value : 0 ) expect set ( editable cell ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-040-partial-move-lr-72","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-040: partial move (LR 7.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-040: partial move (LR 7.2) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-040 class Order { export id : Int export lines : List [ String ] } func keep ( lines : owned List [ String ] ) Int => lines . count ( ) test \"keep\" { let order = Order ( id : 1 , lines : List . of ( \"tea\" ) ) expect keep ( move order . lines ) == 1 // reject: E-OWN-040 } Fortis // accept class Order { export id : Int export lines : List [ String ] } func keep ( lines : owned List [ String ] ) Int => lines . count ( ) test \"keep\" { let order = Order ( id : 1 , lines : List . of ( \"tea\" ) ) let Order ( id : _ , lines : lines ) = move order expect keep ( move lines ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-051-a-loan-of-a-temporary-lr-76","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-051: a loan of a temporary (LR 7.6)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-051: a loan of a temporary (LR 7.6) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-051 class User { export name : String export pure func guest ( ) User => User ( name : \"guest\" ) } pure func displayName ( user : User ) StringView from user => alias user . name test \"guest\" { let name = displayName ( User . guest ( ) ) // reject: E-OWN-051 expect name == \"guest\" } Fortis // accept class User { export name : String export pure func guest ( ) User => User ( name : \"guest\" ) } pure func displayName ( user : User ) StringView from user => alias user . name test \"guest\" { let user = User . guest ( ) let name = displayName ( alias user ) expect name == \"guest\" } A value of the function's own is the same case where the result is declared from a parameter: return xs.get(0) ?? move d , return move d or a fresh value would leave the caller holding a loan of what ended with the call (D-373). A from result is a loan, so what produces it must lend: a place read through a loan, alias p , a call whose result lends, a ?? both of whose sides lend, an if or match every one of whose results lends, or present of one. A tuple or record literal ( return (xs.get(0) ?? move d, 1) ), a constructor, a lambda, a call whose result is owned, and an arm or branch that gives any of these are the function's own too (D-377, D-381). Bind the loan and return an owned value from a function whose result is owned, or lend on every path. Fortis // reject: E-OWN-051 class Token { export label : String } func pick ( xs : List [ Token ] , d : owned Token ) alias Token from xs { return xs . get ( 0 ) ?? move d // reject: E-OWN-051 } Fortis // accept class Token { export label : String } func pick ( xs : List [ Token ] , d : owned Token ) Token { let v = xs . get ( 0 ) return copy v ?? move d } test \"pick\" { let xs : List [ Token ] = List . default ( ) let t = pick ( xs , Token ( label : \"fallback\" ) ) expect t . label == \"fallback\" }"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-050-a-second-origin-lr-76","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-050: a second origin (LR 7.6)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-050: a second origin (LR 7.6) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-050 class User { export name : String } pure func longer ( a : User , b : User ) StringView from a { if a . name . scalarCount ( ) > b . name . scalarCount ( ) { return alias a . name } return alias b . name // reject: E-OWN-050 } test \"longer\" { let a = User ( name : \"ada\" ) let b = User ( name : \"brian\" ) expect longer ( alias a , b ) == \"brian\" } Fortis // accept class User { export name : String } pure func longer ( a : User , b : User ) String { if a . name . scalarCount ( ) > b . name . scalarCount ( ) { return copy a . name } return copy b . name } test \"longer\" { let a = User ( name : \"ada\" ) let b = User ( name : \"brian\" ) expect longer ( a , b ) == \"brian\" }"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-053-a-loan-in-a-field-lr-77","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-053: a loan in a field (LR 7.7)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-053: a loan in a field (LR 7.7) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-053 class Line { // reject: E-OWN-053 export number : Int export text : StringView } test \"line\" { expect true } Fortis // accept class Line { export number : Int export text : String } test \"line\" { let line = Line ( number : 1 , text : \"first\" ) expect line . text == \"first\" }"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-002-copy-of-a-shared-lr-75-lr-78","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-002: copy of a Shared (LR 7.5, LR 7.8)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-002: copy of a Shared (LR 7.5, LR 7.8) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-002 class Settings { export name : String } test \"share\" { let first = Shared . create ( Settings ( name : \"prod\" ) ) let second = copy first // reject: E-OWN-002 expect second . name == \"prod\" } Fortis // accept class Settings { export name : String } test \"share\" { let first = Shared . create ( Settings ( name : \"prod\" ) ) let second = first . retain ( ) expect second . name == \"prod\" }"},{"u":"docs/agent/07-ownership.html#e-own-090-a-loan-stored-in-shared-lr-710-lr-c2","p":"Ownership (LR 7)","h":"E-OWN-090: a loan stored in Shared (LR 7.10, LR C.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-090: a loan stored in Shared (LR 7.10, LR C.2) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-090 class User { export name : String } pure func displayName ( user : User ) StringView from user => alias user . name func run ( user : User ) Int { let seen = displayName ( alias user ) let shared = Shared . create ( move seen ) // reject: E-OWN-090 return shared . scalarCount ( ) } test \"run\" { expect run ( User ( name : \"ada\" ) ) == 3 } Fortis // accept class User { export name : String } func run ( user : User ) Int { let shared = Shared . create ( copy user . name ) return shared . scalarCount ( ) } test \"run\" { expect run ( User ( name : \"ada\" ) ) == 3 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#functions-and-closures-lr-8","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","m":"agent","x":"Functions and closures (LR 8) # Agent mode. Human mode: Functions and closures . Source of truth: LR 8 ."},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#rules","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-08-01 Every parameter has a type and the success type is written, except that a function or method head leaves out Void and a lambda's are inferred as LR 8.2 allows; modifiers in the order [export] [Contract \u2026] [pure] [noalloc] [async] [try] [recovers] func ; aspects on the lines above. LR 8.1 LANG-08-02 A lambda writes its kind ( fn , fn mut , fn once ) and its capture list (brackets only when non-empty); a body needing a stronger kind than declared is rejected. LR 8.2 LANG-08-03 A name not in the capture list is inaccessible in the lambda body. LR 8.3 LANG-08-04 Callable assignability: kind fn \u2264 fn mut \u2264 fn once ; parameter modes match exactly; modifiers at least as strong; an owned closure's region at least as long. The backend boxes a closure only where dynamic fn is written. LR 8.4"},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#facts","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"Facts","m":"agent","x":"Facts # Expression body: func f(x: T) R => expr , exactly one expression ( LR 8.1 ). A Void result is left out of every function and method head, expression bodies and cleanup included: func reset(c: editable Counter) { \u2026 } . A function type keeps it ( fn(Int) Void ), and so does a block-bodied lambda. Older code writes ) Void { ; fortis fmt drops it ( LR 8.1 ). A named function is not a value; its name where a callable is expected becomes a non-capturing fn of the matching type ( LR 8.1 ). try , async , recovers must be written when the summary has them (functions and lambdas); pure and noalloc are optional claims, inferred for lambdas; the formatter prints no inferred one back on a lambda and keeps one the author wrote (the callee's function type states them and the checker enforces it there) ( LR 8.1 , LR 8.2 , X-345). Lambda shape below; => expr for an expression body; a block body writes its return type, an expression body may leave it out: it is the callee's (or annotation's) result type when that names one, else the body's type widened as an unannotated let ( LR 8.2 , X-345); parameter types may be omitted only when passed directly to a parameter of known function type; lambdas are not generic ( LR 8.2 ). Fortis // illustrative [ pure ] [ noalloc ] [ async ] [ try ] [ recovers ] fn [ captures ] ( params ) ReturnType { body } fn ( params ) ReturnType => expression fn ( params ) => expression Kinds ( LR 8.2 ): Kind Captures may be Call Binding Lowers to fn copy , alias , move (read-only use) f(x) let Fn fn mut plus editable , and mutated move captures f(x) , an exclusive loan of f let mut FnMut fn once plus move captures moved out move f(x) , once let FnOnce A fn mut call needs no editable where the binding shows it may change ( let mut , an editable parameter or capture), and is checked as the marked call; through a let it is E-OWN-001 , marked or not. Older code writes editable f(x) ( LR 8.2 , LR 7.5 ). A move capture alone does not require fn once ; moving the captured value out does ( LR 8.2 ). Capture entries: copy x , alias x , editable x , move x ; a trivial value may be bare ( [n] = copy). Evaluated left to right when the lambda is evaluated. Module const s, functions and types are not captures ( LR 8.3 ). Capturing a field path ( alias self.lines ) captures that path and loans the root owner for the closure's lifetime; two editable captures of one owner, or editable plus any other capture of it, conflict ( LR 8.3 ). A loan-holding closure passed to a plain fn parameter is a read loan for the call: no region in the type. Owned positions ( owned fn parameter, return) must name its region: fn(Int) Bool from limit ; a field never holds one ( LR 8.3 , LR 7.7 ). A closure capturing a linear value by move is linear: it must be fn once and be called exactly once on every path, or passed to an owned fn once parameter ( LR 8.3 ). fn() (StringView from s) from s : when the result is loan-carrying, from after it belongs to the result; the closure's own region goes on a parenthesised result ( LR 8.4 ). dynamic fn(\u2026) R : boxed, owned, allocates on creation; the only way to hold closures of different concrete types in one collection ( LR 8.4 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#diagnostics","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-FUN-010 the body needs a stronger kind than declared (a fn mutates a capture, a fn mut moves one out); reported at the use, with a correction edit to the kind strengthen the kind ( fn \u2192 fn mut \u2192 fn once ) LR 8.2 E-FUN-011 a name used in the body is not in the capture list add the capture with the right marker LR 8.3 E-OWN-010 two captures of one owner where one is editable capture once, or narrow to disjoint field paths read-only LR 8.3 E-OBL-006 a fn or fn mut captures a linear value by move declare fn once and call it once with move f() LR 8.3 E-OWN-001 a fn mut called through a binding that cannot change (a let ), marked or not; a fn once call without move hold the fn mut in a let mut and call f(\u2026) ; write move f(\u2026) LR 7.5 E-OWN-020 a fn once called a second time (the first call moved it) call it once; restructure LR 7.4 E-OWN-011 a captured place used while the closure holds an exclusive loan of it end the closure's block before using the place LR 8.3 Modifiers appear in exactly the order of LR 8.1 ; any other order is a canonicalisation error that the formatter fixes ( LR 8.1 ). Today's tools disagree with that rule: both checkers report an out-of-order head as E-SYN-000 ( LR 3.2 ) and fortis fmt refuses the file, a disagreement recorded in X-153 and D-59. Write modifiers in order."},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#correction-patterns","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#e-fun-010-kind-too-weak-lr-82","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"E-FUN-010: kind too weak (LR 8.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-FUN-010: kind too weak (LR 8.2) # Fortis // reject: E-FUN-010 func countCalls ( ) Int { let mut calls = 0 { let bump = fn [ editable calls ] ( ) Void { calls = Int . saturatingAdd ( calls , 1 ) // reject: E-FUN-010 } } return calls } test \"calls\" { expect countCalls ( ) == 0 } Fortis // accept func countCalls ( ) Int { let mut calls = 0 { let mut bump = fn mut [ editable calls ] ( ) Void { calls = Int . saturatingAdd ( calls , 1 ) } bump ( ) bump ( ) } return calls } test \"calls\" { expect countCalls ( ) == 2 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#e-fun-011-missing-capture-lr-83","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"E-FUN-011: missing capture (LR 8.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-FUN-011: missing capture (LR 8.3) # Fortis // reject: E-FUN-011 pure func countAbove ( items : List [ Int ] , limit : Int ) Int { return items . countWhere ( fn ( n : Int ) Bool => n > limit ) // reject: E-FUN-011 } test \"above\" { expect countAbove ( List . of ( 1 , 5 , 9 ) , 4 ) == 2 } Fortis // accept pure func countAbove ( items : List [ Int ] , limit : Int ) Int { return items . countWhere ( fn [ limit ] ( n : Int ) Bool => n > limit ) } test \"above\" { expect countAbove ( List . of ( 1 , 5 , 9 ) , 4 ) == 2 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#e-own-001-fn-mut-called-through-a-binding-that-cannot-change-lr-75-lr-82","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"E-OWN-001: fn mut called through a binding that cannot change (LR 7.5, LR 8.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-001: fn mut called through a binding that cannot change (LR 7.5, LR 8.2) # A fn mut held by a let mut binding is called as add(item) ; held by a let , it cannot be called at all. Fortis // reject: E-OWN-001 func sum ( items : List [ Int ] ) Int { let mut total = 0 { let add = fn mut [ editable total ] ( n : Int ) Void { total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , n ) } for item in items { add ( item ) // reject: E-OWN-001 } } return total } test \"sum\" { expect sum ( List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) ) == 6 } Fortis // accept func sum ( items : List [ Int ] ) Int { let mut total = 0 { let mut add = fn mut [ editable total ] ( n : Int ) Void { total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , n ) } for item in items { add ( item ) } } return total } test \"sum\" { expect sum ( List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) ) == 6 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#e-own-010-conflicting-captures-lr-83","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"E-OWN-010: conflicting captures (LR 8.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-010: conflicting captures (LR 8.3) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-010 func reset ( ) Int { let mut count = 5 { let mut both = fn mut [ editable count , alias count ] ( ) Void { // reject: E-OWN-010 count = 0 } both ( ) } return count } test \"reset\" { expect reset ( ) == 0 } Fortis // accept func reset ( ) Int { let mut count = 5 { let mut clear = fn mut [ editable count ] ( ) Void { count = 0 } clear ( ) } return count } test \"reset\" { expect reset ( ) == 0 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#e-obl-006-a-linear-capture-in-a-repeatable-closure-lr-83","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"E-OBL-006: a linear capture in a repeatable closure (LR 8.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBL-006: a linear capture in a repeatable closure (LR 8.3) # The fn mut below also never consumes what it owns, so its binding is dropped with an obligation: E-OBL-001 on the same line ( LR 6.12 ). Fortis // reject: E-OBL-006, E-OBL-001 export error Oops { code : \"demo.oops\" } pure try func risky ( n : Int ) Int { if n > 9 { fail Oops ( ) } return n } pure recovers func later ( n : Int ) Int { let pending = risky ( n ) let mut run = fn mut [ move pending ] ( ) Int { // reject: E-OBL-006, E-OBL-001 return 0 } return run ( ) } test \"later\" { expect later ( 3 ) == 3 } Fortis // accept export error Oops { code : \"demo.oops\" } pure try func risky ( n : Int ) Int { if n > 9 { fail Oops ( ) } return n } pure recovers func later ( n : Int ) Int { let pending = risky ( n ) let run = fn once [ move pending ] ( ) Int { return move pending or recover 0 } return move run ( ) } test \"later\" { expect later ( 3 ) == 3 expect later ( 12 ) == 0 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#a-loan-holding-closure-in-an-owned-position-needs-its-region-lr-83-lr-84","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"A loan-holding closure in an owned position needs its region (LR 8.3, LR 8.4)","m":"agent","x":"A loan-holding closure in an owned position needs its region (LR 8.3, LR 8.4) # A returned closure that holds a loan names its region in the return type ( fn(Int) Bool from limit ); without it the function is rejected ( LR 8.3 ). Which code reports that is not yet settled (X-152): the record names the place and asks for \u2026 from limit . Fortis // accept class Limit { export value : Int } func above ( limit : Limit ) fn ( Int ) Bool from limit { return fn [ alias limit ] ( n : Int ) Bool => n > limit . value } test \"above\" { let limit = Limit ( value : 3 ) let check = above ( alias limit ) expect check ( 5 ) expect ! check ( 2 ) }"},{"u":"docs/agent/08-functions.html#callback-purity-lr-84","p":"Functions and closures (LR 8)","h":"Callback purity (LR 8.4)","m":"agent","x":"Callback purity (LR 8.4) # A non- pure callable where pure fn is required is a type mismatch ( expected_type pure fn(\u2026) ). Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 import fortis . host { Console } pure func applyPure ( f : pure fn ( Int ) Int , x : Int ) Int => f ( x ) func logged ( console : Console , n : Int ) Int { console . print ( \"called\" ) return n } func run ( console : Console ) { discard applyPure ( fn [ alias console ] ( n : Int ) Int => logged ( console , n ) , 1 ) // reject: E-TYP-040 } test \"run\" { expect true } Fortis // accept pure func applyPure ( f : pure fn ( Int ) Int , x : Int ) Int => f ( x ) pure func double ( n : Int ) Int => Int . saturatingMul ( n , 2 ) test \"run\" { expect applyPure ( double , 4 ) == 8 expect applyPure ( fn ( n : Int ) Int => Int . saturatingAdd ( n , 1 ) , 4 ) == 5 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#classes-interfaces-mixins-and-enums-lr-9","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","m":"agent","x":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9) # Agent mode. Human mode: Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums . Source of truth: LR 9 . Preview: interfaces and mixins ( LR 9.5 , 9.6 ) arrive in v0.1. Classes, cleanup, enums and match ( LR 9.1 , 9.2 , 9.3 , 9.4 , 9.7 and 9.8 ) are in v0.0.1."},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#rules","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-09-01 No inheritance, no runtime member lookup, no reflection-based construction. LR 9.1 LANG-09-02 The generated constructor Name(field: v, \u2026) is callable only where every field is visible; private fields are settable only inside the module. LR 9.2 LANG-09-03 An implementation may not strengthen a precondition (narrower parameter refinement) or weaken a guarantee (drop pure , add try / async , widen a return refinement); coherence: implement only in the package defining the interface or the type, at most one implementation per (interface, type). LR 9.5 LANG-09-04 Mixins require methods only, add no fields, constructors or cleanup ; a method name provided by two applied mixins and not declared by the class is rejected. LR 9.6 LANG-09-05 match over a closed type (enum, optional) is exhaustive without otherwise ; each missing variant is named. LR 9.8 LANG-09-06 Pattern ownership: match p takes a read loan and binds read loans; match move p consumes and binds owned values; a match of a fresh value owns it; a linear payload needs the owning form and must be consumed. LR 9.8"},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#facts","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"Facts","m":"agent","x":"Facts # Class shape: [export] [trivial] class Name[T] [derive \u2026] [implements \u2026] [with \u2026] { fields, methods, associated functions, const, cleanup } ; fields and members private unless export ; field defaults are constant expressions; one namespace for fields, methods, associated functions and constants ( LR 9.1 ). Constructor arguments are owned positions: an existing ownership-bearing place needs copy / move ; fields with defaults may be omitted. A field may be given by a pun, Item(sku, wanted) for Item(sku: sku, wanted: wanted) ; a pun passes the place unmarked, so an owned ownership-bearing local is written name: move name ( LR 9.2 , LR 5.8 ). Spread: Name(...copy p, f: v) copies same-named fields of matching type (or with From ), leaves p ; ...move p consumes p , drops untaken fields (never a linear one), and p may not have cleanup . Order: spread, explicit fields left to right, then defaults ( LR 9.2 ). Associated function: no self , called Name.f(\u2026) ; the only path to values with private fields; constructors are named create , of or from ( LR 9.2 , LR 1.1 ). derive Default on a class gives Name.default() , no arguments: each field at its initialiser, a field without one at its type's default ( false , 0 , empty, null , a class's own derived default, the default() of a type that implements Default , a newtype's representation's); a field with neither is E-TYP-090 (a class without Default , a capability, Int<1..10> , an Array , FixedList , FixedMap or OrderedMap , also behind a newtype, a type parameter T ). On an enum it is E-TYP-090 : the enum implements Default and its default() returns the variant. Use it instead of a hand-written create() that sets every field empty ( LR 6.10 , LR 9.2 , X-345). Receivers: self (read; call v.m() ), editable self (exclusive; call p.m() on a let mut binding, an editable parameter or the method's own editable self , as self.items.push(x) ; elsewhere E-OWN-001 , LR 7.5 ), owned self (call move p.m() ); fields as self.field ; associated constants const c = \u2026 read as Name.c ( LR 9.3 ). Older code writes editable p.m() , which stays accepted. A pure method may write through editable self (its footprint), nothing else ( LR 9.3 ). cleanup(editable self) runs at destruction before fields drop; not try , not async , not callable; its effects join every function that may drop the type ( LR 9.4 ). Interfaces: signatures with modifiers and receiver modes, associated functions, associated constants; no bodies, no associated types; where on type parameters; requires other interfaces ( LR 9.5 ). Implementing: implements I with every member, derive I , or implement I for T { \u2026 } at module level (the form for generic implementations); same names alone never implement ( LR 9.5 ). Bundle = requirements, no members: satisfied automatically ( Codec ). Marker = no members, no requirements: explicit only ( Authority ). Copyable , Shareable are structural ( LR 9.5 ). dynamic I : owned and boxed (allocates on creation); alias dynamic I (loan); Shared[dynamic I] . Dynamic-eligible: no associated types, no generic methods, no Self by value. An async method through dynamic allocates its future ( LR 9.5 ). Mixin: requires method signatures; body checked at each application against the class's exported members only; a class method beats a mixin method of the same name ( LR 9.6 ). Enum variants UpperCamel, optional named payloads; E.V , E.V(f: x) ; prefix optional in match patterns and wherever the expected type is that enum (return, argument, field initialiser, annotated let , the right operand of == / != / < \u2026 whose left operand is that enum); a variant name where a function type is expected is its constructor ( LR 9.7 ). A bare name that is also a class, error or type in scope, or one nothing expects the enum of ( let x = Error , an unannotated List.of(Error) ), is E-TYP-040 : write E.V , or annotate the binding ( LR 9.7 , X-345). Patterns: name , _ , literal, Variant(\u2026) , present(x) / null , success(x) / failure(f)"},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#facts","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"Facts","m":"agent","x":"9.7 , X-345). Patterns: name , _ , literal, Variant(\u2026) , present(x) / null , success(x) / failure(f) , is Error(\u2026) [as n] , Name(field: p, \u2026) (consuming only, every field named or _ for non-linear, no cleanup ), (p1, p2) , { f: p } , otherwise (open types only: integers, strings, Failure ). Guards when b ; guarded arms do not count for exhaustiveness. No range patterns, no x @ p ( LR 9.8 ). Or-patterns: A | B | C => v matches when any alternative does, left to right. Alternatives bind nothing ( Circle(radius: _) , literals, null , present(0) ), are never otherwise , and the arm takes no when guard ( E-MAT-003 ); each alternative counts for exhaustiveness ( E-MAT-001 for a variant none names), and a _ alternative is a catch-all ( E-MAT-002 over a closed enum). Use them for tables of equal results ( LR 9.8 , X-345)."},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#diagnostics","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-TYP-070 a name declared twice in one class (fields, methods, associated functions and constants share one namespace) rename one LR 9.1 E-NAM-004 generated constructor called where a field is not visible call an exported associated function ( create ) LR 4.4 , LR 9.2 E-TYP-050 constructor call missing a field with no default and no spread source pass the field LR 9.2 E-OBL-007 ...move p would drop a linear field the target lacks consume the field first (destructure), or give the target a field for it LR 9.2 E-TYP-040 cleanup called directly let destruction run it; expose an explicit method for fallible finalisation LR 9.4 E-TYP-080 missing member, incompatible signature (a dropped pure , an added try ), unmet requires , unmet mixin requirement provide the member with the interface's exact contract LR 9.5 E-TYP-060 interface requirement cycle break the cycle LR 9.5 E-MIX-001 derive plus a hand-written method the derivation generates; or two mixins provide one method the class does not declare drop one; declare the method in the class LR 9.5 , LR 9.6 E-TYP-100 implementation outside the packages defining the interface or the type; two implementations for one type implement in the owning package; keep one LR 9.5 E-TYP-090 derive whose rule is not met (a field does not implement the interface) implement it for the field type, or write the method by hand LR 6.10 E-MAT-001 match over a closed type misses variants (one record, each missing variant named) add an arm per variant LR 9.8 E-MAT-002 catch-all ( _ , a bare binding, otherwise ) as the last arm over a closed enum name every variant LR 9.8 E-MAT-003 an or-pattern alternative binds a name or is otherwise , or the arm has a when guard match the binding or guarded cases in arms of their own; _ for an unused payload LR 9.8"},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#correction-patterns","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#e-typ-070-field-and-method-share-a-name-lr-91","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"E-TYP-070: field and method share a name (LR 9.1)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-070: field and method share a name (LR 9.1) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-070 class Account { balance : Int export pure func balance ( self ) Int => self . balance // reject: E-TYP-070 } test \"balance\" { expect true } Fortis // accept class Account { balance : Int export pure func current ( self ) Int => self . balance } test \"balance\" { expect Account ( balance : 5 ) . current ( ) == 5 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#e-typ-080-implementation-weakens-the-interface-lr-95","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"E-TYP-080: implementation weakens the interface (LR 9.5)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-080: implementation weakens the interface (LR 9.5) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-080 interface Priced { pure func price ( self ) Int } class Pear implements Priced { // reject: E-TYP-080 export pure try func price ( self ) Int => 4 } test \"price\" { expect true } Fortis // accept interface Priced { pure func price ( self ) Int } class Pear implements Priced { export pure func price ( self ) Int => 4 } test \"price\" { expect Pear ( ) . price ( ) == 4 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#e-mix-001-two-mixins-provide-one-method-lr-96","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"E-MIX-001: two mixins provide one method (LR 9.6)","m":"agent","x":"E-MIX-001: two mixins provide one method (LR 9.6) # Fortis // reject: E-MIX-001 mixin English { export pure func hello ( self ) StringView from static => \"hello\" } mixin French { export pure func hello ( self ) StringView from static => \"bonjour\" } class Greeter with English , French { // reject: E-MIX-001 export id : Int } test \"hello\" { expect true } Fortis // accept mixin English { export pure func hello ( self ) StringView from static => \"hello\" } mixin French { export pure func hello ( self ) StringView from static => \"bonjour\" } class Greeter with English , French { export id : Int export pure func hello ( self ) StringView from static => \"hi\" } test \"hello\" { expect Greeter ( id : 1 ) . hello ( ) == \"hi\" }"},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#e-mat-001-and-e-mat-002-closed-matches-lr-98","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"E-MAT-001 and E-MAT-002: closed matches (LR 9.8)","m":"agent","x":"E-MAT-001 and E-MAT-002: closed matches (LR 9.8) # E-MAT-001 is reported at the match ; E-MAT-002 at the catch-all arm. Fortis // reject: E-MAT-001 enum Light { Red Amber Green } pure func code ( l : Light ) Int { return match l { // reject: E-MAT-001 Red => 1 Amber => 2 } } test \"code\" { expect code ( Light . Red ) == 1 } Fortis // reject: E-MAT-002 enum Light { Red Amber Green } pure func code ( l : Light ) Int { return match l { Red => 1 otherwise => 2 // reject: E-MAT-002 } } test \"code\" { expect code ( Light . Red ) == 1 } Fortis // accept enum Light { Red Amber Green } pure func code ( l : Light ) Int { return match l { Red => 1 Amber => 2 Green => 3 } } test \"code\" { expect code ( Red ) == 1 expect code ( Green ) == 3 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#class-pattern-only-with-move-lr-98","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"Class pattern only with move (LR 9.8)","m":"agent","x":"Class pattern only with move (LR 9.8) # A class pattern destructures, so it is permitted only in match move or let \u2026 = move , every field named (or _ for a non-linear one), and never for a class with cleanup . Which code reports a class pattern without move is not yet settled (X-152). Fortis // accept class Parcel { export label : String export grams : Int } pure func heavy ( p : owned Parcel ) Bool { return match move p { Parcel ( label : _ , grams : g ) => g > 1000 } } test \"heavy\" { expect heavy ( Parcel ( label : \"books\" , grams : 2500 ) ) expect ! heavy ( Parcel ( label : \"feather\" , grams : 1 ) ) }"},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#e-typ-040-calling-cleanup-lr-94","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"E-TYP-040: calling cleanup (LR 9.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-040: calling cleanup (LR 9.4) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 class Cell { export value : Int cleanup ( editable self ) { } } test \"cell\" { let mut c = Cell ( value : 1 ) c . cleanup ( ) // reject: E-TYP-040 expect c . value == 1 } Fortis // accept class Cell { export value : Int cleanup ( editable self ) { } } test \"cell\" { { let c = Cell ( value : 1 ) expect c . value == 1 } }"},{"u":"docs/agent/09-classes.html#e-obl-007-move-drops-a-linear-field-lr-92","p":"Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums (LR 9)","h":"E-OBL-007: ...move drops a linear field (LR 9.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBL-007: ...move drops a linear field (LR 9.2) # Fortis // reject: E-OBL-007 class Pending { export label : String export result : Outcome [ Int ] } class Label { export label : String } pure try func compute ( ) Int => 1 pure recovers func relabel ( ) String { let p = Pending ( label : \"p\" , result : compute ( ) ) let l = Label ( ... move p ) // reject: E-OBL-007 return copy l . label } test \"relabel\" { expect relabel ( ) == \"p\" } Fortis // accept class Pending { export label : String export result : Outcome [ Int ] } pure try func compute ( ) Int => 1 pure recovers func relabel ( ) String { let p = Pending ( label : \"p\" , result : compute ( ) ) let Pending ( label : label , result : r ) = move p let value = move r or recover 0 return \"${label}${value}\" } test \"relabel\" { expect relabel ( ) == \"p1\" }"},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#outcomes-and-failures-lr-10","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","m":"agent","x":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10) # Agent mode. Human mode: Outcomes and failures . Source of truth: LR 10 . Preview: linear containers ( LR 10.7 ) arrive in v0.1. The rest of this chapter ( LR 10 ) is in v0.0.1."},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#rules","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-10-01 An Outcome[T] stays wrapped until consumed by an operation of LR 10.2 ; its success value is never reachable otherwise. LR 10.1 LANG-10-02 Linear obligations survive storage in collections, fields and closures; dropping one is rejected. LR 10.7 LANG-10-03 A reported failure is unreachable afterwards; annotate keeps exactly one obligation. LR 10.6 LANG-10-04 noalloc try functions use the bounded failure representation on success and failure paths. LR 10.3 LANG-10-05 recover without a prior report adds the recovers effect. LR 10.4 LANG-10-06 Defects follow the closed taxonomy and are never catchable. LR 10.8"},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#facts","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"Facts","m":"agent","x":"Facts # try func f(\u2026) T returns Outcome[T] ; return v = success, fail f = failure; the call needs no prefix ( LR 10.1 ). Patterns success(x) , failure(f) ; by hand: Outcome.success(v) , Outcome.failure(move f) for a failure binding f ( LR 10.1 ). Consuming operations, the complete list ( LR 10.2 ): Operation Effect Where e or fail propagate try functions e or recover v discharge, v lazy; adds recovers anywhere e catch n { \u2026 } block ends in recover v , return , fail or a Never call anywhere match e { success(x) => \u2026, failure(f) => \u2026 } the failure arm consumes f anywhere report f / report f as r consume; r: Report receipt io allowed (not pure ) move to owned Outcome[T] / owned Failure , store in linear container or field transfer anywhere fail f ( f: Failure ) propagate try functions No bare or value , no ? , no call-site try , no unwrap ( LR 10.2 ). or binds loosest: write let v = f() or fail on its own line ( LR 1.1 ). Integer arithmetic the checker cannot prove safe, as the left operand of an outcome operator, is a checked region handled like a call: total = total + r or fail , value * 10 + d or recover 0 ( LR 6.4 ). In test and property bodies or fail is implied after a fallible call nothing else consumes: expect half(4) == 2 ( LR 14 ). Failure : stable code ( StringView from static ), typed payload, origin, optional cause chain; linear, not copyable; created in a pure function it reads no clock and allocates no identifier; bounded payload (64 bytes inline) satisfies noalloc ( LR 10.3 ). error Name { \u2026 } with the lines code: \"\u2026\" , optionally payload { \u2026 } and message: \"\u2026${field}\u2026\" , one per line: constructor Name(\u2026) producing a Failure , pattern is Name(\u2026) , display template over payload fields only (not rendered in records yet, and Failure is not Displayable : read e.code and payloads, X-154, D-57; constructor calls where a Failure value is expected are refused today, D-52); library failures have no is -nameable kind: test e.code (D-58); codes unique per package and stable; payload fields trivial or length-bounded String / Bytes ( LR 10.3 ). catch : return / fail leave the function; recover v is the expression's value; give not allowed; covers only its left operand; recover after the failure was consumed ( report , fail , move , match ) adds nothing, otherwise it consumes and adds recovers ( LR 10.4 ). Failure match: match move f { is A(x: v) => \u2026, is B as b => { \u2026consume b\u2026 }, otherwise(o) => { \u2026consume o\u2026 } } ; payloads bound by copy; is without as discharges (adds recovers ); otherwise(name) is required (open type); match f without move inspects only and f must still be consumed ( LR 10.5 ). report is a statement; with the log statements, the io needing no capability (diagnostic plane only); a pure function cannot report ( LR 10.6 , LR 11.6 ). A failure is recorded with report e alone, never logged as well; log.warn(\u2026) is for what is not a failure, such as a fallback taken ( LR 13 ). annotate(f: owned Failure, note: StringView) Failure ; Failures.aggregate(list: owned List[Failure]) Failure ( LR 10.6 ). A failing try main : one report on standard error, exit status 1 ( LR 4.6 ). Linear containers: push(move x) in, pop() out as a linear T? , consumeEach(owned self, fn mut(owned T) Void) (in order, no early exit), clear() and dropping rejected, count() / isEmpty() borrow; Outcome.collect(move list) Outcome[List[T]] ; a generic function holding T linearly declares T: linear ( LR 10.7 ). Defects (contract violation in trusted code, stack exhaustion, out of memory, runtime invariant): native aborts, the VM terminates the task; never a Failure , never caught, no completion value; observed only as TaskCompletion.Defect by a supervisor ( LR 10.8 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#diagnostics","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-OBL-001 an outcome (or any linear value) dropped at the end of a statement or block, overwritten or discarded (a never-polled future holding nothing linear and no exclusive loan may be discarded, LR 12.3 ) consume it: or fail , or recover v , catch , match , report , move LR 10.1 E-TYP-040 an Outcome[T] used where T is expected consume it first: let v = f() or fail LR 10.1 E-OBL-002 a path through a catch block ends without recover , return , fail or a Never call end every path LR 10.4 E-ERR-001 fail outside a try function declare the function try LR 5.4 E-EFF-001 a discharged, unreported failure in a function without recovers (the record carries a correction inserting recovers ) write recovers , or report the failure before recover LR 11.1 E-OWN-020 a failure used after report , fail or move consumed it consume it once LR 7.4 E-OBL-005 a linear value live across a cancellation point, unless its type is Abandonable (as Task is); the future or task being awaited is not live across its own await consume it before the await , pass it into the awaited call, or hold an Abandonable handle instead LR 12.4 E-OBL-006 a fn / fn mut closure owning a linear value fn once LR 8.3 E-OBL-007 ...move dropping a linear field destructure and consume it LR 9.2"},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#correction-patterns","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#e-obl-001-dropped-outcome-lr-101","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"E-OBL-001: dropped outcome (LR 10.1)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBL-001: dropped outcome (LR 10.1) # Fortis // reject: E-OBL-001 export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( ) } return key } pure try func check ( key : Int ) Bool { lookup ( key ) // reject: E-OBL-001 return true } test \"check\" { expect check ( 1 ) or fail } Fortis // accept export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( ) } return key } pure try func check ( key : Int ) Bool { let found = lookup ( key ) or fail return found == key } test \"check\" { expect check ( 1 ) }"},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#e-typ-040-an-outcome-where-a-value-is-expected-lr-101","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"E-TYP-040: an outcome where a value is expected (LR 10.1)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-040: an outcome where a value is expected (LR 10.1) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-040 export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( ) } return key } pure func double ( n : Int ) Int => Int . saturatingMul ( n , 2 ) pure try func twice ( key : Int ) Int { return double ( lookup ( key ) ) // reject: E-TYP-040 } test \"twice\" { expect twice ( 2 ) == 4 } Fortis // accept export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( ) } return key } pure func double ( n : Int ) Int => Int . saturatingMul ( n , 2 ) pure try func twice ( key : Int ) Int { let v = lookup ( key ) or fail return double ( v ) } test \"twice\" { expect twice ( 2 ) == 4 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#e-obl-002-a-catch-path-without-an-ending-lr-104","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"E-OBL-002: a catch path without an ending (LR 10.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBL-002: a catch path without an ending (LR 10.4) # The path that falls through also drops e : E-OBL-001 on the same line ( LR 10.1 ). Fortis // reject: E-OBL-002, E-OBL-001 export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( ) } return key } pure recovers func safe ( key : Int ) Int { let v = lookup ( key ) catch e { // reject: E-OBL-002, E-OBL-001 if key > 100 { recover 0 } } return v } test \"safe\" { expect safe ( 1 ) == 1 } Fortis // accept export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( ) } return key } pure recovers func safe ( key : Int ) Int { let v = lookup ( key ) catch e { if key > 100 { recover 0 } recover - 1 } return v } test \"safe\" { expect safe ( 1 ) == 1 expect safe ( 7 ) == - 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#e-eff-001-a-silent-discard-without-recovers-lr-104-lr-111","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"E-EFF-001: a silent discard without recovers (LR 10.4, LR 11.1)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-001: a silent discard without recovers (LR 10.4, LR 11.1) # Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( ) } return key } pure func orZero ( key : Int ) Int => lookup ( key ) or recover 0 // reject: E-EFF-001 test \"orZero\" { expect orZero ( 9 ) == 0 } Fortis // accept export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( ) } return key } pure recovers func orZero ( key : Int ) Int => lookup ( key ) or recover 0 test \"orZero\" { expect orZero ( 9 ) == 0 expect orZero ( 2 ) == 2 } Alternative when the discard must not be silent: a catch e block whose first line is report e and whose last is recover 0 , which adds no recovers but needs io , so the function cannot be pure ( LR 10.4 , LR 10.6 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#e-own-020-reporting-and-then-propagating-lr-106-lr-74","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"E-OWN-020: reporting and then propagating (LR 10.6, LR 7.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-020: reporting and then propagating (LR 10.6, LR 7.4) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-020 export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( ) } return key } try func strict ( key : Int ) Int { let v = lookup ( key ) catch e { report e fail e // reject: E-OWN-020 } return v } test \"strict\" { expect strict ( 1 ) == 1 } Fortis // accept export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( ) } return key } try func strict ( key : Int ) Int { let v = lookup ( key ) catch e { fail annotate ( move e , \"strict lookup\" ) } return v } test \"strict\" { expect strict ( 1 ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#matching-failures-by-kind-lr-105","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"Matching failures by kind (LR 10.5)","m":"agent","x":"Matching failures by kind (LR 10.5) # Fortis // accept export error NotANumber { code : \"parse.not_a_number\" payload { position : Int } } export error Empty { code : \"parse.empty\" } pure try func firstDigit ( text : StringView ) Int < 0 .. 9 > { if text . scalarCount ( ) == 0 { fail Empty ( ) } let s = text . scalarAt ( 0 ) or fail let d = Scalar . digitValue ( s ) if d == null { fail NotANumber ( position : 0 ) } return d } pure try recovers func classify ( text : StringView ) StringView from static { return match firstDigit ( text ) { success ( d ) => \"digit\" failure ( f ) => match move f { is NotANumber ( position : p ) => \"not a number\" is Empty => \"empty\" otherwise ( other ) => fail other } } } test \"classify\" cases { \"digit\" : { text : \"7x\" , expected : \"digit\" } \"letter\" : { text : \"x7\" , expected : \"not a number\" } \"empty\" : { text : \"\" , expected : \"empty\" } } as row { expect classify ( row . text ) == row . expected }"},{"u":"docs/agent/10-outcomes.html#consuming-a-list-of-outcomes-lr-107","p":"Outcomes and failures (LR 10)","h":"Consuming a list of outcomes (LR 10.7)","m":"agent","x":"Consuming a list of outcomes (LR 10.7) # Fortis // accept export error Missing { code : \"store.missing\" } pure try func lookup ( key : Int ) Int { if key > 3 { fail Missing ( ) } return key } pure try func sumAll ( keys : List [ Int ] ) Int { let mut outcomes : List [ Outcome [ Int ] ] = List . default ( ) for k in keys { outcomes . push ( lookup ( k ) ) } let values = Outcome . collect ( move outcomes ) or fail let mut total = 0 for v in values { total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , v ) } return total } test \"sumAll\" { expect sumAll ( List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) ) == 6 let bad = sumAll ( List . of ( 1 , 9 ) ) catch e { expect e . code == \"store.missing\" recover - 1 } expect bad == - 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#effects-and-contracts-lr-11","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","m":"agent","x":"Effects and contracts (LR 11) # Agent mode. Human mode: Effects and contracts . Source of truth: LR 11 . Preview: contracts ( LR 11.5 ) arrive in v0.1. Effects, claims, noalloc and capabilities ( LR 11.1 , 11.2 , 11.3 , 11.4 and 11.6 ) arrive in v0.2. The observation plane and aspects ( LR 11.7 , 11.8 ) arrive in v0.5, except the diagnostic plane that report and the log statements write to ( LR 11.7 , LR 13 ), which is in v0.0.2. main 's capability parameters ( LR 4.6 , 11.6 ) and the core aspect @ignorable ( LR 5.5 , 11.8 ) are in v0.0.2."},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#rules","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-11-01 The effect set is exactly alloc , io , mutate , nondet , suspend , block , fail , recovers ; summaries are computed transitively, closed under recursion by a least fixed point. LR 11.1 , 11.3 LANG-11-02 Writes to fresh storage, and writes through the function's own editable parameters (its write footprint), are not mutate ; writes through captures, guards or atomics are. LR 11.1 , 11.3 LANG-11-03 noalloc is checked against the allocation-pattern whitelist for the actual target and telemetry plan, covering failures, cleanup, closures and every async phase; a conflicting telemetry policy is rejected at the policy. LR 11.4 LANG-11-04 A trusted summary is labelled trusted , never proved ; trusted is rejected outside the fortis package. LR 11.3 LANG-11-05 async , try and recovers are written wherever the summary has the effect; across a module boundary only written pure / noalloc count; weakening an exported function's written modifiers is a reported API change. LR 11.2 , 11.3 LANG-11-06 A contract contains only modifiers, forbids <effect> , requires capability A and excludes capability A ; configuration never adds predicates. LR 11.5 LANG-11-07 io other than report and the log statements requires an Authority parameter, except in the entry main , which receives the capabilities as parameters (or, declaring none, creates them with one direct Host.capabilities() ), and in an adapter's minting function, which itself requires the capability it derives the authority from, so every authority traces back to main ; Host.capabilities() anywhere else, twice, or in a main that takes a capability parameter, is rejected. LR 11.6 LANG-11-08 Contracts are applied in the modifier position, before single-word modifiers, without arguments; @ applies aspects only. LR 11.5 LANG-11-09 Aspects have only the parts needs , before , after , on failure , metadata ; they elaborate before checking ( before in application order, after reversed, on failure innermost first) and the elaborated function passes every ordinary check. LR 11.8"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#modifier-claims","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"Modifier claims","m":"agent","x":"Modifier claims # Modifier Summary must not contain Must be written when summary has pure io , mutate , nondet , suspend , block (optional claim) noalloc alloc (optional claim) async \u2014 suspend try \u2014 fail recovers \u2014 recovers Source: LR 11.2 . Order in a head: [export] [Contract \u2026] [pure] [noalloc] [async] [try] [recovers] func ( LR 8.1 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#summary-transfer-rules-lr-113","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"Summary transfer rules (LR 11.3)","m":"agent","x":"Summary transfer rules (LR 11.3) # Call: imports the callee's summary at its actual type arguments and callback types. fail is imported only when the failure leaves the function: kept by or fail and fail f ; removed by or recover , catch \u2026 recover , and a consuming match , which add recovers unless the failure was reported first ( LR 10.4 ). Write footprint: per editable parameter (and editable self ). At a call each footprint parameter is instantiated with the argument place: fresh in the caller \u2192 nothing; reachable from a caller editable parameter \u2192 joins the caller's footprint; otherwise (capture, Mutex guard, atomic) \u2192 mutate . Fresh: a let mut initialised from a constructor, a literal or an owned call result. A binding holding a loan result is a path to the loan's owner. async call: builds a future, imports only argument evaluation; await (or launch into the awaiting scope) imports the future's summary; launchParallel / launchBlocking import the work's summary minus block . Callback parameter: contributes what its function type permits. A lambda literal with no pure -excluded effect has a pure type. cleanup effects join every function that may drop the type. report and log.info / log.warn / log.error are io but need no capability ( LR 11.6 , LR 13 ); a pure function cannot report or log. Instrumentation ( capture , record , completion records) does not change a summary ( LR 11.7 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#contract-elements-lr-115","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"Contract elements (LR 11.5)","m":"agent","x":"Contract elements (LR 11.5) # Element Effect on the target pure , noalloc , \u2026 the modifier's claim forbids recovers (any of the eight effects) that effect must be absent from the summary requires capability A the function takes a parameter of Authority type A excludes capability A the function neither takes A nor calls, transitively, a function that requires A Contracts compose by conjunction; contradictions are rejected; pure with requires capability is a contradiction because a pure function takes no capability parameter ( LR 11.5 ). The formatter keeps the name and prints the expansion after it: export FastCalculation pure noalloc func square ( LR 11.5 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#authorities-lr-116","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"Authorities (LR 11.6)","m":"agent","x":"Authorities (LR 11.6) # Capabilities (process-wide; created once, only for the entry main , which declares the ones it needs as parameters, func main(console: Console, files: FileSystem) , LR 4.6 ): FileSystem , Network , Clock , Random , Environment , Process , Supervisor , Console , SecretAccess , RawSql . Other functions take them as ordinary parameters. Older code calls Host.capabilities() once in a main with no capability parameter; that stays accepted, and fortis fmt rewrites it. Per-activation authorities ( RequestContext , Identity , Db , Tx , TaskContext , Mail ): minted by a kit's adapter from a capability, passed as a parameter. Shared[A] of an authority A satisfies the rule like A ; share one capability as let c = Shared.create(move out) , then c.retain() per owner (no field stores a loan, LR 7.7 ). Attenuation only narrows: fs.subtree(path) . Clock reads are nondet and need Clock ; a pure function takes time as a plain parameter."},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#aspect-parts-lr-118","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"Aspect parts (LR 11.8)","m":"agent","x":"Aspect parts (LR 11.8) # Part Constraint needs name: Type target has exactly one parameter of Type (two: error); hooks use it with the target's mode needs Contract target carries the contract before { \u2026 } may fail on a non- try target only if an on failure of this or an outer aspect maps every failure after(result: R) { \u2026 } read loan of the result; may fail only when R is not linear on failure(f: Failure) R { \u2026 } owns f ; must return an R or fail metadata arguments readable by libraries through the toolchain Arguments are compile-time constants: literals, record and variant-map literals, enum values, capture-free fn literals, declaration names. First parameter may be positional, the rest named. Specialisation: aspect a(params, ...rest) = b(fixed: v, ...rest) . No around , no body access. Core aspects ( LR 11.8 ): observe(schema) ( LR 13 ), and ignorable() , applied as @ignorable , which lets a call statement drop the function's non-linear result without discard ( LR 5.5 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#diagnostics","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-EFF-001 summary exceeds the modifiers or contract: an effect pure excludes, alloc under noalloc , suspend without async , fail without try , recovers without recovers , a forbidden effect, a missing required or a present excluded authority remove the effect; or, when the effect is intended, write the modifier its type needs ( async , try , recovers ). Dropping a written pure , noalloc or contract is an API change for whoever owns the claim, never a correction ( LR 11.3 ) LR 11.1 , 11.3 , 11.5 E-EFF-010 io reached through a capability without an Authority parameter ( report and log need none); Host.capabilities() outside main , called twice, or in a main that takes a capability parameter take the authority ( console: Console ) as a parameter; declare the capabilities main needs as its parameters and pass them down LR 11.6 E-EFF-020 block inside an async function (or anything it calls), such as lockBlocking await m.lock() , or move the blocking work into launchBlocking LR 11.1 E-EFF-030 the application's telemetry policy attaches an allocating record to a noalloc function change the policy at the reported site LR 11.4 E-EFF-040 (warning) --api-baseline OLD : an exported function lost a written pure / noalloc or gained async / try / recovers intended: bump the API; otherwise restore the modifier LR 11.3 E-TYP-150 unknown aspect, arguments not matching the declaration, unmet or ambiguous needs , ...rest outside an aspect's parameter list fix the application or the target's parameters LR 11.8 E-OBL-008 an after hook that may fail on a linear result type do not fail in after , or apply it to a non-linear result LR 11.8 E-OBL-001 elaborated path leaves a linear owned parameter unconsumed (a failing before ) consume it on that path, or take it by loan LR 11.8"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#correction-patterns","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#e-eff-001-pure-function-performing-io-lr-111","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"E-EFF-001: pure function performing I/O (LR 11.1)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-001: pure function performing I/O (LR 11.1) # Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 import fortis . host { Console } pure func greet ( console : Console ) { // reject: E-EFF-001 (a pure function takes no capability) console . print ( \"hello\" ) // reject: E-EFF-001 } func main ( console : Console ) { greet ( console ) } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func greet ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"hello\" ) } func main ( console : Console ) { greet ( console ) } output hello"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#e-eff-001-missing-recovers-lr-111-lr-112","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"E-EFF-001: missing recovers (LR 11.1, LR 11.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-001: missing recovers (LR 11.1, LR 11.2) # main inherits the effect from its callee and needs the modifier too. Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 export error Odd { code : \"demo.odd\" } try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 == 1 { fail Odd ( ) } return n / 2 } func halfOrZero ( n : Int ) Int => half ( n ) or recover 0 // reject: E-EFF-001 test \"half\" { expect halfOrZero ( 4 ) == 2 } Fortis // accept export error Odd { code : \"demo.odd\" } try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n % 2 == 1 { fail Odd ( ) } return n / 2 } recovers func halfOrZero ( n : Int ) Int => half ( n ) or recover 0 test \"half\" { expect halfOrZero ( 4 ) == 2 expect halfOrZero ( 3 ) == 0 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#e-eff-001-mutate-through-a-capture-in-a-pure-function-lr-111-lr-113","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"E-EFF-001: mutate through a capture in a pure function (LR 11.1, LR 11.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-001: mutate through a capture in a pure function (LR 11.1, LR 11.3) # Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 class Cell { export value : Int pure func set ( editable self , value : Int ) { self . value = value } } pure func viaClosure ( ) Int { let mut cell = Cell ( value : 0 ) let mut poke = fn mut [ editable cell ] ( ) Void { cell . set ( 2 ) } poke ( ) // reject: E-EFF-001 return 0 } test \"closure\" { expect viaClosure ( ) == 0 } Fortis // accept class Cell { export value : Int pure func set ( editable self , value : Int ) { self . value = value } } pure func direct ( ) Int { let mut cell = Cell ( value : 0 ) cell . set ( 2 ) return cell . value } test \"direct\" { expect direct ( ) == 2 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#e-eff-001-allocation-under-noalloc-lr-111-lr-114","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"E-EFF-001: allocation under noalloc (LR 11.1, LR 11.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-001: allocation under noalloc (LR 11.1, LR 11.4) # Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 noalloc func pair ( a : Int , b : Int ) List [ Int ] => List . of ( a , b ) // reject: E-EFF-001 test \"pair\" { expect pair ( 1 , 2 ) . count ( ) == 2 } Fortis // accept pure noalloc func larger ( a : Int , b : Int ) Int { if a > b { return a } return b } test \"larger\" { expect larger ( 1 , 2 ) == 2 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#e-eff-001-contract-violation-lr-111-lr-115","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"E-EFF-001: contract violation (LR 11.1, LR 11.5)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-001: contract violation (LR 11.1, LR 11.5) # Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 import fortis . host { Network } contract Offline { excludes capability Network } func lookup ( net : Network , name : StringView ) Int => name . scalarCount ( ) Offline func viaNetwork ( net : Network ) Int => lookup ( net , \"example\" ) // reject: E-EFF-001 test \"offline\" { expect true } Fortis // accept contract Offline { excludes capability Network } Offline pure func measure ( name : StringView ) Int => name . scalarCount ( ) test \"offline\" { expect measure ( \"example\" ) == 7 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#e-eff-010-hostcapabilities-outside-main-lr-116","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"E-EFF-010: Host.capabilities() outside main (LR 11.6)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-010: Host.capabilities() outside main (LR 11.6) # Only the entry main receives capabilities; it declares them as parameters and lends them down. Fortis // reject: E-EFF-010 import fortis . host { Host } func sneak ( ) { let caps = Host . capabilities ( ) // reject: E-EFF-010 } func main ( ) { sneak ( ) } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func work ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"work\" ) } func main ( console : Console ) { work ( console ) } output work"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#e-eff-010-io-without-an-authority-parameter-lr-116","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"E-EFF-010: I/O without an Authority parameter (LR 11.6)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-010: I/O without an Authority parameter (LR 11.6) # Reported at the I/O statement. Fortis // reject: E-EFF-010 import fortis . host { Capabilities , Host } func write ( caps : Capabilities , text : StringView ) { caps . console . print ( text ) // reject: E-EFF-010 } func main ( ) { let caps = Host . capabilities ( ) write ( caps , \"hello\" ) } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func log ( console : Console , text : StringView ) { console . print ( text ) } func main ( console : Console ) { log ( console , \"hello\" ) } output hello"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#e-eff-020-blocking-inside-async-lr-111","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"E-EFF-020: blocking inside async (LR 11.1)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-020: blocking inside async (LR 11.1) # The block effect is reported in every async function that reaches it, here wait and its caller main . Fortis // reject: E-EFF-020 async try func wait ( m : Mutex [ Int ] ) Int { let g = m . lockBlocking ( ) or fail // reject: E-EFF-020 return 0 } async try func main ( ) { let m = Mutex . create ( 1 ) let v = await wait ( m ) or fail // reject: E-EFF-020 } Fortis // accept async try func wait ( m : Mutex [ Int ] ) Int { { let g = await m . lock ( ) or fail } return 0 } async try func main ( ) { let m = Mutex . create ( 1 ) let v = await wait ( m ) or fail }"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#e-typ-150-unmet-needs-lr-118","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"E-TYP-150: unmet needs (LR 11.8)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-150: unmet needs (LR 11.8) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-150 import fortis . host { Console } export aspect logged ( label : StringView ) { needs console : Console before { console . print ( \"before ${label}\" ) } } @ logged ( \"count\" ) // reject: E-TYP-150 func count ( n : Int ) Int => n func main ( ) { discard count ( 3 ) } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export aspect logged ( label : StringView ) { needs console : Console before { console . print ( \"before ${label}\" ) } } @ logged ( \"count\" ) func count ( console : Console , n : Int ) Int => n func main ( console : Console ) { discard count ( console , 3 ) } output before count"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#e-obl-008-failing-after-on-a-linear-result-lr-118","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"E-OBL-008: failing after on a linear result (LR 11.8)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBL-008: failing after on a linear result (LR 11.8) # Fortis // reject: E-OBL-008 export error Stop { code : \"demo.stop\" } export aspect strict [ R ] ( ) { after ( result : R ) { fail Stop ( ) // reject: E-OBL-008 } } @ strict ( ) try func produce ( n : Int ) Outcome [ Int ] { return Outcome . success ( n ) } func main ( ) { } Fortis // accept export error Stop { code : \"demo.stop\" } export aspect positive ( ) { after ( result : Int ) { if result < 0 { fail Stop ( ) } } } @ positive ( ) try func produce ( n : Int ) Int { return n } test \"produce\" { expect produce ( 3 ) == 3 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/11-effects.html#facts-used-when-writing-code","p":"Effects and contracts (LR 11)","h":"Facts used when writing code","m":"agent","x":"Facts used when writing code # Elaboration order for @a @b func f : a.before , b.before , body, b.after , a.after ; a failure goes to b 's on failure first, then a 's; a handler that returns completes the function with its value, which is a success exit for observation ( LR 11.8 ). The hooks' effects join the summary; a hook that allocates under noalloc , reports under pure or calls Host.capabilities() is rejected by the ordinary rules ( LR 11.8 ). pure and noalloc are independent ( LR 11.2 ). There is no nostate ; no-local-mutation is an opt-in lint ( LR 11.2 ). noalloc on an async function covers construction, every poll, completion and destruction; the VM's own allocations do not count against the native claim ( LR 11.4 ). Target feature sets ( core , alloc , host , async , web , browser ) are not effects and not capabilities ( LR 11.6 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#cleanup-async-and-concurrency-lr-12","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","m":"agent","x":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12) # Agent mode. Human mode: Cleanup, async and concurrency . Source of truth: LR 12 . Preview: async, cancellation, scopes and sharing ( LR 12.3 , 12.4 , 12.5 , 12.6 and 12.7 ) arrive in v0.3. defer and destruction order ( LR 12.1 , 12.2 ) are in v0.0.1."},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#rules","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-12-01 A defer body is checked as if written at every exit of its block (after the block's own loans end, before its locals drop); it cannot alter the outcome; the places it names are initialised at every exit. LR 12.1 LANG-12-02 Failures produced inside a defer are consumed there ( or recover , catch \u2026 recover , report ) and never replace the primary outcome; cleanup reports are labelled as such. LR 12.1 LANG-12-03 Future and Task are linear; a pending future is never destroyed; cancellation is observed only at cancellation points. LR 12.3 , 12.4 LANG-12-04 A scope joins or cancels every task it launched before its block exits. LR 12.5 LANG-12-05 Only Shareable environments without loans cross launchParallel / launchBlocking ; loans cross only cooperative launch within the owner's block. LR 12.5 , 12.7 LANG-12-06 A MutexGuard is never live across await . LR 12.3 , 12.7"},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#exit-and-destruction-order-lr-122-lr-51-lr-79","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"Exit and destruction order (LR 12.2, LR 5.1, LR 7.9)","m":"agent","x":"Exit and destruction order (LR 12.2, LR 5.1, LR 7.9) # At every block exit, including cancellation and or fail : the block's loans end; tasks launched on a scope declared in the block are cancelled and joined (their carried loans end) ( LR 12.4 , LR 12.5 ); deferred blocks run, last registered first ( LR 12.1 ); during an unwind (cancellation or fail ), abandon runs on every live Abandonable local, reverse initialisation order ( LR 12.4 ); owned locals drop, reverse initialisation order; a class value runs its cleanup , then drops its fields in declaration order ( LR 7.9 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#defer-constraints-lr-121","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"defer constraints (LR 12.1)","m":"agent","x":"defer constraints (LR 12.1) # Block form only; no return , fail , give , break , continue , await inside ( E-DEF-001 ); defer await is not in v1. Places it names must be initialised at every exit: no move of them between the defer and an exit ( E-DEF-002 ). It may not use a binding of its own block that holds a loan ( E-OWN-052 ); loans of enclosing blocks still apply. Its effects join the function's summary; a silent recovery inside it adds recovers ."},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#async-model-lr-123-lr-124","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"Async model (LR 12.3, LR 12.4)","m":"agent","x":"Async model (LR 12.3, LR 12.4) # async func f(\u2026) T call: evaluates arguments, returns Future[T] ( Future[Outcome[T]] with try ); the body starts at the first await or at launch . await e : only directly in an async body (not in a non-async lambda, not in defer ). Future[T] is linear: await , launch, or discard . discard only for a never-polled future whose environment holds no linear value and no exclusive loan; it emits a not_started record. A future holds its arguments' loans ( LR 7.3 ). Cancellation points: await (cooperative); await and checkpoint() (parallel and blocking pools). Cancellation is not a failure: not catchable, recoverable or matchable. No linear value live across a cancellation point unless it implements Abandonable ( func abandon(owned self) : synchronous, non-failing, non-suspending). An Abandonable local may also be live at or fail (the unwind abandons it); a normal exit must still consume it. Task implements it. The futures and tasks being awaited are not \"live across\" their own await ."},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#launch-forms-lr-125","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"Launch forms (LR 12.5)","m":"agent","x":"Launch forms (LR 12.5) # Form Executor Environment s.launch(f) \u2192 Task[T] same cooperative executor and thread may borrow any binding declared in or before the scope s.launchParallel(f) \u2192 Task[T] CPU worker pool Shareable , no loans; cancellation at await and checkpoint() s.launchBlocking(work) \u2192 Task[T] bounded blocking pool work: fn once() T , Shareable environment; the only place block is allowed in async code; cancellation at checkpoint() s.detach(move task, supervisor) \u2014 moves the join obligation to a Supervisor ; removes the task from the scope join(a, b) \u2192 (A, B) polled inside the parent consumes both futures; loans allowed; never returns early; failures stay in the tuple A Task is awaited inside its scope or detached. A scope exiting by fail , return , break or cancellation cancels and joins its running tasks before its deferred blocks run; results never awaited are consumed by the scope (a failure inside one is reported, labelled as abandoned by the scope) ( LR 12.5 ). TaskCompletion[T] = Completed(value: T) | Cancelled(reason: Cancellation) | Defect(info: TaskDefect) ; observed only by the supervisor of a detached task; linear when T is; consumed with match ( LR 12.6 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#shareable-lr-127","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"Shareable (LR 12.7)","m":"agent","x":"Shareable (LR 12.7) # Shareable: trivial types; String , Bytes , BigInt ; List , Array , Map , OrderedMap , Set , FixedList , FixedMap of shareables; classes, enums, tuples and records whose fields are all shareable; Shared[T] , Weak[T] , Mutex[T] with T: Shareable ; AtomicInt , AtomicBool ; fn / fn once closures capturing only copy / move of shareables; capability values. Not shareable: loans and views, MutexGuard , fn mut closures, Future , Task , thread-bound resources. List.of(\"a\", \"b\") is a List[StringView] (views of static text), which is not Shareable ; annotate let words: List[String] = \u2026 to make owned strings ( LR 3.5 , LR 12.7 ). Mutation between tasks: Mutex[T] . Async: await m.lock() ; sync: m.lockBlocking() ( block ). Both return Outcome[MutexGuard[T]] from mutex ; the guard is an exclusive loan of T , not Shareable , unlocks when destroyed, and cannot cross await . Atomics: AtomicInt , AtomicBool ( LR 12.7 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#diagnostics","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-DEF-001 return , fail , give , break , continue , await (including or fail ) inside defer consume failures with catch \u2026 recover , or recover or report ; move control flow out of the defer LR 12.1 E-DEF-002 a place the defer names is moved out before an exit keep it owned until every exit (lend it instead of moving it), or do the deferred work before the move and drop the defer ; registering the defer later does not help, since the place is still moved at the exit LR 12.1 E-OWN-052 a defer uses a loan-holding binding of its own block use the owner (or a parameter), or copy the value into a non-loan binding LR 12.1 E-OBL-001 a future, task or outcome dropped at block exit; an outcome unconsumed inside defer await it, launch it, or consume it LR 6.12 , 12.1 , 12.3 E-OBL-005 a linear value live across await whose type is not Abandonable consume it before the await ( let v = e or fail ), or pass it into the awaited call LR 12.4 E-ASY-001 a Task leaves its scope block ( return , give , assignment to an outer binding) await it inside the scope, or detach it to a Supervisor LR 12.3 E-ASY-002 a MutexGuard live across await end the guard's block before the await LR 12.3 E-ASY-003 a pool launch whose future arguments or closure captures include a loan or a non- Shareable value pass owned shareable data ( copy / move ); use cooperative launch for borrowed data LR 12.7 E-EFF-020 block inside an async function or anything it calls await m.lock() , or launchBlocking LR 11.1"},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#correction-patterns","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#e-def-001-or-fail-inside-defer-lr-121","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"E-DEF-001: or fail inside defer (LR 12.1)","m":"agent","x":"E-DEF-001: or fail inside defer (LR 12.1) # Fortis // reject: E-DEF-001 export error FlushFailed { code : \"demo.flush\" } try func flush ( ) { fail FlushFailed ( ) } try func work ( n : Int ) Int { defer { flush ( ) or fail // reject: E-DEF-001 } return n } test \"work\" { expect work ( 1 ) == 1 } Fortis // accept export error FlushFailed { code : \"demo.flush\" } try func flush ( ) { fail FlushFailed ( ) } func work ( n : Int ) Int { defer { flush ( ) catch e { report e recover Void } } return n } test \"work\" { expect work ( 1 ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#e-def-002-a-deferred-place-moved-away-lr-121","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"E-DEF-002: a deferred place moved away (LR 12.1)","m":"agent","x":"E-DEF-002: a deferred place moved away (LR 12.1) # Fortis // reject: E-DEF-002 export class Handle { export open : Bool export func close ( editable self ) { self . open = false } } func keep ( h : owned Handle ) { } func run ( ) { let mut input = Handle ( open : true ) defer { input . close ( ) } keep ( move input ) // reject: E-DEF-002 } test \"run\" { run ( ) expect true } Fortis // accept export class Handle { export open : Bool export func close ( editable self ) { self . open = false } } func keep ( h : owned Handle ) { } func run ( ) { let mut input = Handle ( open : true ) input . close ( ) keep ( move input ) } test \"run\" { run ( ) expect true }"},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#e-own-052-a-loan-binding-used-in-defer-lr-121","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"E-OWN-052: a loan binding used in defer (LR 12.1)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-052: a loan binding used in defer (LR 12.1) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-052 export class Buffer { export used : Int export func peek ( self ) alias Buffer from self => alias self } func run ( items : Buffer ) Int { let seen = items . peek ( ) defer { discard seen . used } // reject: E-OWN-052 return seen . used } test \"run\" { expect run ( Buffer ( used : 1 ) ) == 1 } Fortis // accept export class Buffer { export used : Int export func peek ( self ) alias Buffer from self => alias self } func run ( items : Buffer ) Int { defer { discard items . used } let seen = items . peek ( ) return seen . used } test \"run\" { expect run ( Buffer ( used : 1 ) ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#e-obl-001-a-future-dropped-lr-612-lr-123","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"E-OBL-001: a future dropped (LR 6.12, LR 12.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBL-001: a future dropped (LR 6.12, LR 12.3) # Fortis // reject: E-OBL-001 async func work ( n : Int ) Int => n async func run ( n : Int ) Int { let pending = work ( n ) // reject: E-OBL-001 return n } async func main ( ) { discard await run ( 1 ) } Fortis // accept async func work ( n : Int ) Int => n async func run ( n : Int ) Int { let pending = work ( n ) return await pending } async func main ( ) { discard await run ( 1 ) }"},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#e-obl-005-a-linear-value-across-await-lr-124","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"E-OBL-005: a linear value across await (LR 12.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBL-005: a linear value across await (LR 12.4) # Fortis // reject: E-OBL-005 export error Negative { code : \"demo.negative\" } try func parse ( n : Int ) Int { if n < 0 { fail Negative ( ) } return n } async func pause ( ) { } async try func run ( n : Int ) Int { let pending = parse ( n ) await pause ( ) // reject: E-OBL-005 return pending or fail } async try func main ( ) { discard await run ( 3 ) or fail } Fortis // accept export error Negative { code : \"demo.negative\" } try func parse ( n : Int ) Int { if n < 0 { fail Negative ( ) } return n } async func pause ( ) { } async try func run ( n : Int ) Int { let value = parse ( n ) or fail await pause ( ) return value } async try func main ( ) { discard await run ( 3 ) or fail }"},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#e-asy-001-a-task-escaping-its-scope-lr-123","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"E-ASY-001: a task escaping its scope (LR 12.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-ASY-001: a task escaping its scope (LR 12.3) # Fortis // reject: E-ASY-001 async func work ( n : Int ) Int => n async func run ( n : Int ) Task [ Int ] { scope tasks { let t = tasks . launch ( work ( n ) ) return move t // reject: E-ASY-001 } } async func main ( ) { } Fortis // accept async func work ( n : Int ) Int => n async func run ( n : Int ) Int { scope tasks { let t = tasks . launch ( work ( n ) ) return await t } } async func main ( ) { discard await run ( 2 ) }"},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#e-asy-002-a-guard-across-await-lr-123","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"E-ASY-002: a guard across await (LR 12.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-ASY-002: a guard across await (LR 12.3) # Fortis // reject: E-ASY-002 async func pause ( ) { } async try func run ( m : Mutex [ Int ] ) Int { let g = await m . lock ( ) or fail await pause ( ) // reject: E-ASY-002 return 0 } async try func main ( ) { let m = Mutex . create ( 1 ) discard await run ( m ) or fail } Fortis // accept async func pause ( ) { } async try func run ( m : Mutex [ Int ] ) Int { { let g = await m . lock ( ) or fail } await pause ( ) return 0 } async try func main ( ) { let m = Mutex . create ( 1 ) discard await run ( m ) or fail }"},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#e-asy-003-a-non-shareable-capture-crossing-a-pool-launch-lr-127","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"E-ASY-003: a non-Shareable capture crossing a pool launch (LR 12.7)","m":"agent","x":"E-ASY-003: a non- Shareable capture crossing a pool launch (LR 12.7) # Fortis // reject: E-ASY-003 async func pools ( ) Int { let words = List . of ( \"a\" , \"b\" , \"c\" ) scope tasks { let blocking = tasks . launchBlocking ( fn once [ move words ] ( ) Int => words . count ( ) ) // reject: E-ASY-003 return await blocking } } async func main ( ) { discard await pools ( ) } Fortis // accept async func pools ( ) Int { let words : List [ String ] = List . of ( \"a\" , \"b\" , \"c\" ) scope tasks { let blocking = tasks . launchBlocking ( fn once [ move words ] ( ) Int => words . count ( ) ) return await blocking } } async func main ( ) { discard await pools ( ) }"},{"u":"docs/agent/12-async.html#facts-used-when-writing-code","p":"Cleanup, async and concurrency (LR 12)","h":"Facts used when writing code","m":"agent","x":"Facts used when writing code # main may be async func main or async try func main ; it runs as the root task on the core cooperative executor ( LR 4.6 ). await first or fail leaving a scope cancels the other tasks of that scope; a task cancelled before its first poll never runs ( LR 12.5 ). The v1 executor runs every cooperative task of one scope tree on one thread; launch never needs Shareable ( LR 12.5 ). Shared[T] gives read access only; mutation goes through Mutex[T] or an atomic ( LR 7.8 , LR 12.7 ). No cross-backend agreement is claimed for interleaving timing; write output after await s in a fixed order ( LR 12.7 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#observation-schemas-and-statements-lr-13","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","m":"agent","x":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13) # Agent mode. Human mode: Observation . Source of truth: LR 13 . Preview: observation schemas, capture and record ( LR 13 ) arrive in v0.5. Log statements, log.info , log.warn and log.error ( LR 13 ), are in v0.0.2."},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#rules","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-13-01 capture field = e snapshots e at the statement, copying it into the activation's frame; e is pure, infallible, non-suspending and of the field's declared type; it takes a read loan for the statement only. record Name(field: v, \u2026) has the same requirement for its arguments. LR 13 LANG-13-02 Each required: all field is assigned on every path before every exit; required: success / failure before every exit of that class; at most one capture per field per path; no capture in a loop body. Exits include fail , return , await (a cancellation edge) and cancellation. LR 13 LANG-13-03 Frame contents are bounded by the schema: a String field declares a bound; a longer value is truncated or omitted as the schema declares, and the frame records which. LR 13 LANG-13-04 log.info(m) , log.warn(m) and log.error(m) write one log record (level, message, function) to the diagnostic plane, standard error, never standard output; m is a StringView ; no capability is needed, and the statement has the io effect, so a pure function cannot log. log is not a keyword: where a binding or declaration named log is in scope, log.info(...) is an ordinary call. Record a failure with report e , not a log; log what is not a failure (X-345, X-147). LR 13"},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#declarations-and-statements","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"Declarations and statements","m":"agent","x":"Declarations and statements # Form Meaning LR observation Name { \u2026 } with one item per line: version: n (optional), each field: T required: all|success|failure|none , and encoding: bounded(n) schema; version defaults to 1; every field type Encodable (recursively, as derivation) LR 13 , LR 6.10 @observe(Name) above a function head the core aspect attaching the schema; the frame's fields are the schema's LR 13 , LR 11.8 capture field = e assign the frame field once LR 13 record Name(field: v, \u2026) emit one event per execution; allowed in loops LR 13 start, end, duration, outcome class generated by the compiler; never written in source LR 13 cancelled before start a frame marked not_started , no captured fields LR 13 log.info(m) , log.warn(m) , log.error(m) one log record ( level , message , function ) per execution, to standard error: text on a terminal ( warn: could not take 9 nuts ), a JSON line otherwise; never standard output LR 13 , LR 11.7 Effects: observation does not change a function's effect summary; records go to the diagnostic plane, which feeds nothing back ( LR 11.7 ). A pure function may be observed. A log statement is the exception: it has the io effect, like report , so a pure function cannot log; it needs no capability ( LR 13 , LR 11.6 ). Output: the default task reporter writes frames without field values to standard error; field values leave only through the Telemetry kit, or on the record stream under the development plan FORTIS_TELEMETRY=permit-all ( LR 15 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#diagnostics","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-OBS-001 a required field unassigned on an applicable exit ( return , fail , an await , the end) capture the field before that exit; for an await , before the await LR 13 E-OBS-010 a second capture of one field on one path keep one capture ; compute the value first LR 13 E-OBS-011 capture inside a loop body capture once after the loop, or use record for per-iteration events LR 13 E-OBS-012 capture / record whose expression has an effect; a field the schema lacks; capture with no schema attached bind the fallible or suspending value on its own line and capture the binding; fix the field name; attach @observe(Schema) LR 13 E-TYP-090 a schema field type that is not Encodable ( Secret[T] , an authority, a loan, a closure, \u2026) capture an Encodable projection, never the secret or resource LR 13 , LR 6.10"},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#correction-patterns","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#e-obs-001-required-field-missing-on-a-failure-exit-lr-13","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"E-OBS-001: required field missing on a failure exit (LR 13)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBS-001: required field missing on a failure exit (LR 13) # Fortis // reject: E-OBS-001 export error Rejected { code : \"shop.rejected\" } observation Payment { amount : Int required : all reason : Int required : failure } @ observe ( Payment ) try func pay ( amount : Int < 0 .. 10000 > ) Int { capture amount = amount if amount == 0 { fail Rejected ( ) // reject: E-OBS-001 } return amount } test \"pay\" { expect pay ( 5 ) == 5 } Fortis // accept export error Rejected { code : \"shop.rejected\" } observation Payment { amount : Int required : all reason : Int required : failure } @ observe ( Payment ) try func pay ( amount : Int < 0 .. 10000 > ) Int { capture amount = amount if amount == 0 { capture reason = 1 fail Rejected ( ) } return amount } test \"pay\" { expect pay ( 5 ) == 5 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#e-obs-001-required-field-after-an-await-lr-13-lr-124","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"E-OBS-001: required field after an await (LR 13, LR 12.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBS-001: required field after an await (LR 13, LR 12.4) # Fortis // reject: E-OBS-001 async func pause ( ) { } observation Wait { tier : Int required : all } @ observe ( Wait ) async func slow ( tier : Int ) Int { await pause ( ) // reject: E-OBS-001 capture tier = tier return tier } test \"slow\" { expect true } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Clock , Console } observation Wait { tier : Int required : all } @ observe ( Wait ) async func slow ( clock : Clock , tier : Int ) Int { capture tier = tier await clock . sleep ( 5ms ) return tier } async func main ( clock : Clock , console : Console ) { let t = await slow ( clock , 2 ) console . print ( \"tier ${t}\" ) } output tier 2"},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#e-obs-010-captured-twice-lr-13","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"E-OBS-010: captured twice (LR 13)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBS-010: captured twice (LR 13) # Fortis // reject: E-OBS-010 observation Trace { tier : Int required : all } @ observe ( Trace ) pure func process ( n : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int { capture tier = n capture tier = n + 1 // reject: E-OBS-010 return n } test \"process\" { expect process ( 1 ) == 1 } Fortis // accept observation Trace { tier : Int required : all } @ observe ( Trace ) pure func process ( n : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int { let tier = n + 1 capture tier = tier return n } test \"process\" { expect process ( 1 ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#e-obs-011-capture-in-a-loop-lr-13","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"E-OBS-011: capture in a loop (LR 13)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBS-011: capture in a loop (LR 13) # Fortis // reject: E-OBS-011 observation Batch { last : Int required : none } @ observe ( Batch ) pure func sum ( items : List [ Int < 0 .. 100 > ] ) Int { let mut total = 0 for item in items { capture last = item // reject: E-OBS-011 total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , item ) } return total } test \"sum\" { expect sum ( List . of ( 1 , 2 ) ) == 3 } Fortis // accept observation Batch { count : Int required : all } observation Item { value : Int required : all } @ observe ( Batch ) pure func sum ( items : List [ Int < 0 .. 100 > ] ) Int { capture count = items . count ( ) let mut total = 0 for item in items { record Item ( value : item ) total = Int . saturatingAdd ( total , item ) } return total } test \"sum\" { expect sum ( List . of ( 1 , 2 ) ) == 3 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#e-obs-012-an-effectful-capture-expression-lr-13","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"E-OBS-012: an effectful capture expression (LR 13)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBS-012: an effectful capture expression (LR 13) # Fortis // reject: E-OBS-012 export error Odd { code : \"demo.odd\" } pure try func half ( n : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int { if n % 2 == 1 { fail Odd ( ) } return n / 2 } observation Trace { half : Int required : none } @ observe ( Trace ) pure try func process ( n : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int { capture half = half ( n ) or fail // reject: E-OBS-012 return n } test \"process\" { expect process ( 4 ) == 4 } Fortis // accept export error Odd { code : \"demo.odd\" } pure try func half ( n : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int { if n % 2 == 1 { fail Odd ( ) } return n / 2 } observation Trace { half : Int required : none } @ observe ( Trace ) pure try func process ( n : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int { let h = half ( n ) or fail capture half = h return n } test \"process\" { expect process ( 4 ) == 4 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#e-typ-090-a-field-that-is-not-encodable-lr-13-lr-610","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"E-TYP-090: a field that is not Encodable (LR 13, LR 6.10)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-090: a field that is not Encodable (LR 13, LR 6.10) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-090 import fortis . host { Secret } observation Login { // reject: E-TYP-090 password : Secret [ String ] required : all } test \"nothing\" { expect true } Fortis // accept observation Login { user : String < scalars : 0 .. 64 > required : all } @ observe ( Login ) pure func login ( user : String ) Int { capture user = user return 1 } test \"login\" { expect login ( \"ada\" ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/13-observation.html#facts-used-when-writing-code","p":"Observation schemas and statements (LR 13)","h":"Facts used when writing code","m":"agent","x":"Facts used when writing code # A String field must be bounded: String<scalars: 0..n> ( LR 13 ). capture takes a read loan for its statement and copies; later mutation does not change the frame ( LR 13 ). required: success fields need not be assigned on failure exits, and the reverse ( LR 13 ). A mapped failure (an aspect's on failure that returns a value) is a success exit of the outer function; the frame is shared by hooks and body ( LR 11.8 ). Optimisation preserves required records (trace equivalence); non-required instrumentation may be elided ( LR 11.7 ). Logs ( LR 13 ) are for what is not a failure: progress, a summary, a fallback taken. A failure is recorded with report e alone, never logged as well. The program's own output stays console.print . m is a StringView : a literal, an interpolated literal or a String . log is not a keyword: where a binding or declaration named log is in scope, log.info(\u2026) is an ordinary call; log used any other way with nothing declared is E-NAM-001 ( LR 13 ). Open defects: no backend supplies the duration (D-28); native writes no not_started frame (D-27)."},{"u":"docs/agent/14-tests.html#test-declarations-lr-14","p":"Test declarations (LR 14)","h":"Test declarations (LR 14)","m":"agent","x":"Test declarations (LR 14) # Agent mode. Human mode: Tests . Source of truth: LR 14 . Preview: property tests and shrinking ( LR 14 ) arrive in v0.2. Example tests and cases tables are in v0.0.1."},{"u":"docs/agent/14-tests.html#rules","p":"Test declarations (LR 14)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-14-01 A table test binds its rows explicitly: test \"name\" cases { \"row\": { f: v, \u2026 } \u2026 } as row { \u2026 } ; no implicit names enter scope. LR 14 LANG-14-02 expect e takes a Bool , records a typed actual/expected pair, and fails the test when false. LR 14 LANG-14-03 In test and property bodies or fail is implied: a call of a fallible function that is not the left operand of or or catch or a match scrutinee has its success type, and its failure fails the test with the failure's record. let o = f() keeps the outcome when the body later consumes o with or , catch or match , or annotates it Outcome[\u2026] . Integer arithmetic the checker cannot prove safe is a checked region with the same implied or fail ( LR 6.4 ): write expect a * b == c . A lambda in the body keeps its own rules; outside tests nothing changes (X-345). LR 14 Further statements of LR 14 : Tests are core declarations; no kit is needed to test a package ( LR 14 ). A test body is an implicit try body: or fail is implied after a fallible call and may still be written; an escaping failure fails the test ( LR 14 ). Write expect f() == v , not expect (f() or fail) == v . A row's record type is inferred from the body's uses; every row literal is then checked against it at compile time, exactly as the same literal in that position ( LR 14 ). Expecting a failure consumes its obligation inside the test ( LR 14 ). property \"name\" forall (x: T, \u2026) { \u2026 } draws inputs from the parameter types, refinements included ( LR 14 ). Test declarations are compiled only in test builds and may import test-only modules ( LR 14 ). fuzz and bench are reserved words ( LR A ); fuzzing is --fuzz over every property (X-131)."},{"u":"docs/agent/14-tests.html#commands","p":"Test declarations (LR 14)","h":"Commands","m":"agent","x":"Commands # Command Backend Options fortis test FILE|DIR interpreter --format json fortisc test FILE|DIR VM --test NAME , --case NAME , --seed S , --cases N , --fuzz [--budget N] [--corpus DIR] , --replay FILE , --format json fortisc native FILE --tests native builds and runs the test executable Records (JSON lines with --format json or on a pipe): test-result ( test , case , status , evidence ), property-failure ( seed , original , minimal , shrinks , replay ), and a final test-summary ( passed , failed ) (X-131). Both runners write the results on standard error and the summary on standard output. Exit status 1 when any test fails."},{"u":"docs/agent/14-tests.html#diagnostics","p":"Test declarations (LR 14)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-TYP-010 expect of a non- Bool compare: expect f() == v LR 14 , LR 5.2 E-SYN-000 a cases table without as name add as row and use row.field LR 14 , LR 3.2 E-REF-001 a row literal outside the refinement of the position its field reaches fix the row, or widen the parameter's type LR 14 , LR 3.5 E-OBL-001 a fallible call left unconsumed outside a test body, or in a lambda written in one (its body is its own function); in the body itself or fail is implied or fail , or, to expect the failure, a catch e block that inspects e with expect and ends recover v (one statement per line) LR 14 , LR 10.1"},{"u":"docs/agent/14-tests.html#correction-patterns","p":"Test declarations (LR 14)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/14-tests.html#e-typ-010-expect-of-a-non-bool-lr-14-lr-52","p":"Test declarations (LR 14)","h":"E-TYP-010: expect of a non-Bool (LR 14, LR 5.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-TYP-010: expect of a non- Bool (LR 14, LR 5.2) # Fortis // reject: E-TYP-010 pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * 2 test \"doubling\" { expect double ( 2 ) // reject: E-TYP-010 } Fortis // accept pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * 2 test \"doubling\" { expect double ( 2 ) == 4 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/14-tests.html#e-syn-000-rows-not-bound-with-as-lr-14-lr-32","p":"Test declarations (LR 14)","h":"E-SYN-000: rows not bound with as (LR 14, LR 3.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-SYN-000: rows not bound with as (LR 14, LR 3.2) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-000 pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * 2 test \"doubling\" cases { \"one\" : { n : 1 , expected : 2 } } { // reject: E-SYN-000 expect double ( n ) == expected } Fortis // accept pure func double ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * 2 test \"doubling\" cases { \"one\" : { n : 1 , expected : 2 } \"ten\" : { n : 10 , expected : 20 } } as row { expect double ( row . n ) == row . expected }"},{"u":"docs/agent/14-tests.html#e-ref-001-a-row-outside-the-parameters-refinement-lr-14-lr-35","p":"Test declarations (LR 14)","h":"E-REF-001: a row outside the parameter's refinement (LR 14, LR 3.5)","m":"agent","x":"E-REF-001: a row outside the parameter's refinement (LR 14, LR 3.5) # Fortis // reject: E-REF-001 pure func percent ( part : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int => part test \"percent\" cases { \"low\" : { part : 0 , expected : 0 } \"too high\" : { part : 150 , expected : 150 } // reject: E-REF-001 } as row { expect percent ( row . part ) == row . expected } Fortis // accept pure func percent ( part : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int => part test \"percent\" cases { \"low\" : { part : 0 , expected : 0 } \"high\" : { part : 100 , expected : 100 } } as row { expect percent ( row . part ) == row . expected }"},{"u":"docs/agent/14-tests.html#e-obl-001-an-outcome-dropped-outside-a-test-lr-101-lr-14","p":"Test declarations (LR 14)","h":"E-OBL-001: an outcome dropped outside a test (LR 10.1, LR 14)","m":"agent","x":"E-OBL-001: an outcome dropped outside a test (LR 10.1, LR 14) # In a test or property body or fail is implied after a call of a fallible function ( LANG-14-03 , X-345). Outside tests it is not: an outcome nothing consumes is dropped. Fortis // reject: E-OBL-001 export error Empty { code : \"cart.empty\" } pure try func average ( total : Int < 0 .. 100000 > , count : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int { if count == 0 { fail Empty ( ) } return total / count } func touch ( ) { average ( 10 , 0 ) // reject: E-OBL-001 } Fortis // accept export error Empty { code : \"cart.empty\" } pure try func average ( total : Int < 0 .. 100000 > , count : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int { if count == 0 { fail Empty ( ) } return total / count } test \"empty\" { let r = average ( 10 , 0 ) catch e { expect e . code == \"cart.empty\" recover - 1 } expect r == - 1 expect average ( 10 , 5 ) == 2 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/14-tests.html#patterns","p":"Test declarations (LR 14)","h":"Patterns","m":"agent","x":"Patterns # Property over a refined domain ( LR 14 ): Fortis // accept pure func clampTo ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > , high : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int { if n > high { return high } return n } property \"clamp never exceeds the bound\" forall ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > , high : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) { expect clampTo ( n , high ) <= high } Replay a failing property: run the replay command of its property-failure record ( --test , --seed , --case ) with fortisc test (X-131). Never change an expected value, remove an expect or weaken a property to make a test pass."},{"u":"docs/agent/15-runtime.html#core-runtime-inventory-lr-15","p":"Core runtime inventory (LR 15)","h":"Core runtime inventory (LR 15)","m":"agent","x":"Core runtime inventory (LR 15) # Agent mode. Human mode: The core runtime . Source of truth: LR 15 . Preview: the core runtime inventory ( LR 15 ) arrives in v0.4. LR 15 has no rule table of its own; its components are bound by rules elsewhere: LANG-04-06 ( LR 4.6 ), LANG-11-07 ( LR 11.6 ), LANG-12-04 ( LR 12.5 ), LANG-13-01 ( LR 13 ). A conforming implementation ships these so that a program importing no kit runs; kits add adapters and policy and never replace them ( LR 15 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/15-runtime.html#components","p":"Core runtime inventory (LR 15)","h":"Components","m":"agent","x":"Components # Component Core provides Kits add LR Executor one cooperative executor per process (root task and every scope ); parallel and blocking pools; cancellation and deadlines Tokio adapter, I/O readiness, timers, channels (Runtime host kit) LR 15 , LR 12.5 Task reporter sink for report , cleanup reports, log records, not_started records, completed capture frames; default: reports plus each frame's schema, outcome class and duration to stderr, never a captured field value; FORTIS_TELEMETRY=permit-all puts every field on the record stream exporters, archive, projections (Telemetry kit) LR 15 Authorities Authority ; the ten capabilities, supplied to the entry main 's parameters by type; Host.capabilities() in fortis.host ; the rule for per-activation authorities minting adapters, attenuation helpers, sandboxing LR 15 , LR 11.6 Observation schemas the observation head registry and pipelines (Telemetry kit) LR 15 , LR 13 Entry main in four forms, its parameters supplied by type; one report and exit status 1 on failure the application head and lifecycle (Runtime host kit) LR 15 , LR 4.6 Standard modules fortis.prelude , fortis.text , fortis.collections , fortis.numeric , fortis.time , fortis.money , fortis.host everything else under fortis.* LR 15 Secrets Secret[T] (no codec, no capture, fingerprint display), reveal(secret, cap: SecretAccess) declassify , Sanitiser , authentication adapters (Security kit) LR 15"},{"u":"docs/agent/15-runtime.html#capabilities-and-their-core-operations","p":"Core runtime inventory (LR 15)","h":"Capabilities and their core operations","m":"agent","x":"Capabilities and their core operations # Created once, only for the entry main : the runtime passes each capability main declares as a parameter, by its type ( func main(console: Console, fileSystem: FileSystem) , LR 4.6 ), and main lends them to the functions it calls ( LR 11.6 ). Older code calls Host.capabilities() once in a main with no capability parameter; the fields of the returned Capabilities are fileSystem , network , clock , random , environment , process , supervisor , console , secretAccess , rawSql ( fortis.host ). Capability Operations ( LR 15 ) Notes FileSystem readText(path) String , writeAll(path, text) , readAll(path) Bytes , open(path) File , create(path) File , subtree(path) FileSystem all but subtree are try ; File : readAll , writeAll , close Network resolve(host) String try Clock now() Timestamp ( nondet ), frozen(instant) Clock , sleep(d) ( async ), elapsedSince(t) Duration ( try ) a pure function takes time as a parameter Random below(bound: Int<1..>) Int<0..> ( nondet ), seeded(seed) Random Environment get(name) Secret[String]? Process run(program, arguments: ListView[String]) Int try , block Console print(text) standard output, one line RawSql execute(statement) Int try Supervisor none; authorises scope.detach(move task, supervisor) LR 12.5 SecretAccess none; authorises reveal(secret, cap) Path.parse(text: String) Path is try ( LR 15 ). Per-activation authorities ( RequestContext , Identity , Db , Tx , TaskContext , Mail ) are minted by their kit's adapter from a capability ( LR 15 , LR 11.6 ). Development adapters of the reference toolchain (library contracts in fortis.host , not language rules): file system rooted in a fresh empty directory per run or FORTIS_DEV_ROOT (relative to the working directory; every backend); network offline except localhost ; Random SplitMix64 from seed 0 or FORTIS_DEV_SEED ; Process simulates true (0) and false (1); RawSql fails sql.unavailable ."},{"u":"docs/agent/15-runtime.html#standard-module-contents-named-by-lr-15","p":"Core runtime inventory (LR 15)","h":"Standard module contents named by LR 15","m":"agent","x":"Standard module contents named by LR 15 # Module Contents LR fortis.money Decimal amount with run-time Currency ; Money.of , zero , add , subtract , multiply(money, factor: Int) , divide(money, divisor: Int<1..>) , greaterThan ; arithmetic fails on currency mismatch LR 15 fortis.time Duration.days/hours/minutes/seconds/milliseconds , fallible Duration.add/sub/multiply , d.nanoseconds() , comparisons on Duration , display in the largest exact unit ( 150s , 1h , 250ms , 0s ); Timestamp.epoch() , fallible Timestamp.add(t, d) , Timestamp.after , Timestamp.before LR 15 fortis.host capabilities, Path , Secret[T] , reveal LR 15"},{"u":"docs/agent/15-runtime.html#diagnostics","p":"Core runtime inventory (LR 15)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-EFF-010 Host.capabilities() outside main , a second call in main (reported at main ), a call in a main that takes a capability parameter, or io other than report and log reached without an Authority parameter declare the capability as a parameter of main ; pass it (or a Shared of it) down as a parameter LR 11.6 E-TYP-090 a Secret[T] (or an authority) in an Encodable position, such as an observation field capture or encode a non-secret projection LR 15 , LR 6.10"},{"u":"docs/agent/15-runtime.html#correction-patterns","p":"Core runtime inventory (LR 15)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/15-runtime.html#e-eff-010-capabilities-created-outside-main-lr-116","p":"Core runtime inventory (LR 15)","h":"E-EFF-010: capabilities created outside main (LR 11.6)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-010: capabilities created outside main (LR 11.6) # Fortis // reject: E-EFF-010 import fortis . host { Host } func greet ( ) { let caps = Host . capabilities ( ) // reject: E-EFF-010 } func main ( ) { greet ( ) } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func greet ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"hello\" ) } func main ( console : Console ) { greet ( console ) } output hello"},{"u":"docs/agent/15-runtime.html#e-eff-010-hostcapabilities-in-a-main-that-takes-capabilities-lr-116","p":"Core runtime inventory (LR 15)","h":"E-EFF-010: Host.capabilities() in a main that takes capabilities (LR 11.6)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-010: Host.capabilities() in a main that takes capabilities (LR 11.6) # A main with a capability parameter already has its capabilities; a call of Host.capabilities() there is a second creation. Fortis // reject: E-EFF-010 import fortis . host { Console , Host } func main ( console : Console ) { let caps = Host . capabilities ( ) // reject: E-EFF-010 console . print ( \"ready\" ) } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"ready\" ) console . print ( \"once\" ) } output ready once"},{"u":"docs/agent/15-runtime.html#patterns","p":"Core runtime inventory (LR 15)","h":"Patterns","m":"agent","x":"Patterns # Failing try main (exit status 1, one runtime-failure record on stderr naming code, payload and origin; LR 4.6 ): Fortis // accept (exit status 1) import fortis . host { Path , FileSystem , Console } try func main ( fileSystem : FileSystem , console : Console ) { let path = Path . parse ( \"missing.txt\" ) or fail let text = fileSystem . readText ( path ) or fail console . print ( text ) } Revealing a secret ( LR 15 ): Fortis // accept import fortis . host { reveal , Environment , SecretAccess , Console } func main ( environment : Environment , secretAccess : SecretAccess , console : Console ) { let home = environment . get ( \"HOME\" ) if home != null { let value = reveal ( home , secretAccess ) console . print ( \"${value.byteCount() > 0}\" ) } } output true Time arithmetic ( LR 15 ): Fortis // accept pure try func deadline ( start : Timestamp , slack : Int < 0 .. 60 > ) Timestamp { let wait = Duration . add ( Duration . minutes ( 5 ) , Duration . seconds ( slack ) ) or fail return Timestamp . add ( start , wait ) or fail } test \"deadline is later\" { let t = deadline ( Timestamp . epoch ( ) , 30 ) expect Timestamp . after ( t , Timestamp . epoch ( ) ) }"},{"u":"docs/agent/16-kits.html#reserved-declaration-heads-for-kits-lr-16","p":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16)","h":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16)","m":"agent","x":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16) # Agent mode. Human mode: Kits and declaration heads . Source of truth: LR 16 . Preview: declaration heads and kits ( LR 16 ) arrive in v0.5. LR 16 has no rule table; the rule that binds it is LANG-03-04 ( LR 3.4 ): every reserved word, head words included, is rejected in declaration and binding positions and admitted as a member or method name."},{"u":"docs/agent/16-kits.html#facts","p":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16)","h":"Facts","m":"agent","x":"Facts # Fact LR A kit is a named, versioned bundle of packages shipped with the distribution; it extends Fortis only through libraries, aspects, declaration heads, embedding bindings and verifiers. Toolchain components (daemon, LSP, SDK generators) are not kits. LR 16 A kit never changes core semantics and is never a prerequisite of Language 1.0. LR 16 Kits extend the language with aspects ( LR 11.8 ) and, for non-function-shaped declarations, declaration heads. LR 16 A head's body is validated by a versioned schema and elaborated into core declarations before type checking. LR 16 Elaboration contract: a head produces only classes, functions, enums, contracts and observation schemas that obey every LR rule. LR 16 Elaborated code is checked like any other code; heads have no ownership or effect rules of their own. LR 16 Packages cannot add heads, tokens or grammar rules. LR 16 Head words are reserved words, never imported; importing a kit's package makes its heads available; its aspects are imported by name. LR 16 Raw template region: a statement [export] [server|client|hybrid] view [name [(params)]] { opens a region; the lexer takes the text to the balancing } (counting all braces, including ${\u2026} ) as one unlexed token handed to the Web UI kit. LR 16"},{"u":"docs/agent/16-kits.html#reserved-heads","p":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16)","h":"Reserved heads","m":"agent","x":"Reserved heads # Head Kit Elaborates to LR application Runtime host generated main , capability acquisition, lifecycle ordering LR 16 explore Developer tooling an exploration declaration LR 16 context (body word provide ), class member inject Dependency injection constructor parameters, generated scope struct LR 16 model Data nominal data class, schema, codecs, query metadata LR 16 view (standalone), component (body words state , action , view , resource ) Web UI render function; class with explicit state struct, action functions, render function LR 16 observation core ( LR 13 ), consumed by Telemetry frame type and schema LR 16 Reserved also: prefixes server , client , hybrid ; body words inject , provide , state , action , resource ( LR A ). Not reserved (contextual): lazy , launch , launchParallel , launchBlocking , detach , retain , validate , checkpoint , join , annotate , cleanup , capability ( LR A )."},{"u":"docs/agent/16-kits.html#extension-slots-exactly-four-lr-16","p":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16)","h":"Extension slots (exactly four; LR 16)","m":"agent","x":"Extension slots (exactly four; LR 16) # A head block, optionally prefixed server , client or hybrid . [export] inject [lazy] name: Type inside a class body; an exported slot is an exported field (X-138). Aspects declared by the kit, applied with @name(args) (no slot of their own). The cases table syntax of tests ( LR 14 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/16-kits.html#diagnostics","p":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-SYN-002 a head word, prefix or head-body word used as a binding, parameter, field, type or free-function name rename ( page , pageView ); a method or associated function may keep the keyword name LR 3.4 , LR 16"},{"u":"docs/agent/16-kits.html#correction-patterns","p":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/16-kits.html#e-syn-002-a-head-word-as-a-field-name-lr-34-lr-16","p":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16)","h":"E-SYN-002: a head word as a field name (LR 3.4, LR 16)","m":"agent","x":"E-SYN-002: a head word as a field name (LR 3.4, LR 16) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-002 export class Page { export view : Int // reject: E-SYN-002 } test \"page\" { expect true } Fortis // accept export class Page { export views : Int export func view ( self ) Int => self . views } test \"page\" { let p = Page ( views : 3 ) expect p . view ( ) == 3 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/16-kits.html#e-syn-002-a-head-word-as-a-binding-lr-34-lr-16","p":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16)","h":"E-SYN-002: a head word as a binding (LR 3.4, LR 16)","m":"agent","x":"E-SYN-002: a head word as a binding (LR 3.4, LR 16) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-002 pure func size ( ) Int { let model = 1 // reject: E-SYN-002 return model } test \"size\" { expect size ( ) == 1 } Fortis // accept pure func size ( ) Int { let dataModel = 1 return dataModel } test \"size\" { expect size ( ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/16-kits.html#e-syn-002-a-head-word-as-a-free-function-name-lr-34-lr-16","p":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16)","h":"E-SYN-002: a head word as a free function name (LR 3.4, LR 16)","m":"agent","x":"E-SYN-002: a head word as a free function name (LR 3.4, LR 16) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-002 pure func component ( n : Int ) Int => n // reject: E-SYN-002 test \"component\" { expect component ( 1 ) == 1 } Fortis // accept export class Widget { export id : Int export func component ( id : Int ) Widget => Widget ( id ) } test \"component\" { expect Widget . component ( 1 ) . id == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/16-kits.html#pattern-a-user-aspect-no-slot-needed-lr-16-lr-118","p":"Reserved declaration heads for kits (LR 16)","h":"Pattern: a user aspect (no slot needed; LR 16, LR 11.8)","m":"agent","x":"Pattern: a user aspect (no slot needed; LR 16, LR 11.8) # Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } aspect logged ( label : StringView ) { needs console : Console before { console . print ( \"enter ${label}\" ) } } @ logged ( \"work\" ) func work ( console : Console , n : Int ) Int => n func main ( console : Console ) { console . print ( \"result ${work(console, 3)}\" ) } output enter work result 3"},{"u":"docs/agent/README.html#fortis-for-agents","p":"Fortis for agents","h":"Fortis for agents","m":"agent","x":"Fortis for agents # Agent mode. Human mode: the Fortis guide . Source of truth: the Language Reference (LR). Fortis is a statically checked, compiled, library-first language built so that code written mostly by agents can be reviewed and trusted by people: every ownership operation, mutation permission, failure disposition, effect claim and observation point is written in the source ( LR 1 ). This mode states the rules densely, by rule ID, with the records the tools print and the corrections that fix them. It restates the LR; it never extends it."},{"u":"docs/agent/README.html#precedence-of-sources","p":"Fortis for agents","h":"Precedence of sources","m":"agent","x":"Precedence of sources # The Language Reference ( LANG- rules) is normative for the core; where its prose and the grammar reference/grammar/fortis.ebnf disagree on syntax, the grammar wins; where a rule and an example disagree, the rule wins ( LR 0 ). The Platform Specification ( PLAT- rules) defines the kits. A PROG- ID, in a record's rule , names a requirement on the toolchain itself: its tools, records and process. This agent mode and the human guide are derived from them. A page that contradicts them is a defect in the page. Decisions are logged as register rows X-nnn , and known toolchain defects as D-nn , in the maintainers' planning records; pages cite them by ID. What v0.0.2 runs, and what it still refuses, is Known limitations ; the release note is v0.0.2 ."},{"u":"docs/agent/README.html#tools","p":"Fortis for agents","h":"Tools","m":"agent","x":"Tools # Run from the repository root after cd compiler && cargo build --release . fortis is bin/fortis . Command Output (pipe, or --format json ) Use fortis view PATH / --decls / --symbol NAME / --query Q / --json JSON lines, kind one of line , declaration , symbol , match , query read .ft source; never cat / grep fortis check FILE.ft|DIR --format json one diagnostic record per line; none means clean all static checks fortis run FILE.ft|DIR [ARGS\u2026] the program's standard output; runtime records on standard error run main on the definitional interpreter with ARGS as its arguments; files from FORTIS_DEV_ROOT (relative to the working directory), else a fresh empty directory fortis test FILE.ft|DIR --format json test-result records on standard error, then one test-summary on standard output run tests and properties on the interpreter fortisc test FILE.ft|DIR --format json the same records, on the same streams run tests on the development VM fortisc vm FILE.ft|DIR observed: line|line|\u2026 (the conformance form) run main on the development VM fortis build FILE.ft|DIR --out APP one build record ( binary , manifest ) native executable through safe Rust fortis fmt --check PATH / --write PATH files not in canonical layout / rewritten canonical layout, after rewriting older forms into the forms these pages teach where the module checks and the meaning is unchanged ( --no-migrate : layout only) Every finding and runtime report is a record first; text is rendered from it, and the stream chooses the form (JSON lines on a pipe) unless --format says otherwise. Schemas and record kinds are in records . A diagnostic record ( reference/schemas/diagnostic.schema.json ), here for a use after a move ( LR 7.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-OWN-020 pure func size ( items : owned List [ Int ] ) Int { let kept = move items return items . count ( ) // reject: E-OWN-020 } test \"size\" { expect size ( List . of ( 1 , 2 ) ) == 2 } records {\"class\": \"Ownership\", \"code\": \"E-OWN-020\", \"column\": 10, \"fields\": {\"conflict\": \"use after move\", \"loan_region\": \"\", \"place\": \"items\", \"spans\": \"items\"}, \"file\": \"example.ft\", \"line\": 4, \"message\": \"`items` was moved and is used again (read)\", \"rule\": \"LANG-07-04\", \"severity\": \"error\", \"status\": \"violated\"} code : stable; look it up in codes . rule : the LANG- / PLAT- / PROG- ID it enforces. status : violated (a proved violation), unproved (the checker could not prove a refinement or claim; not proof of a bug), unknown (a trust boundary). fields : the class's mandatory fields. correction , when present, is an applicable edit: {\"kind\": \u2026, \"edits\": [{\"file\", \"line\", \"column\", \"end_line\", \"end_column\", \"replacement\"}]} . A runtime failure is written to standard error once, by report , by a cleanup, by a scope abandoning a task's result, or by a failing try main ( LR 4.6 , LR 10.6 , LR 12.1 , LR 12.5 ), as a record with kind: \"runtime-failure\" , the failure's code and payload , its origin ( file , function ), cause and annotations (the annotate trail), reported_by ( report , main , cleanup or abandoned ) and rule . Tests write test-result records ( test , case , status , evidence ) and one test-summary ( passed , failed )."},{"u":"docs/agent/README.html#the-loop","p":"Fortis for agents","h":"The loop","m":"agent","x":"The loop # Inspect: fortis view --decls DIR , fortis view --symbol NAME DIR , fortis view --query Q DIR (queries: async , try , async-try , pure , noalloc , trusted , recovers , editable-params , owned-params , or-fail , or-recover , catch , fail , return , defer , scope , capture , expect , move , lambda , tests , errors , classes , aspect:NAME ). Edit. Check: fortis check DIR --format json . For each record: read code , rule , fields ; apply correction when present; otherwise use the correction pattern of the code's page. Test: fortis test DIR --format json ; a failing test is evidence, not an obstacle. Format: fortis fmt --write DIR , so inferred facts are printed back and old forms take their reading forms where they mean the same: unmarked editable self receivers and fn mut calls, return local , heads without Void , main 's capability parameters, implied or fail in tests, Map.get keys without copy , puns, or-pattern arms, checked operators, bare variants ( LR 1.1 , X-345). Repeat until check prints nothing and every test passes."},{"u":"docs/agent/README.html#prohibitions","p":"Fortis for agents","h":"Prohibitions","m":"agent","x":"Prohibitions # Do not read .ft files with cat , head , tail , sed -n or less , or search them with grep or rg ; use fortis view . Do not weaken a contract, remove an expect , or change an expected output to make a check or test pass. Do not lower a threshold or delete an assertion without a visible, approved policy change. Do not hand-format Fortis code; run fortis fmt --write . Do not treat unproved as a bug or as safe: add the explicit proof or checked alternative the record names."},{"u":"docs/agent/README.html#example-notation-on-these-pages","p":"Fortis for agents","h":"Example notation on these pages","m":"agent","x":"Example notation on these pages # Every fortis block is checked by python3 -I conformance/tools/docset_check.py on the real toolchain. First line: // accept (both checkers report nothing; it runs: main on the interpreter, the VM and native code with exactly the output block shown, or its tests pass on all three; (exit status N) when main fails on purpose), // reject: CODE (exactly that code on each line carrying the same marker, nothing else), // illustrative (not a program). A records block is the exact JSON the checker prints for the block above it, with the file named example.ft ( LR 0 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/README.html#pages","p":"Fortis for agents","h":"Pages","m":"agent","x":"Pages # Page LR Overview: invariants and notation 1, 2 Lexical structure 3 Modules and packages 4 Statements and expressions 5 Types 6 Proving arithmetic and indexing 6.3, 6.4, 6.7 Ownership 7 Functions and closures 8 Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums 9 Outcomes and failures 10 Effects and contracts 11 Cleanup, async and concurrency 12 Observation 13 Tests 14 Core runtime 15 Kits and declaration heads 16 Embedded languages (the raw template region; blocks are a later edition, X-145) 3, 16 Keywords , closed decisions , lowering , rule index A, B, C, D Records : diagnostic, runtime, test, view and build records 0 Codes : every diagnostic code with its class, rule, section and correction all Glossary : kit, package, toolchain component, head, binding, verifier E Core library : every exported operation of the seven core modules with its contract (generated) LR 15 The skill skills/fortis-code (installed with fortis skill install ) points agents here."},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-a-keywords.html#keyword-inventory-lr-a","p":"Keyword inventory (LR A)","h":"Keyword inventory (LR A)","m":"agent","x":"Keyword inventory (LR A) # Agent mode. Human mode: Keywords . Source of truth: LR A , LR 3.4 ."},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-a-keywords.html#rules","p":"Keyword inventory (LR A)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-03-04 Every word below is reserved in every declaration and binding position (variable, parameter, field, type, declaration name), except the name of a method or associated function; after . every word is a member name ( memberName ). The lexer never reclassifies a keyword. LR 3.4 , LR A"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-a-keywords.html#inventory","p":"Keyword inventory (LR A)","h":"Inventory","m":"agent","x":"Inventory # Group Words LR Declarations func fn let mut const type alias class trivial enum error interface mixin implement implements with derive requires forbids excludes contract aspect needs before after on metadata lifetime from where export import as LR A Modifiers pure noalloc async try recovers editable owned dynamic linear trusted once LR A Ownership copy move LR A Control if else while for in loop break continue return fail give recover match when otherwise is or catch discard defer await scope LR A Values true false null present success failure self Self LR A Observation capture record report observation required version encoding LR A Testing test cases property forall fuzz bench expect LR A Kit heads (reserved by the core) application context model component view explore ; prefixes server client hybrid ; in head bodies inject provide state action view resource LR A , LR 16 Contextual, not reserved launch launchParallel launchBlocking detach retain validate checkpoint join annotate cleanup capability lazy ; log (the log statements log.info / log.warn / log.error , LR 13 ) LR A"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-a-keywords.html#diagnostics","p":"Keyword inventory (LR A)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-SYN-002 a keyword in a declaration or binding position ( let match , a field from , a parameter type ) rename ( pattern , start , kind ); a method may keep the keyword name LR 3.4 Tool behaviour: the checker stops parsing a module at its first E-SYN-002 , so fix each one and re-check; the rule itself is LR 3.4 ."},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-a-keywords.html#correction-patterns","p":"Keyword inventory (LR A)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-a-keywords.html#e-syn-002-keyword-as-a-binding-lr-34","p":"Keyword inventory (LR A)","h":"E-SYN-002: keyword as a binding (LR 3.4)","m":"agent","x":"E-SYN-002: keyword as a binding (LR 3.4) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-002 pure func span ( start : Int , end : Int ) Int { let match = end // reject: E-SYN-002 return match } test \"span\" { expect span ( 1 , 4 ) == 4 } Fortis // accept pure func span ( start : Int , end : Int ) Int { let last = end return last } test \"span\" { expect span ( 1 , 4 ) == 4 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-a-keywords.html#e-syn-002-keyword-as-a-field-keep-it-as-a-method-lr-34-lr-a","p":"Keyword inventory (LR A)","h":"E-SYN-002: keyword as a field; keep it as a method (LR 3.4, LR A)","m":"agent","x":"E-SYN-002: keyword as a field; keep it as a method (LR 3.4, LR A) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-002 class Span { export from : Int // reject: E-SYN-002 } test \"span\" { expect Span ( from : 1 ) . from == 1 } Fortis // accept class Span { export start : Int export pure func from ( start : Int ) Span => Span ( start ) export pure func where ( self , n : Int ) Int => n } test \"span\" { let s = Span . from ( 1 ) expect s . start == 1 expect s . where ( 4 ) == 4 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-b-decisions.html#closed-decisions-lr-b","p":"Closed decisions (LR B)","h":"Closed decisions (LR B)","m":"agent","x":"Closed decisions (LR B) # Agent mode. Human mode: Closed decisions . Source of truth: LR B . Decisions Language 1.0 does not reopen. Never write the rejected form; the normative statement is in the section cited ( LR B )."},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-b-decisions.html#syntax-and-literals","p":"Closed decisions (LR B)","h":"Syntax and literals","m":"agent","x":"Syntax and literals # Topic Decision LR Declaration vs assignment let / let mut declare; = assigns only LR 5.1 Semicolons ; is E-SYN-001 LR 3.2 File extension, comments .ft ; // only; documentation = the // run directly above a declaration; no /* */ , no /// LR 3.1 , LR 3.7 Integer literal type from context, default Int ; must fit the target refinement LR 3.5 Float literal needs . or exponent; f32 suffix for Float32 LR 3.5 Decimal literal d suffix ( 19.99d ) LR 3.5 String literal StringView from static ; converts to String only where a String is expected (allocates) LR 3.5 , LR 6.6 Block value give (not yield ); single-expression match arms need none; catch ends in recover LR 5.4 , LR 9.8 Type of fail Never LR 5.4 Catch binding explicit: catch e { \u2026 } LR 10.4 Named arguments after positionals, any order, each once; names are public API LR 5.8 Default arguments pure constant expressions, re-evaluated per call LR 5.8 Variadics none in user code; take ListView[T] , pass List.of(\u2026) LR 5.8 , LR 6.2 Constructor naming create , of , from ; no new LR 1.1 , LR 9.2 Transparent type aliases none; type is always nominal LR 6.9 Operator overloading none; Type.add(a, b) LR 6.10 Or-patterns narrow: A | B => v , whole patterns of an arm, no bindings, no guard LR 9.8 Not in v1 range patterns, generic lambdas, generic functions over N , associated types, explicit type arguments at calls (annotate the binding) LR 6.2 , LR 6.10 , LR 8.2 , LR 9.5 , LR 9.8 Standard package fortis ; no std LR 4.3 Negative example marker // reject: E-XXX-NNN LR 0 Test rows cases { \u2026 } as row { \u2026 } LR 14"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-b-decisions.html#ownership","p":"Closed decisions (LR B)","h":"Ownership","m":"agent","x":"Ownership # Topic Decision LR Call-site markers move p , copy p , editable p for existing places; fresh values unmarked LR 7.5 Receiver markers move p.m() for owned self ; an editable self call is p.m() on a root that shows it may change ( let mut , editable parameter or capture, exclusive loan, the method's own editable self ) and E-OWN-001 on any other root, marked or not; editable p.m() stays accepted LR 7.5 , LR 7.6 Kept loans at call sites alias p / editable p for an argument the result borrows from LR 7.6 Returning a local return local moves it; return move local stays accepted; parameters, fields and captures keep their marker LR 7.5 , LR 7.6 Partial moves not in v1 ( E-OWN-040 ) LR 7.2 Non-lexical lifetimes not in v1; loans end at their block LR 7.3 Stored loans not in v1 ( E-OWN-053 ): loans live in locals, parameters and from returns LR 7.7 Stored and returned loans both spell from ; within removed LR 7.6 , LR 7.7 Two-phase borrows reserve at argument evaluation, activate at call entry LR 5.7 Two-origin loans rejected LR 7.6"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-b-decisions.html#functions-failures-effects","p":"Closed decisions (LR B)","h":"Functions, failures, effects","m":"agent","x":"Functions, failures, effects # Topic Decision LR Void functions may fall off the end; non- Void need return / fail on every path; a function or method head leaves out Void , a function type keeps it LR 5.4 , LR 8.1 Closure kinds fn , fn mut , fn once , always written; a fn mut held by a let mut is called f() LR 8.2 Lambda modifiers same as named functions; capture brackets only when non-empty LR 8.2 recovers a modifier, written like try LR 8.1 , LR 10.4 or die removed; try main (one report, exit status 1) LR 4.6 report statement; report e as receipt binds the receipt LR 10.6 nostate removed; opt-in lint no-local-mutation only LR 11.2 Effect clauses the vocabulary is pure / noalloc / async / try / recovers ; no effects(\u2026) clause; tools show inferred alloc , block , nondet LR 11.2 , LR B Contracts by name in modifier position ( export FastCalculation func \u2026 ); never @ ; no arguments LR 11.5 Aspects @name(args) , lowercase, the only use of @ ; parts needs , before , after , on failure , metadata ; ...rest specialisation; on failure may re-fail; user packages may declare them LR 11.8 Aspect hooks failure handlers innermost first; no around ; no body re-execution LR 11.8 Observation attachment @observe(Schema) , a core aspect LR 11.8 , LR 13 Interfaces nominal; requires for sub-interfaces; bundles satisfied automatically; no method bodies LR 9.5 Serialisation derive Codec = Encodable + Decodable ; canonical toBytes / toText ; formats as packages with \u2026Serialised mixins; hand-written encode and derive never coexist LR 6.10 Projection ...copy p copies taken fields; ...move p consumes the whole source; derive From[S] ; to() on every type LR 6.10 , LR 9.2 Constants include record and variant-map literals, declaration names, Type.of(\u2026) intrinsics LR 5.1 Variant maps VariantMap[E, V] written { Variant: value } ; optional callable (fn(\u2026) R)? LR 6.9 Entry point main in the manifest's entry module, export implied; its parameters are the capabilities and arguments it needs, supplied by type LR 4.6"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-b-decisions.html#numbers-collections-concurrency","p":"Closed decisions (LR B)","h":"Numbers, collections, concurrency","m":"agent","x":"Numbers, collections, concurrency # Topic Decision LR Float equality IEEE == allowed; no Equatable / Hashable / Comparable LR 6.5 Hash iteration deterministic per build; allowed in pure LR 6.7 Length-refined collections immutable; mutate an unrefined collection and validate LR 6.3 Executor cooperative single thread per scope tree; parallel/blocking pools need Shareable LR 12.5 Blocking in async code forbidden; launchBlocking takes a synchronous fn once LR 11.1 , LR 12.5 Atomics AtomicInt , AtomicBool ; seqcst , acquire / release LR 12.7 defer block form only; defer await is E-DEF-001 LR 12.1"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-b-decisions.html#correction-patterns","p":"Closed decisions (LR B)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-b-decisions.html#or-die--try-main-lr-46","p":"Closed decisions (LR B)","h":"or die \u2192 try main (LR 4.6)","m":"agent","x":"or die \u2192 try main (LR 4.6) # Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } export error Empty { code : \"demo.empty\" } try func first ( items : ListView [ Int ] ) Int { if items . count ( ) == 0 { fail Empty ( ) } return items . get ( 0 ) ?? 0 } try func main ( console : Console ) { let n = first ( List . of ( 4 , 5 ) ) or fail console . print ( \"${n}\" ) } output 4"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-b-decisions.html#-lr-32","p":"Closed decisions (LR B)","h":"; (LR 3.2)","m":"agent","x":"; (LR 3.2) # Fortis // reject: E-SYN-001 pure func one() Int { return 1; // reject: E-SYN-001 } test \"one\" { expect one() == 1 } Fortis // accept pure func one ( ) Int { return 1 } test \"one\" { expect one ( ) == 1 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#lowering-reference-and-the-rust-expressible-subset-lr-c","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","m":"agent","x":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C) # Agent mode. Human mode: Lowering to Rust . Source of truth: LR C , LR 7.10 ."},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#rules","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"Rules","m":"agent","x":"Rules # Rule Statement LR LANG-07-03 No clone, reference count or hidden shared ownership is ever inserted to repair a rejected program. LR 7.10 , LR C.1 LANG-07-09 An ownership shape with no lowering is rejected with E-OWN-090 (or the earlier rule's more specific code), never lowered with unsafe or lifetime erasure. LR 7.10 , LR C.2 LANG-11-03 noalloc is decided by the allocation whitelist of LR C.3 against the actual target and telemetry plan. LR 11.4 , LR C.3 Generated application crates carry #![forbid(unsafe_code)] ; a rustc rejection of frontend-accepted code is a compiler defect only inside the Rust-checked fragment ( LR 7.10 , LR C.1 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#shapes-c1","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"Shapes (C.1)","m":"agent","x":"Shapes (C.1) # Construct Native shape Rust-checked Frontend-checked LR type newtype struct Name(Repr) , private field nominal identity refinement invariant LR C.1 class struct + impl field privacy generated-constructor visibility LR C.1 trivial type Copy struct/enum Copy the trivial table LR C.1 copy p Clone::clone of a generated Clone copyable set; allocation LR C.1 read loan / exclusive loan &T / &mut T loan validity lexical end (stricter); field-freezing LR C.1 move p move use after move LR C.1 from return fn f<'a>(x: &'a T) -> &'a U lifetime single origin LR C.1 standard view / cursor &'a str , &'a [T] , Cursor<'a, T> owned by its holder lifetime standard types only (X-137) LR C.1 Shared[T] / Weak[T] Arc<T> / Weak<T> Send / Sync retain visibility LR C.1 Mutex[T] std::sync::Mutex wrapper Send / Sync guard across await LR C.1 interface / dynamic I trait + generics / Box<dyn I> , &dyn I , Arc<dyn I> trait bounds; object safety substitutability, coherence; dynamic eligibility, allocation LR C.1 mixin generated inherent methods conflicts, required methods LR C.1 lambda closure, concrete environment Fn / FnMut / FnOnce declared kind \u2265 needed kind; capture list LR C.1 try func / catch Result<T, Failure> / match linearity; recover/return semantics LR C.1 T? / match Option<T> / match exhaustiveness (both) binding ownership LR C.1 async func / scope async fn state machine / join set polled by the parent linear futures, cancellation at await ; join on exit LR C.1 launchParallel pool spawn with 'static + Send Send Shareable LR C.1 defer body inlined at every exit (not Drop ) each inlined copy's borrows places initialised at every exit LR C.1 cleanup impl Drop calling the method drop order effects contributed LR C.1 noalloc , effects no representation whitelist (C.3); summaries LR C.1 capture / record frame struct writes, record calls definite assignment LR C.1 capability zero-sized or handle struct, private constructor io requires capability LR C.1"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#unsupported-shapes-c2","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"Unsupported shapes (C.2)","m":"agent","x":"Unsupported shapes (C.2) # Shape Code Alternative LR loan from two parameters E-SYN-000 return owned, or split LR C.2 , LR 7.6 class storing a loan (lifetime parameter, loan or loan-carrying field other than a loan from static ) E-OWN-053 own, copy, Shared , or pass per call LR C.2 , LR 7.7 self-referential class E-OWN-053 indices, or Shared + Weak LR C.2 , LR 7.7 loan stored in Shared / Mutex E-OWN-090 own the value LR C.2 fn mut escaping into Shared E-OWN-090 Mutex around the state + fn closure LR C.2 future holding a loan on a parallel pool E-ASY-003 pass owned data LR C.2 , LR 12.7 partial move E-OWN-040 move the whole, or restructure LR C.2 , LR 7.2 A shape an earlier rule excludes keeps that rule's code (X-126); E-OWN-090 is for shapes only the lowering identifies ( LR C.2 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#allocation-whitelist-c3","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"Allocation whitelist (C.3)","m":"agent","x":"Allocation whitelist (C.3) # Allocation-free Allocating LR arithmetic, comparison; field and array access; trivial and inline values ( Array , FixedList , FixedMap , bounded Failure ); a class or record built from trivial, moved, fresh, call-returned or retained ( retain() , downgrade() ) values; read/exclusive loans; calls of non- alloc functions; match ; defer ; join(a, b) ; capture into a bounded frame List / String / Map growth; Shared.create ; dynamic creation; copy of a heap-owning value; boxed futures of dynamic async calls; extended failure payloads; launchParallel / launchBlocking ; every cooperative launch in v1 (no inline task storage, X-125) LR C.3"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#diagnostics","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"Diagnostics","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostics # Code Trigger Correction LR E-OWN-040 move p.field while p still owns the rest move the whole binding; consume it with let Name(field: x, \u2026) = move p LR 7.2 , LR C.2 E-OWN-053 lifetime parameter, or a field of a loan or loan-carrying type (not from static ) own the data ( String for StringView ), copy it, Shared , or pass it per call LR 7.7 , LR C.2 E-OWN-090 a shape with no lowering (loan or fn mut into Shared / Mutex ) own the value; Mutex or atomic state with a fn closure LR 7.10 , LR C.2 E-ASY-003 a pool launch whose future borrows or holds a non- Shareable value pass owned Shareable data ( move , copy ) LR 12.7 , LR C.2 E-EFF-001 an allocating pattern in a noalloc body remove the allocation (a caller-provided buffer, an inline Array / FixedList ); removing the claim is an API change, never a correction ( LR 11.3 ) LR 11.1 , LR 11.3 , LR C.3"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#correction-patterns","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"Correction patterns","m":"agent","x":"Correction patterns #"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#e-own-040-partial-move-lr-72-lr-c2","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"E-OWN-040: partial move (LR 7.2, LR C.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-040: partial move (LR 7.2, LR C.2) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-040 class Order { export id : Int export lines : List [ Int ] } test \"lines\" { let order = Order ( id : 7 , lines : List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) ) let lines = move order . lines // reject: E-OWN-040 expect lines . count ( ) == 3 } Fortis // accept class Order { export id : Int export lines : List [ Int ] } test \"lines\" { let order = Order ( id : 7 , lines : List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) ) let Order ( id : id , lines : lines ) = move order expect id == 7 expect lines . count ( ) == 3 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#e-own-053-stored-loan-lr-77-lr-c2","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"E-OWN-053: stored loan (LR 7.7, LR C.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-053: stored loan (LR 7.7, LR C.2) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-053 class Line { // reject: E-OWN-053 export text : StringView } test \"line\" { expect 1 == 1 } Fortis // accept class Line { export text : String } test \"line\" { let line = Line ( text : \"first\" ) expect line . text == \"first\" }"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#e-own-090-fn-mut-into-shared-lr-c2","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"E-OWN-090: fn mut into Shared (LR C.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-OWN-090: fn mut into Shared (LR C.2) # Fortis // reject: E-OWN-090 test \"bump\" { let mut count = 0 let bump = fn mut [ editable count ] ( ) Void { count = Int . saturatingAdd ( count , 1 ) } let shared = Shared . create ( move bump ) // reject: E-OWN-090 } Fortis // accept test \"bump\" { let counter = Shared . create ( AtomicInt . create ( 0 ) ) let handle = counter . retain ( ) let bump = fn [ move handle ] ( ) Void { discard handle . fetchAdd ( 1 ) } bump ( ) bump ( ) expect counter . load ( ) == 2 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#e-asy-003-loan-sent-to-a-pool-lr-127-lr-c2","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"E-ASY-003: loan sent to a pool (LR 12.7, LR C.2)","m":"agent","x":"E-ASY-003: loan sent to a pool (LR 12.7, LR C.2) # Fortis // reject: E-ASY-003 import fortis . host { Console } async func total ( items : ListView [ Int ] ) Int { let mut sum = 0 for x in items { sum = Int . saturatingAdd ( sum , x ) } return sum } async func main ( console : Console ) { let numbers = List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) scope tasks { let t = tasks . launchParallel ( total ( numbers ) ) // reject: E-ASY-003 let sum = await t console . print ( \"${sum}\" ) } } Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } async func total ( items : owned List [ Int ] ) Int { let mut sum = 0 for x in items { sum = Int . saturatingAdd ( sum , x ) } return sum } async func main ( console : Console ) { let numbers = List . of ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) scope tasks { let t = tasks . launchParallel ( total ( move numbers ) ) let sum = await t console . print ( \"${sum}\" ) } } output 6"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-c-lowering.html#e-eff-001-allocation-in-noalloc-lr-111-lr-c3","p":"Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset (LR C)","h":"E-EFF-001: allocation in noalloc (LR 11.1, LR C.3)","m":"agent","x":"E-EFF-001: allocation in noalloc (LR 11.1, LR C.3) # The record is reported at the allocating statement. Fortis // reject: E-EFF-001 pure noalloc func grow ( items : editable List [ Int ] ) { items . push ( 1 ) // reject: E-EFF-001 } test \"grow\" { expect 1 == 1 } Fortis // accept pure func grow ( items : editable List [ Int ] ) { items . push ( 1 ) } pure noalloc func square ( n : Int < - 1000 .. 1000 > ) Int => n * n test \"grow\" { let mut items : List [ Int ] = List . default ( ) grow ( editable items ) expect items . count ( ) == 1 expect square ( 12 ) == 144 }"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-d-rules.html#rule-index-lr-d","p":"Rule index (LR D)","h":"Rule index (LR D)","m":"agent","x":"Rule index (LR D) # Agent mode. Human mode: Rule index . Source of truth: each LR section's rule table; LR D for old IDs. Every LANG- rule, its section, a condensed statement and the agent page that carries its codes and correction patterns. A diagnostic record's rule field names one of these IDs, or a PROG- or PLAT- ID. The LR rule text is authoritative; the statements here are condensed ( LR 0 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-d-rules.html#lr-3-6","p":"Rule index (LR D)","h":"LR 3-6","m":"agent","x":"LR 3-6 # Rule LR Statement Page LANG-03-01 LR 3.2 ; outside strings, comments, raw template regions: parse error 03 LANG-03-02 LR 3.2 newline termination and continuation follow the 3.2 token lists exactly 03 LANG-03-03 LR 3.3 identifier category from its first character 03 LANG-03-04 LR 3.4 keywords reserved in declaration/binding positions; allowed as method/associated-function names and after . A LANG-03-05 LR 3.5 literal typing per 3.5; out-of-range literal rejected at compile time 03 LANG-03-06 LR 3.6 interpolations evaluated once, in order; never fallible or suspending 03 LANG-04-01 LR 4.2 module identity = file path; modules hold only declarations and constants 04 LANG-04-02 LR 4.2 importing executes no code 04 LANG-04-03 LR 4.3 only exports: modules importable across packages 04 LANG-04-04 LR 4.1 distinct nominal types per package version and per module 04 LANG-04-05 LR 4.5 shadowing a visible binding rejected 04 LANG-04-06 LR 4.6 entry = the main of the manifest's entry module, exported or not, one of the four heads (each optionally recovers ); each parameter List[String] or a capability type, each type once, supplied by its type 04 LANG-05-01 LR 5.1 let declares, and its pattern matches every value; = assigns only a visible mutable target 05 LANG-05-02 LR 5.1 block exit: end loans, run defers LIFO, drop locals in reverse initialisation order 05 LANG-05-03 LR 5.2 if / while conditions are Bool 05 LANG-05-04 LR 5.3 ranged for refines its index; index immutable 05 LANG-05-05 LR 5.4 non- Void function ends every path in return , fail or Never 05 LANG-05-06 LR 5.6 precedence per 5.6; mixed families without parentheses: parse error 05 LANG-05-07 LR 5.7 evaluation order per 5.7, identical on both backends 05 LANG-05-08 LR 5.5 , LR 12.3 discarding a linear value rejected, except a never-polled future holding nothing linear and no exclusive loan; other non- Void values need discard , except a call statement of an @ignorable function 05 LANG-05-09 LR 5.8 named after positional; each parameter once; names are exported signature; a pun x is x: x 05 LANG-06-01 LR 6.1 , LR 6.9 nominal identity survives calls, storage, serialisation, FFI 06 LANG-06-02 LR 6.3 structural refinement subtyping; interval domain exactly as listed 06 , proofs LANG-06-03 LR 6.3 untrusted values pass validate before holding a refined or nominal type 06 LANG-06-04 LR 6.4 , LR 6.5 integer, Decimal , listed float operations bit-exact across VM and native; a checked operator fails as its function, at the same operation 06 LANG-06-05 LR 6.5 float operators total, strict IEEE; float refinements rejected 06 LANG-06-06 LR 6.7 Map / Set iteration deterministic per build 06 LANG-06-07 LR 6.11 trivial and copyable sets exactly as in 6.11 06 LANG-06-08 LR 6.8 optional, Bool , Outcome never convert into one another 06 LANG-06-09 LR 6.12 linear values consumed exactly once 06 LANG-06-10 LR 6.10 , LR 9.5 coherence: implementation in the package of the interface or the type; no overlap 06 LANG-06-11 LR 6.10 type arguments unfixed by arguments and expected type: rejected 06"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-d-rules.html#lr-7-9","p":"Rule index (LR D)","h":"LR 7-9","m":"agent","x":"LR 7-9 # Rule LR Statement Page LANG-07-01 LR 7.5 existing ownership-bearing place assigned/returned/captured carries copy / alias / move , except that returning a local let binding moves it; owned / editable arguments carry their marker; an unmarked editable self receiver or fn mut call checked as the marked one, rejected where the marker could not be written 07 LANG-07-02 LR 7.6 parameters never reassignable 07 LANG-07-03 LR 7.10 no inserted clones, reference counts or hidden sharing 07 , C LANG-07-04 LR 7.3 , LR 7.4 use after move, conflicting loans, loaned-owner use, escaping loans rejected, incl. closures, defer , await , scope 07 LANG-07-05 LR 7.3 loans lexical; aliasing invariant at every point 07 LANG-07-06 LR 7.6 , LR 7.7 returned loans name one origin; lifetime parameter or loan-carrying field (not from static ) rejected 07 LANG-07-07 LR 7.9 destruction order per 7.9 on both backends 07 LANG-07-08 LR 7.8 sharing explicit ( retain ); copy of Shared rejected 07 LANG-07-09 LR 7.10 unsupported shapes rejected, never lowered with unsafe or erasure C LANG-08-01 LR 8.1 parameter and success types stated (a head may leave out Void ; a lambda's as 8.2 allows); modifiers in 8.1 order 08 LANG-08-02 LR 8.2 , LR 8.3 lambdas list captures (no brackets when none) and declare kind; stronger-kind body rejected 08 LANG-08-03 LR 8.3 names outside the capture list inaccessible 08 LANG-08-04 LR 8.4 callable assignability per 8.4; no boxing without dynamic fn 08 LANG-09-01 LR 9.1 no inheritance, runtime member lookup, reflection-based construction 09 LANG-09-02 LR 9.2 generated constructor only where every field is visible 09 LANG-09-03 LR 9.5 implementations never strengthen preconditions or weaken guarantees; coherence 09 LANG-09-04 LR 9.6 mixins require methods only, add no state; conflicts rejected 09 LANG-09-05 LR 9.8 closed-type match exhaustive without otherwise ; each missing variant named; or-pattern alternatives count, bind nothing, take no guard 09 LANG-09-06 LR 9.8 pattern binding ownership per 9.8 09"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-d-rules.html#lr-10-14","p":"Rule index (LR D)","h":"LR 10-14","m":"agent","x":"LR 10-14 # Rule LR Statement Page LANG-10-01 LR 10.1 , LR 10.2 outcomes wrapped until consumed by a 10.2 operation 10 LANG-10-02 LR 10.7 obligations survive collections, fields, closures; dropping rejected 10 LANG-10-03 LR 10.6 reported failure unreachable afterwards; annotate keeps one obligation 10 LANG-10-04 LR 10.3 noalloc try uses the bounded failure representation 10 LANG-10-05 LR 10.4 recover without a prior report adds recovers 10 LANG-10-06 LR 10.8 defects follow the 10.8 taxonomy; never catchable 10 LANG-11-01 LR 11.1 , LR 11.3 exactly eight effects; transitive fixed-point summaries 11 LANG-11-02 LR 11.1 , LR 11.3 fresh-storage and own- editable -parameter writes are not mutate ; captures, guards, atomics are 11 LANG-11-03 LR 11.4 noalloc against the whitelist for the target and telemetry plan 11 , C LANG-11-04 LR 11.3 trusted summaries labelled trusted , never proved 11 LANG-11-05 LR 11.3 async / try / recovers written; only written pure / noalloc cross modules; weakening exported modifiers reported 11 LANG-11-06 LR 11.5 contracts are conjunctions of 11.5 elements; configuration adds none 11 LANG-11-07 LR 11.6 io (except report and the log statements) needs an Authority ; only the entry main receives capabilities, as parameters, or creates them once with Host.capabilities() when it declares none; every other use of Host.capabilities and every reference to the entry main rejected 11 LANG-11-08 LR 11.5 , LR 11.8 contracts in modifier position, no arguments; @ for aspects only 11 LANG-11-09 LR 11.8 aspects have only the 11.8 parts, elaborate before checking, pass every check 11 LANG-12-01 LR 12.1 defer checked at every exit; cannot alter the outcome; named places initialised at every exit 12 LANG-12-02 LR 12.1 failures in defer consumed there; never replace the primary outcome 12 LANG-12-03 LR 12.3 , LR 12.4 Future / Task linear; pending future never destroyed; cancellation only at cancellation points 12 LANG-12-04 LR 12.5 a scope joins or cancels every task before its block exits 12 LANG-12-05 LR 12.5 , LR 12.7 only Shareable environments cross pools; loans cross only cooperative launch 12 LANG-12-06 LR 12.3 , LR 12.7 no MutexGuard live across await 12 LANG-13-01 LR 13 capture snapshots a pure, infallible expression 13 LANG-13-02 LR 13 required fields assigned exactly once per applicable exit; loops and doubles rejected 13 LANG-13-03 LR 13 frames bounded by the schema; truncation or omission recorded 13 LANG-13-04 LR 13 each log.info / log.warn / log.error writes exactly one log record to the diagnostic plane, never standard output, the same on every backend; needs no Authority ; has the io effect 13 LANG-14-01 LR 14 table rows bound with as 14 LANG-14-02 LR 14 expect takes Bool , records typed evidence 14 LANG-14-03 LR 14 , LR 6.4 in test / property bodies a fallible call nothing else consumes ( or , catch , match , a let later consumed as an outcome or annotated Outcome[\u2026] ), and unproved Int arithmetic, behave as followed by or fail ; outside them nothing changes 14"},{"u":"docs/agent/appendix-d-rules.html#old-ids-fspec-01","p":"Rule index (LR D)","h":"Old IDs (FSPEC-0.1)","m":"agent","x":"Old IDs (FSPEC-0.1) # Old Now LR SYN-001 / SYN-002 / SYN-004 LANG-05-05 + LANG-08-01 / LANG-03-03 / LANG-05-07 LR D , LR 3.3 , LR 5.4 , LR 5.7 , LR 8.1 , LR 3 , LR 5 , LR 8 SYN-003, SYN-005, SYN-006, TYP-005 the toolchain's requirements (formatter, diagnostics, library metadata) LR D TYP-001..004, TYP-006 LANG-06-01 , LANG-06-03 , LANG-06-04 , LANG-06-02 , LANG-06-08 LR D , LR 6.3 , LR 6 OWN-001..006 LANG-07-01 , -02, -03, -04, -06, -08 LR D , LR 7.5 , LR 7 FUN-001, FUN-002 / OO-001 / GEN-001 LANG-08-02 / LANG-09-01 + LANG-09-04 / LANG-06-10 + the toolchain's requirements (bounded solving) LR D , LR 6.10 , LR 8.2 , LR 9.1 , LR 6 , LR 8 , LR 9 MOD-001 / DOC-001 LANG-04-02 / the toolchain's requirements (every documentation example runs as a test) LR D , LR 4.2 , LR 4 ERR-001..006 / ERR-007 LANG-10-01 , -02, -03 (ERR-003, -004), -04, LANG-09-05 / Telemetry and Runtime host kits LR D , LR 9.8 , LR 10.2 , LR 9 , LR 10 EFF-001, -002, -004, -005, -006 / EFF-003 LANG-11-01 , -02, -03, -04, -06 / removed with nostate LR D , LR 11.1 , LR 11 RES-001 / RES-002 LANG-05-02 + LANG-07-07 / LANG-12-02 LR D , LR 5.1 , LR 7.9 , LR 12.1 , LR 5 , LR 7 , LR 12 ASY-001, ASY-003 / ASY-002 / ASY-004 / ASY-005 LANG-12-03 / LANG-12-05 / LANG-12-04 / Runtime host kit LR D , LR 12.3 , LR 12 OBS-001, -002, -006 / OBS-003..005 LANG-13-01 , -02, -03 / Telemetry kit LR D , LR 13"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#diagnostic-codes","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Diagnostic codes","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostic codes # Agent mode. Source of truth: the LR section cited in each row; classes and mandatory fields: records.md . Every diagnostic code the Language Reference names, grouped by class. Rule is the rule ID the toolchain's record carries for the code (most often; where several rules share one code, the record names the one the finding breaks; where the record carries a rule that is not the one the LR states for the code, the row says so, D-57). Pairs is the agent page holding a reject/accept correction pair for the code, or the rules for it. Look a record up by its code , read the cited section, apply the correction, re-check."},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#syntax-e-syn","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Syntax (E-SYN)","m":"agent","x":"Syntax (E-SYN) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-SYN-000 LANG-03-02 LR 3.2 , LR 5.6 , LR C.2 not a sentence of the grammar (also f() or fail ?? 0 ; a loan type naming two origins) fix the syntax; split the expression; let v = f() or fail on its own line 03 , 05 E-SYN-001 LANG-03-01 LR 3.2 a ; outside strings, comments, raw template regions delete it (the record carries the edit) 03 , B E-SYN-002 LANG-03-04 LR 3.4 a keyword in a declaration or binding position rename; a method may keep a keyword name A E-SYN-004 LANG-05-06 LR 5.6 outcome operators, ?? and && / || mixed without parentheses parenthesise 05"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#names-and-modules-e-nam","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Names and modules (E-NAM)","m":"agent","x":"Names and modules (E-NAM) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-NAM-001 LANG-04-05 (as recorded; for an assignment the rule is LANG-05-01 , D-57) LR 4.5 , LR 5.1 , LR 10.3 , LR 13 an undeclared name (assignment to it, a use of it; log other than as a log statement, where nothing named log is declared; a ${name} in an error's message that is not a payload field); also used for other unresolved names and module errors declare it, import it, or fix the name; a log is log.info(m) , log.warn(m) or log.error(m) as a statement; a message names only payload fields 05 , 04 E-NAM-002 LANG-05-01 LR 5.1 assignment to an immutable binding or a parameter let mut ; copy a parameter into a let mut local 05 E-NAM-003 LANG-04-05 LR 4.5 a let shadows a visible binding a new name 05 , 04 E-NAM-004 none stated (records carry the shadowing rule in error, D-57) LR 4.4 a declaration or member not visible here (not exported, or a constructor with a non-visible field) export it, or use an associated function 04 E-NAM-005 LANG-04-06 LR 4.6 a parameter of the entry main the runtime cannot supply: a type other than List[String] or a capability imported from fortis.host , or a mode, default or ... give main only name: List[String] and name: Console (and the other capability types), and take anything else from them 04 E-NAM-006 LANG-04-06 LR 4.6 two parameters of the entry main with the same type keep one parameter per type; lend it to the functions that need it 04 E-NAM-010 LANG-04-03 LR 4.3 dependency names that make the package/module split ambiguous rename a package or module 04 E-NAM-020 PROG-ARC-04 LR 4.1 lockfile does not pin a dependency, pins one no manifest reaches, or is unreadable fortis lock 04 E-NAM-021 PROG-ARC-04 LR 4.1 a package, compiler, standard library or Rust toolchain digest differs from the lockfile restore the pinned input, or re-lock deliberately 04 E-NAM-030 PLAT-01-03 LR 4.1 an import of a standard module the build target's feature set lacks ( check --target , build --target ) remove the import, or choose a target with the feature set 04 E-NAM-031 PLAT-01-01 LR 4.1 a standard-package dependency outside the published compatibility set depend on the set's version 04 E-NAM-032 PROG-CMP-06 LR 4.1 a native build of a package that ships a Cargo build script remove the build script 04 E-NAM-034 PROG-DEL-04 LR 16 a declaration head a kit defines ( export model Customer { \u2026 } , policy , page ) whose kit this toolchain does not install; fields.candidates names the kit install the kit and import it, or remove the declaration (a plain class where no kit is wanted) 16"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#type-e-typ-e-mix-001-lr-610","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Type (E-TYP, E-MIX-001; LR 6.10)","m":"agent","x":"Type (E-TYP, E-MIX-001; LR 6.10) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-TYP-010 LANG-05-03 LR 5.2 , LR 6.8 a non- Bool condition ( if n , if maybe ) compare explicitly ( != 0 , != null ) 05 E-TYP-020 LANG-06-05 LR 6.3 a float refinement refine an integer, or validate by hand 06 E-TYP-030 none stated (records carry the shadowing rule in error, D-57) LR 4.4 , LR 6.9 an exported signature names a non-exported type, or a tuple, record or variant map (aspect parameters and the fortis package's own signatures excepted) export the type; use a nominal class in exported signatures 04 , 06 E-TYP-040 LANG-06-01 LR 3.5 , LR 6.1 , LR 9.4 , LR 9.7 , LR 10.1 a type mismatch (an Outcome[T] where T is expected; an interpolated String bound to StringView ; a non- Displayable interpolation; a direct cleanup call; a bare variant whose name is also a class, error or type in scope, or that nothing expects the enum of) consume the outcome first; fix the type; never call cleanup ; qualify the variant ( Severity.Error ) or annotate the binding 10 , 06 , 09 E-TYP-050 LANG-05-09 LR 5.8 , LR 9.2 a parameter without a default, or a constructor field, left unbound pass it 05 , 09 E-TYP-060 LANG-09-03 LR 9.5 an interface requirement cycle break the cycle 09 E-TYP-070 LANG-09-01 LR 9.1 a name declared twice in a class (fields, methods, constants share one namespace) rename one 09 E-TYP-080 LANG-09-03 LR 9.5 , LR 9.6 incomplete implementation: missing member or required interface, incompatible signature, unmet mixin requirement add the member, implementation or method 09 E-TYP-090 none stated (records carry the trivial-set rule in error, D-57) LR 6.10 , LR 13 a derivation whose rule is not met (a field without the interface; Encodable with a private field; a canonical codec call on a non-codec type; a non- Encodable schema field) implement the interface on the field type, export the fields, or write encode / decode by hand 06 , 13 E-TYP-100 LANG-06-10 LR 6.10 , LR 9.5 coherence: an implementation outside the packages defining the interface or the type, or an overlap implement in the defining package; remove the overlap 09 E-TYP-110 LANG-05-05 LR 5.4 a path reaches the end of a non- Void function; a block arm or if -expression branch without give (reported at the function) return / fail on that path; give in the branch 05 E-TYP-120 LANG-05-05 LR 5.4 give outside a match arm or if expression, recover outside catch , break / continue outside a loop move it into the construct it ends 05 E-TYP-130 LANG-05-09 LR 5.8 positional after named, unknown parameter name, parameter bound twice, too many arguments fix the argument list 05 E-TYP-140 LANG-05-08 LR 5.5 an unused non- Void expression statement (a call of an @ignorable function excepted) discard e , or use the value; or declare the function @ignorable when most callers drop its result 05 E-TYP-150 LANG-11-09 LR 11.8 , LR 5.5 an aspect application that does not match: unknown aspect, wrong arguments, unmet or ambiguous needs , ...rest outside a parameter list; @ignorable on a function whose result is linear ( try , async , a linear type) fix the application or the target's parameters; drop @ignorable from a function whose result must be consumed 11 E-TYP-160 LANG-06-11 LR 6.10 a type argument fixed neither by the arguments nor by the expected type ( let names = List.default() ) annotate the receiving binding 06 E-MIX-001 LANG-09-04 LR 6.10 , LR 9.5 , LR 9.6 two mixins provide one method; a class derives an interface it also implements by hand declare the method in the class; drop the derive or the hand-written method 09"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#ownership-e-own","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Ownership (E-OWN)","m":"agent","x":"Ownership (E-OWN) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-OWN-001 LANG-07-01 LR 7.5 , LR 7.6 an existing ownership-bearing place assigned, returned (other than a local, which return moves), initialised into a field or passed owned without a marker; a loan of a copyable, non-trivial value ( xs.get(0) , a binding holding one) where an owned value is expected (D-341); an editable self receiver or fn mut call, marked or not, on a root that cannot change (a let , a read parameter or loan, a read self ), whatever its type copy p , alias p or move p (the record carries an edit); for a loan, let v = xs.get(0) then copy v , or match \u2026 { present(w) => copy w \u2026 } ; for a receiver, declare it let mut or take it editable (X-345: then it needs no marker) 07 , records E-OWN-002 LANG-07-08 LR 7.5 , LR 7.6 , LR 7.8 copy of a non-copyable place ( Shared , Weak , a resource); a loan of a value that is not Copyable where an owned value is expected, which has no owned copy (D-341) retain() for Shared / Weak ; keep the loan (a read parameter, a from result) or take the value out of its owner ( pop , move ) 07 E-OWN-010 LANG-07-05 LR 7.3 , LR 8.3 a conflicting loan: an exclusive loan while other loans are active; two editable captures of one owner end the earlier loan by closing a block 07 , 08 E-OWN-011 LANG-07-05 LR 7.3 , LR 8.3 the owner used while loaned (suspended by an exclusive child, frozen by a read child) use it after the loan's block ends 07 , 08 E-OWN-012 LANG-07-04 LR 5.7 , LR 7.3 a later argument moves, assigns or exclusively loans a place an earlier argument reserved evaluate the conflicting argument into a binding first 05 E-OWN-020 LANG-07-04 LR 7.4 , LR 7.5 use after move copy before the move, or re-initialise a let mut 07 E-OWN-030 LANG-06-07 LR 6.11 a trivial class with a non-trivial field drop trivial , or make the field trivial 06 E-OWN-040 LANG-07-01 LR 7.2 , LR C.2 , LR 7.5 a partial move move the whole value; destructure it with let Name(\u2026) = move p 07 , C E-OWN-050 LANG-07-06 LR 7.6 a result borrowing from two parameters return an owned value, or split the function 07 E-OWN-051 LANG-07-06 LR 7.6 returning a loan of a local or a temporary; a value of the function's own returned where the result is declared from a parameter, which must lend: move d , a copy, a fresh value, a tuple or record literal, a constructor, a lambda, a call whose result is owned, or a ?? , if or match any of whose results is one of these, at any depth (D-373, D-377, D-381) return an owned value ( move ); for a from result, bind the loan and return an owned value from a function whose result is owned ( let v = xs.get(0) then return copy v ?? move d ), or lend on every path 07 E-OWN-052 LANG-07-04 LR 7.3 , LR 12.1 a defer body using a binding of its block that holds a loan use the owner, or move the deferred work 12 E-OWN-053 LANG-07-06 LR 7.7 , LR C.2 a lifetime parameter, or a field of a loan or loan-carrying type (not from static ) own the value, copy it, hold a Shared , or pass it per call 07 , C"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#unsupported-shape-e-own-090-lr-710","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Unsupported shape (E-OWN-090; LR 7.10)","m":"agent","x":"Unsupported shape (E-OWN-090; LR 7.10) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-OWN-090 LANG-07-09 LR 7.10 , LR C.2 a shape with no lowering: a loan stored in Shared / Mutex , a fn mut escaping into Shared own the value; Mutex or atomic state with a fn closure C"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#obligation-e-obl-e-err-001-lr-54","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Obligation (E-OBL, E-ERR-001; LR 5.4)","m":"agent","x":"Obligation (E-OBL, E-ERR-001; LR 5.4) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-OBL-001 LANG-10-01 LR 5.5 , LR 6.12 , LR 10.1 , LR 11.8 , LR 12.3 , LR 12.4 a linear value dropped, overwritten or discarded (an Outcome , Failure , Future , Task , Must ; a never-polled future holding nothing linear and no exclusive loan may be discarded, LR 12.3 ); an unconsumed linear owned parameter on a failing before path or fail , or recover v , catch , match , report , await , launch, or pass it on 10 , 05 E-OBL-002 LANG-10-01 LR 10.4 , LR 10.1 a catch block path that does not end in recover , return , fail or a Never call end every path 10 E-OBL-005 LANG-06-09 LR 6.12 , LR 12.4 a linear value live across a cancellation point ( await ), or at an or fail , that does not implement Abandonable consume it before the await , or pass it into the awaited call 12 E-OBL-006 LANG-10-02 LR 8.3 , LR 10.7 a fn or fn mut closure capturing a linear value by move declare it fn once 08 E-OBL-007 LANG-10-02 LR 9.2 , LR 10.7 ...move p drops a linear field the target lacks give the target the field, or consume it first 09 E-OBL-008 LANG-11-09 LR 11.8 an after hook that may fail when R is linear do not fail in after , or return a non-linear type 11 E-ERR-001 LANG-10-01 LR 5.4 , LR 10.2 fail outside a try function declare the function try 05 , 10"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#effect-e-eff","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Effect (E-EFF)","m":"agent","x":"Effect (E-EFF) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-EFF-001 LANG-11-01 LR 11.1 , LR 11.2 the summary exceeds the claims: an effect in pure , an allocation in noalloc , suspend without async , fail without try , recovers without recovers (the record names the claim's rule) remove the effect, or write the modifier the summary needs 11 , C E-EFF-010 LANG-11-07 LR 11.6 , LR 4.6 io through a capability with no Authority parameter; Host.capabilities() outside main , twice, or in a main that takes capability parameters take the capability as a parameter; give main the capabilities as parameters ( func main(console: Console) ) instead of calling Host.capabilities() 11 E-EFF-020 LANG-11-01 LR 11.1 the block effect in an async function or anything it calls move the work to launchBlocking 12 , 11 E-EFF-030 LANG-11-03 LR 11.4 the telemetry plan attaches an allocating record to a noalloc function (at the policy site) change the plan, not the function 11 E-EFF-040 LANG-11-05 LR 11.3 warning from fortis check --api-baseline OLD : an exported function lost a written pure / noalloc or gained async / try / recovers restore the claim, or make the API change deliberately 11"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#refinement-unproved-e-ref","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Refinement unproved (E-REF)","m":"agent","x":"Refinement unproved (E-REF) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-REF-001 LANG-06-02 LR 3.5 , LR 6.3 a value (or literal) whose interval lies outside the target's refinement T.validate(x) or fail , or a wider declared type 06 , proofs E-REF-002 LANG-06-02 LR 6.4 , LR 6.3 an integer operator the interval rules cannot prove (overflow, division by zero, shift range, MIN / -1 ) outside a checked region; on a sized integer type ( Int32 , UInt8 , \u2026) or >> , which have no checked operation, under or fail too on Int , the checked operator, a + b or fail ; on any integer type, a refined type or a test that proves it 06 , proofs E-REF-003 LANG-06-02 LR 6.3 an editable loan or mutation of a length-refined collection mutate an unrefined collection, then validate 06"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#patterns-e-mat","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Patterns (E-MAT)","m":"agent","x":"Patterns (E-MAT) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-MAT-001 LANG-09-05 LR 9.8 a match over a closed enum missing variants (each named) add the arms 09 E-MAT-002 LANG-09-05 LR 9.8 otherwise , or a last _ or binding arm, over a closed enum or optional list the variants 09 E-MAT-003 LANG-09-05 LR 9.8 an or-pattern alternative that binds a name or is otherwise , or an or-pattern arm with a when guard give the binding or guarded case an arm of its own; _ for an unused payload 09 E-MAT-004 LANG-05-01 LR 5.1 a let pattern that can fail to match: a variant, a literal, null , present(\u2026) , at any depth (a let destructures a class, tuple or record) use match to handle the other cases 05"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#closures-e-fun","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Closures (E-FUN)","m":"agent","x":"Closures (E-FUN) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-FUN-010 LANG-08-02 LR 8.2 a lambda body needing a stronger kind than declared ( fn that mutates a capture) declare fn mut or fn once 08 E-FUN-011 LANG-08-03 LR 8.3 a name used in a lambda body but not in its capture list add it to the capture list with a marker 08"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#defer-and-async-e-def-e-asy","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Defer and async (E-DEF, E-ASY)","m":"agent","x":"Defer and async (E-DEF, E-ASY) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-DEF-001 LANG-12-01 LR 12.1 return , fail , give , break , continue or await inside defer move it out; consume failures with or recover / catch / report 12 E-DEF-002 LANG-12-01 LR 12.1 a place the defer names is moved out before an exit keep it owned until every exit (lend instead of moving), or do the deferred work before the move 12 E-ASY-001 LANG-12-04 LR 12.3 , LR 12.5 a Task leaves its scope block ( return , give , assignment outside) await it inside the scope, or detach it to a Supervisor 12 E-ASY-002 LANG-12-06 LR 12.3 , LR 12.7 a MutexGuard held across await end the guard's block before the await 12 E-ASY-003 LANG-12-05 LR 12.7 , LR C.2 a pool launch whose future or closure holds a loan or a non- Shareable value pass owned Shareable data 12 , C"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#observation-e-obs","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Observation (E-OBS)","m":"agent","x":"Observation (E-OBS) # Code Rule LR Trigger Correction Pairs E-OBS-001 LANG-13-02 LR 13 a required frame field unassigned on an applicable exit capture it before that exit; changing the schema's required: is a change to the schema, never a correction 13 E-OBS-010 LANG-13-02 LR 13 a second capture of one field on one path capture once 13 E-OBS-011 LANG-13-02 LR 13 a capture inside a loop use record for repeated measurements 13 E-OBS-012 LANG-13-01 LR 13 a capture / record expression with an effect, or naming no field of the attached schema a pure, infallible expression; a declared field 13"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#resource-limits-and-internal-defects-e-lim-e-int","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Resource limits and internal defects (E-LIM, E-INT)","m":"agent","x":"Resource limits and internal defects (E-LIM, E-INT) # These have no automatic correction. Code Rule LR Trigger What to do Pairs E-LIM-001 PROG-SEC-01 LR 6.10 a native build's instantiation budget (32) exhausted: a generic function instantiating itself at ever larger types recurse through a non-generic helper, or a dynamic value 06 E-LIM-002 PROG-SEC-01 LR 3.1 a source file over 16 MiB or 2,000,000 tokens, or nested more than 256 deep split the file; flatten the nesting 03 E-LIM-003 PROG-SEC-01 LR 4.1 a manifest over 1 MiB or 10,000 lines shrink the manifest 04 E-LIM-004 PROG-SEC-01 LR 4.1 a lockfile over 8 MiB or 100,000 lines shrink the dependency set 04 E-LIM-005 PROG-SEC-01 LR 4.1 JSON the toolchain reads (caches, saved fuzz inputs) over 256 MiB or nested more than 128 deep remove the input 04 E-INT-001 PROG-CMP-05 LR 4.1 a checker defect; reproduction names a minimised input and the command report it with the reproduction; never work around it silently 04 E-INT-002 PROG-CMP-05 LR 4.1 a native build whose generated Rust does not compile, at the Fortis function it came from report it with the function it names 04 E-INT-003 PROG-CMP-05 LR 4.1 a native build cannot run a tool it needs (cargo, rustc, the linker driver) install the named tool 04"},{"u":"docs/agent/codes.html#kit-codes","p":"Diagnostic codes","h":"Kit codes","m":"agent","x":"Kit codes # The kits name their own codes in the Platform Specification; the records have the same shape. Prefix Class Codes Source E-DI Dependency injection (mandatory consumer , slot , candidates ) E-DI-001 \u2026 E-DI-006, E-DI-008 \u2026 E-DI-011 Platform 3 E-TEL Telemetry (mandatory plan_line , projection , field ) E-TEL-001 \u2026 E-TEL-007 Platform 6.4"},{"u":"docs/agent/embedded-languages.html#embedded-languages-lr-16","p":"Embedded languages (LR 16)","h":"Embedded languages (LR 16)","m":"agent","x":"Embedded languages (LR 16) # Agent mode. Human mode: Embedded languages . Source of truth: LR 16 . Preview: declaration heads and kits ( LR 16 ) arrive in v0.5. Fact Source The core's only embedded text is the raw template region: a statement [export] [server|client|hybrid] view [name [(\u2026)]] { opens it, recognised lexically with no knowledge of the enclosing declaration; the lexer takes the text to the balancing } (every brace counted, ${\u2026} included) and hands it to the Web UI kit unlexed, as one token. LR 16 Recognition is the lexer's; permission is the kit's: it validates where a region appears, inside a component body or as a standalone view head. Where the view is a declaration, its group must be a parameter list. Template syntax is the kit's; a template adds no core token. LR 16 Elsewhere, another language's text is a string ( StringView or String ). Interpolation never escapes for HTML, SQL or any context: bind or escape values through a library. LR 3.5 , LR 3.6 Fenced embedded-language blocks with typed holes, embed \u2026 from , embedding bindings and their verifiers are not in Language 1.0 (a later edition, X-145). Do not write them: they are not syntax ( LR 3.2 ). X-145"},{"u":"docs/agent/glossary.html#glossary-agent-mode","p":"Glossary (agent mode)","h":"Glossary (agent mode)","m":"agent","x":"Glossary (agent mode) # Agent mode. Human mode: Glossary . One meaning per term; where a word has two meanings both rows are listed. A bundle of packages is a kit. The Language Reference's glossary is LR E ; this page adds the in-language terms."},{"u":"docs/agent/glossary.html#system","p":"Glossary (agent mode)","h":"System","m":"agent","x":"System # Term Definition Source package unit of distribution, versioning, visibility: project.yaml , lockfile, src/ ; two versions are two packages LR 4.1 module one .ft file under src/ ; name = path below src/ with . separators; declarations and constants only LR 4.2 kit named, versioned bundle of packages shipped with the distribution; extends Fortis only by libraries, aspects, heads, embedding bindings, verifiers; never changes core semantics LR 16 compatibility set the standard packages at the versions tested together; a standard dependency names the set's version LR 4.1 toolchain component daemon, LSP, SDK generators: tools, not kits LR 16 backend interpreter ( fortis run , the reference), development VM ( fortisc vm ), native ( fortis build ); must agree LR 1 build mode dev (VM), native-dev , release ; optimisation and debug settings belong to a mode LR 1 lint level baseline (an unused import warns) or strict (it is an error) LR 4.3 target feature set which standard modules exist for a target ( core , alloc , host , async , web , browser ) LR 11.6"},{"u":"docs/agent/glossary.html#declarations","p":"Glossary (agent mode)","h":"Declarations","m":"agent","x":"Declarations # Term Definition Source head (declaration head) reserved keyword introducing a schema-validated declaration elaborated to core declarations before type checking; owner: the core ( observation , LR 13 ), developer tooling ( explore , LR E ), or a kit (the rest) LR 16 , LR 13 , LR E elaboration rewriting a head, or a function with aspects, into core declarations before ownership and effect checking LR 16 , LR 11.8 extension slot head block; inject member; aspect application; cases table: the only four LR 16 raw template region text of view { \u2026 } in a head body, handed unlexed to the Web UI kit LR 16 aspect @name(args) behaviour around a function: needs , before , after , on failure , metadata ; adds LR 11.8 contract named conjunction of modifiers, forbidden effects, required or excluded authorities; subtracts LR 11.5 mixin methods applied with with ; requires methods, adds no state LR 9.6 newtype type Name = Repr ; nominal, private representation LR 6.9 refinement predicate in <\u2026> on a built-in type LR 6.3 binding (name) name introduced by let , let mut , a parameter, a pattern or a capture LR 5.1 , LR 7.2 binding (embedded language) versioned package giving a language fenced blocks via the embedding protocol; meaning compiler-fixed, checking a verifier (SP-23, not yet in the LR) Embedded languages verifier stricter-only package: reads the checked program, returns records never code; pure, deterministic, bounded, no capability Embedded languages"},{"u":"docs/agent/glossary.html#ownership","p":"Glossary (agent mode)","h":"Ownership","m":"agent","x":"Ownership # Term Definition Source place binding; field path rooted in a binding or self ; Array element with a literal index LR 7.2 owner binding holding a value until it is moved out LR 7.2 loan alias (read) or editable (exclusive) reference to a place; lexical LR 7.3 child loan a loan taken through a loan-holding binding, recorded against the owner LR 7.3 marker copy , alias , move , editable before a place; left out where the declaration already shows it (an editable self receiver on a let mut root, return local ) LR 7.5 loan-carrying value standard views and cursors; store a read loan; owned by their binding LR 7.7 trivial duplicated implicitly, never marked LR 6.11 copyable supports copy LR 6.11 linear consumed exactly once on every path LR 6.12 shareable may cross launchParallel / launchBlocking LR 12.7"},{"u":"docs/agent/glossary.html#failures-effects-authority","p":"Glossary (agent mode)","h":"Failures, effects, authority","m":"agent","x":"Failures, effects, authority # Term Definition Source outcome Outcome[T] from a try function; linear LR 10.1 checked region Int arithmetic as the left operand of or fail / or recover / catch ; an unproved operator in it is the checked operation LR 6.4 failure Failure : code, payload, origin, cause chain; linear LR 10.3 obligation the duty to consume a linear value LR 7.1 , LR 10.1 defect broken-assumption condition (stack, memory, trusted contract, runtime invariant); never caught LR 10.8 effect one of alloc , io , mutate , nondet , suspend , block , fail , recovers LR 11.1 effect summary a function's effect subset, computed transitively LR 11.1 , LR 11.3 write footprint editable parameters a function writes through LR 11.3 fresh storage created in this activation, not yet reachable from a parameter, capture or self LR 11.1 authority value implementing Authority ; required for io LR 11.6 capability process-wide authority (ten types), supplied to the entry main 's parameters by type LR 11.6 , LR 4.6 per-activation authority minted by a kit adapter from a capability for one unit of work LR 11.6 trusted bodiless standard intrinsic; claims reported as trusted, never proved LR 11.3"},{"u":"docs/agent/glossary.html#concurrency-and-observation","p":"Glossary (agent mode)","h":"Concurrency and observation","m":"agent","x":"Concurrency and observation # Term Definition Source scope scope name { \u2026 } : owns tasks; joins or cancels them before exit LR 12.5 task Task[T] from a launch; linear LR 12.5 cancellation point await ; on the pools also checkpoint() LR 12.4 observation schema observation declaration of typed frame fields LR 13 observation frame one activation's captured fields LR 13 event one record execution LR 13 task reporter diagnostic-plane sink for reports, logs and frames LR 15 log record one log.info / log.warn / log.error execution: level, message, function; standard error, never standard output LR 13 diagnostic plane output channel nothing can read back LR 11.7 record (tool output) schema-valid object; text on a terminal, JSON line otherwise records"},{"u":"docs/agent/known-limitations.html#known-limitations-of-v002","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","m":"agent","x":"Known limitations of v0.0.2 # Agent mode. Human mode: Known limitations of v0.0.2 . Release note: v0.0.2 . Open, non-blocking defects in v0.0.2's scope, plus loud refusals (X-343): a checked program, a non-zero exit, another backend correct. Do not treat a row as a licence to change the program's meaning. A soundness bug or a wrong result is absent here unless v0.0.1 already had it: those are carried under X-416 in their own section, and code must avoid their shapes, since the checkers do not. The last section is what v0.0.2 leaves for later by design (its release note). Open, non-blocking defects in v0.0.2's scope, plus loud refusals (X-343): a checked program, a non-zero exit, another backend correct. Do not treat a row as a licence to change the program's meaning. A soundness bug or a wrong result on every backend is absent here on purpose. The last section is what v0.0.2 leaves for later (its release note): by design, or a recorded gap with its defect id. Generated from the defect register and the release's own list, with the human-mode page; edit those, not the page."},{"u":"docs/agent/known-limitations.html#valid-programs-the-checkers-reject-and-library-gaps","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"Valid programs the checkers reject, and library gaps","m":"agent","x":"Valid programs the checkers reject, and library gaps # Defect What you meet Workaround D-51.5 An unused import is accepted with no warning. Delete the import. Nothing else changes. D-52.1 An error constructor is typed as its own kind, so a use that the language accepts where a failure is expected is rejected. Match the constructor's own type, or construct the failure where its own name is expected. D-52.4 Returning a call that passes a loan-holding closure is rejected even when the result is a plain integer. None recorded. D-52.6 A class you declare with the same name as a prelude type, such as Task, is checked as the prelude's linear type. Name the class something the prelude does not declare. D-54.6 For a parameter named with a keyword, fortisc vm reports a generic syntax error and fortisc check reports the keyword-specific one. Rename the parameter. Both tools refuse the program. D-58.1 Map has no iterate operation. A for loop over a map yields key and value tuples, which the language reference does not state. Write for over the map when a key and a value together are enough. D-58.7 Map has no remove and no contains. None recorded. Build a new map without the key, or scan it. D-58.9 Every text accessor can fail, so a comparator written as a pure function of two texts must recover. Recover inside the comparator, or do not mark it pure. D-251 A variant whose name is also a class's is rejected by both checkers, so the program does not reach the VM. List.of(Red) with the list's element type written out runs on the interpreter, the VM and native. Write the variant with its enum prefix, or do not declare a class of the same name. The list form already runs when the expected type is written. D-252 Both checkers reject an optional-chained method call such as lights.get(0)?.code(). The call does not reach the VM. Match the optional and call the method on the present value. D-285 A package whose manifest the loader refuses (conformance/packages/bad.policy) makes fortis check, run and test exit 2 with a message on standard error, and fortisc check exit 1 with a names record on project.yaml. Both tools refuse the package. Read fortisc's record; it names the manifest. D-343 A property runs 25 generated cases with fortis test and 100 with fortisc test, from different generators, so a property that fails on few inputs can pass on one and fail on the other. Run properties with fortisc test as well, and turn a failure it finds into an example test. D-344 The executor trace of a native build has no drop event for an owned optional local, for a temporary a ?? fallback made, or for the bindings of a match arm that owns its scrutinee; the interpreter and the VM trace each, and all three trace a fallback a ?? moved into its result. Cleanup runs once on all three, and the output and the log records agree. Compare traces on the interpreter and the VM; the native trace leaves those drops out. D-363 A tuple that holds a loan, such as (xs.get(0), 1), cannot yet be reassigned to a let mut binding that holds one, or returned from a function declared to return one: both checkers reject it as a loan where an owned value is required, although the Language Reference allows both. The same with an optional, t = xs.get(1), is accepted. Keep the loan in a binding of its own (let t = xs.get(0)) and the other values beside it, and reassign or return those separately. D-365 A defer that changes a list, such as defer { let v = xs.pop() }, is rejected while a binding of the same block holds a loan from that list inside an optional (let t = xs.get(0)), although a deferred block runs after its block's own loans have ended. Put the loan in an inner block that ends before the defer's block does, or make the change after the block. D-400 A function that binds a loan of a type whose cleanup does I/O (let t = alias b.t, or a ?? whose fallback fails) is charged that cleanup's effects, so a function that destroys nothing may be refused as not pure or asked to declare io. De"},{"u":"docs/agent/known-limitations.html#valid-programs-the-checkers-reject-and-library-gaps","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"Valid programs the checkers reject, and library gaps","m":"agent","x":"s effects, so a function that destroys nothing may be refused as not pure or asked to declare io. Declare the effect, or keep such loans out of functions declared pure. D-401 A let mut cursor cannot be reassigned (the checkers report a move of a lent owner), although a cursor is owned by its binding ( LR 7.7 ). Make a new cursor in a binding of its own. D-402 After match move o of a binding that holds a loan (let o = xs.get(0)), the loan o held is not released, so the owner stays frozen for the rest of the block (xs.push is refused as a change under a live loan). New with this release's loan holders. Put the match and the binding in an inner block, so the loan ends with it."},{"u":"docs/agent/known-limitations.html#one-backend-refuses-the-program","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"One backend refuses the program","m":"agent","x":"One backend refuses the program # Defect What you meet Workaround D-54.10 A catch that returns inside a for is refused: fortisc test and fortis build exit 1, reporting that the typed IR uses a moved binding. The interpreter's test passes. A move in a returning branch, the other half of this entry, builds and its VM test passes. Run that test with fortis test. Keep the catch out of the loop, or bind the failure's value before the loop continues. D-55.6 A narrowed optional is not unwrapped in the native build. copy of a String? after != null, and a field read through a narrowed shared optional, each make fortis build exit 1 (rustc E0308, or no such field). The interpreter and the VM print the value. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-55.8 A dynamic fn in a list or a field does not build natively (rustc E0308 for the list, E0277 for the field). The interpreter and the VM run the programs. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-55.16 A local optional narrowed by != null and moved into a non-optional field is not unwrapped. fortis build exits 1 (rustc E0308). The interpreter and the VM print 7. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-156 A function-type parameter that owns its argument, fn(owned T), is lowered as a borrowed parameter. fortis build exits 1 (rustc E0631 when a named function is passed, E0308 when a lambda is). The interpreter and the VM run the call. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-185 A function whose result is an exclusive loan of its parameter, editable Cell from c, returning editable c (the Language Reference's own example in 7.6), does not build natively, nor does any returned exclusive loan: fortis build exits 1 (rustc E0308), because the returned loan is lowered as a read reference. The interpreter and the VM run it. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. Or change the value through the parameter inside the function and return nothing. D-157.1 A closure stored in a class field does not build natively (rustc E0277, the field's display). The interpreter and the VM run the program. Assigning a second lambda to one closure binding is a separate item and is rejected by both checkers. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. A dynamic fn is the heterogeneous form, and a field of that type is D-55.8, which also refuses natively. D-244 A type named like an item the generated Rust uses does not build natively: a class Vec, From or String, or a type alias String, in any program, Option beside an optional, and Box beside a recursive type (rustc E0107, E0404 and others). The interpreter and the VM run the program. Result, Rc, HashMap and the variant names Some, None, Ok and Err build. Rename the type, or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-245 Arithmetic on literals at a sized integer type, such as let x: Int32 = -2147483647 - 1 or let y: Int32 = 2 + 3, does not build natively: the literals are emitted as 64-bit integers (rustc E0308). The interpreter and the VM print the value. A single literal builds. Write the folded literal (-2147483648, 5), or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-270 A function declared to return one non-dynamic fn type may return two different lambdas. The interpreter and the VM run both (3 and 4). fortis build exits 1 (rustc E0308). Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. Return one lambda, or declare the result dynamic fn when the two bodies really differ. D-342 A test's expect that compares an optional with a literal, such as expect first(names) == \"a\", does not build natively (rustc E0614). fortis test and fortisc test pass the test. Bind the present value first (let got = first(names) ?? \"none\", then expect got == \"a\"), or run the test with fortis test or fortisc test. D-360 A record literal that holds a loan, such as { item: xs.get(0), n: 1 }, does not build natively (rustc E0106), nor does a tuple with a loan passed to a read parameter of tuple type (E0308). The interpreter and the VM run them, and each cleanup runs once. A tuple holding a list's loan, such as (xs.get(0), 1), o"},{"u":"docs/agent/known-limitations.html#one-backend-refuses-the-program","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"One backend refuses the program","m":"agent","x":"he VM run them, and each cleanup runs once. A tuple holding a list's loan, such as (xs.get(0), 1), or an alias loan of a local, such as (alias own, make(\"p\")), builds. A tuple bound first around a value of the function's own and returned as a from result (let t = (empty.get(0) ?? move d, 1), then return t from alias (Token, Int) from empty, which the Language Reference allows and the interpreter and the VM run correctly) does not build either (E0308), so a bearing aggregate from result has no spelling that builds natively yet. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. Or keep the loan in its own binding and pass it on its own. D-361 A binding that holds a loan on one path and a value of its own on another does not build natively (rustc E0308): an if that gives xs.get(0) on one branch and move d on the other, or assigning a new class value, which the binding then owns, to a let mut binding that held a loan. The interpreter and the VM run it, and the binding destroys only a value it owns. Run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. Or give the binding one kind of value: copy the loan, or keep the owned value in a binding of its own. D-353 A function whose result is an optional loan of text, such as (alias String from xs)? returning xs.get(0), does not build natively (rustc E0308). The interpreter and the VM run it. The same result of a class builds, except through a generic from function (D-387). Return an owned copy (let t = xs.get(0), then return copy t from a function returning String?), or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-387 A generic function whose result is an optional loan of its type parameter, such as func first[T](xs: List[T]) (alias T from xs)? => xs.get(0), does not build natively when it is instantiated at a class (rustc E0277). The interpreter and the VM run it. Write the function for the class (func first(xs: List[Token]) (alias Token from xs)?), which builds, or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-390 A byte string returned as the whole BytesView from xs result (=> b\"abc\"), or as a BytesView from static one, does not build natively (rustc E0515), nor does an if or match that gives a string literal on one branch and a view of a String through a loan on another (give \"empty\" beside give alias t.label, as StringView from t; rustc E0308), nor the outcome-expression spellings of that shape: or recover \"missing\" as a (alias StringView from xs)? result, or present(\"none\") in an if branch of one (rustc E0308). The interpreter and the VM run them. A string literal as the whole StringView from xs result builds. Return the literal from a function of its own, or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-415 An anonymous record returned by return r past a deferred block that reads it (let r = { n: 5, m: 8 }, defer { console.print(\"bye ${r.n}\") }, return r) does not build natively (rustc E0382), whatever its field types: the record is moved by the return, where a trivial class, a tuple or an enum is copied. Both checkers accept it and the interpreter and the VM run it. Use a class or a tuple for the value, or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-382 A marker written on a call of a module function, such as let t = alias first(alias xs), passes both checkers and runs on the VM and natively, but the interpreter stops with a dynamic failure (first is not declared). The marker belongs on the argument alone. Write let t = first(alias xs): the call lends what its result declares, and no marker is needed on the call. D-396 first() on a list whose element is a class, such as xs.first() on List[Token], does not build natively (rustc E0277, the element is not Clone). The interpreter and the VM run it. Use xs.get(0), which builds, or run it with fortis run or fortisc vm. D-397 I/O through a capability held in a local's field (let d = make(console, ...); d.console.print(...)) passes both checkers and runs on the interpreter, but the VM's IR validation refuses it (the receiver is not derived from a parameter through places) and native co"},{"u":"docs/agent/known-limitations.html#one-backend-refuses-the-program","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"One backend refuses the program","m":"agent","x":"IR validation refuses it (the receiver is not derived from a parameter through places) and native code reports that refusal as an internal compiler defect. Print through the capability parameter itself, or a binding made from it directly. D-399 Array.filled with its size taken from the expected type, such as let arr: Array[Int, 3] = Array.filled(7), passes both checkers, and every backend then refuses it. Write the size on the call: Array[Int, 3].filled(7). D-354 An owned optional local of a class with cleanup, written without a marker on the left of a ?? that moves its fallback (let p: Token? = none ?? move d), does not build natively (rustc E0382). The interpreter and the VM run it. Write move none ?? move d, which builds and runs alike on every backend."},{"u":"docs/agent/known-limitations.html#defects-carried-from-v001","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"Defects carried from v0.0.1","m":"agent","x":"Defects carried from v0.0.1 # The checkers accept each of these programs although the Language Reference rejects it, so a checked program can still go wrong. v0.0.1 had every one of them; v0.0.2 is no worse, and each is fixed in the release its record names. Avoid the shape, as the second column says. Defect What you meet What to do D-352 Inside a generic function, a loan can be returned or stored as if it were owned: func id[T](x: T) T { return x } and func first[T](xs: List[T]) T? { return xs.get(0) } are accepted, and the caller then holds a second reference to the argument or the list's element. Write the function for the concrete type, where the checkers require copy or reject the loan. D-355 A loan given out of an if or match branch (give xs.get(0)), or lent by a match's scrutinee that is a temporary, such as match xs.get(0), ends with its statement, so the list can be changed while the loan is still in use. Bind the loan with let first (let t = xs.get(0)) and branch or match on t: a let binding holds the loan to the end of its block. D-356 A field read through an optional loan with ?., such as xs.get(0)?.inner, is typed as owned, so a binding of it can change the list's own element on the interpreter and the VM. Treat a value read with ?. through a loan as read-only: do not change it, or a binding made from it. To change a value of your own, build a new one. D-357 An owned optional binding written without a marker on the left of a ?? whose result is owned, as in let p: Token? = some ?? move d, is accepted, so p and some both own the value. Write move some ?? move d, or copy some ?? move d. D-362 A loan held inside an optional by a let mut binding (let mut t = xs.get(0)) is treated as ended after a while or for loop whose body ends it (t = null), although a loop that runs no times, a break or a continue skips that, so the list can be changed while the loan lives. End the loan before the loop (t = null), or keep the loan in a block that ends before the list changes. D-364 A defer that reads a binding of its block holding a loan inside an optional (let t = xs.get(0), then defer { ... t ... }) is accepted, although a deferred block may not use a binding of its block that holds a loan. Read what the deferred block needs before it, into a value of its own (copy), and use that in the defer. D-369 A binding of a ?? whose fallback it owns, such as let t = empty.get(0) ?? move d, is typed as a loan of the list, so it can be stored in a binding of an outer block (keep = present(t)) and read there after the fallback's cleanup has run. Keep such a binding in its own block, or move the fallback into a binding of its own first (let f = move d) and use that. D-370 On the interpreter, an unmarked copy of such a binding, or of a tuple element holding such a value (let a = t, let a = pair.0), becomes a second owner, so the fallback's cleanup runs when the copy ends, before the binding does. Write copy t (or alias t) to say which you mean. D-375 A ?? whose fallback is the statement's own value, lent to a read parameter (show(console, empty.get(0) ?? move d)), is never destroyed on the interpreter or the VM, so its cleanup does not run; native code destroys it when the statement ends. Bind the fallback first (let v = empty.get(0) ?? move d) and pass v, so the binding's end runs its cleanup. D-376 On the interpreter, assigning to a field of a record literal (rec.side = make(...), or a loan over a field that held a value of the record's own) does not run the old value's cleanup, and a loan assigned over such a field is cleaned up with the record while its owner still holds it. The VM runs each cleanup once, at its owner's end. Use a class for a value whose fields are reassigned, or run the program on the VM. D-379 A fn once closure may return a loan from a move capture (fn once[move t]() Token from t { return move t }), which the consuming call destroys: both checkers accept it, the interpreter and the VM keep the value until the receiving bi"},{"u":"docs/agent/known-limitations.html#defects-carried-from-v001","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"Defects carried from v0.0.1","m":"agent","x":" destroys: both checkers accept it, the interpreter and the VM keep the value until the receiving binding ends or read it destroyed, and native code refuses the build. Return the captured value itself from a closure returning the owned type, or let the caller keep the binding and lend it to the closure instead of moving it in. D-388 A tuple built around a value of the function's own (let t = (empty.get(0) ?? move d, 1)), bound first and returned inside present as a from result, passes both checkers; the interpreter destroys the value at the call and the caller then reads it destroyed, and the VM never runs its cleanup. Return the owned value from a function returning the owned type, or keep the fallback in a binding of its own (let f = move d) that outlives the result. D-389 Inside a returned match arm, an if giving a loan on one branch and the function's own value on the other (let v = if flag { give x } else { give move d }; give v) passes both checkers, and both running backends destroy the moved value at the call, so later reads see it destroyed. Give every branch a loan, or return the owned value from a function returning the owned type. D-392 A loan taken from a temporary receiver, such as let t = fresh().get(0) (also .first(), ?? make(...), or a cursor of a temporary), passes both checkers; the temporary is destroyed at the statement's end and t then reads the destroyed element on the interpreter and the VM; native refuses the build. Bind the temporary first (let xs = fresh()) and take the loan from the binding. D-393 A pure function may print through a dropped value's cleanup: the io of a cleanup is not attributed to the function that drops the value, so both checkers accept it and every backend prints. Do not drop a value whose cleanup does I/O inside a function declared pure; give the value to the caller. D-394 A lent match payload (match xs.get(0) { present(x) => ... }) is destroyed at the arm's end on the interpreter, and on the VM for a plain optional, rather than at its owner's end, so a read of the element after the match sees it destroyed; native is right. Read what you need inside the arm, or bind the loan first (let t = xs.get(0)) and match on t. D-395 A result declared from one parameter (from xs) that lends another (ys) is rejected for a direct return but not through give, a match expression or an expression body, so the loan is recorded against the wrong owner and ys can change while the result lives. Declare the result from the parameter it lends, and return it directly. D-413 On the VM, an owned value recovered by a catch expression whose result the statement discards (discard (g() catch e { report e; recover make(...) })) is never destroyed, so its cleanup does not run; the interpreter and native code run it. The or recover form, and a catch whose value a let binds, run it on the VM too. Bind the catch's value (let x = g() catch e { ... }), so the binding's end runs the cleanup, or write or recover. D-414 An expect outside a test body, in an ordinary function or in a deferred block, is accepted by both checkers, and a failing one has no defined outcome: the interpreter stops with a Python traceback and writes no record, the VM reports a dynamic check failure, and native code does not build the program. Write expect in test bodies only. In a function, test the condition with if, and fail or return."},{"u":"docs/agent/known-limitations.html#what-v002-leaves-for-later","p":"Known limitations of v0.0.2","h":"What v0.0.2 leaves for later","m":"agent","x":"What v0.0.2 leaves for later # Defect What you meet What to do The older forms are accepted alongside the new ones, so a reader may meet both until the code is migrated. Run fortis fmt --write on the code. It rewrites each older form where the program means the same. D-404 fortis fmt never rewrites Int.shiftLeft(a, b) or fail to a << b or fail, although the two are the same operation; Int.add, sub, mul, div and rem are rewritten. Write the operator form by hand, or leave the named call. The two mean the same. fortis fmt does not rewrite a logging call, add @ignorable or derive Default, or rewrite a main that reads its Capabilities record other than field by field (a capability inside an interpolation, or the record moved whole). Make those changes by hand. Each is the author's choice. D-406 fortis fmt keeps a pure or noalloc written on a lambda, although the function type the lambda is passed to already states it; it prints no inferred one. Remove the modifier by hand if you want the shorter form. It changes nothing the checker decides. Checked operators cover Int only. The sized integer types (Int32, UInt8 and the rest) have no checked, wrapping or saturating operations yet; they are planned for v0.4. Give the operands a refined type, or test them first, so that the operation is proved. Or compute in Int. fortis fmt prints a module's inferred facts back only when the module checks on its own, so a module of a package that needs the rest of its package to check is not given them. Nothing changes in what the program means; the checker infers the facts either way. Write a fact by hand where you want it shown."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#the-core-library","p":"The core library","h":"The core library","m":"agent","x":"The core library # Both modes. Generated by conformance/tools/libref.py from the declarations of library/fortis ; do not edit. Source of truth: LR 15 for the inventory, and each declaration's own contract. Preview: the core runtime inventory ( LR 15 ) arrives in v0.4. Every public declaration of the seven core modules ( LR 15 ): each exported declaration, every member of an exported interface (whose members share its visibility, LR 4.4 ) and every variant of an exported enum, with its signature as the library declares it and the contract its documentation comment states. The check counts these against the module sources independently of this page. A trusted operation is implemented by the toolchain, and its comment is its review record ( LR 11.3 ). fortis.prelude is imported into every module; the other modules need an import ( LR 4.3 ). What is not listed here does not exist in Language 1.0's core library; the gaps against the Language Reference are recorded as D-58."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#fortisprelude","p":"The core library","h":"fortis.prelude","m":"agent","x":"fortis.prelude # Imported into every module: the built-in types, the optional and outcome operations, the built-in interfaces ( LR 4.3 , LR 6.2 , LR 6.10 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-void","p":"The core library","h":"class Void","m":"agent","x":"class Void #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-never","p":"The core library","h":"class Never","m":"agent","x":"class Never #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-bool","p":"The core library","h":"class Bool","m":"agent","x":"class Bool #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-int","p":"The core library","h":"class Int","m":"agent","x":"class Int # Declaration Contract trusted pure try func add(a: Int, b: Int) Int checked 64-bit two's-complement addition; fails numeric.overflow outside [-2^63, 2^63-1]; O(1), no allocation trusted pure try func sub(a: Int, b: Int) Int checked subtraction; fails numeric.overflow ; O(1), no allocation trusted pure try func mul(a: Int, b: Int) Int checked multiplication; fails numeric.overflow ; O(1), no allocation trusted pure try func div(a: Int, b: Int) Int truncating division; fails numeric.divide_by_zero when b = 0 and numeric.overflow for -2^63 / -1; O(1) trusted pure try func rem(a: Int, b: Int) Int remainder with the sign of a; fails numeric.divide_by_zero when b = 0; O(1) trusted pure try func shiftLeft(a: Int, bits: Int) Int a * 2^bits; fails numeric.shift_range unless 0 <= bits < 64 and numeric.overflow when the result is out of range; O(1) trusted pure func wrappingAdd(a: Int, b: Int) Int addition modulo 2^64 into [-2^63, 2^63-1]; total; O(1) trusted pure func wrappingSub(a: Int, b: Int) Int subtraction modulo 2^64; total; O(1) trusted pure func wrappingMul(a: Int, b: Int) Int multiplication modulo 2^64; total; O(1) trusted pure func saturatingAdd(a: Int, b: Int) Int addition clamped to [-2^63, 2^63-1]; total; O(1) trusted pure func saturatingSub(a: Int, b: Int) Int subtraction clamped to [-2^63, 2^63-1]; total; O(1) trusted pure func saturatingMul(a: Int, b: Int) Int multiplication clamped to [-2^63, 2^63-1]; total; O(1) trusted pure try func validate(value: Int) Int the value when it lies in the refinement of the receiver type; fails refinement.out_of_range otherwise; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-int8","p":"The core library","h":"class Int8","m":"agent","x":"class Int8 #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-int16","p":"The core library","h":"class Int16","m":"agent","x":"class Int16 #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-int32","p":"The core library","h":"class Int32","m":"agent","x":"class Int32 #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-int64","p":"The core library","h":"class Int64","m":"agent","x":"class Int64 #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-uint8","p":"The core library","h":"class UInt8","m":"agent","x":"class UInt8 #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-uint16","p":"The core library","h":"class UInt16","m":"agent","x":"class UInt16 #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-uint32","p":"The core library","h":"class UInt32","m":"agent","x":"class UInt32 #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-uint64","p":"The core library","h":"class UInt64","m":"agent","x":"class UInt64 #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-uint","p":"The core library","h":"class UInt","m":"agent","x":"class UInt #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-byte","p":"The core library","h":"class Byte","m":"agent","x":"class Byte #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-float","p":"The core library","h":"class Float","m":"agent","x":"class Float # Declaration Contract trusted pure func sqrt(x: Float) Float IEEE 754 binary64 correctly rounded square root; NaN for negative input; O(1) trusted pure func totalOrder(x: Float) Int64 the IEEE 754 totalOrder key of x as a signed 64-bit integer; O(1) trusted pure func bitsEqual(a: Float, b: Float) Bool true exactly when a and b have the same 64-bit pattern; O(1) trusted pure func isNaN(x: Float) Bool true exactly for NaN; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-float32","p":"The core library","h":"class Float32","m":"agent","x":"class Float32 # Declaration Contract trusted pure func sqrt(x: Float32) Float32 IEEE 754 binary32 correctly rounded square root; O(1) trusted pure func isNaN(x: Float32) Bool true exactly for NaN; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#enum-roundingmode","p":"The core library","h":"enum RoundingMode","m":"agent","x":"enum RoundingMode # Variants: HalfEven , HalfAwayFromZero , TowardZero , AwayFromZero ."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-decimal","p":"The core library","h":"class Decimal","m":"agent","x":"class Decimal # Declaration Contract trusted pure try func add(a: Decimal, b: Decimal) Decimal the sum at the larger operand scale, rounded half to even when it needs more than 96 coefficient bits; fails numeric.overflow when the integer part does not fit (6.4); O(digits) trusted pure try func sub(a: Decimal, b: Decimal) Decimal the difference at the larger operand scale, rounded half to even when needed; fails numeric.overflow (6.4); O(digits) trusted pure try func mul(a: Decimal, b: Decimal) Decimal the product at the sum of the scales, rounded half to even above 28 fractional digits or 96 coefficient bits; fails numeric.overflow (6.4); O(digits^2) trusted pure try func div(a: Decimal, b: Decimal) Decimal the quotient at the largest scale that fits, rounded half to even; fails numeric.divide_by_zero and numeric.overflow (6.4); O(digits^2) trusted pure try func rem(a: Decimal, b: Decimal) Decimal the remainder with the sign of the dividend at the larger operand scale; fails numeric.divide_by_zero (6.4); O(digits^2) trusted pure func round(value: Decimal, scale: Int<0..28>, mode: RoundingMode) Decimal value rounded to scale by mode; total; O(digits) trusted pure try func fromFloat(x: Float, scale: Int<0..28>, mode: RoundingMode) Decimal the binary value of x rounded to scale by mode; fails numeric.overflow for a non-finite x or an integer part that does not fit (6.4); O(digits) trusted pure func scale(self) Int<0..28> the number of fractional digits; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-bigint","p":"The core library","h":"class BigInt","m":"agent","x":"class BigInt #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-string","p":"The core library","h":"class String","m":"agent","x":"class String # Declaration Contract trusted pure func from(text: StringView) String an owned copy of the view; allocates; O(n) in bytes pure func empty() String \u2014 trusted pure func scalarCount(self) Int the number of Unicode scalar values; O(n) in bytes trusted pure func byteCount(self) Int the UTF-8 length in bytes; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-stringview","p":"The core library","h":"class StringView","m":"agent","x":"class StringView # Declaration Contract trusted pure func scalarCount(self) Int the number of Unicode scalar values; O(n) in bytes trusted pure func byteCount(self) Int the UTF-8 length in bytes; O(1) trusted pure try func slice(self, start: Int, end: Int) StringView the scalars [start, end) as a view of the same owner; fails text.slice_bounds unless 0 <= start <= end <= scalarCount; O(n) in bytes, no allocation trusted pure try func scalarAt(self, index: Int) Scalar the scalar at index; fails text.index past the end; O(n) in bytes"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-bytes","p":"The core library","h":"class Bytes","m":"agent","x":"class Bytes # Declaration Contract trusted pure func byteCount(self) Int the length in bytes; O(1) trusted pure func get(self, index: Int) Byte? the byte at index, or null outside [0, byteCount); never allocates; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-bytesview","p":"The core library","h":"class BytesView","m":"agent","x":"class BytesView # Declaration Contract trusted pure func byteCount(self) Int the length in bytes; O(1) trusted pure func count(self) Int the length in bytes (same as byteCount); O(1) trusted pure func get(self, index: Int) Byte? the byte at index, or null outside [0, byteCount); never allocates; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-scalar","p":"The core library","h":"class Scalar","m":"agent","x":"class Scalar # Declaration Contract trusted pure func of(text: StringView) Scalar the single scalar of a one-scalar view; a constant expression for a literal; O(1) trusted pure func digitValue(s: Scalar) Int<0..9>? the value 0-9 of an ASCII decimal digit, null otherwise; O(1) trusted pure func hexValue(s: Scalar) Int<0..15>? the value 0-15 of an ASCII hexadecimal digit (either case), null otherwise; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-duration","p":"The core library","h":"class Duration","m":"agent","x":"class Duration # Declaration Contract trusted pure func days(n: Int<0..>) Duration n * 86400 * 10^9 nanoseconds; O(1) (n bounded by the 64-bit count) trusted pure func hours(n: Int<0..>) Duration n * 3600 * 10^9 nanoseconds; O(1) trusted pure func minutes(n: Int<0..>) Duration n * 60 * 10^9 nanoseconds; O(1) trusted pure func seconds(n: Int<0..>) Duration n * 10^9 nanoseconds; O(1) trusted pure func milliseconds(n: Int<0..>) Duration n * 10^6 nanoseconds; O(1) trusted pure try func add(a: Duration, b: Duration) Duration sum of the signed 64-bit nanosecond counts; fails numeric.overflow ; O(1) trusted pure try func sub(a: Duration, b: Duration) Duration difference of the nanosecond counts; fails numeric.overflow ; O(1) trusted pure try func multiply(d: Duration, factor: Int) Duration the count times factor; fails numeric.overflow ; O(1) trusted pure func nanoseconds(self) Int the signed 64-bit nanosecond count; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-instant","p":"The core library","h":"class Instant","m":"agent","x":"class Instant #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-timestamp","p":"The core library","h":"class Timestamp","m":"agent","x":"class Timestamp # Declaration Contract trusted pure func epoch() Timestamp 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z as a signed 64-bit nanosecond count of 0; O(1) trusted pure try func add(t: Timestamp, d: Duration) Timestamp t + d in nanoseconds since the epoch; fails numeric.overflow ; O(1) trusted pure func after(a: Timestamp, b: Timestamp) Bool a is strictly later than b; O(1) trusted pure func before(a: Timestamp, b: Timestamp) Bool a is strictly earlier than b; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-listt","p":"The core library","h":"class List[T]","m":"agent","x":"class List[T] # Declaration Contract trusted pure func default() List[T] an empty list with no allocation until the first push; O(1) trusted pure func of(...items: owned T) List[T] a list holding the arguments in order; allocates once; O(n) trusted pure func count(self) Int the number of elements; O(1) trusted func push(editable self, item: owned T) appends item; amortised O(1) (capacity doubles), worst case O(n) for the growth step trusted func pop(editable self) T? removes and returns the last element, null when empty; O(1) trusted pure func get(self, index: Int) (alias T from self)? a read loan of the element at index, null outside [0, count); O(1) trusted pure try func at(self, index: Int) T the element at index; fails collection.index outside [0, count); O(1) trusted try func set(editable self, index: Int, value: owned T) replaces the element at index, dropping the old value; fails collection.index ; O(1) pure func isEmpty(self) Bool \u2014 pure func first(self) (alias T from self)? \u2014 pure func reversed(self) List[T] where T implements Copyable \u2014 pure func concat(self, other: ListView[T]) List[T] where T implements Copyable \u2014 pure func countWhere(self, keep: pure fn(T) Bool) Int \u2014 pure func map[U](self, f: pure fn(T) U) List[U] \u2014 pure func filter(self, keep: pure fn(T) Bool) List[T] where T implements Copyable \u2014 trusted pure func iterate(self) ListCursor[T] from self a cursor over read loans of the elements in order; O(1) to create, O(1) per step trusted func consumeEach(owned self, visit: fn mut(owned T) Void) consumes the list (10.7): no early exit, so no element is left unvisited; an empty list makes no calls intrinsic: calls visit once per element in order, transferring each element out; O(n) plus the callback per element"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-listcursort-implements-iteratort","p":"The core library","h":"class ListCursor[T] implements Iterator[T]","m":"agent","x":"class ListCursor[T] implements Iterator[T] # Declaration Contract trusted pure func next(editable self) (alias T from self)? a read loan of the next element, null at the end; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-listviewt","p":"The core library","h":"class ListView[T]","m":"agent","x":"class ListView[T] # Declaration Contract trusted pure func count(self) Int the number of elements; O(1) trusted pure func get(self, index: Int) (alias T from self)? a read loan of the element at index, null outside [0, count); O(1) pure func isEmpty(self) Bool \u2014 pure func reversed(self) List[T] where T implements Copyable \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-arrayt-n","p":"The core library","h":"class Array[T, N]","m":"agent","x":"class Array[T, N] # Declaration Contract trusted pure func filled(value: T) Array[T, N] N copies of value; O(N), inline storage trusted pure func count(self) Int N; O(1) trusted func set(editable self, index: Int, value: owned T) replaces the element at index; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-mapk-v","p":"The core library","h":"class Map[K, V]","m":"agent","x":"class Map[K, V] # Declaration Contract trusted pure func default() Map[K, V] an empty map; O(1) trusted pure func get(self, key: K) (alias V from self)? a read loan of the value for key, null when absent (on a Map[String, V] the key is a StringView, which hashes and compares as the String with its text: LR 6.7 , X-345); expected O(1) under the release's fixed hash function trusted func set(editable self, key: owned K, value: owned V) inserts or replaces the value for key, dropping a replaced value; amortised expected O(1) trusted pure func count(self) Int the number of keys; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-orderedmapk-v","p":"The core library","h":"class OrderedMap[K, V]","m":"agent","x":"class OrderedMap[K, V] #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-sett","p":"The core library","h":"class Set[T]","m":"agent","x":"class Set[T] # Declaration Contract trusted pure func default() Set[T] an empty set; O(1) trusted func add(editable self, item: owned T) Bool inserts item, true when it was absent; amortised expected O(1) trusted pure func contains(self, item: T) Bool membership; expected O(1) trusted pure func count(self) Int the number of items; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-fixedlistt-n","p":"The core library","h":"class FixedList[T, N]","m":"agent","x":"class FixedList[T, N] #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-fixedmapk-v-n","p":"The core library","h":"class FixedMap[K, V, N]","m":"agent","x":"class FixedMap[K, V, N] #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-variantmape-v","p":"The core library","h":"class VariantMap[E, V]","m":"agent","x":"class VariantMap[E, V] #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-must","p":"The core library","h":"class Must","m":"agent","x":"class Must # Declaration Contract trusted pure func create() Must a zero-sized linear witness; O(1), no allocation trusted pure func discharge(m: owned Must) consumes the witness; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-outcomet","p":"The core library","h":"class Outcome[T]","m":"agent","x":"class Outcome[T] # Declaration Contract trusted pure func success(value: owned T) Outcome[T] an outcome holding value; O(1) trusted pure func failure(f: owned Failure) Outcome[T] an outcome holding f; O(1) trusted pure try func collect(list: owned List[Outcome[T]]) List[T] the successes in order when every element succeeds, otherwise the first failure in order, consuming every other element's obligation; O(n)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-failure","p":"The core library","h":"class Failure","m":"agent","x":"class Failure #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-report","p":"The core library","h":"class Report","m":"agent","x":"class Report #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-failures","p":"The core library","h":"class Failures","m":"agent","x":"class Failures # Declaration Contract trusted pure func aggregate(list: owned List[Failure]) Failure one failure with code failure.aggregate whose cause chain is the list in order; O(n)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-futuret","p":"The core library","h":"class Future[T]","m":"agent","x":"class Future[T] #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-taskt-implements-abandonable","p":"The core library","h":"class Task[T] implements Abandonable","m":"agent","x":"class Task[T] implements Abandonable # Declaration Contract trusted func abandon(owned self) cancels the task and waits for it to finish (12.4); its result is consumed by the scope"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-cancellation","p":"The core library","h":"class Cancellation","m":"agent","x":"class Cancellation #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-taskdefect","p":"The core library","h":"class TaskDefect","m":"agent","x":"class TaskDefect #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#enum-taskcompletiont","p":"The core library","h":"enum TaskCompletion[T]","m":"agent","x":"enum TaskCompletion[T] # Variants: Completed(value: T) , Cancelled(reason: Cancellation) , Defect(info: TaskDefect) ."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-sharedt","p":"The core library","h":"class Shared[T]","m":"agent","x":"class Shared[T] # Declaration Contract trusted pure func create(value: owned T) Shared[T] a reference-counted owner holding value, strong count 1; allocates once; O(1) trusted pure func retain(self) Shared[T] a new strong handle, strong count + 1; O(1) trusted pure func downgrade(self) Weak[T] a weak handle to the same value; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-weakt","p":"The core library","h":"class Weak[T]","m":"agent","x":"class Weak[T] # Declaration Contract trusted func upgrade(self) Shared[T]? a strong handle when the value is alive, null after the last strong handle was dropped; O(1) trusted pure func retain(self) Weak[T] a new weak handle; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-mutext","p":"The core library","h":"class Mutex[T]","m":"agent","x":"class Mutex[T] # Declaration Contract trusted pure func create(value: owned T) Mutex[T] a mutex owning value; O(1) trusted async try func lock(self) MutexGuard[T] from self an exclusive guard over the value, released when the guard is dropped, suspending until the lock is free; fails concurrency.poisoned if a holder was terminated by a defect (unreachable on the interpreter, where a defect ends the program) trusted try func lockBlocking(self) MutexGuard[T] from self an exclusive guard over the value, released when the guard is dropped, blocking the thread until free (the block effect); fails concurrency.poisoned"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-mutexguardt","p":"The core library","h":"class MutexGuard[T]","m":"agent","x":"class MutexGuard[T] # Declaration Contract trusted pure func get(self) T where T implements Copyable a copy of the protected value, read through the exclusive guard (12.7); allocates when the value owns heap storage trusted func set(self, value: owned T) replaces the protected value with value and drops the old one; a write to storage shared between tasks ( mutate , 11.1) trusted func replace(self, value: owned T) T replaces the protected value with value and returns the old one, which the caller now owns; a write to storage shared between tasks ( mutate , 11.1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-atomicint","p":"The core library","h":"class AtomicInt","m":"agent","x":"class AtomicInt # Declaration Contract trusted pure func create(value: Int) AtomicInt an atomic integer holding value; O(1) trusted pure func load(self) Int the current value (seqcst); O(1) trusted func store(self, value: Int) replaces the value (seqcst); O(1) trusted func fetchAdd(self, delta: Int) Int adds delta with wrapping and returns the previous value (seqcst); O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-atomicbool","p":"The core library","h":"class AtomicBool","m":"agent","x":"class AtomicBool # Declaration Contract trusted pure func create(value: Bool) AtomicBool an atomic boolean holding value; O(1) trusted pure func load(self) Bool the current value (seqcst); O(1) trusted func store(self, value: Bool) replaces the value (seqcst); O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-scope","p":"The core library","h":"class Scope","m":"agent","x":"class Scope # Declaration Contract trusted func launch[T](self, work: owned Future[T]) Task[T] a task running work on the cooperative executor of this scope (12.5); O(1) trusted func launchParallel[T](self, work: owned Future[T]) Task[T] a task running work on the parallel pool (12.5); the environment is Shareable; O(1) trusted func launchBlocking[T](self, work: owned fn once() T) Task[T] a task running work on the bounded blocking pool (12.5); O(1) trusted func detach[T](self, task: owned Task[T], supervisor: Supervisor) removes task from the scope and hands its join obligation to supervisor (12.5); O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-observationschema","p":"The core library","h":"class ObservationSchema","m":"agent","x":"class ObservationSchema #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-hasher","p":"The core library","h":"class Hasher","m":"agent","x":"class Hasher #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#enum-ordering-derive-equatable","p":"The core library","h":"enum Ordering derive Equatable","m":"agent","x":"enum Ordering derive Equatable # Variants: Less , Equal , Greater ."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-encoder","p":"The core library","h":"class Encoder","m":"agent","x":"class Encoder #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-decoder","p":"The core library","h":"class Decoder","m":"agent","x":"class Decoder #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-equatable","p":"The core library","h":"interface Equatable","m":"agent","x":"interface Equatable # Declaration Contract pure func equals(self, other: Self) Bool \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-hashable-requires-equatable","p":"The core library","h":"interface Hashable requires Equatable","m":"agent","x":"interface Hashable requires Equatable # Declaration Contract pure func hash(self, into: editable Hasher) \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-comparable-requires-equatable","p":"The core library","h":"interface Comparable requires Equatable","m":"agent","x":"interface Comparable requires Equatable # Declaration Contract pure func compare(self, other: Self) Ordering \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-displayable","p":"The core library","h":"interface Displayable","m":"agent","x":"interface Displayable # Declaration Contract pure func display(self, into: editable Builder) \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-iterablet-i-where-i-implements-iteratort","p":"The core library","h":"interface Iterable[T, I] where I implements Iterator[T]","m":"agent","x":"interface Iterable[T, I] where I implements Iterator[T] # Declaration Contract pure func iterate(self) I from self \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-iteratort","p":"The core library","h":"interface Iterator[T]","m":"agent","x":"interface Iterator[T] # Declaration Contract pure func next(editable self) (alias T from self)? \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-consumablet-i-where-i-implements-consumert","p":"The core library","h":"interface Consumable[T, I] where I implements Consumer[T]","m":"agent","x":"interface Consumable[T, I] where I implements Consumer[T] # Declaration Contract func consume(owned self) I \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-consumert","p":"The core library","h":"interface Consumer[T]","m":"agent","x":"interface Consumer[T] # Declaration Contract func next(editable self) T? \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-copyable","p":"The core library","h":"interface Copyable","m":"agent","x":"interface Copyable #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-shareable","p":"The core library","h":"interface Shareable","m":"agent","x":"interface Shareable #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-abandonable","p":"The core library","h":"interface Abandonable","m":"agent","x":"interface Abandonable # Declaration Contract func abandon(owned self) \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-default","p":"The core library","h":"interface Default","m":"agent","x":"interface Default # Declaration Contract pure func default() Self \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-encodable","p":"The core library","h":"interface Encodable","m":"agent","x":"interface Encodable # Declaration Contract pure func encode(self, into: editable Encoder) \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-decodable","p":"The core library","h":"interface Decodable","m":"agent","x":"interface Decodable # Declaration Contract try func decode(source: editable Decoder) Self \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-codec-requires-encodable-decodable","p":"The core library","h":"interface Codec requires Encodable, Decodable","m":"agent","x":"interface Codec requires Encodable, Decodable #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-authority","p":"The core library","h":"interface Authority","m":"agent","x":"interface Authority #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#interface-froms","p":"The core library","h":"interface From[S]","m":"agent","x":"interface From[S] # Declaration Contract func from(source: S) Self \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-builder","p":"The core library","h":"class Builder","m":"agent","x":"class Builder # Declaration Contract trusted pure func create() Builder an empty builder; O(1) trusted func append(editable self, text: StringView) appends the view; amortised O(len) trusted func appendLine(editable self, text: StringView) appends the view and a line feed; amortised O(len) trusted func appendScalar(editable self, s: Scalar) appends one scalar; amortised O(1) trusted pure func finish(self) String the accumulated text as an owned String; O(total length)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#functions-and-aspects","p":"The core library","h":"Functions and aspects","m":"agent","x":"Functions and aspects # Declaration Contract trusted pure func annotate(f: owned Failure, note: StringView) Failure f with note added to its annotation trail; the result carries the single obligation of f; O(1) amortised trusted async func join[A, B](a: owned Future[A], b: owned Future[B]) (A, B) runs both futures to completion inside the parent and returns both results (12.5) trusted func checkpoint() a cancellation point for parallel and blocking tasks (12.4); no effect in cooperative tasks trusted func injectDefect(kind: StringView) Never the conformance corpus's fault-injection hook ( LR 10.8 ); not part of the language aspect observe(schema: ObservationSchema) \u2014 aspect ignorable() the core aspect @ignorable (5.5, 11.8; X-345): callers may drop the function's non-linear result without discard"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#fortistext","p":"The core library","h":"fortis.text","m":"agent","x":"fortis.text # Text building, scalars and graphemes ( LR 6.6 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-builder-1","p":"The core library","h":"class Builder","m":"agent","x":"class Builder # Declaration Contract trusted pure func create() Builder an empty builder; O(1) trusted func append(editable self, text: StringView) appends the view; amortised O(len) trusted func appendLine(editable self, text: StringView) appends the view and a line feed; amortised O(len) trusted func appendScalar(editable self, s: Scalar) appends one scalar; amortised O(1) trusted pure func finish(self) String the accumulated text as an owned String; O(total length)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-scalar-1","p":"The core library","h":"class Scalar","m":"agent","x":"class Scalar # Declaration Contract trusted pure func of(text: StringView) Scalar the single scalar of a one-scalar view; a constant expression for a literal; O(1) trusted pure func digitValue(s: Scalar) Int<0..9>? the value 0-9 of an ASCII decimal digit, null otherwise; O(1) trusted pure func hexValue(s: Scalar) Int<0..15>? the value 0-15 of an ASCII hexadecimal digit (either case), null otherwise; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-text","p":"The core library","h":"class Text","m":"agent","x":"class Text # Declaration Contract trusted pure func words(text: StringView) List[String] the maximal runs of non-whitespace scalars (Unicode White_Space), in order, as owned strings; allocates; O(n)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#functions-and-aspects-1","p":"The core library","h":"Functions and aspects","m":"agent","x":"Functions and aspects # Declaration Contract trusted pure func graphemes(text: StringView) Int the number of extended grapheme clusters (UAX #29, the rules GB3-GB9c and GB11-GB13 over combining marks, ZWJ sequences and regional-indicator pairs; Hangul syllable rules GB6-GB8 are not yet implemented); O(n)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#fortiscollections","p":"The core library","h":"fortis.collections","m":"agent","x":"fortis.collections # Collection algorithms ( LR 6.7 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#functions-and-aspects-2","p":"The core library","h":"Functions and aspects","m":"agent","x":"Functions and aspects # Declaration Contract pure func sorted(items: ListView[Int]) List[Int] Bottom-up merge sort: stable, O(n log n) comparisons and copies, O(n) extra space."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#fortisnumeric","p":"The core library","h":"fortis.numeric","m":"agent","x":"fortis.numeric # Integer helpers ( LR 6.4 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#functions-and-aspects-3","p":"The core library","h":"Functions and aspects","m":"agent","x":"Functions and aspects # Declaration Contract pure func abs(value: Int) Int<0..> \u2014 pure func min(a: Int, b: Int) Int \u2014 pure func max(a: Int, b: Int) Int \u2014"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#fortistime","p":"The core library","h":"fortis.time","m":"agent","x":"fortis.time # Durations and timestamps ( LR 15 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-duration-1","p":"The core library","h":"class Duration","m":"agent","x":"class Duration # Declaration Contract trusted pure func days(n: Int<0..>) Duration n * 86400 * 10^9 nanoseconds; O(1) (n bounded by the 64-bit count) trusted pure func hours(n: Int<0..>) Duration n * 3600 * 10^9 nanoseconds; O(1) trusted pure func minutes(n: Int<0..>) Duration n * 60 * 10^9 nanoseconds; O(1) trusted pure func seconds(n: Int<0..>) Duration n * 10^9 nanoseconds; O(1) trusted pure func milliseconds(n: Int<0..>) Duration n * 10^6 nanoseconds; O(1) trusted pure try func add(a: Duration, b: Duration) Duration sum of the signed 64-bit nanosecond counts; fails numeric.overflow ; O(1) trusted pure try func sub(a: Duration, b: Duration) Duration difference of the nanosecond counts; fails numeric.overflow ; O(1) trusted pure try func multiply(d: Duration, factor: Int) Duration the count times factor; fails numeric.overflow ; O(1) trusted pure func nanoseconds(self) Int the signed 64-bit nanosecond count; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-instant-1","p":"The core library","h":"class Instant","m":"agent","x":"class Instant #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-timestamp-1","p":"The core library","h":"class Timestamp","m":"agent","x":"class Timestamp # Declaration Contract trusted pure func epoch() Timestamp 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z as a signed 64-bit nanosecond count of 0; O(1) trusted pure try func add(t: Timestamp, d: Duration) Timestamp t + d in nanoseconds since the epoch; fails numeric.overflow ; O(1) trusted pure func after(a: Timestamp, b: Timestamp) Bool a is strictly later than b; O(1) trusted pure func before(a: Timestamp, b: Timestamp) Bool a is strictly earlier than b; O(1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#fortismoney","p":"The core library","h":"fortis.money","m":"agent","x":"fortis.money # An amount of a currency ( LR 15 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#enum-currency-derive-equatable","p":"The core library","h":"enum Currency derive Equatable","m":"agent","x":"enum Currency derive Equatable # Variants: GBP , EUR , USD ."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#error-currencymismatch","p":"The core library","h":"error CurrencyMismatch","m":"agent","x":"error CurrencyMismatch #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-money","p":"The core library","h":"class Money","m":"agent","x":"class Money # Declaration Contract pure func of(currency: Currency, amount: Decimal) Money \u2014 pure func zero(currency: Currency) Money \u2014 pure try func add(a: Money, b: Money) Money \u2014 pure try func subtract(a: Money, b: Money) Money \u2014 trusted pure try func multiply(money: Money, factor: Int) Money the amount times factor at the amount's scale (an Int has scale 0), rounded half to even when it needs more than 96 coefficient bits; fails numeric.overflow (6.4); O(digits^2) trusted pure try func divide(money: Money, divisor: Int<1..>) Money the amount divided by divisor at the largest scale that fits, rounded half to even; fails numeric.overflow (6.4); O(digits^2) pure func greaterThan(a: Money, b: Money) Bool Ordered within one currency; amounts in different currencies are incomparable, so greaterThan is false."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#fortishost","p":"The core library","h":"fortis.host","m":"agent","x":"fortis.host # The capabilities, Path , Secret and reveal ( LR 11.6 , LR 15 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-path","p":"The core library","h":"class Path","m":"agent","x":"class Path # Declaration Contract trusted pure try func parse(text: String) Path a path from its text; fails path.invalid for empty text or text containing NUL; O(n)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-secrett","p":"The core library","h":"class Secret[T]","m":"agent","x":"class Secret[T] #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-secretaccess-implements-authority","p":"The core library","h":"class SecretAccess implements Authority","m":"agent","x":"class SecretAccess implements Authority #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-filesystem-implements-authority","p":"The core library","h":"class FileSystem implements Authority","m":"agent","x":"class FileSystem implements Authority # Declaration Contract trusted try func readText(self, path: Path) String the UTF-8 contents of path below this root; fails fs.outside_root when the path is absolute or escapes the root, fs.not_found , fs.not_a_file or fs.invalid_text . Development adapter: the root is a fresh empty temporary directory per run, or FORTIS_DEV_ROOT when set; blocks the calling thread (the block effect, 11.1) trusted try func writeAll(self, path: Path, text: StringView) replaces the contents of path with text, creating the file; fails fs.outside_root or fs.not_found (missing parent directory); blocks the calling thread (the block effect, 11.1) trusted try func readAll(self, path: Path) Bytes the bytes of path; fails fs.outside_root , fs.not_found or fs.not_a_file ; blocks the calling thread (the block effect, 11.1) trusted try func open(self, path: Path) File an open file for reading; fails fs.outside_root or fs.not_found ; blocks the calling thread (the block effect, 11.1) trusted try func create(self, path: Path) File an open file for writing, created or truncated; fails fs.outside_root or fs.not_found ; blocks the calling thread (the block effect, 11.1) trusted func subtree(self, path: Path) FileSystem a FileSystem confined below path; a path that escapes this root yields a confinement that refuses every operation with fs.outside_root (attenuation never widens)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-file","p":"The core library","h":"class File","m":"agent","x":"class File # Declaration Contract trusted try func readAll(editable self) Bytes the remaining bytes of a file opened for reading; fails fs.closed after close and fs.wrong_mode on a file opened for writing; blocks the calling thread (the block effect, 11.1) trusted try func writeAll(editable self, data: BytesView) appends data to a file opened for writing; fails fs.closed after close and fs.wrong_mode on a file opened for reading; blocks the calling thread (the block effect, 11.1) trusted func close(editable self) closes the file; closing twice has no effect; dropping an open file closes it"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-network-implements-authority","p":"The core library","h":"class Network implements Authority","m":"agent","x":"class Network implements Authority # Declaration Contract trusted try func resolve(self, host: StringView) String the address of host. Development adapter: offline; localhost resolves to 127.0.0.1 and every other host fails network.unreachable ; blocks the calling thread (the block effect, 11.1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-clock-implements-authority","p":"The core library","h":"class Clock implements Authority","m":"agent","x":"class Clock implements Authority # Declaration Contract trusted async func sleep(self, duration: Duration) suspends the task for duration of the clock's time (a cancellation point, 12.4). Development adapter: virtual time on the controlled scheduler trusted func now(self) Timestamp the current instant in UTC (nondet). Development adapter: the system clock; a frozen clock returns its instant trusted func frozen(self, instant: Timestamp) Clock a clock whose now() is always instant (for tests and replay) trusted try func elapsedSince(self, earlier: Timestamp) Duration now() minus earlier; fails clock.future when earlier is after now()"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-random-implements-authority","p":"The core library","h":"class Random implements Authority","m":"agent","x":"class Random implements Authority # Declaration Contract trusted func below(self, bound: Int<1..>) Int<0..> a uniformly distributed integer in [0, bound) (nondet; rejection sampling, no modulo bias). Development adapter: SplitMix64 from seed 0, or FORTIS_DEV_SEED trusted func seeded(self, seed: Int) Random a generator with its own deterministic sequence from seed (replay)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-environment-implements-authority","p":"The core library","h":"class Environment implements Authority","m":"agent","x":"class Environment implements Authority # Declaration Contract trusted func get(self, name: StringView) Secret[String]? the variable as a Secret, or null when unset or when name is empty or contains = (nondet). Development adapter: the process environment"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-process-implements-authority","p":"The core library","h":"class Process implements Authority","m":"agent","x":"class Process implements Authority # Declaration Contract trusted try func run(self, program: StringView, arguments: ListView[String]) Int runs program with arguments to completion and returns its exit status (the block effect). Development adapter: starts no process; simulates true (0) and false (1) and refuses every other program with process.denied"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-supervisor-implements-authority","p":"The core library","h":"class Supervisor implements Authority","m":"agent","x":"class Supervisor implements Authority #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-console-implements-authority","p":"The core library","h":"class Console implements Authority","m":"agent","x":"class Console implements Authority # Declaration Contract trusted func print(self, text: StringView) writes text and a line feed to standard output"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-rawsql-implements-authority","p":"The core library","h":"class RawSql implements Authority","m":"agent","x":"class RawSql implements Authority # Declaration Contract trusted try func execute(self, statement: StringView) Int runs one SQL statement and returns the affected row count. Development adapter: has no database; every statement fails sql.unavailable ; blocks the calling thread (the block effect, 11.1)"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-capabilities","p":"The core library","h":"class Capabilities","m":"agent","x":"class Capabilities #"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#class-host","p":"The core library","h":"class Host","m":"agent","x":"class Host # Declaration Contract trusted func capabilities() Capabilities the ten process-wide capabilities, created by one direct call in the entry main (11.6): every other use is rejected statically ( E-EFF-010 ), and a second creation stops the run with the runtime-invariant defect (10.8) ( E-EFF-010 : LR 4.6 )"},{"u":"docs/agent/library.html#functions-and-aspects-4","p":"The core library","h":"Functions and aspects","m":"agent","x":"Functions and aspects # Declaration Contract trusted func reveal[T](secret: Secret[T], cap: SecretAccess) T the secret value (a copy); requires the SecretAccess capability (15); O(size)"},{"u":"docs/agent/records.html#records","p":"Records","h":"Records","m":"agent","x":"Records # Agent mode. Source of truth: the schema reference/schemas/diagnostic.schema.json , and LR 10.6 , LR 10.8 , LR 13 and LR 14 for the runtime records. Every finding, runtime failure report, test result and observation the toolchain produces is a record first: a JSON object of a known kind, from which the text a person sees is rendered. Read the record, not the text."},{"u":"docs/agent/records.html#streams","p":"Records","h":"Streams","m":"agent","x":"Streams # Situation What is written standard output is a pipe one JSON object per line standard output is a terminal text rendered from the same record --format json / --format text forces that rendering a running program's records (failure reports, defects, logs, observation frames and events) standard error, same rule; the program's own output is standard output ( LR 4.6 ) Where each command writes, as the toolchain does today: Command Standard output Standard error fortis check , fortisc check diagnostic records \u2014 fortis run , a native executable the program's output runtime records fortis test (interpreter) the test-summary record test-result and other runtime records fortisc test (VM) the test-summary record test-result and other runtime records, as fortis test writes them fortis build , fortisc build the build record diagnostic records if the check fails fortisc vm the conformance format: === FILE , observed: \u2026 (output lines joined by | ), trace: \u2026 , record: \u2026 \u2014 A finding has one identity: fortis check , the daemon and the agent protocol produce byte-equal records for the same snapshot. fortis check (the reference checker) and fortisc check (the production checker) print records of this one schema, with the same codes at the same lines."},{"u":"docs/agent/records.html#diagnostic-records","p":"Records","h":"Diagnostic records","m":"agent","x":"Diagnostic records # Schema: reference/schemas/diagnostic.schema.json . Field Type Meaning code string stable diagnostic code, E-XXX-NNN class string the diagnostic class (table below) rule string the normative rule the record enforces ( LANG- , PROG- or PLAT- ID) file , line , column string, integer, integer primary location, the user's nearest responsible operation (1-based) severity error or warning a warning does not fail the check status violated , unproved or unknown a proved violation, an inability to prove, an unknown trust boundary message string one-line explanation fields object the class's mandatory fields correction object, optional {kind, edits: [{file, line, column, end_line, end_column, replacement}]} , an applicable edit unproved means the checker could not prove a bound, not that the program is wrong: supply the proof or use the checked alternative the message names ( LR 6.3 ). A correction 's kind is a short description of the edit (for example add the marker ). A fix preserves behaviour, changes it or weakens a contract, but today's records do not carry that class, so judge it from the edit itself; the compiler never removes noalloc or a security requirement to make code compile, and an agent never applies an edit that weakens a claim."},{"u":"docs/agent/records.html#classes-and-mandatory-fields","p":"Records","h":"Classes and mandatory fields","m":"agent","x":"Classes and mandatory fields # Class Code prefix Mandatory fields Correction Type E-TYP , E-MIX-001 ( LR 6.10 ) expected_type , actual_type , primary_span behaviour-preserving or behaviour-changing fix Ownership E-OWN place , conflict , spans , loan_region marker, block restructuring, copy Obligation E-OBL , E-ERR-001 ( LR 5.4 ) obligation_origin , unconsumed_path or fail , or recover , report , match Effect E-EFF required_contract , violating_effect , witness_path contract-weakening or behaviour-changing fix Refinement unproved E-REF required_range , proved_interval , widening_point a checked operator ( a + b or fail ), validate Unsupported shape E-OWN-090 ( LR 7.10 ) lowering_row none (restructure) Resource limit E-LIM budget , operation none Internal defect E-INT reproduction none Syntax E-SYN expected_tokens , found_token where the grammar allows one Names and modules E-NAM name , candidates import, rename Patterns E-MAT missing_variants , arm_set add arms Closures E-FUN violation , capture_span , use_span capture entry, stronger kind Defer and async E-DEF , E-ASY offending_statement , rule_reference move the statement out of defer , add an await site Observation E-OBS frame_field , exit_path add or remove capture Dependency injection (kit, Platform 3 ) E-DI consumer , slot , candidates target, replaces , lifetime: Telemetry (kit, Platform 6.4 ) E-TEL plan_line , projection , field bind_at , a new metric name, a new schema version Every code, its class, its rule and its correction pattern: codes.md ."},{"u":"docs/agent/records.html#example-a-violation","p":"Records","h":"Example: a violation","m":"agent","x":"Example: a violation # The record for an assignment to an immutable binding ( LR 5.1 ): Fortis // reject: E-NAM-002 pure func next ( n : Int < 0 .. 9 > ) Int { let x = n x = 1 // reject: E-NAM-002 return x } test \"next\" { expect next ( 1 ) == 1 } records {\"class\": \"Names and modules\", \"code\": \"E-NAM-002\", \"column\": 3, \"fields\": {\"candidates\": [\"let mut\"], \"name\": \"x\"}, \"file\": \"example.ft\", \"line\": 4, \"message\": \"`x` is immutable\", \"rule\": \"LANG-05-01\", \"severity\": \"error\", \"status\": \"violated\"}"},{"u":"docs/agent/records.html#example-an-unproved-bound","p":"Records","h":"Example: an unproved bound","m":"agent","x":"Example: an unproved bound # An integer + the checker cannot prove in range is E-REF-002 with status unproved ; proved_interval is what the checker established and required_range what the type needs ( LR 6.4 ): Fortis // reject: E-REF-002 pure func sum ( a : Int , b : Int ) Int { return a + b // reject: E-REF-002 } test \"sum\" { expect sum ( 1 , 2 ) == 3 } records {\"class\": \"Refinement unproved\", \"code\": \"E-REF-002\", \"column\": 12, \"fields\": {\"proved_interval\": \"[-18446744073709551616, 18446744073709551614]\", \"required_range\": \"[-9223372036854775808, 9223372036854775807]\", \"widening_point\": \"\"}, \"file\": \"example.ft\", \"line\": 3, \"message\": \"`+` may overflow Int: operands [-9223372036854775808, 9223372036854775807] and [-9223372036854775808, 9223372036854775807] give [-18446744073709551616, 18446744073709551614]; use Int.add, or write the operator as the checked operation: `a + b or fail` (or `or recover`, `catch`)\", \"rule\": \"LANG-06-02\", \"severity\": \"error\", \"status\": \"unproved\"}"},{"u":"docs/agent/records.html#example-a-record-with-a-correction","p":"Records","h":"Example: a record with a correction","m":"agent","x":"Example: a record with a correction # The text rendering lists the fields, then the correction's kind and each edit as file:line:column-end_line:end_column -> \"replacement\" . A missing ownership marker ( LR 7.5 ): Fortis // reject: E-OWN-001 class Note { export text : String } test \"note\" { let a = Note ( text : \"x\" ) let b = a // reject: E-OWN-001 expect b . text == \"x\" } output example.ft:8:11: error E-OWN-001 [LANG-07-01] (Ownership, violated): `a` is an existing ownership-bearing place: write `copy a`, `alias a` or `move a` conflict: \"marker required\" loan_region: \"\" place: \"a\" spans: \"a\" correction: add the marker example.ft:8:11-8:11 -> \"copy \" The edit inserts copy (a behaviour-preserving choice); move a or alias a are the other repairs the message names, chosen by what the code does with a afterwards ( LR 7.5 )."},{"u":"docs/agent/records.html#runtime-records","p":"Records","h":"Runtime records","m":"agent","x":"Runtime records # Written while a program or its tests run (see Streams above for where each command writes them). The schemas are interpreter/records.py ; field values below are real records of the reference toolchain. kind Written when Fields Source runtime-failure report f , a failure reported during cleanup, a failure in a task result the scope abandoned, or a failing try main (exactly one report, exit status 1) code , message , payload , origin ( file , function ), cause , annotations , reported_by ( report , main , cleanup , abandoned ), rule LR 4.6 , LR 10.6 , LR 12.1 , LR 12.5 defect a defect terminates a task class ( stack_exhaustion , \u2026), task , rule LR 10.8 observation-frame an observed activation completes schema , function , outcome , fields , truncated , rule LR 13 observation-event a record statement runs schema , fields , rule LR 13 log a log.info , log.warn or log.error statement runs; text warn: a fallback was taken level ( info , warn , error ), message , function , rule ( LANG-13-04 ) LR 13 test-result each test, table row or property case test , case , status ( pass , fail , error ), evidence (typed actual and expected), rule LR 14 property-failure a property or fuzz target fails test , mode , seed , case , original , minimal (the shrunk input), shrinks , evidence , replay (the command that replays it), rule LR 14 test-summary end of a test run passed , failed , rule LR 14 task-lifecycle a never-polled future is discarded, or a task is cancelled before it starts ( state not_started ) function , state , rule LR 12.3 , LR 13 task-completion a detached task completes, as its supervisor observes it task , outcome , rule LR 12.6 buffer-overflow the task reporter's buffer overflowed: one record, its dropped count growing bound , dropped , rule LR 15 executor-trace a native program run with FORTIS_TRACE=1 events ( launch , start , suspend , resume , complete , join , loans-end ) LR 12.5 build fortis build / fortisc build finishes binary , manifest LR 1 The backends do not yet agree on every field (D-57): the interpreter counts each property case as a test, native code writes evidence as a string, and a failure's origin has no line on any backend; fortis test and fortisc test write the same evidence , its line included. Match on kind , code , test and status . Captured field values leave the process only through the Telemetry kit's pipeline; with no telemetry plan the task reporter writes a frame's schema, outcome class and duration but never a captured value ( fields is empty), and FORTIS_TELEMETRY=permit-all is the explicit development plan that writes them unchanged ( LR 15 ). A failure's origin.file is never an absolute path, on any backend, so an executable does not depend on where it was built: a module of a package is named relative to the package's directory ( src/pricing.ft ), and a standalone module relative to the current directory when it lies below it, else by its file name. A report of a declared error, as written by the interpreter and the native executable ( LR 10.6 ): json {\"annotations\": [], \"cause\": [], \"code\": \"demo.not_even\", \"kind\": \"runtime-failure\", \"message\": \"\", \"origin\": {\"file\": \"examples/02_outcomes.ft\", \"function\": \"half\"}, \"payload\": {\"value\": \"9\"}, \"reported_by\": \"report\", \"rule\": \"LANG-10-06\"} A frame and an event under FORTIS_TELEMETRY=permit-all ( LR 13 , LR 15 ): json {\"fields\": {\"value\": \"5\"}, \"kind\": \"observation-event\", \"rule\": \"LANG-13-01\", \"schema\": \"Line\"} {\"fields\": {\"items\": \"3\", \"total\": \"23\"}, \"function\": \"process\", \"kind\": \"observation-frame\", \"outcome\": \"success\", \"rule\": \"LANG-13-02\", \"schema\": \"OrderProcessing\", \"truncated\": []} A failed expect ( LR 14 ): json {\"case\": \"\", \"evidence\": {\"actual\": \"4\", \"expected\": \"== 5\", \"line\": 4}, \"kind\": \"test-result\", \"rule\": \"LANG-14-02\", \"status\": \"fail\", \"test\": \"deliberately wrong\"}"},{"u":"docs/agent/records.html#fortis-view---json-records","p":"Records","h":"fortis view --json records","m":"agent","x":"fortis view --json records # fortis view reads Fortis source as the compiler does and never writes; --json is its primary form. One object per line, kind one of: kind From Fields line fortis view FILE path , line , text , spans ( [column, length, class] , class keyword , name , type , punctuation , comment , \u2026) declaration --decls path , line , column , name , declaration , head , modifiers , contracts , aspects , doc , exported , member_of symbol --symbol NAME path , name , line , end_line , text match --query Q path , line , column , query , text query --queries name , description json {\"aspects\": [], \"column\": 10, \"contracts\": [], \"declaration\": \"func\", \"doc\": \"\", \"exported\": false, \"head\": \"try func half(n: Int) Int\", \"kind\": \"declaration\", \"line\": 11, \"member_of\": null, \"modifiers\": [\"try\"], \"name\": \"half\", \"path\": \"examples/02_outcomes.ft\"} {\"column\": 21, \"kind\": \"match\", \"line\": 45, \"path\": \"examples/02_outcomes.ft\", \"query\": \"or-fail\", \"text\": \"or\"}"},{"u":"skills/fortis-code/SKILL.html#working-with-fortis-code","p":"fortis-code","h":"Working with Fortis code","m":"agent","x":"Working with Fortis code # Fortis source is read through the language's tools, never through text tools. fortis view sees the code as the compiler does: it knows which { opens a body, which word is a keyword used as a name, and where a statement ends. grep and cat do not, and they miss what matters in Fortis (parameter modes, effects, failure disposition)."},{"u":"skills/fortis-code/SKILL.html#read-fortis-view","p":"fortis-code","h":"Read: fortis view","m":"agent","x":"Read: fortis view # Run from the repository root ( bin/fortis , or fortis when installed). It never writes. To Run read a file, a directory or a glob fortis view src/orders.ft , fortis view src , fortis view 'src/**/*.ft' list declarations (kind, name, modifiers, contracts, aspects, documentation, head) fortis view --decls src read one declaration whole fortis view --symbol process src or --symbol Order.total answer a structural question fortis view --query async-try src list the queries fortis view --queries The queries answer what text search cannot: async , try , async-try , pure , noalloc , trusted , recovers , editable-params , owned-params , or-fail , or-recover , catch , fail , return , defer , scope , capture , expect , move , lambda , tests , errors , classes , and aspect:NAME ( aspect:get finds every @get route). Each answer is file:line:column: name . Add --json for machine-readable output: one JSON object per line, with kind one of line , declaration , symbol , match or query . JSON is the primary form, and the terminal highlighting is derived from it."},{"u":"skills/fortis-code/SKILL.html#check-fortis-check---format-json","p":"fortis-code","h":"Check: fortis check --format json","m":"agent","x":"Check: fortis check --format json # fortis check FILE.ft or fortis check DIR runs every static check: syntax, names, types, ownership, obligations, effects, contracts and aspects. With --format json , each finding is one record per line. A record has code (for example E-OWN-001 , LR 7.5 ), class , rule (the Language Reference rule it enforces), file , line , column , message , status ( violated , unproved or unknown ) and class-specific fields ; the schema is reference/schemas/diagnostic.schema.json . A record with a correction carries the exact edit that fixes it. Read the record before changing code: unproved means the checker could not prove a refinement, not that the code is wrong. Run fortis test FILE.ft or fortis test DIR for the tests."},{"u":"skills/fortis-code/SKILL.html#format-fortis-fmt","p":"fortis-code","h":"Format: fortis fmt","m":"agent","x":"Format: fortis fmt # fortis fmt --check PATH lists the files whose layout is not canonical, and fortis fmt --write PATH fixes them. Formatting changes only whitespace: it refuses, and writes nothing, if a result would not be the same program."},{"u":"skills/fortis-code/SKILL.html#rules-codes-and-corrections-the-agent-mode","p":"fortis-code","h":"Rules, codes and corrections: the agent mode","m":"agent","x":"Rules, codes and corrections: the agent mode # The documentation's agent mode, docs/agent/ (start at docs/agent/README.md ), states every rule of the Language Reference by ID, one page per section, with the diagnostic codes of that section and a rejected-then-accepted pair of programs for each correction. Every example there is checked and run by the real toolchain. To Read fix a record docs/agent/codes.md : the code's class, rule, Language Reference section, trigger and correction, with a link to its correction pair read a record's fields docs/agent/records.md : the diagnostic record and every runtime record (failures, tests, observation, builds) know the rules of a construct docs/agent/NN-*.md : the rules of Language Reference section NN by ID check a rule's exact words docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.md , the source of truth: LR 7.3 is its section 7.3 A correction pattern shows the smallest change that satisfies the rule. Apply the one whose trigger matches the record's code and fields ; when the record has a correction , apply that edit. Never apply a pattern that weakens a claim ( pure , noalloc , a contract) or discards an outcome to silence a record."},{"u":"skills/fortis-code/SKILL.html#prohibitions","p":"fortis-code","h":"Prohibitions","m":"agent","x":"Prohibitions # Do not read .ft files with cat , head , tail , sed -n or less , and do not search them with grep or rg . Use fortis view . Do not weaken a contract, remove an expect , or change an expected output to make a check or test pass. Do not hand-format Fortis code; run fortis fmt --write ."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Specification.html#fortis--specification-overview","p":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","h":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","m":"spec","x":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview # Document: FSPEC-0.2 \u2022 Date: 22 October 2026 \u2022 Supersedes: FSPEC-0.1 ( Fortis_Full_Specification.md , now this file) \u2022 Status: normative baseline for Fortis Language 1.0 and the kits; not an implemented or formally proved language."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Specification.html#contents","p":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","h":"Contents","m":"spec","x":"Contents # 1. What Fortis is and why it exists 2. How the language works, in one page 3. What is in the core and what is a library 4. Document map 5. Epistemic conventions 6. Traceability index 7. Status of decisions and open items 8. Glossary"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Specification.html#1-what-fortis-is-and-why-it-exists","p":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","h":"1. What Fortis is and why it exists","m":"spec","x":"1. What Fortis is and why it exists # Fortis is a general-purpose, statically checked programming language that compiles to safe Rust for production and runs on a development VM with hot reload. It exists for one reason: most new code is now written by AI agents, and the people who must review, operate and be accountable for that code cannot keep up unless the code answers the reviewer's questions on its face. A reviewer of a Fortis file or diff can answer these questions from the file and the signatures it imports, without an IDE, a debugger or the bodies of other functions: Question Where the answer is visible Who owns this value, and did this line copy it, borrow it or give it away? copy / alias / move on every assignment of an existing ownership-bearing value (trivial values such as integers need nothing); move x , editable x and alias x at call sites Can this call change my data? editable on the parameter, at the call site, and before a receiver whose method mutates it Can this call fail, and who deals with it? try on the function; or fail , or recover , catch or match at the call; dropping a failure does not compile Does this function do I/O, suspend, fail, swallow a failure, or write outside its own storage? pure , async , try , recovers on the signature; editable on the parameters it may write; a capability parameter for any I/O other than report . noalloc answers the separate allocation question When is this observation recorded, and from which value? capture field = expression at the exact point Is this value optional, false or failed? Three different types with three different operators ( ?? , ` , or`) The design accepts more words on the page in exchange for fewer things a reader must infer. It rejects the usual sources of hidden behaviour: inheritance, exceptions, implicit conversions (with one exception: a string literal becomes a String where one is expected), macros, reflection, hidden clones and reference counts, truthiness, ambient service locators. Fortis is library-first . A program is a set of packages; a package is a set of modules; a module is one file with explicit imports and explicit exports. Everything beyond the core (HTTP, databases, web UI including SPA and PWA, telemetry, jobs, dependency injection) is a library that an application imports. The distribution ships those libraries (\"batteries included\"), but the language does not depend on them and an application links only what it imports."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Specification.html#2-how-the-language-works-in-one-page","p":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","h":"2. How the language works, in one page","m":"spec","x":"2. How the language works, in one page # Fortis // accept \u2014 src/report.ft in package acme.reporting import fortis . text import fortis . host { FileSystem , Console } import acme . billing . invoices { Invoice , CustomerId } import acme . reporting . store { Archive , Invoices } // illustrative dependency // A nominal type: a ReportId is an Int, but it is not an Int. export type ReportId = Int < 1 .. > export error NothingToReport { code : \"reporting.empty\" message : \"No invoices were supplied\" } // `try` says this can fail. `pure` says it does no I/O, reads no clock, // and mutates nothing it did not create. export pure try func summarise ( invoices : List [ Invoice ] ) String { if invoices . isEmpty ( ) { fail NothingToReport ( ) } let mut out = text . Builder . create ( ) // fresh local storage: mutation is still pure for invoice in invoices { // read loan of each invoice let total = invoice . total ( ) or fail // propagate failure, keep the success value out . appendLine ( \"${invoice.customer}: ${total}\" ) } return out . finish ( ) } // Ownership is explicit when an existing value is involved. export func archive ( store : editable Archive , invoices : owned List [ Invoice ] ) { let count = invoices . count ( ) store . add ( move invoices ) // `store` changes, as its parameter says; `invoices` is given away store . note ( \"archived ${count}\" ) } // The entry point. `try main` that fails prints one report and exits 1. try func main ( fileSystem : FileSystem , console : Console , args : List [ String ] ) { let invoices = Invoices . load ( fileSystem , args ) or fail let reportText = summarise ( invoices ) catch e { // explicit binding; must end in recover/return/fail report e // consumes the failure; it cannot also be propagated recover \"no report\" } console . print ( reportText ) } Reading top to bottom: Modules and imports. import binds a module alias or named members; nothing runs at import time. export is the only visibility word. Types are nominal. type ReportId = Int<1..> is a new type, not an alias. Refinements like Int<1..> are checked by a published interval analysis; when the analysis cannot prove a bound, the program must call an explicit validate . Functions declare what they are. async , try and recovers are written wherever a function has those effects, because they change its type; pure and noalloc are checked claims a function makes when it wants callers to rely on them. All five are computed from a closed set of eight effects, and across a module boundary only what is written counts, so a body change cannot silently change a caller's contract. Ownership is written down. copy , alias , move on assignment of an existing value; editable and owned on parameters, mirrored by editable x and move x at the call and before a receiver whose method mutates or consumes it. Loans are lexical: a loan held by a binding ends with that binding's block, a loan taken for a synchronous call ends when the call returns, and a loan taken for an async call is held by its future until that completes; nothing is inferred from later uses. Failures are linear. An Outcome must be consumed exactly once. or fail propagates; or recover v discharges; catch e { \u2026 } handles; report e consumes it into the diagnostic plane. There is no way to drop one. Three different \"no\". T? is optional and uses ?? ; Bool uses || ; Outcome uses or . None converts to another. Cleanup and async are structured. defer { } runs at every exit of its block; async / await with cancellation observed only at await (and at checkpoint() on the parallel and blocking pools); scope name { } joins or cancels every task it launched before it exits. Observation is a statement. capture field = expr snapshots a value into the activation's frame; the checker proves every required field is captured exactly once on every exit. The Language Reference defines each of these precisely, with accept/reject/execute examples and the rules a conformance suite will test."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Specification.html#2-how-the-language-works-in-one-page","p":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","h":"2. How the language works, in one page","m":"spec","x":"ith accept/reject/execute examples and the rules a conformance suite will test."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Specification.html#3-what-is-in-the-core-and-what-is-a-library","p":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","h":"3. What is in the core and what is a library","m":"spec","x":"3. What is in the core and what is a library # Layer Contents Versioned as Fortis Language 1.0 (core) grammar; modules, packages, imports; statements; types and refinements; ownership and loans; closures; classes, interfaces, mixins, enums; linear outcomes; closed effect set and contracts; synchronous defer ; async / await / scope on the core cooperative executor and pools; observation schemas and statements; test declarations; the core runtime inventory (executor, task reporter, capability types, Host.capabilities() ); the fortis core modules ( prelude , text , collections , numeric , time , money , host ); VM; Rust lowering; conformance suite one language version with a compatibility promise Kits (libraries) Runtime host; Dependency injection; HTTP; Data (PostgreSQL, SQLite, jobs, cache, mail); Telemetry (OTel, archive, FinOps); Web UI ( fortis.web.ssr , fortis.web.components , fortis.web.forms , fortis.web.spa , fortis.web.pwa ); Security; Configuration and deployment ( fortis.config ) each kit has its own version, gate and acceptance application; published together as a compatibility set Toolchain components Developer tooling (daemon, LSP, reload, replay, agent protocol, dashboard; the explore head); Packages and scaffolds (registry client, lockfile, scaffold engine); SDK generators delivered with their own gates; not kits (PROG-PRD-38) A kit is an ordinary set of packages: it obeys every language rule, enters a module only through the four extension slots the core reserves (heads, inject members, aspects, test tables), and can ship a new version without a new language version. The Runtime host kit supplies adapters (Tokio, I/O readiness, timers) for the executor interface the core defines; the core runs without it. The web UI kit, including SPA navigation and PWA support, is deliberately a library with a lower delivery priority than the HTTP and data kits; an application that does not import it gets none of it."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Specification.html#4-document-map","p":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","h":"4. Document map","m":"spec","x":"4. Document map # Document Purpose Normative IDs Fortis_Specification.md (this file) purpose, orientation, conventions, traceability none Fortis_Language_Reference.md the core language: syntax, static rules, execution semantics, lowering reference, keyword inventory, closed decisions LANG-<section>-<nn> reference/grammar/fortis.ebnf the normative grammar \u2014 Fortis_Platform_Specification.md the kits as libraries: runtime host, DI, HTTP, data, telemetry, web UI, security, packages, configuration, developer tooling, standard contracts PLAT-<kit>-<nn> the Delivery Programme (kept with the maintainers' planning records) release boundary, planned artefacts, requirements register, compiler and VM implementation, diagnostics, testing, exploration and agent protocol, non-functional requirements, threat model, proof spikes, gates, acceptance applications, evidence rules, risks, governance PROG-<family>-<nn> the decision register (kept with the maintainers' planning records) the FSPEC-0.1 review register with the decision recorded for every item review item IDs Read the Language Reference first if you want to write or review Fortis code. Read the Platform Specification if you are building an application. Read the Delivery Programme if you are implementing the compiler or planning the release."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Specification.html#5-epistemic-conventions","p":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","h":"5. Epistemic conventions","m":"spec","x":"5. Epistemic conventions # These conventions apply to all four documents and are not repeated in them. MUST / MUST NOT are requirements of a conforming implementation or release. SHOULD needs a recorded justification for an exception. MAY is a permitted choice. Evidence states are proved , generated_checked , tested , measured , trusted , unknown and violated . They are never collapsed into a score. proved means that the production checker and the definitional interpreter agree on the conformance corpus for the rule in question (Delivery Programme, section VM, parity and the definitional interpreter); it is never a claim about business correctness. No example has been compiled. Every code block is illustrative until the conformance suite derived from the Language Reference runs it. Examples carry // accept , // reject: E-\u2026 or // execute: \u2026 markers stating what the suite must establish, or // illustrative when they are fragments or templates that are not fixtures. The conformance/ directory holds the G0 corpus extracted from those markers and the syntax-layer runner that parses it against the grammar. Guarantees have scopes. Every guarantee names its checker, its scope and its trust assumptions. The language does not guarantee correct requirements, termination, delivery over a network, absence of denial of service or optimal performance for every abstraction. Safe memory does not sandbox a process. A contradiction is a defect. Where two documents disagree, the Language Reference wins on language semantics, the Platform Specification on kit behaviour and the Delivery Programme on process; the disagreement is recorded and fixed, never resolved silently by an implementer. Green agent summaries are not evidence. Release evidence is executed, content-addressed and independently verified (Delivery Programme, evidence and anti-gaming rules)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Specification.html#6-traceability-index","p":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","h":"6. Traceability index","m":"spec","x":"6. Traceability index # Requirement identifiers from FSPEC-0.1 were renumbered into three families. Each document carries its own mapping table; this index says where to look. Old family New family Mapping table SYN, TYP, OWN, FUN, OO, GEN, MOD, ERR, EFF, RES, ASY, OBS-001/002/006 LANG- Language Reference, Appendix D API, DAT, JOB, CAC, WEB, SEC (adapters), PKG, SCF, CFG, SCH, DEP, TEL, FIN, DI, STD, APP, OBS-003/004/005, VM-002\u2026004 PLAT- Platform Specification, Appendix A FND, ARC, CMP, VM-001/005, DIA, TST, EXP, AGT, NFR, SEC (threat model), DEL, DOC PROG- Delivery Programme, section Requirements register Review items ( R- , G- , T- , O- , F- , E- , C- , D- , I- , B- , P- , V- , N- ) map to the sections that resolved them in the decision register, under each item's Decision line."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Specification.html#7-status-of-decisions-and-open-items","p":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","h":"7. Status of decisions and open items","m":"spec","x":"7. Status of decisions and open items # Decided in FSPEC-0.2 (full list in the Language Reference, Appendix B): let / let mut declarations; give for match-arm values; explicit catch e ; call-site move / editable ; closure kinds fn / fn mut / fn once ; lexical loans with no partial moves; linear outcomes with or fail / or recover / catch ; a closed set of eight effects and the removal of nostate ; block-form synchronous defer only; cooperative single-thread executor with scope ; Shareable for parallel work; identifier case deciding grammatical category; main as the entry point and the removal of or die ; the trivial, copyable, shareable and linear type tables; the interval domain for refinements. Open, with an owner in the Delivery Programme: Item Where it is tracked Annotation burden of proof-required arithmetic on ordinary code spike SP-05b Asynchronous cleanup ( defer await ) spike SP-06; parse error until closed Implementation of the specified executor, scope and Shareable rules spike SP-16 (the rules are fixed; the spike proves the lowering) Borrowed work on the parallel pools (a scoped scheduler) not in 1.0; cooperative launch and join cover borrowed siblings Definitional interpreter and the meaning of \"proved\" spike SP-15 Trademark check for the name \"Fortis\" (classes 9 and 42) governance, release prerequisite JSON schemas, work-package graph, conformance scenarios, skill definitions, ambiguity corpus planned artefacts table"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Specification.html#8-glossary","p":"Fortis \u2014 Specification Overview","h":"8. Glossary","m":"spec","x":"8. Glossary # Term Meaning activation one execution of a function body; observation frames and freshness are per activation capability a nominal, unforgeable value that carries authority for I/O ( FileSystem , Network , Clock , \u2026), passed explicitly contract a named conjunction of modifiers, forbidden effects and required capabilities, written in the modifier position of a function head aspect declared behaviour applied around a function with @name(args) : a before hook, an on failure mapping and metadata declaration head a reserved word ( component , context , model , \u2026) whose body a kit validates and elaborates into ordinary declarations effect one of eight Boolean facts about a function: alloc , io , mutate , nondet , suspend , block , fail , recovers frame the per-activation record that capture writes into linear a value that must be consumed exactly once ( Outcome , Failure , Future , Task , and anything containing them) loan a read-only ( alias ) or exclusive ( editable ) reference to a place, lexically scoped marker copy , alias , move , editable : a word that states how a place is used obligation the duty to consume a linear value place a binding, a field path or a constant-indexed array element kit a separately versioned set of packages providing one area of platform behaviour refinement a bound on a built-in type, written <\u2026> , checked by interval analysis scope a block that owns tasks and joins or cancels them on exit shareable a type whose values may cross to a parallel or blocking task trivial a type copied implicitly without a marker (closed table in the Language Reference) view a borrowed slice: StringView , BytesView , ListView[T]"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#fortis-language-reference","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"Fortis Language Reference","m":"spec","x":"Fortis Language Reference # Document: FSPEC-0.2-LR \u2022 Date: 22 October 2026 \u2022 Status: normative reference for Fortis Language 1.0 (the core). Companion documents: Fortis_Specification.md (overview and index), Fortis_Platform_Specification.md (kits), the Delivery Programme (requirements register, gates, evidence; kept with the maintainers' planning records). The normative grammar is reference/grammar/fortis.ebnf ."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#contents","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"Contents","m":"spec","x":"Contents # 0. How to read this document 1. What Fortis is 2. A complete worked module 3. Lexical structure 4. Modules and packages 5. Statements and expressions 6. Types 7. Ownership 8. Functions and closures 9. Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums 10. Outcomes and failures 11. Effects and contracts 12. Cleanup, async and concurrency 13. Observation schemas and statements 14. Test declarations 15. Core runtime inventory 16. Reserved declaration heads for kits Appendix A. Keyword inventory Appendix B. Closed decisions Appendix C. Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset Appendix D. Rule index and old requirement IDs Appendix E. Glossary"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#0-how-to-read-this-document","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"0. How to read this document","m":"spec","x":"0. How to read this document # This document defines the Fortis core language: the syntax, the static rules and the execution semantics that every conforming implementation (the development VM and the Rust backend) must agree on. It does not describe HTTP, databases, web UI, telemetry pipelines or dependency injection; those are kits and live in the Platform Specification. Rules. Normative rules are numbered LANG-<section>-<nn> and collected in a table at the end of each section with their acceptance evidence. Prose between the tables is also normative unless marked illustrative or rationale . Examples. Every example is marked on its first line. // accept means a conforming implementation must accept the program. // reject: E-XXX-NNN means it must reject it with that diagnostic code at the marked line. // execute: <result> states the required observable result. // illustrative marks a fragment, a signature list or a template with \u2026 or bracketed placeholders; it shows a shape and is not a fixture. An accept , reject or execute example that is not a complete module is a statement sequence and is checked as the body of a function. The conformance tooling in conformance/ extracts every marked block into the G0 corpus and parses it against the EBNF; no example has yet been run through a Fortis compiler, and that suite is the mechanism that will make the semantic markers binding. Judgements. Static rules are written as judgements of the form \u0393 \u22a2 e : \u03c4 \u25b7 loans, effects, obligations , read \"under environment \u0393, expression e has type \u03c4 , creates these loans, has these effects and leaves these obligations\". The full judgement is defined in section 7.1 and extended in sections 10 and 11. Precedence of sources. Where this document and the EBNF disagree on syntax, the EBNF wins and the disagreement is a defect to fix. Where this document and an example disagree, the rule text wins."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#1-what-fortis-is","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"1. What Fortis is","m":"spec","x":"1. What Fortis is # Fortis is a general-purpose, statically checked, compiled programming language designed so that software written mostly by AI agents can be read, reviewed and trusted by humans. It optimises for a reader who sees a file or a diff and needs to answer, without tooling: who owns this value, can this call change it, can this call fail and who handles that, what effects does this function have, and when is this observation recorded. Every one of those questions has a visible answer in the source. What a reader can see in the file is every local decision: ownership operations, mutation permission, failure disposition, effect claims and observation points. Transitive facts (what an imported function ultimately does, which provider a dependency resolves to, what a deployment plan allocates) come from imported signatures and the build configuration, which the toolchain prints next to the source; the language guarantees that those imported signatures cannot be weaker than the bodies behind them. The language is library-first. A Fortis program is a set of packages; a package is a set of modules; a module is one file. Applications, command-line tools, servers and libraries are all built from the same core with explicit imports. Platform capabilities (HTTP, data access, web UI including SPA and PWA, telemetry, jobs) are ordinary libraries that an application imports; nothing in the core depends on them. The core design choices are: Explicit ownership. Assigning or passing an existing owned value states copy , alias or move . Mutable access is a visible editable loan. There are no hidden clones or reference counts. Three distinct answers. An optional value ( T? ), a Boolean and a fallible outcome ( Outcome[T] ) are different types with different operators ( ?? , || , or ). Nothing is truthy. Linear failures. A fallible result must be consumed exactly once: propagated with or fail , recovered with or recover , handled with catch or match , or reported with report . Dropping an outcome is a compile error. A closed effect set. pure , noalloc , async and try are checked facts about a function, computed transitively from a closed set of eight effects. Contracts are conjunctions of them and nothing else. Lexical loans and synchronous cleanup. Loans end at the end of the block that holds them; defer runs at block exit; async code has one cancellation point ( await ) and structured scope s. Two backends, one semantics. A development VM with hot reload and a production backend that emits safe Rust ( forbid(unsafe_code) ) execute the same checked intermediate representation. Fortis is not a scripting language, has no runtime reflection, no inheritance, no macros, no exceptions and no implicit conversions. A reader can determine every binding's type, ownership and effects from the file and the signatures it imports."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#11-notation-economy","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"1.1 Notation economy","m":"spec","x":"1.1 Notation economy # Fortis puts more words on the page than most languages, so every piece of notation has to earn its place. The rule is: a symbol or keyword exists only if it changes what a reader may conclude about ownership, mutation, failure, effects or timing. Decoration, repetition and punctuation that carries no decision are removed. This is the complete inventory of non-alphabetic notation and the question each answers; a construct that answers no question in this table is not in the language. Notation Answers Not used for anything else { } where a block, record literal or record type begins and ends \u2014 ( ) call, parameter list, tuple, grouping \u2014 Name[ ] generic arguments after an uppercase name \u2014 place[ ] index after a lowercase place no slices fn[ ] the capture list of a lambda, only when it is non-empty \u2014 + - * / % << >> & | ^ integer and float arithmetic, proof-required on integers; the operator form of the checked Int.add family no overloading == != < <= > >= Equatable and Comparable , plus IEEE comparison on floats \u2014 && || ! Boolean logic only no truthiness += and friends sugar for x = x op e \u2014 < > a refinement on a type, in type position only comparison in expression position only : \"has type\", named argument, record field, label no ternary, no slicing = assignment; initialiser in let no equality => expression body of a function, lambda or match arm no map literals . member access and module paths no range ( .. is a separate token) .. ..< integer ranges in for headers and refinements \u2014 ? ?. optional type suffix; optional chaining never error propagation ?? absent-value fallback \u2014 @ apply an aspect: @name(args) on the line above a function head nothing else; contracts are not applied with @ ${ } string interpolation \u2014 // comment; documentation when directly above a declaration \u2014 , separator in every list \u2014 ... spread of a place into a constructor; forwarding of remaining aspect arguments \u2014 Keywords that look like ceremony but carry a decision: let (a new name, not a reassignment), mut (this binding changes), copy / alias / move / editable (how this place is used, including as a receiver), owned / editable on a parameter (what the callee may do), try / pure / noalloc / async / recovers and a named contract in the same position (what the function may do), or fail / or recover / catch (what happens to a failure), give (this block produces a value), export (visible outside the module). Each of these would otherwise be inferred from somewhere the reader cannot see. Silence also has exactly one meaning per position: an unmarked argument or receiver is a read loan that ends when the call returns (or, for an async call, with the future it produces), an unmarked for source is a read loan that lasts for the loop, and an unmarked assignment or return is a fresh value or a trivial one. A reader who sees no marker knows the line neither mutates nor transfers nor keeps a loan beyond the call or the future it is in. Two deliberate costs. (f() or fail).amount needs its parentheses because or binds loosest; the idiom is let v = f() or fail on its own line, which is also where a reviewer wants the failure decision to be. And an async call looks like a call: it builds a Future , and the await or launch that runs it is the visible point. What was removed to keep noise down: semicolons; /// and /* */ comment forms; yield ; or die ; nostate ; a separate self: editable Self spelling (now editable self , like any parameter); alias editable (now editable ); alias type transparent aliases (so alias has one meaning and type X = \u2026 is always nominal); within (a stored loan says from r like a returned one); give in single-expression match arms (only block arms need it); empty fn[] capture lists; :: paths; operator overloading; implicit it ; postfix ? for errors; new (constructors are named create , of or from ); return in expression-bodied functions. The formatter prints every inferred fact in canonical form, so a formatted file shows the "},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#11-notation-economy","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"1.1 Notation economy","m":"spec","x":"unctions. The formatter prints every inferred fact in canonical form, so a formatted file shows the closure kind, the loan modes and the modifiers the checker used; a reader of a formatted file never has to infer them. The one exception is a lambda's inferred pure and noalloc , which the formatter does not print back (one the author wrote is kept): the function type the lambda is passed to states what it must be, and the checker enforces it there (8.2, X-345)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#2-a-complete-worked-module","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"2. A complete worked module","m":"spec","x":"2. A complete worked module # This module is the running example for the rest of the document. It is a small invoicing library in a package named acme.billing , in the file src/invoices.ft , so its module path is invoices and other packages import it as acme.billing.invoices . Fortis // accept import fortis . text import fortis . money { Money , Currency } // A customer identifier. Distinct from every other Int-backed id. export type CustomerId = Int < 1 .. > // A line on an invoice. All fields are exported, so the generated // constructor Line(description: ..., quantity: ..., unitPrice: ...) // is usable from other modules. export class Line derive Equatable { export description : String export quantity : Int < 1 .. 10000 > export unitPrice : Money // Read-only method: `self` borrows read-only by default. export pure try func total ( self ) Money { return Money . multiply ( self . unitPrice , self . quantity ) or fail } } // Invoice status is a closed enum with payloads. export enum InvoiceStatus derive Equatable { Draft Issued ( at : Timestamp ) Paid ( at : Timestamp , reference : String ) Voided ( reason : String ) } // Declared failure kinds with stable codes. export error EmptyInvoice { code : \"billing.invoice.empty\" message : \"An invoice must have at least one line\" } export error CurrencyMismatch { code : \"billing.invoice.currency_mismatch\" payload { expected : Currency , found : Currency } message : \"Line currency ${found} does not match invoice currency ${expected}\" } // An interface: anything that can be rendered to text. export interface Renderable { pure func render ( self ) String } // A mixin contributes methods and requires methods, never fields. export mixin Summarised { requires pure func title ( self ) String requires pure func lineCount ( self ) Int export pure func summary ( self ) String { return \"${self.title()} (${self.lineCount()} lines)\" } } export class Invoice implements Renderable with Summarised { export customer : CustomerId export currency : Currency lines : List [ Line ] // module-private field status : InvoiceStatus // Associated constructor function: no `self`, called as Invoice.create(...). export pure try func create ( customer : CustomerId , currency : Currency , lines : owned List [ Line ] ) Invoice { if lines . isEmpty ( ) { fail EmptyInvoice ( ) } for line in lines { if line . unitPrice . currency != currency { fail CurrencyMismatch ( expected : currency , found : line . unitPrice . currency ) } } return Invoice ( customer : customer , currency : currency , lines : move lines , status : InvoiceStatus . Draft ) } export pure func title ( self ) String => \"Invoice for customer ${self.customer}\" export pure func lineCount ( self ) Int => self . lines . count ( ) // Mutating method: the receiver is an exclusive loan. export pure func issue ( editable self , now : Timestamp ) Void { self . status = InvoiceStatus . Issued ( at : now ) } export pure try func total ( self ) Money { let mut sum = Money . zero ( self . currency ) for line in self . lines { let lineTotal = line . total ( ) or fail sum = Money . add ( sum , lineTotal ) or fail } return move sum } export pure func render ( self ) String { let mut out = text . Builder . create ( ) editable out . appendLine ( self . summary ( ) ) for line in self . lines { editable out . appendLine ( \"${line.quantity} x ${line.description}\" ) } let statusText = match self . status { Draft => \"draft\" Issued ( at : issuedAt ) => \"issued ${issuedAt}\" Paid ( at : paidAt , reference : ref ) => \"paid ${paidAt} (${ref})\" Voided ( reason : why ) => \"void: ${why}\" } editable out . appendLine ( statusText ) return out . finish ( ) } } // A free function using a closure with an explicit capture list and kind. export pure func countExpensive ( invoice : Invoice , threshold : Money ) Int { let isExpensive = pure fn [ alias threshold ] ( line : Line ) Bool { return Money . greaterThan ( line . unitPrice , threshold ) } return invoice . lines . countWhere ( isExpensi"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#2-a-complete-worked-module","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"2. A complete worked module","m":"spec","x":"ney . greaterThan ( line . unitPrice , threshold ) } return invoice . lines . countWhere ( isExpensive ) } // Defer runs at every exit of the block, before locals are dropped; its body // is checked at each exit, so `audit` may be used freely in between. export recovers func withAudit ( audit : editable List [ String ] , invoice : Invoice ) Money ? { defer { editable audit . push ( \"checked ${invoice.customer}\" ) } let total : Money ? = invoice . total ( ) catch e { report e recover null } return move total } test \"total sums lines\" { let lines = List . of ( Line ( description : \"Widget\" , quantity : 2 , unitPrice : Money . of ( Currency . GBP , 10d ) ) , Line ( description : \"Gadget\" , quantity : 1 , unitPrice : Money . of ( Currency . GBP , 5d ) ) , ) let invoice = Invoice . create ( customer : CustomerId ( 42 ) , currency : Currency . GBP , lines : move lines ) or fail expect ( invoice . total ( ) or fail ) == Money . of ( Currency . GBP , 25d ) } test \"quantity boundary\" cases { \"minimum\" : { quantity : 1 , expected : 10d } \"ten\" : { quantity : 10 , expected : 100d } } as row { let line = Line ( description : \"x\" , quantity : row . quantity , unitPrice : Money . of ( Currency . GBP , 10d ) ) expect ( line . total ( ) or fail ) . amount == row . expected } An application in another package uses this library through an import and an entry point: Fortis // accept \u2014 src/app.ft in package acme.invoicing-cli; project.yaml has `entry: app` import fortis . host { Host } import acme . billing . invoices { Invoice , Line , CustomerId } import fortis . money { Money , Currency } export try func main ( args : List [ String ] ) Void { let caps = Host . capabilities ( ) // the only source of I/O authority (section 11.6) let lines = List . of ( Line ( description : \"Consulting\" , quantity : 3 , unitPrice : Money . of ( Currency . GBP , 120d ) ) ) let mut invoice = Invoice . create ( customer : CustomerId ( 7 ) , currency : Currency . GBP , lines : move lines ) or fail editable invoice . issue ( Timestamp . epoch ( ) ) caps . console . print ( invoice . render ( ) ) } The rest of this document explains every construct used above."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#3-lexical-structure","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"3. Lexical structure","m":"spec","x":"3. Lexical structure #"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#31-source-text","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"3.1 Source text","m":"spec","x":"3.1 Source text # Source files are UTF-8 with the extension .ft . Identifiers are ASCII in v1: a letter or underscore followed by letters, digits or underscores. Unicode is permitted in string literals, comments and data. A byte-order mark is rejected. Source text is an untrusted input with quotas (PROG-SEC-01): a file over 16 MiB or 2,000,000 tokens, or nesting brackets, blocks, prefix operators or string interpolations more than 256 deep, is E-LIM-002 and is not parsed."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#32-lines-statements-and-continuation","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"3.2 Lines, statements and continuation","m":"spec","x":"3.2 Lines, statements and continuation # A newline ends a statement unless one of the following holds: the newline is inside ( \u2026 ) , [ \u2026 ] or { \u2026 } that belongs to the same expression or argument list; the last token on the line is one of the trailing continuation tokens : , ( [ = => + - * / % << >> & | ^ && || ?? == != < <= > >= .. ..< . ; the < that opens a refinement and the > that closes one are distinct tokens from the comparison operators (the lexer knows it is in a refinement, Appendix lexer note 2) and are not continuation tokens, so type Score = Int<0..100> ends its line normally; the first token on the next line is one of the leading continuation tokens : . or catch ?? else ) ] with where implements derive from . Parsing decisions. The grammar is parsed with a bounded lookahead over token classes and no symbol table ( LANG-03-03 ). Three decisions resolve the places where the grammar alone would allow more than one reading, and a generated parser must make exactly these (X-94): (P1) the lexer emits one NL for any run of newlines, blank lines and comment-only lines; (P2) an option or repetition whose body can start is taken, so operator and postfix chains are as long as possible and an expression-bodied lambda or match arm extends to the end of the enclosing expression; (P3) where alternatives overlap they are tried in grammar order and the first that parses wins, within the current statement: a statement that starts with a place is an assignment when an assignment operator follows the place and an expression otherwise, a for header is read as move place , then a range, then an expression, let x = move p is the plain let , a parenthesised type is tried before a tuple type, and .name( is a method call. Braces are of two kinds. A body brace opens a statement block (after a function head, if , else , while , for , loop , defer , scope , a match arm's => , catch e , or an inner block statement) or a declaration body ( class , enum , interface , mixin , error , contract , aspect , observation , implement , match , a cases table). Inside a body, items are separated by one or more newlines; newlines directly after the { and directly before the } are permitted and ignored, so a body may be empty, multi-line, or written on one line as { statement } . An expression brace (record literal, record type) is an ordinary delimiter under the first rule above. The kind is decided by the token before the { : after = , ( , , , : , [ , ?? , == , != , a compound assignment operator, return , give , fail or recover it is an expression brace; after anything else, including the name or parameter list of a declaration head, it is a body brace. The first rule applies to the innermost enclosing delimiter: a newline inside a body brace that is itself inside ( or [ still ends a statement, so a multi-line closure body in an argument list is written like any other body. A record type or record literal tolerates newlines directly after its { and directly before its } whichever kind the lexer assigned, and , already continues a line, so a record whose fields each sit on one line parses under either classification. The classification is visible only where a record's { is classified as a body brace, which happens for a record type directly after ) (a return type) and a record literal directly after => (an expression body): inside such a record a field broken across lines is a parse error, so it is written on one line or in parentheses. There is no automatic semicolon insertion and no semicolon token. A ; anywhere outside a string, a comment or a raw template region (section 16) is a parse error ( E-SYN-001 ). A token sequence that is not a sentence of the grammar and matches no more specific syntax code is E-SYN-000 . return , fail and give take their operand on the same logical line; a bare return on its own line returns Void ."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#33-identifier-categories-by-case","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"3.3 Identifier categories by case","m":"spec","x":"3.3 Identifier categories by case # The first character of an identifier decides its grammatical category. This rule is lexical, needs no symbol table, and is what lets the parser distinguish generic application from indexing and refinement from comparison. First character Category Examples Uppercase letter type, interface, mixin, enum, enum variant, contract, error kind, observation schema Invoice , List , Draft , FastCalculation Lowercase letter or _ value, function, method, field, parameter, module alias, label invoice , total , text , outer Module path segments are lowercase ( billing.invoices ). A package name is a dotted sequence of lowercase segments ( acme.billing ). Constants are ordinary lowercase bindings declared with const ."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#34-keywords","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"3.4 Keywords","m":"spec","x":"3.4 Keywords # Keywords are reserved: they cannot be used as identifiers in any declaration or binding position ( E-SYN-002 ). The one relaxation is member position: after . , and as the name of a method or associated function, a keyword is an ordinary name (the grammar's memberName ), which is what lets the library spell String.from(view) and Customer.where(...) . The lexer never reclassifies a keyword; the grammar admits it where memberName appears. The complete inventory, with the section that defines each word, is Appendix A. Kits add declaration heads ( application , model , component , context , \u2026); these are also reserved by the core grammar so that no module using any kit can use them as identifiers (section 16)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#35-literals","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"3.5 Literals","m":"spec","x":"3.5 Literals # Literal Form Type Integer 0 , 42 , 1_000_000 , 0xFF , 0b1010 , 0o17 from context; default Int . The literal's value must fit the target type and refinement, checked at compile time ( E-REF-001 , a violated refinement: the literal's interval is exact). Float 1.5 , 2e10 , 3.0f32 Float ; suffix f32 gives Float32 . A float literal needs a . or an exponent. Decimal 10d , 19.99d Decimal Boolean true , false Bool String \"text ${expr}\" StringView from static for literals without interpolation (static storage); String (allocates) when interpolation is present: a fresh value that a call may borrow as a StringView for the statement, but that a binding annotated StringView cannot hold ( E-TYP-040 ); every interpolated expression must be Displayable ( E-TYP-040 naming Displayable as the expected type) Raw string \"\"\"\u2026\"\"\" StringView from static ; no interpolation, no escapes Byte string b\"abc\" BytesView from static Duration 250ms , 3s , 2min , 1h Duration Null null the absent case of any T? Underscores may separate digit groups. A literal of a trivial type is a fresh value and never needs an ownership marker."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#36-strings-and-interpolation","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"3.6 Strings and interpolation","m":"spec","x":"3.6 Strings and interpolation # ${expression} inside a double-quoted string interpolates. The interpolated text is on one line and contains no comment. The expression is evaluated exactly once, left to right with the rest of the string, and must have a type implementing Displayable . An interpolated expression cannot be a fallible call or an await ; write the call on its own line and interpolate the binding. Interpolation takes an implicit read loan of the places it names. A backslash escapes the character after it, so \\${ is the text ${ and opens no hole: a literal whose every ${ is escaped has no interpolation and is a StringView from static (3.5, 6.6). Escaping for HTML or other contexts is never part of string interpolation; templates in the Web UI kit apply contextual escaping separately."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#37-comments","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"3.7 Comments","m":"spec","x":"3.7 Comments # There is one comment form: // to the end of the line. There are no block comments and no separate documentation-comment token. A run of // lines immediately above a declaration, with no blank line between the run and the declaration, is that declaration's documentation; the documentation tool extracts it. A fenced fortis code block inside such a run is compiled as an example and must carry a // accept , // reject , // execute or // illustrative marker on its first line (section 0). Those markers are recognised by the documentation tool and are never attached to a declaration as documentation, so a marker directly above a declaration does not become its description. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence LANG-03-01 A ; outside strings, comments and raw template regions MUST be a parse error. Negative lexer corpus. LANG-03-02 Newline termination and continuation MUST follow the token lists in 3.2 exactly. Continuation corpus with every listed token. LANG-03-03 Identifier category MUST be decided by the first character as in 3.3. Parser ambiguity corpus (no symbol table). LANG-03-04 Every keyword in Appendix A MUST be reserved in every declaration and binding position except the name of a method or associated function; member position after . and that name position MUST admit it as a name ( memberName ). Keyword-as-identifier negative tests. LANG-03-05 Literal typing MUST follow 3.5; an out-of-range literal MUST be rejected at compile time. Literal corpus. LANG-03-06 Interpolated expressions MUST be evaluated once, in order, and MUST NOT be fallible or suspending. Side-effect order and negative tests."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#4-modules-and-packages","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"4. Modules and packages","m":"spec","x":"4. Modules and packages #"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#41-packages","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"4.1 Packages","m":"spec","x":"4.1 Packages # A package is the unit of distribution, versioning and visibility. It is a directory with project.yaml , a lockfile and src/ . The manifest names the package ( name: acme.billing ), its version, its dependencies, its public modules ( exports: ) and, for an application, its entry module ( entry: ). Packages are nominal: two different versions of the same package in one build are different packages, and their types never interchange. YAML schema: fortis.project/v1 name: acme.billing version: 1.4.0 exports: [invoices, money] dependencies: fortis: \"1.0\" The lockfile fortis.lock (written by fortis lock ) pins what a build depends on (PROG-ARC-04). It names the root package's identity, the digests of the compiler, the standard library and the Rust toolchain, and the identity ( name@version ) and content digest of every package the manifests reach, so two versions of one package are two entries. When a package has a lockfile, resolution uses only it. A dependency a manifest names that the lockfile does not pin at that version, a pinned package no manifest reaches, or an unreadable lockfile is E-NAM-020 . A package, compiler, standard library or Rust toolchain whose digest differs from the pinned one is E-NAM-021 . Each record names the divergence. Nothing is fetched to resolve a lockfile. fortis check verifies the package pins and the compiler and standard-library digests, which it computes without running anything; a native build also verifies the Rust toolchain (X-133). A dependency on a standard package ( fortis or fortis.\u2026 ) must name a package of the published compatibility set ( library/fortis/compatibility.yaml ) at the set's version, because kits are versioned separately and tested only together within a set ( PLAT-01-01 ); a version outside the set, or a name that is not in it, is E-NAM-031 . A build target's feature set decides which standard modules exist (Platform Specification 1.5): fortis check --target T and fortis build --target T reject an import of a module the target lacks with E-NAM-030 , naming the module, the feature set it needs and the target's, before any native compilation ( PLAT-01-03 ). A package that ships a Cargo build script is refused by a native build with E-NAM-032 (PROG-CMP-06). A program that links two versions of one package checks with the versions' types kept apart and runs on every backend, each version's functions and types running where they are named; so does a program in which two packages, or two modules, declare the same name (X-136). A type's nominal identity is its name, its declaring package and version, and its declaring module: two modules' types of one name are different types, and a type written through a module alias ( one.Node ) is that module's type. A manifest over 1 MiB or 10,000 lines is E-LIM-003 , a lockfile over 8 MiB or 100,000 lines E-LIM-004 , and JSON the toolchain reads (caches, saved fuzz inputs) over 256 MiB or nested more than 128 deep E-LIM-005 (PROG-SEC-01). A failure inside the toolchain itself is never a crash of the command: a checker defect is E-INT-001 , whose reproduction names a directory holding the input minimised to what still fails and the command that reproduces it, a native build whose generated Rust does not compile is E-INT-002 , at the Fortis function the code came from, and a native build that cannot run a tool it needs (cargo, rustc or the linker driver missing or failing before any generated code is compiled) is E-INT-003 , naming the tool (PROG-CMP-05)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#42-modules","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"4.2 Modules","m":"spec","x":"4.2 Modules # A module is one source file under src/ . Its path is the file path relative to src/ , with directory separators replaced by . and the extension removed: src/invoices.ft is invoices and src/ledger/entries.ft is ledger.entries . The import path seen by other packages is the package name followed by the module path: acme.billing.invoices , acme.billing.ledger.entries . A directory is not itself a module. A module has no header line; its identity comes from its path. Modules of one package may import each other freely, including cyclically for type declarations. Value initialisation across modules is impossible because modules have no initialisation code: a module may contain only declarations and const bindings whose initialisers are compile-time constant expressions. Importing a module therefore never executes anything."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#43-imports","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"4.3 Imports","m":"spec","x":"4.3 Imports # An import statement appears only at the top of a module, before any declaration. Fortis // accept import acme . billing . invoices // package acme.billing, module invoices; binds the alias `invoices` import acme . billing . invoices as inv // binds the alias `inv` import acme . billing . invoices { Invoice , Line } // binds the named members directly import fortis . text // binds `text` An import path is a package name followed by a module path. The split is decided by the manifest: the longest prefix of the path that names the current package or one of its declared dependencies is the package, and the remainder is the module path; if no prefix matches, the import is an error naming the undeclared package. A package may declare a root module in its manifest ( root: data ), which an import of the bare package name denotes ( import fortis.data { Db } ); without one, an empty remainder is an error. Package names and the module paths of other packages a project depends on must not be prefixes of one another in a way that makes the split ambiguous; the toolchain rejects a dependency set in which they are ( E-NAM-010 ). The core package fortis has the modules prelude , text , collections , numeric , time , money and host , and every kit package is named fortis.<kit> with a kit name that is not one of those module names. Only modules listed in the exporting package's exports: can be imported from another package. Inside a package, any module can be imported by its siblings. A plain import binds the final path segment as a module alias; members are accessed as alias.member . A braced import binds the listed members directly and nothing else. Importing the same name twice, or importing a name that collides with a local declaration, is an error. Imports are unused-checked: an unused import is a warning at the baseline lint level and an error at the strict lint level. The standard package is fortis . Its module fortis.prelude is imported implicitly into every module and provides the core types ( Int , String , List , Map , Outcome , the optional operators, \u2026), the built-in interfaces of section 6.10 and nothing else. Every other standard module needs an import."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#44-visibility","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"4.4 Visibility","m":"spec","x":"4.4 Visibility # There is one visibility modifier, export . A declaration without export is private to its module. An exported declaration is visible to every module that imports the module. Whether it is visible outside the package depends on the manifest: only modules listed in exports: are importable from other packages, so an export in an unlisted module reaches sibling modules only. Fields, methods and associated functions of a class are private unless marked export . A use of a declaration or member that is not visible in the using scope is rejected at the use site ( E-NAM-004 not visible ). An exported signature may name only exported types: a non-exported class, enum, newtype or interface in an exported function, method, field or constant signature is rejected ( E-TYP-030 , the same code as for structural types in 6.9). An exported field is readable through any loan and writable through an editable loan. The generated constructor of a class (section 9.2) is available in a scope only if every field of the class is visible in that scope. Enum variants, interface members and mixin-required methods have no visibility of their own: they are visible wherever their enum, interface or mixin is."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#45-names-and-resolution","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"4.5 Names and resolution","m":"spec","x":"4.5 Names and resolution # Each module has one namespace containing its declarations, imported aliases and imported members. A name resolves to the innermost enclosing block that declares it, then to the module namespace. Qualified names have the forms module.member , Type.associated , Enum.Variant and value.field or value.method(...) . There is no :: . A type is not a value: a type name in an expression is a constructor call, the qualifier of an associated member or a variant, or a variant the expected enum type names (9.7); anything else is E-TYP-040 . Void alone is also its one value. Shadowing a visible binding with a let is an error ( E-NAM-003 ). Fields are always accessed through a receiver ( self.lines , invoice.lines ); a bare field name is not in scope inside a method."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#46-entry-point","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"4.6 Entry point","m":"spec","x":"4.6 Entry point # An application package names an entry module in project.yaml ( entry: app ). That module must declare a function named main with one of these heads, where \u2026 is the parameter list described below: Fortis // illustrative func main(\u2026) try func main(\u2026) async func main(\u2026) async try func main(\u2026) main returns nothing: its result type is Void , written or left out (8.1, X-345). Each form may also carry recovers , which a main whose summary has that effect writes like any function (8.1, 11.2; X-155): it is transitive, so without it no program could recover a failure anywhere main reaches, and it changes nothing a caller relies on, since main has none. An async entry runs on the core cooperative executor as the root task; its completion ends the process. export may be written before any form and is implied when it is not: the entry module's main is the entry whether or not it is exported (X-345). Parameters, supplied by type. The runtime supplies each parameter of the entry main by its type, so the parameters say what the program needs and nothing else (X-345). A parameter is name: T with no mode, default or ... , in any order and under any name, where T is either List[String] (the prelude's), which receives the command-line arguments, or one of the ten capability types of 11.6, imported from fortis.host , which receives that capability. Each type appears at most once, and a main that needs nothing takes no parameters. A parameter of any other type is rejected ( E-NAM-005 ), as is a second parameter of a type already taken ( E-NAM-006 ). main owns its capability parameters, as it owns a value it created: it lends them to the functions it calls, and it may move one, for example into a Shared ( Shared.create(move console) , 7.8). The command-line arguments are lent to main , as before. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console , FileSystem , Path } try func main ( console : Console , files : FileSystem ) Void { let path = Path . parse ( \"greeting.txt\" ) or fail files . writeAll ( path , \"hello from main\" ) or fail console . print ( files . readText ( path ) or fail ) } The v0.0.1 form, export func main(args: List[String]) Void with Host.capabilities() called in the body, stays accepted: args is one parameter of that list, and a main that takes no capability parameter may still create its capabilities with one direct call of Host.capabilities() (11.6). A main that takes a capability parameter has had its capabilities created by the runtime, so it may not call Host.capabilities() ( E-EFF-010 ). The formatter rewrites the v0.0.1 form to this one wherever the rewrite keeps the program's meaning: the capabilities the body reaches through caps.console and its siblings, or through a Capabilities(...) destructuring, become parameters named after their fields or bindings, args is dropped when nothing reads it, and the export is dropped. The entry main is that function: the main of the manifest's entry module, or of a single-file program. It obtains I/O authority through its capability parameters, or by calling Host.capabilities() from fortis.host exactly once, directly in its own body (section 11.6; the capability types and Host.capabilities() are part of the core runtime inventory, section 15). Only the root program's entry main receives capabilities (X-271): a function named main anywhere else is an ordinary function, and every other function receives a capability as an ordinary parameter. Only the runtime calls the entry main , once: Fortis code never refers to it ( E-EFF-010 ). If a try main fails, the runtime prints exactly one report of the failure to standard error and exits with status 1. That report, like every record written by report , is a structured failure record (code, payload, receipt) rendered as text on a terminal and as one JSON line otherwise (Delivery Programme 7.4). There is no or die operator. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence LANG-04-01 Module identity MUST be derived from the file path; modules MUS"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#46-entry-point","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"4.6 Entry point","m":"spec","x":"ement Acceptance evidence LANG-04-01 Module identity MUST be derived from the file path; modules MUST contain only declarations and constant bindings. Module with statement at top level rejected. LANG-04-02 Importing a module MUST NOT execute any code. (old MOD-001) Module initialisation tests. LANG-04-03 Only modules listed in the exporting package's exports: MAY be imported across packages. Cross-package negative tests. LANG-04-04 Different versions of one package, and different modules, MUST have distinct nominal types. Diamond-dependency corpus; same-name types of two modules. LANG-04-05 Shadowing a visible binding MUST be rejected. Nested block corpus. LANG-04-06 The entry point MUST be the main of the manifest's entry module, exported or not, with a head from 4.6 (the four forms, each optionally recovers ); each of its parameters MUST be List[String] or a capability type, each type at most once ( E-NAM-005 , E-NAM-006 ), and the runtime MUST supply each by its type. Missing/wrong entry fixtures; a recovers entry; capability-parameter fixtures (unknown, duplicate, moved into Shared ) executed on every backend."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#5-statements-and-expressions","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"5. Statements and expressions","m":"spec","x":"5. Statements and expressions #"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#51-blocks-and-bindings","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"5.1 Blocks and bindings","m":"spec","x":"5.1 Blocks and bindings # A block is { statements } . A block introduces a scope. Leaving a block by any route (falling off the end, return , fail , break , continue , give ) first ends every loan whose holding binding was declared in the block (section 7.3), then runs the block's registered defer s in reverse order of registration, then drops the block's owned locals in reverse order of initialisation. let name = expression declares an immutable binding; let mut name = expression declares a mutable one. let Pattern = move place destructures a class, tuple or record (section 9.8) into new bindings, so its pattern matches every value of the place's type: a pattern that can fail to match, a variant, a literal, null , present(\u2026) , success(\u2026) , failure(\u2026) or is \u2026 , at any depth, is E-MAT-004 , and match handles the other cases (D-243). The binding is visible from the next statement to the end of the enclosing block. A let without an initialiser is not permitted. The type is inferred from the initialiser; let name: Type = expression states it explicitly and is required when the initialiser is a literal that should take a refined or wider type. An inner { \u2026 } block is itself a statement; its only purpose is to end loans and drop locals early (section 7.3). name = expression is assignment. It is well-formed only when name is a visible let mut binding, or a field path whose root is a let mut binding, an editable parameter or an editable loan. Assigning to an immutable binding, a parameter or an unknown name is an error. Assignment is a statement, not an expression, and does not chain. Fortis // reject: E-NAM-001, E-NAM-002 (at the marked lines) let a = 1 let mut b = a // trivial copy, no marker b = b + 1 // proof: a is 1, b is 2, no overflow // reject: E-NAM-001 \u2014 `c` is not declared c = 3 // reject: E-NAM-002 \u2014 `a` is immutable a = 2 const name = expression declares a module-level constant whose initialiser is a constant expression : a literal, a const , arithmetic over those, an enum value, a record or variant-map literal of constant expressions, the name of a function or declaration, a call to one of the of constructors ( List.of , Routes.of and the other Type.of(\u2026) constructors the fortis package marks as constant-foldable) with constant arguments, or Type.default() of a type whose Default is derived. Constants are built once at load with no code of the program running; they are not captures and need no marker."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#52-conditionals","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"5.2 Conditionals","m":"spec","x":"5.2 Conditionals # Fortis // illustrative if condition { \u2026 } else if condition { \u2026 } else { \u2026 } The condition is a Bool expression; nothing else is accepted ( E-TYP-010 ). if is a statement. It is also an expression when it has an else and every branch block ends with give value ; the branch types must unify to one type. Fortis // accept let label = if count == 0 { give \"none\" } else { give \"some\" } Refinement facts established by the condition apply inside the branch (section 6.3). The else branch sees the negation."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#53-loops","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"5.3 Loops","m":"spec","x":"5.3 Loops # Fortis // illustrative while condition { \u2026 } for item in iterable { \u2026 } for i in start..<end { \u2026 } // also start..end (inclusive) loop { \u2026 } while evaluates its Bool condition before each iteration. for item in iterable requires the iterable's type to implement Iterable[T, I] for some cursor I ; item is bound once per iteration, immutably. Iterating a collection place by name ( for line in self.lines ) takes a read loan of the place for the duration of the loop and binds item as a read loan of each element; iterating a move of the place consumes it through Consumable and binds owned elements. for i in a..<b iterates integers; when a and b are constants or interval-tracked places, i has the refined type Int<a..<b> (section 6.3). The index binding is immutable and may not be assigned. loop repeats until break . break and continue apply to the innermost loop unless given a label: outer: for \u2026 { \u2026 break outer } . Labels are lowercase identifiers followed by : before while , for or loop . break carries no value in v1. break , continue , return and fail leave every block between the statement and the target; each block is exited in order, innermost first, with the exit sequence of 5.1. The Iterable and Iterator interfaces are: Fortis // accept interface Iterable [ T , I ] where I implements Iterator [ T ] { pure func iterate ( self ) I from self // I is a standard cursor: an owned value that stores a read loan of self } interface Iterator [ T ] { pure func next ( editable self ) ( alias T from self ) ? // a read loan of the next element, drawn from the cursor's stored loan } interface Consumable [ T , I ] where I implements Consumer [ T ] { func consume ( owned self ) I // an owned cursor that owns the collection } interface Consumer [ T ] { func next ( editable self ) T ? // the next element, owned } A collection names its concrete cursor type as the second parameter ( List[T] implements Iterable[T, ListCursor[T]] ), which is how the result type is static without associated types. Cursors are standard library types (7.7): a user collection implements Iterable by returning the cursor of the standard collection it holds. for item in p desugars to let mut cursor = alias p.iterate() followed by a loop that calls editable cursor.next() until null . The cursor is an owned loan-carrying value (7.7): the binding owns it, so editable cursor.next() loans the cursor itself (the child-loan rule applies to loan-holding bindings, not to owners of loan-carrying values); the read loan of p it stores stays a read loan for the binding's lifetime; advancing the cursor writes the cursor's own storage and is therefore footprint-free in a pure function (11.3); and item is the read loan of the element that next returns, which lasts for that iteration. The lowering is fn iterate<'a>(&'a self) -> ListCursor<'a, T> and fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a T> , the element reference carrying the cursor's stored lifetime 'a , not the exclusive borrow of the call, so the exclusive loan of the cursor ends when next returns and two consecutive next results may both be live (Appendix C). for item in move p uses Consumable instead and binds owned elements. The compiler may elide the cursor for standard collections; the observable order is the collection's documented order. The compiler may elide the iterator for standard collections; the observable order is the collection's documented order."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#54-function-exits","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"5.4 Function exits","m":"spec","x":"5.4 Function exits # return expression leaves the enclosing function with a success value. A function whose success type is Void may use bare return or fall off the end of its body. A function with a non- Void success type must end every path in return , fail or a diverging call; there is no implicit value. A path that reaches the end of such a body is E-TYP-110 ; the same code reports a match arm block or if -expression branch in expression position that does not end in give . fail failure leaves the enclosing try function with a failure. It is an expression of type Never , so it may appear where any type is expected, including as a match arm. fail outside a try function is an error ( E-ERR-001 ). give expression ends the enclosing match arm block or if expression branch with a value; a catch block ends with recover , never give . recover expression ends a catch block with a replacement success value (section 10.4). return and fail always refer to the enclosing function , never to an enclosing closure body being written inline as an argument: inside a lambda they refer to the lambda. give and recover never cross a function or lambda boundary. A give outside a match arm or if -expression branch, a recover outside a catch block, and a break or continue outside a loop are E-TYP-120 ."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#55-expression-statements-and-discard","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"5.5 Expression statements and discard","m":"spec","x":"5.5 Expression statements and discard # An expression used as a statement must have type Void , Never , or be explicitly discarded: discard expression ; an unused non- Void value without discard is E-TYP-140 . Discarding an Outcome , a Future , a Task or any other linear value (section 6.12) is an error ( E-OBL-001 ). discard exists so that an unused non-linear result is visibly intentional. The single exception is a never-polled future with a non-linear environment (12.3). A function whose result most callers do not need says so once, on its declaration, with the core aspect @ignorable (11.8; X-345): a call of it used directly as a statement ( found.note(line, problem, detail) , with or without a marker on its receiver) may drop a non-linear result without discard . Writing discard there stays accepted. Every other unused result still needs discard , and a linear result can never be dropped: @ignorable on a function whose result is linear (a try or async function, or a linear success type) is rejected ( E-TYP-150 ), and a call whose instantiated result is linear is E-OBL-001 as before. Fortis // accept @ ignorable func remember ( seen : editable Set [ Int ] , n : Int ) Bool { return editable seen . add ( n ) } func collect ( ) Int { let mut seen : Set [ Int ] = Set . default ( ) remember ( editable seen , 3 ) discard remember ( editable seen , 3 ) return 1 }"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#56-operators-and-precedence","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"5.6 Operators and precedence","m":"spec","x":"5.6 Operators and precedence # All binary operators are left-associative except where stated. Higher rows bind tighter. Level Operators Associativity Notes 1 a.b a.b(\u2026) a[i] a(\u2026) a?.b left postfix 2 -a !a await a ; copy p alias p editable p move p right unary; the markers take a place (optionally followed by a call on it), never an expression, so await editable db.insert(x) is await of the marked call and copy await f() is a parse error 4 * / % left integer operands need proof (6.4) 5 + - left 6 << >> left shift amount must be proved in range 7 < <= > >= none comparisons do not chain 8 == != none 9 & left bitwise and 10 ^ left bitwise xor 11 | left bitwise or 12 && left short-circuit 13 || left short-circuit 14 a ?? b right optional coalescing; b evaluated only when a is null 15 a or fail , a or recover b , a catch e { \u2026 } right outcome handling; b and the catch body are evaluated only on failure; an Int arithmetic a is a checked region (6.4) 16 a..b a..<b none ranges; only in for headers and refinement types Outcome operators (level 15), ?? (level 14) and the Boolean short-circuit operators (levels 12\u201313) may not be mixed in one expression without parentheses: a || b ?? c and a ?? b || c parse and are rejected on the parse tree ( E-SYN-004 ); f() or fail ?? 0 is not a sentence of the grammar at all, because or fail ends the expression, and is the generic parse error ( E-SYN-000 ). await f() or fail parses as (await f()) or fail . copy await f() is a parse error: markers take a place, and await f() is not a place; write let v = await f() first."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#57-evaluation-order","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"5.7 Evaluation order","m":"spec","x":"5.7 Evaluation order # Operands and arguments are evaluated left to right, exactly once. The receiver of a method call is evaluated before the arguments. Short-circuit operators evaluate their right operand only when needed. move x || move x is rejected because the second move uses a moved place on a reachable path (section 7). A call f(a1, \u2026, an) executes in four steps: Evaluate. Receiver and arguments are evaluated left to right. An argument that is a marked place ( editable p , move p , copy p ) or an unmarked ownership-bearing place reserves a loan or move of that place but does not activate it. An argument that is an expression (a nested call, arithmetic, a literal) is evaluated to a value here; every loan taken during that evaluation (for example the receiver loan of buffer.length() ) ends when the value is produced, unless the value itself carries a loan, in which case the carried loan is reserved like a marked place. Three kinds of value carry loans: a from result, a view, and a future (an async call's result carries every loan its arguments took, 7.3). A loan-carrying value keeps its loans wherever it is moved, into a binding, a launch , or another call, and a synchronous call's release step never ends a loan carried by one of its arguments. A reservation is broken by any later argument that moves, assigns or exclusively loans the reserved place or a prefix of it; this is an error at the breaking argument ( E-OWN-012 ). Activate. All reservations become active at once, after the last argument has been evaluated. Conflicts among them, or with loans already active from enclosing statements, are reported here. Invoke. The callee runs. Release. Loans activated for the call end when the call returns , unless the result carries them: a from result (7.6), a view, or the future of an async callee (7.3), in which case the loan is held by the value and by the binding that receives it. Because release happens before the enclosing statement finishes, sum = Money.add(sum, lineTotal) or fail is legal: the read loan of sum taken for the call has ended by the time the assignment drops the old value and stores the new one (7.9). Fortis // reject: E-OWN-012 (at the marked lines) \u2014 the read loan for buffer.length() ends in step 1; the editable loan activates in step 2 update ( editable buffer , buffer . length ( ) ) // reject: E-OWN-012 \u2014 the second argument moves a place reserved by the first update ( editable buffer , consume ( move buffer ) ) Temporaries created in step 1 are dropped at the end of the full statement, in reverse order of creation."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#58-calls-named-arguments-and-defaults","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"5.8 Calls, named arguments and defaults","m":"spec","x":"5.8 Calls, named arguments and defaults # Arguments may be positional or named ( name: value ). Positional arguments come first and bind in declaration order; named arguments follow in any order. Each parameter is bound once. A pun writes a named argument whose value is the local of the label's name once: wanted stands for wanted: wanted (X-345). A bare local name is a pun where a positional argument cannot stand: after a named argument or another pun, and anywhere in a class's generated constructor, whose arguments are all named (9.2). Before the first named argument of any other call a bare name is positional, as before, and binds the parameter in its position. A pun is matched by its label as the written form is, so a name that is no parameter or field, or one bound twice, is E-TYP-130 ; it passes the place unmarked, so where the parameter or field takes ownership of an ownership-bearing local the marker is written and the argument is not a pun ( name: move name ). The formatter writes the pun wherever it means the same. A positional argument after a named one, a name that is not a parameter, a parameter bound twice and an argument beyond the parameter list are E-TYP-130 ; a parameter without a default that no argument binds is E-TYP-050 , the code that also reports a constructor call missing a field. Parameter names are part of a function's public signature: renaming an exported parameter is a breaking change. A parameter may declare a default: func page(size: Int<1..100> = 20) . A default is a pure, non-allocating constant expression re-evaluated at each call that omits the argument. User functions have no variadic parameters; a function that takes many values takes a ListView[T] and the caller passes List.of(\u2026) or an existing collection. The Type.of(\u2026) constructors in the fortis package are the one variadic form, implemented as compiler intrinsics that accept any number of arguments of the element type. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence LANG-05-01 let MUST declare, and a let pattern MUST match every value of its type ( E-MAT-004 ); = MUST only assign to a visible mutable target. Declaration/assignment corpus; refutable- let corpus. LANG-05-02 Every block exit MUST end the block's loans, run defers LIFO, then drop locals in reverse initialisation order. Exit-order execute tests for each exit kind. LANG-05-03 if , while conditions MUST be Bool . Negative corpus. LANG-05-04 A ranged for MUST refine its index as in 5.3; the index MUST be immutable. Refinement execute tests. LANG-05-05 Non- Void functions MUST end every path in return , fail or Never . (old SYN-001 part) Missing-return corpus. LANG-05-06 Precedence and associativity MUST follow 5.6; mixing the listed operator families without parentheses MUST be a parse error. Precedence corpus. LANG-05-07 Evaluation MUST follow 5.7 and be identical on both backends. (old SYN-004) Side-effect order differential tests. LANG-05-08 Discarding a linear value MUST be rejected; discarding any other non- Void value MUST require discard , except the result of a call statement of a function declared @ignorable (5.5). Discard corpus; @ignorable fixtures. LANG-05-09 Named arguments MUST follow positionals, bind each parameter once, and be part of the exported signature; a pun MUST bind the parameter or field of its name, exactly where 5.8 allows one (X-345). Signature-diff tests; pun corpus."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#6-types","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6. Types","m":"spec","x":"6. Types #"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#61-overview","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.1 Overview","m":"spec","x":"6.1 Overview # Fortis is nominally typed. A type is one of: a built-in type; a type newtype; a class; an enum; an interface used as a dynamic type; a function type; a tuple or anonymous record; a refinement of a built-in; a generic instantiation of any of these; a loan or view type. There is no top type, no Any , no implicit conversion between distinct types and no runtime type mutation. A value used where a type it is not assignable to is expected (an argument, an initialiser, an assignment, a return, an operand) is rejected with E-TYP-040 , naming both types, unless a more specific code applies (a non- Bool condition is E-TYP-010 , an unproved refinement E-REF-001 ); sections 3.5, 9.4 and 10.1 give instances (X-152)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#62-built-in-types","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.2 Built-in types","m":"spec","x":"6.2 Built-in types # Group Types Unit Void (one value), Never (no value) Boolean Bool Integers Int8 Int16 Int32 Int64 UInt8 UInt16 UInt32 UInt64 ; Int = Int64 ; UInt = UInt64 ; Byte = UInt8 Floating Float32 (binary32), Float (binary64) Exact Decimal (sign, 96-bit coefficient, scale 0..28), BigInt (heap, allocates) Text String (owned UTF-8), StringView (borrowed UTF-8 view), Bytes (owned), BytesView Time Duration , Instant (monotonic), Timestamp (wall clock, UTC) Collections List[T] , Array[T, N] , ListView[T] , Map[K, V] , OrderedMap[K, V] , Set[T] , FixedList[T, N] , FixedMap[K, V, N] Compound tuples (A, B) , anonymous records { name: A, other: B } , variant maps VariantMap[E, V] written { Found: 200, NotFound: 404 } (one entry per variant of E , checked for completeness) Optional T? Outcome Outcome[T] , Failure , Report Concurrency Future[T] , Task[T] , TaskCompletion[T] , Shared[T] , Weak[T] , Mutex[T] , AtomicInt , AtomicBool Callable fn(Params) Ret , fn mut(Params) Ret , fn once(Params) Ret , with modifiers (section 8.4) N in Array[T, N] and FixedList[T, N] is an integer literal. Functions generic over N are not supported in v1. List.of(a, b, c) builds a list from zero or more arguments. It is declared with a variadic last parameter , of(...items: T) , a form allowed only in trusted bodiless signatures of the standard package: there are no user-defined variadic functions in v1, and a variadic parameter takes positional arguments only."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#63-refinements","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.3 Refinements","m":"spec","x":"6.3 Refinements # A refinement restricts a built-in integer, text, byte or collection type by a predicate written in <\u2026> after the type name. Refinement syntax is accepted only in type position. Form Meaning Int<0..100> inclusive range Int<0..<100> exclusive upper bound Int<1..> / Int<..0> one-sided String<scalars: 1..120> Unicode scalar count Bytes<length: 0..65536> , List[T]<length: 0..100> element count A refinement is a structural subtype of its base and of any refinement whose range contains it: Int<0..50> is assignable to Int<0..100> and to Int without any operation. The reverse direction never happens implicitly. Float refinements are not supported in v1 ( E-TYP-020 ). Facts and the interval domain. The checker tracks, for every integer-typed place in a function, an interval [lo, hi] within the bounds of the base type. The rules are: A literal has the exact interval of its value. A parameter or field has the interval of its declared type. A call result has the interval of the callee's declared return type, except that the success value of a checked integer intrinsic ( Int.add(a, b) and the other members of the checked family, 6.4) has the interval of the operation on its operands clipped to the type's range, because the call fails instead of overflowing (X-93); so has a checked operator (6.4, X-345). + , - , * combine intervals by interval arithmetic; / and % require the divisor's interval to exclude zero and produce the interval of the quotient or remainder; << requires the amount's interval to lie in [0, width-1] . An if , while or match condition of the forms p < c , p <= c , p > c , p >= c , p == c , p != c where p is a tracked place and c is a literal, a const or another tracked place narrows p (and c when it is a place) in the true branch; && narrows by both conjuncts; the else branch receives the negation of a single comparison, and so does the rest of the enclosing block when the true branch ends in return , fail , break , continue or a Never call (the same diverging-branch rule as null tests, 6.8), which is what lets a guard clause like if value < low { return low } narrow value afterwards. || and function calls do not narrow. At a join of control-flow paths, the interval is the hull of the incoming intervals. At a loop header, every tracked place assigned anywhere in the loop body is widened to its declared type's full range, except the index of a ranged for , which has the interval of the range. Loop-carried facts therefore come from the declared type of the let mut binding, so a counter should be declared with a refined type: let mut i: Int<0..1000> = 0 . Invalidation. Every fact about a place is discarded when the place, a prefix of it, or a field of it is passed as an editable argument or receiver, exclusively loaned, or reachable from an editable capture of a closure that is called; the place then has the interval of its declared type. Assignment to the place itself discards the old facts and establishes the interval of the assigned expression. Inside a loop, a place invalidated anywhere in the body is widened at the header like an assigned place. Length facts. In the true branch of i < p.count() , where i is a tracked integer place and p a collection place, i is known to be below p 's length; the index of a ranged for i in a..<p.count() is known to be below p 's length throughout the body when the body neither assigns nor exclusively loans p (X-93); i <= p.count() gives only i \u2264 length , which does not permit indexing; i >= p.count() gives the bound in its else branch, while the else branch of i > p.count() gives only i \u2264 length (X-260). These facts say nothing about the lower bound, which must come from i 's type or a separate i >= 0 test, and are invalidated by the invalidation events on either place. With a lower bound of at least 0 and the strict upper bound, list[i] is accepted. Length refinements ( List[T]<length: 0..100> ) are properties of a value, so a place of such a type may not be loaned edit"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#63-refinements","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.3 Refinements","m":"spec","x":"ist[T]<length: 0..100> ) are properties of a value, so a place of such a type may not be loaned editable or mutated ( E-REF-003 ); mutate an unrefined collection and validate the result. Remainder. For a % d with d 's interval excluding zero and m the largest |d| : if a 's interval is within [0, \u221e) the result is [0, m-1] ; if within (-\u221e, 0] , [-(m-1), 0] ; otherwise [-(m-1), m-1] . Assigning a value whose interval lies outside the target's declared refinement is an error ( E-REF-001 unproved refinement ), with the fix being an explicit validate or a wider declared type. There is no solver beyond these rules. UserId.validate(x) for a nominal refined type, and Int<0..100>.validate(x) , are pure fallible conversions returning Outcome[T] ; they are the explicit fallback whenever the interval rules cannot prove a bound. Fortis // accept pure func clamp ( value : Int , low : Int < 0 .. 100 > , high : Int < 0 .. 100 > ) Int < 0 .. 100 > { if value < low { return low } if value > high { return high } return value // interval of value here: [low, high] \u2286 [0, 100] } Fortis // reject: E-REF-001 (at the marked line) pure func sumTo ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int < 0 .. 500500 > { let mut total : Int < 0 .. 500500 > = 0 for i in 0 .. n { // i : Int<0..1000> total = total + i // reject: E-REF-001 \u2014 interval of total+i is [0, 501500] } return total } The second example shows the limit of the domain: the loop widens total to its declared range and the addition then exceeds it. The accepted repair accumulates into an unrefined Int with a checked operation and validates once at the end: Fortis // accept pure try func sumTo ( n : Int < 0 .. 1000 > ) Int < 0 .. 500500 > { let mut total : Int = 0 for i in 0 .. n { total = Int . add ( total , i ) or fail // checked: no proof needed, fails on overflow } return Int < 0 .. 500500 > . validate ( total ) or fail }"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#64-integer-arithmetic","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.4 Integer arithmetic","m":"spec","x":"6.4 Integer arithmetic # Integer operators + - * / % << >> and unary - are proof-required : the operation is accepted only when the interval rules of 6.3 show that overflow, division by zero, out-of-range shift and MIN / -1 cannot occur. Otherwise the operation is rejected with E-REF-002 naming the operand intervals and the checked alternative, or, where there is none, asking for a refined type or a test that proves it. The result type of a proof-required operation is the base type refined to the computed interval. fortis.prelude declares three families of named operations on Int : Family Example Result Declared on Checked Int.add(a, b) , Int.sub , Int.mul , Int.div , Int.rem , Int.shiftLeft Outcome[Int] ; fails with numeric.overflow , numeric.divide_by_zero or numeric.shift_range Int only, in this release Wrapping Int.wrappingAdd(a, b) , \u2026 total, two's complement wrap Int only, in this release Saturating Int.saturatingAdd(a, b) , \u2026 total, clamps to the type bounds Int only, in this release The sized integer types ( Int8 , Int16 , Int32 , UInt8 , UInt16 , UInt32 , UInt64 ) have none of the three families yet; theirs are planned with the complete standard library (v0.4, section 15). Until then an unproved operator on a sized type is made safe by a refined type or a test (X-345). Division truncates toward zero; % takes the sign of the dividend; >> on signed types is arithmetic, on unsigned types logical. Compound assignment a += b is sugar for a = a + b and follows the same proof rule. Integer behaviour never depends on the build mode. Checked operators (X-345). The left operand of an outcome operator ( or fail , or recover , catch ; 10.2) that is integer arithmetic is a checked region : the operators + - * / % << >> and unary - reachable from it through those operators and parentheses, and not through a call's arguments, an index, a field access, a lambda or any other operator. In a checked region an operator on Int that the interval rules cannot prove safe is not rejected: it is the checked operation , Int.add , Int.sub , Int.mul , Int.div , Int.rem or Int.shiftLeft (unary -x is Int.sub(0, x) ), and fails exactly as that function fails, with numeric.overflow , numeric.divide_by_zero or numeric.shift_range and the same payload, on every backend. Its success value has the interval of the operation clipped to Int 's range, as the function's does (6.3); an operator the rules prove safe stays a plain value. A region with a checked operation is an Outcome[Int] , and the outcome operator handles it by the rules of 10.2, as it handles a call: or fail needs a try function ( E-ERR-001 ), and or recover or a catch that recovers adds recovers . Operands are evaluated left to right as everywhere (5.7), and the first checked operation that fails ends the region with its failure: a * b + c or fail fails at the multiplication and does not add. One handler covers the whole arithmetic because the outcome operators bind loosest (5.6), so a * b + c or fail is (a * b + c) or fail . In a test or property body, integer arithmetic outside such an operand is a checked region too, with or fail implied (14). A region whose operators are all proved safe cannot fail, and handling it is E-TYP-040 , as handling any value that is not an outcome is. An unproved operator outside a region stays E-REF-002 , and its record names the checked form: in a binding ( let s = a + b ; write let s = a + b or fail ), under a comparison ( a + b == c or fail , whose region would be a Bool ; write (a + b or fail) == c ), in a call's argument ( f(a + b) or fail handles the call; write f(a + b or fail) or fail ), in a compound assignment ( x += 1 ; write x = x + 1 or fail ), and on the integer types other than Int , which have no checked family. An operand that is itself an outcome is not taken into a region ( a + f() or fail is E-TYP-040 ; handle f() first), and >> has no checked operation, so an unproved >> is E-REF-002 in a region too. The named functions stay: Int.add(a, b)"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#64-integer-arithmetic","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.4 Integer arithmetic","m":"spec","x":"d operation, so an unproved >> is E-REF-002 in a region too. The named functions stay: Int.add(a, b) or fail means what a + b or fail means where the operator is unproved, and the formatter writes the operator form where the two are equivalent."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#65-floating-point-and-decimal","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.5 Floating point and Decimal","m":"spec","x":"6.5 Floating point and Decimal # Float and Float32 operators are total IEEE 754 binary operations with round-to-nearest-even; there is no proof requirement and no overflow failure. The implementation must not reassociate, contract into fused multiply-add or otherwise alter results (strict IEEE is the only mode). + - * / and Float.sqrt are bit-exact across backends and targets, with every NaN result canonicalised to the positive quiet NaN with zero payload before it is stored or compared, so that parity is defined on canonical bits. Transcendental functions live in fortis.numeric.math , document a tolerance in ULPs and are excluded from bit-exact parity claims. Float and Float32 do not implement Equatable , Hashable or Comparable , because NaN breaks their laws. == on floats is IEEE equality (NaN \u2260 NaN) and is allowed; to use floats as map keys or sort them, convert with Float.totalOrder(x) (an Int64 with the IEEE total-order mapping) or Float.bitsEqual(a, b) . Decimal is a sign, a 96-bit unsigned coefficient and a scale 0..28, matching rust_decimal and .NET decimal . It has no NaN or infinity. There are no arithmetic operators on Decimal in v1 (the interval domain is integer-only): arithmetic uses the checked functions Decimal.add , sub , mul , div , rem , which return Outcome[Decimal] and fail with numeric.overflow or numeric.divide_by_zero . Result scale: add / sub use the larger operand scale; mul uses the sum of the scales; div uses the largest scale that fits; when a result needs more than 28 fractional digits or more than 96 coefficient bits it is rounded half to even and, if the integer part still does not fit, fails. Decimal.round(value, scale, mode) is explicit, with mode a RoundingMode ( HalfEven , HalfAwayFromZero , TowardZero , AwayFromZero ); Decimal.rem takes the larger operand scale and the sign of the dividend. Converting from Float requires Decimal.fromFloat(x, scale, mode) and is fallible. A Decimal displays with exactly its scale ( 1.50d as 1.50 ); Float.isNaN(x) and Float.sqrt(x) (NaN for a negative argument) are in the prelude. A Float displays as the shortest digit string that reads back to the same value, in plain notation with a fractional part ( 2e3 as 2000.0 , 0.1 + 0.2 as 0.30000000000000004 ), and the non-finite values as NaN , Infinity and -Infinity ; Float32 displays likewise for binary32."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#66-text-and-views","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.6 Text and views","m":"spec","x":"6.6 Text and views # String is an owned, growable, valid UTF-8 value with value identity: copying it with copy produces an independent string. StringView is a borrowed, immutable, valid UTF-8 slice with a from owner. Bytes and BytesView are the byte equivalents; both have byteCount() and get(index) Byte? , which reads one byte in place and is null outside the buffer, so a noalloc parser can walk a BytesView . A string literal is a StringView from static and may be used wherever a StringView is expected. Where a String is expected (a String field, parameter, let x: String , or a match / if expression whose other arms are String ), a literal converts implicitly by allocating; this is the one implicit conversion in the language, it adds alloc to the summary, and it is therefore rejected inside noalloc code. String.from(view) makes the allocation explicit. copy applies to places only, never to a literal. Indexing a string by integer is not provided. Byte length ( view.byteCount() , O(1)), scalar count ( view.scalarCount() , O(n) in bytes) and grapheme count ( fortis.text.graphemes , O(n)) are separate operations; slicing and scalarAt are O(n) in bytes because UTF-8 scalars have variable width, and the text.Builder operations are amortised O(length appended) with finish() O(total length). Slicing is by scalar offsets through view.slice(from, to) returning Outcome[StringView from view] , failing on invalid boundaries; view.scalarAt(i) returns Outcome[Scalar] , failing past the end. Scalar (in fortis.text ) is a trivial Unicode scalar value with Scalar.of(text) (the single scalar of a one-scalar view, a constant expression for a literal), Scalar.digitValue(s) and Scalar.hexValue(s) returning Int<0..9>? and Int<0..15>? . text.Builder accumulates text: Builder.create() , append(view) , appendLine(view) , appendScalar(s) , finish() String . Normalisation and case mapping are in fortis.text and allocate."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#67-collections","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.7 Collections","m":"spec","x":"6.7 Collections # List[T] is an owned growable sequence. Array[T, N] is an owned fixed-size inline sequence. ListView[T] is a borrowed contiguous view from a List , Array or another view. Map[K, V] and Set[T] are hash containers requiring K: Hashable, Equatable ; OrderedMap[K, V] requires K: Comparable . FixedList[T, N] and FixedMap[K, V, N] have inline capacity N , never allocate, and fail with collection.full when inserting beyond capacity. Map and Set use a deterministic hash function fixed per compiler release and iterate in first-insertion order (replacing a value keeps its key's position). Their iteration order is therefore deterministic for one build, identical on both backends, and may be used inside pure functions; code that needs an order independent of insertion uses OrderedMap or sorts. Indexing list[i] is proof-required: i must have a lower bound of at least 0 from its type or a test, and an upper bound below the length, which the checker knows for Array[T, N] , for List values refined by <length: \u2026> , and from a i < list.count() comparison that is still valid (6.3); an index the rules do not prove is rejected with E-REF-002 , like an unproved operator (6.4; X-152). Otherwise use list.get(i) returning T? (by read loan) or list.at(i) returning Outcome[T] . The named operations of List[T] are List.default() , List.of(...items: T) (variadic, 6.2), count() , push(editable self, item: owned T) , pop(editable self) T? , get , at , set(editable self, index: Int, value: owned T) Outcome[Void] (failing past the end), isEmpty() , first() (alias T from self)? , reversed() , concat , countWhere , map , filter , iterate() , consumeEach(owned self, visit: fn mut(owned T) Void) (10.7); Array[T, N] has Array.filled(value) , count() (= N ), indexing and set ; Map[K, V] has Map.default() , get(key) (alias V from self)? , set(editable self, key: owned K, value: owned V) , count() , iterate() . get only reads its key, so on a Map[String, V] the key is a StringView (X-345): counts.get(word) takes a view or a String alike, with no copy to make a key that is thrown away, and a view hashes and compares equal to the String with the same text. set still takes an owned key, so the allocation shows where it is real. Eager transformation methods ( map , filter , countWhere ) take a pure fn callback and allocate a new collection where they return one ( filter , reversed and concat copy elements and so require T implements Copyable ); a lambda literal whose inferred summary is pure qualifies (11.3), and side-effecting traversal is a for loop or iterate() . No collection operation performs hidden I/O. Complexity: count , get , at , set , pop , first and isEmpty are O(1); push is amortised O(1); List.of , reversed , concat , countWhere , map and filter are O(n) plus the callback's cost per element; a cursor step is O(1); Map.get , Map.set , Set.add and Set.contains are expected O(1) ( set and add amortised); fortis.collections.sorted is O(n log n)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#68-optional-values","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.8 Optional values","m":"spec","x":"6.8 Optional values # T? is either null or a present T . A value of type T is accepted where T? is expected (present injection is implicit and free); nothing converts in the other direction. a ?? b yields a 's value when present, otherwise b (evaluated lazily). a?.field is null when a is null . match over T? uses the patterns present(x) and null . Null tests narrow flow-sensitively: after if value != null { \u2026 } the place value has type T inside the branch; after if value == null { \u2026 } whose block ends in return , fail , break , continue or a Never call, value has type T for the rest of the enclosing block; and in a != null && e , a has type T inside e . Narrowing ends at any invalidation event on the place (6.3). T? is never an error and never a Boolean: if maybe { } is rejected ( E-TYP-010 )."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#69-tuples-anonymous-records-and-newtypes","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.9 Tuples, anonymous records and newtypes","m":"spec","x":"6.9 Tuples, anonymous records and newtypes # (A, B) is a tuple; fields are t.0 , t.1 . { name: A, other: B } is an anonymous record type; a value is written { name: x, other: y } ; record types carry no field defaults. VariantMap[E, V] is a map from the variants of an enum E to values of V , written with the variant names as keys ( { Found: 200, NotFound: 404 } ) and checked for one entry per variant. A type may be parenthesised for grouping, which is how an optional callable is written: (fn(Int) Bool)? is an optional function, while fn(Int) Bool? is a function returning an optional. Tuples, records and variant maps are structural, local conveniences: they may not appear in an exported signature ( E-TYP-030 ) except as aspect parameters and in the signatures of the fortis package itself ( join returns (A, B) , 12.5), and do not satisfy interfaces. type Name = Representation declares a nominal newtype with a private representation; it is the only form type has, so a reader never has to ask whether a type is transparent. CustomerId and OrderId over the same Int<1..> never interchange. The only way to construct a value is Name.validate(raw) (fallible, pure) or a literal that the checker proves in range, written Name(42) . There are no transparent type aliases in v1. A newtype over a trivial representation implements Equatable , Hashable , Comparable and Displayable exactly when its representation does (display shows the underlying value), so CustomerId can be interpolated and used as a map key without a declaration."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#610-generics-and-built-in-interfaces","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.10 Generics and built-in interfaces","m":"spec","x":"6.10 Generics and built-in interfaces # Type parameters are written [T] on functions, classes, enums and interfaces. Constraints use where : Fortis // illustrative export pure func largest[T](items: ListView[T]) T? where T implements Comparable, T implements Copyable { \u2026 } A generic function is compiled once per instantiation, so one that calls itself at an ever larger type ( nest(Box(value: copy x)) inside nest[T] ) has no last instantiation; a native build stops with E-LIM-001 , naming the instantiation-depth budget (32) and the chain, before lowering (X-134). where follows the return type (and a from clause when present) and precedes { or => . A generic body is checked once against its bounds; a use at a type that satisfies the bounds cannot fail to compile. Type arguments at a call are always inferred; there is no f[Int](x) syntax in v1 (a lowercase name followed by [ is an index). A type argument is fixed by the call's arguments or by the call's expected type: an annotated binding, a return, a parameter, the success type a catch recovers to, or the type a match or if-expression gives. A call that leaves a type argument unfixed ( let names = List.default() ) is E-TYP-160 , repaired by annotating the receiving binding. Static dispatch is the default and the backend monomorphises. Higher-kinded types, recursive associated types and overloading are not supported. The built-in interfaces in fortis.prelude are: Interface Methods Notes Equatable pure func equals(self, other: Self) Bool == and != require it Hashable pure func hash(self, into: editable Hasher) Void requires Equatable Comparable pure func compare(self, other: Self) Ordering < <= > >= require it; requires Equatable Displayable pure func display(self, into: editable text.Builder) Void interpolation requires it Iterable[T, I] , Iterator[T] , Consumable[T, I] , Consumer[T] section 5.3 read and consuming iteration Copyable structural; no methods section 6.11 Shareable structural; no methods section 12.7 Abandonable func abandon(owned self) Void lets a linear value live across a cancellation point or an or fail ; the unwind abandons it (12.4) Default pure func default() Self associated function Encodable pure func encode(self, into: editable Encoder) Void describes the value as a tree of fields, lists, scalars, optionals and variants; format-agnostic Decodable try func decode(source: editable Decoder) Self the reverse, running refinements and nominal constructors; associated Codec bundle: requires Encodable, Decodable derive Codec derives both Authority marker implemented by capabilities and per-activation authorities (11.6) From[S] func from(source: S) Self associated; from takes a read loan of the source, and derive From[S] generates Self(...copy source) : every field of Self must have a visible source field of the same name whose type is equal or has From , and must be Copyable There are no user-defined operators and no operator overloading: Money + Money is written Money.add(a, b) . The operators == != < <= > >= dispatch to the built-in interfaces only. derive on a class or enum generates an implementation of Equatable , Hashable , Comparable , Displayable , Encodable , Decodable or Codec field-wise, and is accepted only when every field (or payload) implements the interface ( From[S] and Default have their own rules; a derivation whose rule is not met is E-TYP-090 ); derive Default builds the value from each field's declared initialiser, falling back to the field type's Default only for fields without one, so a class whose fields all have initialisers derives Default regardless of their types. On a class it generates Name.default() , the interface's associated function, which takes no arguments: a class whose fields all start at a default is created without a hand-written constructor, and the header says so (X-345); a field that starts otherwise is written with its initialiser. A field with neither an initialiser nor a type that has Default (a class that does not de"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#610-generics-and-built-in-interfaces","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.10 Generics and built-in interfaces","m":"spec","x":"itialiser. A field with neither an initialiser nor a type that has Default (a class that does not derive or implement it, a capability, a refinement that excludes 0 or empty, an Array , a FixedList , a FixedMap , an OrderedMap , or a type parameter, which a class has no where clause to require a default of) makes the derivation E-TYP-090 , and so does derive Default on an enum, where nothing chooses the variant: an enum that has a default implements Default itself (D-242). A newtype's default is its representation's, so the rule looks through newtypes, at any depth; the containers are the prelude's, known by their declaration, so a class a program names Array is judged by what it declares. A default that contains itself has no end: a field without an initialiser whose type unfolds, through newtypes and classes that derive Default , back to a class or newtype whose default is being built ( type Wrap = Inner with class Inner derive Default { w: Wrap } , two classes that hold each other, a class that holds itself) makes the derivation E-TYP-090 , naming the cycle (D-335, D-336); an optional field ( null ), a collection (empty), an initialiser or a class that implements Default itself ends it. OrderedMap , FixedList and FixedMap have no default until every backend builds an empty one, and until then a field of one without an initialiser is E-TYP-090 (X-345). Bool ( false ), the unrefined integers ( 0 ), Float ( 0.0 ), Duration ( 0s ), String , Bytes , List , Map , Set (empty) and T? ( null ) implement Default ; a refined type does only when its refinement admits the representation's default; a class or enum that implements Default gives its own default() ; Encodable and Decodable additionally require every field to be exported, so a class with private state writes encode / decode by hand or stays off the wire. A class that declares a method an interface would derive cannot also derive that interface ( E-MIX-001 ). Comparable derivation orders by declaration order of fields and variants. Every type has one generic method, func to[T](self) T where T implements From[Self] , usable wherever the target type is already known (an argument, a return, a field initialiser, an annotated let ): Found(customer: customer.to()) . Where it is not known, write CustomerResponse.from(customer) . Encodability is recursive: a derived Encodable requires every field type to be Encodable , so a class containing a Secret[T] or a resource cannot be derived, and the same holds for Decodable . A type newtype implements Encodable / Decodable exactly when its representation does, encoding as that representation and decoding through validate . On top of the two interfaces the core defines one canonical pair that needs no format package, with these signatures in fortis.prelude : pure func toBytes(self) Bytes and try func fromBytes(input: BytesView) Self (deterministic, versioned binary, identical on both backends; the byte format is published with the conformance suite), and pure func toText(self) String and try func fromText(input: StringView) Self (canonical text: JSON with keys in declaration order, no insignificant whitespace). All four allocate; decoding follows the expected type and fails with codec.invalid (malformed input, a value that does not fit its type, a missing field that has neither an initialiser nor an optional type, trailing data) or codec.unknown_field (a text key the type does not declare), and a value outside a refinement or a newtype's refinement fails with that refinement's own failure, exactly as validate would (both formats, version 1, are published as conformance/codec/FORMAT.md ; a nested optional (T?)? has no canonical form and is not Encodable ). Calling the pair on a type that is not Encodable / Decodable is E-TYP-090 . Named formats ( fortis.codec.json , \u2026yaml , \u2026csv , \u2026toml , \u2026msgpack ) consume the same tree and add their own methods through mixins: JsonSerialised requires Encodable provides toJson() , JsonDeserialised requires D"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#610-generics-and-built-in-interfaces","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.10 Generics and built-in interfaces","m":"spec","x":"ds through mixins: JsonSerialised requires Encodable provides toJson() , JsonDeserialised requires Decodable provides fromJson(input) , so an output-only type takes just the first. The canonical pair is never overridable. Displayable stays separate from encoding: ${value} is a human rendering and never the encoded record. Secret[T] , capabilities, loans, resources, closures, futures and tasks implement none of these."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#611-trivial-and-copyable-types","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.11 Trivial and copyable types","m":"spec","x":"6.11 Trivial and copyable types # A trivial type is duplicated implicitly: assigning or passing a trivial value never needs an ownership marker. The set is closed: Trivial types Void , Never , Bool , all integer types, Float32 , Float , Decimal , Byte , Scalar , Duration , Instant , Timestamp type newtypes over a trivial representation enums whose payload fields are all trivial; T? where T is trivial tuples and anonymous records all of whose fields are trivial trivial class Name { \u2026 } \u2014 explicit opt-in; rejected if any field is non-trivial ( E-OWN-030 ) Everything else is ownership-bearing and needs copy , alias or move when an existing place is assigned, returned or passed to an owned parameter. A class is ownership-bearing unless declared trivial , so adding a field to an ordinary class never changes the meaning of an assignment elsewhere. A copyable type supports copy . The set is structural: trivials; String , Bytes , BigInt ; List , Array , Map , OrderedMap , Set , FixedList , FixedMap with copyable elements; classes, enums, tuples and records all of whose fields are copyable. Not copyable: Shared , Weak , Mutex , atomics, loans and views, resources (any class holding a native handle), Future , Task , Outcome , Failure , closures. copy is a compiler-generated field-wise duplicate; it cannot fail and never shares mutable state. A type that needs a fallible or custom duplication exposes an ordinary try func clone(self) Self . copy of a String , List or other heap-owning type allocates and is rejected inside noalloc ."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#612-linear-types","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"6.12 Linear types","m":"spec","x":"6.12 Linear types # A linear value must be consumed exactly once on every path. The linear types are Outcome[T] , Failure , Future[T] , Task[T] , the zero-sized witness Must (created by Must.create() and consumed only by Must.discharge(move m) , which a library uses as a private field to make a handle such as a transaction linear), fn once closures that capture one, and any type whose values can contain a linear value: T? , classes, enums, tuples, records and collections over a linear type. pop() and Consumer.next() on a linear element type therefore return a linear T? , which must be consumed with match or ?? ; Iterator.next() only loans the element, leaving the obligation in the collection. A type parameter is non-linear unless declared T: linear . The bound matters only where the function holds a T by value (an owned T parameter, a local, a field, a return): such a function cannot be instantiated with a linear type unless it says T: linear and consumes the value. A function that only takes T by loan ( x: T or x: editable T ) never owns one, so it may be instantiated with a linear type freely; the obligation stays with the caller. The consuming operations are listed per type in sections 10 and 12. Dropping a linear value at block exit, overwriting it by assignment or discard ing it is an error ( E-OBL-001 ), with the single exception for cold futures in 12.3. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence LANG-06-01 Nominal identity MUST survive calls, storage, serialization and FFI. (old TYP-001) Wrong-id compile-fail and decoder tests. LANG-06-02 Refinement subtyping MUST be structural as in 6.3; the interval domain MUST be implemented exactly as listed, with no additional inference. (old TYP-004) Accept/reject interval corpus; definitional-interpreter agreement. LANG-06-03 Untrusted values MUST pass validate before holding a refined or nominal type. (old TYP-002) Boundary corpus. LANG-06-04 Integer, Decimal and listed float operations MUST agree bit-exactly across VM and native; a checked operator MUST fail as its checked function fails, at the same operation, on every backend (X-345). (old TYP-003) Differential numeric corpus; checked-operator corpus. LANG-06-05 Float operators MUST be total and strict IEEE; float refinements MUST be rejected. Float corpus. LANG-06-06 Map / Set iteration MUST be deterministic per build. Repeated-run tests. LANG-06-07 The trivial and copyable sets MUST be exactly those of 6.11. Marker-required negative corpus per type. LANG-06-08 Optionality, Bool and Outcome MUST NOT convert into one another. (old TYP-006) Three-outcome suite. LANG-06-09 Linear values MUST be consumed exactly once. Drop/overwrite/discard negative corpus. LANG-06-10 Coherence: an implementation of an interface for a type MUST live in the package that defines one of them; overlaps MUST be rejected. Orphan/overlap corpus. LANG-06-11 A call whose type arguments are fixed neither by its arguments nor by its expected type MUST be rejected ( E-TYP-160 ). Uninferred/inferred corpus; checker agreement."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#7-ownership","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7. Ownership","m":"spec","x":"7. Ownership #"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#71-the-judgement","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.1 The judgement","m":"spec","x":"7.1 The judgement # Every expression is checked under an environment \u0393 that records, for each binding: its type, whether it is mutable, whether it currently owns a value (or has been moved out), and the set of active loans on it. The judgement is \u0393 \u22a2 e : \u03c4 \u25b7 L, E, O where L is the set of loans the expression creates, E its effect set (section 11) and O the linear obligations it leaves open (section 10). A statement transforms \u0393 to \u0393\u2032. The rules below define L ; the remaining components are defined in their own sections. The checker is a finite dataflow analysis over the function's control-flow graph with the join rules stated in each section; it does not call a solver."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#72-places-and-owners","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.2 Places and owners","m":"spec","x":"7.2 Places and owners # A place is a path that denotes storage: Place form Example a binding invoice a field path rooted in a binding or self invoice.lines , self.status an Array element with a literal index grid[3] Nothing else is a place: list[i] with a variable index, call results, a ?? b and literals are values. A marker ( copy , alias , move , editable ) is applied to a place. Partial moves are not supported: move invoice.lines out of a binding that still owns invoice is rejected ( E-OWN-040 ); move the whole binding or restructure. A binding owns the value it was initialised with until the value is moved out. A let binding that owns an ownership-bearing value drops it at block exit unless it has been moved. Parameters are bindings that cannot be reassigned; an owned parameter owns its argument, a plain or editable parameter holds a loan."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#73-loans","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.3 Loans","m":"spec","x":"7.3 Loans # A loan is a read-only ( alias ) or exclusive ( editable ) reference to a place. Loans are lexical: A loan created by let r = alias p or let r = editable p is held by r and lasts until the end of the block that declares r . A loan activated for a synchronous call's argument or receiver ends when the call returns, unless the result carries it (5.7). A loan taken by a comparison, an interpolation or a capture ends with the statement; one taken by a for header lasts for the whole loop. A loan activated for an argument of an async call is held by the future the call returns, exactly like a from result: it lasts until that future completes or is discarded, which is what lets a launched task keep using a borrowed net after the launch statement has ended (12.3, 12.5). A loan returned from a call with a from clause is held by whatever binding receives the result and lasts as that binding's loan. So is a loan the result holds inside an optional, a tuple or a record ( xs.get(0) is (alias T from self)? ), whether the binding receives it by let , by assignment (then until the end of the assigned binding's block), by move of another binding that holds it, inside a tuple or record literal, or through a ?? whose present side lends; a loan of a trivial value is that value and holds nothing (D-348). The kept loan has the mode the result declares, so editable cursor.next() leaves a read loan of what the cursor lends, and the cursor may be advanced while the item lives. A loan captured by a closure lasts as long as the closure binding. There is no non-lexical lifetime shortening. To end a loan early, close the block: { let r = alias p \u2026 } . Loans through loans. A binding that holds a loan (an editable or plain parameter, or a let r = alias p / let r = editable p ) is a path to the owner, and every loan is recorded against the owner, not the path. Accessing a place through a loan-holding binding creates a child loan of the same owner with these rules: A read child of a read parent is a read loan; a read child of an exclusive parent is a read loan that freezes the parent (the parent may be read but not written or exclusively re-loaned) until the child ends. An exclusive child requires an exclusive parent and suspends the parent (no access through the parent at all) until the child ends. A child held by a binding ends with that binding's block; a child created for a synchronous call ends when the call returns unless the result carries it (5.7); a child taken for an async call is carried by its future. A parent cannot be moved, reassigned or dropped while any child is live; a loan-holding binding cannot be reassigned at all. That is a binding this paragraph names, one that holds a loan whole and is a path to the owner: a parameter, let r = alias p , let r = editable p , or a binding that receives a from result that is itself a loan. A let mut binding that receives a loan inside an optional, a tuple or a record ( let mut t = xs.get(0) ) is not a path to the owner, and may be reassigned: the new value's loans replace the old ones at the assignment, and the old value is not destroyed (7.9). After t = xs.get(1) the list is lent to t until the end of t 's block; after t = null , t lends nothing. At a control-flow join, a loan is live if it is live on any incoming path. Fortis // reject: E-OWN-011 (at the marked line) class Cell { export value : Int } func inspect ( c : Cell ) Int => c . value func use ( c : editable Cell ) Void { discard inspect ( c ) // accept: read child of the exclusive parent, ends when inspect returns let r = editable c // exclusive child held by r: c is suspended until the block ends r . value = 1 discard c . value // reject: E-OWN-011 \u2014 c is suspended while r is live } Aliasing invariant. At any program point, an owner has either exactly one active exclusive loan or any number of active read loans, never both. An exclusive loan of any field path freezes the whole root owner. Read loans of disjoint field paths of one own"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#73-loans","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.3 Loans","m":"spec","x":"e loan of any field path freezes the whole root owner. Read loans of disjoint field paths of one owner may coexist with each other. While any loan is active, the owner may not be moved, dropped, reassigned or (for a read loan) mutated. Violations are E-OWN-010 (conflicting loan) and E-OWN-011 (owner used while loaned). Fortis // reject: E-OWN-010 (at the marked line) let mut invoice = Invoice . create ( customer : CustomerId ( 7 ) , currency : Currency . GBP , lines : move lines ) or fail let title = alias invoice . customer // read loan on field path let count = alias invoice . lines // disjoint read loan: fine editable invoice . issue ( Timestamp . epoch ( ) ) // reject: E-OWN-010 \u2014 exclusive loan while read loans are active Fortis // accept let mut invoice = Invoice . create ( customer : CustomerId ( 7 ) , currency : Currency . GBP , lines : move lines ) or fail { let items = alias invoice . lines discard items . count ( ) } // loan ends here editable invoice . issue ( Timestamp . epoch ( ) ) // accept"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#74-moves","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.4 Moves","m":"spec","x":"7.4 Moves # move p transfers ownership out of the place p . After a move the place is uninitialised on that path: reading it, loaning it, moving it again or dropping it is an error ( E-OWN-020 use after move ). At a control-flow join, a place is initialised only if it is initialised on every incoming path. A let mut place may be re-initialised by assignment after a move. The backend may implement a move as a bit copy; the source rule is unaffected."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#75-markers-and-the-positions-that-require-them","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.5 Markers and the positions that require them","m":"spec","x":"7.5 Markers and the positions that require them # The markers are place operators: they do not evaluate, they select how the place is used. Marker Meaning Requires copy p independent duplicate p: Copyable , not noalloc if it allocates; copy of a place whose type is not copyable (6.11), including Shared and Weak , is rejected ( E-OWN-002 ) alias p read loan any place editable p exclusive loan (held by a binding, or for a call argument) p rooted in a let mut , editable parameter or exclusive loan move p transfer ownership p owns its value, no active loans Which positions need a marker and which take an implicit read loan: Position Rule for an ownership-bearing place let x = p , x = p , field initialiser name: p , return p marker required: copy , alias (loan) or move ; return x of a local let binding is a move without one (below) argument to a plain parameter; receiver of a self method implicit read loan; no marker argument or receiver that the result borrows from alias p (or editable p ) required: the loan outlives the statement receiver of an editable self method editable p.method(\u2026) ; the marker may be left out on a root that shows it changes (below) receiver of an owned self method move p.method(\u2026) required (or a fresh value) call of a fn mut / fn once closure binding editable f(\u2026) / move f(\u2026) required; the editable may be left out as for a receiver (below) argument to an editable parameter editable p required argument to an owned parameter move p or copy p required closure capture list marker required (section 8.3) operands of == != < \u2026 and of interpolation implicit read loan match p { \u2026 } implicit read loan; payload bindings are read loans. match move p consumes and binds owned payloads for x in p implicit read loan; for x in move p consumes capture field = p.path (section 13) implicit read loan, snapshot copied A fresh value (constructor call, function result, literal, arithmetic result) needs no marker anywhere. A trivial value needs no marker anywhere. Fortis // reject: E-OWN-001, E-OWN-020 (at the marked lines) let user = User ( name : copy name , age : 30 ) let mut independent = copy user let seen = alias user rename ( editable independent , \"Richard\" ) enqueue ( move independent ) // reject: E-OWN-001 \u2014 assignment of an existing ownership-bearing place without a marker let other = user // reject: E-OWN-020 \u2014 independent was moved let again = alias independent What the declaration already shows (X-345). A marker is written where it tells the reader something the line does not. Two markers say what a declaration the reader can see already says, and may be left out; the checker then checks the unmarked form exactly as the marked one, with the same loans, moves, discarded facts and codes: Receivers and fn mut calls. The receiver of an editable self method, and a fn mut closure being called, need no editable wherever editable could be written (the table above): on a place rooted in a let mut binding, an editable parameter, an editable or move capture, a binding that holds an exclusive loan ( let r = editable p , or an editable \u2026 from result) or, inside a method, its own editable self . readings.push(40) on let mut readings is editable readings.push(40) , and self.order.push(w) in an editable self method is editable self.order.push(w) . This holds whatever the receiver's type: a trivial value takes no marker, but its editable self call still takes the exclusive loan, so it conflicts with a live read loan ( E-OWN-010 ) as the marked call does (D-303). On any other root the call is rejected, marked or not ( E-OWN-001 ): a let binding, a read parameter, capture or loan, and the self of a read method. Returning a local. return x , where x is a local let or let mut binding of the function (or of the lambda whose body it is), moves x as return move x does: nothing in the function can use x afterwards. A local still loaned or already moved is rejected with the codes the marked form gives ( E-OWN-011 , E-OWN-020 ; E-DEF-002 when a "},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#75-markers-and-the-positions-that-require-them","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.5 Markers and the positions that require them","m":"spec","x":"y moved is rejected with the codes the marked form gives ( E-OWN-011 , E-OWN-020 ; E-DEF-002 when a defer names it). Returning anything else keeps the table's rule: a parameter (an owned one included), a field, a capture, a pattern or for binding, any other expression. Fortis // accept export class Tally { order : List [ String ] export func add ( editable self , word : StringView ) { self . order . push ( String . from ( word ) ) // the method's own editable self } } func fill ( t : editable Tally ) { t . add ( \"a\" ) // an editable parameter } func build ( ) Tally { let mut t = Tally ( order : List . default ( ) ) t . add ( \"b\" ) // a let mut binding fill ( editable t ) // an argument keeps its marker return t // a local: the return moves it } The written marker stays accepted in both positions, and fortis fmt removes it. Everything else in the table is unchanged, because each marker says something the line would not otherwise show: copy and alias in bindings and field initialisers, the markers of a capture list (8.3), editable and move on arguments, and move everywhere but the return of a local."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#76-parameters-receivers-and-returns","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.6 Parameters, receivers and returns","m":"spec","x":"7.6 Parameters, receivers and returns # Parameter form Caller passes Callee holds x: T (ownership-bearing) the place, implicitly a read loan for the call x: editable T editable p an exclusive loan for the call x: owned T move p , copy p , or a fresh value ownership x: T (trivial) the value a copy Parameters are never reassignable. editable grants permission to mutate the referent through the parameter, not to rebind the parameter. Methods declare their receiver as self (read loan), editable self (exclusive loan) or owned self (consumes). The call site mirrors the mode exactly as for arguments: invoice.render() , editable invoice.issue(now) , move invoice.archive() , except that the editable may be left out where the receiver's declaration already shows it may change (7.5, X-345): invoice.issue(now) on let mut invoice . A marker before a receiver applies to the receiver place, so editable a.b.issue() loans a.b exclusively. A function that returns a loan names its origin with from : Fortis // accept import acme . accounts { User } export pure func displayName ( user : User ) StringView from user { return alias user . name } from names exactly one parameter, which must be a plain or editable parameter (never owned ), and the caller's argument for it must be a place, not a temporary, written with a marker that follows the mode of that parameter or receiver : alias p for a read parameter, editable p for an exclusive one, even when an exclusive receiver returns only a read loan ( editable cursor.next() yields (alias T from self)? ). The marker shows at the call site that a loan is being kept: let name = displayName(alias user) , let mut cursor = alias list.iterate() , let item = editable cursor.next() . This applies whether the result is a loan (a loan type or a view) or carries one (an owned [lifetime r] value, 7.7). The kept loan has the result's declared mode, not the marker's: editable cursor.next() leaves cursor exclusively loaned only until the call returns, and item holds a read loan drawn from the cursor's stored loan. The caller's loan on that argument is then held by the binding that receives the result instead of ending at the statement. The mode of the returned loan is written in the signature: StringView from user is a read loan; editable Cell from c is an exclusive loan, permitted only when c is itself editable , and it suspends the caller's c as a child loan (7.3) for as long as the result binding lives. A function needing loans from two parameters in its result is rejected ( E-OWN-050 ); return an owned value or split the function. A loan of a local or temporary cannot be returned ( E-OWN-051 ). Nor can a value of the function's own where the result is declared from a parameter: it would be held by the caller as a loan of what ends with the call, and is E-OWN-051 too (D-373). A from result is a loan, so the expression that produces it, by return , by an expression body or by a give into it, must lend: a place read through a loan (a parameter, self , a binding that holds a loan, a field or element path through one), alias p , a call whose result is declared a loan, a ?? both of whose sides lend, an if or match every one of whose results lends, an or recover or catch whose value side and every recover lend ( lookup(alias xs, want) or recover xs.get(0) ; or fail , and a handler that fails or returns, produce nothing), present of one, parentheses around one, and, where the result is a view, a string literal without interpolation, which is a view of static storage ( StringView from xs => \"plain\" ; 6.6). Anything else is a value of the function's own: return move d , a copy, a fresh value, an interpolated string ( \"x${\u2026}\" allocates a fresh String ; 6.6), a literal where a String loan is declared (it converts by allocating), a tuple or record literal ( return (xs.get(0) ?? move d, 1) , whatever its parts), a constructor, a lambda, a call whose result is owned, an arithmetic or string result, and a ?? , if , match , or recov"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#76-parameters-receivers-and-returns","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.6 Parameters, receivers and returns","m":"spec","x":"mbda, a call whose result is owned, an arithmetic or string result, and a ?? , if , match , or recover or catch any of whose results is one of these ( return xs.get(0) ?? move d , null => move d , or recover move d , recover move d ), at any depth (D-377, D-381, D-385, D-386). A trivial value holds nothing and is exempt. The same holds for a closure's from result. Bind the loan and return an owned value from a function whose result is owned, let v = xs.get(0) then return copy v ?? move d , or lend on every path. Fortis // accept import acme . cells { Cell } func project ( c : editable Cell ) editable Cell from c { return editable c } func bump ( cell : editable Cell ) Void { let result = project ( editable cell ) // cell is suspended while result lives result . value = 1 // accept } Returning an existing owned local is return local , which moves it (7.5, X-345; return move local is the same); returning an owned parameter uses return move param ; returning a field of a loaned object uses return copy obj.field or a from signature; returning a fresh expression needs no marker. A loan is not an owned value. A loan stays a loan wherever it goes (X-95): a call's result that lends ( xs.get(0) is (alias T from self)? , m.get(k) likewise), a binding that holds one ( let v = xs.get(0) , narrowed by a null test or not), and an a ?? b whose result can be one: a lends, b lends (a nested lent ?? , at any depth), or either is a place read through a loan (a read or editable parameter, a binding that holds a loan, self in a method that borrows it). Where an owned value is expected (the return or expression body of a function whose result is not a loan, a binding or assignment of an owned type, an owned argument, a field initialiser, a variant's payload, a recover value), a loan of a trivial value is that value; a loan of any other value is made owned only by copy , and without it the position is E-OWN-001 for a copyable value and E-OWN-002 for one that is not Copyable, which has no owned copy at all (6.11; D-341). copy v of a binding that holds a loan copies the value it lends and has the owned type: T? , or T where a null test narrowed v . A call's result is bound first, let v = xs.get(0) then return copy v , or matched, present(w) => copy w ; a place is copied where it is read, return copy s ?? fallback ; a value that cannot be copied stays lent (a read parameter, a from result) or is taken out of its owner ( pop , move )."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#77-loan-carrying-values-no-stored-loans","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.7 Loan-carrying values; no stored loans","m":"spec","x":"7.7 Loan-carrying values; no stored loans # A loan lives in a local binding, a parameter or a returned value that names its origin with from ; it is never stored in a field. A loan type is written [alias | editable] Type from origin , where alias is the default and the origin is a parameter, the receiver self or static . The standard library's views ( StringView , BytesView , ListView[T] ) and cursors ( ListCursor[T] and the cursor of every standard collection, 5.3) are loan-carrying types : compiler-known types whose values store a read loan. A loan-carrying value is owned by the binding that holds it (so it may be loaned editable like any owned value, and the child-loan rule does not treat its holder as a path to the origin), while the loan it stores keeps its own mode and region. It is used in exactly three ways: as the type of a local binding, whose region is that of the loan it was created from ( let label = alias user.name ); as a parameter type ( view: StringView , a loan for the call); and as a return type with from ( StringView from user ). Values of a loan-carrying type may not outlive the owner they were created from and may not cross launchParallel or launchBlocking (cooperative launch within the owner's block is permitted, 12.5). Read loans are covariant in their region (a loan within a longer region may be used where a shorter region is expected); exclusive loans are invariant. Stored loans are not in Language 1.0 (X-137). A class or record may not declare a lifetime parameter, and no field may have a loan type or a loan-carrying type other than a loan from static (which borrows from no owner); both are rejected with E-OWN-053 , naming the field or the parameter. A value that must live as long as something else is owned ( move ), copied ( copy ), held through an explicit Shared[T] (7.8), or passed to each call that needs it. A collection of user code that implements Iterable returns a standard cursor ( func iterate(self) ListCursor[T] from self => self.items.iterate() ), and a parser that would keep a view of its input keeps offsets into it instead. Fortis // reject: E-OWN-053 export class Line { // reject: E-OWN-053 \u2014 a field may not store a loan export number : Int export text : StringView } Rationale. Stored loans are the only place in the language where a reader would have to follow a region through a value's type, and nothing in the core library or the acceptance applications needs them: parameters, locals and from returns already express every loan the core uses, and the standard views and cursors are compiler-known. Adding stored loans in a later version breaks no program; removing them after a release would."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#78-shared-ownership","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.8 Shared ownership","m":"spec","x":"7.8 Shared ownership # Shared[T] is a reference-counted owner created by Shared.create(move value) . Its operations, all in fortis.prelude , are: Operation Type Meaning Shared.create(move value) Shared[T] takes ownership of value ; one strong handle s.retain() Shared[T] another strong handle (atomic increment); the only way to duplicate a Shared , since copy of a Shared is rejected s.downgrade() Weak[T] a non-owning handle; does not keep the value alive w.upgrade() Shared[T]? a strong handle while at least one strong handle exists, null once the last strong handle has dropped; w.upgrade() ?? fallback is the ordinary spelling w.retain() Weak[T] another weak handle; copy of a Weak is rejected like Shared Reading through a Shared[T] is a read loan of the value ( alias s.value is written as a plain field path: s.field , s.method() ); dropping the last strong handle drops the value. Effects: retain and downgrade have none, because the reference count is not a Fortis place and no Fortis operation can observe it, so a pure function may create and pass handles; upgrade carries nondet , because its result depends on whether another task has dropped the last strong handle, so a pure function cannot call it; dropping a handle has the cleanup effects of T when it is the last. Shared[T] gives read access only: mutation requires Mutex[T] (section 12.7) or an atomic. Neither Shared nor Weak is linear: dropping either is permitted. Cycles through Shared leak by construction and the checker does not detect them; owner/back-reference pairs use Weak . Shared[T] and Weak[T] are Shareable exactly when T is (12.7), and lower to Arc<T> and Weak<T> (Appendix C.1)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#79-destruction","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.9 Destruction","m":"spec","x":"7.9 Destruction # A value is destroyed at exactly one of these events: Event Order block exit the block's loans end, then defers LIFO, then locals in reverse initialisation order assignment to a let mut owner the new value is evaluated first, then the old value is dropped, then the store happens temporaries at the end of the full statement, in reverse creation order break / continue / return / fail each exited block in turn, innermost first, using the block-exit order failed construction (a fail inside an associated constructor function) already-initialised locals in reverse order; the partially built object does not exist class value destroyed the class's own cleanup method if declared (section 12.2), then fields in declaration order tuple or record value destroyed its elements in index order, a record's fields in written order, except the loans it holds closure value destroyed the captures it owns, its copy , move and bare ones (8.3), in capture-list order, except those its body moved out; its alias and editable captures are loans, which end a call that consumes a closure ( move f(\u2026) , 8.2; the work a task runs) the closure, when the call returns, after the call's own locals and owned parameters; an async closure's call hands it to the future, which destroys it when the body has run or when the future itself is destroyed Mutex[T] destroyed its value (12.7) Only an owner destroys (D-338). A binding that holds a loan is a path to the owner (7.3), and its end destroys nothing: the loan ends. That holds for a binding written alias p or editable p , one that receives a result declared from a parameter or the receiver ( xs.get(0) , m.get(k) , a cursor's next() , xs.first() ), a ?? whose present side is such a loan, a pattern binding of a borrowed match and the binding of a for over a borrowed collection, and for a plain or editable parameter and a closure's alias or editable capture. A field or element reached through a loan is lent too. A tuple or a record that holds a loan beside values of its own ( (xs.get(0), make()) ) owns only those: its end, and the end of a binding of the loan taken out of it by a pattern, destroys nothing it borrows (D-349). Which of its parts are loans is decided as for a binding, by what each part was given: where a part's type is a loan, a ?? whose fallback was taken, an if that gave a value of its own or a binding moved in that owns one gives the tuple or record that value, and it is destroyed with the other parts (D-366). A closure's move capture is decided the same way: a binding moved in that holds a loan gives the closure that loan (D-374), and one that owns its value gives it that value, which the closure destroys with the captures it owns, when the consuming call ends or in index order inside a tuple the body moved it into (D-378). Assigning to a let mut binding that holds a loan replaces the loan and destroys nothing; the binding then holds what it is given, a loan or a value of its own (D-350). So a class value's cleanup runs exactly once, when its last owner destroys it, and never when a loan, an alias or a view of it ends. A value moved out takes the obligation with it: its new owner runs the cleanup , once. A loan-carrying value (7.7) is owned by its binding, so dropping a cursor or a guard ends that value, never the elements it lends. Destruction of a linear value is never generated: the obligation rules make it unreachable."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#710-the-rust-expressible-subset","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"7.10 The Rust-expressible subset","m":"spec","x":"7.10 The Rust-expressible subset # The ownership rules are chosen so that every accepted program has a documented lowering to safe Rust. Appendix C lists the lowering shape for each construct. An ownership situation that the frontend can describe but for which no shape exists (for example a loan returned from a receiver and stored in a Shared ) is rejected with E-OWN-090 unsupported shape and a pointer to the nearest supported pattern. Widening the subset is a language change with a decision record, never an implementation shortcut. A rustc rejection of frontend-accepted code is a compiler defect only within the Rust-checked fragment of Appendix C; everything else is checked by the frontend alone. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence LANG-07-01 Assigning, returning or capturing an existing ownership-bearing place MUST carry copy , alias or move , except that returning a local let binding moves it; owned and editable arguments MUST carry move / copy or editable ; an unmarked editable self receiver or fn mut call MUST be checked exactly as the marked one, taking the same exclusive loan whatever the receiver's type, and rejected where the marker could not be written (X-345, D-303). (old OWN-001) Marker corpus per position in 7.5. LANG-07-02 Parameter bindings MUST never be reassignable. (old OWN-002) Negative tests. LANG-07-03 The checker MUST NOT insert clones, reference counts or hidden shared ownership to repair a rejected program. (old OWN-003) Generated-IR audit. LANG-07-04 Use after move, conflicting loans, owner use while loaned and escaping loans MUST be rejected statically, including through closures, defer , await and scope . (old OWN-004) Adversarial corpus; definitional-interpreter agreement. LANG-07-05 Loans MUST be lexical as in 7.3; the aliasing invariant MUST hold at every program point. Loan-region corpus. LANG-07-06 Returned loans MUST name one origin region; two-origin signatures MUST be rejected; a lifetime parameter or a loan-carrying field MUST be rejected ( E-OWN-053 , X-137). (old OWN-005) Region corpus, stored-loan rejections and Rust lowering fixtures. LANG-07-07 Destruction MUST follow 7.9 on both backends. (old RES-001 part) Drop-order execute tests. LANG-07-08 Shared ownership costs MUST be explicit ( retain ), and copy of Shared MUST be rejected. (old OWN-006) Provenance and cost reports. LANG-07-09 Unsupported ownership shapes MUST be rejected with E-OWN-090 , never lowered with unsafe or erasure. Shape corpus; forbid(unsafe_code) on generated application crates."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#8-functions-and-closures","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"8. Functions and closures","m":"spec","x":"8. Functions and closures #"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#81-named-functions","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"8.1 Named functions","m":"spec","x":"8.1 Named functions # Fortis // illustrative [@aspect(args) \u2026] [export] [Contract \u2026] [pure] [noalloc] [async] [try] [recovers] func name[TypeParams](params) [ReturnType [editable|from \u2026]] [where \u2026] { body } [export] [Contract \u2026] [pure] [noalloc] [async] [try] [recovers] func name(params) [ReturnType] => expression Modifiers appear in exactly this order; any other order is a canonicalisation error that the formatter fixes. Named contracts (section 11.5) are modifiers and stand in the modifier position, before the single-word modifiers; aspects (section 11.8) are applied on the lines above the head. recovers states that the function discharges a failure without reporting it (10.4); like try , it must be written on an exported function whose summary has the effect, so a signature diff shows who swallowed a failure. Every parameter has a type. The success return type follows ) immediately. A Void return type may be left out (X-345): a head that ends at ) , or at its where clause, returns Void , exactly as the head with Void written, which stays accepted and which fortis fmt rewrites. This holds for every function and method head: free and associated functions, methods, expression bodies ( func log(e: Entry) => console.print(e.word) ), interface members, mixin members and requirements, implement blocks and cleanup (9.4). A function type is a type, written whole, so it keeps its result: fn(Int) Void (8.4). A record result is still written after ) : in func bounds(n: Int) { low: Int, high: Int } { \u2026 } the first braces are the result type and the second the body. try states that the function may fail (its actual return type is Outcome[ReturnType] ); async that it may suspend (callers receive Future[ReturnType] or Future[Outcome[ReturnType]] ). pure and noalloc are checked claims (section 11). A named contract written in the modifier position expands to its modifiers and capability requirements. Expression-bodied functions ( => ) have exactly one expression as their body. Block-bodied functions follow the exit rules of 5.4. Named functions are not values; to pass one, write a lambda that calls it, or use the function's name where a callable is expected, in which case the compiler creates a non-capturing fn of the matching type."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#82-lambdas-and-closure-kinds","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"8.2 Lambdas and closure kinds","m":"spec","x":"8.2 Lambdas and closure kinds # Fortis // illustrative [ pure ] [ noalloc ] [ async ] [ try ] [ recovers ] fn [ captures ] ( params ) ReturnType { body } fn ( params ) ReturnType => expression // no captures: no brackets fn ( params ) => expression // an expression body may leave out its return type fn mut [ captures ] ( params ) ReturnType { body } fn once [ captures ] ( params ) ReturnType { body } The bracket group after fn , fn mut or fn once is the capture list ; it is written only when it is non-empty. A lambda takes the same modifiers as a named function, in the same order, and must write async , try and recovers when its body has those effects; pure and noalloc may be written to assert them and are otherwise inferred; the formatter prints no inferred one back and keeps one the author wrote, because the function type the lambda is passed to states what it must be and the checker enforces it there (X-345). Lambdas are not generic. Parameter types may be omitted only when the lambda is passed directly to a parameter of a known function type, in which case they are taken from it. A block body writes its return type. An expression body ( => ) may leave it out (X-345): the return type is then the result type of the function type the lambda is passed to or bound to (a parameter, an annotated let , a field), when that type names one, and the body is checked against it as against a written type; otherwise, where that result is a type parameter the call has not fixed or there is no expected function type, the return type is the body's type, widened as an unannotated let widens its initialiser (5.1): a computed integer interval or a literal's value gives the base type ( fn(x) => x + 1 over Int<0..9> returns Int ). The kind states how the closure may be invoked: Kind Captures may be Callable Lowers to fn copy , alias , move (used read-only) any number of times Fn fn mut as fn , plus editable and move captures that are mutated any number of times; the binding holding it must be let mut and a call is written bump() or editable bump() (7.5, X-345), taking an exclusive loan of it FnMut fn once as fn mut , plus move captures that are moved out of in the body exactly once; the call is written move finish() and consumes the closure FnOnce A lambda whose body needs a stronger kind than declared is rejected ( E-FUN-010 ), naming the capture and use. Declaring a stronger kind than needed is permitted but the formatter reports it at the strict lint level. A move capture does not by itself make a closure fn once ; what matters is whether the body moves the captured value out."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#83-capture-lists","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"8.3 Capture lists","m":"spec","x":"8.3 Capture lists # Each entry names a visible local or parameter with a marker: copy x , alias x , editable x , move x ; a trivial value may be listed bare ( [threshold] ), which copies it, consistent with trivial values never needing a marker. A name not in the list is not accessible in the body ( E-FUN-011 ). Capture expressions are evaluated left to right when the lambda expression is evaluated. Module-level const s, functions, types and module aliases are not captures: the body names them directly, and an entry that names one is rejected ( E-FUN-011 ). Capturing a field path ( alias self.lines ) captures that path only, not the whole owner, and takes the corresponding loan on the root owner for the closure's lifetime. Two editable captures of field paths of one owner are rejected ( E-OWN-010 ), as are an editable capture and any other capture of the same owner. A closure holding loans has the region of the shortest loan and cannot escape it. Passing it to a plain callback parameter ( f: fn(Line) Bool ) is an ordinary argument: the callee receives a read loan of the closure for the duration of the call, which is within the region, so countWhere , map , filter and every synchronous callback-taking function accept a loan-holding closure without saying so in their types. A closure is owned by exactly three positions: an owned fn \u2026 parameter, a field, and a return. A loan-holding closure may occupy such a position only when the position's type names its region ( owned f: fn(Line) Bool from threshold as a parameter, fn(Line) Bool from threshold as a return type); it is never a field, because a field never stores a loan (7.7). Loaning it to a plain fn parameter for a call is not ownership and needs none of this. A closure holding only copy and move captures of Shareable values is itself Shareable . A closure that captures a linear value by move is itself linear and must be declared fn once (a fn or fn mut could run zero or many times, so it may not own a linear value, E-OBL-006 ). A linear closure must be called exactly once on every path, or passed to an owned fn once parameter whose callee is bound by the same rule. Boxing as dynamic fn once preserves linearity. Fortis // reject: E-OWN-011 (at the marked lines) let prefix = String . from ( \"Hello\" ) let greet = fn [ alias prefix ] ( name : StringView ) String { return \"${prefix}, ${name}\" } let mut counter = 0 let mut bump = fn mut [ editable counter ] ( ) Void { counter = Int . saturatingAdd ( counter , 1 ) } editable bump ( ) // calling a fn mut takes an exclusive loan of `bump` // reject: E-OWN-011 \u2014 counter is loaned exclusively by `bump` until the block ends let now = counter"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#84-function-types","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"8.4 Function types","m":"spec","x":"8.4 Function types # A function type names the kind, parameter types (with editable / owned modes), the return type and modifiers: Fortis // illustrative fn ( Int ) Bool pure fn ( StringView ) Int fn mut ( editable Buffer ) Void try fn once ( owned Job ) Void async fn ( Request ) Response A callable value is assignable to a function type when its kind is at most as demanding ( fn \u2264 fn mut \u2264 fn once ), its parameter modes match exactly, its modifiers are at least as strong ( pure satisfies non- pure ; non- try satisfies try ), and, where the callable is owned by the receiving position (an owned fn parameter, a field, a return), its region is at least as long; a callback loaned for a call has no region requirement beyond the call itself (8.3). Function types are nominal in their modifiers: an unconstrained callback cannot be passed where pure fn is required. A from written after a function type's result names the closure's region ( fn(Line) Bool from threshold ); when the result is itself a loan-carrying type, the from belongs to the result and the closure's region, if needed, is written on a parenthesised result ( fn() (StringView from s) from s ). The backend represents each lambda as a concrete environment type; generic callers are monomorphised. A heterogeneous collection of closures needs the explicit type dynamic fn(\u2026) \u2026 , which is a boxed, owned representation and allocates on creation. Universal boxing of closures is forbidden. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence LANG-08-01 Every function MUST state parameter types and the success return type, which a function or method head MAY leave out when it is Void and a lambda as 8.2 allows (inferred parameter types; an expression body's inferred return type) (X-345); modifiers MUST follow the order in 8.1. (old SYN-001) Grammar corpus; inferred-result fixtures. LANG-08-02 Every lambda MUST list its captures (no brackets when it has none) and declare its kind; a body needing a stronger kind MUST be rejected. (old FUN-001, FUN-002) Capture/kind corpus. LANG-08-03 Names not in the capture list MUST be inaccessible in the body. Negative corpus. LANG-08-04 Callable assignability MUST follow 8.4; the backend MUST NOT box closures unless dynamic fn is written. (old CMP-004 part) Codegen inspection."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#9-classes-interfaces-mixins-and-enums","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"9. Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums","m":"spec","x":"9. Classes, interfaces, mixins and enums #"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#91-classes","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"9.1 Classes","m":"spec","x":"9.1 Classes # Fortis // illustrative [export] [trivial] class Name[TypeParams] [derive I1, I2] [implements I3, I4] [with M1, M2] { [export] field: Type [export] field: Type = constantDefault [export] \u2026 func method([editable | owned] self, params) Ret { \u2026 } [export] \u2026 func associated(params) Ret { \u2026 } cleanup(editable self) [Void] { \u2026 } } A class is a nominal product type with fields and methods. There is no inheritance, no virtual dispatch between classes and no runtime member lookup. Fields are private unless exported (section 4.4). Field defaults are constant expressions. Fields, methods, associated functions and constants of one class share one namespace; a name declared twice is rejected ( E-TYP-070 )."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#92-construction","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"9.2 Construction","m":"spec","x":"9.2 Construction # Every class has a generated constructor Name(field: value, \u2026) taking every field as a named argument (fields with defaults may be omitted); a field may be given by a pun, Item(sku, wanted) for Item(sku: sku, wanted: wanted) (5.8, X-345). A class that derives Default also has Name.default() , which takes no arguments (6.10, X-345). It is callable only where every field is visible. Field arguments that are existing ownership-bearing places need a marker exactly as in 7.5; the constructor takes ownership of every argument. All fields are initialised by the constructor; there is no partially constructed object visible to source. A constructor call may take a spread , ...copy p or ...move p , where p is a place of a class or record type whose fields are visible at the call. It fills every field of the target that the source has with the same name and the same type (or a type with From of it), and nothing else; remaining fields are given explicitly after it, and a field given both ways is an error. A target field with no source counterpart, no explicit value and no default is E-TYP-050 ; defaults are applied last, after the spread and the explicit fields. The two forms differ in what happens to the source: ...copy p copies the taken fields (each must be Copyable ) and leaves p untouched. ...move p consumes the whole of p : the taken fields move, the fields the target lacks are dropped, which is an error if any of them is linear ( E-OBL-007 ), and p may not have a cleanup . There is no partial move. Evaluation order is the spread first, then the explicit fields, left to right. This is how a value is projected into a narrower type (a response from a row) without naming each field. Fortis // accept let response = CustomerResponse ( ... copy customer ) let summary = CustomerSummary ( ... copy customer , invoiceCount : count ) A class whose construction must validate or may fail provides an associated function : a function declared inside the class without a self parameter, called as Name.function(\u2026) . Associated functions are the only path to a value whose fields are private, which is how a type newtype's or a class's invariants are enforced against every caller, including decoders and generated code."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#93-methods","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"9.3 Methods","m":"spec","x":"9.3 Methods # A class may also declare associated constants ( const columns = \u2026 ), referenced as Name.columns . A method is a function declared inside a class whose first parameter is self . The receiver forms are self (read loan), editable self (exclusive loan) and owned self (consumes the value); the mode word precedes self just as it precedes a parameter type. Inside a method, fields are accessed as self.field . A method is invoked as value.method(args) for a read receiver, editable place.method(args) for an exclusive receiver and move place.method(args) for a consuming receiver (7.5); the editable may be left out wherever it could be written: a place rooted in a let mut binding, an editable parameter, an editable or move capture, an exclusive loan or the method's own editable self (7.5, X-345). A reviewer therefore sees at the call, or in the declaration of the place it names, whether the receiver can change. A method declared pure may write through editable self (that write is its footprint, section 11.3) but may not perform I/O, read nondeterministic sources, suspend, block or write to anything else."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#94-cleanup","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"9.4 Cleanup","m":"spec","x":"9.4 Cleanup # A class may declare cleanup(editable self) (with Void written or left out, 8.1), run when a value is destroyed (7.9), before its fields are dropped. cleanup cannot be try or async , cannot be called directly ( E-TYP-040 ), and its effects are part of the effect summary of every function that may drop the type. A failing finalisation ( flush , commit ) must be an explicit method the caller invokes; cleanup can only release."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#95-interfaces","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"9.5 Interfaces","m":"spec","x":"9.5 Interfaces # Fortis // accept export interface Renderable requires Displayable { pure func render ( self ) String func reset ( editable self ) Void func create ( name : String ) Self // associated: no self const limit : Int // associated constant } An interface declares method signatures (including modifiers and receiver modes), associated functions and associated constants. There are no associated types in v1; an interface that needs a type parameter takes it as a generic parameter ( Iterable[T, I] ). An interface may carry a where clause constraining its type parameters before its body ( interface Iterable[T, I] where I implements Iterator[T] { \u2026 } ), checked at every implementation and use. An interface may require other interfaces: interface Hashable requires Equatable { \u2026 } . A type implementing Hashable must also implement Equatable (declared or derived), or the implementation is rejected naming the missing requirement ( E-TYP-080 incomplete implementation , also the code for a missing member, an incompatible signature and an unmet mixin requirement); a dynamic Hashable is usable where a dynamic Equatable is expected. Interfaces carry no method bodies: default behaviour on top of an interface is a mixin that requires it. An interface with requirements and no members of its own is a bundle ( interface Codec requires Encodable, Decodable {} ) and is satisfied by every type that implements everything it requires, without being named, because it adds nothing that coherence would have to disambiguate. An interface with neither members nor requirements is a marker ( interface Authority {} , interface ServerContext {} ) and is implemented only by explicit declaration; automatic satisfaction never applies to it. Interfaces are nominal: a type implements one only by implements , by derive , or by an implement I for T block. Having methods with the right names is not an implementation. The exceptions are exactly the memberless ones with requirements or structural rules: Copyable and Shareable (structural, 6.11 and 12.7) and bundles; markers are nominal. A requirement cycle ( A requires B , B requires A ) is rejected ( E-TYP-060 ); deriving an interface a type also implements by hand is rejected ( E-MIX-001 ); a dynamic A where A requires B converts to dynamic B without allocation. A class or enum implements an interface by listing it in implements and providing every member. An implementation may not strengthen a precondition (narrower parameter refinement) or weaken a guarantee (drop pure or noalloc , add try , async or recovers , widen a return refinement, or drop a contract's clause; 11.2). Callers through the interface see the interface's contract only. Coherence. A package may implement an interface for a type only if the package defines the interface or the type. Two implementations of one interface for one type are rejected. Both coherence violations, an implementation outside the defining packages and an overlap, are E-TYP-100 . Generic implementations ( implements Displayable for List[T] where T implements Displayable ) are declared at module level with implement Interface for Type { \u2026 } and follow the same rule. Dynamic dispatch. An interface is not itself a type a value has: a field, variant payload, parameter or result declared with a bare interface name is rejected at its declaration ( E-TYP-040 ), naming dynamic I , Shared[dynamic I] and a type parameter bounded by I . dynamic Interface is a type whose value is any implementation, dispatched at run time. It is written with an ownership form: dynamic Interface (owned, boxed; allocates on creation), alias dynamic Interface (loan), Shared[dynamic Interface] . An interface is dynamic-eligible only if it has no associated types, no generic methods and no method taking or returning Self by value. An async method called through dynamic allocates its future; such a call is rejected in noalloc code."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#95-interfaces","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"9.5 Interfaces","m":"spec","x":" rejected in noalloc code."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#96-mixins","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"9.6 Mixins","m":"spec","x":"9.6 Mixins # A mixin contributes method implementations to classes that apply it with with . A mixin declares what it requires as method signatures, never as fields, and may not add fields, constructors or cleanup : Fortis // accept export mixin Summarised { requires pure func title ( self ) String requires pure func lineCount ( self ) Int export pure func summary ( self ) String => \"${self.title()} (${self.lineCount()} lines)\" } A method declared in the class takes precedence over a mixin method of the same name. Two applied mixins providing the same method name, with no class method of that name, is an error ( E-MIX-001 ); the class resolves it by declaring the method itself. Mixin bodies are checked at the application site and see only the class's exported members, even when the mixin is declared in the same module; a mixin therefore never reads a private field."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#97-enums","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"9.7 Enums","m":"spec","x":"9.7 Enums # Fortis // accept export enum InvoiceStatus derive Equatable { Draft Issued ( at : Timestamp ) Paid ( at : Timestamp , reference : String ) } Variants are UpperCamel, optionally with named payload fields. A variant is constructed as InvoiceStatus.Draft or InvoiceStatus.Issued(at: now) . The prefix is omitted inside a match over the enum type, and may be omitted in any position whose expected type is already that enum: a return in a function returning it, an argument to a parameter of that type, a field or payload initialiser, an annotated let , and the right operand of a comparison ( == , != , < , \u2026) whose left operand has that enum type or is that enum optional (X-345). return Found(customer: move customer) in a function returning GetCustomerResponse is therefore complete, and so is if severity == Error . A bare name that also reaches a declaration in scope (a class, an error kind, another type) is ambiguous in such a position, and is E-TYP-040 asking for the qualified form, so a name never silently changes meaning (X-345); a pattern has no qualified form, and there the scrutinee's variants are read first, as before. Where nothing expects the enum (an unannotated let , a generic argument no expected type fixes, a binding of another type, the left operand of a comparison) a bare variant would have no type, and is E-TYP-040 asking for the qualified form (D-304). The formatter drops the prefix where the bare form means the same. Enums are closed: every match must cover every variant. Enums may have methods and implement interfaces. Enum values are trivial when every payload is trivial. A variant name used where a function type is expected denotes its constructor, with the payload fields as positional parameters ( fallback: Unavailable passes fn(PublicProblem) GetCustomerResponse ). Code generated from an external schema (the HTTP kit) produces a transport enum with an explicit Unknown variant and a fallible conversion to the closed domain enum; closed source enums are never silently extended."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#98-patterns-and-match","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"9.8 Patterns and match","m":"spec","x":"9.8 Patterns and match # Fortis // illustrative match scrutinee { Pattern [when guard] => expression Pattern [when guard] => { \u2026 give value } Pattern | Pattern \u2026 => expression // alternatives: no bindings, no guard } match evaluates the scrutinee once, tests arms top to bottom, takes the first matching arm and has no fallthrough. In expression position every arm yields a value of one unified type, using give in block arms. As a statement, arms are blocks or Void expressions. The patterns are: Pattern Matches name anything; binds name _ anything; binds nothing literal equal value ( Equatable ); integers, strings, booleans Variant , Variant(field: name, \u2026) , Variant(name, \u2026) enum variant, binding payload fields by name or position present(name) , null optional success(name) , failure(name) outcome (section 10.4) is ErrorName , is ErrorName(field: name, \u2026) , either followed by as name a Failure of a declared kind, optionally binding the whole failure (section 10.5) Name(field: p, \u2026) a class whose fields are all visible; every field must be named (or _ for non-linear fields); only with match move / let \u2026 = move (consuming destructure); rejected for classes with cleanup (p1, p2) tuple { field: p, \u2026 } anonymous record otherwise catch-all; permitted only when the scrutinee type is open (integers, strings, Failure ), not for closed enums or optionals. A top-level _ or bare binding pattern is likewise rejected as the last arm of a match over a closed enum ( E-MAT-002 ), so adding a variant always surfaces every match that must handle it Patterns nest. An arm may list alternatives , A | B | C => value , which matches when any alternative does, tried left to right (X-345). | separates whole patterns of an arm, never parts of a payload or of another pattern. The form is narrow, so the arm body sees the same bindings, none, whichever alternative matched: an alternative binds no names (no bare name, no named or positional payload binding, no as ), an alternative is not otherwise , and an arm with alternatives takes no when guard; each is E-MAT-003 . Exhaustiveness counts each alternative as covering its case, as an arm of its own would: a variant of a closed enum that no alternative names is still reported ( E-MAT-001 ), and a _ alternative makes the arm a catch-all ( E-MAT-002 over a closed enum). There is no unreachable-arm check: as an arm repeating an earlier unguarded arm's case is accepted and never runs, so is an alternative that repeats a case. Range patterns and binding-with-subpattern ( x @ p ) are not in v1. A when guard is a Bool expression over the bindings; a guarded arm does not count toward exhaustiveness. Missing variants of a closed enum are reported individually ( E-MAT-001 ). A consuming destructure of a class is the way to take a linear field out of a linear class: let Pending(result: r) = move p or match move p { Pending(result: r) => \u2026 } ; the whole value is consumed, every field is bound, and non-linear fields may be discarded with _ . There is no partial move. Ownership: match p takes a read loan of the place for the match, and payload bindings are read loans. match move p consumes the place, and payload bindings own their values. match of a fresh value (a call result) owns it. Binding a linear payload (an Outcome inside an enum) requires the owning form and the arm must consume it. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence LANG-09-01 Inheritance, runtime member lookup and reflection-based construction MUST be absent. (old OO-001) Grammar and negative corpus. LANG-09-02 The generated constructor MUST be callable only where every field is visible; private fields MUST be settable only inside the module. Visibility corpus. LANG-09-03 Implementations MUST NOT strengthen preconditions or weaken guarantees; coherence MUST hold. Substitution corpus. LANG-09-04 Mixins MUST require methods only and MUST NOT add state; conflicts MUST be rejected. Mixin corpus. LANG-09-05 match on a closed type MUST be exhaustive without otherwise , "},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#98-patterns-and-match","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"9.8 Patterns and match","m":"spec","x":"be rejected. Mixin corpus. LANG-09-05 match on a closed type MUST be exhaustive without otherwise , with each missing variant named; the alternatives of an or-pattern MUST each count as covering their case, MUST bind no names and MUST NOT be otherwise , and an arm with alternatives MUST NOT take a guard (X-345). (old ERR-006) Pattern corpus; or-pattern corpus. LANG-09-06 Pattern binding ownership MUST follow 9.8. Owning/borrowing match corpus."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#10-outcomes-and-failures","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"10. Outcomes and failures","m":"spec","x":"10. Outcomes and failures #"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#101-fallible-functions","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"10.1 Fallible functions","m":"spec","x":"10.1 Fallible functions # try func f(\u2026) T returns Outcome[T] : either success(value) or failure(failure) . These two words are pattern forms; a test or adapter that needs to build an outcome by hand uses Outcome.success(v) and Outcome.failure(move f) . Inside its body return v produces success and fail f produces failure. A call to a try function needs no prefix; the result is an Outcome[T] value that is linear and must be consumed by one of the operations below before the end of the enclosing block. The success value is never accessible without consuming the outcome. Fortis // reject: E-OBL-001, E-TYP-040 (at the marked lines) import acme . accounts { CustomerId , loadAccount , save } func a ( id : CustomerId ) Void { loadAccount ( id ) // reject: E-OBL-001 \u2014 outcome dropped at end of block } func b ( id : CustomerId ) Void { save ( loadAccount ( id ) ) // reject: E-TYP-040 \u2014 save expects Account, got Outcome[Account] } try func c ( id : CustomerId ) Void { let account = loadAccount ( id ) or fail // accept save ( account ) }"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#102-consuming-operations","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"10.2 Consuming operations","m":"spec","x":"10.2 Consuming operations # Operation Effect on the obligation Available in e or fail propagates the failure from the enclosing try function try functions e or recover v discharges the failure, yields v (lazily evaluated); adds the recovers effect anywhere e catch name { \u2026 } binds the failure as name inside the block, which must end in recover v , return , fail , or a Never call; see 10.4 anywhere match e { success(x) => \u2026, failure(f) => \u2026 } the failure arm must consume f anywhere report e (where e: Failure ) consumes; report e as receipt also binds Report , an inert receipt functions with the io effect move /pass to an owned Outcome[T] or owned Failure parameter transfers anywhere store into a linear container or field transfers anywhere fail e (where e: Failure ) propagates try functions Bare or value does not exist: every discard of a failure is spelled recover . There is no ? , no try at call sites and no unwrap ."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#103-failure-values","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"10.3 Failure values","m":"spec","x":"10.3 Failure values # Failure is the single failure type. It carries a stable code ( StringView from static , dotted, declared by an error declaration), a typed payload, an origin (module, function, line) and an optional cause chain. Failure is linear and not copyable. A failure created by fail ErrorName(\u2026) in a pure function is deterministic: it reads no clock and allocates no identifier. The bounded representation (payload up to 64 bytes inline) satisfies noalloc ; a payload that cannot be bounded makes the failure allocate and the function non- noalloc . Fortis // accept import fortis . money { Currency } export error CurrencyMismatch { code : \"billing.invoice.currency_mismatch\" payload { expected : Currency , found : Currency } message : \"Line currency ${found} does not match invoice currency ${expected}\" } An error declaration defines a failure kind: a constructor CurrencyMismatch(expected: \u2026, found: \u2026) producing a Failure , a pattern is CurrencyMismatch(\u2026) , and a display template. The message string is a template, not an interpolated expression: ${name} in it may name only payload fields (any other name is E-NAM-001 ), and is rendered when the failure is displayed. The template is kept as written between its quotes, and rendering replaces each ${name} with that payload field's text, as a record's payload shows it, on every backend (D-340). Codes are unique per package and stable across versions. The payload fields are trivial or String / Bytes with a declared length bound, so that failures are bounded and serialisable without secrets."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#104-catch-and-recover","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"10.4 catch and recover","m":"spec","x":"10.4 catch and recover # Fortis // accept let account = loadAccount ( id ) catch e { report e recover Account . guest ( ) } catch binds the failure to an explicit name. The block runs only on failure. Inside it, return and fail leave the enclosing function, recover v ends the catch with v as the expression's value, and give is not permitted. If the block ends without having consumed e (by report , fail , move or match ), recover consumes it and adds the recovers effect; if e was consumed earlier, recover adds nothing. Every path through the block must end in recover , return , fail or a Never call ( E-OBL-002 ). A catch never covers an operation other than its left operand."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#105-matching-on-failures","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"10.5 Matching on failures","m":"spec","x":"10.5 Matching on failures # Fortis // accept let maybe : Invoice ? = match Invoice . create ( customer : id , currency : gbp , lines : move lines ) { success ( invoice ) => move invoice failure ( f ) => match move f { is EmptyInvoice => null // discharged here: adds `recovers` is CurrencyMismatch ( expected : want , found : got ) as mismatch => { report mismatch // consumed by the arm give null } otherwise ( other ) => { report other give null } } } A match move f over a Failure uses is ErrorName(\u2026) patterns. Payload fields are bound by copy (they are trivial or bounded strings). An is arm may bind the whole failure with as name ; the arm must then consume name . An is arm without as discharges the failure when it matches, which adds the recovers effect to the function. Because Failure is an open type, the match must end with otherwise(name) , which receives ownership of any other failure and must consume it. Matching a failure through a read loan ( match f without move ) is permitted for inspection only: payloads are copied, no arm may consume, and f must still be consumed after the statement."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#106-reports-annotation-and-aggregation","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"10.6 Reports, annotation and aggregation","m":"spec","x":"10.6 Reports, annotation and aggregation # report f is a statement: it consumes the failure and emits it to the task's failure reporter. report f as receipt additionally binds a Report , a trivial receipt that cannot be converted back to a Failure , for use in a public response. Reporting and then propagating the same failure is impossible because the value is gone. The diagnostic operations are ordinary functions in fortis.prelude : annotate(f: owned Failure, note: StringView) Failure , Failures.aggregate(list: owned List[Failure]) Failure , and report (a statement). annotate(move f, note) returns a new Failure with the same code and payload, the original as its cause and the given note; it is the only way to add context, and it keeps exactly one obligation alive. Failures.aggregate(move list) consumes a List[Failure] and yields one Failure with code failure.aggregate (a dotted code, as 10.3 requires) and the inputs as causes; it is the rule for parallel work and retry loops. Metrics, span status and debug logging take a read loan of a failure and consume nothing. report has the io effect but is the one io operation that needs no capability value: it writes only to the diagnostic plane of the current task, which cannot feed anything back into the program (section 11.7). A pure function therefore cannot report; it propagates or recovers deterministically. The canonical boundary pattern, in a server handler of the HTTP kit, is: Fortis // accept import acme . customers { CustomerId , CustomerRepo , GetCustomerResponse } import fortis . http . server { PublicProblem } export func show ( id : CustomerId , repo : CustomerRepo ) GetCustomerResponse { return match repo . find ( id ) { success ( present ( customer ) ) => GetCustomerResponse . Found ( customer : customer . to ( ) ) success ( null ) => GetCustomerResponse . NotFound failure ( f ) => { report annotate ( move f , \"GetCustomer\" ) as receipt give GetCustomerResponse . Unavailable ( problem : PublicProblem . from ( receipt ) ) } } } The failure is reported once, the public response carries only the receipt's correlation identifier, and the internal failure cannot leak into the response because PublicProblem.from accepts a Report , not a Failure ."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#107-linear-containers-and-generic-code","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"10.7 Linear containers and generic code","m":"spec","x":"10.7 Linear containers and generic code # A collection of outcomes ( List[Outcome[T]] ) is linear. The standard library declares, in each operation's signature, what happens to linear elements: push(move item) transfers in; pop() returns T? transferring out; consumeEach(owned self, fn mut(owned T) Void) consumes the collection and calls the repeatable callback once per element, in order, running to completion (there is no early exit, so no element is left unvisited; an empty collection is consumed with no calls); clear() and dropping are rejected for linear element types; count() and isEmpty() take a read loan. Outcome.collect(move List[Outcome[T]]) is Outcome[List[T]] and consumes all inputs. A generic function may only hold values of a type parameter T in linear positions if it declares T: linear , and must then consume them."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#108-defects","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"10.8 Defects","m":"spec","x":"10.8 Defects # A defect is a condition that the checker cannot represent as an Outcome because the program's assumptions are already broken. The taxonomy is closed: Defect Source Native behaviour VM behaviour contract violation in trusted code a trusted adapter breaks its declared contract process abort ( panic = abort ) terminate the task with a TaskDefect , keep the process stack exhaustion unbounded recursion abort task terminated out of memory allocator failure abort task terminated runtime invariant VM or runtime bug abort task terminated Ordinary source cannot cause a defect: there is no unwrap , no unchecked index, no assertion that aborts; the conformance corpus injects each class through the reference toolchain's injectDefect(kind) hook, which is not part of the language. A defect is never a Failure , is never caught by catch , and produces no completion value; TaskCompletion.Defect (section 12.6) is observed only by the supervisor of the terminated task. Expected conditions (bounds, parsing, I/O, arithmetic) are always outcomes. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence LANG-10-01 Outcomes MUST remain wrapped until consumed by an operation in 10.2. (old ERR-001) Nested-call and access corpus. LANG-10-02 Linear obligations MUST survive storage in collections, fields and closures; dropping one MUST be rejected. (old ERR-002) Container/closure/drop corpus. LANG-10-03 A reported failure MUST be unreachable afterwards; annotate MUST keep exactly one obligation. (old ERR-003, ERR-004) Lineage corpus. LANG-10-04 noalloc try functions MUST use the bounded failure representation on success and failure paths. (old ERR-005) Allocator probes. LANG-10-05 recover without a prior report MUST add the recovers effect. Effect summary tests. LANG-10-06 Defects MUST follow the taxonomy of 10.8 and MUST NOT be catchable. Fault injection per backend."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#11-effects-and-contracts","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11. Effects and contracts","m":"spec","x":"11. Effects and contracts #"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#111-the-effect-set","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.1 The effect set","m":"spec","x":"11.1 The effect set # Every function has an effect summary : a subset of the closed set below, computed from its body and its callees. The set cannot be extended by packages or configuration. A body whose summary exceeds what the function's modifiers admit ( recovers without the modifier, an allocation in noalloc , an effect in pure , a suspension without async ) is rejected with E-EFF-001 , naming the effect and the statement that introduces it. Effect Present when the function may\u2026 alloc allocate heap memory (including inside callees, failures, cleanup and closures it creates) io perform input/output through a capability, including report mutate write to storage that is neither fresh in this activation nor reachable from one of the function's own editable parameters (see the write-footprint rule in 11.3) nondet read a non-deterministic source (clock, random, environment, address identity) suspend reach an await block block the calling thread (synchronous lock wait, blocking I/O); forbidden in async functions and in anything they call ( E-EFF-020 ), because the cooperative executor has one thread. Blocking work goes to launchBlocking , whose callback is invoked by the pool, not by the async function, so its block is not imported (11.3) fail return a failure recovers discharge a failure that was not reported (10.4) Mutation of fresh storage is not mutate : storage is fresh in an activation if that activation (or a callee during it) created it and it has not yet been stored anywhere reachable from a parameter, capture or self . Building a list locally and returning it is therefore pure. Writes through a function's own editable parameters are recorded in its write footprint rather than as mutate ; callers instantiate the footprint (11.3)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#112-modifiers-as-claims","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.2 Modifiers as claims","m":"spec","x":"11.2 Modifiers as claims # Modifier Claim (checked) pure io , mutate , nondet , suspend , block are all absent noalloc alloc is absent async suspend may be present (and is permitted) try fail may be present (and is permitted) recovers recovers may be present (and is permitted) A function without async is rejected if its summary contains suspend ; without try , if it contains fail ; without recovers , if it contains recovers . pure and noalloc are independent: a pure function may allocate, a noalloc function may do I/O. recovers is written as a modifier on any function whose summary has the effect (8.1); a contract may forbid it. Modifiers on interface methods bind every implementation, whether written or carried by a contract (11.5): the implementation claims each modifier the member's claims expand to, rules out each effect its contracts forbid and excludes each capability they exclude, written or through a contract of its own, or the implementing type is rejected ( E-TYP-080 ). Its body is then checked against those claims, so a caller through an interface or dynamic relies on the interface's claims, and sees only those. There is no nostate . The toolchain offers an opt-in lint no-local-mutation for teams that want to forbid let mut in selected modules; it is not part of the language and has no effect on typing."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#113-transitive-analysis","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.3 Transitive analysis","m":"spec","x":"11.3 Transitive analysis # A function's summary is computed by expression-level transfer rules, then closed under recursion by a least fixed point over the finite set: Calls. A call imports the callee's summary at the callee's actual type arguments and callback types, with two adjustments. fail is imported only if the call's outcome leaves the function unhandled: or fail and fail f keep it; or recover , catch (ending in recover ) and a match that consumes the failure remove it, adding recovers where 10.4 says so. mutate is imported per the callee's write footprint (below). Async calls. Calling an async function constructs a future and imports only the effects of evaluating the arguments. The future's type carries the callee's summary; await imports it, as does launch into the awaiting scope. launchParallel and launchBlocking import the work's summary minus block , since the pool, not the async function, runs it. Write footprints. A function's summary records which of its editable parameters (and editable self ) it writes through, directly or via callees: its write footprint. A write through an editable parameter is not mutate inside the function itself. At a call site, each footprint parameter is instantiated with the argument place: if that place is fresh in the caller, nothing is imported; if it is reachable from one of the caller's own editable parameters, it joins the caller's footprint; otherwise (a mutable capture, a Mutex guard, an atomic) the caller gets mutate . Fresh storage. A let mut local initialised from a constructor, a literal or an owned call result (a value, not a loan type) is fresh for the activation; writes to it, including through methods with editable self called on it, are footprint-free. A binding holding a loan result ( let mut r = project(editable c) ) is a path to the loan's owner, and writes through it are attributed to that owner. Returning fresh storage gives the result \"fresh\" provenance for the caller. Fortis // accept pure try func increment ( x : Int ) Int => Int . add ( x , 1 ) or fail pure recovers func fallback ( x : Int ) Int => increment ( x ) or recover 0 // accept: fail removed, recovers added class Cell { export value : Int pure func set ( editable self , value : Int ) Void { self . value = value } // footprint: {self} } pure func build ( ) Cell { let mut cell = Cell ( value : 0 ) editable cell . set ( 1 ) // accept: cell is fresh, so the footprint imports nothing return move cell } Hashable.hash(self, into: editable Hasher) and Displayable.display(self, into: editable text.Builder) are pure with footprint {into} under the same rule. Callback parameters contribute the effects permitted by their function type, not those of any particular argument. A lambda literal's own type carries pure when its inferred summary has none of the effects pure excludes, so it may be passed where a pure fn is required without writing the modifier. cleanup methods contribute to every function that may drop their type. Trusted intrinsics in the fortis package are declared with the trusted modifier before the other modifiers ( trusted pure noalloc func \u2026 ), have no Fortis body (the backend supplies one), and carry their review record in the documentation comment above them. Only the packages of the published compatibility set ( Platform 1.5 : the standard library and its audited first-party adapters) may declare trusted ; a program's own trusted function or method is rejected ( E-EFF-001 ), so no declaration can bypass the effect analysis. A trusted operation's summary is its modifiers' effects ( fail , suspend , recovers ), io when it is a method of an Authority type or takes an Authority parameter (11.6), and the effects its signature cannot show ( alloc , block , io , mutate , nondet ), which its standard package declares as data shipped with it ( reference/library/effects/<package>.json ), reviewed with its trusted operations and read by every checker. A trusted claim is reported as trusted evidence, nev"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#113-transitive-analysis","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.3 Transitive analysis","m":"spec","x":"s trusted operations and read by every checker. A trusted claim is reported as trusted evidence, never as proved . A function may declare a claim that its summary satisfies; it may not declare a weaker set of effects than it has. Every function must write async , try and recovers when its summary has the effect, because each changes the function's type. pure and noalloc are claims a function may make: a caller, generic bound or contract can rely on them only when they are written, so leaving one off never breaks a caller, it only withholds a guarantee. Private callers inside the module see the inferred summary; across a module boundary only the written claims count. Removing a written pure / noalloc , or adding async / try / recovers , on an exported function is a public API change, which fortis check --api-baseline OLD reports as the warning E-EFF-040 at the declaration (a contract's modifiers count as written; X-130); the strict lint level reports an exported function whose inferred summary would support a claim it does not make."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#114-noalloc-and-plans","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.4 noalloc and plans","m":"spec","x":"11.4 noalloc and plans # noalloc is a property of a function under a target plan and a telemetry plan . A library function declared noalloc claims: no allocation given the bounded instrumentation plan it was checked with. The build checks the claim again against the application's actual plan; if the application's telemetry policy attaches an allocating record to the function, the build is rejected at the policy application site ( E-EFF-030 ), not by silently removing the instrumentation or by failing the library. For an async function, noalloc covers four phases: future construction, each poll, completion and destruction. The checker decides allocation by matching lowering patterns against the whitelist in Appendix C, not by inspecting optimiser output; a pattern not on the list is an allocation. The VM may allocate its own objects to execute logically noalloc code; that overhead is labelled separately and does not invalidate the native claim."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#115-contracts","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.5 Contracts","m":"spec","x":"11.5 Contracts # Fortis // accept export contract FastCalculation { pure noalloc } export FastCalculation func square ( value : Int < - 1000000 .. 1000000 > ) Int => value * value A contract is a named conjunction of modifiers, forbidden effects ( forbids recovers , over the eight effects only), required authorities ( requires capability Clock , requires capability TaskContext : the function must take a parameter of that Authority type, capability or per-activation) and excluded authorities ( excludes capability Network : the function may not receive that authority type and may not call, directly or through callees, a function that requires it; summaries carry the set of authority types a function requires, so the check is transitive and closes over wrappers and callbacks). The word capability in both clauses names any Authority type. Nothing else may appear in a contract. A contract subtracts : applying it never changes what a function does, it only makes the checker reject a body that does something the claims rule out. Because a contract is a set of modifiers, it is applied where modifiers are written: export FastCalculation func \u2026 , FastCalculation fn(\u2026) \u2026 ; several names may be listed and compose by conjunction. The toolchain shows the expansion of a contract name on request; the formatter never rewrites the name, because an aspect may needs it by name, and prints the single-word modifiers it expands to after it ( export FastCalculation pure noalloc func square , X-130). A contract never takes arguments and is never written with @ ; behaviour around a function is an aspect (11.8). Contracts compose by conjunction; contradictory contracts are rejected, and a pure function may not take a capability parameter at all, so pure with requires capability is a contradiction. The origin of every obligation (modifier, contract, interface, mixin, aspect) is recorded for diagnostics. Project configuration never attaches or selects contracts: a contract applies to a function only by appearing in its head. Configuration chooses destinations, sampling, retention and provider implementations within what the source declares."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#116-capabilities","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.6 Capabilities","m":"spec","x":"11.6 Capabilities # An io effect requires an authority value in the signature: a nominal, non-copyable, non-serialisable value that implements the marker interface Authority (9.5), passed as a parameter. Authorities come in two closed sets. Capabilities ( FileSystem , Network , Clock , Random , Environment , Process , Supervisor , Console , SecretAccess , RawSql ) are process-wide and are created only for the entry main : by the runtime, for its capability parameters (4.6), or by its one call of Host.capabilities() . Per-activation authorities are minted by an adapter from a capability for one unit of work and handed to it as a parameter: a server adapter mints RequestContext , Identity , Db and Tx per request from Network and the database connection, a job adapter mints TaskContext per run, a mail adapter mints Mail from Network and configuration. Both sets are listed in section 15; a kit adds a per-activation authority by declaring a type that implements Authority and naming the capability it is minted from. The runtime creates the capabilities once, before the entry main runs, and passes the ones main declares as parameters (4.6). A main that declares no capability parameter may create them itself with Host.capabilities() in fortis.host , which is called once, directly in the entry main 's own body: not inside a loop (its condition included), a lambda or an aspect's hook, which could run it again, and never taken as a function value. A main that declares a capability parameter may not call it, since its capabilities already exist. A function named main in any other module or package is an ordinary function, and Fortis code never refers to the entry main , which only the runtime calls. A backend that is nevertheless asked for a second creation stops the run with the runtime-invariant defect (10.8): the checker's guarantee is broken. Capabilities are attenuated by methods ( fs.subtree(path) ) and passed explicitly. A Shared[A] of an authority A carries A 's authority: a parameter of that type satisfies the rule below exactly as an A parameter does, which is how several owners hold one capability ( let console = Shared.create(move c) , then console.retain() per owner), since no field stores a loan (7.7, X-137). A per-activation authority is created only by its adapter's minting function, which itself requires the capability it derives from, so every authority traces back to main . The capability types are defined by the core (section 15) and re-exported by fortis.host ; the Runtime host kit adds attenuation helpers and adapters. report (section 10.6) and the log statements (section 13) are the io operations exempt from this rule: they write only to the diagnostic plane (11.7). A function with the io effect therefore names, in its signature, the authority it uses. A method's receiver is one of its parameters: a method may also use an authority that its receiver's class holds in a field whose type is that authority or a Shared of it, which is how an owner holding a Shared[Console] prints from its methods and its cleanup (X-137). A field of any other type names none (a Capabilities record names none, as a Capabilities parameter names none), and an associated function names its authority by a parameter, like any function. The static rule is only that the capability type must be in scope by type; which directory or host it reaches is determined by the value and enforced by the runtime adapter. Reading a clock is nondet and requires Clock ; a pure function receives time as an ordinary parameter. A function other than the entry main whose summary reaches io through a capability without taking an Authority value, any use of Host.capabilities other than that one direct call (a call elsewhere, a second call, a call inside a loop, lambda or hook, a call in a main that takes a capability parameter, the function taken as a value), and any reference to the entry main are rejected ( E-EFF-010 ). Capabilities, effects and target feature sets "},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#116-capabilities","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.6 Capabilities","m":"spec","x":"ference to the entry main are rejected ( E-EFF-010 ). Capabilities, effects and target feature sets are three different things: a target feature set ( core , alloc , host , async , web , browser ) decides which standard modules exist for a compilation target; an effect is a fact about a function; a capability is a run-time authority value."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#117-observation-plane","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.7 Observation plane","m":"spec","x":"11.7 Observation plane # Instrumentation that the compiler generates for capture , record and completion events observes a pure function without changing its effect summary: capture expressions are themselves pure and infallible, and the emitted records go to a diagnostic plane that cannot feed values back into the program. The diagnostic plane is also where report (10.6) and the log statements (13) write: its records go to the task reporter (15), never to the program's standard output, and nothing written there can be read back by the program. Two equivalences are distinguished: application equivalence (same results and same application effects) and trace equivalence (also the same required observation records). Optimisation must preserve application equivalence always and trace equivalence for records declared required in an observation schema; non-required instrumentation may be elided or merged."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#118-aspects","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.8 Aspects","m":"spec","x":"11.8 Aspects # An aspect is declared behaviour applied around a function. Where a contract subtracts, an aspect adds: code that runs before the body, code that runs after a successful body, a mapping for failures the body leaves unhandled, and metadata that a library reads. Aspects are how kits attach routes, authorisation, limits and schemas to ordinary functions, and how a team writes its own reusable conventions ( @transactional , @audited ). They are declared in ordinary modules, user packages included, and are typed, checked and lowered like any other code; there is no reflection and no runtime registry. Fortis // accept import fortis . http . server { HttpMethod , HttpStatus , HttpLimits , PublicProblem , Forbidden } import fortis . security { Authorisation , Identity } export aspect http [ R ] ( method : HttpMethod , path : StringView , responses : VariantMap [ R , HttpStatus ] , auth : dynamic Authorisation ? = null , fallback : ( fn ( PublicProblem ) R ) ? = null , limits : HttpLimits = HttpLimits . default ( ) , ) { needs who : Identity // minted per request by the server adapter; never a request field before { if auth != null && ! auth . grantedTo ( who ) { fail Forbidden ( ) } } on failure ( f : Failure ) R { if fallback == null { fail move f // no mapping: propagate } report annotate ( move f , path ) as receipt // mapping: report once, then respond return fallback ( PublicProblem . from ( receipt ) ) } metadata } export aspect get [ R ] ( path : StringView , ... rest ) = http [ R ] ( method : GET , path : path , ... rest ) Declaration. aspect name[R](params) { parts } . The name is lowercase (it does something). R is an optional type parameter bound at each application to the target function's success type. Parameters are ordinary named parameters with defaults; their types may be anonymous record types, so an application can carry nested properties ( limits: { bodyBytes: 65536, timeout: 5s } ). A parameter name cannot be a keyword, like any parameter. The parts are a closed set: Part Meaning needs name: Type the target must have exactly one parameter of this type (two is an error naming both); the hooks use it by this name with the mode the target declared ( needs db: editable Db requires an editable parameter) needs Contract the target must carry this contract in its modifier position before { \u2026 } runs before the body, with the aspect's arguments and needs parameters in scope; its locals end when it ends after(result: R) { \u2026 } runs after a successful body with a read loan of the result; may fail under the same rule as before , but only when R is not linear, since a failing after would leave the result with no consumer ( E-OBL-008 ) on failure(f: Failure) R { \u2026 } receives any failure that before , the body or after leaves unhandled, owning its obligation; must return an R or fail (re-failing to map a low-level failure to a domain one is permitted) metadata marks the arguments as data readable by a library through the toolchain (a router, a schema generator) A specialisation aspect name[R](params, ...rest) = other[R](fixed: value, ...rest) declares one aspect as another with some arguments fixed; ...rest forwards every remaining named argument unchanged. There is no around hook and no access to the body: an aspect can never change what a line inside the function means, and cannot re-run the body (a retry is a library function taking a fn once , not an aspect). Application. @name(args) , or @name alone for an aspect whose parameters all have defaults or that has none, on the lines directly above a function head (after any documentation comment, before export ). Arguments are compile-time constant expressions: literals, record and variant-map literals of them, enum values, fn literals without captures, and names of declarations such as observation schemas, functions or enum variants (a variant name used where a function type is expected denotes its constructor, 9.7). The first declared parameter may be passed posit"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#118-aspects","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.8 Aspects","m":"spec","x":"ion type is expected denotes its constructor, 9.7). The first declared parameter may be passed positionally; all others are named. An application whose arguments do not match the declaration, an unknown aspect, an unmet or ambiguous needs , or ...rest outside an aspect's parameter list is rejected ( E-TYP-150 ). Elaboration. @a(args) @b(args) func f(params) R { body } elaborates, before ownership and effect checking, to: an inner function f.body(params) R holding the original body, marked try if the original is or if any applied hook can fail; an outer f(params) R with the same signature that runs the before hooks in application order (first line first), then calls f.body , then the after hooks in reverse application order; around all of that, the on failure hooks, innermost first : a failure from any step goes to the last-applied aspect's handler; if it re-fails, to the next outer one; a handler that returns a value completes the function with it. The first line is therefore the outermost wrapper both for before (runs first) and for failure handling (sees a failure last). The elaborated outer function is checked as a whole: a before that fails with a linear owned parameter still unconsumed is E-OBL-001 like any other path (a non-linear owned parameter is simply dropped on that path); a hook that moves a needs parameter the target holds by loan, allocates under noalloc , reports under pure , or calls Host.capabilities() is rejected by the ordinary rules. A before may fail on a non- try target only when an on failure of the same or an outer aspect maps every failure to a value; otherwise the target must be try . Cancellation unwinds through hooks like any blocks. For observation purposes, \"success\" and \"failure\" describe the completion of the outer function, so a mapped failure is a success exit, and the frame is shared by hooks and body. The hooks' effects join the function's summary. There is no aspect at run time. Core aspects. The core declares two aspects. observe(schema: Schema) attaches an observation schema (section 13) to a function. ignorable() , applied as @ignorable , lets callers drop the function's non-linear result without discard (5.5; X-345); it has no hooks, only metadata the checker reads, and an application of it to a function whose result is linear is rejected ( E-TYP-150 ). Everything else is a library. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence Rule Statement Acceptance evidence --- --- --- LANG-11-01 The effect set MUST be exactly the eight effects of 11.1; summaries MUST be computed transitively with the fixed-point rule of 11.3. (old EFF-001) Transitive positive/negative graph fixtures. LANG-11-02 Fresh-region mutation and writes through the function's own editable parameters MUST NOT count as mutate (the latter form the write footprint); mutation through captures, guards or atomics MUST. (old EFF-002) Builder/caller-buffer test pair. LANG-11-03 noalloc MUST be checked against the pattern whitelist for the actual target and telemetry plan, covering failures, cleanup, closures and all async phases; a conflicting policy MUST be rejected at the policy site. (old EFF-004) Allocation audit corpus. LANG-11-04 Trusted summaries MUST be labelled trusted , never proved , and only the packages of the published compatibility set MAY declare trusted . (old EFF-005) Foreign-boundary reports; a program's trusted declarations rejected. LANG-11-05 async , try and recovers MUST be written wherever the summary has them; across a module boundary only written pure / noalloc claims MAY be relied on; weakening an exported function's written modifiers MUST be a reported API change. Semantic-diff corpus. LANG-11-06 Contracts MUST be conjunctions of 11.5 elements; configuration MUST NOT introduce predicates absent from source. (old EFF-006, CFG-003 part) Contract-origin fixtures. LANG-11-07 io effects other than report and log MUST require an Authority value in the signature, except in the entry main (4.6), which receives the capabili"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#118-aspects","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"11.8 Aspects","m":"spec","x":"ire an Authority value in the signature, except in the entry main (4.6), which receives the capabilities as parameters or creates them with one direct call of Host.capabilities() outside every loop, lambda and hook, and in an adapter's minting function, which requires the capability it derives from; every other use of Host.capabilities (including one in a main that takes a capability parameter), and every reference to the entry main , MUST be rejected. Missing-authority corpus; capability-creation corpus (loops, conditions, lambdas, hooks, function values, other modules and packages, references to the entry main , a main with capability parameters); a second creation is the runtime-invariant defect on every backend. LANG-11-08 Contracts MUST be applied in the modifier position and MUST NOT take arguments; @ MUST apply aspects only. Grammar corpus. LANG-11-09 Aspects MUST have only the parts of 11.8, MUST elaborate as in 11.8 before checking (hooks in application order, failure handlers innermost first), and the elaborated function MUST pass every ordinary check. Aspect corpus incl. unconsumed-owned-parameter, moved-needs, noalloc, pure and capability negatives; elaborated-source golden tests."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#12-cleanup-async-and-concurrency","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"12. Cleanup, async and concurrency","m":"spec","x":"12. Cleanup, async and concurrency #"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#121-defer","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"12.1 defer","m":"spec","x":"12.1 defer # Fortis // illustrative defer { statements } defer registers a block to run when the enclosing block exits by any route. Only the block form exists; there is no call form. Deferred blocks run in reverse order of registration, before the block's locals are dropped. A deferred block: is checked as if its body were written at every exit point of the block, after the loans held by the block's own bindings have ended and before the block's locals are dropped. The places it names, inside an interpolation hole as anywhere else (3.6), must therefore be initialised at every exit: moving one of them out between the defer and the exit is an error ( E-DEF-002 ). Between registration and exit the rest of the block may use those places freely. Loans held by bindings of enclosing blocks still apply at the exit and conflict as usual. Because the block's own loans have ended by then, a deferred block may not use a binding of its block that holds a loan (a view, or a loan returned from a parameter): E-OWN-052 . may not contain return , fail , give , break , continue or await ( E-DEF-001 ); it cannot alter the enclosing function's outcome. consumes every failure it produces itself, with or recover , catch \u2026 recover or report ; or fail is unavailable because there is no outcome to propagate to. A failure reported from a deferred block reaches the task reporter like any other report, labelled as raised during cleanup. Recovering a cleanup failure silently is permitted and adds recovers to the function's summary. contributes its effects to the enclosing function's summary. This is possible because defer lowers to explicit cleanup code at each exit (Appendix C), not to a closure or a destructor, so no loan has to be held across the block. Fortis // accept import fortis . host { FileSystem , Path } try func copyFile ( fs : FileSystem , source : Path , target : Path ) Void { let mut input = fs . open ( source ) or fail defer { editable input . close ( ) } // checked at each exit; input must still be owned there let mut output = fs . create ( target ) or fail defer { editable output . close ( ) } let data = editable input . readAll ( ) or fail // the defer holds no loan, so this is fine editable output . writeAll ( data ) or fail } defer await is not in v1 ( E-DEF-001 , like any await inside a deferred block); asynchronous cleanup is performed by the owning scope (12.5) or by explicit await calls before the block ends. The Delivery Programme's spike SP-06 gates any future extension."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#122-destruction-order","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"12.2 Destruction order","m":"spec","x":"12.2 Destruction order # Section 7.9 defines when values are destroyed. In summary, at a block exit: deferred blocks in reverse registration order; then owned locals in reverse initialisation order; for each class value, its cleanup method, then its fields in declaration order. The order is the same on both backends and under every exit kind, including cancellation observed at an await . A local that holds a loan is not an owned local: its end runs no cleanup , which runs once per value, when the value's owner destroys it (7.9, D-338)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#123-async-functions-and-futures","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"12.3 Async functions and futures","m":"spec","x":"12.3 Async functions and futures # An async func f(\u2026) T call produces a Future[T] (or Future[Outcome[T]] when also try ) without running the body. Arguments are evaluated at the call; the body starts at the first await of the future or when a scope launches it. Future[T] is linear: it must be await ed, launched on a scope, or discard ed. discard of a never-polled future is the one exception to the rule that linear values cannot be discarded (5.5, 6.12): it is permitted only when the future's captured environment contains no linear value and no exclusive loan, and it emits a not_started lifecycle record through the task reporter of the current task. A future holds the loans of its arguments (7.3); it may not outlive them, and may not be stored in a type without a lifetime parameter unless it holds none. A temporary argument or receiver (a call's result, a constructed value, a copy , an interpolated string) ends with its statement (5.7), unless its future is launched directly ( s.launch(f(\u2026)) , s.launchParallel(f(\u2026)) ), in which case the task owns it until the task has run. Otherwise a future that borrows a temporary is awaited within that statement: a statement that binds, assigns, returns or gives such a future, or an expression body that returns it, is rejected ( E-OWN-051 , X-251). Bind the argument first, or take the parameter owned . await e suspends the current task until e completes and yields its value. It consumes e : a future (or a task) is awaited through a place that owns it, and once. One reached through a loan or a Shared belongs to another owner and cannot be awaited ( E-OWN-001 , as no field can be moved out of a loan), a field of an owned value would be a partial move ( E-OWN-040 ), and a second await is a use after move ( E-OWN-020 ). await is permitted only directly in an async function body (not inside a lambda that is not itself async , not inside defer ). Loans held across an await remain active; an exclusive loan of a Mutex guard may not be held across an await ( E-ASY-002 ). A Task that leaves its scope block, by return , give or assignment to a binding declared outside it, is rejected ( E-ASY-001 )."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#124-cancellation","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"12.4 Cancellation","m":"spec","x":"12.4 Cancellation # Cancellation is a signal delivered to a task. It is observed only at a cancellation point : await on the cooperative executor, and await or an explicit checkpoint() call on the parallel and blocking pools. Cancellation is not a failure: it cannot be caught, recovered or matched. When a task observes it, the current function unwinds through every block to the task boundary, running deferred blocks on the way (12.2), and the task completes as cancelled. A future is never destroyed while suspended: a cancelled task resumes at its cancellation point, unwinds and finishes. Synchronous code runs to completion. A Duration deadline attached to a scope delivers the same signal when it expires. Because unwinding runs no user code other than defer blocks and Abandonable.abandon , a linear value live at a cancellation point would have no consumer. The rule is therefore: no linear value may be live across a cancellation point ( E-OBL-005 ) unless its type implements Abandonable : interface Abandonable { func abandon(owned self) Void } , whose abandon must be synchronous, non-failing and non-suspending. The order at each block during an unwind is the block-exit order of 5.1 with one addition: first the block's loans end and the tasks launched on a scope declared in the block are cancelled and joined (12.5), which also ends the loans those tasks carried; then the deferred blocks run; then abandon is invoked on every other live Abandonable local that owns its value, in reverse initialisation order (a local that holds a loan of one is not abandoned: its owner is, once; D-338); then the locals are dropped. Unwinding means leaving the function by cancellation or by fail , so an Abandonable local is also the one linear value that may be live at an or fail : the failure path abandons it, while a normal exit still has to consume it explicitly ( E-OBL-001 on falling off the end). Unwinding a statement first ends every loan that statement activated (the loan the suspended call holds on tx , for example), so abandon always runs on a local with no active loan, exactly as a block exit ends loans before dropping locals (5.1). Task implements it (its abandon is what the owning scope invokes when it cancels and joins, so a task handle is never abandoned after the block's defer s), and a library handle such as a database transaction implements it by marking itself abandoned for its pool to roll back, which is exactly the \"failure rolls back, success must commit\" rule a transaction wants. So let b = tasks.launch(\u2026) or let tx = db.transaction() may be live while an await suspends, but an Outcome , a Failure or a Future may not. Consume such a value before the await , pass it into the awaited call, or restructure. The futures and tasks the function is awaiting are not \"live across\" their own await : an awaited child future is cancelled with its parent and the parent waits for its unwind to complete."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#125-structured-scopes-and-launch","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"12.5 Structured scopes and launch","m":"spec","x":"12.5 Structured scopes and launch # Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Network } import acme . net { Url , Body , fetch } async try func fetchBoth ( net : Network , a : Url , b : Url ) ( Body , Body ) { scope tasks { let first = tasks . launch ( fetch ( net , a ) ) let second = tasks . launch ( fetch ( net , b ) ) return ( await first or fail , await second or fail ) } } scope name { \u2026 } is a block that owns tasks. name.launch(future) consumes a future and returns a Task[T] , which is linear and must be await ed inside the scope or passed to name.detach(move task, supervisor) , which transfers the join obligation to a Supervisor capability (section 11.6) and removes the task from the scope. await task yields T (the body's result, which may be an Outcome ): a child task cannot complete as cancelled or defective without the same event reaching the parent, because cancellation of a scope cancels every task in it and a defect in any task terminates the whole scope tree up to its supervisor (10.8). TaskCompletion[T] (12.6) is therefore observed only by supervisors of detached tasks. When the block exits by fail , return , break or cancellation while tasks are still running, the scope cancels them and waits for every one to finish as part of ending the block's loans, before the block's deferred blocks run (12.4), so a defer in the scope block never sees a loan still carried by a task; the results of tasks that were never awaited are consumed by the scope (a failure inside such a result is reported, labelled as abandoned by the scope). await first or fail leaving the block therefore cancels second . A scope never leaks a task and never leaves a loan live after its owner's block, which is what allows cooperative launch of futures holding loans without a separately proved scoped scheduler: the tasks run on the parent's thread and are joined before the block ends. Variants of launch : Form Executor Requirements s.launch(f) same cooperative executor, same thread the future may hold loans of any place that outlives the task: every binding declared in or before the scope block qualifies, because the scope joins its tasks before those bindings are dropped s.launchParallel(f) CPU worker pool the future's environment is Shareable and holds no loans; cancellation observed at await and at explicit checkpoint() calls s.launchBlocking(work) bounded blocking pool work is a synchronous fn once() T (not a future) whose environment is Shareable ; it is the only place the block effect is permitted inside async code; it observes cancellation only at checkpoint() The v1 executor runs every cooperative task of one scope tree on one thread; launch never requires Shareable . Borrowed sibling concurrency without a scope uses join(a, b) , which consumes both futures, polls them inside the parent and returns (A, B) once both have completed; it accepts futures holding loans. join never returns early: if the parent is cancelled, both futures are cancelled and run to completion first, and a failure value produced by one future is simply part of the returned tuple, so the caller consumes both outcomes."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#126-task-completion","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"12.6 Task completion","m":"spec","x":"12.6 Task completion # A supervisor observing a detached task receives TaskCompletion[T] : Fortis // accept enum TaskCompletion [ T ] { Completed ( value : T ) Cancelled ( reason : Cancellation ) Defect ( info : TaskDefect ) } Completed carries the body's result, which may itself be an Outcome . Cancelled and Defect are distinct from domain failures and are never seen as a Failure . TaskCompletion[T] is linear when T is linear; a supervisor consumes it with match ."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#127-sharing-data-between-tasks","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"12.7 Sharing data between tasks","m":"spec","x":"12.7 Sharing data between tasks # A value crosses launchParallel / launchBlocking only if its type is Shareable , a structural property: Shareable trivial types; String , Bytes , BigInt List , Array , Map , OrderedMap , Set , FixedList , FixedMap of shareables classes, enums, tuples and records whose fields are all shareable Shared[T] and Weak[T] where T: Shareable ; Mutex[T] where T: Shareable ; AtomicInt , AtomicBool fn / fn once closures whose captures are all copy or move of shareables capability values Not shareable: loans and views, MutexGuard , fn mut closures, Future , Task , resources that declare themselves thread-bound. A pool launch whose future's arguments or closure's captures include a loan or a value that is not Shareable is rejected ( E-ASY-003 ). Mutation shared between tasks goes through Mutex[T] . In async code await m.lock() suspends until the lock is free; in synchronous code (including launchBlocking bodies) m.lockBlocking() has the block effect. Both return Outcome[MutexGuard[T]] from mutex ; the guard is an exclusive loan of T , is not Shareable , cannot cross an await , and unlocks when destroyed. The value is read and written through it: guard.get() copies it (for a Copyable T ), guard.set(value) replaces it, dropping the old value, and guard.replace(value) replaces it and returns the old one, so any T can be taken out, changed and put back; a write through a guard is mutate (11.1). AtomicInt and AtomicBool offer load , store , fetchAdd , compareExchange with orderings seqcst (default) and acquire / release . There are no other orderings. Because an exclusive loan can never be shared and shared data is immutable unless behind a Mutex or atomic, Fortis programs are data-race free by construction; this is a property of the source rules and the VM and native backends both preserve it. No agreement between backends is claimed for the timing of interleavings. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence LANG-12-01 defer bodies MUST be checked at every exit as in 12.1, MUST NOT alter the function's outcome, and the places they name MUST be initialised at every exit. Defer corpus with every exit kind. LANG-12-02 Failures inside defer MUST be consumed in the body and MUST NOT replace the primary outcome; reports from cleanup MUST be labelled as such. (old RES-002) Multiple-failure execute tests. LANG-12-03 Future and Task MUST be linear; a pending future MUST never be destroyed; cancellation MUST be observed only at the cancellation points of 12.4. (old ASY-001, ASY-003) Cancellation corpus. LANG-12-04 A scope MUST join or cancel every task it launched before its block exits. (old ASY-004) Scope corpus and leak tests. LANG-12-05 Only Shareable environments MAY cross launchParallel / launchBlocking ; loans MUST NOT cross any launch except cooperative launch within the owner's block. (old ASY-002) Borrowed-spawn rejection and native compile tests. LANG-12-06 A MutexGuard MUST NOT be live across await . Guard corpus."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#13-observation-schemas-and-statements","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"13. Observation schemas and statements","m":"spec","x":"13. Observation schemas and statements # The core defines the observation schema declaration and two statements that record facts about an activation; everything downstream (destinations, projections, export) is the Telemetry kit. Fortis // accept import acme . orders { CustomerTier , Region , TransactionId } observation OrderProcessing { version : 1 // optional; 1 when omitted customerTier : CustomerTier required : all region : Region required : all transactionId : TransactionId ? required : success encoding : bounded ( 512 ) } A schema names typed fields, each with required: all | success | failure | none , an optional version and an encoding bound. Every field type must be Encodable , checked recursively like any derivation (6.10); since Secret[T] , authorities, loans, resources, closures, futures and tasks are not Encodable , none of them can occur anywhere inside a captured value. A schema is attached to a function with the core aspect @observe(OrderProcessing) (11.8), or by a library aspect that takes a schema as a property. Fortis // accept import acme . orders { Order , Payments , Receipt , OrderProcessing } @ observe ( OrderProcessing ) async try func processOrder ( order : Order , payments : Payments ) Receipt { capture customerTier = order . customer . tier capture region = order . customer . address . region let receipt = await payments . charge ( order ) or fail capture transactionId = receipt . transactionId return move receipt } capture field = expression assigns a snapshot of expression to the named field of the observation frame of the current activation. The frame's fields and their types come from the observation schema attached to the function ( @observe(OrderProcessing) ). The expression must be pure , infallible, non-suspending and of the field's declared type; it takes a read loan for the statement and copies the value into the frame, so later mutation cannot affect it. A String field must declare a bound in the schema; values longer than the bound are truncated or omitted as the schema declares, and the frame records which happened. The checker treats the frame as a set of definitely-assigned variables over the function's control-flow graph, including fail , return , await and cancellation edges: a field declared required: all must be assigned on every path before every exit; a field declared required: success (or failure ) must be assigned before every exit of that class; a required field left unassigned on some applicable exit is rejected ( E-OBS-001 , naming the field and the exit); a second capture of the same field on one path is rejected ( E-OBS-010 ); a capture inside a loop body is rejected ( E-OBS-011 ); repeated measurements use record ; a capture or record whose expression has an effect, or that names no field of the attached schema, is rejected ( E-OBS-012 ). record Name(field: value, \u2026) emits an event of the named schema immediately, as many times as it executes; it has the same purity requirement for its arguments. A frame carries its schema and version, the observed function, the captured fields, the fields cut to their bound, and the activation's outcome class ( success , failure , cancelled , defect ), which the compiler generates and source cannot forge. An activation that is cancelled before it starts produces a frame marked not_started without any captured fields. The core frame carries no timing: an activation's start, end and duration are the Telemetry kit's to define (X-252). Logs. log.info(message) , log.warn(message) and log.error(message) are observation statements: each execution writes one log record, carrying its level, its message and the function that wrote it, to the diagnostic plane (11.7). Logging is a platform-native concern (X-147), so it needs no capability and no configuration in the program (X-345): the task reporter (15) writes the record to standard error, rendered as text on a terminal ( warn: could not take 9 nuts ) and as one JSON line otherwise, where the pla"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#13-observation-schemas-and-statements","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"13. Observation schemas and statements","m":"spec","x":" as text on a terminal ( warn: could not take 9 nuts ) and as one JSON line otherwise, where the platform's log collection picks it up. A log record never mixes into the program's standard output, which is its own product ( console.print ), and every backend writes the same records in the same order. message is a StringView , so a literal, an interpolated literal or a String may be passed; it is evaluated like any argument before the record is written. A log statement has the io effect, like report , so a pure function cannot log; with report , it is the io that needs no authority (11.6). Fortis // accept import fortis . host { Console } func main ( console : Console ) Void { log . info ( \"starting\" ) console . print ( \"the result\" ) log . warn ( \"finished with a warning\" ) } log is not a keyword. The statement is log. followed by info , warn or error and one argument, written as a statement where log names no binding or declaration in scope, so a program that already names something log keeps it. log used any other way is an ordinary name, unresolved unless the program declares it ( E-NAM-001 , which also names the three levels). A failure is recorded with report e alone; log is for what is not a failure (a fallback taken, progress, a summary), so a handled failure is reported, not logged as well. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence LANG-13-01 capture MUST snapshot at the statement, copying the value; expressions MUST be pure and infallible. (old OBS-001) Mutation-after-capture tests. LANG-13-02 Required fields MUST be definitely assigned on every applicable exit exactly once; loops and double assignment MUST be rejected. (old OBS-002) Branch/failure/cancellation corpus. LANG-13-03 Frame contents MUST be bounded by the schema; truncation or omission MUST be recorded. (old OBS-006) Oversize corpus. LANG-13-04 Each execution of log.info , log.warn or log.error MUST write exactly one log record to the diagnostic plane, never to standard output, the same on every backend; it MUST need no Authority and MUST have the io effect. Log execute fixtures on every backend; a pure function that logs is rejected."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#14-test-declarations","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"14. Test declarations","m":"spec","x":"14. Test declarations # Tests are declarations and part of the core grammar, so every package can be tested without a kit. The runner, property generation, fuzzing, mutation and benchmarks are in the Delivery Programme: fortis test runs example tests, table rows, properties and (with --fuzz ) every property as a coverage-guided fuzz target, and reports a failing property's minimal input with the seed that replays it (X-131). Fortis // accept import acme . accounts { isAdult , loadAccount , CustomerId , Account } test \"adult boundary\" cases { \"below\" : { age : 17 , expected : false } \"equal\" : { age : 18 , expected : true } } as row { expect isAdult ( row . age ) == row . expected } test \"guest account on failure\" { let account = loadAccount ( CustomerId ( 999 ) ) catch e { expect e . code == \"accounts.not_found\" recover Account . guest ( ) } expect account . isGuest ( ) } property \"reversing twice is identity\" forall ( items : List [ Int < 0 .. 100 > ] ) { expect items . reversed ( ) . reversed ( ) == items } A test body is a block whose implicit function is try ( or fail is available and a failure fails the test). A test can only react to a failure it did not expect by failing, so in test and property bodies or fail is implied (X-345): a call of a fallible function there has its success type, and its failure fails the test with the failure's record, exactly as or fail would. expect (f() or fail) == v is written expect f() == v , and let x = f() binds the success value. The rule covers every call in the body whose callee is fallible ( try ), except a call that is the left operand of or or catch or the scrutinee of a match , which consume the outcome themselves, so expecting a failure stays as explicit as before; it does not reach into a lambda written in the body, whose body is its own function. A let that binds such a call keeps the outcome, as before, when the body later consumes that binding as an outcome ( let o = f() , then o catch , o or \u2026 or match o ) or when the binding is annotated Outcome[\u2026] . Integer arithmetic in the body is a checked region in the same way (6.4): an Int operator the checker cannot prove safe is the checked operation, and its failure fails the test, so expect (a * b or fail) == c is written expect a * b == c ; arithmetic that is the left operand of or or catch keeps its own region. Writing or fail stays accepted. Outside tests nothing changes: an unconsumed outcome is still E-OBL-001 or E-TYP-040 . Fortis // accept export error Negative { code : \"demo.negative\" } try func half ( n : Int ) Int { if n < 0 { fail Negative ( ) } return n / 2 } test \"half of an even number\" { expect half ( 10 ) == 5 let quarter = half ( half ( 8 ) ) expect quarter == 2 } test \"a negative number fails\" { let r = half ( - 2 ) catch e { expect e . code == \"demo.negative\" recover 0 } expect r == 0 } cases { \u2026 } as name { \u2026 } binds each row explicitly to name . The row record type is inferred from how the body uses the fields (bidirectionally, as for any literal), and every row literal is then checked against it at compile time, so quantity: 1 in a row passed to an Int<1..10000> parameter is checked exactly like the literal 1 would be. expect condition is a statement that records a typed actual/expected pair and fails the test when condition is false; expect of a non- Bool is an error. Expecting a failure consumes its obligation inside the test. Test declarations are compiled only in test builds and may import test-only modules. Rule Statement Acceptance evidence LANG-14-01 Table rows MUST be bound explicitly with as ; no implicit names enter scope. Grammar corpus. LANG-14-02 expect MUST take a Bool and MUST record typed evidence. Runner golden tests. LANG-14-03 In test and property bodies, a call of a fallible function that is not the left operand of or or catch or a match scrutinee, nor bound by a let that the body consumes as an outcome or annotates Outcome[\u2026] , MUST behave as that call followed by or fail , and integer a"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#14-test-declarations","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"14. Test declarations","m":"spec","x":"as an outcome or annotates Outcome[\u2026] , MUST behave as that call followed by or fail , and integer arithmetic there outside such an operand MUST be a checked region followed by or fail (6.4); outside them nothing changes. Test fixtures with implied or fail on calls and on arithmetic, passing and failing, on every backend; the same call outside a test, and arithmetic in a test's lambda, rejected."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#15-core-runtime-inventory","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"15. Core runtime inventory","m":"spec","x":"15. Core runtime inventory # The core is not only a compiler: a conforming implementation ships the runtime pieces below, so that a program that imports no kit can still run. Kits add adapters and policy on top; they never replace these. Component Provided by the core Kits add Executor one cooperative executor per process running the root task and every scope ; the parallel and blocking pools; cancellation signalling and deadlines the Tokio-backed adapter, I/O readiness, timers, channels (Runtime host) Task reporter the diagnostic-plane sink for report , log records (13), cleanup reports and not_started records, and the holder of completed capture frames; by default it writes reports and each frame's schema identity, outcome class and duration to standard error, and never a captured field value : field values leave the process only through the Telemetry kit's pipeline, after redaction ( Platform 6.1 , 6.3 ), and without that kit they are retained in memory for the debugger and REPL and then discarded; the toolchain's development plan permit-all ( FORTIS_TELEMETRY=permit-all , which the conformance runners set) permits every field unchanged on the record stream, an explicit choice like any plan exporters, archive, projections (Telemetry) Authorities the marker interface Authority ; the capabilities FileSystem , Network , Clock , Random , Environment , Process , Supervisor , Console , SecretAccess , RawSql , created once per run for the entry main 's parameters or by Host.capabilities() in fortis.host ; the rule for per-activation authorities ( RequestContext , Identity , Db , Tx , TaskContext , Mail , minted by the owning kit's adapter) the adapters that mint per-activation authorities, attenuation helpers, sandbox integration Observation schemas the observation head (section 13) schema registry and pipelines (Telemetry) Entry main in its four forms (4.6), its parameters supplied by type, one report and exit status 1 on failure the application head and lifecycle (Runtime host) Standard modules fortis.prelude , fortis.text , fortis.collections , fortis.numeric , fortis.time , fortis.money ( Decimal plus a run-time Currency ; Money.of , Money.zero , Money.add , Money.subtract , Money.multiply(money, factor: Int) , Money.divide(money, divisor: Int<1..>) , Money.greaterThan , the arithmetic fallible on currency mismatch), fortis.time ( Duration.days/hours/minutes/seconds/milliseconds , Duration.add(a, b) , Duration.sub(a, b) and Duration.multiply(d, factor) fallible on overflow of the signed 64-bit nanosecond count, d.nanoseconds() , comparison operators on Duration , a Duration displaying as its count in the largest unit that divides it exactly ( 150s , 1h , 250ms , 0s ), Timestamp.epoch() , Timestamp.add(t, d) fallible on overflow, Timestamp.after(a, b) , Timestamp.before(a, b) ), fortis.host (which also defines Path , built from text by try func parse(text: String) Path , and gives each capability its core operations: FileSystem readText , writeAll , readAll , open , create , subtree and the File it opens; Network.resolve ; Clock.now , frozen , sleep , elapsedSince ; Random.below(bound: Int<1..>) and seeded ; Environment.get ; Process.run ; Console.print ; RawSql.execute ; kits add the rest. The operations that wait on the outside block the calling thread and have the block effect (11.1): FileSystem 's readText , writeAll , readAll , open and create , File 's readAll and writeAll , Network.resolve , Process.run and RawSql.execute , so async code reaches them through launchBlocking ) everything else under fortis.* Secrets Secret[T] (no codec, no capture, fingerprint display: ${secret} shows a per-process keyed hash, never the value) and reveal(secret, cap: SecretAccess) in fortis.host , because the core Environment.get returns Secret[String]? declassify , Sanitiser , authentication adapters (Security)"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#16-reserved-declaration-heads-for-kits","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"16. Reserved declaration heads for kits","m":"spec","x":"16. Reserved declaration heads for kits # Kits extend the language with aspects (11.8) and, where a declaration is not function-shaped, with declaration heads : fixed keywords that introduce declarations whose bodies are validated by a versioned schema and elaborated into ordinary core declarations before type checking. The core grammar reserves them so that no identifier collides with a head of any kit, and defines the elaboration contract: a head produces classes, functions, enums, contracts and observation schemas that obey every rule in this document, and nothing else. Packages cannot add heads, tokens or grammar rules. Head Kit Elaborates to application Runtime host a generated main , capability acquisition and lifecycle ordering explore Developer tooling an exploration declaration (Delivery Programme) context (with provide inside its body) and the inject class member Dependency injection constructor parameters and a generated scope struct model Data a nominal data class, schema, codecs, query metadata view (standalone, from fortis.web.ssr ) and component (with state , action , view , resource inside its body, from fortis.web.components ) Web UI a render function; a class with an explicit state struct, action functions, render function observation core (section 13); consumed by Telemetry a frame type and schema A head's elaboration happens before ownership and effect checking, so kit declarations have no ownership or effect rules of their own: the generated code is checked like any other. A head body is a balanced token sequence of the core lexer, with one exception: a raw template region . The region is opened lexically, with no knowledge of the enclosing declaration: a statement (in the sense of 3.2, so a continued line is not a new statement) whose tokens are view , optionally preceded by export and by one of server , client , hybrid , followed by nothing, by a name, or by a name and a balanced parenthesised group, and then { . Where the view is a declaration the grammar requires that group to be a parameter list; inside a head body the owning kit validates it. The lexer takes the text from that { to the } that balances it, counting every { and } in between (including those of ${\u2026} interpolations), and hands that text to the Web UI kit unlexed as one token. Where such a region may appear (inside a component body, or as a standalone view head) is validated by the kit like the rest of a head body. Template syntax is therefore the kit's to define and never adds tokens to the core. The core grammar provides exactly four extension slots through which a kit's syntax enters a module, and nothing else: a head block, optionally prefixed by server , client or hybrid ; an [export] inject [lazy] name: Type member inside a class body (an exported slot becomes an exported field, so a consumer declared in one module can be constructed by a context in another, X-138); aspects (11.8) declared by the kit and applied with @name(args) , which need no slot of their own because they are ordinary declarations; the cases table syntax of test declarations. Head words are reserved words and are never imported; importing a kit package makes its heads available, and its aspects are imported by name like any declaration. The Platform Specification lists the heads, prefixes and aspects each kit defines. A head is checked only through its kit's validator and elaboration, so a head whose kit the toolchain does not install is rejected at check with E-NAM-034 , which names the kit: the core never accepts a declaration it can neither validate nor elaborate, and a test runner never reports a module with one as passing."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#appendix-a-keyword-inventory","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"Appendix A. Keyword inventory","m":"spec","x":"Appendix A. Keyword inventory # Reserved words of the core, with the defining section. A keyword cannot name a declaration, binding or parameter, except that it may name a method or associated function; after . it is an ordinary member name (3.4). Group Words Declarations func fn let mut const type alias class trivial enum error interface mixin implement implements with derive requires forbids excludes contract aspect needs before after on metadata lifetime from where export import as Modifiers pure noalloc async try recovers editable owned dynamic linear trusted once Ownership copy move Control if else while for in loop break continue return fail give recover match when otherwise is or catch discard defer await scope Values true false null present success failure self Self Observation capture record report observation required version encoding Testing test cases property forall fuzz bench expect Kit heads (reserved by the core, defined by kits) outer heads application context model component view explore ; prefixes server client hybrid ; words used inside head bodies inject provide state action view resource Contextual words that are not reserved: launch , launchParallel , launchBlocking , detach (methods on a scope value), retain , validate , checkpoint , join , annotate , cleanup , capability , lazy . Keywords are reserved in declaration and binding positions, and are ordinary names after . and as method or associated-function names (3.4)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#appendix-b-closed-decisions","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"Appendix B. Closed decisions","m":"spec","x":"Appendix B. Closed decisions # Topic Decision Declaration vs assignment let / let mut declare; = assigns only. Semicolons Parse error. Void functions May fall off the end; non- Void functions need return / fail on every path. A head may leave out Void (X-345). Markers the declaration shows An editable self receiver or fn mut call needs no editable wherever the marker could be written, and is checked as the marked call; return of a local let binding needs no move (X-345). Type of fail Never . Match-arm value give . Catch binding Explicit: catch e { \u2026 } . Unsuffixed integer literal Type from context, default Int ; must fit the target refinement. Float literal Needs . or exponent; f32 suffix for Float32 . Decimal literal d suffix. String literal StringView from static ; converts implicitly to String where one is expected (allocates). Named arguments After positionals, any order, each once; names are public API. Default arguments Pure constant expressions, re-evaluated per call. Variadics ListView[T] parameter. Negative example marker // reject: E-XXX-NNN . Call-site markers move p , copy p , editable p required for existing places; fresh values unmarked. Closure kinds fn , fn mut , fn once , always written. Generic lambdas Not in v1. Const generics N literal only; no generic functions over N . Partial moves Not in v1. Non-lexical lifetimes Not in v1; loans are lexical. Stored loans (lifetime parameters on user types) Not in v1 (X-137); E-OWN-053 . Loans live in locals, parameters and from returns; the standard views and cursors are compiler-known. Two-phase borrows Yes: reserve at argument evaluation, activate at call entry. Two-origin loans Rejected. Operator overloading None; Type.add etc. Or-patterns Yes, narrow (X-345): A | B => v , whole patterns of an arm, no bindings, no guard (9.8). Range patterns Not in v1. nostate Removed; lint only. or die Removed; try main . yield Replaced by give . defer call form Removed; block form only. defer await Not in v1; E-DEF-001 . Executor Cooperative single thread per scope tree; parallel/blocking pools need Shareable . Atomics AtomicInt , AtomicBool ; seqcst , acquire / release . Hash iteration Deterministic per build; allowed in pure . Float equality IEEE == allowed; no Equatable / Hashable / Comparable . Entry point main in the manifest's entry module. File extension .ft . Comments // only; documentation is the // run directly above a declaration. No /* */ , no /// . Transparent type aliases Not in v1; type is always nominal. Stored and returned loans Both say from ; within is removed. recovers A modifier, written like try . Lambda modifiers Same as named functions; capture brackets only when non-empty. Receiver markers editable p.m() and move p.m() required for editable self / owned self methods. Kept loans at call sites alias p / editable p required for an argument the result borrows from . Constructor naming create , of , from ; there is no new . Test rows cases { \u2026 } as row { \u2026 } . report A statement; report e as receipt binds the receipt. Contracts Applied in the modifier position by name ( export FastCalculation func \u2026 ); never with @ ; no arguments. Aspects @name(args) , lowercase, the only use of @ ; parts needs , before , after , on failure , metadata ; specialisation with ...rest ; on failure may re-fail; user packages may declare them. Observation attachment @observe(Schema) , a core aspect. Interfaces Nominal only; requires for sub-interfaces; bundles satisfied automatically; no method bodies. Serialisation derive Codec (= Encodable + Decodable ); canonical toBytes / toText ; formats as packages with \u2026Serialised mixins; hand-written encode and derive cannot coexist. Projection constructor spread ...copy p (copies taken fields) / ...move p (consumes the whole source); derive From[S] is a borrowed copy; to() on every type. Aspect hooks before , after , on failure , metadata ; failure handlers innermost first; no around , no body re-execution. Constants Constant expressions inc"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#appendix-b-closed-decisions","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"Appendix B. Closed decisions","m":"spec","x":"ilure handlers innermost first; no around , no body re-execution. Constants Constant expressions include record/variant-map literals, declaration names and the Type.of(\u2026) intrinsics. Variant maps VariantMap[E, V] written { Variant: value } ; optional callables need parentheses (fn(\u2026) R)? . Explicit type arguments at calls Not in v1; inferred, or annotate the binding. Associated types Not in v1; interfaces take generic parameters. Variadic parameters Not in v1; pass a ListView[T] . Blocking in async code Forbidden; launchBlocking takes a synchronous fn once . Length-refined collections Immutable; mutate an unrefined collection and validate . Effect clauses pure / noalloc / async / try / recovers stay as the signature vocabulary; a full effects(\u2026) clause was considered and rejected as noise on every function. Inferred alloc , block , nondet are shown by tooling. Standard package fortis ( fortis.prelude , fortis.text , \u2026); there is no std ."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#appendix-c-lowering-reference-and-the-rust-expressible-subset","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"Appendix C. Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset","m":"spec","x":"Appendix C. Lowering reference and the Rust-expressible subset #"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#c1-lowering-shapes","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"C.1 Lowering shapes","m":"spec","x":"C.1 Lowering shapes # Each row names which checker is authoritative. Rust-checked means rustc 's own type and borrow checking independently verifies the lowered shape, and a rustc rejection of frontend-accepted code is a compiler defect. Frontend-checked means the property has no Rust type; rustc acceptance is not evidence, and the IR validator plus the whitelisted Rust subset are the controls. Source construct Native representation Rust-checked Frontend-checked type newtype struct Name(Repr) with private field nominal identity refinement invariant class struct + impl field privacy generated-constructor visibility trivial type Copy struct/enum Copy the trivial table copy p Clone::clone of a generated Clone impl copyable set; allocation read loan &T loan validity lexical loan end (frontend is stricter) exclusive loan &mut T loan validity field-freezing rule move p move use after move from return fn f<'a>(x: &'a T) -> &'a U lifetime single-origin rule standard view or cursor &'a str , &'a [T] , struct Cursor<'a, T> owned by its holder ( fn iterate<'a>(&'a self) -> Cursor<'a, T> , fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a T> ) lifetime standard types only: no user type stores a loan (X-137) Shared[T] / Weak[T] Arc<T> / Weak<T> Send / Sync once emitted retain visibility Mutex[T] std::sync::Mutex wrapper Send / Sync guard-across-await rule interface trait + generics trait bounds effect/refinement substitutability, coherence dynamic I Box<dyn I> / &dyn I / Arc<dyn I> object safety dynamic-eligibility, allocation mixin generated inherent methods conflicts, required methods lambda closure with concrete environment Fn / FnMut / FnOnce declared kind is at least the kind the body needs; capture list lambda whose result is a function type closure returning Box<dyn Fn\u2026> (a Rust closure cannot name an impl Fn result), so the function it returns is boxed and the lambda allocates (C.3) Fn / FnMut / FnOnce as for a lambda try func Result<T, Failure> linearity (no Drop of a live Failure is ever emitted; a #[must_use] plus an aborting Drop is the trusted backstop) catch match recover/return semantics T? Option<T> no implicit absence/failure equivalence match match exhaustiveness (both) ownership of bindings async func async fn state machine linear futures, cancellation at await scope generated join set polled by the parent future cancellation and join on exit launchParallel spawn on a worker pool with 'static + Send bounds Send Shareable defer body inlined at every exit path (not Drop ) borrow validity of each inlined copy places initialised at every exit cleanup impl Drop calling the method drop order effects contributed noalloc no representation pattern whitelist (C.3) pure , effects no representation summaries capture / record explicit frame struct writes and record calls definite assignment capability zero-sized or handle-carrying struct with private constructor io requires capability Generated application crates carry #![forbid(unsafe_code)] . Runtime and adapter crates are audited separately."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#c2-unsupported-shapes","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"C.2 Unsupported shapes","m":"spec","x":"C.2 Unsupported shapes # Rejected before lowering, with the listed alternative. A shape that only the lowering can identify is rejected with E-OWN-090 . A shape that an earlier rule already excludes keeps that rule's more specific code (X-126). No shape is ever lowered with unsafe or lifetime erasure. Shape Rejected with Alternative a loan returned from two parameters E-SYN-000 : a loan type names one origin (7.6) return an owned value or split a class that stores a loan (a lifetime parameter, or a field of a loan or loan-carrying type) E-OWN-053 : stored loans are not in v1 (7.7, X-137) own, copy, hold a Shared , or pass per call a loan stored in Shared / Mutex E-OWN-090 own the value a fn mut closure escaping into a Shared E-OWN-090 Mutex around the state and a fn closure a future holding a loan launched on a parallel pool E-ASY-003 : only Shareable environments without loans cross a pool launch (12.7) pass owned data a self-referential class E-OWN-053 : no field stores a loan, of its own storage or of anything else (7.7, X-137) indices or Shared + Weak a partial move E-OWN-040 : no partial moves (7.2) move the whole or restructure"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#c3-allocation-pattern-whitelist","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"C.3 Allocation pattern whitelist","m":"spec","x":"C.3 Allocation pattern whitelist # A lowering is allocation-free if every operation in it is one of: arithmetic and comparison; field and array access; construction of trivial or inline types ( Array , FixedList , FixedMap , bounded Failure ), and of any class or record from values that are trivial, moved, freshly constructed, returned by a call, or retained ( retain() , downgrade() ), since a class's own storage is inline and a heap value's allocation is counted where that value is created (X-137); read and exclusive loans; calls to functions whose summary lacks alloc ; match ; defer lowering; cooperative launch of a future whose state fits the scope's inline storage declared by the runtime host; capture into a frame of statically bounded size. Everything else is alloc : List / String / Map growth, Shared.create , dynamic creation, copy of heap-owning types, boxed futures from dynamic async calls, extended failure payloads, launchParallel / launchBlocking , a function value returned by a lambda (boxed, C.1), and a cooperative launch beyond the host's inline storage. The v1 runtime host declares no inline task storage, so in v1 every cooperative launch is alloc (X-125); join(a, b) , which polls both futures inside the parent, is not."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#appendix-d-rule-index-and-old-requirement-ids","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"Appendix D. Rule index and old requirement IDs","m":"spec","x":"Appendix D. Rule index and old requirement IDs # Old ID (FSPEC-0.1) New rule SYN-001 LANG-05-05 , LANG-08-01 SYN-002 LANG-03-03 (case rule replaces symbol-category disambiguation) SYN-003 PROG-CMP (formatter), see Delivery Programme SYN-004 LANG-05-07 SYN-005, SYN-006 PROG-DIA / PROG-CMP, see Delivery Programme TYP-001 LANG-06-01 TYP-002 LANG-06-03 TYP-003 LANG-06-04 TYP-004 LANG-06-02 TYP-005 PROG-TST (library metadata), see Delivery Programme TYP-006 LANG-06-08 OWN-001 LANG-07-01 OWN-002 LANG-07-02 OWN-003 LANG-07-03 OWN-004 LANG-07-04 OWN-005 LANG-07-06 OWN-006 LANG-07-08 FUN-001, FUN-002 LANG-08-02 OO-001 LANG-09-01 , LANG-09-04 GEN-001 LANG-06-10 and PROG-CMP (bounded solving) MOD-001 LANG-04-02 DOC-001 PROG-TST, see Delivery Programme ERR-001 LANG-10-01 ERR-002 LANG-10-02 ERR-003, ERR-004 LANG-10-03 ERR-005 LANG-10-04 ERR-006 LANG-09-05 ERR-007 PLAT Telemetry / Runtime host (disposition policy) EFF-001 LANG-11-01 EFF-002 LANG-11-02 EFF-003 removed with nostate EFF-004 LANG-11-03 EFF-005 LANG-11-04 EFF-006 LANG-11-06 RES-001 LANG-05-02 , LANG-07-07 RES-002 LANG-12-02 ASY-001, ASY-003 LANG-12-03 ASY-002 LANG-12-05 ASY-004 LANG-12-04 ASY-005 PLAT Runtime host OBS-001 LANG-13-01 OBS-002 LANG-13-02 OBS-003, OBS-004, OBS-005 PLAT Telemetry OBS-006 LANG-13-03"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Language_Reference.html#appendix-e-glossary","p":"Fortis Language Reference","h":"Appendix E. Glossary","m":"spec","x":"Appendix E. Glossary # The terms that name the parts of Fortis and its distribution (PROG-PRD-38, X-150). A term defined by a section of this document is defined there; this table says which, and adds the terms that the rest of the specification uses for what surrounds the language. Term Meaning binding Two senses, always distinguished by context. A name binding is a name introduced by let , let mut , a parameter, a pattern or a capture (5.1, 7.2). An embedding binding is the versioned package through which another language (SQL, HTML, a pattern) gains fenced blocks in Fortis: a run-time library and a verifier (Programme PROG-PRD-30). build mode How a program is built and run: dev (the development VM), native-dev and release (Programme 5.4). What other toolchains call build profiles. Integer behaviour never depends on the build mode (6.4). declaration head (head) A reserved keyword that introduces a declaration whose body is validated by a schema and elaborated into ordinary core declarations before type checking, such as application or component (16). Every head is reserved by the core; observation is the core's own (13), explore belongs to developer tooling (Programme 9.1), and the others are defined by kits (16). kit A named, versioned bundle of packages that ships with the distribution and extends Fortis only through libraries, aspects (11.8), declaration heads (16), embedding bindings and verifiers: the HTTP kit, the Data kit, the Web UI kit. A kit never changes the meaning of core code (Programme PROG-FND-07, PROG-PRD-38). The kits and their packages are defined by the Platform Specification. lint level How strictly the toolchain reports lints: the baseline level and the strict level (4.3, 8.2, 11.3). A lint never changes typing. module One source file under src/ ; its path is its identity (4.2). package The unit of distribution, versioning and visibility: a directory with project.yaml , a lockfile and src/ (4.1). Packages are nominal: two versions of one package are two packages (4.1). toolchain component A tool distributed with the toolchain that is neither the language nor a kit and adds nothing to a program: the daemon, the language server and the SDK generators (Programme PROG-PRD-38). verifier A package that can only make the check stricter: it reads the checked program and returns records, never code, is pure, deterministic and bounded, and holds no capability (Programme PROG-PRD-36)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#fortis-platform-specification","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"Fortis Platform Specification","m":"spec","x":"Fortis Platform Specification # Document: FSPEC-0.2 / Platform \u2022 Date: 22 October 2026 \u2022 Status: normative baseline for the kits. Companion to the Fortis Language Reference and the Fortis Delivery Programme. The overview document ( Fortis_Specification.md ) defines the epistemic conventions that apply here; every code block in this document is illustrative and has not been run through a Fortis compiler."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#contents","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"Contents","m":"spec","x":"Contents # 1. Overview: kits, packages and the compatibility set 2. Runtime host kit 3. Dependency injection kit 4. HTTP kit 5. Data kit 6. Telemetry kit 7. Web UI kit (a library) 8. Security kit 9. Packages, scaffolds and supply chain 10. Configuration and deployment 11. Developer tooling 12. Standard contracts Appendix A. Requirement ID rename table"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#1-overview-kits-packages-and-the-compatibility-set","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"1. Overview: kits, packages and the compatibility set","m":"spec","x":"1. Overview: kits, packages and the compatibility set # A kit is a named, separately versioned set of packages that an application imports to get one area of platform behaviour: an HTTP server, a database layer, telemetry, a web UI. Kits sit on top of Fortis Language 1.0 and are ordinary libraries: they are written in Fortis (with trusted Rust adapters where the Language Reference permits), they obey every language rule, and nothing in the language core depends on any of them. An application that imports no kit is a plain Fortis program."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#11-what-a-kit-is","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"1.1 What a kit is","m":"spec","x":"1.1 What a kit is # Each kit consists of: one or more packages whose names begin with fortis. and are listed in 1.2 ( fortis.http with modules such as server ; the Web UI kit's five packages fortis.web.ssr , fortis.web.components , fortis.web.forms , fortis.web.spa , fortis.web.pwa , each with a root module); no kit package is named after a core module ( prelude , text , collections , numeric , time , money , host ), and the Language Reference's longest-declared-prefix rule splits every import unambiguously because 1.2 is the only source of package names; a kit version, independent of the language version; a published feature-set table stating on which targets the kit's packages exist; its own gate in the Delivery Programme and its own acceptance application; the aspects it declares (such as get , post , transactional ) and, for declarations that are not function-shaped, the declarative heads it defines ( model , component , context ), each with a versioned validator and a documented elaboration into ordinary typed declarations. A declarative head is not a parser extension. The Language Reference, section Reserved declaration heads for kits, reserves the head words and fixes their outer syntax; the kit defines what the body means and how it elaborates. The core grammar provides exactly four extension slots, and a kit MUST use only these: Head blocks : model , component , view , context , application , explore , each optionally prefixed by server , client or hybrid . The body is validated by the owning kit's versioned schema. The inject member slot : [export] inject [lazy] name: Type inside a class body (Dependency injection kit; export exports the field the slot becomes, X-138). Aspects : declared by the kit with the core aspect mechanism (Language Reference, section Effects and contracts) and applied with @name(args) above a function head; these are ordinary declarations and need no slot of their own. There are no attributes on enum variants. Test tables : the cases syntax of test declarations. A kit MUST NOT introduce tokens, grammar rules or effects that the core does not already know. Head words and the observation schema words ( observation , required , version , encoding ) are reserved words of the core and are never imported; an import lists the types, functions and aspects a kit package exports."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#12-kits-defined-by-this-document","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"1.2 Kits defined by this document","m":"spec","x":"1.2 Kits defined by this document # Kit Packages (minimum) Depends on Heads it defines Runtime host fortis.runtime (root module with Application , Server -style lifecycle types; module executor ); capability types stay in the core module fortis.host core application Dependency injection fortis.di host context ; the inject and provide declarations HTTP one package fortis.http with a root module and the modules server , client , openapi , sdk (imported as fortis.http.server , \u2026) host, di, telemetry none; aspects http , get , post , put , patch , delete Data one package fortis.data with a root module and the modules postgres , sqlite , jobs , cache , mail host, di, telemetry model ; aspects job , schedule Telemetry one package fortis.telemetry with a root module and the modules otel , archive , finops host none (the observation head is core) Web UI five separate packages, each with a root module: fortis.web.ssr , fortis.web.components , fortis.web.forms , fortis.web.spa , fortis.web.pwa http, di, telemetry view (standalone, fortis.web.ssr ); component (with state , resource , action , view inside its body, fortis.web.components ) Security one package fortis.security with a root module and the modules auth , secrets host none Codecs one package fortis.codec with a root module and the modules json , yaml , csv , toml , msgpack , each providing its \u2026Serialised / \u2026Deserialised mixins over the core Encodable / Decodable tree core ( alloc feature set) none Packages and scaffolds toolchain ( fortis package , fortis scaffold ) core none (scaffold manifests are YAML) Configuration and deployment toolchain and fortis.config host none Developer tooling toolchain (VM, daemon, agent protocol, dashboard) core explore The last three rows are mostly toolchain components (the package manager, the scaffold engine, the VM, the daemon, the agent protocol), which are not kits (Programme PROG-PRD-38); they are listed for what a project uses of them: the fortis package and fortis scaffold commands, fortis.config and the explore head."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#13-the-compatibility-set","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"1.3 The compatibility set","m":"spec","x":"1.3 The compatibility set # A compatibility set is a published tuple: one language version, one version of each kit, one toolchain version. Kits are tested together only within a published set. An application's project.yaml names the set it builds against; the lockfile pins every package digest within it. A kit MAY release a new version without a new language version. A kit MUST NOT require a change to the language core; a kit that needs one files a language change through the Delivery Programme's evolution process and waits for it. Kit versions follow semantic versioning. A compatibility set is immutable once published."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#14-how-a-kit-is-used","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"1.4 How a kit is used","m":"spec","x":"1.4 How a kit is used # Fortis // illustrative import fortis . host { Host } import fortis . runtime { Application } import fortis . http . server { Router } import fortis . data . postgres as pg try func main ( ) { let caps = Host . capabilities ( ) // see section 2 let app = Application . build ( move caps ) app . run ( ) or fail // a failing main reports once and exits 1 } Importing a kit package has no side effects: the Language Reference's rule that imports resolve declarations only applies unchanged. A kit's runtime work begins only when the application calls into it."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#15-target-feature-sets","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"1.5 Target feature sets","m":"spec","x":"1.5 Target feature sets # Target feature sets select which kit packages exist for a target; they never change the meaning of code that compiles. The Language Reference separates three things that were previously called \"capability\": target feature sets (this section), effects (the closed effect set in the Language Reference) and runtime authority values (section 2.2 below). A reference to a package that does not exist on the selected target is a compile error before any native compilation starts. Feature set Available kits core none (language core only) alloc codecs ( fortis.codec ); enables fortis.collections growth APIs host runtime host, di, data, telemetry, security, configuration ( fortis.config ), packages async required by host; enables fortis.runtime.executor web http, web UI (server side) browser web UI client subset ( fortis.web.spa , fortis.web.pwa client half) desktop desktop host adapter (scaffold; see section 9.4) ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-01-01 Kits MUST be separately versioned and tested only within a published compatibility set. Set manifest validation; mixed-version negative tests. FND-001 (part), STD-001 PLAT-01-02 A kit MUST NOT add grammar, tokens or effects beyond those the core reserves. Extension boundary tests. PKG-003 PLAT-01-03 A kit package that is absent on the selected target MUST fail at check , before native compilation. Cross-target negative corpus. ARC-003 PLAT-01-04 Importing a kit MUST NOT perform runtime work. Module initialisation tests. restates LANG-04-02 for kits PLAT-01-05 Unused kits MUST NOT be linked into a native executable. Binary/dependency/startup inspection. STD-002, NFR-014"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#2-runtime-host-kit","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"2. Runtime host kit","m":"spec","x":"2. Runtime host kit # The runtime host kit turns a checked Fortis program into a long-running process: it supplies the native executor adapter (Tokio), I/O readiness, timers and signals, the application head and an orderly start and stop. It builds on the core runtime inventory that the Language Reference defines and every Fortis program already has without any kit: the cooperative executor interface and its cancellation rule, the task reporter that report writes to, the observation schema head, the capability types ( FileSystem , Network , Clock , Random , Environment , Process , Supervisor , Console , SecretAccess , RawSql ), Host.capabilities() , and async try func main as an entry form that the core executor runs. A command-line program that reads files and runs scoped async work therefore needs no kit; an application imports this kit for the production executor adapter, lifecycle and the application head."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#21-executor-adapter","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"2.1 Executor adapter","m":"spec","x":"2.1 Executor adapter # fortis.runtime.executor implements the executor interface that the Language Reference, section Cleanup, async and concurrency, defines: one cooperative thread per executor for launch ; await as the only cancellation point on the cooperative executor; launchParallel and launchBlocking as separate pools whose tasks require Shareable captures and observe cancellation at await and at explicit checkpoint() calls. Tokio is the reference native adapter and is wrapped behind this interface; application code never names Tokio. The executor provides: task scheduling; I/O readiness; timers and deadlines; channels; task supervision; cancellation signalling; shutdown; profiling hooks. Every adapter operation documents cancellation safety and partial effects."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#22-capabilities-are-authority-values","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"2.2 Capabilities are authority values","m":"spec","x":"2.2 Capabilities are authority values # A capability is a value of a nominal type that carries authority to perform a class of I/O. The ten capability types ( FileSystem , Network , Clock , Random , Environment , Process , Supervisor , Console , SecretAccess , RawSql ) are defined by the language core (Language Reference, section Effects and contracts) and re-exported by fortis.host ; this kit adds the attenuation methods and the adapter that enforces each one. A function with the io effect that reads a file MUST receive a FileSystem value (or an attenuated derivative) as a parameter; the standard library's I/O functions take their authority explicitly (a capability, or a per-activation authority such as Db ). Rules: Capabilities are created once per process: by the runtime, which supplies them to the entry main as the parameters it declares, or, in an entry main that declares none, by one direct call of Host.capabilities() (Language Reference 4.6, 11.6). The checker rejects every other use of Host.capabilities() ( E-EFF-010 ); a backend asked for a second creation stops the run with the runtime-invariant defect (Language Reference 10.8). Capabilities are attenuated by methods that return a narrower value: fs.subtree(path) , net.allowHosts(list) , clock.frozen(instant) . Capabilities are Shareable and may be transferred to tasks; a task that was not given a capability cannot acquire one. A capability cannot be constructed from its fields, serialized, captured by an observation, or recovered from a Failure . The static check is by type: an io -effect call type-checks only when the required Authority type (a capability, or a per-activation authority minted from one) is supplied. OS-level enforcement (sandboxing) is outside this version; the Delivery Programme lists it as future work. Fortis // illustrative try func loadConfig ( fs : FileSystem , path : Path ) Config { let text = fs . readText ( path ) or fail return Config . parse ( text ) or fail }"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#23-the-application-head-and-lifecycle","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"2.3 The application head and lifecycle","m":"spec","x":"2.3 The application head and lifecycle # Fortis // illustrative application Billing { entry : billing . main capabilities : [ FileSystem , Network , Clock ] shutdownDeadline : 30s } application elaborates to a generated main wrapper that: parses typed configuration; obtains capabilities; builds the provider graph (if fortis.di is imported); opens resources; registers routes and workers; signals readiness. Startup failure disposes what was constructed, in reverse order, and reports once at the entry boundary. The process exits with code 1 and one report, as the Language Reference specifies for a failing main . Shutdown, on signal or on request: stop intake; cancel and join tasks under the deadline; finalise resources; flush permitted telemetry; exit. Liveness and readiness are distinct endpoints; readiness names the critical dependencies it checks. ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-02-01 The executor MUST implement the Language Reference execution model exactly: cooperative single-thread launch with await -only cancellation; Shareable -gated parallel and blocking pools with cancellation at await and checkpoint() . Executor conformance suite shared with the VM. implements LANG-12-03 , LANG-12-05 in the adapter PLAT-02-02 Capabilities MUST be unforgeable nominal values obtainable only as the entry main 's parameters, or from one direct call of Host.capabilities() in an entry main that takes none. Construction/serialization/capture negative tests. new PLAT-02-03 Every I/O function in fortis MUST take its authority (a capability or a per-activation authority) explicitly. API inventory lint. new PLAT-02-04 Startup and shutdown MUST follow the typed order with accountable cleanup and one report on failure. Partial startup/drain/deadline tests. APP-001 PLAT-02-05 Every executor adapter MUST document cancellation safety and partial effects. Adapter metadata and fault tests. ASY-005 PLAT-02-06 Detached work MUST hold a Supervisor capability and a valid owned context. Request escape/shutdown tests. ASY-004"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#3-dependency-injection-kit","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"3. Dependency injection kit","m":"spec","x":"3. Dependency injection kit # The dependency injection kit lets an application declare what a class needs ( inject ), what supplies it ( provide ) and in which environment ( context ), and then generates the constructors and scope classes that wire everything together. An application imports it to replace hand-written wiring with declarations the compiler checks. It is a library with a code generator, not a runtime registry. This section is the kit's first version (DI schema v1), revised to the subset SP-08 established (Delivery Programme 12; SP-08's decision record)."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#31-elaboration","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"3.1 Elaboration","m":"spec","x":"3.1 Elaboration # inject , provide and context are declarative heads defined by this kit. They elaborate to ordinary code before ownership checking, so the Language Reference has no DI-specific borrow or effect rules, and the kit never changes a type: a slot is filled with the most direct ordinary expression, which the core checks like any other, and a core diagnostic inside generated code is reported at the declaration it was generated from (with the generated line as generated_line ). every inject slot becomes a field of its class where it is written (only the word inject leaves the line), so it is a parameter of the class's generated constructor; export inject makes it an exported field (X-138), which a context in another module needs, since a generated constructor is callable only where every field is visible (Language Reference 9.2); every context becomes a class C with the associated function application(inputs) and one class per scope level it uses ( CApplication , CRequest , \u2026) holding the instances that live at that level; every provide becomes a factory: a field of its level's class for an instance held at a level, or a method on the outermost level class from which it is resolvable for a transient binding. A consumer can therefore be constructed manually in tests by calling its generated constructor with explicit arguments. Fortis // illustrative class CheckoutService { export inject gateway : Shared [ dynamic PaymentGateway ] export inject clock : Clock } context Production ( clock : owned Clock ) { provide Clock using instance move clock provide Shared [ dynamic PaymentGateway ] using StripeGateway { lifetime : application } provide Shared [ dynamic PaymentGateway ] using AdyenGateway to [ CheckoutService . gateway , PreviewService . gateway ] provide CheckoutService } A slot's type is an ordinary field type and says how the slot is filled: an owned value is built fresh by a transient binding or copied from a held one (the core rejects a copy of a value that is not Copyable ); a Shared[\u2026] or Weak[\u2026] slot takes retain() of a held instance; a factory's plain parameter takes a loan for the call. Since no field stores a loan (Language Reference 7.7, X-137), instances shared between consumers are shared through Shared , visible in the consumer's own source, and an interface is injected as dynamic I (one boxed value per consumer) or Shared[dynamic I] (one instance). A context's body is one statement per line, in any order, a line that starts with to , replaces or { continuing the provide above it; a group, a level's inputs or a context declared twice, or a context whose generated classes the module already declares, is E-DI-010 . A class or factory a binding names is one the context's module declares or imports by name ( import pkg.module { Name } ). Statement Meaning parent Name the context extends another of its module: its bindings, groups and inputs, the parent's first session(params) , request(params) , task(params) , transaction(params) the inputs of that level's scope; the head's parameters are the application level's group Name = [Target, \u2026] a named set of consumers and slots provide Key [using Source] [to Target | to [Target, \u2026]] [replaces] [{ lifetime: level }] a binding; Source is a class (the key itself when omitted), instance <expression> or factory <function> Binding forms have distinct typed meanings: using Concrete constructs through the class's generated constructor with every inject slot filled (an ordinary field needs a default, E-DI-009 ); using instance value evaluates an existing owned value in its level's constructor, where that level's inputs are in scope; using factory Factory.create calls a function whose parameters are filled like slots and which may be try and async : its effects and fallibility appear on every generated function that calls it ( try measured by SP-08; async factories pending DEL-40s). Source no lifetime: lifetime: transient lifetime: L class or factory built per use built per"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#31-elaboration","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"3.1 Elaboration","m":"spec","x":"L-40s). Source no lifetime: lifetime: transient lifetime: L class or factory built per use built per use held at L instance held at application E-DI-010 held at L"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#32-scopes","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"3.2 Scopes","m":"spec","x":"3.2 Scopes # Scopes are application , session , request , task , transaction , transient . They form a partial order by containment: application contains session and request ; request contains task and transaction ; session and request MUST NOT contain each other; transient is contained by everything. A provider at a longer-lived scope MUST NOT hold a value from a shorter-lived scope or from an incomparable one ( E-DI-005 ). A contained level is created by a method of its container ( app.request(inputs) ) and holds its own handle of each enclosing instance it uses (a retain() of a Shared , a copy of a Copyable value), so a level class stores no loan; an enclosing instance that is neither is bound as a Shared to be used below its level (the core rejects the copy otherwise, at that instance's binding). Instances a scope holds are destroyed in reverse dependency order when the scope ends; an instance a consumer holds through its own Shared handle lives as long as that handle, which its declared type says."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#33-provider-selection","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"3.3 Provider selection","m":"spec","x":"3.3 Provider selection # Selection is static and deterministic: nearest context first; within a context, exact slot, then exact consumer, then explicit group, then untargeted binding; then the parent context. Equal specificity is an error ( E-DI-002 , as is a binding declared twice); no binding is E-DI-001 . Registration, declaration, module and import order are irrelevant: the generated code and the plan are the same for every order. A child binding that overlaps a more specific parent binding MUST declare replaces ( E-DI-003 , corrected by adding it); replaces that overlaps nothing is E-DI-004 . The resolved graph, with candidates and the reason each was chosen or rejected, is part of the build manifest (3.5). Activated contexts are immutable. Changing configuration creates a new context value used by scopes created afterwards; existing instances keep their dependencies. Runtime-dependent provider choice MUST be a closed enum declared in the manifest ( select PaymentGateway by config.payment.provider in [Stripe, Adyen] ); the chosen variant is recorded on the scope instance. SP-08 did not run it (pending DEL-40s): its elaboration is a closed enum and a match in the generated factory, which raises no ownership question."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#34-construction","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"3.4 Construction","m":"spec","x":"3.4 Construction # Eager construction happens at scope creation in dependency order; an eager cycle, or a cycle through transient bindings, is a compile error ( E-DI-006 ). A construction that fails ( or fail on a try factory) drops what was already constructed, in reverse order, and reaches the caller once (Platform 2.3). Lazy edges are not in DI schema v1 ( inject lazy , E-DI-011 ). SP-08 found no representation that meets this section's lazy rule (one instance per scope, bounded cycle detection, nothing registered after a failed construction, retry as a new resolution) without sharing or locking the source does not write: a provider that borrows its scope stores a loan, which Language 1.0 never allows; one that owns what it needs constructs on every resolve; caching the first construction needs a lock-protected shared cell whose cost the slot would not show. A later version may add lazy edges with a provider type whose sharing and locking are written at the slot."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#35-provenance","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"3.5 Provenance","m":"spec","x":"3.5 Provenance # Provenance is a host capability feature, not part of the type checker. Static provenance is the plan ( fortis.di.plan/v1 , fortisc elaborate --plan ): for every context, level and slot, the consumer, the slot, the chosen binding, its package and version, the context, the scope, the specificity and the selection reason, and every rejected candidate with the reason it lost; it has no line numbers, so it is the same for every declaration order, and nothing of it is in the generated code. Live provenance is recorded only when the fortis.di.provenance package is imported and the Supervisor capability enables it (the application function then takes it as its last input): the generated code records one event per creation (of a held instance or a transient value), per injection of a held instance and per release of a scope's handle (its cleanup : a Shared instance another holder retains lives on), carrying the event kind, the binding's index in the plan's order and the level, trivial values only, so no instance's field value is ever recorded and nothing needs redaction. A record of trivial values is allocation-free natively, so a noalloc caller keeps its claim with live provenance on. Correlating the events of one scope instance (instance IDs), the disposal of transient values and of instances a failed construction drops, and the authenticated, redacted inspection below are not in DI schema v1's first measurement and are owned by DEL-40s. Inspection is authenticated, read-only and redacted, and in production is opt-in. History reads the recorded build and configuration, never the current source. Code Rule Meaning E-DI-001 PLAT-03-02 no binding provides a slot's key E-DI-002 PLAT-03-02 two bindings of equal specificity for one slot, or one binding declared twice E-DI-003 PLAT-03-02 a child binding overlaps a more specific parent binding without replaces E-DI-004 PLAT-03-02 replaces overlaps no parent binding E-DI-005 PLAT-03-04 an instance depends on one held at a level that is neither its own nor a container of it E-DI-006 PLAT-03-04 a construction cycle E-DI-008 PLAT-03-04 a lifetime: that is not a scope level, or a parent that is not a context of the module, or a parent cycle E-DI-009 PLAT-03-03 using Class of a class the module neither declares nor imports by name, a generic class, or a class with an ordinary field without a default; a factory the module neither declares nor imports E-DI-010 PLAT-03-01 a statement that is not in DI schema v1, an instance bound transient, or a name declared twice (an input, a group, a context, a generated class) E-DI-011 PLAT-03-03 inject lazy : lazy edges are not in DI schema v1 (3.4) (The numbering skips 007: the design reviewed before SP-08 ran had a loan rule there, a DI-specific borrow rule, and it was removed.) ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-03-01 DI declarations MUST elaborate to constructor parameters and scope classes before ownership checking. The core's verdict on each elaborated text equals the program's; manual construction in tests ( conformance/di , di_check.py ). new (D-01) PLAT-03-02 Provider selection MUST be deterministic and ambiguity-free. Slot/group/default/context matrix; permutation tests with byte-identical generated code and plans. DI-001 PLAT-03-03 Factories MUST satisfy type, effect and failure contracts. Construction-failure and interface tests. DI-002 PLAT-03-04 Longer-to-shorter scope edges and eager cycles MUST fail at check . Scope graph negative corpus. DI-003 PLAT-03-05 Active contexts MUST be immutable and versioned. Existing/new-instance reload tests. (pending DEL-40s: not run by SP-08) DI-004 PLAT-03-06 Live provenance MUST be opt-in, authenticated and redacted. Multi-tenant reconstruction; redaction tests. (pending DEL-40s: not run by SP-08) DI-005, DI-006 PLAT-03-07 Test overrides MUST NOT leak across parallel tests. Parallel contamination suite. (pending DEL-40s: not run by SP-08) DI-007"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#35-provenance","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"3.5 Provenance","m":"spec","x":"tamination suite. (pending DEL-40s: not run by SP-08) DI-007"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#4-http-kit","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"4. HTTP kit","m":"spec","x":"4. HTTP kit # The HTTP kit gives an application a typed HTTP server and client, generated OpenAPI documents and generated native SDKs, all derived from ordinary Fortis declarations. An application imports it to declare endpoints whose request, response and failure shapes are checked at compile time and published as a contract."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#41-endpoints-are-functions-with-aspects","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"4.1 Endpoints are functions with aspects","m":"spec","x":"4.1 Endpoints are functions with aspects # An endpoint is an ordinary exported function carrying one of the kit's aspects: @get , @post , @put , @patch , @delete (specialisations of @http for methods without a shortcut). The aspect carries the route, the authorisation requirement, the status map for the response enum, the failure destination, limits and an optional observation schema; there is no endpoint head, no DTO requirement and no attribute on enum variants. Fortis // illustrative import fortis . data { Db } import fortis . http . server { PublicApi , PublicProblem , get , post } import fortis . security { Authorisation , Identity , Role } export enum CustomerAccess implements Authorisation { Read Create export pure func grantedTo ( self , who : Identity ) Bool => match self { Read => who . hasRole ( Role . Staff ) || who . hasRole ( Role . Admin ) Create => who . hasRole ( Role . Admin ) } } export enum GetCustomerResponse { Found ( customer : Customer ) // the model encodes itself (section 4.5) NotFound Unavailable ( problem : PublicProblem ) } export enum CreateCustomerResponse { Created ( id : CustomerId ) Unavailable ( problem : PublicProblem ) } @ get ( \"/customers/{id}\" , auth : CustomerAccess . Read , responses : { Found : 200 , NotFound : 404 , Unavailable : 503 } , fallback : Unavailable , observe : CustomerOps , ) export PublicApi async try func getCustomer ( db : Db , who : Identity , id : CustomerId ) GetCustomerResponse { let customer = await Customer . find ( db , id ) or fail if customer == null { return NotFound } capture tier = customer . tier return Found ( customer : move customer ) } @ post ( \"/customers\" , auth : CustomerAccess . Create , responses : { Created : 201 , Unavailable : 503 } , fallback : Unavailable , limits : { bodyBytes : 65536 , timeout : 5s } , ) export PublicApi async try func createCustomer ( db : editable Db , who : Identity , name : owned String < scalars : 1 .. 120 > , tier : CustomerTier ? , ) CreateCustomerResponse { let created = await Customer . create ( editable db , name : move name , tier : tier ?? CustomerTier . Standard , accountId : who . accountId , ) or fail return Created ( id : created . id ) } Request binding. A handler parameter is bound from exactly one place, decided in this order: (1) server context , if its type implements the marker interface ServerContext (Language Reference 9.5: a marker is implemented only by explicit declaration, so String never qualifies). The implementations are the capability types and the per-request authorities RequestContext , Identity , Db and Tx , which the server adapter mints for each request from the process capabilities; a kit adds one with implement ServerContext for MyContext {} in the module that declares the type; a route template or request field naming such a parameter is an error at the aspect ( E-HTTP-004 ); (2) the path , if the template names it ( {id} ); (3) otherwise the query string on GET / DELETE and the request body on POST / PUT / PATCH . A body or query field with no matching parameter is rejected (422), a required parameter the request does not supply is rejected, an optional parameter ( T? ) may be absent, a parameter may be owned (the decoder produces the value), and refinements and nominal constructors run before the body executes. A handler that wants a whole structured body takes one parameter of a Decodable type instead; a model type is never accepted as a request parameter ( E-HTTP-002 ), so a request shape is always written out. What this guarantees is exactly that no request is ever assigned into an existing object or a row: the values a request may supply are the handler's parameters and nothing else. It does not decide which of those values the caller is entitled to send; whether accountId may come from the request or must come from who.accountId is the handler's authorisation decision, and writing it as a named argument makes that decision visible, not automatically correct. The kit declares th"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#41-endpoints-are-functions-with-aspects","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"4.1 Endpoints are functions with aspects","m":"spec","x":"g it as a named argument makes that decision visible, not automatically correct. The kit declares the aspects (the Language Reference, section Effects and contracts, defines the mechanism): Aspect Properties Behaviour http[R](method, path, responses, auth?, fallback?, observe?, limits, retry?, cache?, location?, lenient?) ; needs who: Identity responses: VariantMap[R, HttpStatus] or entries { status, media } ; auth is any Authorisation ; fallback is the variant constructor that receives an unhandled failure; limits , retry and cache are nested records with defaults; lenient lists parameters that ignore unknown fields before : bind and validate the request into the handler's parameters, enforce limits, check auth against who (every handler therefore takes who: Identity ; an anonymous request binds the anonymous identity). on failure : with a fallback , report once, build a PublicProblem from the receipt and return the variant; without one, re-fail get , post , put , patch , delete path positional, then every http property except method = http(method: \u2026, \u2026rest) ; the canonical spelling, which the formatter produces from @http(method: GET, \u2026) The aspects needs PublicApi , so every handler carries the PublicApi contract in its modifier position (section 12). Route table and conflicts. Routes are registered as a compile-time constant so that conflicts are compile errors: Fortis // illustrative \u2014 src/routes.ft export const routes = Routes . of ( customers . getCustomer , customers . createCustomer , invoices . getInvoice ) Two routes conflict when they have the same method and some request path matches both templates. Literal segments must be equal; a {param} segment matches any single segment that parses as the parameter's type . So that this is decidable, a path parameter's type must come from the closed path-type domain : integer types and refinements (decided by interval intersection), Bool , enums without payloads (decided by literal sets), String with or without a scalar-count refinement (overlaps any literal within the count), and nominal newtypes over those, compared by representation (so CustomerId and OrderId , both Int<1..> , overlap). Any other parameter type in a path is rejected ( E-HTTP-003 ). Hence /customers/{id} with id: CustomerId and /customers/new do not conflict, {a: Int<1..9>} and {b: Int<10..20>} do not, and {id: String} with /customers/new does ( E-HTTP-001 , naming both functions). There is no precedence rule between literal and parameter segments. Paths are normalised once, by the same rule in the checker and the server: split on / first, then percent-decode each segment, then reject a segment that is empty, . , .. , or contains a decoded / , then NFC; so /files/a%2Fb is a 400, never a one-segment or two-segment match. A template parameter with no handler parameter of that name is an error at the aspect, as is a path-bound parameter the template does not mention. Server.create(network, listen:, routes:, \u2026) takes the constant; dynamic registration ( Router.add(...) at run time) is outside PLAT-04-09 and carries the documented reduced guarantee that conflicts are a startup failure."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#42-boundary-behaviour","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"4.2 Boundary behaviour","m":"spec","x":"4.2 Boundary behaviour # External input is decoded, validated and converted to refined nominal types before the handler runs. Wire encodings for 64-bit integers, Decimal , timestamps, enums, nullability and bytes are explicit per schema; a client that cannot represent a value receives an error, never a truncated value. Body, header, decompression, depth and time quotas and streaming backpressure have safe defaults. Identity and authorisation are separate; object and tenant checks run at the action boundary. Domain outcomes map to declared variants; operational failures go to one reporter/response adapter. Public problems follow RFC 9457 with safe codes and correlation IDs. Pagination uses one encoding: an opaque cursor ( Cursor nominal type, base64url of a signed, versioned token). A timeout after a mutating request is an unknown remote outcome; retry requires an idempotency key."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#43-closed-enums-on-the-wire","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"4.3 Closed enums on the wire","m":"spec","x":"4.3 Closed enums on the wire # A closed source enum cannot hold a value a newer server adds. For every enum that crosses the wire, the codec generator emits a transport enum with an explicit unknown case and a fallible conversion to the domain enum: Fortis // illustrative, generated export enum CustomerTierWire { Standard Business Enterprise Unknown ( raw : String < scalars : 0 .. 64 > ) export try func toDomain ( owned self ) CustomerTier { return match move self { Standard => CustomerTier . Standard Business => CustomerTier . Business Enterprise => CustomerTier . Enterprise Unknown ( raw : value ) => fail UnknownWireValue ( field : \"CustomerTier\" , raw : move value ) } } } Compatibility is directional. The generator classifies every change as safe-for-requests, safe-for-responses, safe-for-stored-values, or breaking, and the release verifier blocks a breaking change without a version bump."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#44-schema-and-sdks","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"4.4 Schema and SDKs","m":"spec","x":"4.4 Schema and SDKs # OpenAPI is generated from the source contract. The pinned version is OpenAPI 3.1.1 ( https://spec.openapis.org/oas/v3.1.1.html ). Constructs that cannot be expressed produce a diagnostic, never an omission. Swift, Kotlin and TypeScript SDKs preserve nominal wrappers, optionality, response cases, validators, auth hooks, pagination, cancellation and idempotent retry. Each SDK package ships contract fixtures and compatibility metadata. The oldest supported SDK version is a test target of every release. ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-04-01 Endpoints MUST be checked against handler signatures and the responses map of their aspect. Route/schema mismatch fixtures. API-001 PLAT-04-02 Framework-generated responses MUST appear in the resolved OpenAPI document. Actual-versus-OpenAPI contract tests. API-002 PLAT-04-03 Wire enums MUST have a generated unknown case and fallible domain conversion. Old-client/new-server roundtrip. new (T-08) PLAT-04-04 Generated SDKs MUST preserve wire semantics and precision. Cross-language roundtrip tests. API-003 PLAT-04-05 API changes MUST undergo directional compatibility analysis. Breaking/nonbreaking corpus. API-004 PLAT-04-06 Boundaries MUST validate, authorise and report under explicit contracts. Tenant/object/error integration tests. API-005 PLAT-04-07 The OpenAPI version MUST be pinned to 3.1.1 by versioned URL. Generator golden tests. new (P-01)"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#45-serialisation","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"4.5 Serialisation","m":"spec","x":"4.5 Serialisation # Request and response bodies use the structural codec of the Language Reference (section Types, built-in interfaces). A response enum is an envelope , not a body: it is never encoded, and it does not implement Encodable . What must be encodable, recursively, is every payload field of every variant that produces a body, and every parameter bound from a request must be Decodable ; a type failing that is rejected at the aspect, not at run time. Secret[T] implements neither, so it can appear in no body. The wire rules are fixed by the kit, not chosen per type, so two services never disagree: Fortis JSON nominal type newtype its representation enum without payloads the variant name as a string enum with payloads { \"kind\": \"Issued\", \"on\": \"\u2026\" } T? the value or null Decimal , Timestamp strings ( \"19.99\" , RFC 3339 UTC) Int64 / UInt64 a number within the IEEE-safe range, otherwise a string; the schema states which, so no client truncates silently Bytes base64url A response body is derived from the responses: map: a variant with one payload field encodes that value as the body; with several, an object keyed by field name; with none, an empty body. PublicProblem encodes as RFC 9457 with application/problem+json . Media type is JSON unless the map says otherwise ( Found: { status: 200, media: \"text/csv\" } ); every media encoder consumes the Encodable tree, so the payload type must still implement Encodable , and the encoder only decides the bytes. Headers are never part of the response enum: standard ones come from aspect properties ( cache: { maxAge: 60s } , location: \"/customers/{id}\" ), and a payload field of type ResponseHeaders is removed before the body is derived (so one remaining field is still encoded as a bare value); ResponseHeaders holds only HeaderValue fields (a bounded ASCII String refinement), so the same eligibility walk covers headers and a Secret[T] cannot be placed in one. Unknown fields on input are rejected by default; the http property lenient: [name] lists the parameters that accept and ignore them. Hand-written encode / decode implementations must also implement Schema ( func schema() CodecSchema ), which is what OpenAPI generation reads. Request enums decode through their generated transport type (4.3), so a value from a newer client is a 422 problem, not a crash. Projections from a row to a response use derive From[Model] and to() ; the model itself may be returned directly when the row is the wire shape. ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-04-08 Request binding MUST follow the one-place rule of 4.1: a request can supply only the handler's path-, query- and body-bound parameters, never a server-context parameter and never a field of an existing object or row. A body- or query-bound parameter whose name equals the key, a timestamp or a belongsTo foreign key of a model the handler writes MUST raise W-HTTP-002 , since that is where entitlement decisions are usually missed. Binding corpus; mass-assignment negative tests; lint fixtures. new PLAT-04-09 Route conflicts MUST be detected at check over the constant route table using parameter types. Conflict corpus. new PLAT-04-10 Wire encodings MUST follow the table in 4.5; every body payload type, header field and media-encoder input MUST be Encodable and every request-bound parameter Decodable , checked recursively at the aspect; the response enum itself is an envelope and is never encoded. Codec golden tests; eligibility negatives. new"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#5-data-kit","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"5. Data kit","m":"spec","x":"5. Data kit # The data kit gives an application typed access to PostgreSQL and SQLite, migrations, transactions, background jobs, schedules, caches and mail. An application imports it to declare models whose storage, queries and migrations are checked offline against a schema manifest, with every query an explicit, visible effect."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#51-models-and-queries","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"5.1 Models and queries","m":"spec","x":"5.1 Models and queries # A model head declares a table-backed class. It elaborates to an ordinary exported class with the declared fields exported, explicit generated implementations of Equatable and Codec that cover the columns only (not ordinary derive , which would require every field; the generated decoder initialises relations as unloaded and the dirty-set as empty), a typed query builder CustomerQuery , finders, relations, scopes and the migration input; fortis explain Customer prints the generated class. The body admits the closed vocabulary below plus field lines and ordinary methods ( func ); a model cannot have private fields (the database can see every column), a cleanup , or be trivial . The generated layout is: the columns as exported fields; one exported Relation[T] field per relation ( customer.invoices ), where Relation[T] implements neither codec nor Equatable , which is why the model's implementations are generated column-only rather than derived; the associated constants columns and relations holding the typed descriptors for filter , orderBy and including ; and a private dirty-set. Returning a model directly from an endpoint therefore exposes exactly its columns, and adding a column is a wire change wherever that happens, which the semantic diff reports. Fortis // illustrative export model Customer { table : \"customers\" timestamps // createdAt / updatedAt, maintained by save softDelete // deletedAt; queries exclude deleted rows by default key id : CustomerId // primary key; integer newtypes are generated by the database name : String < scalars : 1 .. 120 > tier : CustomerTier = CustomerTier . Standard accountId : AccountId belongsTo account : Account via accountId hasMany invoices : Invoice via customerId scope business = where ( tier : CustomerTier . Business ) } The head's vocabulary is closed: table , key , timestamps , softDelete , belongsTo , hasMany , via , scope . key marks the primary key (exactly one); a key whose type is an integer newtype is generated by the database and is not an argument of create , any other key is. timestamps adds createdAt: Timestamp and updatedAt: Timestamp , set by create and save from the connection's clock and not arguments of either; softDelete adds deletedAt: Timestamp? , set by delete and likewise never an argument. The generated class also carries two associated constants, columns (one typed descriptor per column: Customer.columns.createdAt.after(x) ) and relations (one descriptor per relation: Customer.relations.invoices ), so descriptors never collide with field names. Column names map from camelCase to snake_case. The generated API: query-building members are pure ; every member that executes is generic over its connection, func find[C](conn: C, \u2026) where C implements Connection for reads and func create[C](conn: editable C, \u2026) where C implements Connection for create , save and delete , where Db and Tx both implement Connection and the parameter is taken by loan, so the linear Tx may instantiate C (Language Reference 6.12) and stays with the caller for commit ; the type argument is inferred, so a read inside a transaction is Customer.find(tx, id) and a write is Customer.create(editable tx, \u2026) , with tx still available for commit afterwards. The table writes db for brevity; tx is accepted in every position: Member Meaning Customer.find(db, id) \u2192 Customer? by primary key Customer.where(field: value, \u2026) \u2192 CustomerQuery equality predicates by named field; Customer.columns.createdAt.after(x) and the other typed descriptors for ranges, membership and null tests via .filter(\u2026) ; never operators, never strings .orderBy , .limit , .offset , .including(Customer.relations.invoices) chainable; including eager-loads a relation with one extra query .all(db) , .first(db) , .count(db) , .paginate(db, cursor:) execution; each is async try and returns owned values Customer.create(editable db, name: \u2026, \u2026) \u2192 Customer insert; takes every column except a generated key and the server-ma"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#51-models-and-queries","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"5.1 Models and queries","m":"spec","x":"table db, name: \u2026, \u2026) \u2192 Customer insert; takes every column except a generated key and the server-maintained columns ( createdAt , updatedAt , deletedAt ) as named arguments, and nothing else customer.save(editable db) writes the changed columns (dirty tracking is generated); nothing is written without this call move customer.delete(editable db) soft-deletes when softDelete is declared Customer.business() a declared scope, chainable Customer.withDeleted() , Customer.unscoped() explicit opt-outs, chainable customer.invoices a Relation[Invoice] : loaded() gives List[Invoice]? ( null unless the query used including ); await customer.invoices.load(db) returns List[Invoice] without caching it, so it takes a read loan Property access never runs SQL, there is no global connection and no lazy loading: an N+1 is a load written inside a loop, which the kit reports statically ( W-DATA-001 ) and the test budget enforces. Rows are validated on the way in with the same refinement and nominal checks as HTTP input, so schema drift surfaces at the query. There is no $fillable / $guarded : create and save take declared fields only, and untrusted input reaches them as named arguments the handler writes (4.1). Raw SQL exists only as Sql.raw(cap: RawSql, text, params) , an io + fail operation with no static checking, excluded by the PublicApi and BusinessOperation contracts."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#52-type-differences","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"5.2 Type differences","m":"spec","x":"5.2 Type differences # Fortis type PostgreSQL SQLite Notes Int , IntN bigint / integer / smallint INTEGER SQLite stores up to 64-bit; UInt64 above Int64.max is rejected for SQLite. Float double precision REAL NaN rejected on write for both. Decimal numeric(38, s) TEXT (canonical string) SQLite has no decimal; comparisons in SQL are rejected, only equality. Bool boolean INTEGER 0/1 String text TEXT UTF-8 enforced on read. Bytes bytea BLOB Timestamp timestamptz TEXT RFC 3339 UTC Ordering by text is correct only in canonical form; the codec guarantees it. Duration interval (restricted) INTEGER nanoseconds nominal type underlying representation underlying representation Nominal identity restored by the codec. closed enum (no payload) text with check constraint TEXT with check Unknown stored values surface as Unknown via the transport enum. T? nullable column nullable column Isolation: PostgreSQL defaults to read committed , SQLite to serializable (its only level). Locking differences ( SELECT ... FOR UPDATE absent on SQLite) are documented per API; an API unavailable on one backend is a compile error for that target, not a runtime no-op."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#53-migrations","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"5.3 Migrations","m":"spec","x":"5.3 Migrations # Published migrations are ordered, checksummed and immutable. The diff tool classifies data loss, lock and backfill implications and old/new binary compatibility. Applying requires a Process capability and a migration lock. Expand/migrate/contract is the supported rolling pattern. Backfills are resumable, idempotent, bounded and observed. Rollback never promises to restore deleted data. Production startup checks compatibility and never applies destructive migrations silently."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#54-transactions","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"5.4 Transactions","m":"spec","x":"5.4 Transactions # A transaction is a linear handle: the generated Tx holds a private field of the core witness type Must (Language Reference 6.12), so it is linear by the ordinary rule, and it implements Abandonable (Language Reference 12.4), so it may be live across the await of the queries it carries and across an or fail : on cancellation or failure the unwind calls abandon , which marks the transaction abandoned and the pool rolls it back when the connection is returned, so await Customer.find(tx, id) or fail inside a transaction is the ordinary spelling and a failure always rolls back. db.transaction() returns it and it MUST be consumed by move tx.commit() (fallible) or move tx.rollback() , which discharge the witness. Falling off a block with a live Tx is E-OBL-001 , so a forgotten commit is a compile error rather than an implicit rollback. Nested transactions use savepoints explicitly. Rollback failure preserves the primary failure and is reported with report from the cleanup path; the supervisor labels it as raised during cleanup. Isolation level is a parameter. External effects (payments, mail) inside a database-only transaction are excluded by the DatabaseOnly contract ( excludes capability Network , excludes capability Mail ); outbox and saga helpers are the supported pattern."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#55-jobs-schedules-cache-mail","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"5.5 Jobs, schedules, cache, mail","m":"spec","x":"5.5 Jobs, schedules, cache, mail # A job is a function carrying the @job(...) aspect, whose properties name the payload parameters, deadline, retry/backoff, idempotency key, acknowledgement boundary, attempt limit and dead-letter destination; @schedule(...) adds the timing rules. Delivery is at-least-once; no adapter is labelled exactly-once. Queues: in-process, PostgreSQL durable, Redis adapter. Schedules pin time-zone data and declare DST, missed-run, overlap and lease rules. Detached work receives a task context, never an HTTP request. Caches have typed keys, tenant scope, TTL/invalidation, and explicit effects; a hit never bypasses authorisation. Feature flags are typed contextual state. Mail has typed templates, test sinks, rate limits and failure/idempotency policy. ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-05-01 Field access and rendering MUST NOT execute queries. Query-count tests. DAT-001 PLAT-05-02 The typed builder MUST be the only statically checked query path; raw SQL MUST require the RawSql capability so contracts can exclude it. Injection/schema/cancellation tests. DAT-002 PLAT-05-03 The PostgreSQL/SQLite type table MUST be enforced by codecs and target checks. Per-type roundtrip on both backends. new (P-02) PLAT-05-04 Migrations MUST be checksummed, reviewed and restart/concurrency safe. Upgrade/crash/destructive-diff corpus. DAT-003 PLAT-05-05 Transactions MUST be linear handles; SQL rollback and external effects MUST remain distinct. Forgotten-commit compile-fail; outbox/ack-loss tests. DAT-004 PLAT-05-06 Jobs and schedules MUST define retries, delivery, idempotency and lifecycle. Duplicate/lease/DST/dead-letter suite. JOB-001 PLAT-05-07 Cache and flag state MUST be typed and tenant-scoped. Cross-tenant/staleness tests. CAC-001"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#6-telemetry-kit","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"6. Telemetry kit","m":"spec","x":"6. Telemetry kit # The telemetry kit carries the typed observations that source code captures through validation, archive, projection, aggregation and export. An application imports it to get OpenTelemetry-compatible signals, a complete archive of permitted detail, operator-defined projections and cost attribution, all driven by schemas declared in source and policy declared in YAML."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#61-division-of-responsibility","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"6.1 Division of responsibility","m":"spec","x":"6.1 Division of responsibility # The Language Reference, section Observation statements, defines the whole language-level rule: capture is a pure, infallible snapshot into a bounded per-activation frame, checked by definite assignment over every exit including failure and await ; privacy transforms run before any sink. Everything in this section happens after the frame is complete and is a library and pipeline concern. An observation schema is versioned by a version: n field in its body; a schema without one is version 1. A field change that is not backward compatible (a removed field, a changed type, a tightened bound) requires a new version, and stored records carry the version they were captured under. Projections and exporters name a schema as Name (latest) or Name.vN (pinned). The @ symbol applies aspects only and is not used for versions. Two checks are distinct. Type checking of source against its declared contracts (required fields, outcome classes, noalloc claims) is configuration-independent: policy that affects it is written in source as a contract applied to the function. Plan compatibility checking happens at build time: the target plan and the telemetry plan (destinations, sampling, retention, cardinality limits, provider implementations selected in YAML) are checked against the declared claims, and an incompatible plan fails the build with a diagnostic at the plan or policy site. A plan can fail a build; it can never change whether source type-checks. A YAML file MUST NOT reference object fields, choose capture timing, or add a required field. Privacy is declared in the telemetry plan, as privacy: { permitted_fields: all | none, transforms: { <Schema> | <Schema.vN>: { <field>: redact | hash | drop } } } . A transform under Schema applies to every version of the schema; one under Schema.vN applies to that version alone, ahead of the schema's entry. The runtime's telemetry adapter applies the policy inside the producing process, before any byte of a captured value leaves it: redact writes [redacted] ; hash writes h1:<key-id>: and the first 16 bytes of HMAC-SHA256 under the deployment key, over the field's declared type and its value; drop omits the field; a field the policy does not name is dropped. A transformed field's truncation to its bound is never told. The adapter writes the canonical permitted record to the producer's channel. It carries the producer's incarnation (128 random bits for each process start) and its sequence, and the schema version the record was captured under. Without a policy, the task reporter withholds every captured value (Language Reference 15). The toolchain's development plan permit-all permits every field unchanged on the record stream. What each transform reveals is part of the contract: drop : nothing; redact : that the field was captured; hash : equality of values of one declared type under one deployment key (so distinct counts and the order of first occurrence), never the value. A hashed field is never a metric label. Fortis // illustrative observation OrderProcessing { version : 1 // optional; 1 when omitted customerTier : CustomerTier required : all region : Region required : all provider : PaymentProvider required : failure transactionId : TransactionId ? required : success encoding : bounded ( 512 ) } @ observe ( OrderProcessing ) async try func processOrder ( order : Order ) Receipt { capture customerTier = order . customer . tier capture region = order . customer . address . region capture provider = order . payment . provider let receipt = await Payments . charge ( order ) or fail capture transactionId = receipt . transactionId return receipt }"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#62-completeness-per-stage","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"6.2 Completeness per stage","m":"spec","x":"6.2 Completeness per stage # Completeness is defined per stage, because each stage admits different loss: Stage Guarantee Admitted loss Capture Every applicable required field assigned exactly once (static). None. Frame completion Duration and outcome supplied by the runtime; cannot be forged. None. Aggregation (standard metrics) Every completed frame counted. None within process memory bounds; overflow is counted. Persistence (archive/spool) Per delivery class (6.5). Declared drop rule. Export Per delivery class and exporter. Declared; loss counters emitted. \"Standard metrics remain complete\" means complete at the aggregation stage. Sampling applies to detailed records on a separate branch; sampled counts are labelled as estimates."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#63-pipeline","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"6.3 Pipeline","m":"spec","x":"6.3 Pipeline # capture \u2192 validate/redact (in the producing process: the adapter, 6.1) \u2192 canonical permitted record \u2192 channel \u2192 validation and the ledger \u2192 optional archive/spool and projections \u2192 aggregation \u2192 exporters. Archive-before-filter preserves information; it does not make every request write to object storage synchronously. Durable acknowledgement is a separate visible contract. The archive holds every valid record of every registered schema, whether or not a projection selects it. A record is valid when its schema version is registered, every field it carries is declared there, and every value has its post-transform type (the declared type in full, with its integer range and scalar bound, for a permitted field). A record that is not valid goes to the ledger as its identity and a code, never its content. A record with a key written twice at any depth is not valid. The archive keeps the validated record's canonical serialization, never the delivered bytes. Each consumer keeps the exact set of identities it has applied and commits it atomically with its own state. The consumers are the archive, each projection epoch and the ledger. A delivery is acknowledged only when every consumer has committed it, so after any crash, redelivery is applied by exactly the consumers that lack it. Every directory a consumer writes in, and every ancestor, is durable before anything is written in it. On recovery, every state file the pipeline reads is made durable before anything is acknowledged. At every start, each epoch applies the archived records it lacks, in archive order. Projections do not depend on the archive. With the archive disabled, full or failing, aggregation continues. A full archive either blocks acknowledgement until there is room, or drops; a drop is final for that identity and is counted."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#64-projections","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"6.4 Projections","m":"spec","x":"6.4 Projections # YAML schema: fortis.project/v1 telemetry: privacy: permitted_fields: all transforms: OrderProcessing: customerEmail: hash # joinable within a deployment, never readable archive: enabled: true permitted_fields: all spool: filesystem format: parquet destination: archive.primary projections: premium_orders: source: OrderProcessing.v1 # pinned version; `OrderProcessing` alone means latest bind_at: lifecycle_start where: all: - field: region in: [UK, IE] - field: customerTier in: [Business, Enterprise] dimensions: [region, customerTier, provider, outcome] metrics: - name: business.premium_order.duration instrument: histogram value: duration unit: s cardinality: limit: 1000 overflow: aggregate export: operational.otlp Predicates: typed equality, membership, exclusion, numeric ranges, presence, all / any / not , bounded regex on text fields declared regex-safe, bucketing, explicit sampling. No coercion, no code. A misspelled enum value fails validation against the source schema. Predicates and dimensions are checked against each field's post-transform type : Policy action In the permitted record Allowed in a plan permit the declared type everything the type allows hash a hash present ; a lifecycle key redact [redacted] present drop absent nothing A comparison on an absent field is false; present tests presence. Stateful correctness: an up-down projection MUST declare bind_at: lifecycle_start . Its membership and dimension keys are evaluated on the lifecycle's start event only, so no field of the end event is ever consulted, and nothing can change between increment and decrement. A lifecycle is one metric's, identified by the producer incarnation and the key field, which must be permitted or hashed. These are counted as anomalies, and a series' balance is never negative: an end that arrives before its start; a duplicate start or end; a key reused; a missing key. Every admitted record takes its tuple's series, in the order the epoch applies records, up to cardinality.limit ; past the limit, records go to the overflow series. An epoch applies records in archive order wherever the archive holds them, so a live epoch allocates as a replay of the archive would. Dropping a dimension reaggregates. Gauge merging needs an explicit rule. Plans are versioned. A projection epoch is identified by the projection's definition without export . A change to export alone is compatible; any other change is a new epoch, which needs a metric name no other epoch has used. A new or reactivated epoch catches up from the archive. Its coverage is: complete when every record ever accepted reached it; archive_partial after archive drops; since_activation with no archive to catch up from. Privacy actions are fixed per schema version for the life of an archive, so a changed policy needs a new schema version. A rejected plan changes nothing. Backfills use isolated destinations or the live destination's deduplicated epochs. Code Rule Meaning E-TEL-001 PLAT-06-04 a predicate value not of the field's type (not a variant of its enum, a non-number for range ), a range or histogram value on a field that is not numeric, or an unknown unit E-TEL-002 PLAT-06-04 a schema, version or field the registry does not declare (for an unpinned source, in its latest version) E-TEL-003 PLAT-06-05 an up-down projection without bind_at: lifecycle_start , or a lifecycle without its key, start or end E-TEL-004 PLAT-06-01 a plan outside its own schema (an unknown key, a wrong shape, an option the instrument does not take, an unsupported YAML escape); a transform naming a field or version not declared, or an unknown action; a key that adds a required field, chooses capture timing or names an object field E-TEL-005 PLAT-06-04 a use of a transformed field that its post-transform type does not allow, including a redacted or dropped lifecycle key E-TEL-006 PLAT-06-06 an incompatible projection change whose epoch reuses another epoch's metric name E-TEL-007 PLAT-06-08 a privacy a"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#64-projections","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"6.4 Projections","m":"spec","x":"le projection change whose epoch reuses another epoch's metric name E-TEL-007 PLAT-06-08 a privacy action changed for a schema version the state has already taken records under"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#65-delivery-classes-and-archive","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"6.5 Delivery classes and archive","m":"spec","x":"6.5 Delivery classes and archive # Class Meaning Admitted loss best_effort bounded memory queue drop-oldest/newest, sampling or bounded block, declared spooled local durability point with retry loss before spool fsync; loss on spool-media failure durable explicit application operation acknowledging a replicated log, object store or outbox loss only beyond the acknowledged boundary Required sinks: S3-compatible object storage and filesystem. Parquet is the analytic format, JSON Lines for inspection, a versioned binary spool for buffering. Partitioning uses bounded time/service/schema keys. Writes are checksummed with atomic manifest publication; restarts are idempotent. Record identity is the producer incarnation plus its sequence, both fixed by the adapter at capture; redelivery resends the same bytes, and at-least-once delivery is deduplicated by each consumer's applied set (6.3). Replay checks every closed segment's digest and that the archive's records were transformed under the plan's privacy actions, and refuses on a mismatch, naming it, before anything is applied. An exporter outage cannot block application logic. Loss counters use an emergency path that cannot recurse."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#66-opentelemetry-conventions","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"6.6 OpenTelemetry conventions","m":"spec","x":"6.6 OpenTelemetry conventions # The distribution pins the OpenTelemetry API, OTLP protocol and semantic-convention versions in the compatibility set. HTTP duration is in seconds with route templates. Instrument kinds, aggregation and temporality are separate settings. Views select attributes and aggregations with defined overflow. Logging is structured and typed; free text is a debug facility. Trace context crosses supported boundaries without credentials; sensitive baggage is off by default. Sampling a trace does not drop required audit or metric accounting."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#67-finops","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"6.7 FinOps","m":"spec","x":"6.7 FinOps # A first-party analytics adapter joins a FOCUS-compatible cost dataset with telemetry and explicit shared-resource allocation models. Outputs state methodology, billing period, cost type, coverage, confidence and unallocated remainder. Sampled traces are not per-request cost. Reprojection records its model version. High-cardinality attribution is archived, not exported as metric labels. ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-06-01 Type checking MUST be configuration-independent; a telemetry or target plan MAY fail plan compatibility checking at the plan site but MUST NOT change the accept/reject result of type checking. Same source type-checked under every plan; incompatible-plan diagnostics at the policy site. OBS-003, CFG-003 (part) PLAT-06-02 Completeness MUST be defined and tested per stage. Stage-loss matrix tests ( conformance/telemetry , telemetry_check.py : S1, A1). TEL-001 (part) PLAT-06-03 Full permitted detail MUST be preservable before projection filters. Archive/metric branch comparison (A1). TEL-001 PLAT-06-04 Predicates MUST validate against source schema types. Wrong-enum/unit/presence tests; the plan corpus at its lines (L1). TEL-002 PLAT-06-05 Up-down projections MUST bind membership at lifecycle start or be rejected. Tier-change lifecycle tests at every prefix of several delivery orders (U1). TEL-003 PLAT-06-06 Reload and backfill MUST be versioned and prevent double accounting. Restart/replay/dedup tests: crashes at every disk operation, SIGKILL, reordered and repeated delivery, epochs (D1-D4, E1). TEL-004 PLAT-06-07 Export MUST have bounded backpressure and declared durability. Network/disk-full/partial-upload tests. (pending DEL-55s: the archive's full modes only were run by SP-09) TEL-005 PLAT-06-08 Privacy MUST run before every sink and spool. Secret/PII egress tests: every byte written, on every backend, scanned for canaries; paired inputs (P1-P4). TEL-006 PLAT-06-09 Standard telemetry MUST pass the pinned OpenTelemetry conventions. Golden OTLP tests. (pending DEL-55s) OBS-004 PLAT-06-10 Cost outputs MUST disclose data, methodology and unexplained remainder. Synthetic billing reconciliation. (pending DEL-55s) FIN-001 PLAT-06-11 Operators MUST preview and reload compatible plans without changing capture source. Hot-plan and reprojection tests (compatible reload and catch-up run by SP-09; preview pending DEL-55s). FIN-002 PLAT-06-12 report , audit events, span status and metric increments MUST stay distinct effects; only report consumes a failure. Valid multi-signal and duplicate-report cases. OBS-005"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#7-web-ui-kit-a-library","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"7. Web UI kit (a library)","m":"spec","x":"7. Web UI kit (a library) # The web UI kit is a set of optional packages for server-side rendering, server-managed components, single-page navigation and progressive web apps. An application imports exactly the packages it wants ( fortis.web.ssr alone gives templates; adding fortis.web.components gives reactive components; fortis.web.spa and fortis.web.pwa add client navigation and offline support). Nothing in the language core, the runtime host or the HTTP kit depends on these packages; a Fortis API server never links them. The kit is \"batteries included\" in the sense that it ships in the distribution and works with the rest of the platform out of the box, and \"a library\" in the sense that it is ordinary importable code with its own version. It also has its own release train. The web platform changes every few weeks and the language far less often, so the fortis.web.* packages, fortis.web.platform included (7.8), are released separately from the compiler and the core library. Each release states the Language versions it supports and is tested against each of them, and a release of the web packages never waits for, or forces, a compiler release. The distribution carries the latest compatible release, so scaffolds work out of the box, and a project pins whichever compatible release it wants in its lockfile."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#71-packages","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"7.1 Packages","m":"spec","x":"7.1 Packages # Package Provides Requires fortis.web.ssr templates with contextual escaping, the standalone view head, SafeHtml http fortis.web.components the component head (with state , resource , action body words); server protocol ssr, di fortis.web.forms accessible form widgets, validation binding components fortis.web.spa client navigation, history, prefetch, asset revisions; the client half compiles to WASM under the browser feature set components fortis.web.pwa manifest, service worker, cache policy, local outbox spa"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#72-execution-boundaries","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"7.2 Execution boundaries","m":"spec","x":"7.2 Execution boundaries # server component , client component and hybrid component state where a component runs. Server components reach browser APIs only through typed bridges. Client components cannot resolve secret or database providers; the DI elaboration rejects it. Server-only packages never enter a browser bundle. Browser code is untrusted by the server."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#73-component-model","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"7.3 Component model","m":"spec","x":"7.3 Component model # A component declares typed inputs, an explicit state struct, actions, resources and a view. Rendering is a pure function of the state struct: version 1 rerenders the whole component from its state after each action; there is no derived dependency tracking. Actions have typed inputs and returns, normal effects and failures, a validated request context and their own authorisation check. Fortis // illustrative import fortis . web . components { SafeHtml } server component Counter { state { count : Int < 0 .. 1000000 > } try action increment ( editable self ) Void { let next = self . state . count + 1 or fail self . state . count = Int < 0 .. 1000000 > . validate ( next ) or fail } view { <button on:click=${increment}>Count: ${self.state.count}</button> } } A view body is a raw template region (Language Reference 16): the core lexer does not tokenise it, so template syntax is defined here and nowhere else. Templates support contextual escaping, typed attributes, conditionals, exhaustive matches, keyed loops and child components. Raw HTML requires SafeHtml . Event handlers name actions or explicit client handlers. Resources have a closed state model ( loading , ready , failed , optional idle / stale ). Superseded requests are cancelled or ignored by generation token. Every state is rendered or delegated to a declared boundary."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#74-server-protocol","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"7.4 Server protocol","m":"spec","x":"7.4 Server protocol # Initial HTML is server rendered. Action calls carry a signed, versioned component reference, the action ID, validated input and a concurrency revision. All client state is untrusted; signatures do not replace authorisation, validation or fresh reads. Private fields and injected services are never serialised. Revision and idempotency controls reject stale and duplicate actions. Multi-instance deployment requires compatible keys and stores. DOM patching preserves focus, selection, composition and keyed identity. Hot reload pins compatible generations or resynchronises without discarding input."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#75-spa-and-pwa","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"7.5 SPA and PWA","m":"spec","x":"7.5 SPA and PWA # fortis.web.spa handles back/forward, scroll, redirects, downloads, prefetch limits and asset revisions. fortis.web.pwa generates the manifest, icons and service worker with explicit cache and update policy; capabilities are feature-detected. Service workers intercept requests and serve caches; they never execute server actions locally. Offline data has identity, TTL, size, privacy and logout/tenant invalidation. Mutations use a typed local outbox with stable idempotency keys and pending/failure/conflict UI. Conflict resolution is a user-supplied function: Fortis // illustrative try func resolveDraft ( local : Draft , remote : Draft ) Resolved There is no built-in last-write-wins. Reconnect revalidates authentication, permissions and schemas. Local migrations are versioned."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#76-parity-and-budgets","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"7.6 Parity and budgets","m":"spec","x":"7.6 Parity and budgets # VM/native parity for this kit covers pure functions and server components. Client (WASM) bundles have measured startup, size and memory budgets separate from server performance. Browser behaviour is tested with real browsers (section 11.5) and is outside VM parity."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#77-accessibility-defaults","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"7.7 Accessibility defaults","m":"spec","x":"7.7 Accessibility defaults # Official widgets provide semantic labels, keyboard and focus behaviour, error association, localisation and RTL, time and currency formatting and reduced motion. Scaffolds target WCAG 2.2 AA; reports separate automated evidence from manual requirements. Uploads have quotas, safe paths and scanning hooks. Server validation is authoritative."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#78-typed-client-applications","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"7.8 Typed client applications","m":"spec","x":"7.8 Typed client applications # A single-page or progressive web app is checked as thoroughly as a server, so that a client is correct by construction rather than by testing alone: The client model. A client component's messages are an enum. Its update is a function from its state and a message to a new state and the commands to run, and its view is a pure function of its state (the Elm architecture). Commands carry declared capabilities and effects; an update performs none itself. So every state transition is a pure function that can be property-tested, specified (Programme PROG-PRD-19) and replayed from a message log. Browser APIs. Fetch, storage, history, notifications and the rest are bound from the web platform's WebIDL. Each operation carries its capability, effects and failures, and other JavaScript is reachable only through a declared binding. Templates. Views are checked against a typed model of HTML and ARIA: what each element may contain, attribute names and value types, event handler signatures, required accessible names and roles. Contextual escaping is proved for every interpolation. Routes, styles and messages. Routes have typed parameters, and a link to no route is a compile error. Class names and design tokens are typed identifiers, and message catalogs are typed: a missing translation, placeholder or plural form fails the build. One validation. A form field's validation is the refined type of the server's input, run on the client for feedback and again on the server for authority ( PLAT-07-05 ). With an api contract (Programme PROG-PRD-15), a client that disagrees with its server does not compile. Beyond types. Invariants and specifications over updates, model-based exploration of message sequences in the VM and in real browsers, and convergence of the offline outbox under reordering, duplication and conflict. Keeping the definitions current. The HTML, ARIA and WebIDL definitions are generated, not written. They live in fortis.web.platform , a package of the Web UI kit that ships in the distribution with its own version. The core library never depends on it ( PLAT-07-01 ), and a new web platform release needs no compiler release. Sources. Each release is generated from pinned snapshots of machine-readable sources: the curated extracts of every web specification (WebIDL, elements, events and CSS, as w3c/webref publishes them); the WAI-ARIA roles and properties; the HTML content models, as the Nu HTML Checker's schemas encode them; browser support (MDN's browser-compat-data and the web-features Baseline data). A small reviewed overlay adds what no source states in machine-readable form: escaping contexts, accessible-name requirements, and event target types. Cadence and compatibility. A scheduled job regenerates the package from new snapshots and runs the whole Web UI corpus and the ecosystem regression run (Programme PROG-PRD-25). It proposes the release with its fortis diff , which classes each change by compatibility: an added element or attribute is compatible; an obsoleted one becomes a deprecation warning that carries the specification's status, and is removed only after the deprecation window. A change an agent proposes is approved by a person (Programme PROG-AGT-04). Baselines. A project declares the browsers it supports, as a Baseline year or an explicit list. Using a feature outside that baseline is an error unless the code detects it first with a typed supported(...) check, so a feature that is not yet everywhere is handled by construction. An independent oracle. The generated types are checked against their sources (every element, attribute and WebIDL member has a type, and every type has a source). Every page of the template corpus, rendered, is validated by the Nu HTML Checker, a validator Fortis did not write. ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-07-01 Web UI packages MUST be optional imports; no core, host or HTTP package MAY depend on them. Dependency graph lint; API-only binary inspe"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#78-typed-client-applications","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"7.8 Typed client applications","m":"spec","x":"orts; no core, host or HTTP package MAY depend on them. Dependency graph lint; API-only binary inspection. new (P-02, user direction) PLAT-07-02 Client/server/hybrid boundaries MUST be type and capability checked. Server-secret/import rejection tests. WEB-001 PLAT-07-03 Server components MUST rerender from an explicit state struct; no derived tracking in v1. Render golden tests. new (P-02) PLAT-07-04 Resource races and stale responses MUST have defined handling. Reordered/cancelled response tests. WEB-002 PLAT-07-05 Every action MUST validate and authorise independently of client state. Tamper/replay/object-ID tests. WEB-003 PLAT-07-06 SPA navigation MUST preserve history, focus and keyed state. Cross-browser journeys. WEB-004 PLAT-07-07 Offline sync MUST declare outbox, identity and a user conflict function. Offline/logout/schema/conflict tests. WEB-005 PLAT-07-08 UI defaults MUST support accessibility and localisation and disclose manual gaps. Automated/manual evidence. WEB-006 PLAT-07-09 Browser modules MUST exclude server dependencies; parity is limited to pure functions and server components. WASM/native/VM subset parity. WEB-007 PLAT-07-21 The fortis.web.* packages MUST be released on their own release train, separately from the compiler and the core library: each release states the Language versions it supports and passes the kit's suite against each, and the distribution ships the latest compatible release. Release records with the supported-version matrix and its suite results; a web release made with no compiler release. new (adoption) PLAT-07-22 The supported matrix MUST include the browser target for the browser feature set ( wasm32-unknown-unknown with its generated JavaScript glue), beside the WASI target, with async running single-threaded on the browser's event loop; every client bundle of the kit's suite builds, starts and passes in three browsers on it. Matrix row; the suite's client bundles in three browsers. new (adoption) PLAT-07-10 Client components MUST follow the typed message architecture of 7.8: messages are an enum; update maps state and message to state and commands; the view is a pure function of the state; commands carry their capabilities and effects, and an update performs none. Client-model corpus; an effect inside an update rejected; message-log replay equal in the VM and three browsers. new (adoption) PLAT-07-11 Browser APIs MUST be reached through bindings generated from the web platform's WebIDL, each operation carrying its capability, effects and failures; other JavaScript only through a declared binding. WebIDL coverage report; a call without its capability rejected. new (adoption) PLAT-07-12 Templates MUST be checked against a typed model of HTML and ARIA (element content, attribute names and value types, event handler signatures, accessible names and roles), with contextual escaping proved for every interpolation. Template corpus with one rejected case per class; escaping proof fixtures. new (adoption) PLAT-07-13 Routes MUST be typed: path and query parameters have refined types, a link names a route and supplies its parameters, and an internal link to no route is a compile error. Route corpus; broken-link rejection. new (adoption) PLAT-07-14 Styles SHOULD be referenced through typed identifiers (class names and design tokens) declared in the package; an undefined style is an error and an unused one is reported. Style corpus. new (adoption) PLAT-07-15 Message catalogs MUST be typed: every message a view uses exists in every shipped locale with matching placeholders and plural forms, or the build fails. Catalog corpus with a missing translation and a mismatched placeholder rejected. new (adoption) PLAT-07-16 A form field's validation MUST come from one declaration: the refined type of the server's input, run on the client for feedback and on the server for authority ( PLAT-07-05 ). Shared-validator fixtures; tampered-client tests. new (adoption) PLAT-07-18 The HTML, ARIA, WebIDL and CSS "},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#78-typed-client-applications","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"7.8 Typed client applications","m":"spec","x":"validator fixtures; tampered-client tests. new (adoption) PLAT-07-18 The HTML, ARIA, WebIDL and CSS definitions MUST be generated into fortis.web.platform , a separately versioned package of the kit, from pinned snapshots of machine-readable sources plus a reviewed overlay; each regeneration is released with its compatibility-classed diff, and an obsoleted definition is deprecated before it is removed. Regeneration log with source digests; diff per release; deprecation-window fixtures. new (adoption) PLAT-07-19 A project MUST declare its browser baseline; a feature outside it is an error unless guarded by a typed supported(...) check. Baseline fixtures with guarded and unguarded uses. new (adoption) PLAT-07-20 The generated definitions MUST be checked against their sources in both directions, and every rendered page of the template corpus MUST pass the Nu HTML Checker. Source-coverage report; validator log over the corpus. new (adoption) PLAT-07-17 Client correctness SHOULD be checked beyond types: invariants and specifications over updates (Programme PROG-PRD-19), model-based exploration of message sequences in the VM and in real browsers, and convergence of the offline outbox under reordering, duplication and conflict. Invariant, exploration and convergence corpora. new (adoption)"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#8-security-kit","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"8. Security kit","m":"spec","x":"8. Security kit # The security kit supplies authentication adapters, session and CSRF handling, HTTP hardening, and the second exit from a secret: declassify with registered sanitisers. Secret[T] itself and reveal are core ( fortis.host ), because the core Environment.get returns Secret[String]? ; the kit re-exports them. An application imports it to get reviewed defaults instead of writing its own cryptography or secret handling."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#81-secrett","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"8.1 Secret[T]","m":"spec","x":"8.1 Secret[T] # Secret[T] is a nominal wrapper with no codec, no capture eligibility, no Equatable , and a Displayable that renders only a fingerprint ( Secret(sha256:ab12\u2026) ), so interpolating a secret is legal and shows nothing. Exactly two operations return the underlying value: reveal(secret, cap: SecretAccess) T \u2014 returns the value; requires the SecretAccess capability; carries the io effect because it writes an audit record. declassify(secret, sanitiser: Sanitiser[T, U]) U \u2014 applies a registered sanitiser (tokenisation, keyed hashing, truncation) and returns its result; carries the io effect because it writes an audit record naming the sanitiser. Sanitiser[T, U] is itself a non-copyable Authority minted by Sanitiser.register from SecretAccess , which is what satisfies the authority rule for declassify and for any function that wraps it. Sanitiser[T, U] is a nominal type whose only constructor is Sanitiser.register(cap: SecretAccess, name: StringView, sanitiser: pure fn(T) U) . Registration requires the same capability as reveal , so a sanitiser cannot be created by code that could not have revealed the secret; the registered name appears in every audit record. There is no information-flow analysis in version 1: a Bool computed from a secret is a Bool . Side channels (timing, length, error messages) are outside the guarantee. A hashed identifier's cardinality is a schema classification attribute on the observation field, not taint. Configuration stores secret references; debugging prints fingerprints."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#82-authentication-and-sessions","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"8.2 Authentication and sessions","m":"spec","x":"8.2 Authentication and sessions # Adapters: cookie sessions, OAuth/OIDC, password credentials, WebAuthn/passkeys, via maintained libraries with pinned, reviewed parameters and a migration policy. Authentication is separate from object and tenant authorisation. Coarse authorisation at each HTTP or action boundary is the auth: property of the http and action aspects, checked in their before hooks against the request identity; object and tenant checks are explicit calls in the body. The kit checks that the property or the call is present, not that the rule is right. Cookie sessions rotate, expire and revoke; state-changing cookie-authenticated actions require CSRF tokens. CORS is explicit."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#83-hardening","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"8.3 Hardening","m":"spec","x":"8.3 Hardening # HTTP clients verify TLS, limit redirects, bodies and time, and enforce egress policy. Templates escape by context. XML external entities are disabled; decompression is bounded. All parsers have quotas. Inspection and agent endpoints bind locally by default and need explicit configuration in production. ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-08-01 Security adapters MUST be maintained, reviewed and version-pinned. Inventory/review/security suite. SEC-001 PLAT-08-02 The underlying value of a Secret[T] MUST leave only through reveal with capability or declassify with a registered sanitiser; its display MUST be the fingerprint; it MUST NOT be encodable or capturable, directly or nested. Display/codec/capture compile-fail incl. nested; audit tests. PLAT-08-03 Identity MUST NOT substitute for object/tenant authorisation. Cross-tenant/escalation tests. SEC-003 PLAT-08-04 Untrusted inputs MUST have bounded processing. Fuzz/resource-exhaustion suite. SEC-004 PLAT-08-05 Debug and agent interfaces MUST be authenticated and capability-restricted. Exposure/privilege tests. SEC-005, NFR-024"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#9-packages-scaffolds-and-supply-chain","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"9. Packages, scaffolds and supply chain","m":"spec","x":"9. Packages, scaffolds and supply chain # This section defines how Fortis code is distributed and reused: packages, their identity and locking, the registry, and scaffolds that generate project and feature code. A library author publishes a package; an application depends on it by name and version and builds offline from a lockfile."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#91-package-model","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"9.1 Package model","m":"spec","x":"9.1 Package model # A package is the distributable unit named in project.yaml ( name: acme.billing ). Its public surface is the set of modules listed under exports: ; export declarations in other modules are visible only within the package. Identity is registry, namespace, name, version and content digest. Two incompatible versions of one package have distinct nominal types. YAML schema: fortis.project/v1 name: acme.billing version: 1.4.0 language: \"1.0\" compatibility_set: \"2026.4\" exports: [billing, billing.invoices, billing.money] entry: billing.main # applications only dependencies: fortis.http: \"^1\" acme.ledger: \"=2.1.3\" A package declares what it contains: library modules, contracts, adapters (with trusted Rust), declarative-head schemas, scaffolds, tests, docs, skills. A lockfile pins every transitive package, feature, native crate, compiler compatibility and provenance record. Vendored, offline builds are required."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#92-registry-and-build-safety","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"9.2 Registry and build safety","m":"spec","x":"9.2 Registry and build safety # Registry operations authenticate, checksum, and check archive path/symlink/size safety, signatures, provenance, revocation and licences. A signature proves origin, not semantics. Install-time and build-time execution of package-supplied code is not supported in version 1: a package that contains a Cargo build script or any executable generator of its own is rejected at install. This is distinct from compiler-owned declarative elaboration : declaration heads, codecs, DI constructors and scaffold templates are expanded by the toolchain from data (schemas, YAML manifests, templates) that packages supply; packages supply data, never code that runs during install or build. Runtime and FFI crates needed by fortis ship vendored and prebuilt per tier-1 target. Sandboxed build scripts are a later deliverable listed in the Delivery Programme."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#93-scaffolds","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"9.3 Scaffolds","m":"spec","x":"9.3 Scaffolds # Scaffolds are project, feature or component blueprints with a YAML manifest declaring typed parameters, dependencies, provided and required interfaces, generated files, conflicts and verification. plan returns a semantic diff; apply is bound to the workspace hash and atomic; verify runs check, contracts, tests and schemas. Repeated application is idempotent or creates a distinct named instance. Provenance records template version, parameters and generated symbols. Upgrade in version 1 compares the content hash of each generated file with the hash recorded at generation. Unchanged files are replaced; changed files are left untouched and listed in a conflict report with the new version placed alongside ( file.ft.new ). There is no semantic three-way merge. Official scaffolds: library, CLI, HTTP API, SSR app, SPA/PWA app, worker, scheduler, event consumer, PostgreSQL, SQLite, cache, authentication, API client (Swift/Kotlin/TS), telemetry/archive, desktop host."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#94-supported-combinations","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"9.4 Supported combinations","m":"spec","x":"9.4 Supported combinations # The scaffold option matrix is published machine-readably in three classes: full (every combination integration-tested), representative (a named subset tested, others compile-checked), unsupported (rejected by plan ). \"Every combination tested\" is not claimed. ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-09-01 Packages and toolchains MUST be content-locked and build offline. Clean vendored rebuild. PKG-001 PLAT-09-02 Install and build MUST NOT execute package code; build scripts MUST be rejected. Malicious package tests. PKG-002, CMP-006 PLAT-09-03 Package public surface MUST be the exports: module list. Visibility corpus. new (G-02) PLAT-09-04 Scaffolds MUST plan/apply/verify atomically with provenance. Rollback/stale-workspace tests. SCF-001 PLAT-09-05 Upgrade MUST replace only unmodified files and report every diverged file. Hash-diverged corpus. SCF-002 PLAT-09-06 The scaffold matrix MUST be published in full/representative/unsupported classes. Matrix evidence. SCF-003"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#10-configuration-and-deployment","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"10. Configuration and deployment","m":"spec","x":"10. Configuration and deployment # Project configuration is typed YAML that selects runtime mechanisms, providers, destinations and deployment values. It never changes what source means. This section defines the layering rules, waivers, versioned public schemas and the contents of a deployment bundle."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#101-configuration","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"10.1 Configuration","m":"spec","x":"10.1 Configuration # Configuration is an ordinary class, loaded once in main through capabilities and passed as a value; there is no global configuration and no ambient env() . Nested sections are ordinary classes too, so every field has a type, a default and a name a reviewer can find. Fortis // illustrative export class ListenConfig derive Default { export host : String = \"127.0.0.1\" export port : Int < 1 .. 65535 > = 8080 } export class DatabaseConfig { export url : Secret [ Url ] export poolSize : Int < 1 .. 64 > = 10 } export class HttpConfig derive Default { export requestTimeout : Duration = 30s export corsOrigins : List [ Url ] = List . of ( ) } export class AppConfig { export stage : Stage = Stage . Development export debug : Bool = false export listen : ListenConfig = ListenConfig . default ( ) export database : DatabaseConfig export http : HttpConfig = HttpConfig . default ( ) } Inside main , which declares the Environment and FileSystem capabilities and the arguments as its parameters: Fortis // illustrative let config : AppConfig = Config . load ( environment , fileSystem , args ) or fail Layers , lowest to highest precedence: field defaults; config/base.yaml then config/<stage>.yaml , where <stage> is the environment variable FORTIS_STAGE (default development ), read before anything else; a .env file in the project root; the process environment; the command-line arguments passed to load . Values merge leaf by leaf (a later layer replaces a field, never a whole section); a null in a file means \"unset, use the lower layer\"; a key appearing twice in one source is an error. Keys map mechanically: database.poolSize is database.pool_size in a file, APP__DATABASE__POOL_SIZE in the environment and .env , --database.pool-size on the command line. The loader reads only environment variables under the APP__ prefix (the application may choose another prefix at load ), so inherited variables such as PATH are ignored; a prefixed variable, a file key or a command-line flag that names no field is a load failure naming the key. A required field with no value is a load failure; a value outside its refinement is a load failure with the compiler's refinement diagnostic. The loader keeps per-field provenance ( config.origin(field) ). Security rules the loader enforces: A Secret[T] field may be sourced from .env , the environment or a secret-provider reference ( ref:vault:prod/db ); a config file may hold only a reference for it, never a literal. A non-secret string or Url value that carries credentials (userinfo in a URL, or a field or variable whose name, split on case changes and underscores and compared case-insensitively, contains the word password , secret , token , key , credential or dsn , so apiKey and API_KEY both match) is a load failure unless the field is Secret[T] . The name check is a backstop, not a classification: credential-bearing configuration must be declared Secret . A Secret[T] field is never read from the command line; a flag naming one is a load failure, because argv is visible to every process on the host. Environment.get(name) on the capability returns Secret[String]? , never a plain String , so a variable read outside the loader is a secret until revealed; Config.load unwraps a variable only into a non-secret field whose name passes the check above. .env is read only when the stage is development or test ; in any other stage its presence is a startup error. A world-readable .env is refused on POSIX hosts. Secret[T] is not Encodable and not capturable, and its Displayable rendering is a fixed fingerprint ( Secret(sha256:ab12\u2026) ) that reveals nothing; the fingerprint, reveal(secret, caps.secrets) and declassify (8.1) are the only three ways anything derived from a secret reaches a non-secret value. Reading the environment needs the Environment capability, which only main creates, so no library reads variables behind the application's back. Defaults fail closed: debug: false , loopback listen address, TLS veri"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#101-configuration","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"10.1 Configuration","m":"spec","x":"ehind the application's back. Defaults fail closed: debug: false , loopback listen address, TLS verification on, empty CORS origins. Aspect arguments are compile-time constants, so a route's limits: is a fixed ceiling on the endpoint; values that vary per stage (ports, timeouts, origins, rate limits) are configuration applied server-wide at Server.create , never above the endpoint's ceiling."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#102-public-schemas","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"10.2 Public schemas","m":"spec","x":"10.2 Public schemas # Versioned public schemas: APIs (OpenAPI), observations, jobs, component state, DI manifest, agent protocol, package manifests, evidence. Internal IR and bytecode are compiler-version-specific. The JSON Schemas are planned deliverables tracked in the Delivery Programme; they validate shape, and the compiler validates semantics."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#103-deployment-bundle","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"10.3 Deployment bundle","m":"spec","x":"10.3 Deployment bundle # A bundle contains the native binary, assets, configuration schema, OpenAPI document, migration plan, SBOM, provenance and verification evidence, plus health/readiness contracts, graceful shutdown and resource limits. Containers are optional packaging. ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-10-01 Configuration MUST be strict, typed, deterministic and provenance-tracked. Duplicate/unknown/layer tests. CFG-001 PLAT-10-02 Reload MUST be atomic and remain within compiled contracts. Concurrent/invalid-plan tests. CFG-002 PLAT-10-03 Selectors and waivers MUST NOT weaken guarantees; soundness MUST have no waiver. Rename/expiry/policy tests. CFG-003 PLAT-10-04 Persisted and wire schemas MUST have explicit compatibility rules. Old/new decoder suite. SCH-001 PLAT-10-05 Release bundles MUST carry deployment, schema, security and evidence manifests. Clean deployment and rollback exercise. DEP-001 PLAT-10-06 Configuration MUST be loaded through capabilities into a typed class with the layering, key mapping and secret rules of 10.1; a literal secret in a config file or an unknown key MUST fail the load. Loader corpus; secret-in-file negative tests. new"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#11-developer-tooling","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"11. Developer tooling","m":"spec","x":"11. Developer tooling # Developer tooling is the development VM with hot reload, the REPL, replay, the language daemon, the agent protocol, browser testing and the dashboard: toolchain components, not a kit (Programme PROG-PRD-38); what a program sees of them is the explore head (1.2). A developer or an agent uses it to edit, check and run a Fortis program with sub-second feedback; production binaries never include it."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#111-development-vm","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"11.1 Development VM","m":"spec","x":"11.1 Development VM # The VM executes checked IR with source, contract and provenance metadata. It may use tagged values and reference counts internally but never changes source semantics or accepts invalid code. The standard library is implemented once (Rust adapters plus Fortis source); the VM calls it as intrinsics, so VM/native parity applies to the Fortis-source parts. A missing development adapter is an explicit unsupported-capability error."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#112-reload-policy","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"11.2 Reload policy","m":"spec","x":"11.2 Reload policy # A reload compiles the edited source into a new generation : a checked, validated program whose declarations keep identities across edits. A function is identified by its qualified name. A lambda inherits the identity of the previous generation's lambda of the same parent with the same structure (environment and body, its own lambdas included), or with a unique environment. An ambiguous correspondence is a removal and an addition, never a rematch. Each change between the generations is classified. A compatible edit changes only non-generic function bodies (the exported summary, meaning signature, effects, allocation summary and obligations, and every closure environment layout unchanged) or adds declarations. Genericity is declared: a body under type parameters is generic even when no parameter survives in its IR. Every other change is incompatible: a summary, an environment, a layout, a generic body, a constant, a removal, a method's implementation, or anything the DI elaborator generated. Generations are pinned per root invocation (a request, a job run, a REPL evaluation, and every task it launches or detaches): a frame started on generation n calls generation n code until it completes, so one operation never mixes two versions. New root invocations use the newest generation. Closures, function values and unstarted futures carry their function's identity and deep fingerprint (summary, environment, and the layouts of what they mention), not their generation. A frame resolves them in its own generation, and one that does not resolve is a version conflict: the invocation ends, runs none of the stale code, and its scope restarts. A root invocation's result, and a detached task's, is destroyed by the generation that made it. A compatible reload restarts nothing for its changes: every scope's state is carried as it is (the caps below may still evict). An incompatible edit restarts, with an explanation, each affected scope : dependency : the changed declaration is in what the scope's factory and handlers reach (calls, dispatch, values, lambdas, constants and their initializers, types mentioned), in either generation; state : the state may hold a value the new generation cannot use (a type it reaches through fields, payloads, implementors, captures, future arguments or failures, with type arguments substituted, changed or was removed); entry : the scope's factory no longer accepts its recorded arguments, so it stops. Additions restart nothing, but an old invocation that could meet a new value through the state's interior mutability (a Mutex ) is cancelled ( old reader ). A restart signals the scope's tasks, drains them without advancing time, releases the old state on the generation it was valid in, installs the new generation and rebuilds the state there. A task that cannot finish without time stalls the drain: the scope stops, and its state is kept, pinning its generation, until the task finishes. The explanation names the rule, the change, and the path from the scope's state or entry point to the changed declaration. Old generations are reclaimed when no task and no stalled scope's kept state references them; carried scope state, code values and completed tasks pin nothing. A generation cap (live generations) and a memory cap (the old generations' IR bytes) evict the oldest generation an eviction can free, by restarting the scopes with invocations on it. A reload is rejected ( bounded admission ) when its drain stalls past a cap, when kept state pins generations no eviction can free, or when old readers did not drain. Browser DOM state is outside VM parity."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#113-repl-and-replay","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"11.3 REPL and replay","m":"spec","x":"11.3 REPL and replay # The REPL has typed cells; redefining a binding uses versioned reload and cannot change a binding's type silently. Replay modes state their fidelity: input-only, controlled-environment, controlled-scheduler, snapshot-inspection. Clock, random, network, filesystem and context adapters are the replayable boundaries; external processes and databases are not rewound. Recordings pin build, configuration, schema and privacy policy."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#114-agent-protocol","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"11.4 Agent protocol","m":"spec","x":"11.4 Agent protocol # Versioned JSON-RPC over stdio or a local socket; authenticated HTTP and an MCP adapter expose the same services. Read operations: workspace snapshot; symbol search and inspect; type/effect/ownership explain; contract origins; DI plan, live and history; test coverage and findings; telemetry plan preview; generated-source mapping. Write/run operations: scaffold plan/apply/upgrade; atomic semantic edit bound to the workspace hash; format and check; selected tests, fuzz, mutation, benchmarks, browser runs; explore/replay/promote; native build; evidence verification. Every operation states capabilities, snapshot, budget and idempotency. Runs return a job ID and structured progress. Permissions separate explanation, code change, command execution, secrets and deployment; skill prose cannot override them. CLI exit codes: 0 success; 1 user diagnostics or test failure; 2 invalid invocation or configuration shape; 3 infrastructure or dependency unavailable; 4 internal tool defect; 5 cancelled."},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#115-browser-testing-and-dashboard","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"11.5 Browser testing and dashboard","m":"spec","x":"11.5 Browser testing and dashboard # Browser tests use real Chromium, Firefox and WebKit behind a pinned driver adapter (Playwright initially) with role/label locators, auto-waiting and isolated contexts; failure bundles include screenshots, DOM and accessibility snapshots, console and network logs, backend traces and clock controls. Browser binaries are installed explicitly and cached; nothing downloads silently. The dashboard shows routes and OpenAPI, component and resource state, DI provenance, query and migration plans, traces, captures and projections, findings and replay, tasks and contract explanations. It is a client of the same authenticated APIs as agents. Development binds locally; production requires explicit access configuration. ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-11-01 The VM MUST share checked semantics with native; the standard library MUST be implemented once. Differential and negative corpus. restates PROG-VM-01 for developer tooling PLAT-11-02 Compatible edits (unchanged summary and layouts) MUST reload without restarting unrelated scopes. Active frame/future tests: conformance/reload isolation, migration and classification (SP-07). VM-002 PLAT-11-03 Incompatible edits MUST restart the affected scope with an explanation. Layout/effect/provider change tests: conformance/reload restart and explanation checks (SP-07). VM-003 PLAT-11-04 Generations MUST be pinned per root invocation and later reclaimed. Mixed-generation and soak tests: conformance/reload retention and soak checks (SP-07). VM-004 PLAT-11-05 Replay MUST disclose uncontrolled boundaries. Mixed real/mock fixtures. VM-005 PLAT-11-06 Human and agent tools MUST share semantic and evidence services. Cross-surface consistency tests. AGT-001 PLAT-11-07 Agent edits MUST be atomic and snapshot-bound. Concurrent/stale edit tests. AGT-002 PLAT-11-08 Browser tests MUST be isolated, safe and evidence-producing. Session/external-effect tests. TST-005"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#12-standard-contracts","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"12. Standard contracts","m":"spec","x":"12. Standard contracts # Named contracts are conjunctions of exactly these elements, and nothing else: the modifiers pure , noalloc , async , try , recovers ; forbids <effect> over the eight core effects; requires capability X ; and excludes capability X (the function may not take a parameter of that capability type and may not call, directly or through callees, a function that requires it; the check is transitive over summaries). A contract subtracts; anything a kit wants to do around a function (check authorisation, map failures, attach a schema, enforce limits) is an aspect, and anything it wants to check about a declaration that is not function-shaped is a head validator. Contracts are declared with the contract head and applied by name in the modifier position of a function head ( export PublicApi async try func \u2026 ). Carrying a contract's name does not satisfy it; the checker verifies every predicate. Fortis // illustrative contract FastCalculation { pure noalloc } contract PublicApi { excludes capability RawSql forbids recovers } contract DatabaseOnly { excludes capability Network excludes capability Mail } Contract Expansion PureCalculation pure FastCalculation pure noalloc PublicApi excludes capability RawSql , forbids recovers ; the http aspects needs PublicApi and themselves supply authorisation, limits, the status map and the HttpRequest observation BusinessOperation excludes capability RawSql , forbids recovers ; the observation schema is attached with @observe(Schema) , whose required fields include the business outcome and denominator BackgroundJob contract part: requires capability TaskContext , forbids recovers ; the policy (payload schema, deadline, idempotency/retry/ack, dead letter, observation) is the @job(...) aspect, which needs BackgroundJob DatabaseOnly excludes capability Network , excludes capability Mail DurableAudit not a contract: @audit(Schema) is a Telemetry aspect whose after hook performs an acknowledged persisted write and whose on failure re-fails; it needs no contract but is rejected on pure targets by the ordinary effect rule NativeAdapter not a contract: the trusted modifier (Language Reference, Effects and contracts) plus the package validator for adapter metadata (ownership, layout, allocation, cancellation, VM parity adapter) BrowserAction forbids recovers ; the action aspect of the Web UI kit supplies validation, per-action authorisation, revision/idempotency, safe response and resource invalidation OfflineMutation contract part: excludes capability Network ; the outbox, identity/schema validation, replay/idempotency and the user conflict function are the @offline(...) aspect, which needs OfflineMutation ReusableLibrary not a contract: a package validator (explicit exports: , conformance suite present, documentation for every exported declaration, target support and compatibility baseline declared) Money lives in the core module fortis.money : Decimal paired with a Currency value carried at run time ( Money.of(Currency.GBP, 10d) ); arithmetic between different currencies fails with money.currency_mismatch , arithmetic through named functions ( Money.add ) returning Outcome , and explicit versioned rate conversion. There is no operator overloading. ID Requirement Acceptance evidence Old ID PLAT-12-01 A named contract MUST expand only to modifiers, forbids <effect> , requires capability and excludes capability ; every operational or declaration-level requirement MUST be an aspect or a named validator, and the table in 12 MUST be the single authoritative expansion of each name. Contract-expansion golden tests; validator inventory. PLAT-12-02 A contract name MUST NOT satisfy a contract; every predicate MUST be checked. Mis-labelled implementation negative tests. App. D"},{"u":"docs/Fortis_Platform_Specification.html#appendix-a-requirement-id-rename-table","p":"Fortis Platform Specification","h":"Appendix A. Requirement ID rename table","m":"spec","x":"Appendix A. Requirement ID rename table # Old ID New ID Note ARC-003 PLAT-01-03 MOD-001 LANG-04-02 ( PLAT-01-04 restates it) STD-001, STD-002 PLAT-01-01 , PLAT-01-05 ASY-004, ASY-005 PLAT-02-06 , PLAT-02-05 ASY-001, ASY-002, ASY-003 are LANG-12-03 /05 ( PLAT-02-01 implements them) APP-001 PLAT-02-04 DI-001 \u2026 DI-007 PLAT-03-02 \u2026 PLAT-03-07 API-001 \u2026 API-005 PLAT-04-01 , -02, -04, -05, -06 DAT-001 \u2026 DAT-004, JOB-001, CAC-001 PLAT-05-01 \u2026 PLAT-05-07 OBS-003, OBS-004, OBS-005 PLAT-06-01 , PLAT-06-09 , PLAT-06-12 OBS-001, -002, -006 moved to the Language Reference TEL-001 \u2026 TEL-006, FIN-001, FIN-002 PLAT-06-02 \u2026 PLAT-06-11 WEB-001 \u2026 WEB-007 PLAT-07-02 \u2026 PLAT-07-09 SEC-001 \u2026 SEC-005, NFR-024 PLAT-08-01 \u2026 PLAT-08-05 PKG-001 \u2026 PKG-003, SCF-001 \u2026 SCF-003, CMP-006 PLAT-01-02 , PLAT-09-01 \u2026 PLAT-09-06 CFG-001 \u2026 CFG-003, SCH-001, DEP-001 PLAT-10-01 \u2026 PLAT-10-05 VM-002 \u2026 VM-004, AGT-001, AGT-002, TST-005 PLAT-11-02 \u2026 PLAT-11-08 VM-001 is PROG-VM-01; VM-005 split (PROG-VM-05 core part) NFR-014 PROG-NFR-14 ( PLAT-01-05 restates it) Appendix D section 12 FastCalculation is now pure noalloc ; nostate removed"},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.1.html#fortis-v001","p":"Fortis v0.0.1","h":"Fortis v0.0.1","m":"home","x":"Fortis v0.0.1 # Released 27 October 2026 by Pickering Technologies Ltd, under the MIT License ( LICENSE ). v0.0.1 lets you write a real program in one package, and check, run, build, format and test it. The program can use modules and imports, statements and expressions, the core types, ownership, functions and closures, classes, enums and match , outcomes and failures, defer , and example tests. You can run it three ways: on the definitional interpreter, on the development VM, or as a native binary. This release starts a ladder of releases from v0.0.1 to v1.0.0; What is next gives the next rungs. What it still refuses is listed in Known limitations (agent mode: the same list ). Every release blocker was audited before the release, with its fix and the fixture that checks it."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.1.html#commands","p":"Fortis v0.0.1","h":"Commands","m":"home","x":"Commands # Run these from the repository, after cd compiler && cargo build --release . fortis is bin/fortis . Command What it does fortis check FILE Static checks. No output means the file is clean. fortis run FILE Run main on the definitional interpreter. fortisc vm FILE Run main on the development VM. fortis test FILE Example tests and cases tables, on the interpreter. fortisc test FILE The same tests, on the VM. fortis build FILE --out APP Native executable, lowered to safe Rust. fortis fmt --check PATH List files whose layout is not canonical. --write rewrites whitespace only. fortis view PATH Read Fortis source the way the compiler does. Each command also takes a package directory. A file under a package's src/ is checked, run and built as a module of that package. A failing main exits 1, and an invalid invocation, such as a missing path, exits 2."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.1.html#scope","p":"Fortis v0.0.1","h":"Scope","m":"home","x":"Scope # Language Reference sections in this release: 3, lexical structure. 4.1 to 4.6, one package of modules, imports, visibility and the entry point. 5, statements and expressions. 6.1, 6.2, 6.4, 6.5, 6.6 without normalisation and case mapping, 6.7 for List , Map and Set , 6.8, 6.9 for tuples and anonymous records, 6.11 and 6.12. 7, ownership. 8, functions and closures. 9.1 to 9.4, classes, and 9.7 and 9.8, enums, patterns and match . 10, outcomes and failures, except 10.7. 12.1 and 12.2, defer and destruction order. 14, example tests and cases tables. Chapters that go past this subset carry a one-line preview naming the release that brings the rest. The guide and the agent mode both carry it."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.1.html#known-limitations","p":"Fortis v0.0.1","h":"Known limitations","m":"home","x":"Known limitations # Three kinds of limitation are listed, each with a workaround: valid programs the checkers reject, gaps in the library (such as missing Map operations), and checked programs that one backend refuses while another runs them. Known limitations for people. Known limitations for agents. For a native refusal, the usual workaround is to run the program with fortis run or fortisc vm . fortis build exits non-zero in that case and writes no executable."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.1.html#how-it-was-checked","p":"Fortis v0.0.1","h":"How it was checked","m":"home","x":"How it was checked # The merge gate passes on the release commit. It runs the syntax corpus, both static checkers, the conformance programs and packages, the acceptance programs, the pilot application's tests, the documentation examples and the plan checks. The three backends agree. The execution conformance suite runs each program on the interpreter, the VM and native, and compares the output and the order in which values are destroyed. The documentation examples run as written. Every example in the guide and the agent mode is checked by both checkers and run on the backends. 498 of 511 pass. The other 13 are recorded known failures. Two of them are listed limitations (D-52.1, D-55.8). The other eleven use features that arrive in v0.1: refinements, newtypes, generics, interfaces and derive . Every change was reviewed. Each pull request had a Codex review and a re-check, with its blocking findings fixed before merging. The release candidate v0.0.1-rc.1 had a whole-release review by Codex and Grok, in four areas: the checkers, the runtime, native, and the tools and documentation. Codex's checker review could not run, so Grok's covered that area. Every blocker the review found is fixed. The readiness audit records each blocker with its fix and fixture. This release makes no performance claims. Performance is judged at v1.0."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.1.html#what-is-next","p":"Fortis v0.0.1","h":"What is next","m":"home","x":"What is next # v0.0.2 is the reading release (X-345). Fortis optimises for reading, so a program marks only what the reader can't already see. In v0.0.2, the receiver of an editable self method is unmarked. main receives its capabilities as parameters. Logging is platform-native, with log.info , log.warn and log.error . The guide also gets a reading pass. v0.1 is for modelling a domain: refinements ( LR 6.3 ), newtypes ( LR 6.9 ), generics ( LR 6.10 ), interfaces and mixins ( LR 9.5 , LR 9.6 ), linear containers ( LR 10.7 ), and contracts ( LR 11.5 ). After that, v0.2 brings effects, claims and property tests; v0.3 async and tasks; v0.4 the rest of the standard library and the core runtime; v0.5 observation and kits."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#fortis-v002","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Fortis v0.0.2","m":"home","x":"Fortis v0.0.2 # Released 29 October 2026 by Pickering Technologies Ltd, under the MIT License ( LICENSE ). v0.0.2 is the reading release. Fortis is optimised for reading, and v0.0.2 applies one principle to the language: mark only what the reader can't already see. A marker earns its place when it says something surprising about the line; if a declaration already says it, the marker is noise. Seventeen changes follow from that, listed below with the Language Reference section that states each one. Nothing you could write in v0.0.1 stops working. Every older form is still accepted, and fortis fmt --write rewrites it into the newer one wherever the two mean the same ( Moving your code ). Everything in v0.0.1's scope is still in scope; v0.0.1 describes it."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#what-reads-differently","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"What reads differently","m":"home","x":"What reads differently # The same program, written for v0.0.1: Fortis // illustrative: v0.0.1 import fortis . host { Host } export try func main ( args : List [ String ] ) Void { let caps = Host . capabilities ( ) let mut readings : List [ Int < 0 .. 1000 > ] = List . of ( 12 , 30 , 18 ) editable readings . push ( 40 ) let total = Int . add ( Int . mul ( readings . count ( ) , 100 ) or fail , 5 ) or fail caps . console . print ( \"${readings.count()} readings, total ${total}\" ) } test \"the mean of four\" { let readings : List [ Int < 0 .. 1000 > ] = List . of ( 10 , 20 , 30 , 40 ) expect ( mean ( readings ) or fail ) == 25 } and for v0.0.2: Fortis // illustrative: v0.0.2 import fortis . host { Console } try func main ( console : Console ) { let mut readings : List [ Int < 0 .. 1000 > ] = List . of ( 12 , 30 , 18 ) readings . push ( 40 ) let total = readings . count ( ) * 100 + 5 or fail log . info ( \"summing ${readings.count()} readings\" ) console . print ( \"${readings.count()} readings, total ${total}\" ) } test \"the mean of four\" { let readings : List [ Int < 0 .. 1000 > ] = List . of ( 10 , 20 , 30 , 40 ) expect mean ( readings ) == 25 } main asks for the console it uses, and the runtime supplies it. readings is declared let mut , so the call that changes it needs no marker of its own. The arithmetic is written as arithmetic, and one or fail covers the expression. The test's call fails the test if it fails. What stays marked is what a reader could not otherwise know: an argument a callee changes ( normalise(editable readings) ), a transfer ( move ), a copy or a loan in a binding ( copy , alias ), and every failure decision outside tests."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#the-changes","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"The changes","m":"home","x":"The changes # Change Language Reference R1 The receiver of an editable self method, and a fn mut closure being called, are unmarked on a let mut binding: readings.push(40) , bump() . The call takes the same exclusive loan the marked form does, and is E-OWN-001 on a place that cannot change. LR 7.5 , 7.6 R2 The same on an editable parameter, the method's own editable self , an editable or move capture, and a binding holding an exclusive loan. LR 7.5 R3 copy and alias in bindings, capture-list markers, and editable and move on arguments stay: each says something the line does not. LR 7.5 , 8.3 R4 The entry main declares the capabilities it needs as parameters, supplied by type: func main(console: Console, args: List[String]) . Names and order are yours; a type the runtime cannot supply is E-NAM-005 , a type written twice E-NAM-006 . Only the entry main receives capabilities, and it owns them. LR 4.6 , 11.6 , 11.7 R5 log.info , log.warn and log.error write one log record to the diagnostic plane, never to standard output. They need no capability and have the io effect, like report . A failure is still recorded with report e . LR 13 , 11.6 , 11.7 R6 A Void return type may be left out of any head, cleanup and main included. A function type keeps its result. LR 8.1 R7 In test and property bodies a failing call fails the test: or fail is implied, and unproved Int arithmetic there is checked too. A call that or , catch or match consumes keeps its outcome. LR 14 , 6.4 R8 export is implied on the entry main , and args is declared only when it is used: func main() { \u2026 } . LR 4.6 R9 A labelled argument puns: wanted for wanted: wanted , in a class's generated constructor and after a named argument. LR 5.8 , 9.2 R10 The formatter no longer prints a lambda's inferred pure and noalloc back, and an expression-bodied lambda may leave out its result type. LR 8.2 , 1.1 R11 Map[String, V].get takes its key as a StringView , so a lookup needs no copy or String.from . LR 6.7 R12 Or-patterns in match : A | B => v . The alternatives bind no names and take no guard ( E-MAT-003 ). LR 9.8 R13 derive Default gives Name.default() for a class whose fields all have defaults. LR 6.10 , 9.2 R14 Checked arithmetic as operators: an Int operation the checker cannot prove safe, under or fail , or recover or catch , is the checked operation, so value * 10 + d or fail replaces Int.add(Int.mul(value, 10) or fail, d) or fail . LR 6.4 , 6.3 , 5.6 R15 An enum variant may be written bare where the expected type is that enum: Error for Severity.Error . A bare name that also names something else in scope must be qualified. LR 9.7 R16 return local moves a local let or let mut binding without move . LR 7.5 R17 @ignorable on a function declares that its result may be dropped, instead of discard at every caller. LR 5.5 , 11.8 The guide and the agent mode teach the new forms throughout, and mention each older form once. The closed decisions give the reason for each change."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#decisions-for-the-owners-review","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Decisions for the owner's review","m":"home","x":"Decisions for the owner's review # The owner reviewed the side-by-side examples and the reading pass and took every finding into v0.0.2. These are the narrower decisions made while building it, each written into the Language Reference; any of them may be reversed before the release. The eight open questions take the integrator's recommendation: a receiver is unmarked on an editable parameter (Q1), on the method's own editable self (Q2) and on an editable capture (Q5); copy and alias in bindings (Q3) and capture-list markers (Q4) stay; other functions take capabilities as ordinary parameters (Q6); main owns its capabilities and may wrap one in Shared (Q7); a failure is recorded with report e , and log.* is for what is not a failure (Q8). Receivers. A receiver is unmarked wherever editable could be written, including a binding that holds an exclusive loan and a move capture of a fn mut . An editable self call on a trivial value still needs a place that may change. A from self result is unmarked like any other. Void and returns. R6 covers every head, and a function type keeps its result. R16's \"local\" is a binding a let or let mut statement declares, not a destructuring, a catch binding, a pattern, a parameter or a capture. The entry main . Each parameter is plain name: T , with no mode and no default. Capability types are those of fortis.host . The entry is the entry module's main function, exported or not. Host.capabilities() is still accepted in a main with no capability parameter; in a main with one it is E-EFF-010 , a second creation. Logging. A log statement has the io effect, so a pure function cannot log. Its record names the function that wrote it. log is not a keyword: where log names something, log.info(...) is an ordinary call. Tests. Only calls of try functions get the implied or fail ; a function that returns an Outcome it built is not a failing call. A let that the body later consumes with or , catch or match , or annotates Outcome[\u2026] , keeps the outcome. Checked operators. R14 covers Int only, since the other integer types have no checked family, and >> has none either. The region is the handler's left operand through the arithmetic operators and parentheses; it stops at calls, indexes, fields and comparisons. There is no Outcome -valued arithmetic, and x += 1 stays proof-required. An unproved operator on a sized type under a handler is reported once, as E-REF-002 asking for a refined type or a test. The integer families. LR 6.4 's table claimed every integer type has three families; only Int has them, so the table now says so, and the sized families are planned for v0.4. Or-patterns, bare variants, puns, defaults. R12 is the narrow form. R15's ambiguity is E-TYP-040 , naming the qualified form, and only a comparison's right operand takes the left operand's enum. R9 keeps leading bare names positional outside constructors. R13 follows the Language Reference's Default interface: Name.default() . Map lookups and @ignorable . R11 covers get with String keys only. @ignorable is an aspect, and the parentheses of any aspect are now optional. Reassigning a binding that holds a loan. LR 7.3 's \"a loan-holding binding cannot be reassigned at all\" means a binding that holds a loan whole and is a path to the owner: a parameter, let r = alias p , let r = editable p , or a binding that receives a from result that is itself a loan; both checkers reject reassigning one. A let mut binding that receives a loan inside an optional, a tuple or a record ( let mut t = xs.get(0) ) may be reassigned, as both checkers and v0.0.1 already allowed: the new value's loans replace the old ones at the assignment, and the old value is not destroyed ( LR 7.9 ). So after t = xs.get(1) the list stays lent until the end of t 's block (D-348), and after t = null it may change. Loans stay lexical: the assignment replaces the value that held the loan, and a loan's last use never ends it. For a tuple or a record that holds a loan, both checkers still rej"},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#decisions-for-the-owners-review","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Decisions for the owner's review","m":"home","x":" a loan's last use never ends it. For a tuple or a record that holds a loan, both checkers still reject the reassignment, and returning one, although the rule allows both (D-363, a known limitation). The migration. fortis fmt rewrites old forms only where the meaning is the same, and checks the result before keeping it. A module of a package is rewritten in its package. There is no rewrite for logging, @ignorable or derive Default : each is the author's choice. A string result of a from function. The wording of LR 7.6 that came with D-381's fix called every literal a value of the function's own, which contradicted 6.6 (a literal without interpolation is a StringView from static ) and native code, which builds it. The narrower reading is taken (D-386): StringView from xs => \"plain\" lends, from static, and is accepted; \"x${\u2026}\" allocates a fresh String and is E-OWN-051 , as is \"plain\" where a String loan is declared ( alias String from xs ), since the conversion allocates. 7.6 now says so, citing 6.6."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#defects-fixed","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Defects fixed","m":"home","x":"Defects fixed # Defect Fixed by D-158 Both checkers rejected a pattern nested inside another ( Next(tail: End(value: _)) , present(Node(value: b, next: _)) ): a nested pattern's fields were looked up in the outer variant, and its bindings were collected twice. Each variant or class pattern names its own fields only, and its bindings are collected once; nested patterns check and run on every backend, with or without an or-pattern. D-240 Native code aborted on Int.MIN % -1 . Native code computes the language's remainder. D-242 Both checkers accepted derive Default where no default exists (a field of a type parameter, of an Array , FixedList , FixedMap or OrderedMap , also behind a newtype, or an enum), and then no backend could build the value; they rejected a program's own class named Array that has a default; and no backend built a field of a newtype, or of a type that implements Default itself, which both checkers accepted. A field of a type parameter or of one of those containers without an initialiser, and derive Default on an enum, are E-TYP-090 ; an enum with a default implements Default itself. The checkers look through newtypes and know the containers by their declaration, not their spelling; every backend builds a newtype's default from its representation's, and a type's own default() . D-243 Both checkers accepted a let pattern that can fail to match ( let End(value: a) = move c ), which then stopped at run time on the interpreter and the VM and did not build natively. A let pattern with a variant, a literal, null or present(\u2026) at any depth is E-MAT-004 ; match handles the other cases. D-246 Native code ran the fallback of a ?? whose result is lent ( show(xs.get(0) ?? fallback()) ) before it tested the left side, so a fallback that prints or fails did so with the value present. The fallback runs only when the left side is null, on every backend; the same lowering builds a lent map lookup's fallback ( m.get(k) ?? fallback() ), which did not build natively. D-302 A native build of a package depended on where the package lay: fortisc build wrote the absolute path of every module into the executable, so two clones built different executables. A runtime record names a module relative to its package, on every backend; the build check builds one package in two directories and compares the executables byte for byte. D-303 An unmarked call of an editable self method on a receiver held through a loan, or of a trivial type, took no loan in either checker, so it could change what cannot change. The unmarked call is checked exactly as the marked one, wherever editable could be written, and is E-OWN-001 elsewhere. D-304 Both checkers accepted a bare variant where nothing expects its enum ( let x = Error ); the VM and native could not build it. Both checkers reject it with E-TYP-040 , naming the qualified form. D-307 A bare variant of a generic enum inside another variant's payload ( Chain.Next(tail: Next(tail: End(value: 4))) ) did not build natively; the interpreter and the VM ran it. The checker gives the bare variant the type arguments its payload fixes, and native code builds it. D-308 In a test body, a call that is all primary ( Code.validate(s) , Name.parse(s) ) did not get its implied or fail on the VM and native, and the interpreter passed a failed validate 's outcome on as a value. Every backend gives the success value, and a failure fails the test. D-309 fortis fmt 's rewrite of the entry main ran on rejected modules and could change their verdict. The rewrite is kept only for a module that checks before and after it. D-313 return (r) ran a local's cleanup twice on the interpreter. Every backend moves a parenthesised returned local. D-314 An aspect a module declares as ignorable lost its hooks. Only the prelude's @ignorable , resolved where the function is declared, has no hooks. D-319 A kept outcome passed to a recovery helper failed a test on the interpreter. The implied or fail applies only at the calls the checker rec"},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#defects-fixed","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Defects fixed","m":"home","x":"lper failed a test on the interpreter. The implied or fail applies only at the calls the checker records. D-323 Log records from a lambda or a cleanup named the caller on the interpreter. Every backend names lambda@LINE:COLUMN and cleanup T . D-324 fortis fmt turned (a + b or fail) * c into a + b * c . The grouping is kept. D-329 fortis fmt rewrote a key copy for any method named get . Only lookups on the prelude's Map[String, V] are rewritten. D-335 derive Default through a newtype that leads back to the class ( type Wrap = Inner , class Inner derive Default { w: Wrap } ) passed both checkers, then overflowed the stack on every backend. A default that contains itself is E-TYP-090 , naming the cycle; an optional field, a collection or an initialiser breaks it. Every backend stops on a cycle with an error, never a stack overflow. D-336 Two classes that derive Default and hold each other, or a class that holds itself, crashed the same way. The same rule covers them. D-337 A chain of 64 or more newtypes had a default on the VM and natively, but not on the interpreter. The interpreter unfolds a chain of any length. D-338 A loan of a class with cleanup ( let t = xs.get(0) , m.get(k) , an alias binding, a field reached through a loan) ran that cleanup when the loan ended, on the interpreter and the VM, and the owner ran it again; native code ran it for the copy a map lookup or a cursor made. cleanup runs once, when the value's owner is destroyed, on every backend; a loan, an alias or a view ending runs nothing, and a loan of an Abandonable value is not abandoned. D-339 A ?? whose fallback is another lent ?? ( xs.get(0) ?? ys.get(0) ?? fallback() ) did not build natively. It builds, and each fallback still runs only when everything to its left is null. D-340 A failure's message was its template with its quotes on the interpreter and without them on the VM and native, and ${field} was never filled in. Every backend renders the template with the payload when it writes the record. D-341 Both checkers accepted a loan where an owned value is expected ( return xs.get(0) from a function returning Item? ), and the interpreter and the VM aliased the element, so a read-only value could be changed through the returned or stored loan; for a value that is not Copyable it made a second owner. The same held through a ?? ( s ?? move d with s a read parameter, or a nested lent ?? ). A loan stays a loan, and so does a ?? whose result can be one: an owned value is made from it with copy ( E-OWN-001 without it), and a loan of a value that is not Copyable is E-OWN-002 . copy of a binding that holds a loan now copies what it lends. D-345 Native code did not build give xs.get(0) ?? alias d inside an if expression. A ?? whose sides are both borrowed is a reference into them, so it can be an if expression's value. D-348 Both checkers dropped a loan that a result holds inside an optional, a tuple or a record, so after let t = xs.get(0) the list could be popped, changed or moved while t still lent its element; the interpreter and the VM then read an element the list no longer held, and the VM lost its cleanup . The binding that receives the loan holds it to the end of its block, whether it gets it by let , by assignment, by move of another binding, in a tuple or record, or through a ?? . Changing the owner meanwhile is E-OWN-010 , and moving it is E-OWN-011 . D-349 A tuple holding a loan ( let pair = (xs.get(0), 1) ) ran the borrowed element's cleanup when the tuple ended, on the interpreter, and the list's owner ran it again; the same held for a record literal, a tuple temporary, and a moved, destructured or matched tuple. A tuple or a record owns only the values of its own beside the loan, on every backend: the loan's cleanup runs once, when its owner ends. D-350 Assigning to a let mut binding that holds a loan ( t = null , t = xs.get(1) ) ran the old element's cleanup on the interpreter, and the owner ran it again. The assignment replaces the loan and destr"},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#defects-fixed","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Defects fixed","m":"home","x":"s cleanup on the interpreter, and the owner ran it again. The assignment replaces the loan and destroys nothing, on every backend. D-351 The VM's executor trace announced a drop whose cleanup did not run (a destructured tuple's loan), so it disagreed with the other backends. Every backend announces a drop exactly when its cleanup runs. D-366 A tuple or record element whose type is a loan but whose value is the aggregate's own ( (empty.get(0) ?? move d, make(\"own\")) , an if that moved a value in) never ran that value's cleanup on the VM, and assigning a loan to a record's field that held one ran the old element's. Which parts of a tuple or record are loans is decided by what each was given, as for a binding: the aggregate destroys the values of its own on every backend, and a loan in it destroys nothing. D-367 Native code ran that value's cleanup after the tuple's other elements, or after the block ended, and did not build such a tuple as a statement's temporary or an if 's value. The tuple holds the value itself and destroys its elements in index order, as the interpreter and the VM do. D-368 A loan moved into a tuple from a binding ( (move t, 1) after let t = xs.get(0) ) was destroyed with the tuple on the interpreter. A binding that holds a loan moves that loan, and the tuple destroys nothing it borrows. D-372 An expression match that moves a tuple or record holding a value of its own ( let n = match move pair { (a, b) => \u2026 } after (empty.get(0) ?? move d, make(\"own\")) ) never ran that value's cleanup on the VM, nor for a part given out of the arm, a nested pattern or an expression arm. The arm's binding owns the part the aggregate owned, as in the statement form, and destroys it at the end of the arm on every backend. D-373 A function whose result is declared from a parameter could return a value of its own ( return empty.get(0) ?? move d , return move d , a fresh value): both checkers accepted it, the caller held it as a loan and nobody ran its cleanup on the interpreter or the VM, and native code refused the program. Both checkers reject it with E-OWN-051 , as a loan of a local returned; and a from result the callee gave as a value of its own is owned by what receives it on the interpreter and the VM, so the cleanup runs even where a check is missed. D-374 A closure capturing a binding that holds a loan by move t , and moving the capture into a tuple, ran the borrowed element's cleanup inside the closure on the VM, while the list still owned it. A capture the closure does not own is a loan its body holds: moving it on moves the loan, and the tuple destroys nothing it borrows, on every backend. D-377 A function whose result is declared from a parameter could return a match or an if one of whose arms gives a value of its own ( present(x) => x , null => move d ): both checkers accepted it, the interpreter and the VM ran the fallback's cleanup and native code refused the program. Rejected with E-OWN-051 by the rule of D-381: every arm of a from result must lend. D-378 A binding of a ?? whose fallback it owns ( let t = empty.get(0) ?? move d ), captured by a closure with move t and consumed by move f() , was destroyed by native code when the enclosing block ended: after the call returned, and after a tuple the body moved it into and that tuple's other elements. The closure takes the binding's holder, so it owns the value and destroys it when the consuming call ends, or in index order inside a tuple the body moved it into, as the interpreter and the VM do. D-380 Native code destroyed a binding of a ?? whose fallback it owns at Rust's scope end rather than with its block: after a local declared before it, without the drop trace event; and a binding moved from it ( let q = move t ) left the value with the first binding's block. The binding's holder is destroyed in its block's exit, in reverse declaration order, traced and dropped only when it owns the value; a binding moved from it takes the holder. D-381 A function whose result "},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#defects-fixed","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Defects fixed","m":"home","x":"nly when it owns the value; a binding moved from it takes the holder. D-381 A function whose result is declared from a parameter could return a tuple or a record built around a value of its own ( return (empty.get(0) ?? move d, 1) from a result alias (Token, Int) from empty ), from a function or a closure, or present of one: both checkers accepted it, the interpreter ran the fallback's cleanup , the VM lost it and native code refused the program. One rule in both checkers: a from result is a loan, so what produces it must lend, and anything the function constructs is E-OWN-051 . The VM reports a value the callee constructed as the callee's own, so the caller's binding runs the cleanup even where a check is missed. D-385 A function whose result is declared from a parameter could recover a value of its own into it ( return lookup(alias xs, want) or recover move d , or recover move d in a catch handler): both checkers accepted it, the interpreter ran the fallback's cleanup , the VM lost it and native code refused the program. The rule of D-381 enters an outcome expression in both checkers: its value side and every recover must lend, E-OWN-051 otherwise. The VM decides an or recover or catch by the side it took, as it does a ?? , so the caller's binding runs the cleanup even where a check is missed. D-386 A function whose result is a view declared from a parameter could return an interpolated string ( StringView from xs { return \"count ${xs.count()}\" } ), a fresh String the caller would hold as a loan of what ends with the call, or a literal where a String loan is declared: both checkers accepted them, the interpreter and the VM ran the program and native code refused the interpolation. Both checkers reject them with E-OWN-051 . A literal without interpolation where a view is declared stays accepted: it is a view of static storage ( LR 6.6 ) and builds on every backend. D-391 A deferred block that named a local only inside a string interpolation ( defer { console.print(\"read ${t.label}\") } ) was invisible to the defer analysis of both checkers, so return t moved the local out without E-DEF-002 , and a loan-holding binding of the block so named escaped E-OWN-052 ; the interpreter then read the returned value, the VM stopped the deferred block silently with exit 0 and native code refused the program. Both checkers collect the names inside a deferred block's interpolation holes with its bare ones, so E-DEF-002 and E-OWN-052 fire for either spelling. Where a check is missed, the VM lets a deferred block's dynamic failure win over the block's return , and the interpreter treats a returned local as moved, so both stop loudly. D-411 A value of a trivial class returned past a deferred block that reads the local ( defer { console.print(\"bye ${p.x}\") } then return p ), alone or inside a tuple, a record or an enum payload, stopped the interpreter with a dynamic failure ( p was moved), while both checkers accept the program and the VM and native code run it; v0.0.1 ran it everywhere. The interpreter reads a class's trivial modifier, so return p copies the value as the checkers say it does ( LR 6.11 , 7.5 ), and every backend prints the same. D-412 Both checkers rejected a string literal whose only ${ is escaped ( StringView from xs => \"a\\${b}c\" ) where a view from a parameter is declared, with E-OWN-051 , though \\${ opens no hole and every backend prints it literally; v0.0.1 accepted it. Both checkers decide \"without interpolation\" by the lexer's recorded holes, as the defer analysis does, so the escaped form lends as a view of static storage and a real interpolation stays E-OWN-051 ; LR 3.6 says that \\${ is text. D-307, D-308 and D-309 were found by migrating this repository's own code with fortis fmt . D-240, D-242, D-313, D-314, D-319, D-323, D-324 and D-329 were found by the release candidate's review, and D-243 while fixing what it found. The review also found that a ?? whose fallback is an or fail did not build natively; native code now"},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#defects-fixed","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Defects fixed","m":"home","x":"The review also found that a ?? whose fallback is an or fail did not build natively; native code now builds it, and it runs the same on every backend. The second candidate's re-check found D-246 and the newtypes, containers and class names D-242 had left, and that fortisc test wrote a test's log records but not the failures it reports ( report e ); it now writes both, in order, as fortis test does. The third candidate's re-check found D-335, D-336, D-337, D-338 (there since v0.0.1), D-339 and D-340, and that fortisc test wrote its records on another stream than fortis test , named a file by its absolute path and left out the failing line; it now writes the same records, on the same streams. D-341 was found while fixing them, and D-345 while fixing D-338. The fourth candidate's re-check found D-348 (there since v0.0.1), D-349, D-350 and D-351, and that the VM decided which handle of a value owned it by counting handles, so an owner that ended while a loan still held the value skipped its cleanup and the loan then dropped it without one; the interpreter and the VM now decide by ownership alone, and run a value's cleanup at most once even in a program the checker should have refused. The fifth candidate's re-check found D-366 and D-367, and D-368 was found while fixing them. The seventh candidate's re-check found D-372 and D-373, and D-374 (there since v0.0.1, when the VM ran the cleanup twice), which is fixed with them; the sweep that followed found D-375 and D-376, which v0.0.1 also had and which are recorded for v0.1, D-377, and D-378, native code's late destruction of a captured ?? fallback, fixed before the eighth candidate; the sweep of that fix found D-379, which v0.0.1 also had and which is recorded for v0.1, and D-380, native code's late destruction of such a binding at its block's end, fixed with D-378. The eighth candidate's re-check found D-381, a tuple or a record around the fallback, which the check of D-373 did not enter; one rule for what a from result may be made of replaces the walk of each shape, and fixes D-377 with it. The ninth candidate's re-check (Kimi K3 on OpenCode, and GLM 5.3) found D-385, an or recover or catch whose recovery is the function's own, which that rule did not enter, and D-386, an interpolated string returned as a view from a parameter, which it judged by the text's recorded type; the rule now enters an outcome expression and judges text by what it is. The wide review of the ninth candidate found D-391, a deferred block that named a local only inside an interpolation, which the checkers let return t move out: the unmarked return t is new in this release, so a program v0.0.1 refused loudly ran wrong silently on two backends; the checkers now read a deferred block's interpolation holes for the places they name, and the VM and the interpreter stop loudly where such a check is missed. The wide review also found the documentation's stale claims, corrected here: two known-limitations rows that no longer reproduced, on v0.0.1 either (D-52.9, D-55.11), a clause each of D-344's and D-360's that native code handles, the rustc code D-156's row cited, the guide's claim that every integer type has the named operations (only Int has them), the pages that taught v0.1, v0.2 and v0.3 material without a preview marking, the agent mode's missing row for E-NAM-034 , and a test record it showed without the line that fortisc test now writes. The tenth candidate's re-check found D-411 and D-412 in the fixes themselves, fixed in place for the eleventh candidate, the release."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#programs-now-rejected","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Programs now rejected","m":"home","x":"Programs now rejected # Some programs that the earlier checkers accepted were unsound, and are now rejected. Each has a sound form. A let pattern that can fail to match (D-243): let End(value: a) = move c is E-MAT-004 . Write a match that handles the other cases. A loan where an owned value is expected (D-341). func first(xs: List[Item]) Item? { return xs.get(0) } is E-OWN-001 , because get lends the list's element. Bind the loan and copy it, let v = xs.get(0) then return copy v , or match it, match xs.get(0) { present(w) => copy w ... } . A value that cannot be copied (a Shared , a class holding a capability) is E-OWN-002 : keep the loan, or take the value out of its owner. The same holds for a ?? whose result can be a loan: return s ?? fallback with s a read parameter is written return copy s ?? fallback . An error whose message names a field its payload does not have ( message: \"late ${hour}\" with no hour ) is E-NAM-001 : the rendered message would show it unfilled. Name a payload field, or add it to the payload. A class whose derived default contains itself (D-335, D-336): class Node derive Default { next: Node } is E-TYP-090 . Make the field optional ( next: Node? ) or give it an initialiser. A change to an owner while a binding holds a loan of its element (D-348): after let t = xs.get(0) , xs.pop() , xs.push(\u2026) or xs.set(\u2026) is E-OWN-010 and move xs is E-OWN-011 until t 's block ends, after t 's last use too, since loans are lexical ( LR 7.3 ). The same holds for xs.first() , m.get(k) , a cursor's item and a tuple, record or ?? that holds the loan. Close a block around the loan, or copy what it lends, before changing the owner. Native code already refused these programs when the loan is used after the change; when its last use comes first, every backend ran them. A value of the function's own returned where the result is declared from a parameter (D-373, D-377, D-381, D-385, D-386): func pick(xs: List[Token], d: owned Token) alias Token from xs { return xs.get(0) ?? move d } is E-OWN-051 , because the caller would hold a loan of a value that ended with the call. A from result is a loan, so what produces it must lend: a place read through a loan, alias p , a call whose result lends, a ?? both of whose sides lend, an if or match every one of whose results lends, an or recover or catch whose value side and every recover lend, present of one, or, where the result is a view, a literal without interpolation, which is a view of static storage ( StringView from xs => \"plain\" is accepted). Anything the function constructs is rejected: return move d , a copy, a fresh value, an interpolated string ( StringView from xs { return \"count ${xs.count()}\" } allocates a String ), a literal where a String loan is declared ( alias String from xs => \"plain\" converts by allocating), a tuple or record literal ( return (xs.get(0) ?? move d, 1) ), a constructor, a lambda, a call whose result is owned, and a ?? , if , match , or recover or catch any of whose results is one of these ( null => move d , or recover move d , recover move d in the handler), at any depth; a trivial value holds nothing and is exempt. Bind the loan and return an owned value from a function returning Token , let v = xs.get(0) then return copy v ?? move d , or lend on every path; a value that cannot be copied is taken out of its owner, or the caller keeps the fallback. The interpreter and the VM ran such a program without the fallback's cleanup , or the VM alone did for a tuple, a record or a recovery; native code refused it. A deferred block that names, only inside a string interpolation, a local the function moves out (D-391): defer { console.print(\"read ${t.label}\") } followed by return t , return move t or eat(move t) is E-DEF-002 , as the bare console.print(t.label) always was, and defer { discard \"${seen.used}\" } where seen holds a loan of its block is E-OWN-052 . A name inside a hole is a use of the place ( LR 3.6 ). Read the local in the body and keep the deferred blo"},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#programs-now-rejected","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Programs now rejected","m":"home","x":"inside a hole is a use of the place ( LR 3.6 ). Read the local in the body and keep the deferred block to what stays, or return another value. The interpreter ran such a program reading the moved value, the VM stopped the deferred block silently with exit 0, and native code refused it."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#moving-your-code","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Moving your code","m":"home","x":"Moving your code # sh bin/fortis fmt --write PATH fortis fmt now runs the v0.0.2 migrations before laying the code out ( --no-migrate turns them off). It drops a Void return type, a receiver marker the declaration already shows and the move of return move local ; it moves main 's capabilities into its parameters and drops export and an unused args ; it removes a test's or fail and a map lookup's key copy; and it writes puns, or-patterns, checked operators and bare variants. Each rewrite is kept only where the program means the same: the module must check before and after, on its own or, for a module of a package, in its package. A rejected module gets only the syntactic rewrites that leave its records where they were. The formatter prints how many rewrites it made to each file on standard error. This repository was migrated that way: the examples, the pilot application, both acceptance applications, the library and the conformance corpus, and every example of the guide, the agent mode, the README and the specifications except the Language Reference, which states both forms. A documentation example that demonstrates an open defect is written in the new forms too, and the defect register records its new text. Fixtures that pin an older form on purpose keep it."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#known-limitations","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Known limitations","m":"home","x":"Known limitations # Known limitations lists the open defects in this release's scope, each with a workaround ( agent mode ). v0.0.2 adds these to them, in a section of their own: The older forms are accepted alongside the new ones. A reader may meet both until code is migrated. fortis fmt does not rewrite a logging call, add @ignorable or derive Default , or rewrite a main that reads its Capabilities record other than field by field (a capability inside an interpolation, or the record moved whole). Those are left to the author. fortis fmt never rewrites Int.shiftLeft(a, b) or fail to a << b or fail , although the two are the same operation (D-404), and it keeps a pure or noalloc written on a lambda, although the function type the lambda is passed to already states it (D-406). Both are cosmetic: write the shorter form by hand. The Language Reference's own example of an exclusive loan returned from a parameter ( LR 7.6 , editable Cell from c returning editable c ), like any returned exclusive loan, does not build natively; the interpreter and the VM run it (D-185, there since v0.0.1 and recorded for v0.1; now on the known-limitations page). Checked operators cover Int only. The sized integer types have no checked, wrapping or saturating operations yet (planned for v0.4), so an unproved operator on one needs a refined type or a test. A module of a package is not given its inferred facts back by the formatter unless it checks on its own. The fourth, fifth and seventh candidates' re-checks, and the work on D-348 and D-366, found defects that v0.0.1 already had. Under the rule that a release is no worse than the last (X-416) they are recorded for v0.1, each with a reproduction, and the known-limitations page lists them in a section of their own, \"Defects carried from v0.0.1\", with the shape to avoid: a generic body is not checked for a loan made owned (D-352); a loan given out of an if or match branch, or lent by a match 's temporary scrutinee, ends too early (D-355); a field read through an optional loan with ?. is typed as owned (D-356); an owned place written without a marker on the left of a ?? whose result is owned is accepted, so two bindings own one value (D-357: write move some ?? fallback ); a loan held inside an optional is treated as ended after a loop that may skip the statement ending it (D-362); a defer may read a binding of its block that holds a loan inside an optional (D-364); a binding of a ?? whose fallback it owns can be copied into a binding that outlives it (D-369), and the interpreter runs that fallback's cleanup when an unmarked copy of the binding ends (D-370); a ?? whose fallback is the statement's own, lent to a read parameter, is never destroyed on the interpreter or the VM (D-375); the interpreter loses a record field's old value's cleanup when the field is assigned (D-376); a fn once closure may return a loan from a move capture that the consuming call destroys (D-379); a tuple built around a value of the function's own, bound first and returned inside present as a from result, passes both checkers, and the interpreter destroys the value at the call while the VM never does (D-388); and an if nested in an arm of a returned match , giving a loan on one branch and the function's own value on the other, passes both checkers, and both running backends destroy the value at the call (D-389); a loan of a call's temporary receiver ( fresh().get(0) ) outlives the temporary (D-392); a pure function prints through the cleanup of a value it drops (D-393); a lent match payload is destroyed at the arm's end on the interpreter, and for a plain optional on the VM (D-394); and a from result given out of an if or produced by an expression body may lend the wrong parameter (D-395). Avoid those shapes: the checkers accept them. Native code does not yet build a function whose result is an optional loan of text (D-353), or that unmarked ?? when its left side is null (D-354), or a generic function whose result is an optional l"},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#known-limitations","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"Known limitations","m":"home","x":" unmarked ?? when its left side is null (D-354), or a generic function whose result is an optional loan of its type parameter instantiated at a class (D-387), or a byte string as a BytesView from xs result and an if giving a string literal beside a view through a loan (D-390), or the bind-first spelling of a bearing aggregate from result ( let t = (xs.get(0) ?? move d, 1) then return t ), so such a result has no spelling native code builds yet (D-360); and the interpreter stops on a marker written on a call of a module function ( alias first(alias xs) , where the marker belongs on the argument alone), which the VM and native code run (D-382). Native code does not build first() on a list of a class (D-396), nor an anonymous record returned past a deferred block that reads it, whatever its fields (D-415, found by the fixture of D-411), and every backend refuses Array.filled sized by its expected type (D-399); I/O through a local's capability field is refused by the VM and mislabelled an internal defect natively (D-397); a binding that holds a loan is charged the lent type's cleanup effects (D-400); a let mut cursor cannot be reassigned (D-401); and after match move o of a binding that holds a loan the owner stays frozen, a limit of this release's loan holders (D-402). Each is on the known-limitations page. The new loan rules also reject two valid programs: a tuple that holds a loan cannot yet be reassigned or returned (D-363), and a defer cannot change a list while its block holds a loan from it inside an optional (D-365). All four are on the known-limitations page."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#how-it-was-checked","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"How it was checked","m":"home","x":"How it was checked # The release workflow passes at the release's tag: the gate's full tier with every step, cargo test in release and debug, the determinism check (the compiler from two clean clones, and native programs built twice, byte for byte) and a secrets scan. The three backends agree on every conformance program, and every documentation example is checked by both checkers and run on the interpreter, the VM and native. Every change was reviewed, and each of the three implementation streams passed the full gate before merging. Six release candidates. The first had a review of each implementation stream by Codex and a whole-release review by Codex and Grok; Codex's review of the expressions stream was blocked by its provider's content filter, so Grok reviewed that area. Each later candidate had a re-check by Codex and Grok that re-ran every earlier reproduction. The re-checks of the second, third and fourth candidates found blocking defects, each fixed before the next candidate. At the fifth, Codex was blocked by the same filter twice, so Grok ran the re-check and then a second pass on the runtime changes, which found D-366 and D-367 in the fourth candidate's own fixes. The fifth candidate's release run also failed in one debug-build test, whose thread was too small for the checker; the sixth candidate fixed the test, and the seventh fixed D-366, D-367 and D-368. The seventh candidate's re-check, by Codex and Grok, found D-372 and D-373 in those fixes and the loan rules, and D-374, which v0.0.1 also had; the eighth candidate fixed them, and D-378, which the fix's own sweep found. The eighth candidate's re-check (Codex alone: Grok's usage balance was exhausted) found D-381, the tuple-shaped neighbour of D-373. Rather than fix one more shape, the ninth candidate replaces the shape-by-shape rule with a structural one: a from result must be made of an expression that lends. Its re-check ran on OpenCode, with Kimi K3 and GLM 5.3 through Fireworks, two model families; it found one branch the rule had missed (D-385, an owned recovery value) and two shapes that evade it, which v0.0.1 also accepted and which are recorded (D-388, D-389). Rather than another narrow round, one wide review of the ninth candidate then ran, by scope, on four OpenCode runs: the loan and cleanup rules as a matrix, the seventeen reading changes and the formatter, and the release as a whole against its documents. They found one defect this release introduced (D-391, a defer that names a local only inside an interpolation, which R16's unmarked return then moves), eleven that v0.0.1 also had, recorded for v0.1, four cosmetic gaps in the formatter's rewrites, recorded as limits of this release, and fourteen stale or missing statements in the documentation, corrected. The tenth candidate fixes everything those reviews found that this release introduced or made worse, and its own re-check covered those fixes alone, on Kimi K3 and GLM 5.3: every fix held, and both reviewers found the same two defects in the fixes themselves (D-411, the interpreter's new rule for a returned local not knowing a trivial class ; D-412, an escaped \\${ misread as an interpolation), fixed in place for the eleventh candidate, with no further review round. The release is the eleventh candidate. This release makes no performance claims. Performance is judged at v1.0."},{"u":"docs/releases/v0.0.2.html#what-is-next","p":"Fortis v0.0.2","h":"What is next","m":"home","x":"What is next # v0.1 is for modelling a domain: refinements ( LR 6.3 ), newtypes ( LR 6.9 ), generics ( LR 6.10 ), interfaces and mixins ( LR 9.5 , LR 9.6 ), linear containers ( LR 10.7 ), and contracts ( LR 11.5 ). After that, v0.2 brings effects, claims and property tests; v0.3 async and tasks; v0.4 the rest of the standard library and the core runtime; v0.5 observation and kits."}];
